window.__QB=(window.__QB||[]);window.__QB.push("adrenaline.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline\",\"e\":\"Noradrenaline, not adrenaline, is the most abundant post-ganglionic neurotransmitter.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renin-angiotensin\",\"e\":\"The renin-angiotensin system is involved in the maintenance of fluid status and blood pressure, but is not used in the post-ganglionic transmission of impulses.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Acetylcholinesterase\",\"e\":\"This enzyme breaks down acetylcholine and, although is involved in neurotransmission, it is not a neurotransmitter as such\"}]},{\"t\":51,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"You are assessing a 75-year-old man for their fitness for a massive colorectal procedure to remove a large tumour that seems to be invo ving the bony pelvis. You have not met them before and they appear frail.\\n\\nWhat is the most common cause of autonomic dysfunction in patients like this?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most common cause of autonomic dysfunction in patients like this?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Multiple system atrophy\",\"e\":\"This progressive fatal disorder presents most commonly with loss of coordination along with autonomic dysfunction. It is twice as common in men but overall is rare. Some unusual presentations of Parkinson's disease are misdiagnosed as multiple system atrophy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS)\",\"e\":\"At around 0.3/100 000, this is a rare autoimmune disorder, which often presents as a result of a paraneoplastic syndrome in small-cell lung cancer. However, only a tiny fraction of those with small-cell cancer actually have LEMS.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Parkinson's disease\",\"e\":\"Parkinson's disease is the second most common degenerative disorder after Alzheimer's disease. The prevalence of autonomic dysfunction varies greatly between studies due to the methodology used, but has been reported to be as high as 80%.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spinal trauma\",\"e\":\"Trauma to the spinal cord can damage its autonomic function at the level of injury. Bladder and bowel control are the most frequently affected. This, however, remains a relatively uncommon cause of autonomic dysfunction compared with other causes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pure autonomic failure\",\"e\":\"This occurs in around 0.1-0.9/100 000 and can present with orthostatic hypotension, erectile dysfunction, urinary system dysfunction and even Horner syndrome. =>^=\"}]},{\"t\":51,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old patient is admitted with a mass in her left thorax around 1cm lateral to her spine at the level of T8. Where are most of the ganglia for the sympathetic nervous system found?\",\"lead\":\"Where are most of the ganglia for the sympathetic nervous system found?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left paraspinal region\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic chain sits just lateral to the spine on both sides of the spine and are the location of ganglia that connect the pre-ganglionic nerve fibres to the post-ganglionic nerve fibres.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Right parasinal region a N\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic chain sits just lateral to the spine on both sides of the spine, and are the location of ganglia that connect the pre-ganglionic nerve fibres to the post-ganglionic nerve\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Paraspinally, bilaterally\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic chain sits paraspinally from the upper neck to the coccyx. It is the site of connections of pre- and post-ganglionic nerve fibres of the sympathetic chain.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Within the spine\",\"e\":\"The ganglia are the site of connection of the pre- and post-ganglionic nerve fibres; they sit outside the spine bilaterally.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Adjacent to end-organs\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic chain sits adjacent to the spine from the upper neck to the coccyx; it is the site of connections for the sympathetic nervous system. Parasympathetic ganglia lie adjacent to the end-organs that they innervate.\"}]},{\"t\":51,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman is seen in the clinic suffering from focal hyperhidrosis of the trunk and upper limbs. She has had a botulinum toxin injection and her symptoms have, again worsened. She has been referred for possible surgical intervention What would be a potential surgical procedure that could be offered?\",\"lead\":\"She has been referred for possible surgical intervention What would be a potential surgical procedure that could be offered?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"No surgical intervention\",\"e\":\"Although surgical intervention for such conditions is controversial, many patients with uncontrolled symptoms will be willing to attempt an intervention that has shown some success.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"cervical sympathetony\",\"e\":\"This would not affect her upper limb sympathetic innervation but, importantly, it would lead to Horner syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Upper thoracic sympathectomy\",\"e\":\"Yes, a sympathectomy of the upper thoracic sympathetic chain could help her symptoms. The most common approach today is a video-assisted thoracoscopic surgical (VATS) procedure, sometimes bilaterally at one operation, sometimes staged. Ti would be spared to avoid Horner syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lower thoracic sympathectomy\",\"e\":\"This would not target the trunk or upper limbs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lumbar sympathectomy\",\"e\":\"Although this would be possible for hyperhidrosis, it wouldn't target the trunk or upper limbs, so it wouldn't be useful in the case described. 5330\"}]},{\"t\":51,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old is seen with a dry mouth on one side after a previous injury to the same side of the head\\n\\nWhich of the following are cranial parasympathetic ganglia?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following are cranial parasympathetic ganglia?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Otic\",\"e\":\"The otic ganglia receive parasympathetic innervation from the inferior salivary nucleus by the glossopharyngeal nerve, along with the lesser petrosal nerve. It has sympathetic branches passing through from the superior cervical ganglion, a sensory root to the parotic gland, and a motor supply to the medial pterygoid, tensor tympani and tensor veli palatini muscies.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Submandibular\",\"e\":\"The parasympathetic root comes from the superior salivary nucleus through the facial nerve, chorda tympani and lingual nerve to supply the oral mucosa and submandibular and sublingual glands. Sympathetic roots come with the facial artery from the carotid plexus. and a sensory root via the lingual nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pterygopalatine\",\"e\":\"This has parasympathetics from the greater petrosal branch of the facial nerve to the mucous membranes, via deep branches of the trigeminal nerve. Sympathetic branches arise from the superior cervical ganglion. There is a sensory component passing through the ganglion, from the sphenopalatine branches of the maxillary nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ciliary\",\"e\":\"The parasympathetic roots come from the Edinger-Westphal part of the oculomotor nucleus. Sympathetic branches that pass through come from the superior cervical ganglion, and a sensory root is from a branch of the nasociliary nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"All are cranial parasympathetic ganglia\",\"e\":\"Correct, all four are cranial parasympathetic ganglia where pre- and post-ganglionic parasympathetic fibres synapse. They also have sympathetic and sensory branches that pass through the ganglia without synapsing.\"}]},{\"t\":51,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old patient suffering from end-stage cardiomyopathy receives a heart transplant. What effect does removal of the vagal innervation have on the resting heart rate?\",\"lead\":\"What effect does removal of the vagal innervation have on the resting heart rate?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"RI Increases heart rate\",\"e\":\"The vagal tone slows the heart rate and so removal increases the heart rate\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreases heart rate\",\"e\":\"The vagal tone acts in a parasympathetic way, so its removal allows the resting heart rate to increase.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has no effect on heart rate\",\"e\":\"Vagal tone in a resting patient inhibits the rate of the sinoatrial node.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Causes asystole\",\"e\":\"A myocyte, and the heart as an organ, will continue to beat at a set speed as long as they receive oxygenated blood.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A ventricular escape rhythm\",\"e\":\"The vagus acts on the sinoatrial node. Atrioventricular node conduction is not blocked by removal of vagal innervation.\\n\\nEach myocyte has its own intrinsic rate. A contraction is usually initiated by the cell with the fastest intrinsic rate, the sinoatrial (SA) node. At rest the SA node is under significant vagal influence, which acts in a parasympathetic manner, slowing heart rate. Removal of this speeds the heart rate up.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"Regarding the cranial foramina, which of the following statements is the correct answer?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding the cranial foramina, which of the following statements is the correct answer?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The cribriform plate allows passage of the optic nerve\",\"e\":\"The cribriform plate allows passage of the olfactory nerve, whereas the optic nerve passes through the optic canal in the sphenoid bone, along with the ophthalmic artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The foramen lacerum allows passage of the external carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The foramen lacerum is located in the middle cranial foramen. It allows passage of the meningeal branch of the ascending pharyngeal artery and the emissary vein (coming from the cavernous sinus), throughout its whole length. Structures that only partially transverse the foramen lacerum are the internal carotid artery and the petrosal nerves (greater and lesser).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The foramen ovale allows passage of the mandibular nerve only\",\"e\":\"The foramen ovale allows passage of multiple structurest: otic ganglion (lies just inferior). V3 cranial nerve (mandibular branch of trigeminal nerve), accessory meningeal artery, lesser petrosal nerve, and emissary veins.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The foramen rotundum allows passage of the maxillary nerve\",\"e\":\"There are no vessels passing through the foramen rotundum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The internal acoustic meatus allows passage of the facial nerve only\",\"e\":\"The internal acoustic meatus allows passage of the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves, as well as the labyrinthine artery.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A patient is found to have a pituitary adenoma on CT imaging of the brain following investigation of Cushing syndrome. They undergo detailed visual field testing. The classic visual field defect produced by a pituitary adenoma will be which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The classic visual field defect produced by a pituitary adenoma will be which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Homonymous hemianopia\",\"e\":\"The cause of a homonymous hemianopia may be congenital, or secondary trauma, stroke, or neoplastic lesions occurring from the optic tract to the visual cortex.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bitemporal hemianopia\",\"e\":\"The central location of the pituitary gland within the sella turcica causes compression of the medial aspects of the optic chiasm. The resultant visual field defect is bitemporal hemianopia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bitemporal inferior quadrantanopia\",\"e\":\"Craniopharingioma may initially present as a lower bitemporal quadrantopia, the lesion is usually found superior to the optic chiasm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Binasal hemianopia\",\"e\":\"This visual field defect is rarely seen in clinical practice. It is caused by bilateral lesions affecting the uncrossed optic fibres for example atheroma/calcification of the internal carotid arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Unilateral visual loss\",\"e\":\"Unilateral field loss is caused by a lesion that is anterior to the optic chiasm.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A patient has suspected meningitis and is started immediately on broad spectrum antibiotics. To obtain MC&S to further guide therapy a lumbar puncture is performed Cerebrospinal fluid is connected to the subarachnoid space from the ventricles via which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"To obtain MC&S to further guide therapy a lumbar puncture is performed Cerebrospinal fluid is connected to the subarachnoid space from the ventricles via which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cisterna magna\",\"e\":\"The cisterna magna is the largest of the 3 main openings in the subarachnoid space, located between the cerebellum and the dorsal surface of the medulla oblongata. The CSF produced in the ventricular system drains into it from the fourth ventricle via the foramina of Magendie and Lushka.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Arachnoid villi\",\"e\":\"This is where CS is reabsorbed to return to the venous system.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Choroid plexus\",\"e\":\"CSF is produced by the choroid plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Foramen of Monro\",\"e\":\"The interventricular foramen of Monro connects the two lateral ventricles to the third ventricle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Foramina of Magendie and Lushka\",\"e\":\"The foramina of Magendie (midline) and Lushka (lateral) in the roof of the fourth ventricle communicate directly into the subarachnoid space.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A patient has suspected meningitis and is started immediately on broad spectrum antibiotics. To obtain MC&S to further guide therapy a lumbar puncture is performed. Which of the following statements is correct regarding cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements is correct regarding cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is produced by specialist endothelial cells covering the choroid plexus\",\"e\":\"CSF is produced largely by the choroid plexus, which are capillary loops covered by specialised ependymal cells located in the ventricular system.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It has a similar composition to plasma EXCEPT for glucose\",\"e\":\"CSF has lower concentrations of potassium, glucose and protein but greater concentrations of sodium and chloride than blood\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is produced at 1,5ml/min allowing it to be turned over 4 times a day\",\"e\":\"It is produced at a rate of 0.35-0.5 ml/min.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"About half the volume is distributed in the ventricles at any one time\",\"e\":\"CSF is distributed approximately as 20ml in the ventricles, 45ml in the cranial subarachnoid space, and the rest in the spinal subarachnoid space and central canal.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It plays a role in providing nutritive support\",\"e\":\"CSF is important in the regulation of brain function in many ways, for example:\\n• CSF pH and PCO2 on respiration\\n• CSF sodium chloride on vasopressin (AVP)\\n• CSF carriage of neuromodulators, eg opioids\\n\\nIt plays a minor role in supplying glucose to inaccessible areas also.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with reduction in hearing and worsening tinnitus. They undergo an MRI head which demonstrates a probable acoustic neuroma Which one of the following statements regarding acoustic neuroma is correct?\",\"lead\":\"They undergo an MRI head which demonstrates a probable acoustic neuroma Which one of the following statements regarding acoustic neuroma is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acoustic neuromas make up 1% of all intracranial tumours\",\"e\":\"They make up around 8% of all intracranial tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The majority are bilateral\",\"e\":\"The majority are unilateral (approximately 95%).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Recurring and severe headache is a common presenting symptom\",\"e\":\"Recurring and severe headaches are uncommon and occur only with very large tumours. Common symptoms include hearing loss, tinnitus, pressure in the ear, and vertigo. The most common presenting symptom is hearing loss.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Acoustic neuromas arise from Schwann cells\",\"e\":\"Acoustic neuroma is also known as a vestibular schwannoma, it is a benign, slow growing tumour that arises from the Schwann cells of the vestibulocochlear (8\\\"h) cranial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Most patients have normal hearing\",\"e\":\"Up to 90% of patients present with a gradual progressive unilateral deafness, often associated with tinnitus.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 79-year-old man is seen in the Emergency Department after collapsing at home. He has a headache and appears confused. He has atrial fibrillation and is on digoxin and warfarin. There is no evidence of head injury. His Mini Mental scoré is 24/30, and he is drowsy but readily roused. He has mild weakness of the left arm and leg and the left plantar response is\\n\\nUrgent blood results:\\n\\nResult Normal value Investigation\\n\\n13:5-18 g/l 11.1 g/l Haemoglobin (Hb)\\n\\nInternational normalised ratio (INR) 5.7 2-3\\n\\n9.6 mmol/l 2.5-6.7 mmol/l Urea\\n\\nOther blood tests are within the normal range An urgent CT scan is requested. What is this most likely to show?\",\"lead\":\"What is this most likely to show?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Intraparenchymal haematoma in the right temporal lobe with mass effect\",\"e\":\"Intraparenchymal haematoma usually occurs in the context of trauma, hypertension or presence of an underlying neoplastic lesion. It is less common than a subdural haematoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Right sided extradural haematoma\",\"e\":\"Other forms of intracranial bleeding may of course occur in the context of coagulopathy but are less common. Extradural haematoma is associated with significant head trauma\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Right sided chronic subdural haematoma\",\"e\":\"Right-sided chronic subdural haematoma (Option C) is incorrect. Chronic subdural haematoma presents with a much longer, insidious course (often over weeks/months). In this case, the occurrence of headache, impaired conscious level and focal signs, as well as the over-anticoagulation, make an acute subdural haematoma much more likely.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right sided acute subdural haematoma:\",\"e\":\"This elderly man is on warfarin therapy for atrial fibrillation and the current level of anticoagulation is higher than the usual therapeutic limits (INR is normally kept between 2 and 3 for atrial fibrillation). In such patients, an acute subdural haematoma can occur without significant head injury. The history of trauma followed by fluctuating confusion and conscious level in an elderly patient is a classical presentation of an acute subdural haematoma.\\n\\nThe elderly in general are more at risk of subdural haematomas because of various factors, including thinner cortical bridging veins that tear readily, increased subdural space, as well as increased probability of falls and use of medications that alter blood clotting (including aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right sided subarachnoid haemorrhage\",\"e\":\"Right-sided subarachnoid haemorrhage (Option E) is incorrect. Subarachnoid haemorrhage is usually associated with significant trauma or a ruptured aneurysm.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with sudden onset of incoherent speech. Examination reveals right-sided weakness in the upper and lower extremities, right facial droop, and reduced sensation in the upper and lower extremities. A CT scan of the head shows no acute changes. Treatment with tissue plasminogen activator is initiated. Which of the following arterial territories is most likely compromised by this neurologic event?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following arterial territories is most likely compromised by this neurologic event?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Posterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"Posterior cerebral artery (Option A) is incorrect. The posteroventral nucleus of the thalamus and inferior temporal gyrus are supplied by the posterior cerebral artery. Infarctions involving the posterior cerebral artery result in contralateral hemianopsia with macular sparing.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"Anterior cerebral artery (Option B) is incorrect. The anterior one-third of the corpus callosum, including the superior frontal gyrus, is supplied by the anterior cerebral arteries. Infarctions involving the anterior cerebral artery result in contralateral paralysis and sensory loss of the lower limb.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Middle cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The middle cerebral artery (MCA) is the largest cerebral artery and is the most commonly affected vessel by cerebrovascular accident. The artery supplies the majority of the outer convex brain surface such as the lateral aspect of the parietal lobe, the basal ganglia and the posterior and anterior internal capsules. Infarctions that occur in the MCA distribution result in contralateral paralysis and sensory loss of the face and upper limb. Aphasia occurs if the infarction is in the dominant hemisphere, and hemineglect if the non-dominant side is affected\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery\",\"e\":\"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (Option D) is incorrect. PICA occlusion may cause infarction of the posterior inferior cerebellum, inferior cerebellar vermis and lateral medulla. Vertigo, nausea and truncal ataxia are the most common presenting features.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Basilar artery\",\"e\":\"Acute occlusion of the basilar artery may cause brainstem or thalamic ischaemia or infarction. Patients with acute occlusion of the basilar artery will present with sudden and dramatic neurological impairment.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old right-hand dominant man suddenly starts to mumble. He cannot understand what his wife is telling him and can neither write nor read. His wife also notices that he tries to grasp his glass of water but seems unable to lift his right arm. Terrified, she calls an ambulance and brings him to the A&E department. She tells you that he is a smoker and has been taking statins because of hypercholesterolaemia. Clinical examination reveals an irregularly irregular pulse, a blood pressure of 150/90 mmg, and a pulse of 97 beats per minute. Apart from not being able to communicate, you also notice that he has a sensory and motor deficit over the right part of his body and face. Which of the following arteries is most likely responsible for his symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following arteries is most likely responsible for his symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"AVI Basilar artery\",\"e\":\"Strokes involving the vertebrobasilar circulation lead to a focal brainstem syndrome. This patient's symptoms are caused by occlusion of the middle cerebral artery of the dominant hemisphere (left in this case).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left middle cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The most common stroke is from infarction in the territory of the middle cerebral artery (MCA), which does not usually cause a coma. The MCA supplies the lateral surface of the cortex and temporal pole. The cortical branches of the MCA supply the lateral surface of the hemisphere except for (1) the frontal pole and a strip along the superomedial border of the frontal and parietal lobes supplied by the anterior cerebral artery, and (2) the lower temporal and occipital pole convolutions supplied by the posterior cerebral artery. Occlusion of its inferior division is commonly associated with Wernicke's aphasia, while occlusion of the superior division causes Broca's aphasia. Strokes that involve the entire MCA distribution in the left hemisphere cause combined dysfunction of the two areas. Speech output is non-fluent, and comprehension of spoken language is severely impaired, as well as naming. repetition, reading and writing. Most patients are initially mute or say a few words. Related signs include right hemiplegia, hemisensory loss and homonymous hemianopia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Posterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"This patient's symptoms can be attributed to occlusion of the middle cerebral artery of the dominant hemisphere (left in this case). The posterior cerebral artery supplies the optic tract, cerebral peduncle, internal capsule and thalamus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Anterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"This patient's symptoms can be attributed to occlusion of the middle cerebral artery of the dominant hemisphere (left in this case), not the anterior. The anterior cerebral arteries supply most of the medial and superior surfaces and the frontal pole, while the middle cerebral arteries supply the lateral surface and temporal pole.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Internal carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid, apart from providing the primary supply to the brain via the anterior and middle cerebral arteries, also supplies the walls of the cavernous sinus, the pituitary gland and the trigeminal ganglion. Strokes related to the carotid artery and its cerebral branches are usually associated with focal epilepsy, a contralateral sensory/motor deficit and a psychological deficit. This answer is wrong as it would also give signs and symptoms from the occlusion of the anterior cerebral artery, not only of the middle as in the scenario.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man is seen with neck pain after lifting weights at home. A vertebral artery dissection is diagnosed. What do the vertebral arteries run into?\",\"lead\":\"What do the vertebral arteries run into?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The middle cerebral arteries\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Basilar artery\",\"e\":\"The two vertebral arteries branch from the respective subclavian arteries and then come together as the basilar artery.\\n\\nThe middle cerebral arteries are branches of the circle of Willis, chiefly supplied by the internal carotids.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Posterior communicating arteries\",\"e\":\"The posterior communicating arteries run between the posterior and middle cerebral arteries on both sides as part of the circle of Willis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The two vertebral arteries join to form the basilar artery and this then divides into the two posterior cerebral arteries\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Internal carotid arteries\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid arteries are a branch of their respective common carotid arteries and, although they do eventually join the circle of Willis, they are not connected to the vertebral arteries.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old man is admitted with a large periorbital abscess and is complaining of a headache. A provisional diagnosis of a cavernous sinus thrombosis is made Which one of the following does not pass through the cavernous sinus?\",\"lead\":\"A provisional diagnosis of a cavernous sinus thrombosis is made Which one of the following does not pass through the cavernous sinus?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Optic nerve\",\"e\":\"Although the optic nerve is closely related to the cavernous sinus, it does not pass directly through it.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Internal carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid artery passes through the sinus with its associated sympathetic nerves.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cranial nerve (CA) VE Y\",\"e\":\"The abducens nerve (CN VI) passes through the cavernous sinus and can be affected in thrombosis. It lies very close to the internal carotid artery whereas CN III, IV and V sit laterally in the wall of the cavernous sinus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sympathetics\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid artery passes through the cavernous sinus with the associated sympathetics\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Blood\",\"e\":\"The cavernous sinus sits anteriorly to the petrosal sinuses, which then connect it to the internal jugular vein. The cavernous sinus is a blood-filled space.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old man is involved in a road traffic collision and a CT scan of his head reveals a base-of-skull fracture with possible extension towards the foramen magnum What does not pass through the foramen magnum?\",\"lead\":\"A 75-year-old man is involved in a road traffic collision and a CT scan of his head reveals a base-of-skull fracture with possible extension towards the foramen magnum What does not pass through the foramen magnum?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Medulla oblongata\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Meninges\",\"e\":\"The meninges continue through the foramen to surround the spinal cord\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Accessory nerve\",\"e\":\"The spinal roots of the accessory nerve enter into the skull inferiorly through the foramen magnum; they exit through the jugular foramen with the vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The hypoglossal nerve\",\"e\":\"The hypoglossal nerve exits the skull through its own foramen (the hypoglossal foramen)\\n\\nThe lower end of the medulla passes through the foramen. Sometimes the cerebellar tonsils also pass, to a varying degree, inferiorly through t and therefore can be compressed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Apical ligament of the dens\",\"e\":\"This strong fibrous band connects the second cervical vertebra to the skull in the anterior portion of the foramen magnum.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old patient is brought into A&E after sustaining a head injury when travelling at 60mph on the motorway. He is showing evidence of raised intracranial pressure. What part of the central nervous system is not involved in control of the respiratory rate and depth?\",\"lead\":\"What part of the central nervous system is not involved in control of the respiratory rate and depth?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cerebral cortex\",\"e\":\"Although not normally required, the cerebral cortex can control the breathing rate and Gepth using conscious control.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Medulla oblongata\",\"e\":\"The medulla, where the respiratory group of neurones lies, includes the dorsal respiratory group and the ventral respiratory group.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pons\",\"e\":\"The pons includes the pneumotaxic and apneustic centres, the latter of which is under constant inhibition from the medulla.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypothalamus\",\"e\":\"The hypothalamus is one of the many influences on the respiratory centres that control breathing.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cerebellum\",\"e\":\"The cerebellum has no significant influence on the breathing rate or depth.\\n\\nAlthough not normally required, the cerebral cortex can control the breathing rate and depth using conscious control.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old woman suffers a postoperative stroke while in hospital for a hip replacement. What supplies the blood to the left temporal lobe?\",\"lead\":\"What supplies the blood to the left temporal lobe?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"R'i Posterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior cerebral artery is the terminal branch of the basilar artery and, although it does supply some of the temporal lobe, it is not the only vessel that does so.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Posterior cerebral artery (PCA) and middle cerebral artery (MCA)\",\"e\":\"Both the posterior and middle cerebral arteries supply the temporal lobe. The MCA supplies the superior aspect, whereas the PCA supplies the inferior surface. Similar to many regions of the brain, there is a watershed area in which two major origins of blood supply overlap.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Middle cerebral artery (MiCA)\",\"e\":\"The MCA does supply the temporal lobe; however, the posterior circulation also supplies a large segment of the inferior surface.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Middle cerebral artery and anterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The anterior cerebral artery is not involved in blood supply to the temporal lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Anterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The anterior cerebral artery does not contribute to the temporal lobe.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man who takes warfarin is admitted to neurosurgery with confusion and dizziness that have developed several days after he fell and hit his head. A CT scan of his brain reveals a subdural haematoma. The decision is taken to drill burr holes to relieve intracranial pressure.\\n\\nWhich one of these statements is most accurate regarding the meninges?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these statements is most accurate regarding the meninges?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The meninges comprises the outermost arachnoid mater, innermost pia mater and dura mater in the middle\",\"e\":\"The meninges comprises the outermost dura mater, innermost pia mater and arachnoid mater in between these two.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The arachnoid mater is thick and fibrous, and is in contact with the dura mater\",\"e\":\"The arachnoid mater is delicate and impermeable. It is in contact with the dura mater, which is thick and fibrous.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The pia mater invests brain and spinal cord tissue, and is poorly vascularised\",\"e\":\"The pia mater is the innermost layer of the meninges, and runs deep into the fissures. However, it is highly vascular.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Folds of the dura mater form the falx cerebri and the tentorium cerebelli\",\"e\":\"The falx cerebri is a crescent-shaped fold of dura mater that descends vertically. The tentorium cerebelli is an extension of the dura mater separating the occipital lobes from the cerebellum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The subdural space separates the arachnoid from the dura and contains cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and arachnoid strands\",\"e\":\"The subdural space separates the arachnoid from the dura but nothing lies in it except a thin film of lymph. The subarachnoid space contains CSF and the delicate meshwork of arachnoid strands.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"The magnetic resonance angiography images of an in-patient on the neurosurgery ward are reviewed. The patient has a basilar artery thrombosis: Which paired arteries are given off at the bifurcation of the basilar artery?\",\"lead\":\"The patient has a basilar artery thrombosis: Which paired arteries are given off at the bifurcation of the basilar artery?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The superior cerebellar arteries\",\"e\":\"The superior cerebellar arteries arise from the distal basilar artery. but not from its terminal bifurcation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The posterior cerebral arteries.\",\"e\":\"The basilar artery arises from the right and left vertebral arteries joining. It gives off numerous branches to the cerebellum and pons before ending as a bifurcation to form the paired posterior cerebral arteries. These supply the occipital and posteromedial temporal lobes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The middle cerebral arteries\",\"e\":\"The middle cerebral arteries arise from the internal carotid arteries. They supply most of the lateral hemispheres.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The anterior communicating arteries\",\"e\":\"The anterior communicating artery is not paired. It connects the right and left anterior cerebral arteries, which are formed as the end point of the internal carotid arteries. They supply the frontal and superior medial parietal lobes\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The ophthalmic arteries\",\"e\":\"The ophthalmic arteries arise from the internal carotid arteries to supply all the structures in the orbit\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with sudden, electric shock-like pains to the face. She is diagnosed with trigeminal neuralgia. Which one of these is the most correct with regard to the affected nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is the most correct with regard to the affected nerve?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The trigeminal nerve innervates the intrinsic muscles of the tongue\",\"e\":\"This statement would be true of the hypoglossal nerve, which supplies the intrinsic and most of the extrinsic muscles of the tongue. It passes laterally across the posterior cranial fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The trigeminal nerve is divided into three terminal divisions - the ophthalmic nerve [V11, the maxillary nerve [V2] and the facial nerve [V3]\",\"e\":\"The trigeminal nerve's three terminal divisions are the ophthalmic nerve [V1], the maxillary nerve [V2] and the mandibular nerve [V3]. The facial nerve is a separate cranial nerve [VIl].\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The trigeminal nerve's three terminal divisions exit the skull via the superior orbital fissure, the foramen rotundum and the foramen ovale\",\"e\":\"This is correct - the ophthalmic nerve [V1] exits from the superior orbital fissure, the maxillary nerve [V2] from the foramen rotundum and the mandibular nerve [V3] from the foramen ovale.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The trigeminal nerve is the major sensory nerve of the head, and is also the longest cranial nerve\",\"e\":\"Although the trigeminal nerve is the major general sensory nerve of the head and innervates some muscles of the lower jaw, it is the vagus nerve [X] that is the longest of the cranial nerves. However, the trigeminal nerve is the largest of the cranial nerves, exiting as a large sensory root and small motor root from the anterolateral surface of the pons.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The trigeminal nerve derives embryologically from the second pharyngeal arch\",\"e\":\"The trigeminal nerve develops from the first arch. It is the facial nerve that is developed from the second arch.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"You are a CT1 in neurosurgery and are assisting in a craniotomy The consultant is teaching you about cranial nerves and describes one as the only cranial nerve that does not arise from the brain itself. Which nerve is this most likely to be?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve is this most likely to be?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Olfactory nerve [I]\",\"e\":\"The olfactory nerve arises from the brain and exits at the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone. Its function is the sense of smell and aromatic taste\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Optic nerve [!]\",\"e\":\"The optic nerve does not join with the brainstem, but to the primary visual cortex of the brain to transmit sensory information from the retina.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Abducent nerve [VI]\",\"e\":\"The abducent nerve arises from the brainstem between the pons and medulla. It innervates the lateral rectus muscle and exits the skull in the superior orbital fissure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Accessory nerve ExiT\",\"e\":\"The accessory nerve is unique in that its roots arise from motor neurons in the upper five segments of the cervical spinal cord. It leaves through the jugular foramen to innervate the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal nerve [XII]\",\"e\":\"The hypoglossal nerve arises from the anterior surface of the medulla and exits in the hypoglossal canal to innervate the intrinsic muscles of the tongue, as well as hyoglossus, genioglossus and styloglossus. 3924\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"Which ventricle in the brain is bounded laterally by the thalamus?\",\"lead\":\"Which ventricle in the brain is bounded laterally by the thalamus?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The first ventricle\",\"e\":\"The first and second ventricles of the brain are together known as the lateral ventricles due to their position in each of the cerebral hemispheres. They are superior to the thalamus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The second ventricle\",\"e\":\"The second ventricle is known as a lateral ventricle (along with the first). It lies above the thalamus and is not laterally related to it.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The third ventricle\",\"e\":\"The third ventricle is bounded laterally by the thalamus. It communicates superolaterally with the lateral ventricles. The ventricular system of the brain comprises four communicating cavities which are responsible for the production and transport of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). CSF travels from the lateral (first and second) ventricles to the third ventricle via the foramen of Monro, and from there to the fourth ventricle via the cerebral aqueduct (of Sylvius). The system is continuous with the central spinal canal to bathe the spinal cord. Clinically this is relevant in hydrocephalus, which occurs when there is an abnormal accumulation of CSF in the ventricles of the brain. The most common site of obstruction is at the cerebral aqueduct.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The lateral ventricle\",\"e\":\"The lateral ventricles are paired - the first and second ventricles. They are the largest ventricles in the ventricular system of the brain and lie superior to the thalamus. Their volume increases with advancing age.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The fourth ventricle\",\"e\":\"The fourth ventricle receives CSF from the third ventricle. It is situated between the pons and the medulla oblongata.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old woman presents to clinic with weight gain, striae and hypertension. She is diagnosed with Cushing syndrome and following investigations, it is discovered that she has a pituitary adenoma. Which one of these is most accurate regarding the anatomy of the pituitary gland?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is most accurate regarding the anatomy of the pituitary gland?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The pituitary gland sits within the anterior cranial fossa in a small depression in the sphenoid bone known as the sella turcica\",\"e\":\"The pituitary gland does sit in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, but this is part of the middle cranial fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The superior aspect of the pituitary gland is covered in a reflection of the pia mater known as the diaphragma sellae\",\"e\":\"The diaphragma sellae is a reflection of the dura mater - not the pia mater. It covers the superior surface of the pituitary gland, with a small round opening for the passage of the infundibulum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The anterior lobe of the pituitary gland is known as the neurohypophysis\",\"e\":\"It is the posterior lobe of the pituitary gland that is known as the neurohypophysis. It has embryological origins from the forebrain. It secretes antidiuretic hormone (ADH) and oxytocin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The anterior and posterior lobes of the pituitary are drained by separate venous drainage - the anterior and posterior hypophyseal veins respectively\",\"e\":\"Both lobes of the pituitary share the same venous drainage. It is the arterial supply that differs between the lobes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The pituitary gland is superior in relation to the sphenoid sinus\",\"e\":\"The pituitary gland is superior in relation to the sphenoid sinus, which lies inferiorly. This is clinically significant in pituitary surgery, as the gland is accessed via a trans-sphenoidal approach.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following is most accurate with regard to the anatomy of the brainstem?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most accurate with regard to the anatomy of the brainstem?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The brainstem is generally divided into three main parts - the mesencephalon, the metencephalon and the diencephalon\",\"e\":\"The brainstem is usually divided into three parts - the midbrain (mesencephalon), the pons (metencephalon) and the medulla oblongata (myelencephalon). The diencephalon is occasionally included, but is generally felt to be above the brainstem.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The brainstem is the site of origin of all the cranial nerves\",\"e\":\"The first two cranial nerves arise from the cerebrum, Ill - X and XII originate in the brainstem. Cranial nerve XI has a component arising from the brainstem (the cranial accessory) and component arising from the upper segment of the spinal cord (the spinal accessory).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The pons, medulla and midbrain make up the components of the brainstem\",\"e\":\"These are the three main components of the brainstem - the midbrain (mesencephalon), the pons (metencephalon) and the medulla oblongata (myelencephalon).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The third cerebral ventricle lies in the brainstem\",\"e\":\"The fourth cerebral ventricle is closely related to the brainstem, lying behind the pons and medulla oblongata.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The midbrain is the largest part of the brainstem\",\"e\":\"The pons is the largest part of the brainstem. The cerebellopontine angle is significant since intracranial growths can be found here. Early symptoms come from the compression of cranial nerve 8 - the vestibulocochlear nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man presents to his General Practitioner with progressive confusion, ataxia and urinary incontinence. He is sent for a CT scan of his brain that reveals enlarged ventricles. After further investigations the patient is diagnosed with normal pressure hydrocephalus. It is explained to him that his symptoms are caused by abnormal accumulation of cerebrospinal fluid. Which one of the following is most correct with regard to cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most correct with regard to cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"There is more CSF in the ventricles than is contained in the subarachnoid spaces\",\"e\":\"CSF total volume is around 150 ml, with around one-sixth of this found in the ventricles. The rest is found in the subarachnoid spaces of the cranium and spine\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"CSF flows from the lateral ventricles to the third ventricles via the cerebral aqueduct of Sylvius\",\"e\":\"CSF flows from lateral ventricles to the third ventricles via the foramen of Monro, and then to the fourth ventricle via the cerebral aqueduct of Sylvius.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"CSF flows from the third ventricle to the fourth ventricle via the foramen of Monro\",\"e\":\"CSF flows from third ventricles to the fourth ventricle via the cerebral aqueduct of Sylvius.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The ventricular system of the brain and the central canal of the spinal cord are lined by a type of neuroglia known as ependyma\",\"e\":\"The ependymal is a layer of ciliated epithelial cells that lines the ventricles and central canal of the spinal cord. Ependymal cells are one of the glial cells of the central nervous system. The others are astrocytes, phagocytic microglia and oligodendrocytes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"CSF is produced by the arachnoid granulations of the lateral and third ventricles\",\"e\":\"CSF is produced by the choroid plexuses of the lateral, third and fourth ventricles. A small amount is produced by the ependymal cells. CSF is absorbed by the arachnoid granulations. which act as valves. Arachnoid granulations allow CSF to flow from the subarachnoid space to the venous system, but do not themselves produce CSF\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"A patient who has recently been diagnosed with central diabetes insipidus presents to the endocrine clinic. It is explained to the patient that this may be as a result of damage to the hypothalamus Which one of the following is most accurate with regard to the anatomy of the hypothalamus?\",\"lead\":\"It is explained to the patient that this may be as a result of damage to the hypothalamus Which one of the following is most accurate with regard to the anatomy of the hypothalamus?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The anterior portion of the hypothalamus contains the nerve endings of neurosecretory cells, which run down through the infundibular stalk to the pituitary gland\",\"e\":\"This would be true about the posterior hypothalamus, also known as the median eminence:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The hypothalamus makes up the floor of the fourth ventricle\",\"e\":\"The hypothalamus makes up the floor of the third ventricle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The hypothalamus is located immediately inferior to the optic chiasm\",\"e\":\"The optic chiasm is located immediately below the hypothalamus. This is relevant clinically as growing tumours in the hypothalamus can compress the optic chiasm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The mamillary bodies lie on the inferior surface of the hypothalamus\",\"e\":\"The mamillary bodies are small round structures consisting of two groups of nuclei. They have a role in memory and form part of the limbic system.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The hypothalamus is continuous with the pituitary gland via the hypothalamic stalk\",\"e\":\"The hypothalamus is continuous with the pituitary gland via the pituitary stalk, also known as the infundibulum. The infundibulum passes down through the dura mater of the diaphragma sellae.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man is admitted with left iliac fossa pain and pyrexia. It is difficult to take a history because the patient has residual dysphasia from a stroke 12 years previously. Which one of the following statements is the most accurate regarding the neuroanatomy of language processing?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is the most accurate regarding the neuroanatomy of language processing?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Broca's area is responsible for language production and is always found in the frontal lobe of the left hemisphere\",\"e\":\"Broca's area is responsible for language production but it is found in the frontal lobe of the dominant hemisphere. This is usually, but not always. the left hemisphere.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Broca's area is responsible for language production and is always found in the frontal lobe of the right hemisphere\",\"e\":\"Broca's area is responsible for speech production but it is found in the frontal lobe of the dominant hemisphere, which is generally the left hemisphere. Patients with Broca's aphasia can understand language but cannot form speak fluently.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Wernicke's area is responsible for language production and is always found in the frontal lobe of the left hemisphere\",\"e\":\"Wernicke's area is responsible for comprehension of written and spoken language, not language production. It is found in the temporal lobe of the dominant cerebral hemisphere.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Wernicke's area is responsible for comprehension of language and is located in the temporal lobe of the dominant cerebral hemisphere\",\"e\":\"Wernicke's area is responsible for language comprehension. Damage to this area causes receptive dysphasia, in which speech will be fluent but lack meaning. Patients with this condition are generally unaware that their words lack meaning. Wernicke's aphasia is mostly caused by stroke.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vernicke's area is responsible for language production and is located in the emporal lobe of the dominant cerebral hemisphere\",\"e\":\"Although this is the correct location for Wernicke's area, it is responsible for comprehension of language but not language production.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":54,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old boy is rushed to the Emergency Department after a stone was thrown at him, striking the back of his head. His parents reported a 5 min period of unconsciousness followed by several vomiting. Subsequent imaging is suspicious of a fracture of the occipital bone extending down to the posterior fossa of the skull. Which one of these structures is located in the posterior fossa of the skull?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these structures is located in the posterior fossa of the skull?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The olfactory nerves\",\"e\":\"The are many olfactory nerves that pass from the nasal cavity through each side of the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone and into the anterior fossa of the skull. They gather at the olfactory bulbs before passing posteriorly as the olfactory tracts.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The abducens nerve\",\"e\":\"The abducens nerve supplies the lateral rectus muscle and exits via the superior orbital fissure in the middle fossa of the skull\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The internal carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid artery enters the skull through the carotid canal in the middle cranial fossa of the skull base.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The middle meningeal artery\",\"e\":\"The middle meningeal artery is a branch of the external carotid artery It passes through the foramen spinosum and then runs across the floor of the middle fossa of the skull up to the pterion. Fractures at the pterion region can lacerate the artery and cause an extradural haematoma\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The facial nerve\",\"e\":\"From the pons the facial nerve crosses the posterior fossa of the skull before it passes laterally upwards into the petrous temporal bone to exit through the skull the internal acoustic meatus.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"An overweight 4-year-old boy is referred as his parents are concerned about his short stature and learning difficulties. They describe slow development and floppiness as a newborn and say it is impossible to stop his overeating. The consultant suspects Prader-Willi syndrome and suggests genetic testing. If this diagnosis was correct, which part of the forebrain would be involved?\",\"lead\":\"If this diagnosis was correct, which part of the forebrain would be involved?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The medulla oblongata\",\"e\":\"The medulla oblongata (myelencephalon) is part of the hindbrain (rhombencephalon), which is composed of the pons, the medulla oblongata and the cerebellum. The medulla oblongata connects the pons to the spinal cord.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cerebral peduncles\",\"e\":\"The cerebral peduncles are part of the midbrain (mesencephalon). The cerebral peduncies contain the crus cerebri.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The tectum\",\"e\":\"The tectum is the posterior part of the midbrain (mesencephalon). It has two superior and two inferior colliculi, and a small glandular structure call the pineal body\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The cerebrum\",\"e\":\"The cerebrum (telencephalon) is part of the forebrain and is the largest part of the brain. It is divided into right and left hemispheres and is involved with higher brain functioning. It is not involved in the control of hunger and growth.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The diencephalon\",\"e\":\"The diencephalon is part of the forebrain. The forebrain comprises the cerebrum (telencephalon) and the diencephalon. The diencephalon consists of the thalamus and hypothalamus. The hypothalamus regulates growth via the production of growth hormone-releasing hormone, sexual maturation from gonadotrophin-releasing hormone and suppresses appetite with corticotropin-releasing hormone. The hypothalamus is affected in Prader-Willi syndrome, which is a chromosomal disorder usually due to partial deletion of paternal chromosome 15. Symptoms are wide ranging and are related to the hormones secreted by the hypothalamus.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"A 49-year-old man with a history of hypertension is rushed to his local Emergency Department. He is found to have global aphasia, homonymous hemianopia and hemiplegia He undergoes an urgent CT scan of the brain, which shows a new infarct Which one of the following is most likely to have been affected?\",\"lead\":\"He is found to have global aphasia, homonymous hemianopia and hemiplegia He undergoes an urgent CT scan of the brain, which shows a new infarct Which one of the following is most likely to have been affected?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The basilar artery\",\"e\":\"The basilar artery arises from the confluence of the vertebral arteries: It supplies the brainstem and cerebellum. Occlusion of this vessel causes severe functional deficit and has a wide range of presentations depending on the anatomical region affected, but is unlikely to cause global aphasia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The anterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The anterior cerebral arteries arise from the circle of Willis. They supply the medial frontal and parietal lobes. Strokes here are rare because of collateral circulation from the anterior communicating artery. They can cause upper motor neurone weakness, unilateral contralateral motor weakness and sensory loss and occasionally urinary incontinence.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The middle cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The middle cerebral arteries arise from the internal carotid artery. They supply the lateral frontal and parietal lobes and the superior temporal lobe. Strokes in the middle cerebral artery territory can cause hemiplegia on the contralateral side of the body, contralateral sensory loss, homonymous hemianopia and language impairment (dysphasia) if Broca's area or Wernicke's area are affected.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The posterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior cerebral arteries supply the occipital lobes and the inferior portion of the temporal lobes. Strokes here cause contralateral homonymous hemianopia, but do not affect speech.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The posterior communicating artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior communicating artery runs between the middle and posterior cerebral arteries in the circle of Willis. This connections within circle of Willis provide an anastomotic system to compensate if blood supply was to be compromised. Compromise here can however cause contralateral homonymous hemianopia, but would not cause global aphasia.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man is admitted after a high-speed road traffic collision. His Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score is 3 on arrival and he is immediately intubated and ventilated. A CT scan of the head shows a skull fracture and an extradural haematoma. He is transferred to the local neurosurgical unit. What is the thinnest part of the adult skull?\",\"lead\":\"What is the thinnest part of the adult skull?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The pterion\",\"e\":\"The pterion is the point at which frontal parietal, temporal and sphenoid bones join and is the thinnest point of the skull. Clinically this is relevant because the anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery runs beneath it. A blow to the head in this area can injure this artery causing an extradural haematoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The pterygoid\",\"e\":\"The pterygoid could relate to several anatomical structures including the medial and lateral pterygoid muscles and the pterygoid processes of the sphenoid bone. It does not relate to the thinnest part of the skull.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The lambda\",\"e\":\"The lambda is the point in the skull where the lambdoid and sagittal sutures meet. It is: palpable as a depression between the occipital and two parietal bones, but in the adult is not the thinnest part of the skull. In the newborn this the site of the posterior fontanelle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The external occipital protuberance\",\"e\":\"The external occipital protuberance is a thickened prominence of the occipital bone and so is not a thin part of the skull. The trapezius muscle and nuchal ligament attach to it.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The bregma\",\"e\":\"The bregma of the skull is the bony landmark where coronal and sagittal sutures meet. It is located at the top of the skull, and in the neonate is the anterior fontanelle. It closes at around 18 months. In the adult, however, it is not the thinnest part.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old woman complains of severe facial pain that manifests itself as electric shock-like spasms and is affecting her quality of life. Initial treatment for trigeminal neuralgia offers little improvement to her symptoms. A procedure is proposed in which percutaneous radiofrequency ablation is introduced via the foramen ovale. Which one of the following is one of the contents of the foramen ovale?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is one of the contents of the foramen ovale?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The accessory nerve\",\"e\":\"The accessory nerve innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles. It exits the skull through the jugular foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The abducens nerve\",\"e\":\"The abducens nerve innervates the lateral rectus nerve. It exits the skull through the superior orbital fissure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The lesser petrosal nerve\",\"e\":\"The lesser petrosal nerve is the parasympathetic root of the otic ganglion. It exits the skull through the foramen ovalet.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The greater petrosal nerve\",\"e\":\"The greater petrosal nerve is a branch of the facial nerve. It exits the temporal bone via the hiatus for the greater petrosal nerve\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (V1) passes through the superior orbital fissure. It divides into three terminal branches - the frontal nerve, lacrimal nerve and nasociliary nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"A 41-year-old man presents to the Neurosurgery Department with seizures after a 1-month history of headaches. Subsequent investigations show a meningioma. An ST1 doctor assists during the resection of the tumour and during the dissection, the dural partitions of the brain come into view. Which one of the dural partitions has an opening through which the pituitary stalk passes?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the dural partitions has an opening through which the pituitary stalk passes?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Falx cerebri\",\"e\":\"The falx cerebri is a crescent-shaped downward projection, a double fold of dura mater that runs between the cerebral hemispheres. Anteriorly, it attaches to the ethmoid bone at the crista galli and the frontal crest of the frontal bone It attaches to the tentorium cerebelli inferior-posteriorly.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diaphragma sellae\",\"e\":\"The diaphragma sellae is a small dural structure that covers hypophysial fossa in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone. An opening in the middle of the diaphragma sellae allows the passage of the infundibulum. This structure connects the hypothalamus to the pituitary gland. The dural partitions of the brain are projections of dura mater that subdivide the cranial cavity: the falx cerebri, the tentorium cerebelli, the falx cerebelli and the diaphragma sellae.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tentorium cerebelli\",\"e\":\"The tentorium cerebelli is a double fold of dura mater that forms the roof the posterior fossa and separates the supratentorial and infratentorial compartments.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Falx cerebelli\",\"e\":\"The falx cerebelli is a variably present and projection of dura mater in the posterior cranial fossa. It forms attachments to the occipital bone and to the tentorium cerebelli superiorly. It is found in the midline between the cerebellar hemispheres.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tentorial notch\",\"e\":\"The tentorial notch is an oval opening formed by the anterior and medial borders of the tentorium cerebelli.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A 53-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with headaches, nausea, vomiting and bilateral horizontal diplopia. Subsequent investigations reveal a large tumour in the right hemisphere with surrounding oedema causing mass effect. On examination, the patient has bilateral sixth cranial nerve palsies\\n\\nWhich muscle is supplied by the 6th cranial nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscle is supplied by the 6th cranial nerve?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Medial rectus\",\"e\":\"The medial rectus muscle is supplied by the inferior division of the oculomotor nerve (CN III) The medial rectus muscle is responsible for adduction of the eye.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Superior oblique\",\"e\":\"The superior oblique muscle is supplied by the trochlear nerve (fourth cranial nerve), and is the only extraocular muscle to be supplied by this nerve. It abducts and depresses the\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lateral rectus\",\"e\":\"The lateral rectus muscle* is responsible for abduction of the eye and is supplied by the abducens (6* cranial nerve). The 6* cranial nerve is particularly susceptible to being stretched (eg due to raised intracranial pressure) due to its long course along the skull base. An oculomotor nerve palsy is remembered as 'down and out' - as the superior oblique muscle acts unopposed leaving the eyeball infraducted and abducted. Ptosis may be\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inferior oblique\",\"e\":\"The inferior oblique muscle is supplied by the oculomotor nerve (the third cranial nerve). It inserts into the temporal lower quadrant of the eyeball and moves the eye superiorly and laterally\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior rectus\",\"e\":\"The superior rectus muscle is supplied by the oculomotor nerve (the third cranial nerve). It moves the eyeball superiorly.\"}]},{\"t\":52,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department complaining of severe facial pain after colliding with an opponent during a football match. CT imaging reveals a horizontal maxillary fracture through the alveolar ridge, and nasal bones, separating the palate from the maxilla. The orbits are intact\\n\\nWhich type of fracture does this represent?\",\"lead\":\"Which type of fracture does this represent?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Le Fort I\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Le Fort lI\",\"e\":\"Le Fort fractures are facial fractures involving the maxillary bone and extending into surrounding structures. The anatomy of these fractures was first described by Rene Le Fort in 1901. He proposed that fractures occur along three weak lines in the bony structure of the face, leading to his description of three fracture patterns known as Le Fort I, Il and III. Le Fort 1 describes horizontal maxillary fractures as described in the question. Treatment is surgical.\\n\\nLe Fort Il is described as a pyramidal fracture, with teeth as the base and the nasofrontal suture the apex. Starting from the nasal bridge it runs through the maxillary frontal processes, inferolaterally through to the medial wall of each orbit.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Le Fort III\",\"e\":\"Le Fort Ill fractures are transverse and result in craniofacial dysjunction. They run through the nasofrontal suture, maxilla-frontal suture, orbital wall and invariably, the zygomatic arch.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Le Fort IV\",\"e\":\"There is a modification of the Le Fort system (Marciani 1993) in which Le Fort IV represents a Le Fort Il or lIl fracture when it occurs with a fracture of the cranial base. However this system is not as commonly used, and modified Le Fort IV does not describe the injury in the\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Unilateral Le Fort I\",\"e\":\"Unilateral Le Fort I and Il fractures are possible, however the question describes a bilateral Le Fort I fracture\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"The neurosurgical consultant asks an anatomy question during the morning meeting The patient clearly has a cerebral venous sinus thrombosis.\\n\\nThe superior cerebral veins drain into which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The superior cerebral veins drain into which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cavernous sinus\",\"e\":\"The superior and inferior ophthalmic veins, sphenoparietal sinus, superficial middle cerebral veins drain into the cavernous sinus but not the superior cerebral veins.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Great cerebral vein\",\"e\":\"This vein drains the internal cerebral veins.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inferior petrosal sinus\",\"e\":\"This vein drains the cavernous sinus. It meets the sigmoid sinus at the level of the jugular foramen to form the internal jugular vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superior petrosal sinus\",\"e\":\"The superior petrosal sinus connects the cavernous sinus to the transverse sinus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior sagittal sinus\",\"e\":\"The superior cerebral veins, 8 to 12 in number, drain the superior, lateral and medial surfaces of the cerebral hemispheres and are mainly lodged in the sulci between the gyri, but some run across the gyri. They open into the superior sagittal sinus, which can be seen below running in the superior aspect of the falx cerebri.\\n\\nSuperior sagittal sinus\\n\\nnferior agittol sinut\\n\\nGreat cerebral ven\\n\\nSuperior & inferior petrosal\\n\\nConfluence of sinuses\\n\\nInternal jugular vein Anatomy\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"You are the neurosurgery CT2 attending a trauma call for a patient with head and facial injuries. Following a CT head, the radiologist calls you to say the patient has a fracture through the superior orbital fissure. Which one of the following nerves pass through the superior orbital fissure?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following nerves pass through the superior orbital fissure?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ophthalmic division of the trigeminal passes through the superior orbital fissure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ophthalmic division of the trigeminal passes through the superior orbital fissure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oculomotor nerve\",\"e\":\"The oculomotor nerve travels through the superior orbital fissure as the superior and inferior divisions, along with the trochlear and abducens nerves, and branches of the the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (the frontal, lacrimal and nasociliary nerves). The cranial nerve foramiae are shown in the image below.\\n\\nOptic canal Opticin (CNID Supenor orbital fissure Oculomotor n (CNIM) Trochlear n. (CNIV) Ophthalmic n (CNV) Abducens n (CAVI)\\n\\nForamen Oxale Mandibular n (CNV)\\n\\ni...: Jugular foramen. Glossopharyngeal in (CNO Vagus n (CNX) Accessory n ICNXI) -/ Hypegiossal cana Hypoglossal» (CNXID\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Orbital parasympathetic branches of the pterygopalatine ganglion\",\"e\":\"The orbital branches the ptergopalatine ganglion pass through the inferior orbital fissure before being carried on the zygomatic nerve. Their final destination is the lacrimal gland.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supraorbital nerve\",\"e\":\"It is the frontal nerve (division of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve) that passes through the superior orbital fissure. The frontal nerve then divides into the supraorbital nerve and supra-trochlear nerve which supply the frontal scalp skin and skin above the medial canthus respectively\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient on the neurosurgical ward who has a complex skull base tumour described on the MRI brain report as involving the superior orbital fissure. You undertake a neurological examination and carefully document the abnormalities found prior to their resectional surgery. Which one of the following nerves pass through the superior orbital fissure?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following nerves pass through the superior orbital fissure?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Facial nerve\",\"e\":\"The facial nerve passes into the internal auditory meatus with the vestibulocochlear nerve. It gives off the greater petrosal nerve, chorda tympani and nerve to stapedius before exiting the inferior aspect of the temporal bone through the stylomastoid foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"The branches of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve pass through the superior orbital fissure whilst the mandibular division passes through the foramen ovale.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Branches of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"The branches of the ophthalmic division (frontal, lacrimal and nasociliary) exit through the superior orbital fissure along with oculomotor nerve, trochlea nerve, and abducens nerve.\\n\\nOptic canal: Option ICNIC Superior orbital Issue Oculomotor n (CNAI) trochlear n (CNIV) Ophthalmic n (CNVI Abducens n (CHil\\n\\nForamen Ovale Mandibular n (CNVI\\n\\nJugular foramen Glosopheryngeal n (CNID Vagus n CNX Accessory n (CAMI Hypoglossal canal Hypoglossain (CAIXI):\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Orbital branches of the ptergopalatine ganglion\",\"e\":\"These branches pass through the inferior orbital fissure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supra-orbital nerve\",\"e\":\"This nerve is a branch of the frontal nerve which passes through the supraorbital foramen. This division has occurred after the frontal nerve has exited through the superior orbital fissure.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"The foramen ovale at the base of the skull transit several structures from the skull. You have found a neurological deficit in a cranial nerve examination of a patient with a basal skull fracture that involves the foramen ovale.\\n\\nWhich structure always passes through the foramen ovale?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure always passes through the foramen ovale?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The Maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"This nerve passes through the foramen rotundum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"The mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve is the correct answer. This nerve passes through the foramen ovale+ with the accessory meningeal artery, lesser petrosal nerve, and emissary veins; the otic ganglion is at the entrance to the foramen.\\n\\nOptic canal Option ICNIC Supenos orbital fessore Oculomotorn (CNNI) Trochlear n. (CNIV) Ophthalmic n (CNV,)\\n\\nForamen Ovale: Mandibular n (CNV)\\n\\nJugular foramen Glosopharyageal n (CND) Vagus n (CNX) Accessory n (CNXI) Hypoglossal canal _ Hypoglossal n/CNXIO\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The middie meningeal artery\",\"e\":\"The accessory meningeal artery passes through the foramen ovale whilst the middle meningeal passes through the foramen spinosum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The greater petrosal nerve\",\"e\":\"This nerve follows are tortuous course from the facial nerve. First it travels in the petrous temporal bone, travels over the foramen lacerum, then through the ptergoid canal to reach the pterygopalatine ganglion. It is the lesser petrosal nerve that passes through the foramen ovale.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The optic nerve\",\"e\":\"The optic nerve passes through the optic canal.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"During resection of a mandibular tumour, there is concern the lingual nerve may have been inadvertently damaged The lingual nerve is a branch of which of the following nerves?\",\"lead\":\"During resection of a mandibular tumour, there is concern the lingual nerve may have been inadvertently damaged The lingual nerve is a branch of which of the following nerves?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chorda tympani\",\"e\":\"The chorda tympani is a branch of the facial nerve that carries taste fibres from the anterior two-thirds2/3 of the tongue, and parasympathetic fibres to the submandibular and\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal nerve C...\",\"e\":\"The hypoglossal nerve and lingual nerve are in close proximity but do not share neural connections.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"The lingual nerve (shown in blue) is one of three major branches of the posterior division of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve. The other two major nerves are the auriculo-temporal nerve and the inferior alveolar nerve.\\n\\nMandibular nerve (V3) Lingual nerve Inferior alveolar nerve\\n\\nMental nerve\\n\\nteachmoanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Marginal mandibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The marginal mandibular nerve is a branch of the facial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"The lingual nerve is a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve, not the maxillary division.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A dental surgeon carries out a block of the inferior alveolar nerve by infiltrating local anaesthetic at the inferior alveolar foramen. Which clinical feature may result from this procedure?\",\"lead\":\"Which clinical feature may result from this procedure?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Numbness of the lower lip on the injected side\",\"e\":\"The inferior alveolar nerve enters the deep aspect of the mandible at the mandibular foramen and exits at the mental foramen as the mental nerve. The mental nerve innervates the skin of the ipsilateral lower lip and chin, hence the numbness of the lip. The inferior alveolar nerve can be seen in the image branching from the mandibular (V3) branch of the trigeminal nerve.\\n\\nMandibular nerve (V3) Lingual nerve Inferior alveolar nerve\\n\\nMental nerve\\n\\nteachmoanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ineffective block for the lower incisor teeth\",\"e\":\"Effective anaesthesia for the incisor teeth would be expected as the inferior alveolar nerve supplies sensation to the lower teeth during its course through the mandible.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Numbness of hard palate\",\"e\":\"The hard palate is supplied by the greater palatine nerve, which is a branch of the maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inability of the patient to clench his jaws\",\"e\":\"The muscles of mastication involved in jaw-clenching are innervated by branches of the anterior division of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve. The inferior alveolar nerve is derived from the posterior division.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transient weakness of the facial muscles on the injected side\",\"e\":\"The muscles of facial expression are innervated by the facial nerve and not the trigeminal nerve. The inferior alveolar nerve is a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"A patient undergoes a radical parotidectomy for a malignant parotid tumour, at which time it is found necessary to perform a total division of the left facial (VII) nerve at this level.\\n\\nPostoperatively, which one option is the most likely sequel?\",\"lead\":\"Postoperatively, which one option is the most likely sequel?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyperacusis on the affected side\",\"e\":\"The stapedius muscle within the middle ear dampens sound. It is innervated by one of the branches of the facial nerve (nerve to stapedius), which is given off before the facial nerve exits the stylomastoid foramen. Therefore injury to the facial nerve at the level of the parotid would not affect this muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Numbness over the cheek on the left side\",\"e\":\"The somatic sensation to the face is supplied by the branches of the trigeminal nerve, not the facial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ptosis of the upper eyelid on the left side\",\"e\":\"Levator palpebrae superioris is supplied by the superior division of the oculomotor nerve and sympathetic fibres carried on the internal carotid artery. The facial nerve is not involved.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Loss of taste sensation over the anterior two-thirds of the tongue on the lett side\",\"e\":\"The taste sensation from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue are carried by the chorda tympani, which joins the lingual nerve (branch of the trigeminal nerve) in its extra-cranial portion. The intra-cranial portion is carried by the facial nerve but would not be affected by damage to the facial nerve at the level of the parotid. 563\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tendency for food and fluids to collect in the buccal sulcus after meals\",\"e\":\"The facial nerve supplies the muscles of facial expression including innervating buccinator. This muscle is involved in emptying the buccal sulcus during mastication.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A patient undergoes excision of the left submandibular salivary gland for sialectasia. Unfortunately, his hypoglossal (CN XII) nerve on that side is damaged What is the most likely outcome?\",\"lead\":\"Unfortunately, his hypoglossal (CN XII) nerve on that side is damaged What is the most likely outcome?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"There is numbness of the posterior one-third of the tongue\",\"e\":\"The somatic sensation to the anterior two thirds of the tongue is supplied by the lingual nerve, a branch of the trigeminal nerve. Taste to the anterior two thirds is supplied by chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve. Taste and somatic sensation to the posterior one-third is carried on the glossopharyngeal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"On protruding the tongue, it deviates towards the right\",\"e\":\"Although the extrinsic muscles of the tongue are innervated by the hypoglossal nerve (with the exception of palatoglossus), the tongue deviates towards the side of the injury, not away.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The uvula deviates towards the left\",\"e\":\"The muscles of the soft palate are innervated by the vagus nerve. Injury causes deviation away from the side of injury\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"All the extrinsic muscles of the left side of the tongue, except the palatoglossus, are paralysed\",\"e\":\"The extrinsic muscles of the tongue are supplied by the hypoglossal nerve. The palatoglossus muscle however is supplied by the vagus nerve:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The genioglossus muscle is spared\",\"e\":\"The genioglossus is innervated by the hypoglossal nerve and will therefore be affected. The palatoglossus muscle is innervated by the vagus nerve, and is therefore the muscle that is spared.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"A patient is stabbed in the forehead, the blade does not penetrate the skull. After the local anaesthetic wears off the patient complains of persistent anaesthesia The supratrochlear nerve is a direct terminal branch of which nerve?\",\"lead\":\"After the local anaesthetic wears off the patient complains of persistent anaesthesia The supratrochlear nerve is a direct terminal branch of which nerve?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Frontal\",\"e\":\"The supratrochlear nerve is a direct terminal branch of the frontal nerve and supplies the superior-medial orbital skin, superior to the medial canthus: It is medial to the supra-orbital nerve. The nerve passes above the pulley of the superior oblique and gives off a descending filament to join the infratrochlear branch of the nasociliary nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Nasociliary\",\"e\":\"The supratrochlear nerve is a branch of the frontal nerve and not the nasociliary nerve. The nasociliary nerve has five main branches: the long and short ciliary nerves, the anterior and posterior ethmoidal nerves, the infra-trochlear nerve and the communicating branch to the ciliary ganglion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Maxillary\",\"e\":\"The supratrochlear nerve is a branch of the frontal nerve, which in turn is a branch of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal and not the maxillary division.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"P/- Ophthalmic\",\"e\":\"The ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve divides into the three main branches: the frontal nerve, the lacrimal nerve and the nasociliary nerve. The supratrochlear is a direct branch of the frontal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lacrimal\",\"e\":\"The lacrimal nerve is one of the three main branches of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve. However, the supra-trochlear nerve is not one of its branches. t does not have any branches, and supplies the skin above the lateral canthus. It also carries parasympathetic fibres (from the pterygopalatine ganglion) to the lacrimal gland from the zygomaticotemporal nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A patient undergoes parotidectomy for a malignant lesion. At surgery the cancer is very adherent to the facial nerve and on waking from the anaesthetic there is a degree of facial paralysis. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the muscles of facial expression?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the muscles of facial expression?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The digastric muscle is a muscle of facial expression\",\"e\":\"The digastric muscle's action is to aid swallowing and depress the mandible. It is interesting to note that the anterior belly is supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal while the posterior belly is innervated by the facial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Some muscles of facial expression are innervated by the long buccal nerve\",\"e\":\"The long buccal nerve innervates the buccal mucosa and the lateral ptergoid. It is a minor branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are in the same subcutaneous plane as the platysma muscle\",\"e\":\"The facial muscles are subcutaneous (just under the skin, in the same plane as the platysma) muscles that control facial expression. They generally originate on bone and insert on the skin of the face. The facial muscles are innervated by cranial nerve Vil, also known as the facial nerve. The facial muscles are derived from the second pharyngeal arch.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Some muscles of facial expression receive their motor supply via the zygomaticofacial nerve\",\"e\":\"Both the zygomaticofacial and zygomaticotemporal nerves are both entirely sensory:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"All muscles of facial expression are attached to bone\",\"e\":\"There are several small muscles of facial expression that do not have a bony attachment. For example, risorius arises from the parotid fascia and inserts to the modiolus-a meeting place of several facial muscles at the lateral aspect of the mouth\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"A patient with a severe head injury is found to have clear fluid as well as blood leaking from their nose. On CT scanning a base of skull fracture is present Which of the following is true of the olfactory foramina?\",\"lead\":\"On CT scanning a base of skull fracture is present Which of the following is true of the olfactory foramina?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are located in the middle cranial fossa\",\"e\":\"The olfactory foramina, also know as the cribriform foramina, are located in the anterior cranial fossa\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are located in the anterior cranial fossa\",\"e\":\"The olfactory foramina are located in the anterior cranial fossa. They are located within the cribriform plate, a part of the ethmoid bone. The fibres of the olfactory nerve originate from the superior surface of the superior concha, medial aspect of the superior nasal septum, and the inferior surface of the cribriform plate. These fibres pass through the olfactory foramina and synapse in the olfactory bulb on the superior surface of the cribriform plate. The cranial nerve exits from the skull are summarised in the image below.\\n\\nin (GNIO Superior orbital fissure Oculomotor n (CNHI) Trochlear n (CNIV Ophthalmic n (CNV.) Abducera n (CNVI\\n\\nForamen Ovale Mandibular n (CNV)\\n\\nJugular foramen Glossopharyngeal n (CNIX) Vagus n (CNX) Accessory n (CNX)! Hypoglossal canal Hypoglossaln (CNXHI\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are located immediately inferior to the optic foramen\",\"e\":\"The superior orbital fissure lies infero-lateral to the optic foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They have motor neurones running through them\",\"e\":\"The olfactory nerve only carries special sensory fibres for smell.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They are located in the sphenoid bone\",\"e\":\"Olfactory foramina are located within the cribriform plate, which is part of the ethmoid bone.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A patient sustains significant trauma to their eye, the on call ophthalmologist is dealing with an emergency in a different hospital several miles away. You are concerned the patient has orbital compartment syndrome and perform a lateral canthotomy. Which one of the following structures separates the anterior and posterior chambers in the eyeball?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures separates the anterior and posterior chambers in the eyeball?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The lens\",\"e\":\"The lens represents the posterior limit of the anterior segment. It is the iris that separates the anterior and posterior chambers.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The cornea\",\"e\":\"The cornea represents the most anterior structure of the eyeball. It is transparent, and has protective qualities. Its curvature assists in refracting light.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The iris\",\"e\":\"The iris separates the anterior and posterior chambers. These regions, however, should not be confused with the anterior and posterior segments. The posterior segment consists of the vitreous humour, and structures posterior to this including the optic nerve and retina.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The pupil\",\"e\":\"The pupil represents the aperture within the iris muscle that allows light to fall on the retina.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The ciliary processes\",\"e\":\"The ciliary processes are continuous with the choroid. The suspensory ligaments of the lens originate from the posterior surface. It is the iris that separates the anterior and posterior chambers of the eye.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"A patient is suffering from ocular haemorrhage and you are concerned they are developing orbital compartment syndrome. You ask the A&E resus team to prepare equipment for a lateral canthotomy.\\n\\nThe ophthalmic artery emerges through which one of the following foramina to reach the eye?\",\"lead\":\"The ophthalmic artery emerges through which one of the following foramina to reach the eye?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Optic canal\",\"e\":\"The ophthalmic artery arises from the internal carotid, just as that vessel is emerging from the cavernous sinus, on the medial side of the anterior clinoid process and enters the orbital cavity through the optic foramen (canal). below and lateral to the optic nerve. It then passes over the nerve to reach the medial wall of the orbit and thence horizontally forward, beneath the lower border of the superior oblique and divides it into two terminal branches, the frontal and dorsal nasal. As the artery crosses the optic nerve it is accompanied by the nasociliary nerve and is separated from the frontal nerve by the rectus superior and levator palpebrae superioris.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Foramen spinosum\",\"e\":\"The foramen spinosum transmits the middle meningeal artery and a meningeal branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"superior orbital fissure\",\"e\":\"The superior orbital fissure transmits cranial nerves III, IV, VI and branches of the Vi. It also transmits the superior ophthalmic vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Foramen rotundum\",\"e\":\"The foramen rotundum transmits the maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve (Vz).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"inferior orbital fissure\",\"e\":\"The inferior orbital fissure transits the inferior ophthalmic vein, zygomatic branches of the maxillary division of trigeminal nerve, and ascending branches of the ptergopalatine ganglion.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"A patient is suffering with severe epistaxis that is not stopped despite use of a rapid rhino system. They are taken to theatre to arrest the haemorrhage. Which of the following statements is true regarding the sphenopalatine artery?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements is true regarding the sphenopalatine artery?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It accompanies the infraorbital nerve to its termination\",\"e\":\"Both the sphenopalatine artery and the infraorbital artery are both branches of the maxillary artery but it is the infraorbital artery that accompanies the infraorbital nerve, passing through the infraorbital foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It enters the infratemporal fossa through the pterygomaxillary fissure\",\"e\":\"The sphenopalatine artery passes through the sphenopalatine foramen to enter the nasal cavity. It enters the nasal cavity posterior to the posterior limit of the superior turbinate. It is the posterior superior alveolar nerve and artery that travels through the pterygomaxillary fissure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It supplies blood to the lateral nasal wall and nasal septum\",\"e\":\"The sphenopalatine artery supplies the posterior nasal mucosa. It is a branch of the third part of the internal maxillary artery, passes through the sphenopalatine foramen into the cavity of the nose, at the back part of the superior meatus. Here it gives off its posterior lateral nasal branches, which spread forward over the conchae and meatuses, anastomose with the ethmoidal arteries and the nasal branches of the descending palatine and assist in supplying the frontal, maxillary, ethmoidal and sphenoidal sinuses. Crossing the undersurface of the sphenoid, the sphenopalatine artery ends on the nasal septum as the posterior septal branches; these anastomose with the ethmoidal arteries and the septal branch of the superior labial. In severe posterior nasal bleeds that are uncontrolled with non-operative management the artery can be ligated or can undergo haemostasis with diathermy. The sphenopalatine artery is also one of the vessels that contributes to Kiesselbach's plexus (Little's area - shown below in the image on the anterior nasal septum), in addition to anterior ethmoidal/posterior ethmoidal arteries, superior labial arteries, and greater palatine\\n\\nAnterior and posterior ethmoidal arteries Sphenopalatine artery Little's area\\n\\nSuperior labial artery (septal branch) Greater palatine artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is a terminal branch of the middle superior alveolar artery\",\"e\":\"These arteries are both branches of the maxillary artery but the sphenopalatine artery is not a branch of the superior alveolar artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It sends a branch into the pharyngeal canal\",\"e\":\"The sphenopalatine artery supplies the nasal cavity. One branch of the sphenopalatine artery descends in a groove of the vomer to the incisive canal.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with pain just before and during eating. On examination, a hard mass can be bimanually palpated. You suspect sialolithiasis. Where does the submandibular duct open?\",\"lead\":\"Where does the submandibular duct open?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Near the maxillary second molar\",\"e\":\"This is the opening of the parotid duct (Stenson's duct).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Near the mandibular first molar\",\"e\":\"The opening of the parotid duct (Stenson's duct) is opposite the second upper molar tooth.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"From the incisive foramen\",\"e\":\"The incisive foramen transmits the nasopalatine nerves and branches of the sphenopalatine artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Near the midline in the anterior aspect of the floor of the mouth\",\"e\":\"The submandibular duct (Wharton's duct) is about 5 cm long and its wall is much thinner than that of the parotid duct. It begins from numerous branches from the deep surface of the submandibular gland and runs forward between the mylohyoid and the hyoglossus and genioglossus, then between the sublingual gland and the genioglossus and opens by a narrow orifice on the summit of a small papilla, at the side of the frenulum linguae, near the midline in the anterior aspect of the floor of the mouth. On the hyoglossus it lies between the lingual and hypoglossal nerves, but at the anterior border of the muscle it is crossed laterally by the lingual nerve. The terminal branches of the lingual nerve ascend on its medial side.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Into the buccal vestibule near the mandibular ramus\",\"e\":\"The opening of the parotid duct. (Stenson's duct) is opposite the second upper molar tooth on the buccal mucosa.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"You are watching your ENT consultant perform FESS. They point out various landmarks to you to enable you to be oriented The nasolacrimal duct empties into which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"They point out various landmarks to you to enable you to be oriented The nasolacrimal duct empties into which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Sphenoethmoidal recess\",\"e\":\"The sphenoid sinus empties into this recess.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior meatus\",\"e\":\"The nasolacrimal duct is a membranous canal, about 18 mm in length, which extends from the lower part of the lacrimal sac to the inferior meatus of the nose. It ends as the plica lacrimalis (Hasner's fold), formed by a fold of the mucous membrane. It is contained in an osseous canal, formed by the maxilla, the lacrimal bone and the inferior nasal concha. It is narrower in the middle than at either end and is directed downward, backward and a little lateralward. The mucous lining of the lacrimal sac and nasolacrimal duct is covered with columnar epithelium, which in places is ciliated.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Middle meatus\",\"e\":\"The anterior and middle ethmoidal air cells, and maxillary sinus drain into the middle meatus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Maxillary sinus\",\"e\":\"No structures drain into the maxillary sinus. The maxillary sinus drains into the middle meatus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Infundibulum\",\"e\":\"The frontal sinus drains via the frontonasal duct then infundibulum of the hiatus semilunaris. then into the middle meatus. ===\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"In surgery of the thyroid gland there a several nearby structures that must be preserved to prevent long term morbidity. You are assisting you consultant in a total thyroidectomy. The external laryngeal nerve may be injured and must be identified before ligating which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The external laryngeal nerve may be injured and must be identified before ligating which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inferior thyroid artery\",\"e\":\"The inferior thyroid artery is related to the recurrent laryngeal nerve. It may be injured in the inferior sub-platysmal dissection and dissection of the inferior thyroid pedicle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Superior thyroid artery 17.\",\"e\":\"The superior thyroid artery is closely related to the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. It may be injured in dissection of the superior pedicle of the thyroid lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Superior pharyngeal artery\",\"e\":\"Arteries of the pharynx are not normally at risk of damage during dissection of the thyroid gland. The structures at risk are classically the recurrent laryngeal nerve which is most at risk, followed by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inferior laryngeal artery\",\"e\":\"The inferior laryngeal artery is one of the branches of the inferior thyroid artery. The inferior laryngeal artery is accompanied by the recurrent nerve, not the external laryngeal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ascending palatine artery\",\"e\":\"The ascending palatine artery is a branch of the facial artery and is closely related to the superior pharyngeal constrictor, not the superior laryngeal nerve\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"You are assessing a patient's neurological status following a significant head injury. You note that on the right side they are unable to track your finger as you move it laterally Which one of the following extraocular muscles is most important for eye abduction?\",\"lead\":\"You note that on the right side they are unable to track your finger as you move it laterally Which one of the following extraocular muscles is most important for eye abduction?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lateral rectus muscle\",\"e\":\"The lateral rectus muscle is an extraocular muscle that serves primarily to abduct the eye from the midline. It arises from the Isteral part of the tendinous ring within the posterior orbit, and inserts into the lateral sclera of the eye anterior to the equator but posterior to the sclerocorneal junction. The lateral rectus muscle is innervated by the abducens nerve (CN VI).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior rectus muscle\",\"e\":\"The inferior rectus muscle depresses the eye.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"superior oblique muscle\",\"e\":\"The superior oblique muscle depresses, internally rotates and abducts the eye.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inferior oblique muscle\",\"e\":\"The inferior oblique elevates, externally rotates and abducts the eye.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Medial rectus muscle\",\"e\":\"The medial rectus muscle is responsible for adduction of the eye.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"You are assisting the spinal surgeon in managing a patient an emergency condition. You perform the initial approach and the consultant asks you about the anatomy. Which one of the following statements regarding the spinal arachnoid mater is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding the spinal arachnoid mater is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is very thick compared with the cranial part\",\"e\":\"The spinal part of the arachnoid is a thin, delicate, tubular membrane loosely investing the spinal cord\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is separated from the dura by the subarachnoid space\",\"e\":\"It is separated from the dura mater by the subdural space, but here and there this space is traversed by isolated connective-tissue trabeculae, which are most numerous on the posterior surface of the spinal cord.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It forms the denticulate ligaments\",\"e\":\"The denticulate ligaments are part of the pia mater which help to connect the spinal cord to the arachnoid and dura mater. The arachnoid consists of bundles of white fibrous and elastic tissue intimately blended together. Its outer surface is covered with a layer of low cuboidal mesothelium. The inner surface and the trabeculae are likewise covered by a somewhat low type of cuboidal mesothelium, which in places is flattened to a pavement type.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It invests the cauda equina\",\"e\":\"Above, the spinal arachnoid mater is continuous with the cranial arachnoid Below. it widens out and invests the cauda equina and the nerves proceeding from it\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It has a rich plexus of nerves derived from the vagus\",\"e\":\"A rich plexus of nerves derived from the motor root of the trigeminal, the facial and the accessory nerves, are found in the arachnoid.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"In head anatomy. the middle meningeal artery could be ruptured in head trauma. In particular where there is trauma to the vulnerable pterion of the skull vault. Which of the following is true of the middle meningeal artery?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true of the middle meningeal artery?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is a branch of the maxillary artery which is a branch of the internal carotid artery\",\"e\":\"Although the middle meningeal artery is indeed a branch of the first part of the maxillary artery, the maxillary artery is a branch of the external carotid rather than internal carotid artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It branches off the carotid artery in the infratemporal fossa\",\"e\":\"The middle meningeal artery is a branch of the first part of the maxillary artery, not the carotid artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The posterior branch of the middle meningeal artery is the section most easily damaged during head trauma\",\"e\":\"It is the anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery which is damaged. This branch runs beneath the pterion where it is vulnerable to injury.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"When it ruptures in head injury, it typically causes extradural haematoma\",\"e\":\"Injury to the middle meningeal artery typically causes injury at the level of the pterion. The lentiform shape of the haematoma is formed by the suture lines of the skull stopping further expansion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It passes through the foramen ovale\",\"e\":\"The middle meningeal artery passes through the foramen spinosum. Passing through the foramen ovalet are the mandibular branch of trigeminal nerve, accessory meningeal artery. emissary veins, and occasionally the lesser petrosal nerve.\\n\\nThe middle meningeal artery is a branch of the maxillary artery in the infratemporal fossa. The maxillary artery enters the infratemporal fossa before running forwards between the heads of lateral pterygoid passing into the pterygopalatine fossa. The middle meningeal artery passes through the foramen spinosum. The dura mater is supplied by the anterior. middle and posterior meningeal arteries.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man is brought to hospital following an assault. He has a deep laceration over his right temporal scalp which is bleeding profusely. Which one of the following describes the anatomy of scalp most accurately?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following describes the anatomy of scalp most accurately?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Contains lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The scalp drains to the lymph nodes of the neck. The submandibular lymph and deep cervical nodes drain the anterior scalp while the retro-auricular and occipital nodes drain the posterior scalp.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Contains the C1 dermatome \\\\ -\",\"e\":\"There is no C1 dermatome. There is however a C1 nerve root.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has motor innervation supplied by the facial nerve\",\"e\":\"The occipitofrontalis is supplied by the facial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is supplied exclusively from branches of the external carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The blood supply to the scalp is from branches of both the internal and external carotid arteries. Two such tributaries from the internal carotid are the supraorbital and supratrochlear arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is tightly attached to the cranium\",\"e\":\"The aponeurosis of the scalp is separated from the epicranium by loose connective tissue (the plane of cleavage in scalping), facilitating gliding movements. From superficial to deep, the scalp has five basic layers.+\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman is referred to the neurosurgical out-patient clinic by her GP after she complained of gradual loss of hearing in her left ear, with associated tinnitus and facial weakness. What one is the most likely site of the lesion?\",\"lead\":\"What one is the most likely site of the lesion?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The foramen lacerum\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid artery, a branch of the ascending pharyngeal artery, and the greater petrosal nerve traverse the foramen lacerum. This would not cause the symptoms described.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The foramen magnum\",\"e\":\"The foramen magnum transmits the medulla, spinal arteries, the spinal accessory nerve, and the vertebral arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The foramen rotundum\",\"e\":\"The foramen rotundum transmits the maxillary branch of the trigeminal nerve. This would not account for the symptoms described.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The foramen spinosum\",\"e\":\"The foramen spinosum transmits the middle meningeal artery and vein and the meningeal branch of mandibular nerve. This would not account for the symptoms described.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The internal auditory canal\",\"e\":\"This patient has presented with symptoms consistent with a vestibular schwannoma also known as an acoustic neuroma. These are benign primary intracranial tumours arising from the myelin forming cells of the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII). These can cause symptoms associated with hearing and vertigo. As they grow in size they can compress the facial nerve, although this tends to be late in the disease process, or often due to damage of the nerve from surgical excision. More commonly, the trigeminal nerve is affected.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"With regard to the middle nasal meatus, which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"With regard to the middle nasal meatus, which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It contains the bulla ethmoidalis\",\"e\":\"The middle meatus contains the bulla ethmoidalis (or the ethmoid bulla). The ethmoid bulla is caused by bulging lamella of the middle ethmoidal cells and may be a pneumatised cell - and thereby forming a large prominent anterior air cell - or just a bony prominence - termed torus ethmoidalis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"it drains the nasolarmal duct\",\"e\":\"The nasolacrimal duct drains into the inferior meatus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It drains the posterior ethmoidal air sinus\",\"e\":\"The posterior ethmoidal air sinus drains into the superior meatus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It drains the sphenoidal air cells\",\"e\":\"The sphenoidal air sinus drains into the sphenoethmoidal recess, which is posterior and superior to the superior concha\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is lined by olfactory epithelium containing the primary olfactory neurones\",\"e\":\"Olfactory epithelium containing the primary olfactory neurones lines the superior nasal recess (roof of the nasal cavity) under cover of the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"You are performing a cranial nerve examination on a patient who has a mild head injury. You ask them to stick out their tongue With regards to the examination of the tongue, which of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"You ask them to stick out their tongue With regards to the examination of the tongue, which of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"it is depressed by the hyoglossus\",\"e\":\"The tongue is retracted up and back by the styloglossus muscle, protruded by genioglossus and depressed by the hyoglossus. All these muscles are innervated by the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is passive during the voluntary phase of swallowing\",\"e\":\"The tongue is active during this phase\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is protruded by the styloglossus\",\"e\":\"The tongue is retracted up and back by the styloglossus muscle, protruded by genioglossus and depressed by the hyoglossus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is retracted by the hyoglossus muscle\",\"e\":\"The tongue is retracted by the styloglossus muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It receives sensory innervation from the vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"Anterior two-thirds - special sensory sense (taste) is carried by the chorda tympani (a branch of the facial nerve CN VII). General sensation is from the lingual nerve, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve. Posterior one-third - both taste and general sensation are supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX). Base of the tongue - the internal laryngeal nerve, a branch of the vagus (CN X) supplies this.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant in a parotidectomy for suspected cancer. Pre-operatively the patient had an element of facial nerve palsy accompanying the lump. They are asking you about the anatomical structures you encounter during the operation. Which of the following is true regarding the parotid gland?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true regarding the parotid gland?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Has a duct that passes posterior and deep to the masseter\",\"e\":\"The duct passes anteriorly and superficial to the masseter.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is encapsulated by an investing layer of superficial cervical fascia\",\"e\":\"The fibrous capsule of the parotid gland is an upward extension of the deep investing layer of cervical fascia that attaches to the zygomatic arch\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is separated from the carotid sheath by the styloid process\",\"e\":\"The medial wall of the capsule is separated from the carotid sheath by the styloid process and associated muscles (stylopharyngeus, stylohyoid, styloglossus)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is traversed by the facial artery\",\"e\":\"The external carotid artery passes through the gland, supplying it as it does so, before dividing into its terminal branches, the superficial temporal and maxillary arteries. The facial nerve passes through the gland, but does not innervate it, and divides into its five terminal branches. Other structures that lie within the gland include the retromandibular vein and, often, lymph nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Receives its blood supply directly from the internal carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The parotid receives blood from the external carotid artery as it passes through the gland. The posterior auricular artery comes off from the external carotid, before the external carotid artery divides into its two terminal branches, the maxillary and the superficial temporal branches.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the ear, nose and throat (ENT) clinic with a painful swelling around the jaw and you suspect there is a mass in the submandibular salivary gland. You confirm your findings when you bimanually palpate the gland through the mouth. Which of the following is true regarding the submandibular gland?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true regarding the submandibular gland?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Has the facial artery running posterior it\",\"e\":\"The facial artery arises from the external carotid artery, on the anteromedial surface. It gives off an ascending palatine artery and tonsillar artery. The facial artery passes deep to the posterior belly of digastric and then runs on the posterior surface of the submandibular\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Has the hypoglossal nerve running through it\",\"e\":\"The hypoglossal nerve runs deep to the submandibular gland rather than running through it. This nerve is at risk of injury in submandibular gland excision.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is deep to the hyoglossus muscle\",\"e\":\"The submandibular gland lies superficial to the hyoglossus muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lies entirely below the lower mandible\",\"e\":\"Only part of the gland lies below the mandible.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lies below the digastric muscle\",\"e\":\"The submandibular gland lies within the digastric triangle (part of the anterior triangle). This triangle is bound inferiorly by the two bellies of the digastric muscle and superiorly lies bound by the lower border of the mandible. Therefore the submandibular gland lies superior to the digastric muscle.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"You are examining a child in the Ear, Nose and Throat clinic whose mother says has had previous problems with his Eustachian tubes but has no other details: Which of the following is true regarding the Eustachian tube?\",\"lead\":\"You are examining a child in the Ear, Nose and Throat clinic whose mother says has had previous problems with his Eustachian tubes but has no other details: Which of the following is true regarding the Eustachian tube?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Can be obstructed by an enlarging palatine tonsil\",\"e\":\"The palatine tonsils are situated in the oropharynx while the Eustachian tube opens into the nasopharynx. Its opening is surrounded by the tubal tonsil. It can be obstructed by enlarged adenoids, which can increase the risk of glue ear (chronic otitis media with effusion).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Closes during swallowing\",\"e\":\"The Eustachian tube opens on swallowing under the action of the salpingopharynges and tensor palati muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Drains the inner ear\",\"e\":\"The Eustachian tube drains the middle ear. The opening to the Eustachian tube from the middle ear is situated in the inferior anterior and medial aspect of the middle ear.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gives attachment to the tensor veli palatini muscle\",\"e\":\"The cartilaginous part gives attachment to the tensor veli palatini muscle. This muscle is innervated by the nerve to medial ptergoid, which is a branch of the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pierces the pharyngobasilar fascia to drain into the oropharynx\",\"e\":\"The pharyngotympanic tube drains into the nasopharynx rather than oropharynx.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient with a facial nerve palsy after resection of an acoustic neuroma. The facial nerve supplies which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The facial nerve supplies which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Buccinator muscle\",\"e\":\"The buccal branch of the facial nerve supplies the buccinator.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Medial pterygoid muscle\",\"e\":\"The medial ptergoid muscle is a muscle of mastication, most of which are supplied by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve. The medial ptergoid is specifically supplied by the nerve to medial ptergoid, an branch of the mandibular branch.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Parasympathetic to the parotid gland\",\"e\":\"The parasympathetic supply to the parotid gland is from the inferior salivatory nucleus via the tympanic and lesser petrosal branches of the glossopharyngeal nerve that project to the otic ganglion (preganglionic). Postganglionic fibres pass from the otic ganglion to the parotid via the auriculotemporal nerve (a branch of the mandibular nerve).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sensation to the inside of the mouth\",\"e\":\"Sensation to the inside of the mouth is supplied by the buccal nerve - a branch of the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve. This nerve is not to be confused with the buccal branch of the facial nerve, which supplies muscles of facial expression.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sympathetics to the lacrimal gland\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic supply to the lacrimal gland is via the nerve of the pterygoid canal, which runs with the lacrimal nerve, one of the three major branches of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve. The lacrimal nerve also carries parasympathetic fibres from the zygomatic branch of the maxillary nerve from pterygopalatine ganglion.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"You are in the Ear, Nose and Throat clinic reviewing a patient with chronic infection of the middle ear - chronic otitis media. Which of the following regarding the middle ear is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following regarding the middle ear is true?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"it contains all the auditory ossicles\",\"e\":\"The middle ear contains the malleus, incus and stapes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It has a promontory in the projection of the lateral semicircular canal\",\"e\":\"The semicircular canals are in the inner ear. The medial wall of the middle ear has a rounded projection representing the first turn of the cochlea.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It has the facial nerve running through its roof\",\"e\":\"The facial nerve runs medial to the middle ear cavity and superior to the promontory and oval window.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It has the internal carotid artery running medially and posteriorly\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid artery runs anterior and inferior to the middle ear cavity.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It has the internal jugular vein running medially and superiorly\",\"e\":\"The internal jugular vein is located inferior to the middle ear cavity. Thin bone separates the middle ear from the bulb of the internal jugular vein.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a child in the Ear, Nose and Throat out-patient clinic following a tonsillectomy some weeks ago. You are pleased they did not suffer post-tonsillectomy haemorrhage as a complication. Which of the following is true regarding the palatine tonsillar bed?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true regarding the palatine tonsillar bed?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Contains the superior pharyngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The palatine tonsillar bed contains the glossopharyngeal nerve. This nerve is responsible for the referred otalgia when the tonsils become inflamed or when patients have undergone tonsillectomy. The glossopharyngeal nerve has a tympanic nerve called Jacobson's nerve, which is responsible for this phenomenon.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is drained by the external palatine vein\",\"e\":\"The external palatine vein drains the tonsil, into the pharyngeal plexus which subsequently drains into the internal jugular vein. The venous plexus around the tonsillar bed is a common source of bleeding after tonsillectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is floored by the middle constrictor muscle\",\"e\":\"The floor the palatine tonsillar bed is bound by the superior constrictor muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is pierced by branches of the mandibular artery\",\"e\":\"The tonsil is supplied by the tonsillar branch of the facial artery, which runs alongside the styloglossus, after which it penetrates the superior pharyngeal constrictor to enter the palatine fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lies in the oral cavity\",\"e\":\"The palatine fossa lies in the oropharynx, not the oral cavity. The oropharynx is bound anteriorly by the base of the tongue and palatoglossal folds. The superior boundary is the soft palate. The inferior boundary is the superior surface of the epiglottis. The posterior boundary is the posterior pharyngeal wall.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":73,\"stem\":\"You are in neurosurgical outpatients' clinic reviewing a patient with trigeminal neuralgia.\\n\\nWhich of the following is true regarding the trigeminal nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true regarding the trigeminal nerve?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Has a mandibular division that is purely sensory\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Has ophthalmic and maxillary divisions that are purely motor\",\"e\":\"The ophthalmic and maxillary divisions of the trigeminal nerve are largely sensory, although they do carry parasympathetic and sympathetic nerve fibres. They do not innervate skeletal muscle however.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Supplies sensation to the angle of the mandible\",\"e\":\"The angle of the mandible is supplied by the great auricular nerve (C2-C3)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Supplies the buccinator muscle\",\"e\":\"The buccinator muscle is supplied by the buccal branch of the facial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supplies the masseter muscle\",\"e\":\"The mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve supplies the muscles of mastication - temporalis, masseter and medial and lateral ptergoid muscles\\n\\nThe mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve supplies the muscles the mastication - temporalis, masseter and the pterygoid muscles. It also supplies the tensor veli palatine and tensor tympani via the nerve to the medial ptergoid. It also supplies the mylohyoid and anterior belly of the digastric muscle via the nerve to the mylohyoid.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"The foramen magnum is one of several openings at the base of the skull. Which important neurological structure/s pass through this?\",\"lead\":\"Which important neurological structure/s pass through this?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Facial nerve VIl\",\"e\":\"The facial nerve exits the middle cranial fossa via the internal auditory meatus along with the vestibulocochlear nerve. It enters the internal auditory meatus as two nerve roots: motor nerve root and the nervus intermedius.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal nerve XII\",\"e\":\"The hypoglossal nerve passes through the hypoglossal canal, lateral to the foramen magnum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Optic nerve I\",\"e\":\"The optic nerve passes through the optic canal.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The medulla oblongata and the spinal accessory nerve\",\"e\":\"Both the medulla of the brainstem and the spinal portion of the spinal accessory nerve pass through the foramen magnum. The anterior and posterior spinal arteries, and vertebral arteries also pass through the foramen magnum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vestibulocochlear nerve VIII\",\"e\":\"The vestibulocochlear nerve passes into the internal auditory meatus, lying inferior to the facial nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"Following parotid gland surgery in a 51-year-old woman, the facial nerve is damaged What is the most likely consequence for the patient?\",\"lead\":\"Following parotid gland surgery in a 51-year-old woman, the facial nerve is damaged What is the most likely consequence for the patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Altered taste to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue\",\"e\":\"The chorda tympani carries taste sensation from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. It separates from the facial nerve within the temporal bone and is therefore not part of the facial nerve at the level of the parotid gland. The chorda tympani, after separating, form the facial nerve, then runs back into the middle ear to run on the posterior wall. It travels between the pars flaccida and over the handle of the malleus. It passes through the petrous temporal bone before leaving the skull via the petrotympanic fissure. The chorda tympani then enters into the infratemporal fossa to join the lingual nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Difficulty in chewing food therefore weakness in the muscle of mastication\",\"e\":\"The muscles of mastication are supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inability to rotate the neck because of weakness in sternocleidomastoid\",\"e\":\"The sternocleidomastoid muscles are supplied by the spinal root of the accessory nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Slurred speech due to weakness in tongue movements\",\"e\":\"Injury to the facial nerve would result in weakness in the muscles of facial expression, and not the motor supply of the tongue. The intrinsic muscles of the tongue are innervated by the hypoglossal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Weakness in buccinator and orbicularis oris\",\"e\":\"The buccal branch of the facial nerve supplies the buccinator and orbicularis oris muscle: Injury to the facial nerve can occur in parotid surgery as well as middle ear and temporal bone surgery, as this represents the course of the facial nerve. Facial nerve monitoring is used to assess facial nerve proximity and to minimise the risk of intra-operative injury.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":79,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man presents to clinic with pain and parotid swelling, which is worse with eating. You palpate a stone in the parotid duct Which of the following is true of the parotid duct?\",\"lead\":\"You palpate a stone in the parotid duct Which of the following is true of the parotid duct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Has an opening located on the floor of the mouth next to the frenulum\",\"e\":\"The submandibular duct opens to the floor of the mouth next to the frenulum. The parotid duct usually opens into the mouth next to the upper second molars:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Has an opening opposite the first lower molar\",\"e\":\"The opening of Stensen's duct (parotid duct) opens opposite the upper second molar: Ascending parotitis can occur from dental infection in this area.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is approximately 1cm long\",\"e\":\"The parotid duct is approximately 5 cm long\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lies in the middle third of a line between the intertragic notch of the auricle and the mid-point of the philtrum\",\"e\":\"The parotid duct opens on the middle third of a line between the intertragic notch of the auricle and the midpoint of the philtrum, opposite the second upper molar.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Runs between mylohyoid and hyoglossus\",\"e\":\"The parotid duct crosses the masseter, turning around its anterior border to pass through the buccal fat pad and pierce the buccinator. When intraoral pressure is raised, the submucous part of the parotid duct is compressed by the buccinator. 733\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":81,\"stem\":\"You are performing a parotidectomy Which one of the following options lies within the gland you are excising?\",\"lead\":\"You are performing a parotidectomy Which one of the following options lies within the gland you are excising?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"External carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The external carotid artery runs through the parotid gland giving off the superficial temporal and maxillary arteries within the gland.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"MS Masseter\",\"e\":\"The masseter lies anterior and medial to the parotid gland. The parotid duct travels over the anterior border of the masseter.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The buccal artery\",\"e\":\"The buccal artery is a branch of the second part of the maxillary artery. The maxillary artery is a direct branch of the external carotid artery, which travels through the gland.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Internal carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid supplies the brain and is not related to the parotid.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"The trigeminal nerve does not lie within the parotid gland. Sensory innervation to the gland is via the auriculotemporal nerve, a branch of the mandibular division.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old patient with a malignant submandibular tumour is undergoing a submandibular gland excision with which you are assisting. Which one of these structures lies posterior to the superficial part of the submandibular gland?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these structures lies posterior to the superficial part of the submandibular gland?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cervical branch of the facial nerve\",\"e\":\"The cervical branch of the facial nerve passes inferiorly to the submandibular gland.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Facial vein\",\"e\":\"The facial vein is superficial to the submandibular gland.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Facial artery\",\"e\":\"The facial artery passes over the posterior surface of the submandibular gland in a groove.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lingual nerve\",\"e\":\"The lingual nerve lies laterally to the submandibular duct and medial to the gland. It is at risk in submandibular gland excision along with the hypoglossal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Digastric muscle\",\"e\":\"The digastric muscle is inferior to the submandibular gland. The mylohyoid lies deep to the superficial lobe of the submandibular gland. The mylohyoid separates the superficial and deep lobes of the submandibular gland\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":85,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant in the maxillofacial surgery clinic. A patient is being reviewed following a mandibular dislocation which was successfully treated. They complain of some ongoing pain in the temporomandibular joint Which one of the following statements is true regarding the TMJ?\",\"lead\":\"They complain of some ongoing pain in the temporomandibular joint Which one of the following statements is true regarding the TMJ?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The TMJ contains three synovial cavities.\",\"e\":\"The TMJ contains two synovial cavities - the superior and inferior synovial cavity separated by an articular disc.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"the upper compartment is responsible for elevation and depression or the mandible\",\"e\":\"The upper compartment of the TMJ is responsible for protrusion and retraction of the jaw, as well as side-to-side movement. The lower compartment is responsible for elevation and depression of the mandible.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The TMJ is a typical synovial joint\",\"e\":\"The TMJ is an atypical synovial condyloid joint. It consists of two cavities separated by an articular disc. The articular surfaces of the temporal bone and the mandible are covered by fibrocartilage rather than hyaline cartilage\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The articular surfaces of the TMJ are covered by hyaline cartilage.\",\"e\":\"Both the articular surface of the mandibular fossa and the head of the mandible are covered by fibrocartilage rather than hyaline cartilage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The TMJ lies anteriorly to the squamo-tympanic fissure.\",\"e\":\"The capsule of the TMJ lies anteriorly to the squamo-tympanic fissure. The temporomandibular joint is a synovial joint situated between the condyle of the mandible below and the mandibular fossa of the temporal bone above. Although it a synovial joint, it is lined by fibrous cartilage (rather than hyaline cartilage typical of synovial joints). The joint is surrounded by a capsule that is attached beyond the limits of the articular surfaces. strengthened medially and laterally by collateral ligaments. Posteriorly, the capsule is attached to the anterior edge of the squamo-tympanic fissure. Medially, it runs along the suture between the temporal and sphenoid bones and attaches anteriorly to the anterior end of the articular eminence. Laterally, the capsule is attached to the articular tubercle, which forms the lateral limit of the articular eminence and the prominent ridge of the bone forming the lateral lip of the glenoid cavity. The joint is stable anteriorly but is laxed posteriorly as it is attached well below the articular surface to the neck of the condyle.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":88,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old man is seen in a routine out-patient clinic follow up 6 weeks after a right submandibular gland excision. Movement of his face, neck and tongue are all normal, but he is noted to have ulceration on the right anterior part of the tongue\\n\\nInjury to which of the following structures could have caused this?\",\"lead\":\"Injury to which of the following structures could have caused this?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inferior alveolar nerve\",\"e\":\"The inferior alveolar nerve is not classically at risk in submandibular nerve excision. It is a relatively deep and medial structure that enters the mandibular foramen and supplies sensation to the ipsilateral gum and lower teeth. It gives off a small nerve to the mylohyoid before it enters this foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lingual nerve\",\"e\":\"The lingual nerve supplies the somatic sensation to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. Therefore, loss of sensation to the tongue ipsilaterally to the side of nerve injury may result in ulceration (eg from accidentally biting the tongue). The lingual nerve is a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve. Taste sensation from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue is derived from the chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve, which runs with the lingual nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Marginal mandibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The marginal mandibular nerve is one of the five main terminal branches of the facial nerve that supply the muscles of facial expression. Injury to this nerve would result in weakness in the muscles supplying the lower lip on the ipsilateral side - classically presenting as drooling and inability to close the mouth on the affected side. It would not affect the tongue, as tongue movement is derived from the hypoglossal nerve. It is one of the three main nerves at risk during submandibular nerve excision, the other two being the lingual nerve and hypoglossal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spinal accessory nerve\",\"e\":\"The spinal accessory nerve is not at risk during submandibular gland excision, and injury to this nerve does not affect the tongue as the spinal accessory nerve supplies the sternocleidomastoid muscle and trapezius muscles. Injury to this nerve would be expected to result in weakness in shoulder elevation and turning of the head in the ipsilateral direction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal nerve\",\"e\":\"Although the hypoglossal nerve is one of the three major nerves most at risk in submandibular gland excision, injury to this nerve is expected to result in ipsilateral weakness of tongue protrusion, as the hypoglossal nerve supplies all the intrinsic muscles of the tongue. This would lead to the classical sign of tongue deviation towards the side of the\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":91,\"stem\":\"The facial nerve is a structure of utmost importance in head and neck surgery. Which one of the following muscles is supplied by the facial nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following muscles is supplied by the facial nerve?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"RI Anterior belly of digastric\",\"e\":\"The posterior belly of the digastric muscle is supplied by the digastric branch of the facial nerve. The anterior belly of digastric is supplied by the nerve to mylohyoid, from the inferior alveolar nerve - a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Buccinator\",\"e\":\"The buccinator is one of the muscles of facial expression, and is therefore supplied by the facial nerve (buccal branch): The facial nerve exits the skull via the stylomastoid foramen and enters the parotid gland, where it splits into five terminal branches - temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular and cervical.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medial pterygoid\",\"e\":\"The medial ptergoid muscle is supplied by the V3 nerve (mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve), via the nerve to the medial ptergoid muscle. The nerve to the medial pterygoid also supplies two other muscles: the tensor veli palatini and the tensor tympani.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mylohyoid\",\"e\":\"The mylohyoid muscle is supplied by the mylohyoid nerve (or nerve to mylohyoid), a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Temporalis\",\"e\":\"The temporalis muscle is supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve, as are the other muscles of mastication.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":93,\"stem\":\"During a superficial parotidectomy the facial nerve is inadvertently injured Which one of the following problems might the patient experience?\",\"lead\":\"During a superficial parotidectomy the facial nerve is inadvertently injured Which one of the following problems might the patient experience?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dry mouth\",\"e\":\"The parasympathetic innervation to the salivary glands of the oral cavity do not pass through the parotid gland. The parasympathetic supply to the parotid comes from the lesser petrosal nerve, which branches from the geniculate ganglion in the middle ear and synapses within the otic ganglion, and is carried on the auriculotemporal nerve to the parotid gland. The parasympathetic nerve supply to the sublingual glands and submandibular gland are carried via the chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve, which joins the lingual nerve to supply the glands. Damage to the facial nerve after it exits from the skull at the stylomastoid foramen would not lead to a dry mouth as the parasympathetic supply has already been given off in the middle ear.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Loss of corneal reflex\",\"e\":\"The afferent limb (sensation) of the corneal reflex is carried on the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve, and the efferent limb (the motor reflex) is via the zygomatic branches of the facial nerve to orbicularis oculi, which results in blinking. Injury at the level of the parotid would lead to lower motor neurone paralysis of the ipsilateral orbicularis oculi and the corneal reflex would therefore be lost.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Loss of taste from anterior two-thirds of tongue\",\"e\":\"Taste sensation to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue is via the chorda tympani nerve. which unites with the facial nerve within the middle ear temporal bone. As this is proximal to the parotid gland it would not be affected with injury to the facial nerve at the level of the parotid\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Loss of taste from posterior third of tongue\",\"e\":\"Taste from the posterior third of the tongue is carried by the glossopharyngeal nerve and not by the facial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Numbness of cheek\",\"e\":\"Somatic sensation to the cheek is supplied by the maxillary and mandibular divisions of the trigeminal nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":96,\"stem\":\"Within the orbital socket, there are three main foramina: the superior and inferior orbital fissures, and the optic canal.\\n\\nWhich one of the following enters the orbit via the optic canal?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following enters the orbit via the optic canal?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abducens nerve\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lacrimal nerve\",\"e\":\"The lacrimal nerve (a branch of the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal) enters the orbit via the superior orbital fissure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ophthalmic artery\",\"e\":\"The ophthalmic artery enters the orbit via the optic canal along with the optic nerve. The optic veins, however, exit from the orbit via the superior and inferior orbital fissures.\\n\\nThe abducens nerve enters the orbit via the superior orbital fissure, within the tendinous ring.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superior and inferior ophthalmic veins\",\"e\":\"The superior and inferior optic veins exit from the orbit via the superior and inferior orbital fissure respectively. The inferior ophthalmic vein occasionally exits via the superior orbital fissure, or joins the superior orbital vein.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trochlear nerve\",\"e\":\"The trochlear nerve enters the orbit via superior orbital fissure, outside of the tendinous ring and above the superior oblique muscle.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old boy presents to the Accident and Emergency department with erythematous, swollen tonsils and left ear pain. Examination of the ear is unremarkable\\n\\nReferred pain from which nerve is most likely to be responsible for these symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"Referred pain from which nerve is most likely to be responsible for these symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Facial nerve\",\"e\":\"The facial nerve does not innervate the tonsils, although it does give off a somatic sensory branch to the auditory canal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glossopharyngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The glossopharyngeal nerve innervates the tonsillar fossa. It also gives a sensory branch to the ear (Jacobsen's nerve) from which referred pain can originate:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal nerve\",\"e\":\"The hypoglossal nerve supplies only motor function to the tongue. It does not provide sensation to the tonsils, and has no auricular branches.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lesser palatine nerve\",\"e\":\"Although the lesser palatine nerve innervates the tonsil as well as the soft palate, it does not have auricular branches and therefore is not responsible for referred pain to the ear.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The superior laryngeal nerve innervates the larynx above the level of the vocal cords via its internal branch. Its external branch innervates the cricothyroid muscle.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":100,\"stem\":\"A patient is hit in the face with a piece of shrapnel. During exploration of the wound in theatre, it is clear there has been injury to the mandibular nerve (CN VC) Transection of the anterior division of CN Vc in the infra-temporal fossa is most likely to result in which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"During exploration of the wound in theatre, it is clear there has been injury to the mandibular nerve (CN VC) Transection of the anterior division of CN Vc in the infra-temporal fossa is most likely to result in which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Deviation of the jaw away from the side of the lesion on protrusion\",\"e\":\"The anterior division of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve contains branches to lateral pterygoid, masseter and temporalis muscies and buccal skin. Injury to this nerve produces deviation of the jaw towards the side of the lesion on protrusion due to the unopposed action of the contralateral lateral pterygoid.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"sivi- Dysphagia\",\"e\":\"The muscles of swallowing are innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve, not the anterior division of the mandibular nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ipsilateral anaesthesia of the mandibular teeth\",\"e\":\"Sensation to the lower teeth and gums is via the inferior alveolar nerve, which comes from the posterior division of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ipsilateral anaesthesia of the mucosa of the oral vestibule\",\"e\":\"The inner cheek mucosa is supplied by the buccal branch of the anterior division of the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve - the only sensory branch of the anterior division.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ipsilateral paralysis of the buccinator muscle\",\"e\":\"The buccinator muscles supplied by the buccal branch of the facial nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"A 15-year-old rugby player sustains a head injury during a match and collapses after initially playing on. He is taken to Accident and Emergency where a computed tomography (CT) scan demonstrates a large left extradural haematoma. At operation, damage to the middle meningeal artery is noted to be the cause:\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding this artery?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding this artery?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arises from the superficial temporal branch of the external carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The middle meningeal artery is derived from the first part of the maxillary artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Enters the skull through the foramen ovale\",\"e\":\"The middle meningeal artery enters the cranium via the foramen spinosum. The accessory meningeal artery enters the skull via the foramen ovale.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is unlikely to be damaged in fractures at the pterion\",\"e\":\"The pterion represents a weak point of the skull at the junction of the parietal, frontal, squamous temporal bone, and greater wing of the sphenoid. Fractures occurring here classically have a tendency to injure the middle meningeal artery, the anterior division of which runs upon its deep surface.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Originates in the pterygopalatine fossa\",\"e\":\"The middle meningeal artery originates from the first part of the maxillary artery, lateral to the pterygopalatine fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supplies part of the dura mater and the calvaria\",\"e\":\"The middle meningeal artery divides from the maxillary branch of the external carotid. entering the cranium through the foramen spinosum. It is the largest artery that supplies the dura and also the calvaria. While the maxillary artery does give off branches in the pterygopalatine fossa region, the middle meningeal artery comes off more proximally, deep to the ramus of the mandible. It is particularly prone to damage caused by temporal bone fractures.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":105,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man presents with a swelling in the anterior triangle of the neck. Biopsy confirms a superficial submandibular gland carcinoma. Which structure crosses superficial to the submandibular gland?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure crosses superficial to the submandibular gland?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lingual nerve...-\",\"e\":\"The lingual nerve lies deep the submandibular gland and is intimately related to the submandibular duct. It is at risk of injury during these procedures.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mylohyoid muscle\",\"e\":\"The mylohyoid muscle lies between the superficial and deep parts of the submandibular gland.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The marginal mandibular branch of the facial nerve\",\"e\":\"The marginal mandibular branch of the facial nerve lies superiorly and superficially to the submandibular gland. Its close relationship with the gland is the reason why surgeons will have a tendency to maintain more than one finger's breadth from the inferior margin of the mandible before making their initial incision for excision of the gland. Damage to the marginal mandibular branch leads to ipsilateral paralysis of the muscles supplying mouth expression, leading to drooping of the corner of the mouth and drooling.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The internal jugular vein\",\"e\":\"The internal jugular vein lies deep and laterally to the submandibular gland\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The external carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The external carotid artery lies deep and laterally to the submandibular gland 767\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old man presents to clinic concerning a lump on the posterior third of his tongue in the midline. Which one of the following is this most likely to represent?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is this most likely to represent?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Filiform papillae\",\"e\":\"The filiform papillae represent normal structures situated on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fungiform papillae\",\"e\":\"The fungiform papillae represent normal structures and are interspersed among the filiform papillae, usually situated on the tip and sides of the tongue.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lymphoid tissue\",\"e\":\"The lymphoid tissue at the base of the tongue is also known as the lingual tonsil. It forms a part of Waldeyer's ring - a ring of lymphoid tissue within the pharynx. These lymphoid structures may become enlarged from benign causes (eg reactive secondary to infection) or malignancy (primary or secondary).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Palatine tonsil\",\"e\":\"The palatine tonsils are found laterally between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches, rather than in the midline.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Circumvallate papillae\",\"e\":\"The circumvallate papillae are large and flat topped, and found anteriorly to the terminal sulcus of the tongue in two rows forming a V-shape. 755\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":109,\"stem\":\"A 7-year-old boy is seen by his GP for otitis media of his left ear. On further questioning it emerges that he has had recurrent upper respiratory tract infections and has erythematous swollen tonsils at present.\\n\\nWhich part of the skull does the Eustachian tube penetrate?\",\"lead\":\"Which part of the skull does the Eustachian tube penetrate?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Greater wing of the sphenoid C.\",\"e\":\"The cartilaginous part of the Eustachian tube is intimately related to the greater wing of the sphenoid, although the tube does not penetrate it.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lesser wing of the sphenoid\",\"e\":\"The cartilaginous part of the Eustachian tube is intimately related to the greater wing of the sphenoid, not the lesser wing.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Occipital bone\",\"e\":\"The occipital bone is mostly flat, forming the posterior and postero-inferior aspect of the skull. The Eustachian tube is not related to the occipital bone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Petrous temporal bone\",\"e\":\"The Eustachian tube, also known as the pharyngotympanic tube or auditory tube connects the middle ear to the nasopharynx. The posterolateral third of the tube is bony and the remainder cartilaginous. The bony part of the Eustachian tube perforates the petrous temporal bone. The Eustachian tube in a child is shorter and more horizontal. The opening of the auditory tube lies above the soft palate adjacent to the tubal tonsil.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Squamous temporal bone\",\"e\":\"The squamous temporal bone is the flatter part of the temporal bone, which extends superiorly forming the lateral aspect of the skull. It is the petrous temporal bone which houses the Eustachian tube\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":111,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old heavy smoker is referred urgently to a maxillofacial surgery department with a suspected oral cavity malignancy by his GP Which one of the following options is the most common site for an oral cavity malignancy?\",\"lead\":\"A 56-year-old heavy smoker is referred urgently to a maxillofacial surgery department with a suspected oral cavity malignancy by his GP Which one of the following options is the most common site for an oral cavity malignancy?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hard palate\",\"e\":\"Tongue cancers are around four times more common than palatal cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mucosa of vestibule\",\"e\":\"The vestibule of the mouth is a relatively uncommon location of oral cavity cancer, but can be easily missed on clinical examination.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mucosa overlying palatoglossal arch\",\"e\":\"Although tongue cancers as a whole are the most common oral cavity cancers, these are mostly found on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue rather than on its base.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Soft palate\",\"e\":\"The palate is a relatively uncommon location of oral cavity cancer. The tongue is the most common location.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tongue\",\"e\":\"Oral cavity malignancies are usually squamous-cell carcinomas and 35% of these occur on the tongue. They tend to be located on the lateral edge of the tongue. These types of cancer are associated with tobacco smoking, low fruit/vegetable diet, and alcohol consumption.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":113,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old diabetic man who is a lifelong smoker is referred by his GP to a rapid access transient ischaemic attack (TIA) clinic because of episodes of transient vision loss affecting his left eye. In the clinic he is thought to have had episodes of amaurosis fugax due to emboli affecting the ophthalmic artery.\\n\\nThe ophthalmic artery is a branch of which vessel?\",\"lead\":\"The ophthalmic artery is a branch of which vessel?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The anterior cerebral artery is formed from the bifurcation of the internal carotid artery into the anterior and middle cerebral arteries. It passes around the genu of the corpus callosum and mainly supplies the medial aspects of the frontal lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"External carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The ophthalmic artery is a branch of the internal, not external carotid.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Facial artery\",\"e\":\"The facial artery, a branch of the external carotid, supplies much of the arterial supply to the superficial face, although not to the retina.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Internal carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The ophthalmic artery is a direct branch of the internal carotid artery. The artery lies inferolateral to the optic nerve. It gives off a number of branches, the first and one of the smallest being the central retinal artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Middle cerebral artery\",\"e\":\"The middle cerebral artery is a more distal branch of the internal carotid artery\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":115,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old student is brought into the Emergency Department with severe jaw pain. On examination, his mouth is wide open and he cannot close it. His friend reports the pain developed while yawning. A dislocated temporomandibular joint is diagnosed Which one of the following muscles is most important in the active opening of the mouth?\",\"lead\":\"A dislocated temporomandibular joint is diagnosed Which one of the following muscles is most important in the active opening of the mouth?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Buccinator\",\"e\":\"The buccinators has a role of assisting with chewing, although it is not technically regarded as a muscle of mastication. Specifically, it has a role in holding the cheek to the teeth during chewing, and is supplied by the facial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lateral pterygoid\",\"e\":\"The lateral pterygoid muscle is important for active opening of the mouth - drawing the condyle and disc forwards from the mandibular fossa.\\n\\nThe action of the medial ptergoid is to elevate the mandible, protract it, and laterally displace the mandible. It is a muscle of mastication and supplied by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal (nerve to medial ptergoid).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Masseter\",\"e\":\"The masseter is a muscle of mastication, although its main role is closure of the mouth.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Medial pieryguiet\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Temporalis\",\"e\":\"The temporalis muscle is a muscle of mastication whose main role is to close the mouth and elevate the jaw. Its posterior fibres have a role in retracting the jaw as well.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":117,\"stem\":\"You are treating a 33-year-old woman with sinusitis. Scans reveal that the posterior ethmoidal sinus is affected. To where does the posterior ethmoidal sinus drain?\",\"lead\":\"To where does the posterior ethmoidal sinus drain?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inferior meatus\",\"e\":\"None of the sinuses drain into the inferior meatus. It is the nasolacrimal duct that drains into the inferior meatus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Infundibulum of the middle meatus\",\"e\":\"The frontal sinus and the anterior ethmoid cells (sinus) drains into the infundibulum of the middle meatus. The posterior ethmoid sinus drains into the superior meatus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Middle meatus, but not the infundibulum\",\"e\":\"The maxillary sinus and middle ethmoidal cells open into the middle meatus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sphenoethmoidal recess\",\"e\":\"The sphenoidal sinus drains into the sphenoethmoidal recess, which is posterior and superior to the superior concha.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior meatus\",\"e\":\"The posterior ethmoidal sinus drains into the superior meatus.\"}]},{\"t\":53,\"p\":119,\"stem\":\"You are examining a patient's neurological status. You are happy to document in the notes, 'pupils equal and reactive to light!\\n\\nWith which cranial nerve do parasympathetic nerve fibres travel to innervate the ciliary body and sphincter pupillae muscles?\",\"lead\":\"With which cranial nerve do parasympathetic nerve fibres travel to innervate the ciliary body and sphincter pupillae muscles?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abducens nerve\",\"e\":\"The abducens nerve does not carry parasympathetic fibres. The fibres that innervate the ciliary body and sphincter pupillae muscles are carried on the oculomotor nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Accessory nerve\",\"e\":\"The accessory nerve does not carry parasympathetic fibres.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Facial nerve\",\"e\":\"The facial nerve carries multiple parasympathetic fibres. These travel on the greater petrosal nerve through the petrous temporal bone, then deep petrosal nerve. These particular fibres synapse in the ptergopalatine ganglion to subsequently supply the lacrimal, and nasal glands. Other parasympathetic fibres travel with the chorda tympani, synapse in the submandibular ganglion, and innervate the submandibular and sublingual glands.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Glossopharyngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The glossopharyngeal nerve carries parasympathetic fibres to the parotid gland via the otic ganglion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Oculomotor nerve\",\"e\":\"Parasympathetic fibres from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus are carried along the oculomotor nerve and then its inferior division to synapse in the ciliary ganglion. These parasympathetic fibres then supply the ciliaris and sphincter pupillae muscles.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"The blade of a retractor has rested on the psoas muscle during a lengthy operative procedure. This has resulted in a femoral nerve palsy. In the post-operative period, the patient may experience which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"In the post-operative period, the patient may experience which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inability to flex the knee only\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve supplies the anterior thigh muscles - the quadriceps group, which extend the leg at the knee.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inability to flex the knee and numbness over the thigh\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve is responsible for extension of the knee. It does supply sensation to the skin of the thigh.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Numbness over the anterior thigh only\",\"e\":\"A femoral nerve paisy may result in motor deficiencies, as well as paraesthesia\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inability to extend the knee and numbness over the anterior thigh\",\"e\":\"It supplies the anterior thigh muscles - the quadriceps group, which extends the leg at the knee. The femoral nerve also gives several branches to the skin on the anteromedial side of the lower limb. The femoral nerve is the largest branch of the lumbar plexus (L2-4). It forms in the abdomen within the substance of the psoas major muscle and descends posterolaterally through the pelvis to the midpoint of the inguinal ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inability to extend the hip and numbness over the anterior thigh\",\"e\":\"Hip extension occurs by the gluteus maximus (inferior gluteal nerve) and the hamstrings (sciatic nerve)\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old boy with chronic diarrhoea, bloody stools and weight loss also reports left hip pain. Clinically, he is pyrexial with a temperature of 38°C and his left thigh is flexed Straightening the thigh causes pain. A computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen and pelvis reveals a psoas abscess. What is the insertion of the psoas major muscle?\",\"lead\":\"What is the insertion of the psoas major muscle?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Distal femoral shaft\",\"e\":\"The psoas muscle does not insert here.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral neck\",\"e\":\"The psoas muscle does not insert.here.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Greater trochanter\",\"e\":\"The psoas muscle does not insert here.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lesser trochanter\",\"e\":\"The psoas muscle originates from the lateral surfaces of the T12-L5 vertebrae, and inserts alongside the iliacus muscle as the iliopsoas tendon at the lesser trochanter of the femur - it is highlighted in red in the image below. The psoas muscle acts to flex the hip joint. Psoas abscess is a recognised complication of Crohn's disease and can also develop secondary to appendicitis, renal infection and spinal tuberculosis.\\n\\nQuadratus Psoas lumborum minor\\n\\nIliacus Psoas major\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Proximal femoral shaft\",\"e\":\"The psoas muscle does not insert here.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old man has gangrene of the left hallux. There are no pulses to feel below the rather weak emoral pulse on that side. A duplex scan reveals a block in the superficial femoral artery. Which one of the following statements pertaining to the arterial system of the lower limb best accords with usual clinical findings?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements pertaining to the arterial system of the lower limb best accords with usual clinical findings?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The common femoral artery gives off the profunda branch a hand's breadth below the inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"The common femoral artery divides into the superficial femoral artery (also known simply as the femoral artery) and the profunda 3 cm distal to the inguinal ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The femoral vein lies on the lateral side of the common femoral artery at the groin\",\"e\":\"The femoral vein lies medial to the common femoral artery. Medial to the femoral vein is an empty space, which allows the femoral vein to expand during walking (when venous return is increased). This empty space is also known as the femoral canal, and this is where intra-abdominal contents can herniate into, causing a femoral hernia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The removal artery passes into the popliteal rossa, as the popliteal artery, by passing through a gap in adductor longus known as the adductor hiatus.\",\"e\":\"The superficial femoral artery becomes the popliteal artery by passing through the hiatus in the adductor magnus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The popliteal artery lies against the popliteal surface of the femur deep to the popliteal vein, which itself lies deep to the tibial nerve\",\"e\":\"The popliteal artery* is a continuation of the superficial femoral artery (also simply known as the femoral artery) as it passes through the adductor hiatus: It the deepest structure found in the popliteal fossa. The fossa also contains the popliteal vein, tibial nerve, the common peroneal nerve, and lymph nodes. The popliteal fossa can be seen below, with the popliteal artery lying deepest\\n\\nPopliteal artery Tibial nerve\\n\\nPopliteal Common fibular vein nerve\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The pulse of the posterior tibial artery is felt behind the lateral malleolus\",\"e\":\"The posterior tibial pulse is sought behind the medial malleolus.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"From lateral to medial, what are the structures in the femoral triangle?\",\"lead\":\"From lateral to medial, what are the structures in the femoral triangle?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Femoral vein, femoral artery, femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The long saphenous vein is also a structure of the femoral triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral artery, femoral nerve, removal vein\",\"e\":\"The long saphenous vein is also a structure of the femoral triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Femoral nerve, femoral artery, femoral vein, long saphenous vein\",\"e\":\"From lateral to medialt, the femoral triangle contains the femoral nerve and its branches, the femoral artery and its branches, including the profunda femoris and the femoral vein with its main tributary the long saphenous vein. Many people, unsurprisingly, get the following mixed up - femoral triangle, femoral sheath, femoral canal and femoral ring. The femoral triangle is a space that lies under Scarpa's fascia. Within this space lies the femoral nerve laterally and the femoral sheath. Within the femoral sheath, lie the femoral artery laterally and the femoral vein medially; medial to the vein lies the femoral canal. The femoral canal is a term used to the space that lies medially to the femoral vein - it is thought that this is space for the femoral vein to expand into during walking (when there is increased venous return). The femoral ring is essentially the superior opening of the femoral canal. The image shows the femoral triangle with (from lateral to medial): the femoral nerve, artery, vein and canal.\\n\\nFemoral nerve Femoral canal\\n\\nGreat saphenous vein Femoral sheath\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Long saphenous vein, femoral vein. femoral artery, femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"While these are the structures of the femoral triangle, they are not ordered from lateral to medial.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Short saphenous vein, femoral vein, femoral artery, femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The short saphenous vein enters the popliteal vein in the popliteal fossa.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A patient is suspected of having compartment syndrome. Before starting the case in theatre the consultant asks you about different muscular compartments of the leg\\n\\nWhich one of the following muscles is found outside of the anterior compartment?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following muscles is found outside of the anterior compartment?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Tibialis anterior\",\"e\":\"The tibialis anterior is found in the anterior compartment of the leg.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Extensor hallucis longus\",\"e\":\"The extensor hallucis longus is found in the anterior compartment of the leg.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Extensor digitorum longus\",\"e\":\"The extensor digitorum longus is found in the anterior compartment of the leg.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Peroneus tertius\",\"e\":\"The peroneus tertius is found in the anterior compartment of the leg.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Peroneus brevis\",\"e\":\"The peroneus brevis is found in the lateral compartment of the leg, along with the peroneus longus. The muscles of the anterior compartment of the leg are shown below in the image. They are:\\n• tibialis anterior\\n• extensor hallucis longus\\n• extensor digitorum longus\\n• peroneus tertius (not always present).\\n\\nThey are all supplied by the deep peroneal nerve and receive their blood supply from the anterior tibial artery.\\n\\nTibialis Anterior\\n\\nExtensor Digitorum Longus\\n\\nExtensor Hallucis Longus\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with paralysis of the quadriceps femoris muscle. The muscle bulk is markedly reduced when compared to the contralateral side. Which one of the following movements will be most likely to be affected in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following movements will be most likely to be affected in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Extension of the leg\",\"e\":\"The quadriceps femoris is the great extensor muscle of the leg, forming a large fleshy mass sides of the femur. It is subdivided into separate portions, which that covers the front and have received distinctive names - separately coloured and shown in the image below. One. occupying the middle of the thigh and connected above with the ilium, is called from its straight course the rectus femoris. The other three lie in immediate connection with the body of the femur, which they cover from the trochanters to the condyles. The portion on the lateral side of the femur is the vastus lateralis; the portion covering the medial side is the vastus medialis; and the portion in front is the vastus intermedius - this cannot be seen in the image below as it is covered by rectus femoris.\\n\\nPsoas major Iliacus Rectus femoris Vastus medialis Vastus lateralis Sartorius Pectineus\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Flexion of the leg\",\"e\":\"The quadriceps femoris flexes the thigh at the hip, and extends the leg at the knee.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Flexion of the thigh W\",\"e\":\"Although the rectus femoris does act to flex the thigh at the hip, the main function of the quadriceps is extension of the leg at the knee. The main flexors of the hip are iliopsoas, sartorius and rectus femoris.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Adduction of the thigh\",\"e\":\"The adductors of the thigh are adductor brevis, adductor longus, adductor magnus. adductor minimis, gracilis, pectineus and obturator externus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Abduction of the thigh\",\"e\":\"The abductors of the thigh are the glutes medius and glutes minimus.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A patient is undergoing knee arthroscopy. The consultant feels the need to place an additional lateral port to enable them to proceed with a lateral meniscectomy. Which one of the following is the correct superficial to deep sequence of structures traversed by the knife on the lateral aspect of the knee joint?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the correct superficial to deep sequence of structures traversed by the knife on the lateral aspect of the knee joint?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Skin, tibial collateral ligament, lateral meniscus\",\"e\":\"This sequence is missing the fibular collateral ligament and poplites muscle tendon.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Skin, fibular collateral ligament. popliteus muscle tendon, lateral meniscus\",\"e\":\"The correct order of superficial to deep structures on the lateral aspect of the knee joint is skin, fibular (lateral) collateral ligament, popliteus muscle tendon and lateral meniscus. The ligaments of the knee are shown below; the poplites muscle tendon runs between the lateral collateral ligament and the lateral meniscus.\\n\\nAnterior cruciate ligament Posterior cruciate ligament\\n\\nMedial Lateral meniscus meniscus\\n\\nMedial collateral Lateral collateral ligament ligament\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Skin, popliteus muscle tendon, tibial collateral ligament, lateral meniscus\",\"e\":\"It is not the tibial, but the fibular collateral ligament that is one of the structures traversed by the knife.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Skin, poplites muscle tendon, fibular collateral ligament, lateral meniscus\",\"e\":\"While these are all the correct structures, they are not in the sequence traversed by the knife.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Skin, anterior cruciate ligament, popliteus muscle tendon\",\"e\":\"The lateral meniscus is also traversed by the knife, while the anterior cruciate ligament is not.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A patient with a lower limb fracture is being discussed in the trauma meeting. The blood supply influences the decision the surgeons make for operative repair. The medial and lateral femoral circumflex arteries are usually direct branches of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The medial and lateral femoral circumflex arteries are usually direct branches of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Obturator artery\",\"e\":\"The obturator artery is a branch of the internal illac artery, and gives off an anterior and posterior branch.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Popliteal artery\",\"e\":\"The popliteal artery is a continuation of the superficial femoral artery as it passes through the adductor magnus hiatus. It has several branches. These are the anterior and posterior tibial arteries, the sural artery and the five genicular arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Profunda femoris artery\",\"e\":\"The profunda femoris artery (deep femoral artery - shown in green below) is a large vessel arising from the lateral and posterior part of the femoral artery, from 2 to 5 cm below the inguinal ligament. At first it lies laterally to the femoral artery. It then runs behind it and the femoral vein to the medial side of the femur, giving off the medial and lateral femoral circumflex arteries. It passes down behind the adductor longus, ending at the lower third of the thigh in a smail branch that pierces the adductor magnus and is distributed on the back of the thigh to the hamstring muscles.\\n\\nMedial circumflex femoral artery\\n\\nLateral circumflex femoral artery Femoral artery Profunda femoris artery\\n\\nAdductor hiatus\\n\\nLAU\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"External liac artery\",\"e\":\"The external iliac artery becomes the common femoral artery just below the inguinal ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"First perforating artery\",\"e\":\"The perforating arteries, of which there are three, are direct branches of the profunda femoris.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A patient has difficulty flexing their knee joint. They undergo further investigations to establish the cause The short head of the biceps femoris muscle is innervated by which one the following?\",\"lead\":\"They undergo further investigations to establish the cause The short head of the biceps femoris muscle is innervated by which one the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The common fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The short head of biceps femoris is innervated by the common fibular (peroneal) nerve. The long head of biceps femoris is innervated by the tibial nerve. Both heads can be seen highlighted in green below, and the common fibular (peroneal) nerve is seen branching from the sciatic at the lower border of the short head (its branching point is variable in the posterior thigh):\\n\\nPiriformis\\n\\nSciatic nerve Long head of the biceps femoris\\n\\nCommon fibular nerve Tibial nerve\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The same nerve as the pectineus muscle\",\"e\":\"The pectineus muscle is innervated by the femoral nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The same nerve as the piriformis muscle\",\"e\":\"The piriformis muscle is innervated by the nerve to piriformis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The superficial fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The superficial fibular nerve is the terminal branch of the common peroneal nerve. innervating the lateral compartment of the leg and the skin over the dorsum of the foot.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The same portion of the sciatic nerve as innervates the semitendinosus muscle\",\"e\":\"The tibial part of the sciatic nerve innervates the semitendinosus muscle.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A patient sustains a severe open tibial fracture. On examination there is a fragment of bone visible coming out of the lateral compartment of the leg. Which of the following is true regarding the innervation to the peroneus brevis muscle?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true regarding the innervation to the peroneus brevis muscle?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Could be damaged by a fracture of the tibia\",\"e\":\"This could be damaged by a fracture of the neck of the fibula, not the tibia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is by a nerve that is a direct branch of the femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The branches of the femoral nerve supply the anterior thigh muscles - the quadriceps group. which extends the leg at the knee, and also supplies other anterior thigh muscles - iliacus and sartorius, which allows flexion of the thigh at the hip joint. The femoral nerve also gives several branches to the skin on the anteromedial side of the lower limb.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is by a nerve that is accompanied by an artery in the same compartment\",\"e\":\"The peroneus brevis is in the lateral compartment of the leg. The arterial supply for this compartment comes from perforating branches of the posterior tibial artery and the peroneal artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is by the same distal nerve that innervates the peroneus tertius muscle\",\"e\":\"The peroneus tertius muscle is innervated by the deep peroneal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Could be damaged by a fracture of the neck of the fibula\",\"e\":\"The peroneus (or fibularis) brevis is one of the two muscles in the lateral compartment of the leg (the other being perones longus) and is supplied by the fourth and fifth lumbar and first sacral nerves through the superficial peroneal nerve. The superficial peroneal nerve is one of the two terminal branches of the common peroneal nerve (the other being the deep peroneal nerve - seen below). The common peroneal nerve winds around the neck of the fibula and can be injured in cases of fracture neck of fibula. Such an injury can result in paralysis or paresis of the peroneus brevis due to indirect involvement of the superficial peroneal nerve.\\n\\nCommon fibular nerve. Tibial Superficial fibular nerve artery\\n\\nCutaneous branches of Deep fibular the superficial fibular nerve nerve\\n\\n© teachmeanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with severe lower limb pain. On X-ray you think there is a tibial tuberosity avulsion fracture present\\n\\nWhich one of the following muscles is attached to the tibial tuberosity?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following muscles is attached to the tibial tuberosity?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Vastus intermedius\",\"e\":\"The tuberosity of the tibia gives attachment to the patellar tendon. The quadriceps femoris muscle (comprising rectus femoris, vasti medialis, intermedius and lateralis) attaches to the tibial tuberosity via the quadriceps tendon proximally, which becomes the patellar tendon distal to the patella (a sesamoid bone witting within the substance of the tendon).\\n\\nQuadriceps femoris tendon Femur\\n• Patella\\n\\nPatella ligament Tibia\\n\\nsanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Tensor fascia lata\",\"e\":\"The tensor fascia lata arises from the outer surface of the anterior iliac crest, and inserts in the iliotibial tract.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Short head of the biceps femoris\",\"e\":\"The short head of biceps femoris arises from the lateral lip of the linea aspera, the lateral supracondylar ridge of femur and the lateral intermuscular septum of the thigh; it inserts onto the fibular head\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Adductor brevis\",\"e\":\"The adductor brevis arises from inferior ramus and body of the pubis, and inserts into the upper third of the linea aspera..:!.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pectineus\",\"e\":\"The pectineus muscle arises from the pectineal line of the pubis, and inserts into the pectineal line of the femur, at its medial surface.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"You are undertaking a fasciotomy of a patient. with confirmed compartment syndrome. The consultant asks you about the different muscular compartments encountered during the procedure. The lateral compartment of the leg, containing the peroneus longus and brevis muscles, is innervated by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The lateral compartment of the leg, containing the peroneus longus and brevis muscles, is innervated by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Tibial nerve\",\"e\":\"The tibial nerve innervates the posterior compartment of the leg.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Common fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The common fibular (peroneal) nerve gives off the superficial fibular nerve, which innervates the lateral compartment. It also gives off the deep fibular nerve, which innervates the anterior compartment.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"superficial fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The superficial fibular (peroneal) nerve supplies the perones longus and brevis muscles. and sensation to the skin over the greater part of the dorsum of the foot, except the first webspace (which is supplied by the deep peroneal nerve). It passes forwards between the peroneal and the extensor digitorum longus, pierces the deep fascia at the lower third of the leg to become superficial and divides into medial and intermediate dorsal cutaneous nerves. In its course between the muscles, the nerve gives off muscular branches to the peroneus longus and brevis and cutaneous filaments to the skin of the lower part of the leg.\\n\\nCommon fibular nerve Tibial Superficial fibular nerve artery\\n\\nCutaneous branches of Deep fibular the superficial fibular nerve nerve\\n\\n© teachmeanatomy MRAAAAL: L84IF\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Deep fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The deep fibular (peroneal) nerve innervates the anterior compartment of the leg.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sural nerve\",\"e\":\"This is a sensory nerve only to the lateral foot and ankle.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"A footballer fell awkwardly because of a rash challenge. He sustained a blow to his left knee and was stretchered off the playing field. On examination of his injured knee the physiotherapist found excessive posterior movement of the tibia on the femur:\\n\\nThe chief ligament preventing posterior sliding of the tibia on the femur is which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The chief ligament preventing posterior sliding of the tibia on the femur is which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Medial collateral\",\"e\":\"The medial collateral ligament resists a valgus force pushing the knee medially.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lateral collateral\",\"e\":\"The lateral collateral ligament resists a varus force pushing the knee laterally.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oblique popliteal\",\"e\":\"The oblique popliteal ligament stabilises the posterior aspect of the knee joint and limits lateral rotation of the tibia and hyperextension.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior cruciate\",\"e\":\"The posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) is stronger, but shorter and less oblique in its direction, than the anterior. It is shown in yellow in the image below. It is attached to the posterior intercondyloid fossa of the tibia and to the posterior extremity of the lateral meniscus and passes upward, forwards and medialward, to be fixed into the lateral and front part of the medial condyle of the Femur. This configuration allows the PC to resist forces pushing the tibia posteriorly relative to the femur.\\n\\nAnterior cruciate ligament Posterior cruciate ligament\\n\\nMedial Lateral meniscus meniscus\\n\\nMedial collateral Lateral collateral ligament ligament\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Anterior cruciate\",\"e\":\"The anterior cruciate ligament resists anterior movement of the tibia, in relation to the femur.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"The adductor muscle group of the medial compartment of the thigh are generally innervated by the obturator nerve Which of these muscles is not routinely supplied by this nerve?\",\"lead\":\"The adductor muscle group of the medial compartment of the thigh are generally innervated by the obturator nerve Which of these muscles is not routinely supplied by this nerve?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adductor brevis\",\"e\":\"Adductor brevis is innervated by the obturator nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Adductor longus\",\"e\":\"Adductor longus is innervated by the obturator nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Adductor magnus\",\"e\":\"Adductor magnus is innervated by the obturator nerve\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gracilis:\",\"e\":\"Gracilis is innervated by the obturator nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pectineus\",\"e\":\"The medial (adductor) compartment of the thigh contains adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor portion of adductor magnus, gracilis. pectineus and obturator externus. They are all supplied by the obturator nerve except the pectineus, which is supplied by the femoral nerve, although it sometimes receives additional innervation from the obturator nerve. Adductor magnus has dual innervation: it is mostly supplied by the obturator nerve but the vertical / hamstring portion receives innervation from the tibial nerve.\\n\\nObturator externus\\n\\nAdductor brevis\\n\\n_Adductor longus\\n\\nAdductor magnus\\n\\ncoocismoanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old football player sustains an anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) tear and requires surgery to reconstruct it. The surgeon chooses to use a minimally invasive approach to repair the ligament.\\n\\nWhich one of the following tendons is typically used to achieve an ACL reconstruction?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following tendons is typically used to achieve an ACL reconstruction?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The biceps femoris\",\"e\":\"It is the semitendinosus tendon from the hamstrings that is typically used for a graft, in combination with gracilis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The politeus as it is easy to access\",\"e\":\"It is the semitendinosus (with gracilis) or patella tendon that is used for ACL reconstruction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The rectus femoris as it has a big tendon\",\"e\":\"The patella tendon is often used as an ACL graft, as is semitendinosus with gracilis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The sartorius as it could be easily identified at the insertion near the tibial tuberosity\",\"e\":\"It is the semitendinosus and gracilis that are harvested from near the tibial tuberosity. Access to the tendons is through the sartorial fascia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The semitendinosus because it has a high load to failure of 4000 Newtons\",\"e\":\"The natural load to failure of the ACL is about 2000 Newtons. Grafts for ACL reconstruction can be categorised as autograft (patient's own tissue) or allograft (from cadavers). Autografts use either the hamstring (semitendinosus, along with gracilis) or patella tendon. The ACL is shown below in blue.\\n\\nAnterior cruciate ligament Posterior cruciate ligament\\n\\nMedial Lateral meniscus meniscus\\n\\nMedial collateral Lateral collateral ligament ligament\\n\\nSeries\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man was referred with left foot pain after falling from his bike; he has since developed flat foot deformity. Which ligament is likely to have been disrupted in his injury?\",\"lead\":\"Which ligament is likely to have been disrupted in his injury?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The anterior talofibular ligament\",\"e\":\"The anterior talofibular ligament is a lateral ligament of the ankle and prevents the foot from sliding forwards in relation to the leg. it is typically injured in inversion injuries.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament\",\"e\":\"The calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament - labelled in the image of the foot seen from below - connects the plantar surface of the navicular bone to the sustentaculum of the calcaneus. It also gives support to the head of the talus, supports a significant amount of body weight, and maintains the medial longitudinal arch of the foot. Injury to this can lead to a flat foot deformity.\\n\\nTibialis anterior\\n\\nFibularis longus Short plantar Plantar ligament calcaneonavic. ligament Long plantar ligament Tibialis posterior\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The capsular ligaments in front of and behind the ankle joint, which are weak\",\"e\":\"These ligaments are commonly injured, but they would not give the flat foot deformity described. Injury to the spring (calcaneonavicular) ligament would cause the acquired flat foot deformity described\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The deltoid ligament which is attached to the medial malleolus\",\"e\":\"Injury to the deltoid ligament requires high energy and is often injured in association with lateral malleolus fractures causing translation of the talus, not a flat foot deformity.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The syndesmotic ligament complex\",\"e\":\"The ankle syndesmosis is a group of ligaments connecting the distal tibia and fibula. Disruption causes widening of the distal tibial-fibular joint, which would cause instability at the ankle, not a flat foot deformity.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old carpenter presented with swelling and pain anterior to his knee. He is unable to work as a result of the discomfort.\\n\\nWhich knee bursa is likely to be involved?\",\"lead\":\"Which knee bursa is likely to be involved?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The deep infrapatellar bursa!\",\"e\":\"This bursa is between the tibia and patellar tendon. It is the prepatellar bursa that is affected in this scenario.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The popliteal bursa protecting the patellar tendon\",\"e\":\"The popliteal bursa is found behind the knee, not in front, between the poplites tendon and the posterior aspect of the tibia and fibula.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The prepatellar bursa protecting the patella\",\"e\":\"The prepatellar bursa (labelled in green) lies between the patella and the overlying skin. This patient has prepatellar bursitis - sometimes known as 'housemaid's knee'. This is caused by recurrent, minor injury to the kneecap, eg where the patient spends a long time kneeling forwards and putting pressure on their patella. There are four anterior bursae, four posterior bursae, two medially and two laterally - key ones are labelled in the image. They are fluid-filled sacs which reduce friction over bony prominences, and can be either communicating or non-communicating with the knee joint.\\n\\nSuprapatella bursa Prepatella bursa Infrapatella bursae Semimembranosus bursa\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The superficial intrapatellar bursa, which les between the patellar tendon and skın\",\"e\":\"Inflammation of the infrapatellar bursa, found between the skin and tibial tuberosity, is often known as 'Clergyman's knee'. The anterior knee pain in the scenario is most likely to be due to prepatellar bursitis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The suprapatellar bursa which communicates with the knee joint\",\"e\":\"This is found between the femur and quadriceps muscle, communicating with the synovial joint. It is the prepatellar bursa which is affected in this scenario.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"Joints in the body can be described by their function. Each is specialised to optimise its function. Which one of the lower limb joints is a modified synovial hinge joint?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the lower limb joints is a modified synovial hinge joint?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The calcaneocuboid joint\\n• C\",\"e\":\"The calcaneocuboid joint is one of the least mobile in the foot and is not a hinge joint. It has strong ligamentous connections.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The hip joint\",\"e\":\"The hip joint is a ball and socket synovial joint.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The inferior tibiofibular joint\",\"e\":\"The inferior tibiofibular joint is a fibrous joint ie fixed, and held together by fibrous connective tissue, known as the syndesmosis There is no synovial cavity. Other examples of fibrous joints are sutures between cranial bones, and articulations between the teeth and the mouth.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The intermetatarsal joints\",\"e\":\"The intermetatarsal joints between the proximal ends of the metatarsals allow gliding, not flexion/extension.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The knee\",\"e\":\"The knee is a modified synovial hinge joint. The presence of a cavity and synovial fluid makes the joint freely movable. The 'hinge' describes the flexion-extension movements, however the knee also permits rotation and slight varus/valgus movements. It is therefore typically described as a modified hinge joint. There are many different ways of classifying joints in the body. The easiest way is to consider the structure of joints (fibrous, cartilaginous and synovial), and the function of the joints (immovable, slightly movable, and freely movable).\\n\\nAnterior cruciate ligament Posterior cruciate ligament\\n\\nMedial Lateral meniscus meniscus\\n\\nMedial collateral Lateral collateral ligament ligament\\n• Seiirs\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old woman is seen in the orthopaedic outpatients department. She is a keen jogger and has noticed numbness in the first. second and third toes as well as a dull ache. Which nerve is most likely to have been affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve is most likely to have been affected?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The deep peroneal nerve\",\"e\":\"Injury to the deep peroneal nerve usually manifests itself as foot drop and numbness in the first webspace.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh\",\"e\":\"The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh innervates the skin of the lateral aspect of the thigh and would not cause the symptoms described.\\n\\nThe lateral plantar nerve provides cutaneous sensation to the plantar aspect of the 5th and lateral aspect of the 4th toe.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The lateral plantar nerve\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The medial plantar nerve\",\"e\":\"The medial plantar nerve is a branch of the posterior tibial nerve (L4-S3; a branch of the sciatic nerve) and supplies sensation to the medial three and a half toes on the plantar surfaces and the dorsal surfaces to the nail beds - it is highlighted in pink below in the image of the sole of the foot. Compression of the tibial nerve as it travels through the tarsa tunnel can give rise to, among others, the symptoms described\\n\\nMedial plantar nerve Lateral plantar (branch of the ridici nerve) nerve Pravich if the slist\\n\\nSaphenous nerve french of the femoral nerves Sural nerve (branches of the common (buter cou ablalnerves) Medial calcanel branches (teamches) the thint cart)\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The sural nerve\",\"e\":\"This supplies sensation to the lateral aspect of the foot and the posterior lateral aspect of the leg.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old woman presents 2 weeks after sustaining a knee injury while landing from a jump in a game of basketball. There was an audible popping sound at the time of injury and she developed swelling straight away. On physical examination, she has a positive Lachman's test.\\n\\nWhat is the origin and insertion of the damaged ligament?\",\"lead\":\"What is the origin and insertion of the damaged ligament?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"From the medial wall of the lateral femoral condyle to the intercondylar eminence of the tibia\",\"e\":\"This is a classical presentation of anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) injury - a pop' after deceleration followed by immediate swelling. The Lachman's test is used for examination of the ACL when there is suspicion of a torn ACL. The ACL (highlighted in blue) is attached to the anteromedial intercondylar area of the tib a and passes upwards, backwards and laterally to the medial surface of the lateral femoral condyle, limiting anterior movement of the tibia on the femur\\n\\nAnterior cruciate ligament Posterior cruciate ligament\\n\\nMedial Lateral meniscus moniscus\\n\\nMedial collateral Lateral collateral ligament ligament\\n\\n:. Soiles\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"From the medial femoral condyle to the intercondylar eminence of the tibia\",\"e\":\"This describes the origin and insertion of the posterior cruciate ligament - attached to the posterolateral intercondylar area of the tibia and passes upwards, forwards and med ally to the lateral surface of the medial femoral condyle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"From lateral femoral condyle to fibula head\",\"e\":\"This describes the origin and insertion of the lateral (fibula) collateral ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"From medial femoral epicondyle to medial meniscus\",\"e\":\"This describes the medial (tibial) collateral ligament, which inserts into the medial condyle of the tibia, but is also adherent to the medial meniscus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"From medial femoral epicondyle to proximal tibia\",\"e\":\"This describes the medial (tibial) collateral ligament, which inserts into the medial condyle of the tibia, but is also adherent to the medial meniscus.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"An 87-year-old woman presents with severe right hip pain following a fall. She is diagnosed with a fractured neck of femur and undergoes hemiarthroplasty. Which one of the following arteries directly supply the trochanteric anastomosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following arteries directly supply the trochanteric anastomosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior spinal artery\",\"e\":\"Arising from the vertebral arteries, the anterior spinal artery supplies the anterior spinal cord\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"External iac artery\",\"e\":\"The external iliac artery progresses beyond the inguinal ligament as the femoral artery to supply the lower limb.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gluteal arteries\",\"e\":\"The superior and inferior gluteal arteries originate from the internal iliac artery. Their deep branches form the trochanteric anastomosis with the medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries (which arise from the profundal femoris), to supply the femoral head\\n\\nSuperior gluteal artery Inferior gluteal artery\\n\\nPerforating branches of the profunda femoris\\n\\nAdductor hiatus\\n\\nPopliteal artery\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"liolumbar arteries\",\"e\":\"The iliolumbar arteries, from the posterior division of the internal iliac artery, divide into lumbar and iliac branches, and supply the pelvic and spinal muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior spinal arteries\",\"e\":\"The posterior spinal artery supplies the spinal cord.It usually arises from the posterior inferior cerebellar artery. but may arise from the vertebral arteries in 25% of cases.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"The femoral triangle in the thigh contains some important structures. What are the contents of the femoral triangle?\",\"lead\":\"What are the contents of the femoral triangle?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The femoral canal, which lies lateral to the femoral vein\",\"e\":\"The femoral canal is medial to the femoral vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The femoral canal, which is the most lateral structure in the femoral sheath\",\"e\":\"The femoral canal is in the most medial portion of the femoral sheath.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The femoral sheath, which contains the femoral vessels\",\"e\":\"The femoral sheath is a downward continuation of the abdominal fascia (transversalis and iliacus) about 2.5 cm below the inguinal ligament. The sheath lies medially to the femoral nerve. Within the sheath itself, the structures are - from lateral to medial - artery. vein, 'empty space', lymphatics. The 'empty space' and lymphatics are better known as the femoral canal, and this space is for the femoral vein to expand into during periods of increased venous return, eg walking. The femoral canal is where femoral hernias can develop, and commoniy strangulate due to the sharp lacunar ligament on the medial aspect of the canal opening\\n\\nFemoral nerve Femoral canal\\n\\nGreat saphenous vein Femoral sheath\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The short saphenous vein\",\"e\":\"The short saphenous vein is a superficial vein that drains the lateral and posterior lower leg. It drains into the popliteal vein behind the knee.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The pubic branch of the inferior epigastric vein\",\"e\":\"The pubic branch of the inferior epigastric artery runs along the inguinal ligament, along the medial margin of the femoral ring, and forms an anastomosis with the obturator artery via its pubic branch.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"You are examining the anatomy of the back of the knee; the popliteal fossa.\\n\\nWhat are the contents of the popliteal fossa?\",\"lead\":\"What are the contents of the popliteal fossa?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The deep peroneal nerve\",\"e\":\"The deep peroneal nerve is not in the popliteal fossa. It originates from the common peroneal nerve, which does lie in the popliteal fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The deepest structure is the popliteal vein\",\"e\":\"The deepest structure in the popliteal fossa is the popliteal artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The popliteal artery and vein and the tibial nerve\",\"e\":\"The roof of the popliteal fossa is formed by the fascia lata. Within the apex of the fossa the sciatic nerve divides into common peroneal and tibial branches (but may divide higher). The deepest structure is the popliteal artery.\\n\\nPopliteal artery Tibial nerve\\n\\nPopliteal Common fibular nerve vein\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The saphenous nerve\",\"e\":\"The saphenous rerve originates from the femoral nerve. It passes medially to the knee. It has sensory functions only\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The sciatic nerve\",\"e\":\"The sciatic nerve divides at the apex of the popliteal fossa into common peroneal and tibial branches.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"In the anatomy of the pelvis, several landmarks have important clinical relevance. Which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The femoral canal lies medial to the lacunar ligament\",\"e\":\"The lacunar ligament forms the medial wall of the femoral canal, so the canal as a whole lies laterally to this ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ischial spines are palpable per vaginam\",\"e\":\"The ischial spines are palpable per vaginam, and allow the guidance of a needle to the pudendal canal for transvaginal pudendal nerve block.\\n\\nL llium Ischium Pubis Greater sciatic Ischial notch spine\\n\\nAcetabulum\\n\\nObturator foramen ischial tuberosity\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pubic tubercies are palpable laterally to the external ring of the inguinal canal\",\"e\":\"The pubic tubercles are medial to the external (superficial) inguinal ring\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sacral cornua are palpable per rectum\",\"e\":\"In the natal cleft, the sacral cornua are important surface markings for the sacral hiatus, through which a needle is passed for caudal epidural anaesthesia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transtubercular plane passes through the spinous process of the L3 vertebra\",\"e\":\"The transtubercular plane is an important landmark for lumbar puncture and transects the L5 vertebra (the supracristal plane, which passes through the L4 lumbar spine, can also be used).\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man sustains a laceration to his foot just anteriorly to the medial malleolus. He is haemodynamically unstable but improves with resuscitation. Which clinical structure is likely to be damaged in this injury?\",\"lead\":\"Which clinical structure is likely to be damaged in this injury?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Sural nerve\",\"e\":\"The sural nerve is found on the lateral aspect of the ankle, behind the lateral malleolus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Posterior tibial artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior tibial artery is found behind the medial malleolus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The dorsalis pedis artery\",\"e\":\"The dorsalis pedis artery arises from the anterior tibial artery over the anterior aspect of the ankle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The extensor digitorum, which is medial to the tibialis anterior\",\"e\":\"The extensor digitorum longus is found laterally to the tibialis anterior.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The great saphenous vein\",\"e\":\"The great saphenous vein is found anterior to the medial malleolus. This is in contrast to the small saphenous vein which is posterior to the lateral malleolus - shown below.\\n\\nFemoral Vein Popliteal Vein\\n\\nGreat Saphenous Vein\\n\\nSmall Saphenous Vein\\n\\nC teachmeanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with small bowel obstruction. A non-reducible lump is found in their left groin. When operating on a femoral hernia in the femoral triangle, care should be taken to avoid damage to which structure?\",\"lead\":\"When operating on a femoral hernia in the femoral triangle, care should be taken to avoid damage to which structure?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve lies within the inguinal canal, not the femoral triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The cribriform fascia, which transmits the superficial branches of the femoral artery\",\"e\":\"The cribriform fascia overs the saphenous opening and is pierced by the saphenous vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The deep inguinal nodes, which lie laterally to the femoral vein\",\"e\":\"These nodes lie medially to the femoral vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The femoral vein, which lies medially to the femoral artery and nerve\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve, artery and vein lie in that order from lateral to medial. A femoral hernia will be in the femoral canal, which is medial to the vein.\\n\\nFemoral nerve Femoral canal\\n\\nGreat saphenous vein Femoral sheath\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The profunda femoris artery, which lies medially to the superficial femoral artery\",\"e\":\"The profunda femoris artery lies deep and more laterally to the superficial femoral artery\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"The tibial nerve is a branch of the sciatic nerve that passes through the popliteal fossa. A patient has presented with parasthesia in their leg What is the main cutaneous branch of the tibial nerve in the popliteal fossa?\",\"lead\":\"A patient has presented with parasthesia in their leg What is the main cutaneous branch of the tibial nerve in the popliteal fossa?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Articular branch to the knee joint\",\"e\":\"Articular branches are not cutaneous; they supply the knee joint.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Medial sural cutaneous nerve\",\"e\":\"The tibial nerve is the large terminal branch of the sciatic nerve. It arises at the apex of the popliteal fossa, giving off a small cutaneous branch - the medial sural cutaneous nerve. This Te is a purely sensory nerve. The sural nerve is formed by this branch from the tibial and te: another from the common fibular nerve - its distribution is seen below in red\\n\\nSensory Innervation of the Sciatic Nerve\\n\\nSural nerve (formed by branches of the common fibular and tibial nerves) Deop fibular nerve (a branch of the common fibular nerve) Superficial fibular nerve (a branch of the common fibular nerve) Medial calcaneal branches (branches of the tibial nerve)\\n\\n© cheesnatory.....\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Common peroneal branch\",\"e\":\"The common peroneal nerve originates from the sciatic nerve. It gives off the lateral sural cutaneous nerve, which is a sensory nerve to the lateral leg below the knee.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Medial plantar nerve\",\"e\":\"The medial plantar nerve is a terminal branch of the tibial nerve in the foot that innervates abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis and flexor hallucis brevis. Sensory supply is to the sole of the foot on the medial side and to the medial 3½ toes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior femoral cutaneous nerve\",\"e\":\"The posterior femoral cutaneous nerve originates from the sacral plexus. It innervates the skin to the posterior thigh, leg and perineum.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"The tibial nerve is a peripheral branch of the sacral plexus in the leg. Which muscle gets its motor supply from the tibial nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscle gets its motor supply from the tibial nerve?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Extensor digitorum longus\",\"e\":\"Extensor digitorum longus is innervated by the deep fibular (peroneal) nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Extensor hallucis longus\",\"e\":\"Extensor hallucis longus is innervated by the deep fibular (peroneal) nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastrocnemius\",\"e\":\"Gastrocnemius is innervated by the tibial nerve. The tibial nerve is shown in the image below travelling in the popliteal fossa, most superficially (followed by the popliteal vein then popliteal artery as you move deeper). It supplies all the muscles in the posterior compartment of the leg.\\n\\nSciatic nerve\\n\\nCommon fibular nerve\\n\\nTibial nerve\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Peroneus longus\",\"e\":\"Perones longus is innervated by the superficial fibular (peroneal) nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tibialis anterior\",\"e\":\"Tibialis anterior is innervated the deep fibular (peroneal) nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with numbness in the first, second and third toes and dorsum of foot There is no numbness in the first dorsal webspace.\\n\\nThe nerves contributing to the numbness include which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The nerves contributing to the numbness include which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The deep peroneal nerve\",\"e\":\"The deep peroneal nerve has a medial cutaneous branch that innervates the webspace between the 1st and 2nd toe.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The lateral plantar nerve\",\"e\":\"The lateral plantar nerve provides sensation to the lateral half of the 4th toe and the entire 5th toe\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The superficial peroneal nerve\",\"e\":\"The cutaneous sensory innervation of the superficial peroneal nerve includes the anterolateral lower leg and the dorsum of the foot (except the ist webspace, which is innervated by the deep peroneal nerve). The deep peroneal (fibular) nerve distribution is shown in yellow below, and the superficial peroneal nerve in green.\\n\\n* Saphenous (from femoral) Eil Lateral sural (from common fibufor)\\n• Sural (from common fibular and tibial)\\n\\nSuperficial fibular\\n\\nDeep fibular\\n\\nMedial calcaneal (from tibial)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The sural nerve\",\"e\":\"The sural nerve provides sensation to the lateral dorsum of the foot and ankle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The tibial nerve\",\"e\":\"The tibial nerve gives rise to the sural nerve, which provides sensation to the lateral dorsum of the foot and ankle. 436\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"The adductor (Hunter) canal runs behind the sartorius muscle and is in danger during anteromedial approach to the femur\\n\\nWhat passes through this canal?\",\"lead\":\"What passes through this canal?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The deep femoral artery\\n• C\",\"e\":\"The deep femoral artery, also known as profunda femoris, is a branch of the common femoral artery, which is found in the femoral sheath.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve does not pass through the adductor canal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The nerve that supplies tensor fascia lata\",\"e\":\"The nerve to the tensor fascia lata is the superior gluteal nerve (L4-51). which passes through the greater sciatic foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The nerve to the vastus lateralis\",\"e\":\"This muscle is innervated by the femoral nerve, which does not pass through the adductor canal.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The saphenous nerve\",\"e\":\"The adductor canal (also known as the subsartorial or Hunter's canal) is a 15-cm long tunnel bounded by vastus medialis laterally, and adductor longus and magnus posteriorly. The canal serves as a passage for the superficial femoral artery, femoral vein, saphenous nerve and the nerve to vastus medialis to pass into the popliteal fossa - these structures (excepting the nerve to vastus medialis) can be seen in the canal in the image. The adductor canal begins where the sartorius crosses over the adductor longus and ends at the adductor hiatus in the adductor magnus muscle.\\n\\nSartorius\\n\\n_ Adductor Vastus canal medialis\\n\\nAdductor longus\\n\\nC)\\\"\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":92,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old patient presents with pes planus. They are struggling to take part in physical activity and want consideration of surgical intervention. Which tendon, lying immediately posterior to the medial malleolus, may be responsible for this presentation?\",\"lead\":\"Which tendon, lying immediately posterior to the medial malleolus, may be responsible for this presentation?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Flexor Digitorum Longus\",\"e\":\"Injury to flexor digitorum longus typical presents with difficulty walking and pain in the affected foot and ankle. It is classically caused by the patient tripping and falling. Flexor digitorum longus is responsible for the movement of toes 2-5, as well as plantar flexion of the foot.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Flexor Hallucis Longus\",\"e\":\"Injury or pathology to flexor hallucis longus causes pain the affected foot. It is classically seen in ballet dancers, because of their need to repetitively plantar flex the feet, and of course, is so also seen in sportsmen or other dancers who need to do this too\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastrocnemius\",\"e\":\"Gastrocnemius is recruited when the human body needs to undertake explosive movements eg jumping, running. In layman's terms, injury to it is known as a 'calf strain'. There is often tenderness to the medial belly\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Soleus\",\"e\":\"Injury to soleus is known as a 'calf strain' in layman's terms. There is tenderness to the lateral belly on palpation\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tibialis posterior\",\"e\":\"The lateral malleolus is grooved by the peroneus brevis tendon; the medial malleolus by the tibialis posterior tendon. The tibialis posterior's main function is to support the medial arch and the foot when walking. The tibialis posterior tendon is shown in blue in the image below of the foot from below - it can be seen dividing in to two at the level of the plantar calcaneonavicular ligament, with the superficial slip inserting on to the navicular and the deeper slip inserting on to the 2nd - 4* metatarsals.\\n\\nTibialis anterior\\n\\nFibularis longus Short plantar Plantar ligament calcaneonavic. ligament Long plantar ligament Tibialis posterior\\n\\nkailas.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":95,\"stem\":\"Entrapment syndrome could affect the structures in the popliteal fossa causing calf cramps and toes tingling. Which of the following is the deepest structure in the popliteal fossa?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the deepest structure in the popliteal fossa?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Genicular branch of the obturator nerve\",\"e\":\"The obturator nerve and its branches do not pass through or lie in the popliteal fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Long saphenous vein\",\"e\":\"The long saphenous vein does not lie in the popliteal fossa. The short saphenous vein dues.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"posterior tibial artery 5»\",\"e\":\"The posterior tibial artery does not lie in the popliteal fossa.\\n\\nThe tibial nerve lies in the popliteal fossa, but is not the deepest structure\\n\\nThe popliteal fossa is a diamond-shaped space at the back of the knee. The superomedial border is formed by the semitendinous and semimembranosus muscles and the superolateral border by biceps femoris muscle. The inferior borders are formed by the medial and lateral heads of gastrocnemius. The contents (from deep to superficial) include the popliteal artery, popliteal vein, tibial nerve, common peroneal nerve and small saphenous vein. There are also lymph nodes and connective tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Popliteal artery\",\"e\":\"The contents (from deep to superficial) include the popliteal artery, popliteal vein, tibial nerve, common peroneal nerve, small saphenous vein and lymph nodes.\\n\\nPopliteal artery Tibial nerve\\n\\nPopliteal Common fibular vein nerve\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tibial nerve\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"The ilioinguinal approach is indicated to internally fix acetabular fractures. This approach risks injury to the femoral sheath. What is the most medial feature of the femoral sheath?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most medial feature of the femoral sheath?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Femoral artery\",\"e\":\"The femoral artery lies within the sheath, and is lateral to the femoral vein and the femoral canal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral canal\",\"e\":\"The femoral canal is the most medial feature in the sheath (which contains the femoral vein, artery and the femoral canal - the nerve is outside of the sheath). It is sometimes referred to as the 'empty space' - this is because it lies medial to the femoral vein, and this space is where the vein expands into during times of increased venous return.\\n\\nFemoral nerve Femoral canal\\n\\nGreat saphenous vein Femoral sheath\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve is not in the sheath, and lies lateral to it.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Femoral vein\",\"e\":\"The femoral vein is not the most medial structure in the sheath\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lacunar ligament\",\"e\":\"The lacunar ligament lies medial to the sheath itself, it is not in the sheath. The femoral sheath is a thickening derived from the transversalis and iliac fascia. It encloses the femoral artery, femoral vein and femoral canal. Lymphatic vessels run in the femoral canal. The femoral nerve lies laterally to the sheath.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":101,\"stem\":\"A patient presented with a Trendelenburg gait compensating for weakness in her hip abductors. Which of the following muscles abduct the hip?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following muscles abduct the hip?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Biceps femoris\",\"e\":\"Biceps femoris is a knee flexor and hip extensor.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gluteus maximus\",\"e\":\"Gluteus maximus extends the thigh at the hip joint.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gluteus medius\",\"e\":\"Both gluteus medius and gluteus minimus abduct the thigh at the hip joint. They are both innervated by the superior gluteal nerve, which if injured leads to inability to abduct the hip on the affected side, manifesting as the pelvis sagging when standing on the affected limb - remember, the 'sound side sags'.\\n\\nGluteus medius Gluteus maximus\\n\\nGluteus minimus\\n\\neanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Priors\",\"e\":\"Piriformis contributes to external rotation of the thigh.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Quadratus femoris\",\"e\":\"Quadratus femoris contributes to external rotation of the thigh\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":104,\"stem\":\"The obturator nerve enters the thigh through the obturator canal and supplies sensation to medial side of the thigh. The nerve can become impinged in the rare event of an obturator hernia. Which of these muscles are commonly innervated by the obturator nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which of these muscles are commonly innervated by the obturator nerve?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gracilis\",\"e\":\"The obturator nerve (L2, L3. L4) exits the obturator foramen and divides into anterior and posterior branches. The anterior branch innervates adductor brevis, adductor longus, and gracilis - shown in the image below. The posterior branch innervates obturator externus and part of adductor magnus.\\n\\nFemoral nerve Femoral artery\\n\\nAdductor longus (cut)\\n\\n- Adductor brevis Obturator nerve (anterior division) Gracilis\\n\\nteachmeanatomy C\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Obturator internus\",\"e\":\"Obturator internus is innervated by the nerve to obturator internus, which also supplies the superior gemellus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pectineus\",\"e\":\"Pectineus is innervated by the femoral nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Semimembranosus\",\"e\":\"Semimembranosus is one of the hamstring muscles and is innervated by the tibial portion of the sciatic nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vastus medialis\",\"e\":\"This muscie is innervated by the femoral nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"Club foot deformity (congenital talipes equino varus) is a common birth defect. You are seeing a patient with this suspected abnormality Which of the following is responsible for the hind foot equinus deformity?\",\"lead\":\"You are seeing a patient with this suspected abnormality Which of the following is responsible for the hind foot equinus deformity?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Achilles tendon\",\"e\":\"The ankles are rotated internally in club foot deformity. Much of surgery concerns either tenotomy or lengthening of the Achilles tendon.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Peroneus brevis\",\"e\":\"Perones brevis is in the lateral compartment of the leg. It plantarflexes and everts the foot.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"peroneus tertius fa\",\"e\":\"Peroneus tertius dorsiflexes and everts the foot.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tibialis anterior\",\"e\":\"Tibialis anterior dorsiflexes and inverts the foot.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tibialis posterior\",\"e\":\"Tibialis posterior plantarflexes and inverts the foot.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":109,\"stem\":\"The adductor (subsartorius) canal lies in the middle third of the thigh. Which structure runs through the adductor canal?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure runs through the adductor canal?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Common peroneal nerve\",\"e\":\"The common peroneal nerve does not pass through the adductor canal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve does not pass through the adductor canal\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"AAI Femoral vein\",\"e\":\"The adductor canal (Hunter's or subsartorial canal) extends from the apex of the femoral triangle to the opening in adductor magnus. It contains the femoral artery and vein, saphenous nerve and the nerve to vastus medialis.\\n\\nSartorius\\n\\nAdductor Vastus canal medialis\\n\\nAdductor longus\\n\\nMAy Anatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Nerve to vastus intermedius\",\"e\":\"Vastus intermedius is innervated by the femoral nerve; this does not pass through the adductor canal.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Profunda femoris artery\",\"e\":\"The profunda femoris, or the deep femoral artery, arises from the common femoral artery, but orig nates before the adductor canal.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":112,\"stem\":\"The greater trochanter of the femur is a bony prominence of the proximal femur that serves as insertional muscle attachment. Which muscle is attached to the greater trochanter of the femur?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscle is attached to the greater trochanter of the femur?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gluteus maximus\",\"e\":\"Gluteus maximus attaches to the gluteal tuberosity of the femur.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Piriformis\",\"e\":\"Piriformis, gluteus medius. gluteus minimus, obturator internus, obturator externus, vastus lateralis and the gemelli are all attached to the greater trochanter of the femur. The osteology of the proximal femur is shown below from anterior and posterior.\\n\\nNeck Head Greater trochanter ASTEST Lesser Intertrochanteric trochanter line\\n\\nNeck Greater trochanter Head\\n\\nTrochanteric crest Gluteal tuberosity Lesser trochanter\\n\\nPectineal line Linea aspera\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Psoas\",\"e\":\"Psoas inserts on the lesser trochanter.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vastus medialis\",\"e\":\"Vastus medialis inserts into the medial aspects of the quadriceps tendon and the patella tendon.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sartorius\",\"e\":\"Sartorius inserts into the anteromedial surface of the tibia.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":115,\"stem\":\"The linea aspera is the bony ridge on the posterior aspect of the femur. It accommodates both origins and insertions of various muscles. Which muscles are attached to the linea aspera?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscles are attached to the linea aspera?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adductor longus and brevis\",\"e\":\"The linea aspera is a ridge running down the posterior aspect of the femur - highlighted in the image below showing the proximal femur from the posterior aspect It forms a point of attachment for the following muscles: vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, adductor magnus, adductor longus, adductor brevis and the short head of biceps femoris\\n\\nGreater trochanter Nock Head\\n\\nTrochanteric crest Gluteal tuberosity Lesser trochanter\\n\\nPectineal line Linea aspera\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Long and short heads of biceps femoris\",\"e\":\"While the short head originates from the linea aspera, the long head of biceps femoris originates from the ischial tuberosity.\\n\\nAdductor magnus inserts into the linea aspera, but pectines inserts into the pectineal line of the femur.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pectineus and adductor magnus\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Rectus femoris and vastus intermedius\",\"e\":\"Rectus femoris inserts and vastus intermedius insert into the quadriceps tendon.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vastus lateralis and vastus intermedius\",\"e\":\"Although the vastus lateralis attaches to the linea aspera, the vastus intermedius does not.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":118,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old footballer presents with severe pain and swelling of the left ankle. Plain radiographs confirm a bimalleolar ankle fracture The distal tibia articulates with which bone?\",\"lead\":\"Plain radiographs confirm a bimalleolar ankle fracture The distal tibia articulates with which bone?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cuboid.C-\",\"e\":\"The cuboid bone is a tarsal bone. It forms part of the midfoot.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Medial cuneiform\",\"e\":\"This bone is the largest of the cuneiform bones and forms part of the midfoot.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Navicular\",\"e\":\"The navicular bone is a small tarsal bone that forms part of the midfoot.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Os calcis\",\"e\":\"The os calcis is also known as the calcaneus. It is the largest of the tarsal bones and forms part of the midfoot.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Talus\",\"e\":\"The ankle joint is formed from the distal tibia and fibula, articulating with the talus - as shown below.\\n\\nASTE Tibia\\n\\nFibula\\n\\nTalus\\n\\nCalcaneus\\n\\nteam arteries\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":121,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-cld motorcyclist is involved in a road traffic accident and sustains a fracture of the femoral shaft Which prominent ridge runs along the posterior aspect of the femoral shaft?\",\"lead\":\"A 23-year-cld motorcyclist is involved in a road traffic accident and sustains a fracture of the femoral shaft Which prominent ridge runs along the posterior aspect of the femoral shaft?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Intertrochanteric ridge\",\"e\":\"The intertrochanteric ridge is positioned at the proximal anterior femur.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Linea alba\",\"e\":\"The linea alba is the tendinous, white line that runs down the midline of the abdomen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Linea albuginea\",\"e\":\"The tunice albuginea of the penis is a fibrous membrane of the penis that surrounds the corpora cavernosa. It has a role in maintaining penile erection. The tunica albuginea of the testis is a white fibrous covering of the testis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Linea aspera\",\"e\":\"The linea aspera runs along the posterior aspect of the femoral shaft - it is shown in the image below of the proximal femur from the posterior aspect. It is the point of attachment for many muscles of the lower limb, and for the lateral and medial intermuscular septums.\\n\\nNeck Greater trochanter Head\\n\\nTrochanteric crest Gluteal tuberosity Lesser trochanter\\n\\nPectineal line - Linea aspera\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Linea semilunaris\",\"e\":\"The linea semilunaris is a tendinous line that lies lateral to the rectus muscle bilaterally. It is the site of a Spigelian hernia\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":124,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted electively with severe osteoarthritis of the hip. They have tried conservative treatments but their condition is worsening and they are barely mobile. They are therefore undergoing a total hip replacement Which one of the following statements about the hip joint is correct?\",\"lead\":\"They are therefore undergoing a total hip replacement Which one of the following statements about the hip joint is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"it is a diarthrodal joint (diarthrosis)\",\"e\":\"Diarthrosis (or diarthrodal joint) is another term for a synovial joint. The hip joint is a ball and socket synovial joint - its articulations are shown below.\\n\\nFemoral head Acetabulum\\n\\nAcetabular labrum\\n\\nLigament of head of femur\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The hip joint capsule is tight in flexion\",\"e\":\"The capsule of the hip joint is very thick and dense, much more so than the capsule in the shoulder joint. It is thickest and strongest anteriorly, where there is the most resistance. The hip joint capsule is normally tight in extension and more relaxed in flexion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The quadratus femoris is a medial rotator of the hip\",\"e\":\"The lateral rotators of the hip are the quadratus femoris, obturator internus, obturator externus, piriformis. gemellus superior and gemellus inferior muscles. Quadratus femoris also contributes to adduction of the thigh.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The iliofemoral ligament prevents hyperflexion of the hip joint\",\"e\":\"The iliofemoral ligament is the strongest ligament in the human body and it prevents overextension of the hip joint. It originates from the anterior inferior iliac spine, and inserts into the intertrochanteric line of the femur.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The rim of the acetabular labrum decreases hip stability\",\"e\":\"The rim of the acetabular labrum keeps the head of the femur inside the acetabulum. enclosing it beyond its equator, so increasing hip stability\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":127,\"stem\":\"A 13-year-old woman falls from a horse anc presents with a swollen, painful foot. She is given analgesia and proceeds to the radiology department where she is noted to have a fracture of the talus. Which bone articulates with the anterior aspect of the talus?\",\"lead\":\"Which bone articulates with the anterior aspect of the talus?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cuboid\",\"e\":\"The cuboid bone lies lateral to the navicular.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lateral cuneiform\",\"e\":\"The cuneiform bones lie anterior to the navicular bone\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medial cuneiform\",\"e\":\"The cuneiform bones lie anterior to the navicular bone\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Navicular 2\",\"e\":\"The talus (in yellow) articulates with the navicular (blue) anteriorly at the talonavicular joint. as seen below.\\n\\nCalcaneus Talus Navicular Cuboid Cuneiforms\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Os calcis\",\"e\":\"The talus articulates with the os calcis (calcaneus) inferiorly.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":130,\"stem\":\"A 24-year-old footballer presents with a swollen painful knee: On examination he is noted to have significant joint effusion and a positive Lachman's test. Which component of the knee joint has been injured?\",\"lead\":\"Which component of the knee joint has been injured?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior cruciate ligament\",\"e\":\"The anterior cruciate ligament provides stability to the knee joint. High impact sports that involve rapid changes of direction or direct lower limb trauma are often associated with injuries to the ligament. It originates from the medial aspect of the lateral femoral condyle to insert into the intercondyloid tibial eminence - shown in blue in the image. Lachman's test requires the knee to be flexed to 20-30°, and is positive when the tibia can be pulled abnormally anteriorly from a fixed femur\\n\\nAnterior cruciate ligament Posterior cruciate ligament\\n\\nMedial Lateral meniscus moniscus\\n\\nMedial collateral Lateral collateral ligament ligament\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lateral collateral ligament\",\"e\":\"The lateral collateral ligament can be injured when there is a varus force to the knee - that is, a force on the medial aspect of the knee. The varus stress test can be used to assess this.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medial collateral ligament:\",\"e\":\"The medial collateral ligament can be injured when a valgus force is applied to the knee. The valgus stress test can be used to assess for this. The medial collateral ligament is adherent to the medial meniscus, which can easily be damaged at the same time. The McMurray's test may be positive if there is meniscal involvement in the injury\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Patellar tendon\",\"e\":\"Patella tendon injury usually arises from a jumping or explosive load on the knee. There is a 'popping' noise heard at the time of injury. Patients are unable to straight leg raise if it has been torn.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior cruciate ligament\",\"e\":\"Posterior cruciate ligament injury can be diagnosed with the posterior drawer test. The classical description of how this injury is sustained is the knee hitting the dashboard of a car - essentially, a direct blow to a flexed knee.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":133,\"stem\":\"A 53-year-old man presents with intermittent shooting pain along the posterior aspect of the lower limb. On examination, the pain is exacerbated by flexing the hip, and relieved by subsequent knee flexion. The sciatic nerve divides to form which two nerves?\",\"lead\":\"The sciatic nerve divides to form which two nerves?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior cutaneous and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves\",\"e\":\"These arise from the femoral nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anterior cutaneous and sural nerves\",\"e\":\"The sural nerve is formed by the medial sural and lateral sural cutaneous nerves, which arise from the tibial nerve and common peroneal nerve respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anterior cutaneous and tibial nerves\",\"e\":\"The anterior cutaneous nerve arises from the femoral nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Common fibular and anterior cutaneous nerves\",\"e\":\"The anterior cutaneous nerve arises from the femoral nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Common fibular and tibial nerves\",\"e\":\"The sciatic nerve originates from the L4-S3 roots. It can be injured or compressed to give symptoms of pain and paraesthesia in the posterior aspect of the lower limb. The diagnosis can be confirmed if symptoms are exacerbated by passive extension of the knee (which can be combined with foot dorsiflexion to further provoke symptoms, so long as the knee is extended) and immediately relieved by knee flexion. The sciatic nerve emerges in to the thigh from beneath piriformis (as seen in the image), and divides to form the common fibular and tibial nerves - typically at the upper border of the popliteal fossa, but at a variable point in the posterior thigh.\\n\\nPiriformis\\n\\nSciatic nerve Long head of the biceps femoris\\n\\nCommon fibular nerve Tibial nerve\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":136,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old skier presents with an acutely painful and swollen knee joint and is noted to have a positive Lachman's test.\\n\\nWhich ligament has been injured?\",\"lead\":\"Which ligament has been injured?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior cruciate ligament „Cr\",\"e\":\"The anterior cruciate ligament (shown in blue below) provides stability to the knee joint High impact sports that involve rapid changes of direction or direct lower limb trauma are often associated with injuries to the ligament. The ligament originates from the medial aspect of the lateral femoral condyle to insert into the intercondyloid tibial eminence. Lachman's test requires the knee to be slightly flexed, and is positive when the tibia can be pulled abnormally anteriorly from a fixed femur.\\n\\nAnterior cruciate ligament Posterior cruciate ligament\\n\\nMedial Lateral meniscus moniscus\\n\\nMedial collateral Lateral collateral ligament ligament\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Deltoid ligament\",\"e\":\"The deltoid ligament stabilises the ankle joint. The eversion and external rotation tests help evaluate it clinically.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lateral collateral ligament\",\"e\":\"The lateral collateral ligament can be injured when there is a varus force to the knee - that is, a force on the medial aspect of the knee. The varus stress test can be used to assess for this.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Medial collateral ligament\",\"e\":\"The medial collateral ligament can be injured when a valgus force is applied to the knee. The valgus stress test will be positive if it is injured.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior cruciate ligament\",\"e\":\"Posterior cruciate ligament injury can be diagnosed with the posterior drawer test. The classical description of how this injury is sustained is the knee hitting the dashboard of a car - essentiaily, a direct blow to a flexed knee.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":139,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old woman presents with an acutely painful and swollen ankle joint after falling at speed while skiing. She is diagnosed with a bi-malleolar ankle fracture and undergoes open reduction and internal fixation Which ligament attaches to the medial malleolus?\",\"lead\":\"She is diagnosed with a bi-malleolar ankle fracture and undergoes open reduction and internal fixation Which ligament attaches to the medial malleolus?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior talofibular ligament\",\"e\":\"The anterior talofibular ligament originates from the lateral malleolus and inserts into the lateral aspect of the talar neck\\n\\nThe calcaneofibular ligament originates from the lateral malleolus and inserts into the lateral wall of the calcaneus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Calcaneofibular ligament\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Deltoid ligament\",\"e\":\"The deltoid ligament attaches to the medial malleolus of the tibia, and inserts via four separate parts into the sustentaculum tali of the calcaneus, the calcaneonavicular ligament, the navicular tuberosity and the medial surface of the talus - these four parts can be seen in the medial view of the image below. 'ASTES - Fibula Thia Medal maliocus - Dolloid Eçament\\n• Subtalar joint\\n\\nMedial vow\\n\\nTibia Fibula Posterior and anterior inferior tibicibular ligaments Interosseous Anterior talofibular §gamant membrane\\n\\nSubsalar joine Caconcofibular ligament Lateral view\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior talofibular ligament\",\"e\":\"The posterior talofibular ligament originates from the posterior border of the fibula and inserts into the posterior lateral talus.\\n\\nThe syndesmotic ligament is made up of several ligaments - the tibiofibular ligament, the interosseous ligament and the fibular ligaments.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Syndesmotic ligament\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":142,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old smoker undergoes a femoral-popliteal bypass procedure after complaining of intermittent claudication symptoms. The femoral artery is dissected from the adjacent tissues within the adductor canal of the thigh What structure forms the anterolateral border of the canal?\",\"lead\":\"The femoral artery is dissected from the adjacent tissues within the adductor canal of the thigh What structure forms the anterolateral border of the canal?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adductor longus\",\"e\":\"The adductor longus forms the posterior border of the canal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Adductor magnus\",\"e\":\"Adductor magnus, along with adductor longus, forms the posterior border of the canal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sartorius\",\"e\":\"Sartorius is the supero-medial border.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vastus lateralis\",\"e\":\"Vastus lateralis is not one of the borders of the adductor canal\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vastus medialis\",\"e\":\"Vastus medialis is the anterolateral border of the canal, as can be seen below. The adductor canal contains the femoral artery and vein, and the saphenous nerve.\\n\\nSartorius\\n\\n_Adductor Vastus medialis canal\\n\\nAdductor longus\\n\\nFAT Artery\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":145,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old taxi driver presents with severe right hip pain and deformity following a road traffic accident. He is diagnosed with a right posterior hip dislocation. Which fracture is associated with this injury?\",\"lead\":\"Which fracture is associated with this injury?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acetabulum.C-\",\"e\":\"Hip dislocation is frequently associated with a fracture of the posterior rim of the acetabulum. Car drivers or motorcyclists who are sitting with hip and knee flexed and then hit their knee hard are likely to sustain a dislocation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral shaft\",\"e\":\"A fracture of the femoral shaft is usually the result of high impact trauma. Patients may sustain multiple traumatic injuries in association with this.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Intertrochanteric femoral neck\",\"e\":\"This option is not correct.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sacrum\",\"e\":\"This option is not correct.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inferior pubic ramus\",\"e\":\"A fracture of the pelvis is associated with central dislocation, but is relatively uncommon.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":147,\"stem\":\"The greater trochanter is part of the proximal femur and serves as attachment to several muscles. It is an important landmark in operations of the hip. Which muscle is attached to the lateral surface of the greater trochanter?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscle is attached to the lateral surface of the greater trochanter?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The external rotators of the hip\",\"e\":\"The external rotators of the hip are attached to the medial, superior and posterior surfaces of the greater trochanter.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The gluteus maximus\",\"e\":\"The gluteus maximus muscle is attached to gluteal tuberosity of the femur\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The gluteus medius\",\"e\":\"The gluteus medius and minimus (abductors) insert into the greater trochanter on the lateral side. The minimus is seen in yellow in the image, and the medius in green cut through to reveal the minimus beneath.\\n\\nGluteus medius Gluteus maximus\\n\\nGluteus minimus\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The psoas major attach to it\",\"e\":\"The psoas major attaches to the lesser trochanter.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The obturator externus\",\"e\":\"The obturator externus attaches to the trochanteric fossa on the medial aspect of the greater trochanter.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":150,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old man sustained hip dislocation following a rugby injury, he presented with reduced sensation on the dorsum of the foot and weakness of foot dorsiflexion. He undergoes closed reduction under anaesthesia and is advised not to play contact sport in the near future\\n\\nWhich nerve injury is most likely responsible for this presentation?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve injury is most likely responsible for this presentation?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve supplies all the quadriceps muscles. Injury to this nerve usually causes wasting of the quadriceps muscles, difficulty walking, pain and paraesthesia of the thigh.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The sciatic nerve\",\"e\":\"The sciatic nerve is closely related to the posterior aspect of the hip joint where it can be injured. In this case, sciatic nerve injury is likely due to the mechanism of injury, and also the mixed nature of the functional deficit. It is shown in green in the image below forming from the sacral plexus L4-53 nerve roots. It emerges from beneath piriformis and travels in the posterior compartment of the leg. dividing in to its constituent tibial and common fibular parts commonly at the upper aspect of the popliteal fossa\\n\\nL4\\n\\nL5\\n\\n51 Superior gluteal S2 Inferior gluteal\\n\\nSciatic = 54 (Fibular Posterior portion) emoral/ (Tibia/ portion) Pudendal Anatomy\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The tibial nerve.\",\"e\":\"The tibial nerve arises from the sciatic nerve and supplies the ankle flexors, the long digital flexors, the short (intrinsic) foot muscles and sensation to the sole of the foot.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The superficial peroneal nerve\",\"e\":\"The superficial peroneal nerve is a branch of the common peroneal nerve. It supplies the lateral compartment of the leg, allowing foot eversion, and sensation over the dorsum of the foot, except the first web space between the great toe and the second toe.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The sural nerve\",\"e\":\"The sural nerve is a purely sensory nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":153,\"stem\":\"A young man sprains his ankle while playing basketball. His ankle went into eversion and external rotation Which ligament complex is likely to be damaged with this mechanism of injury?\",\"lead\":\"His ankle went into eversion and external rotation Which ligament complex is likely to be damaged with this mechanism of injury?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The anterior and posterior talofibular ligaments\",\"e\":\"The anterior talofibular ligament is a lateral ligament of the ankle and prevents the foot from sliding forward in relation to the shin. It is typically injured in inversion injuries. The posterior talofibular ligament is a lateral ligament of the ankle as well. It less commonly injured. These two ligaments, along with the calcaneofibular ligament, form the lateral collateral ligament of the ankle joint.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The calcaneofibular ligament\",\"e\":\"The calcaneofibular ligament is one of three ligaments that form the lateral collateral ligament of the ankle joint. It vulnerable to inversion injuries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The calcaneonavicular ligament\",\"e\":\"The calcaneonavicular ligament connects the plantar surface of the navicular bone to the sustentaculum of the calcaneus. it also gives support to the head of the talus It thus supports a significant amount of body weight, and is important in flat foot deformities.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The deltoid ligament\",\"e\":\"The deltoid ligament is attached above to the medial malleolus and below to the talus. sustentaculum tali and the spring ligament It Is formed of four components, as can be seen in the image below. An eversion injury can rupture the deltoid due to traction force.\\n\\nThia - 1 Fibula IS PASTEST Medial maliecius Deltoid Egament\\n• Subtalar joint\\n\\nMedia! view\\n\\nFibula Tibia Posterior and anterior inferior tibioibular ligament; Interosseous membrane Anterior talclibular agamant\\n\\nCalcaneus bufar ligament Subcalar joint Lateral view\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The syndesmosis ligaments complex\",\"e\":\"An injury to this complex would cause instability at the ankle but would not be at risk for this mechanism of injury.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":156,\"stem\":\"You are performing a long saphenous vein harvest for a coronary bypass procedure: Regarding the cutaneous nerve supply of lower limb, which nerve supplies the medial aspect of the lower leg and foot?\",\"lead\":\"You are performing a long saphenous vein harvest for a coronary bypass procedure: Regarding the cutaneous nerve supply of lower limb, which nerve supplies the medial aspect of the lower leg and foot?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The deep peroneal nerve of the commonperoneal nerve\",\"e\":\"The deep peroneal nerve provides the cutaneous supply to the first dorsal space of the foot.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The intermediate cutaneous of the femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"This nerve, along with the medial cutaneous nerve of the femoral nerve, provides the cutaneous supply to the anterior thigh.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The medial cutaneous nerve of the femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The medial cutaneous nerve is one of two branches of the anterior division of the femoral nerve, the other branch is the intermediate cutaneous nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The saphenous nerve of the femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The saphenous nerve is a branch of the femoral nerve and supplies skin on the medial side of the lower leg. Its area of distribution is shown in the image below in light blue.\\n\\nAnterior cutaneous branches (of femoral nerve) Saphenous nerve (branch of femoral nerve)\\n\\nPosterior C Anterior MeAnatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The surat nerve of the tibial nerve\",\"e\":\"The sural nerve is a branch of the tibial nerve but it receives a communicating branch from the common peroneal nerve. It is a purely sensory nerve, providing sensation to the posterolateral aspect of the distal third of the leg, and the lateral aspect of the foot, heel and ankle.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":159,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with critical ischaemia of their foot. Regarding blood supply to the lower limb, which artery is typically palpated lateral to the tendon of extensor hallucis longus?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding blood supply to the lower limb, which artery is typically palpated lateral to the tendon of extensor hallucis longus?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The dorsalis pedis\",\"e\":\"The pulse of the dorsalis pedis pulse is palpated lateral to the extensor hallucis longus tendon distal to the dorsal navicular prominence and medial to the extensor digitorum longus tendon. PASTES Dorsalis pedis Medial and intermedial dorsal artery cutaneous nerves\\n\\nDeep fibular nerve\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The peroneal artery\",\"e\":\"The peroneal artery arises from the posterior tibial artery. It runs in the posterior compartment between tibialis posterior and flexor hallicus longus and supplies the muscles of the lateral (peroneal) compartment of the leg. It is palpable at a point superior and anterior to the lateral malleolus, but is rarely palpated as part of a routine lower limb neurovascular examination.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The popliteal artery\",\"e\":\"The popliteal artery is not always easily palpable. To find it, ask the patient to flex their knee to 90°. while pressing your fingers into the popliteal fossa and holding the sides of the knee.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The posterior tibial artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior tibial artery can be palpated at a point anterior to the Achilles' tendon and posterior to the medial malleolus. It is palpated as part of a routine lower limb neurovascular examination.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The profunda femoris artery\",\"e\":\"The profunda femoris is a deep branch of the common femoral artery and is not usually palpable.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":162,\"stem\":\"When performing arthroscopic examination of the knee, the posterolateral portal could be used to visualise the posterior cruciate ligament and posterior horn of the lateral meniscus. If a lateral meniscal tear is present it is possible to perform a meniscectomy using this approach.\\n\\nWhich structure is at particular risk of being damaged through this portal?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure is at particular risk of being damaged through this portal?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The common fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The common fibular (peroneal) nerve winds round the neck of the fibula. The posterolateral portal is usually placed between the lateral collateral ligament and the biceps femoris tendon, and thus the common peroneal nerve is at risk of being damaged here\\n\\nThis structure is not at risk of being damaged\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Patellar tendon\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The popliteal artery\",\"e\":\"This structure is not at risk of being damaged.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The saphenous nerve and vein\",\"e\":\"The saphenous nerve and vein pass along the posteromedial border of the knee, and so could be injured by placement of the posteromedial portal.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The tibial nerve\",\"e\":\"This structure is not at risk of being damaged.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":164,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman sustained a non-contact twisting injury and dislocated her patella. She attends the Emergency Department where the patella is reduced with sedation, she is due to be seen in the fracture clinic to discuss the risks of the injury recurring Which anatomical structure contributes to the dynamic stability of the patella?\",\"lead\":\"She attends the Emergency Department where the patella is reduced with sedation, she is due to be seen in the fracture clinic to discuss the risks of the injury recurring Which anatomical structure contributes to the dynamic stability of the patella?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Distal femoral trochlea\",\"e\":\"This does not contribute to the dynamic stability of the patella as it is not one of the quadriceps muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lateral femoral trochlea\",\"e\":\"This does not contribute to the dynamic stability of the patella as it is not one of the quadriceps muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medial patella femoral ligament\",\"e\":\"This does not contribute to the dynamic stability of the patella as it is not one of the quadriceps muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Achilles tendon\",\"e\":\"This does not contribute to the dynamic stability of the patella as it is not one of the quadriceps muscles.\\n\\nThe dynamic stabilisers of the patella are the quadriceps muscles (rectus femoris, vastus medialis, vastus lateralis, and vastus intermedialis) anteriorly, and the hamstring muscles (semi-membranosus, semi-tendinosus, and biceps femoris) posteriorly, and gastrocnemius medially and laterally.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vastus medialis muscle\",\"e\":\"The vastus medialis is one of the quadriceps muscles - highlighted in red in the image, It is attached to the medial border of the patella. The patella will dislocate laterally because of the pull of quadriceps. PASTES Psoas major liacus Rectus femoris Vastus medialis Vastus lateralis Sartorius Pectineus\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":167,\"stem\":\"The femoral triangle contains the femoral nerve and femoral canal\\n\\nWhich structure forms the lateral boundary?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure forms the lateral boundary?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The adductor longus muscle\",\"e\":\"The adductor longus is the medial border of the femoral triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The fascia lata\",\"e\":\"The fascia lata is the 'roof' of the femoral triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"The inguinal ligament is the superior border of the femoral triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The pectineus muscle\",\"e\":\"The floor of the femoral triangle is formed by the pectines and iliopsoas muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The Sartorius muscle\",\"e\":\"The sartorius muscle is the lateral border of the femoral triangle. It is shown with its borders below (laterally the medial border of sartorius, medially the media border of adductor longus, and superiorly the inguinal ligament). The triangle contains the femoral vein, artery and nerve from medial to lateral.\\n\\ninguinal Ligament\\n\\nSartorius ~\\n\\nAdductor Longus\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":170,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with a hard lump in their groin with bowel obstruction. A femoral hernia is diagnosed and the defect is repaired in the operating theatre. The femoral canal is the most medial structure of the femoral triangle.\\n\\nWhat function does the femoral canal serve?\",\"lead\":\"What function does the femoral canal serve?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Allows for expansion of the femoral vessels\",\"e\":\"This space allows for expansion of the femoral vein, for example, when there is increased venous return during walking. The femoral canal is shown in the femoral triangle in the images below.\\n• Femoral canal Inguinal ligament (anterior border) Pectineus (posterior border) Femoral vein (lateral border)\\n• Lacunar ligament (medial border)\\n\\nFemoral nerve Femoral canal\\n\\nGreat saphenous voin Femoral sheath\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Contains and protects the femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"This option is not correct\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Forms the proximal part of the adductor canal\",\"e\":\"This option is not correct. The adductor canal is bordered by the sartorius (anteriorly). vastus medialis (laterally), and adductor longus and adductor magnus (posteriorly) muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It forms a dead space and has no contents\",\"e\":\"This option is not correct\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prevent herniation of the small bowel\",\"e\":\"This option is not correct It is the defect that can lead to a femoral hernia.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":173,\"stem\":\"The popliteal fossa forms the posterior aspect of the knee Which structure forms the floor of the popliteal fossa?\",\"lead\":\"The popliteal fossa forms the posterior aspect of the knee Which structure forms the floor of the popliteal fossa?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The biceps femoris:\",\"e\":\"Biceps femoris forms the supero-lateral border of the popliteal fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The gastrocnemius\",\"e\":\"The two heads of gastrocnemius form the lateral borders of the popliteal fossa, with the lateral head forming the infero-lateral border, and the medial head forming the infero-medial border.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The knee joint capsule.\",\"e\":\"The floor of the popliteal fossa is formed by the posterior surface of the knee joint capsule, and by the posterior surface of the femur.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The semimembranosus\",\"e\":\"The supero-medial border of the popliteal fossa is formed by the semimembranosus and semitendinosus muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The semitendinosus\",\"e\":\"The semitendinosus is part of the supero-medial border, although the muscle belly finishes above the popliteal fossa and continues as its long tendon.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":175,\"stem\":\"The knee is a hinge joint, but it does move in three planes.\\n\\nWhich function of the knee allows increased flexion?\",\"lead\":\"Which function of the knee allows increased flexion?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"External rotation of the tibia on femur in flexion\",\"e\":\"During knee flexion, the tibia undergoes internal rotation. This is aided by the contraction of popliteus muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Posterior roll back of the femur on tibia\",\"e\":\"The knee joint can flex to about 135 degrees, maximised by the posterior rollback of the femur on the tibia during flexion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The absence of posterior sloping of the proximal tibia\",\"e\":\"In some anatomical variants there is a degree of posterior sloping of the proximal tibia, however this is not the most important factor in allowing increased knee flexion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The presence of the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)\",\"e\":\"The ACL resists the tibia coming anterior in its relationship to the femur, it therefore limits the range of movement in the knee:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tightness of the extensor mechanism\",\"e\":\"The tightness in the extensor mechanism can limit the range of movement in the knee, in conditions in which connective tissues are looser (such as in people who have Ehlers-Danlos syndrome) the knee joint is more mobile.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":177,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old man is involved in a road traffic accident and sustains a 10 cm deep wound over the lateral aspect of the right calf. Radiographs show no fractures. He has sensory loss to the dorsum of the foot except for the first web space (between the great and second toe), and reduced eversion of his right foot. Which nerve has been injured?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve has been injured?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Deep fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sural nerve\",\"e\":\"This nerve provides sensation to the lateral part of the foot and ankle, it runs with the short saphenous vein on the leg.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medial plantar nerve\",\"e\":\"The medial plantar nerve gives off plantar digital nerves that provide sensation to the sole of the foot - the medial two-thirds of it, and the medial three and half digits. It is responsible for flexion of all of the toes, and also abduction of the big toe.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superficial fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The superficial branch of the fibular nerve supplies sensation to the dorsum of the foot, except for the 1st web space (supplied by deep fibular nerve) and the lateral aspect of the foot including the lateral 1½ toes (supplied by the sural nerve). It travels through the lateral compartment of the leg and is most likely to be injured as there are motor and sensory disturbances to its distribution.\\n\\nThe deep fibular nerve supplies sensation to the 1st web space of the dorsum of the foot. It also provides motor function to the extensor muscles in the anterior compartment of the leg. An injury to the deep peroneal nerve results in foot drop.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tibial nerve\",\"e\":\"A tibial nerve injury results in loss of ankle and toe plantarflexion, weakened foot inversion and loss of sensation to the plantar aspect of the foot.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":179,\"stem\":\"During a posterior approach to the right hip joint, you are asked to identify the gluteal muscles. You correctly identify the most superficial gluteal muscle, which is innervated by the inferior gluteal nerve What movements does this muscle produce?\",\"lead\":\"You correctly identify the most superficial gluteal muscle, which is innervated by the inferior gluteal nerve What movements does this muscle produce?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hip adduction and medial rotation\",\"e\":\"The adductors of the hip are the adductors brevis, longus and magnus; and gracilis and pectineus. They are in the medial compartment of the thigh, Glutes medius and minimus medially rotate the hip, and are supplied by the superior gluteal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hip extension and knee flexion\",\"e\":\"Although gluteus maximus extends the hip, it does not play a role in knee flexion - this is controlled by the hamstring muscles (biceps femoris, semitendinosus and semimembranosus), and to a lesser degree gastrocnemius.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hip extension and lateral rotation\",\"e\":\"The gluteus maximus is the most superficial muscle and is supplied by the inferior gluteal nerve. It extends and laterally rotates the hip. The superior and inferior gluteal arteries supply the gluteus maximus and are branches of the internal iliac artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hip flexion alone\",\"e\":\"The hip is flexed by the iliopsoas, sartorius and rectus femoris.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hip flexion and knee extension\",\"e\":\"The knee is extended by the quadriceps femoris muscles and vastus medialis.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":181,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant with an emergency exploration of a stab wound. The patient was stabbed in the left thigh. The consultant points out an anatomical structure that is bordered superiorly by the inguinal ligament, medially by the adductor longus and laterally by the sartorius muscle and contains the femoral artery, femoral vein and femoral nerve. What anatomical structure is this?\",\"lead\":\"What anatomical structure is this?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Femoral canal\",\"e\":\"The femoral canal is the medial space of the femoral sheath; it lies medial to the femoral vein and is a space that the vein can expand into during times of increased venous return.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral sheath\",\"e\":\"The femoral sheath is a fascial space that lies within the femoral triangle. It contains the femoral vessels and the femoral canal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Femoral triangle\",\"e\":\"The femoral (Scarpa's) triangle is a fascial space in the medial upper thigh. its sides are formed superiorly by the inguinal ligament, medially by the medial border of adductor longus and laterally by the medial border of the sartorius muscle. The floor is formed by the iliopsoas, pectineus and adductor longus. The triangle contains the femoral vessels and nerve. Within the femoral triangle, the femoral artery gives off the profunda femoris branch; and the femoral vein receives deep femoral and saphenous tributaries.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hunter's canal\",\"e\":\"Hunter's canal is also known as the subsartorial or adductor canal. Its anterior border is the sartorius muscle, at the postero-medial border are the adductor longus and magnus muscles, and at the lateral border is the vastus medialis muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inguinal canal\",\"e\":\"The inguinal canal is a space in the anterior abdominal wall.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":183,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old woman twists her ankle, sustaining a Maisonneuve fracture. This was managed with syndesmotic screw fixation and a short leg non-weight-bearing cast for 6 weeks. The cast has been removed but the patient reports that her foot drags when walking, as well as a tingling sensation on the dorsum of the foot\\n\\nGiven the likely diagnosis, which of the following is the correct answer?\",\"lead\":\"Given the likely diagnosis, which of the following is the correct answer?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The nerve affected is a branch of the saphenous nerve\",\"e\":\"The common peroneal (fibular) nerve arises from the sciatic nerve in the at the apex of the popliteal fossa, from the L4/L5/S1 and S2 nerve roots.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"As it exits the popliteal fossa, the most likely affected nerve courses around the neck of the fibula in the lateral compartment of the leg\",\"e\":\"At this point the common fibular (peroneal) nerve can be palpable and this is where the nerve can easily be injured.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"This nerve gives rise to one purely sensory branch and three mixed motor and sensory branches\",\"e\":\"The common fibular (peroneal) nerve usually gives off two purely sensory and two mixed branches.\\n\\nSensory innervation Nerve Type\\n\\nSural communicating Sensory Lower posterolateral leg\\n\\nLateral sural cutaneous Sensory Upper lateral leg\\n\\nAnterolateral leg. dorsum of the foot Superficial fibular Mixed\\n\\nDeep fibular Skin between the first and second toe Mixed\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Entrapment of this nerve results in a shuffling gait\",\"e\":\"Common fibular (peroneal) nerve entrapment results in a high stepping gait, where the patient is noted to lift the affected foot excessively from the ground during the swing phase, to overcome their inability to dorsiflex the foot and therefore to avoid the foot hitting the floor. This results in excessive hip and knee flexion. It is sometimes called slapping gait as the affected foot 'slaps' the floor. A shuffling gait is associated with Parkinson's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A branch of this nerve innervates the muscles acting to invert the foot\",\"e\":\"The common fibular (peroneal) nerve innervates the short head of biceps femoris (part of the hamstrings) flexing the knee. It also gives off two branches:\\n• Superficial fibular nerve: Innervates the foot evertors in the lateral compartment of the leg: fibularis longus and fibularis brevis.\\n• Deep fibular nerve: Innervates the muscles of the anterior compartment of the leg: tibialis anterior, extensor digitorum longus, extensor hallucis longus and some intrinsic foot muscles. These muscles are responsible for foot dorsiflexion and extension of the toes. Additionally, tibialis anterior contributes to ankle inversion.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":186,\"stem\":\"A 18-year-old man presents following an injury during a football match. He sustained a tackle to the lateral side of his knee, hearing a 'pop' sound and noted immediate swelling. Since then he reports ongoing pain, and feels the knee locking and being unstable. Concerning the injury above, which of the following is the correct answer?\",\"lead\":\"Concerning the injury above, which of the following is the correct answer?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Recovery after surgery takes 1-2 months\",\"e\":\"Recovery after surgery from an unhappy triad injury is 4-8 months and can in some cases last up to 1 year.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The injuries involved can be described as the O'Donoghue's triad\",\"e\":\"The triad of injury involving tear of the anterior cruciate ligament, medial collateral ligament and meniscal tear is known as the O'Donoghue's or the unhappy triad.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Priority in treatment involves media collateral ligament (MCL) reconstruction\",\"e\":\"Priority in treatment involves reconstruction of the anterior cruciate ligament with a graft either from the patellar tendon or from the semitendinosus. During the procedure the meniscal tear can also be corrected. Depending on the grade of injury, the medial collateral ligament may heal with immobilisation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The meniscal tear happens to the medial meniscus in the majority of cases\",\"e\":\"Meniscal tear associated with this kind of injury occur in the vast majority (80%) to the lateral meniscus and only in 20% in the medial meniscus. While O'Donoghue's original description was of medial meniscal injury, this was revised in the early 1990s to describe lateral meniscal tears.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Injury involves the anterior cruciate ligament, lateral collateral ligament and medial meniscus\",\"e\":\"The injuries described in this scenario, known as the 'unhappy triad' involves partial or complete tear of the anterior cruciate ligament, medial collateral ligament and, most commonly, meniscal tear.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":188,\"stem\":\"An IV drug user is admitted with sepsis of unknown cause, they are drowsy and unable to give a good history. They are started on empirical antibiotics and CT scan demonstrates a psoas abscess. Which of the following is true of the psoas major muscle?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true of the psoas major muscle?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Flexes the thigh at the hip joint C.\",\"e\":\"Psoas major originates from the transverse processes of the L1-L5 vertebrae, and the sides of the T12-L5 vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs, and inserts onto the lesser trochanter of the femur along with the iliacus muscle as the combined iliopsoas tendon. As it runs anterior to the hip joint underneath the inguinal ligament, its action is to flex the hip joint\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Extends the thigh at the hip joint\",\"e\":\"Because the psoas major muscle tendon (along with iliacus) runs anteriorly to the hip joint beneath the inguinal ligament to insert on to the lesser trochanter of the femur, it's action on the femur is to bring it anteriorly, therefore flexing the thigh at the hip joint. Gluteus maximus extends the thigh at the hip\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Adducts the thigh at the hip joint\",\"e\":\"Although psoas major inserts with iliacus on to the lesser trochanter, which is medial on the femur, it's action is to flex the hip joint rather than adduct the thigh. Hip adduction is produced by the medial adductor muscles in the thigh (adductor magnus, longus and brevis), pectineus, and gracilis:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Abducts the thigh at the hip joint\",\"e\":\"As the psoas major inserts on to the lesser trochanter of the femur along with iliacus, it would be unable to abduct the femur through its line of action. The hip abductors are gluteus medius and minimus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Assists in the full contraction of the diaphragm\",\"e\":\"The psoas major muscle originates from the transverse processes of all five lumbar vertebrae, as well as the sides of T12-L5 and the intervertebral discs between them. It does not originate from the diaphragm or ribs, and therefore makes no contribution to respiratory effort.\\n\\nThe psoas major muscle originates from the transverse processes of all five lumbar vertebrae, along with the lateral bodies of T12-L5 and the intervertebral discs between them. It joins the iliacus muscle (which originates broadly over the inner aspect of the iliac wing of the pelvis) to form the iliopsoas muscle. The iliopsoas inserts onto the lesser trochanter of the femur, running anteriorly to the hip joint, and therefore is the major flexor of the hip joint The psoas major also acts to stabilise the hip joint, and contributes to movement of the trunk when standing. The psoas major is innervated by the anterior rami of L1-3, and iliacus is innervated by the femoral nerve (specifically the L2-3 nerve roots).\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":191,\"stem\":\"You are being asked about lower limb anatomy by the vascular consultant. The popliteal artery continues into which arterial segment?\",\"lead\":\"The popliteal artery continues into which arterial segment?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dorsalis pedis artery\",\"e\":\"The dorsalis pedis artery is a branch of the anterior tibial artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Posterior tibial artery\",\"e\":\"The tibio-peroneal trunk gives rise to the posterior tibial artery and the peroneal artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Peroneal artery\",\"e\":\"The peroneal artery arises from the tibio-peroneal trunk along with the posterior tibial artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lateral plantar artery\",\"e\":\"The lateral plantar artery comes off the posterior tibial artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tibio-peroneal trunk\",\"e\":\"The tibio-peroneal (TP) trunk is the direct continuation of the popliteal artery. The TP trunk then divides into the peroneal and posterior tibial arteries.\"}]},{\"t\":54,\"p\":193,\"stem\":\"The arches of the foot are formed by the tarsal and metatarsal bones. The ligaments and fascia of the foot help to support the foot arches. Which ligament supports the medial longitudinal arch?\",\"lead\":\"Which ligament supports the medial longitudinal arch?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Flexor retinaculum of foot\",\"e\":\"The flexor retinaculum of the foot forms the roof of the tarsal tunnel, covering the flexor tendons of the foot. It inserts into the plantar aponeurosis, the medial malleolus of the tibia and medial process of the calcaneus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Calcaneofibular ligament\",\"e\":\"The caicaneofibular ligament is part of the lateral collateral ligament of the ankle joint. along with the anterior and posterior talofibular ligaments. This ligament secures the foot to the lateral malleolus of the distal fibula.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The deltoid ligament\",\"e\":\"The deltoid ligament is a strong triangular ligament that holds the talus to the medial malleolus of the distal tibia. There are three arches in the foot: the medial longitudinal arch, the lateral longitudinal arch and the transverse arch. The image shows the foot from below. Ligaments that support the medial longitudinal arch include the long plantar ligament, short plantar ligament (plantar calcaneocuboid ligament), spring ligament (plantar calcaneonavicular ligament), interosseous ligament (talocalcaneal ligament), deltoid ligament (medial collateral ankle ligament) and the plantar aponeurosis (deep plantar fascia).\\n\\nTibialis anterior\\n\\nFibularis longus Short plantar Plantar ligament\\n• calcaneonavic. ligament Long plantar ligament Tibialis posterior\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The plantar talonavicular ligament\",\"e\":\"The plantar calcaneonavicular ligament (or spring ligament), not the plantar talonavicular ligament supports the medial longitudinal arch.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transverse metatarsal ligament\",\"e\":\"The transverse metatarsal ligament is a fibrous band that connects the four heads of the metatarsals.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"During thyroidectomy, the inferior laryngeal branch of the right recurrent laryngeal nerve was injured, resulting in an inability to abduct the right vocal cord The action of which of the following laryngeal muscles is most likely to be affected?\",\"lead\":\"During thyroidectomy, the inferior laryngeal branch of the right recurrent laryngeal nerve was injured, resulting in an inability to abduct the right vocal cord The action of which of the following laryngeal muscles is most likely to be affected?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Thyroarytenoid\",\"e\":\"Thyroarytenoid relaxes the vocal folds by drawing the arytenoid cartilages towards the thyroid.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Arytenoid\",\"e\":\"Arytenoid adducts the vocal folds.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lateral cricoarytenoid,\",\"e\":\"Lateral cricoarytenoid adducts the vocal folds.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior cricoarytenoid\",\"e\":\"The posterior cricoarytenoid (highlighted in green below) is innervated by the inferior laryngeal nerve, which is a continuation of the recurrent laryngeal nerve. The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only muscle that abducts the vocal folds. If this muscle is denervated, the vocal folds may be paralysed in an adducted position, which would prevent air from entering the trachea.\\n\\nOblique arytenoid muscle\\n\\nTransverse arytenoid muscle\\n\\nPosterior cricoarytenoid muscle\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cricothyroid\",\"e\":\"Cricothyroid is the only laryngeal muscle innervated by the external branch of the superior laryngeal. It tenses the vocal ligaments by tipping the thyroid cartilage forward relative to the cricoid cartilage.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"You see a patient with a prolonged smoking history in the ENT clinic. They have recently noticed an increasingly hoarse voice and you perform nasendoscopy You are concerned to visualise a lesion in the larynx. Which of the following statements is true regarding the vocal ligaments?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements is true regarding the vocal ligaments?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Are formed by the superior free edge of the conus elasticus\",\"e\":\"The conus elasticus is mostly yellow, elastic tissue. It is the lateral part of the cricothyroid membrane. Superiorly, its free edge forms part of the vocal ligaments.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Are formed by the junction of the aryepiglottic and thyroepiglottic ligaments\",\"e\":\"The thyroepiglottic ligament connects the thyroid cartilage to the lower edge of the epiglottis:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Are formed by the inferior. free edge of the thyrohyoid ligament\",\"e\":\"The inferior edge of the medial and lateral thyrohyoid ligaments form the superior border of the thyroid cartilage.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Contain the cricothyroid muscle\",\"e\":\"The vocal ligaments are formed by the superior free edge of the conus elasticus. The cricothyroid muscle attaches to the vocal cords and is the only tensor of the larynx.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Are attached to the superior margin of the epiglottis\",\"e\":\"The epiglottis is part of the larynx. It's purpose is to close during swallowing so that food goes into the oesophagus, rather than the trachea.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with a hoarse voice. You perform flexible nasendoscopy in the ENT clinic and notice the vocal cords are not coming together symmetrically. Of the following intrinsic muscles of the larynx, which tenses (stretches) the vocal folds?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following intrinsic muscles of the larynx, which tenses (stretches) the vocal folds?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Posterior cricoarytenoid\",\"e\":\"Posterior cricoarytenoid opens the vocal cords by abducting and laterally rotating the arytenoid cartilage. They are the only muscles to open the vocal cords.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lateral cricoarytenoid\",\"e\":\"Lateral cricoarytenoid acts in the opposite manner to the posterior cricoarytenoid, ie adducts and medially rotates the arytenoid cartilage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Thyroarytenoid\",\"e\":\"Thyroarytenoid is antagonistic to the cricothyroid muscle - it decreases the tension of the vocal cords\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transverse arytenoid\",\"e\":\"The transverse arytenoid brings the arytenoids towards each other when they contract, so adducting the vocal folds.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cricothyroid muscle\",\"e\":\"The cricothyroid is the only tensor muscle of the larynx. It brings the thyroid forward to tense and elongate the vocal cords.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a neck dissection. The consultant wants to be thorough and ensure all nodes are removed so includes the posterior triangle. What structure divides the posterior triangle of the neck further into an upper and lower triangle?\",\"lead\":\"What structure divides the posterior triangle of the neck further into an upper and lower triangle?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior bely of the digastric CaS.\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior belly of the omooid\",\"e\":\"The borders of the posterior triangle are anteriorly - the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid; the 'apex' of the triangle - the junction of sternocleidomastoid and trapezius; and posteriorly - the anterior border of trapezius; the 'base' of the triangle - the middle third of the clavicle. About an inch superior to the 'base'/middle third of the clavicle lies the inferior belly of omohyoid. This divides the posterior triangle into two further sub-triangles. The more superior of these triangles is known as the occipital triangle, the more inferior one, the subclavian triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Posterior belly of the digastric\",\"e\":\"The anterior belly of the digastric forms the anterior border of the submandibular triangle.\\n\\nThe posterior belly of digastric forms the posterior border of the submandibular triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Scalenus anterior muscle\",\"e\":\"The scalenus anterior muscle is part of the floor of the posterior triangle of the neck. Along with the middle scalene muscle and the first rib, it forms the interscalene triangle. This narrow space can be involved in thoracic outlet syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior belly of the omohyoid\",\"e\":\"The superior belly of the omohyoid further divides the anterior triangle of the neck into the carotid triangle and the muscular triangle.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"The thyroid gland in some cases can have a thyroidea ima artery that supplies the isthmus of the thyroid If present, where would it take origin?\",\"lead\":\"The thyroid gland in some cases can have a thyroidea ima artery that supplies the isthmus of the thyroid If present, where would it take origin?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"From the inter theid artery. X\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Directly from the thyrocervical trunk\",\"e\":\"The inferior thyroid artery is a branch of this trunk.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"From the superior thyroid artery\",\"e\":\"The thyroidea ima artery typically arises from the brachiocephalic trunk.\\n\\nThe superior thyroid artery is a branch of the external carotid artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"From the brachiocephalic trunk\",\"e\":\"The thyroidea ima artery, when present, arises from the brachiocephalic trunk (innominate artery) and ascends in front of the trachea to the lower part of the thyroid gland, which it supplies It varies greatly in size and appears to compensate for deficiency or absence of one of the other thyroid vessels. It occasionally arises from the aorta, the right common carotid. the subclavian or the internal mammary.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Directly from the external carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The external carotid gives off the superior thyroid artery.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"Which of the following is the name of the bone that the internal carotid artery enters to reach the intracranial cavity?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the name of the bone that the internal carotid artery enters to reach the intracranial cavity?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Frontal\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid artery runs through the temporal bone.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Occipital\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid artery runs through the temporal bone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sphenoid\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid artery passes alongside the sphenoid bone, but not through it.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Temporal\",\"e\":\"The carotid canal is found on the inferior surface of the petrous part of the temporal bone. It ascends vertically at first and then bends and runs horizontally forward and medially. It transmits the internal carotid artery and the carotid plexus of nerves into the cranium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Maxillary\",\"e\":\"The internal carotid artery runs through the temporal bone\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"An anaesthetic ST4 was called to the resuscitation bay of the Emergency Department to help secure the airway of a child with breathing difficulties. After recognising that the child would need to be intubated shortly he called a senior colleague for support\\n\\nWhich one of the following reasons make intubating children more difficult?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following reasons make intubating children more difficult?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Although there are no anatomical differences between adults and children the tact that children are smaller makes intubation more difficult\",\"e\":\"There are several anatomical differences between adults and children. One of these is that a child's head is relatively larger than that of an adult.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anatomical differences between adults and children such as larger head size, a U-shaped epiglottis and a relatively larger tongue make intubation more difficult\",\"e\":\"There are several anatomical differences between adults and children that make their intubation more difficult:\\n• Head size: children have a relatively larger head, which tends to flex the head on the neck, making airway obstruction more likely.\\n• Tongue size: the relatively larger tongue in children tends to flop back and obstruct the airway in the obtunded child, which means that there is less room in the mouth when intubation is being carried out.\\n• Larynx: The larynx is positioned more cephalic in children.\\n• Trachea: the trachea is shorter in children.\\n• Finally, the narrowest point of a child's airway is the cricoid ring; in adults, it is the glottis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anatomical differences between adults and children such as a more caudally placed larynx and a smaller angle of the jaw make intubation more difficult\",\"e\":\"The larynx is positioned more cephalic (glottis at C3 in infants compared with C6 in adults).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intubating a child is no more difficult than intubating an adult, this trainee called a senior colleague because he was relatively junior and needed support\",\"e\":\"Children are considered to be more difficult to intubate than adults because of several anatomical differences.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Situations involving children are usually more tense, only because family members are often present\",\"e\":\"There are anatomical differences making intubation of a child more difficult.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"While taking the anatomy viva section of the MRCS examination the examiner takes you to a neck prosection. She then points to a tubular structure lying anterior to scalenus anterior. which receives a branch from the retromandibular vein before piercing the deep cervical fascia and joining the subclavian vein.\\n\\nWhat is this structure?\",\"lead\":\"What is this structure?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"An anatomical variation, as no such structure has this anatomy\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The structure described is the external jugular vein, which drains into the subclavian vein and lies anterior to the scalenus anterior.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The common carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The common carotid arteries supply the neck and head. On the right, it arises from the brachiocephalic artery; on the left, it arises from the aortic arch. Thereafter, the anatomical structures of the right and left are the same. The common carotid artery lies in the carotid sheath with the internal jugular vein and the vagus nerve. The common carotid artery terminates at its bifurcation into the external carotid artery and the internal carotid artery. Anterior to it are sternomastoid, sternohyoid, sterothyroid and platysma. Posterior to the artery are the transverse processes of C4-C6. The oesophagus, thyroid and the airway structures are medial to it.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The external carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The external carotid artery is a branch of the common carotid artery that supplies the head and neck. It begins at the level of C4, which corresponds roughly to the upper border of the thyroid cartilage.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The external jugular vein\",\"e\":\"The external jugular vein drains most of the scalp and side of the face. It begins near the angle of the mandible and is formed from the union of retromandibular and postauricular veins, receiving branches from the posterior external and transverse cervical veins. The external jugular vein has two pairs of valves, which do not prevent regurgitation of the blood upward. The lower pair are placed at its entrance to the subclavian vein, the upper (in most cases) about 4 cm above the clavicle. The external jugular vein lies anterior to scalenus anterior and pierces the deep fascia of the neck, usually posterior to the clavicular head of the sternocleidomastoid muscle before draining into the subclavian vein.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The internal jugular vein\",\"e\":\"The internal jugular vein lies in the carotid sheath. It lies anterior to the anterior scalene muscle.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"How could you best describe the recurrent laryngeal nerve?\",\"lead\":\"How could you best describe the recurrent laryngeal nerve?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies all of the intrinsic laryngeal muscles\",\"e\":\"The superior laryngeal nerve also innervates the intrinsic laryngeal muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid muscles\",\"e\":\"The cricothyroid muscles are innervated by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerves are sensory to the subglottic region and supply all of the intrinsic muscles except the cricothyroid muscle\",\"e\":\"The two recurrent laryngeal nerves (RLN) arise from the vagus nerve. They supply four intrinsic muscles of the larynx - Lateral cricoarytenoid, posterior cricoarytenoid, transverse and oblique interarytenoid and thyroarytenoid. The RLN does not supply the cricothyroid muscle. The RLN also sends branches to the inferior constrictor muscle and cricopharynges muscle before entering the larynx. The RLN also supplies the mucosa of the vocal cord and the subglottis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerves are sensory to the supraglottic region and supplies the sternohyoid muscle\",\"e\":\"The sternohyoid muscle is innervated by the ansa cervicalis nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerves are sensory to the supraglottic region and supply all of the intrinsic muscles except the cricothyroid muscle\",\"e\":\"The supraglottic region receives its sensory innervation from the nternal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"You are examining a patient in clinic who is referred by the GP with a swelling in the neck. You think it lies posterior to the sternocleidomastoid muscle. The posterior triangle of the neck contains which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The posterior triangle of the neck contains which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Facial nerve\",\"e\":\"A cervical branch of the facial nerve is found in the carotid triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Great auricular nerve\",\"e\":\"The great auricular nerve turns upwards round the lateral border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle outside the triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Omohyoid muscle\",\"e\":\"The inferior belly of the omohyoid muscle and its intermediate tendon pass diagonally through the posterior triangle. Other components of the posterior triangle include splenius capitis, levator scapulas, the anterior, middle and posterior scalene muscles and the spinal accessory nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Roots of the brachial plexus\",\"e\":\"Lying medially, outside the triangle, are the roots of the brachial plexus, sandwiched between the scalenus anterior and medius muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vertebral artery\",\"e\":\"The vertebral artery lies within the pyramidal space inferiorly, before ascending through the foramen transversarium of the C1-C6 vertebrae.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your neurosurgical consultant performing an anterior cervical discectomy. He is concerned about injuring the recurrent laryngeal nerve Which one of the following is correct regarding the right recurrent laryngeal nerve?\",\"lead\":\"He is concerned about injuring the recurrent laryngeal nerve Which one of the following is correct regarding the right recurrent laryngeal nerve?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arises from a cranial nerve which exits through the foramen magnum\",\"e\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerve arises from the vagus nerve (cranial nerve X), which passes through the jugular foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is related to the inferior thyroid artery\",\"e\":\"Both right and left recurrent laryngeal nerves run in a groove between the trachea and oesophagus and are closely related to the inferior thyroid artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Passes behind the ligamentum arteriosum\",\"e\":\"The left recurrent laryngeal nerve lies posterior to the ligamentum arteriosum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Runs posterior to the osophagus but anterior to the trachea\",\"e\":\"The right recurrent laryngeal nerve lies in between the oesophagus and the trachea - and so runs anterior to the oesophagus and posterior to the trachea.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"On the right it winds around the aortic arch\",\"e\":\"The left recurrent laryngeal nerve winds around the aortic arch. The right is related to the subclavian artery.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your ENT consultant performing a thyroidectomy for malignancy. Regarding the surgical anatomy of the thyroid gland, which of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding the surgical anatomy of the thyroid gland, which of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The inferior parathyroid glands are more constant in position than the superior parathyroid glands\",\"e\":\"The superior parathyroid glands are more constant in position than the inferior. Because of their embryological migration, the inferior glands may be situated among the pretracheal lymph nodes or in the thymus as far as 10 cm from the thyroid\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The middle thyroid veins are more constant in position than the superior and inferior thyroid veins\",\"e\":\"The middle thyroid veins are the least constant of the thyroid veins. The superior veins drain into the internal jugular vein; the inferior veins are very constant and drain into the brachiocephalic veins; and the middle veins are variable and often multiple.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The thyroid gland does not have a definite capsule\",\"e\":\"Under the middle layer of deep cervical fascia, the thyroid has an inner true capsule, which is thin and adheres closely to the gland. Extensions of this capsule within the substance of the gland form numerous septae, which divide it into lobes and lobules. The lobules are composed of follicles, the structural units of the gland, which consist of a layer of simple epithelium enclosing a colloid-filled cavity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Berry's ligament connects the thyroid to the cricoid cartilage and upper trachea\",\"e\":\"Berry's ligament is a suspensory ligament. It was first described by James Berry in 1888\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve division results in the contralateral vocal cord lying in the mid-or cadaveric position\",\"e\":\"Unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve section results in the ipsilateral vocal cord lying motionless in the mid- or cadaveric position. The voice is hoarse and weak. If both recurrent laryngeal nerves are divided, then the glottic space is narrowed and stridor develops.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"The anterior approach to the cervical spine is indicated to remove tumours and fuse the spine. The platysma forms the most superficial plane of this approach. Which nerve supplies the platysma?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve supplies the platysma?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Facial nerve\",\"e\":\"The facial nerve innervates the muscles of facial expression, including the platysma. The platysma is a superficial sheet of muscle that takes origin from the fascia and skin of the upper chest; it inserts into the mandible and skin of the lower face. It is a wide, paired, flat muscle, with the sheets meeting in the midline.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Recurrent laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerves supply all the intrinsic muscles of the larynx except for the cricothyroid muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Spinal accessory nerve\",\"e\":\"The spinal accessory nerve is also known as cranial nerve XI, and innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sympathetic chain\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic chain is responsible for the fight-or-flight' response.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trigeminal nerve\",\"e\":\"The trigeminal nerve is cranial nerve V and is primarily a sensory nerve, although it also has some motor function to the muscles of mastication.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A 74-year-old man is noted to have a hoarse voice following a right hemithyroidectomy. Which nerve is most likely to have been injured?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve is most likely to have been injured?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PI Ansa cervicalis\",\"e\":\"The superior root of the ansa cervicalis runs on the carotid sheath and, with the inferior root, supplies the infrahyoid muscles\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glossopharyngeal nerve NO\",\"e\":\"An injury to this nerve would affect taste and swallowing. The glossopharyngeal nerve lies between the vagus nerve and accessory in the intermediate part of the jugular foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal nerve:\",\"e\":\"The hypoglossal nerve in its course if related to the occipital, internal carotid, external carotid and lingual arteries. Damage would affect motor function of the tongue, not speech.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Recurrent laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"Injury to the right recurrent laryngeal nerve is a major complication of a right hemithyroidectomy. The recurrent laryngeal nerves both ascend in the trachea-oesophageal groove in close proximity to the thyroid gland and are therefore at risk during surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"Injury to this nerve has a complex effect on voice. Broadly speaking, sufferers of superior laryngeal nerve injury/palsy have difficulty in speaking loudly or changing vocal pitch.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"An 82-year-old man undergoes a left carotid endarterectomy under general anaesthetic. The operation is noted to be very difficult due to the high bifurcation of the common carotid artery. Post-operatively his speech is difficult to understand, and it is noted on protruding that his tongue that it deviates to the left. Which nerve is most likely to be affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve is most likely to be affected?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ansacervicalis\",\"e\":\"Although the ansa cervicalis is often visualised during carotid endarterectomy, it innervates the strap muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Facial\",\"e\":\"The facial nerve provides motor innervation to the facial muscles, and sensory innervation to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glossopharyngeal\",\"e\":\"The glossopharyngeal nerve passes through the superior part of the carotid sheath. An injury to this nerve would affect taste and swallowing.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal\",\"e\":\"The hypoglossal nerve passes through the superior part of the carotid sheath. It supplies all of the muscles of the tongue except the palatoglossus muscle, which is supplied by the pharyngeal plexus and accessory nerve. On protruding the tongue. it will deviate to the affected side.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spinal accessory\",\"e\":\"The accessory nerve pass through the superior part of the carotid sheath. It innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient who has sustained a stab wound to the face that has completely lacerated the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve at its origin. Which one of the following muscles would remain unaffected?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following muscles would remain unaffected?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior belly of digastric\",\"e\":\"This muscle is innervated by the mylohyoid nerve, a branch of the posterior division of the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lateral pterygoid\",\"e\":\"This muscle is innervated by the anterior division of the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve.\\n\\nThis muscle is innervated by a branch from the anterior division of the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve - the masseteric nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Masseter\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Medial pterygoid\",\"e\":\"This muscle is innervated by the medial ptergoid nerve, a branch from the anterior division of the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior belly of digastric\",\"e\":\"The posterior belly of digastric is innervated by the digastric branch of the facial nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"You are inserting a central line into the internal jugular vein.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a tributary of this vein?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a tributary of this vein?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The external jugular vein\",\"e\":\"The external jugular arises from the junction of the posterior auricular vein and the posterior division of the retromandibular vein and drains into the subclavian.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The inferior thyroid vein\",\"e\":\"The inferior thyroid vein drains directly into the brachiocephalic vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The middle thyroid vein\",\"e\":\"The internal jugular vein receives the facial, pharyngeal, lingual and superior and middle thyroid veins. It is formed from the sigmoid and inferior petrosal sinus and continues to the brachiocephalic vein. The external jugular arises from the junction of the posterior auricular vein and the posterior division of the retromandibular vein and drains into the subclavian. Note that the anterior portion of the retromandibular vein proceeds forward to the anterior facial vein, and together they join to form the common facial vein. The transverse cervical vein is a tributary of the external jugular vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The retromandibular vein\",\"e\":\"The posterior division of the retromandibular vein and the posterior auricular drain into the external jugular and the anterior portion of the retromandibular vein proceeds forward to the anterior facial vein, and together they join to form the common facial vein.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The transverse cervical vein\",\"e\":\"The transverse cervical vein is a tributary of the external jugular vein. 732\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"You are performing a lymph node biopsy in the posterior triangle of the neck and you need to mark out the boundaries of the posterior triangle What are these?\",\"lead\":\"You are performing a lymph node biopsy in the posterior triangle of the neck and you need to mark out the boundaries of the posterior triangle What are these?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The anterior border of sternocleidomastoid, the mandible and the midline\",\"e\":\"The anterior triangle lies between the anterior border of sternocleidomastoid, the mandible and the midline.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"the anterior border or sternocleidomastoid, the posterior border or trapezius and the clavicle\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option - the posterior triangle lies between the posterior border of both the sternocleidomastoid, the anterior border of the trapezius and the clavicle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"ine anterior border or sternocleidomastoid, the posterior border or trapezius and the scapula\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option - the scapula does not make up part of the posterior triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The posterior border or sternecieldomastold, the anterior border of trapezius and the scapula\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option. The posterior border of sternocleidomastoid and the anterior border of trapezius is correct. However the scapula is not part of the posterior triangle border\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The posterior border of sternocleidomastoid, anterior border of trapezius and the clavicle\",\"e\":\"The posterior triangle lies between the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid, the anterior border of trapezius and the clavicle. Posterior: The borders of the posterior triangle are formed by the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle, anterior border of trapezius and the clavicle. Lymph node excision from the posterior triangle can result in damage to the accessory nerve. Anterior: The borders of the anterior triangle are formed by the anterior border of sternocleidomastoid muscle, the mandible and the midline. The anterior triangle is further subdivided into four triangles, submental, submandibular, carotid and muscular.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"Following a left carotid endarterectomy, a 74-year-old man is found to have altered tongue movements. When asked to protrude his tongue, it deviates to the left. Which one of the following structures could have been affected intra-operatively to cause this?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures could have been affected intra-operatively to cause this?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ansa cervicalis\",\"e\":\"The ansa cervicalis supplies the sternohyoid, sternothyroid and omohyoid. Injury to the ansa cervicalis may lead to a change in voice quality over time.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the hypoglossal nerve will result in weakness of the extrinsic muscles of the tongue on the ipsilateral side. The tongue will therefore deviate to the weakened side.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lingual nerve\",\"e\":\"The lingual nerve is a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3). It carries sensory information from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. It is at risk of injury during dental surgery; an injury leads to pain and altered sensation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Marginal mandibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The marginal mandibular nerve innervates the muscles of the lower lip. Injury to it affects facial expression, in particular, smiling.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerve, among other things, innervates the palatoglossus muscle, which initiates swallowing and elevates the posterior tongue.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"While performing an elective routine tracheostomy, heavy bleeding occurs intra-operatively Which one of the following vessels is most likely to be the source of this?\",\"lead\":\"While performing an elective routine tracheostomy, heavy bleeding occurs intra-operatively Which one of the following vessels is most likely to be the source of this?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior jugular vein\",\"e\":\"Anterior jugular veins and inferior thyroid veins may be encountered during tracheostomy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"External jugular vein\",\"e\":\"The external jugular vein is too lateral to be encountered in a routine tracheostomy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Internal jugular vein\",\"e\":\"The internal jugular vein is too lateral to be encountered in a routine tracheostomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Middle tyroid vein\",\"e\":\"The middle thyroid vein is too lateral to be encountered in a routine tracheostomy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior thyroid vein\",\"e\":\"The superior thyroid vein is too lateral to be encountered in a routine tracheostomy.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"Atherosclerosis at the carotid bifurcation is a common and potentially treatable cause of stroke. It can be managed with carotid angioplasty or carotid endarterectomy. The right common carotid artery bifurcates at what level?\",\"lead\":\"The right common carotid artery bifurcates at what level?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Angle of mandible\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option. The bifurcation occurs lower down. The facial artery arises from the external carotid artery, near the angle of the mandible.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cricoid cartilage\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option. The bifurcation is at the level of the thyroid cartilage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Jugular notch\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option. The jugular or suprasternal notch lies at the level of T3 vertebrae. The right common carotid bifurcates at a higher level.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Upper border of thyroid cartilage\",\"e\":\"The right common carotid artery branches off the brachiocephalic artery, the left arises from the aortic arch. It bifurcates at the level of the upper border of the lamina of the thyroid cartilage, at approximately the level of the fourth cervical vertebra. It lies posterior to the lobes of the thyroid gland and anterior to both the cervical sympathetic chain and the phrenic nerve on the scalenus anterior muscle; the latter is separated from the artery by prevertebral fascia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Within the substance of the Parotid gland\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option. The external carotid artery is associated closely to the parotid gland tissue. The bifurcation of the right common carotid occurs lower, at around the level of C4.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"The total length of the trachea is 10 cm. Posteriorly to this, runs the trachealis muscle, which is able to constrict the diameter of the trachea.\\n\\nAt what level does the trachea begin?\",\"lead\":\"At what level does the trachea begin?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bifurcation of common carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The bifurcation of the right common carotid artery is associated with the upper border of the lamina of the thyroid cartilage, at approximately the level of the fourth cervical vertebra.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cricoid cartilage\",\"e\":\"The trachea commences just below the cricoid cartilage (at the level of C6). Within the thorax and on the right, the trachea is in contact with the pleura, vagus and subclavian artery. On its left, the trachea is in contact with the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, aortic arch and the left common carotid and subclavian arteries. The trache ends at the upper border of T5. where it bifurcates.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyoid bone\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option. The hyoid bone lies superior to the thyroid cartilage, attached by the thyrohyoid membrane. The trachea commences inferior to the cricoid cartilage.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Suprasternal notch\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option. The trache can be felt at the suprasternal notch as it passes through behind the sternum, and ends at the level of T5 where it bifurcates.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyroid cartilage\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option. The trache begins further down, just below the cricoid cartilage.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"You examine a patient's cranial nerves in clinic, turning their head to the left against direct opposing pressure from your hand In this motion which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"You examine a patient's cranial nerves in clinic, turning their head to the left against direct opposing pressure from your hand In this motion which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Movement is limited by cervical vertebrae\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Movement takes place at the atlanto-occipital joint\",\"e\":\"The atlanto-occipital joint enables flexion and extension of the neck.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Neural impulses pass via the cranial accessory nerve\",\"e\":\"The head is turned using the contralateral sternocleidomastoid muscle, stimulated by impulses passing in the spinal accessory nerve. The cranial component of the accessory nerve joins the vagus nerve soon after exiting the brainstem.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The axis of rotation runs vertically through the odontoid process\",\"e\":\"The movement takes place at the atlantoaxial joint about a vertical axis through the odontoid process of the axis.\\n\\nThis is not the correct option. Lateral rotation of the neck/turning of the head is limited by the vertical alignment of the two attachments of the contralateral sternocleidomastoid muscle (contralateral mastoid process and sternoclavicular joint).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The left sternocleidomastoid muscle is the main agonist\",\"e\":\"The head is turned using the contralateral sternocleidomastoid muscle so, in this case, the right sternocleidomastoid is the main agonist.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":54,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man suffers an embolic stroke. Duplex ultrasonography demonstrates a severe right common carotid artery stenosis and he proceeds to undergo a carotid endarterectomy. Which one of the following best describes the right common carotid artery?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describes the right common carotid artery?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It bifurcates at the level of the upper border of the cricoid cartilage\",\"e\":\"The right common carotid artery bifurcates at the level of the upper border of the lamina of the thyroid cartilage. This is around the level of the third cervical vertebra.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It has the cervical sympathetic chain as an anterior relation\",\"e\":\"The cervical sympathetic chain is posterior to the right common carotid artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is a branch of the aortic arch\",\"e\":\"The right common carotid artery branches off the brachiocephalic artery. The left common carotid artery is a branch of the aortic arch.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is enclosed within the carotid sheath throughout its course\",\"e\":\"This is the correct option. The carotid sheath encloses the common carotid arteries (medial). the internal jugular vein (lateral) and the vagus nerve (between the two.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"it lies lateral to the lateral lobe of the thyroid gland\",\"e\":\"It lies posterior to the lobes of the thyroid gland\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"Thyroidectomy carries with it the risk of significant complications. You are using a new device designed to speed up the procedure that utilizes arterial clips. The application of these during total thyroidectomy is most likely to injure which one of the following nerves?\",\"lead\":\"The application of these during total thyroidectomy is most likely to injure which one of the following nerves?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"External laryngeal\",\"e\":\"When the superior thyroid arteries are ligated, the external laryngeal nerves running alongside can be easily damaged. The external laryngeal nerve is a branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, which is a branch of the vagus nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal\",\"e\":\"The hypoglossal nerves run in the anterior triangle of the neck superior to the hyoid bone and so are not in proximity\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Internal laryngeal\",\"e\":\"The internal laryngeal nerves pass above and behind the root of the superior thyroid arteries and are therefore usually outside the operative field\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Phrenic\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerves are protected as they lie behind the prevertebral fascia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transverse cervical\",\"e\":\"Both transverse cervical nerves run in the subcutaneous fascia. Horizontal skin-crease thyroidectomy incisions run parallel with their course and, therefore, most branches of these cutaneous nerves are spared.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"You are consenting a patient for carotid endarterectomy. The patient is particularly concerned about other structures that can be injured during the procedure.\\n\\nWhich one of the following structures is least likely to be encountered on left carotid endarterectomy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures is least likely to be encountered on left carotid endarterectomy?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Facial vein\",\"e\":\"The facial vein drains into the internal jugular vein. The internal jugular vein is identified and retracted during the procedure and facial vein may be ligated and divided to mobilise the internal jugular vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pleural membranes\",\"e\":\"Pleural membranes are usually a little deeper and lateral to the site of carotid endarterectomy (CEA).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"sympathetic chain fi\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic trunk lies alongside the cervical vertebrae, immediately behind the carotid artery, and has three cervical ganglia (superior, middle and inferior).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thoracic duct\",\"e\":\"During a left carotid endarterectomy (CEA), the thoracic duct may be seen.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerve travels in the carotid sheath in the neck. ===\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":60,\"stem\":\"A 63-year-old woman undergoes a subtotal thyroidectomy for a toxic multinodular goitre Which one of the following statements is most likely to be true?\",\"lead\":\"A 63-year-old woman undergoes a subtotal thyroidectomy for a toxic multinodular goitre Which one of the following statements is most likely to be true?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Damage to the external laryngeal nerve causes loss of low pitched phonation\",\"e\":\"The superior thyroid artery arises from the external carotid artery and enters the upper pole of the thyroid gland close to the external laryngeal nerve, which supplies the cricothyroid muscle, a tensor of the vocal cord. Damage to this nerve causes the loss of high-pitched phonation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The inferior thyroid artery arises from the origin of the external carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The inferior thyroid artery, absent in 5%, arises from the thyrocervical trunk of the subclavian artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The inferior thyroid artery should be ligated in continuity before the inferior thyroid veins\",\"e\":\"Mass ligation should not be performed because of the risk of injury to the recurrent laryngeal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The isthmus lies anterior to the thyroid cartilage\",\"e\":\"The isthmus is normally inferior to the thyroid cartilage and in front of the second and third tracheal rings, although variations are common.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The superior thyroid artery enters the upper pole of the thyroid gland close to the recurrent laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The superior thyroid artery arises from the external carotid artery and enters the upper pole of the thyroid gland close to the external laryngeal nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"A patient requires thyroidectomy for a histologically confirmed papillary carcinoma. The procedure is straightforward with a good oncological outcome Which one of the following is true regarding the thyroid gland?\",\"lead\":\"The procedure is straightforward with a good oncological outcome Which one of the following is true regarding the thyroid gland?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Derives its arterial supply solely from branches of the external carotid\",\"e\":\"While the superior thyroid artery is a branch of the external carotid artery, the inferior thyroid artery arises from the subclavian artery, via the thyrocervical trunk. Additionally. the thyroidea ima artery (present in 5-10% of the population) arises from the aortic arch, the brachiocephalic artery or even the internal mammary artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Has a venous plexus draining into the internal jugular and brachiocephalic veins\",\"e\":\"The thyroid venous plexus usually drains via three pairs of veins: the superior and middle thyroid veins drain into the internal jugular; the inferior thyroid veins drain into the brachiocephalic vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Initially moves down on swallowing\",\"e\":\"The thyroid gland initially moves up on swallowing before returning to its normal position.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lies superficial to the myofascial layer in the neck\",\"e\":\"The thyroid gland lies deep to the myofascial layer (platysma, strap muscles and investing layer of deep cervical fascia) closely applied to the thyroid and cricoid cartilages. Note that during thyroidectomy, the strap muscles should be divided in their upper half, to avoid damaging the ansa cervicalis which is in the lower half.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Receives 20% of the total cardiac output\",\"e\":\"The thyroid gland is highly vascular, normally accounting for 5% of cardiac output.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man is about to undergo a left carotid endarterectomy. The anaesthetist has discussed the anaesthetic options with the patient and they have decided to attempt a cervical plexus block. Which one of the following nerves lie superficial to the sternocleidomastoid muscle and may be affected by this block?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following nerves lie superficial to the sternocleidomastoid muscle and may be affected by this block?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Greater occipital nerve\",\"e\":\"The greater occipital nerve arises from the medial branch of the dorsal primary ramus of C2. Its course is through the trapezius muscle before reaching the occiput.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal nerve\",\"e\":\"The hypoglossal nerve lies in the upper part of the carotid sheath, deep to the sternocleidomastoid.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Phrenic nerve\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve arises from the ventral rami of C3-C5, and lies superficial to the anterior scalene muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Suprascapular nerve\",\"e\":\"The suprascapular nerve arises from the brachial plexus and lies deep to trapezius muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transverse cervical nerve\",\"e\":\"Local anaesthetic can be injected along the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid (between the superior and inferior thirds) - the nerve point of the neck for a cervical plexus block. The transverse cervical nerve emerges as a single trunk behind the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid and is superficial to the muscle.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old woman undergoes a thyroidectomy for papillary carcinoma of the thyroid gland. During this procedure the inferior thyroid arteries are identified and ligated lateral to the gland From which vessel does the inferior thyroid artery arise?\",\"lead\":\"During this procedure the inferior thyroid arteries are identified and ligated lateral to the gland From which vessel does the inferior thyroid artery arise?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Common carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The inferior thyroid artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"External carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The superior thyroid artery is the first branch of the external carotid artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The subclavian artery gives off the thyrocervical trunk, which in turn gives off the inferior thyroid artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thyrocervical trunk\",\"e\":\"The inferior thyroid artery is a branch of the thyrocervical trunk, which arises from the subclavian artery. The inferior thyroid artery supplies the inferior pole of the thyroid gland and gives off oesophageal branches. In addition, it supplies both the superior and inferior. parathyroid glands. The course of the inferior thyroid artery is closely related to the recurrent laryngeal nerve: in a thyroidectomy, therefore, it is preferable to ligate the artery lateral to the gland to avoid neural damage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyroid ima artery\",\"e\":\"In around 10% of people, a thyroid ima artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk or the arch of the aorta.\"}]},{\"t\":55,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"A 19-year-old man is rushed to theatre after being stabbed in the neck during an alleged assault. He is bleeding profusely and on arrival has a pulse of 130 beats/min and blood pressure of 76/40 mmHg. In theatre, he is given 4 units of blood and surgical exploration shows a laceration to the external carotid artery. At what level does the common carotid artery bifurcate into the external and internal carotid arteries?\",\"lead\":\"At what level does the common carotid artery bifurcate into the external and internal carotid arteries?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Level of the cricoid cartilage S\",\"e\":\"The bifurcation is at the level of the thyroid (not the cricoid) cartilage:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Level of the hyoid bone\",\"e\":\"The hyoid bone is the position at which the external carotid artery crosses the hypoglossal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Level of the jugular notch\",\"e\":\"The jugular or suprasternal notch is at T2 vertebral level and, as such, is significantly inferior to the bifurcation of the common carotid artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lower border of the thyroid cartilage\",\"e\":\"The carotid artery divides at the level of the upper border, not the lower border, of the thyroid cartilage\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Upper border of the thyroid cartilage\",\"e\":\"The carotid artery divides into the external and internal carotid branches at the upper border of the thyroid cartilage, which is around the level of the fourth cervical vertebra.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"The uterus is a thick-walled muscular organ of the female reproductive tract that lies in the lesser pelvis. Which one of the following statements is correct with respect to this organ?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct with respect to this organ?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The uterine anatomy consists of twe layers of tissue: the myometrium and endometrium\",\"e\":\"The uterine wall consists of three main layers which are (from inner to outermost) the endometrium, myometrium and perimetrium.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The uterine cervix is anterior in relation to the rectum\",\"e\":\"The uterine cervix is located posterior to the urinary bladder and anterior to the rectum.\\n\\nUterus\\n\\nRectum Bladder Urethra Vagina\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The lymphatic drainage of the uterine fundus is via the internal and external iliac nodes\",\"e\":\"The lymphatic drainage of the uterus is as follows:\\n• fundus: para-aortic nodes\\n• body and cervix: internal and external iliac nodes; superficial inguinal nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The ovarian ligament is the main ligament that provides structural support to the uterus\",\"e\":\"The pelvic floor musculature provides the primary support to the uterus. The ligaments which provide secondary support are the broad ligament, round ligament, ovarian ligament, cardinal ligament and uterosacral ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Only the superior aspect of the uterus is covered by peritoneum\",\"e\":\"The uterus is covered by peritoneum anteriorly and superiorly except for the vaginal part of the cervix.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant in the urology clinic. The first patient presents with erectile dysfunction. The transmission of semen occurs via the ejaculatory duct into which part of the male reproductive system?\",\"lead\":\"The transmission of semen occurs via the ejaculatory duct into which part of the male reproductive system?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Membranous urethra\",\"e\":\"The membranous urethra is the narrowest part of the male urethra. It is responsible for voluntary continence.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Prostatic urethra\",\"e\":\"The prostatic urethra is the most proximal segment of the male urethra. It is a common site of bladder outflow obstruction but also receives the ejaculatory duct. The ejaculatory ducts are paired structures of the male reproductive system that convey seminal fluid. They course through the central zone of the prostate, opening into the prostatic urethra. They are labelled below and can be seen opening in to the prostatic urethra at the vermontanum, either side of the prostatic utricle.\\n\\nPrepubic angle\\n\\nEjaculatory duct / Infrapubic angle\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Spongy urethra\",\"e\":\"The spongy urethra is the most distal and longest part of the male urethra, located immediately inferior to the corpora cavernosa and does not receive semen from the ejaculatory duct.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lateral lobes of the prostate gland\",\"e\":\"The prostate is a fibromuscular and glandular organ that consists of four lobes: anterior. posterior, median and lateral. The lateral lobes lie on either side of the urethra\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Duct of the bulbourethral glands\",\"e\":\"The bulbourethral or Cowper's gland are a pair of small exocrine glands located in the urogenital diaphragm that produce an alkaline mucous secretion before ejaculation that drains into the spongy urethra to neutralise traces of acidic urine in the urethra.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 19-year-old man attends the Day Surgery for an elective open repair of a right inguinal hernia. During the procedure, the spermatic cord is encountered Which one of the following structures is not within the contents of the spermatic cord?\",\"lead\":\"During the procedure, the spermatic cord is encountered Which one of the following structures is not within the contents of the spermatic cord?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve is contained within the spermatic cord.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ilioinguinal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve arises from the L1 nerve root along with the illohypogastric nerve and tends to lie on the cord but not within it.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pampiniform plexus 2 Y\",\"e\":\"The pampiniform plexus lies within the cord.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Testicular artery\",\"e\":\"The testicular artery is one of three arteries that lie within the spermatic cord.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ductus deferens\",\"e\":\"The ductus deferens is a recognised content of the spermatic cord.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old man presents with a 7-month history of a penile lesion. The lesion is biopsied and he is diagnosed with squamous-cell carcinoma of the penis. He undergoes staging, which is later discussed in the urology multi-disciplinary team meeting. A pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan confirms lymph node involvement\\n\\nWhich lymph nodes are most likely to be involved?\",\"lead\":\"Which lymph nodes are most likely to be involved?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"External iliac lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"There are about 8-10 external iliac lymph nodes. These receive efferent lymphatics from the inguinal nodes, the lymphatics of the iliac fossa, and the lower anterior abdominal wall and afferent lymphatics from the pelvic viscera.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Internal iliac lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The internal iliac lymph nodes receive afferents from the pelvic viscera. Their efferents pass to the common iliac nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Para-aortic lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The testes drain into the para-aortic lymph nodes, as the lymphatics follow the testicular vessels back to the aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sacral lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The sacral lymph nodes are a posterior structure and are not involved in draining the penile region\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superficial inguinal lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The superficial inguinal lymph nodes primarily drain the external genitalia of the male.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a short history of bright red per rectum (PR) bleeding. She is not known to have diverticular disease. A digital rectal examination (DRE) is performed with her consent.\\n\\nOn performing a DRE on a woman, which anatomical structures are commonly palpable?\",\"lead\":\"On performing a DRE on a woman, which anatomical structures are commonly palpable?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cervix\",\"e\":\"The cervix is an anterior relation of the rectum. On DRE, the structures that can be palpated in either sex are: the coccyx and lower sacrum posteriorly; the ischial spines and ischiorectal fossas are lateral; and the anorectal ring at the anorectal junction, In men, the prostate can be felt (but normal seminal vesicles are not usually palpable). In women, the cervix can be felt through the vaginal wall, with the uterosacral ligaments laterally, and sometimes the ovaries.\\n\\nUterus\\n\\nRectum Bladder\\n\\nUrethra Vagina\\n\\nteaches tony..\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ischial tuberosity\",\"e\":\"The ischial tuberosities are impalpable during a DRE\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Vesicouterine pouch\",\"e\":\"The vesicouterine pouch is not a direct anatomical relation of the rectum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Seminal vesicles V\",\"e\":\"Females do not possess seminal vesicles\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ureter\",\"e\":\"The female vagina and uterus are related posteriorly to the bladder, and hence prevent direct contact between the rectum and the bladder.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A patient is undergoing a HALO procedure for haemorrhoids and you want to ensure you place your ligating suture at the correct place. The anal canal measures about 3.5-4 cm in length and is bounded proximally by the anorectal junction (the proximal ends of the anal clumns) and distally by the anal verge.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding the anal canal?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the anal canal?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The dentate (pectinate) line separates the anal canal into upper and lower parts however the neurovascular supply remains the same throughout\",\"e\":\"The neurovascular supply is distinct in both parts reflecting the different embryological origins. Above the pectinate line, it is innervated by the inferior hypogastric plexus. Below the pectinate line, the innervation is provided by the inferior rectal nerves.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The arterial supply above the dentate line is provided by the inferior rectal artery and below the dentate line by the superior rectal artery\",\"e\":\"Arterial supply:\\n• above the dentate line: superior rectal artery (from ima)\\n• below the dentate line: inferior rectal artery (from the internal pudendal artery).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The rectum contains distinct taeniae coli\",\"e\":\"Unlike other portions of the colon, the rectum does not have distinct taeniae coli. The taeniae blend with one another in the sigmoid co on five centimeters above the rectum, giving rise to a layer of longitudinal muscle that surrounds the rectum on all sides for its entire length.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Has fibres from the sacral sympathetic outflow to cause internal sphincter contraction\",\"e\":\"Innervation above the dentate line and to the internal anal sphincter is attributed to the pelvic plexus (sympathetic) and parasympathetic with afferent sensory contributions (pelvic splanchnic nerves)\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Venous drainage of the anal canal above the dentate line is via the superior rectal veins into the inferior mesenteric vein\",\"e\":\"Venous drainage above the dentate line occurs via the superior rectal vein into the inferior mesenteric vein. Below the dentate line, venous drainage occurs via the inferior rectal vein into the internal pudendal vein.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A patient presents to clinic with weight loss and a change of bowel habit. You perform a rectal examination and are concerned to feel a hard mass Which one of the following options best describes the rectum?\",\"lead\":\"You perform a rectal examination and are concerned to feel a hard mass Which one of the following options best describes the rectum?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arises as a continuation of the sigmoid colon at the level of s1\",\"e\":\"The sigmoid colon loses its mesentery and becomes the rectum at the level of S3.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Has a well developed mesentery, the mesorectum\",\"e\":\"There is no rectal mesentery - the mesorectum is a clinical term for the visceral pelvic fascia that contains pararectal lymph nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has lymph drainage to the internal illac and inferior mesenteric group of nodes\",\"e\":\"The lymphatic drainage is divided into the upper third and lower two-thirds.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is supplied by the middle and inferior rectal arteries, but not the superior\",\"e\":\"Arterial supply of the rectum is by all three rectal arteries: superior, middle and inferior. The blood supply is predominantly from the superior rectal artery, with variable contributions from the middle and inferior rectal vessels. The inferior rectal vessels may often provide a significant blood supply to the rectum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is covered by peritoneum along its entire length\",\"e\":\"The upper third of the rectum is covered by peritoneum anteriorly and laterally; the middle third only anteriorly and the lower third is not covered by any peritoneum.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"You are called by an FY2 doctor who is having difficulty inserting a urethral catheter into a man with urinary retention. As you are providing urological cover you are expected to be able to catheterise the patient. Which one of the following statements best describes the male urethra?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements best describes the male urethra?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The membranous urethra does not pass through the urogenital diaphragm\",\"e\":\"The membranous urethra does pass through the urogenital diaphragm, surrounded by the external urethral sphincter. The external urethral sphincter is distal to the vermontanum, so during a trans-urethral resection of the prostate the resection is limited to the verumontanum distally to avoid incontinence.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The fossa navicularis' is a normal dilatation of the proximal penile urethra\",\"e\":\"The fossa navicularis' is a normal small dilatation found in the distal penile urethra.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has penile, membranous and prostatic parts\",\"e\":\"The male urethra is typically divided into three parts: prostatic, membranous, and penile (or spongy). In the below image, the prostatic is in blue, membranous in green and spongy in red\\n\\nPrepubic angle\\n\\nEjaculatory duct\\n\\nInfrapubic angle\\n\\n© TeachMoAnatomy\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is entirely lined with transitional-cell epithelium\",\"e\":\"The proximal urethra is lined with pseudostratified columnar epithelium, whereas distally it is lined by squamous epithelium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is narrowest at the prostatic part\",\"e\":\"The urethra is widest at the prostatic part.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old man is about to undergo an elective transurethral resection of bladder tumour (TURBT) Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the urinary bladder?\",\"lead\":\"A 68-year-old man is about to undergo an elective transurethral resection of bladder tumour (TURBT) Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the urinary bladder?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is lined by peritoneum on all its surfaces\",\"e\":\"Only the superior surface and some of the base of the urinary bladder are covered by peritoneum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Vesicoureteric reflux cannot be attributed to dysfunction of the bladder trigone\",\"e\":\"Dysfunction of the bladder trigone can result in vesicoureteric reflux.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Detrusor muscle contraction is initiated via sympathetic innervation to the bladder\",\"e\":\"The parasympathetic system is responsible for innervation of the detrusor muscle which causes contraction.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Venous drainage of the bladder is via the vesical venous plexus\",\"e\":\"The urinary bladder is drained by the vesical venous plexus lying on the inferolateral surface. Veins from this venous plexus then drain into the internal iliac veins:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Receives its main arterial supply from the obturator and inferior aluteal arteries\",\"e\":\"Arterial supply of the bladder:\\n• superior vesical artery\\n• inferior vesical artery\\n• smaller branches: uterine artery (women), obturator artery. internal pudendal artery.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old motorist is brought to the Emergency Department following a high-speed head-on collision. He is resuscitated and proceeds to undergo pelvic radiography. His radiograph reveals a comminuted fracture of the left acetabulum. Which bones form the acetabulum?\",\"lead\":\"Which bones form the acetabulum?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cłl Ilium and pubic bone\",\"e\":\"The ilium and pubic bone are involved in forming the acetabulum but they form an incomplete socket for the femur without the ischium\\n\\nThe hip is a 'ball-and-socket' joint, the head of the femur being the 'ball' and the 'socket' formed by three separate bones: illum, ischium and pubis\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ilium and ischium\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ilium, ischium and pubic bone\",\"e\":\"The ilium, ischium and pubis together form the acetabulum. It can be fractured in road traffic accidents when vehicles collide head-on, driving the engine and steering column into the lower limbs, which drives the femur posteriorly into the acetabulum with force, causing a fracture.\\n\\nlium\\n\\nTriradiate cartilage Pubis\\n\\nIschium\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ilium, ischium and sacrum\",\"e\":\"The sacrum is not involved in the acetabulum and is a posterior structure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ischium and pubic bone\",\"e\":\"The ilium is missing in this combination.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old man presents with a long history of alcohol excess and insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus. He presents with a blood pressure of 110/80 mmHg and a tachycardia of 115 beats/min. He is treated for septic shock and examination confirms a large ischiorectal abscess. He undergoes emergency incision and drainage under general anaesthetic. Post-operatively, he complains of faecal incontinence.\\n\\nWhich nerve supplies fibres to the external anal sphincter?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve supplies fibres to the external anal sphincter?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Genitofemoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The genitofemoral nerve is formed by the branches of the anterior rami of L1 and L2 spinal nerves of the lumbar plexus. It divides into the genital and femoral branches just above the inguinal ligament. In men, it travels with the spermatic cord and supplies motor fibres to the cremaster and dartos muscles. It also supplies sensory fibres to the spermatic fasciae and tunica vaginalis of the testis. In addition, it supplies sensory innervation to the upper part of the scrotum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Post-ganglionic parasympathetic nerves\",\"e\":\"Post-ganglionic parasympathetic nerves that pass through the pelvic splanchnic nerves supply the internal anal sphincter, which is involuntary. This sphincter relaxes in response to pressure generated by faces or gas, which distends the rectal ampulla.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pudendal nerve\",\"e\":\"The external anal sphincter is a voluntary muscle innervated by the somatic inferior rectal nerves, originating from the pudendal nerve. The pudendal nerve arises from the S2-S4 nerve roots of the anterior division of the sacral plexus. It is responsible for innervation of the perineum and the pelvic floor. The internal anal sphincter is involuntary and is innervated by post-ganglionic parasympathetic fibres. Severe, untreated perianal sepsis can lead to sphincter dysfunction and faecal incontinence. Faecal incontinence can also be a complication of drainage of an abscess in that region, which results in damage of the external anal sphincter.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"ilioinguinal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve arises from the L1 nerve root and is predominantly sensory. In men - cutaneous sensation to the root of the penis and anterior scrotum. In women - cutaneous sensation to the proximal medial thigh, mons pubis and labia majora.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Obturator nerve\",\"e\":\"The obturator nerve is responsible for motor innervation of:\\n• adductors: adductor magnus, longus and brevis\\n• gracilis\\n• obturator externus\\n\\nIn terms of sensory innervation, its cutaneous branch supplies the skin of the middle part of the medial thigh.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old woman presents with a 2-week history of bright red rectal bleeding and general malaise. On digital rectal examination (DRE), a firm mass is palpable in the proximal rectum. A rectal biopsy confirms the presence of an invasive rectal adenocarcinoma and she proceeds to abdominoperineal resection.\\n\\nWhich group of lymph nodes primarily drain the lower part of the anal canal?\",\"lead\":\"Which group of lymph nodes primarily drain the lower part of the anal canal?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"External iliac lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The external iliac lymph nodes sit above the pelvic brim, along the external iliac vessels. It receives lymph from:\\n• the leg and buttocks - via superficial and deep inguinal nodes\\n• superior aspect of the bladder.\\n\\nThey do not play a role in lymph drainage of the anal canal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"internal illac lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The internal iliac lymph nodes are found along the internal iliac vessels. They are responsible for the drainage of the\\n• gluteal region\\n• inferior pelvic viscera\\n• deep perineum\\n\\nSubsequently, it drains into the common iliac nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sacral lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The sacral lymph nodes receive lymph from the rectum, bladder, prostate or cervix and the posterior pelvic wall.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Para-aortic lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"Para-aortic nodes drain the iliac nodes, which receive lymph from the pelvis and lower limb In addition, it drains the adrenal glands, kidneys and gonads.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superficial inguinal lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The superficial inguinal lymph nodes run parallel to and below the inguinal ligament and vertically along the great saphenous vein: in the perineum, it drains the lower part of the anal canal and the vagina. It is also involved in drainage of external genitalia (excluding the testes), lower abdominal wall below the umbilicus and lower limb. The pelvic part of the rectum and the anal canal represent different lymph drainage territories. The pectinate line separates the two territories, but communication does exist between them (ie the lymphatics of the anal canal communicate with the perineal and rectal lymphatics). The lymph of the perineum and the anal canal is drained to the superficial inguinal lymph nodes. whereas the lymph of the pelvic territory flows to iliac and lumbar nodes.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman presents with acute onset severe right iliac fossa pain and hypovolaemic shock. b-Human chorionic gonadotrophin levels are significantly raised and she is diagnosed with an ectopic pregnancy\\n\\nWhich is the most likely location for an ectopic pregnancy?\",\"lead\":\"Which is the most likely location for an ectopic pregnancy?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ampulla of the Fallopian (uterine) tube\",\"e\":\"The majority of ectopic pregnancies (80%) occur in the ampullary portion of the Fallopian (uterine) tubes. The uterine (or Fallopian) tube begins at the infundibulum and progresses medially to include the ampulla, the isthmus and the intramural portion.\\n\\nIsthmus Ampulla\\n\\nInfundibulum\\n\\nFimbriae\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AM Fimbriae\",\"e\":\"Only 5% of ectopic pregnancies occur here and therefore is not the most likely site of implantation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ovary\",\"e\":\"In total, 0.2% of all ectopic pregnancies occur here. Along with the cervix, it is the most unlikely site of implantation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Isthmus of uterine tube\",\"e\":\"The isthmus is the second most common site of ectopics at 12%.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Abdominal cavity\",\"e\":\"Only 1.4% of all ectopic pregnancies occur within the abdominal cavity.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old professional cyclist who has recently competed in the Tour de France presents to the Urology Clinic complaining of penile and scrotal numbness which improves slightly on standing and lying down. He reports urinary frequency and episodes of urinary incontinence. Urine dipstick is negative.\\n\\nWhich nerve has been injured?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve has been injured?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Genitofemoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The genitofemoral nerve is formed by the branches of the anterior rami of L1 and L2 spinal nerves of the lumbar plexus. It divides into the genital and femoral branches just above the inguinal ligament. In men, it travels with the spermatic cord and supplies motor fibres to the cremaster and dartos muscles. It also supplies sensory fibres to the spermatic fasciae and tunica vaginalis of the testis. In addition, it supplies sensory innervation to the upper part of the scrotum. Damage to this nerve could result it numbness of the upper scrotum but it would not result in any urinary incontinence.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Obturator nerve\",\"e\":\"The obturator nerve has both motor and sensory innervation. It is responsible for motor innervation of:\\n• adductors: adductor magnus, longus and brevis\\n• gracilis\\n• obturator externus:\\n\\nIn terms of sensory innervation, its cutaneous branch supplies the skin of the middle part of the medial thigh. It does not play a role in cutaneous innervation of the man genitalia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ilioinguinal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve innervates the anterior surface of the scrotum or labia majora, root of the penis or mons pubis as well as the anterior portion of the upper medial thigh. It is not involved in urinary flow or sphincter innervation\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pudendal nerve\",\"e\":\"The pudendal nerve is formed by the ventral rami of the sacral spinal nerves from S2, S3 and S4. It maintains voluntary urinary continence by facilitating contraction of the external urethral sphincter. It also helps to maintain voluntary faecal continence via contraction of the external anal sphincter. Damage to this nerve could result in urinary or faecal incontinence. Cyclists may develop pudendal nerve entrapment syndrome (also known as Alcock canal syndrome), which can present with various symptoms including pain, urinary and faecal incontinence and sensory disturbances of the perineum and genitalia due to irritation. entrapment or damage of the pudendal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prostatic nerve plexus\",\"e\":\"The prostatic nerve plexus contain cavernous nerves which provide parasympathetic innervation. It is responsible for the vascular changes which result in erection:\\n• 'P' - Parasympathetic = Point (erection).\\n• 'S' - Sympathetic = Shoot (ejaculation).\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man presents with acute urinary retention: After further questioning, he reveals a 3-month history of hesitancy, dribbling and poor flow. On catheterisation with a Foley catheter, significant resistance is encountered and a curved-tipped catheter is attempted instead. It is important to understand the male anatomy to avoid unnecessary trauma, which can often result in strictures or false passages. Which is the narrowest part of the normal male urethra?\",\"lead\":\"Which is the narrowest part of the normal male urethra?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Membranous\",\"e\":\"The membranous urethra measures about 1.5-2 cm in length and is the shortest, least dilatable and the narrowest part of the man urethra, often resulting in resistance on catheterisation. The male urethra is a narrow fibromuscular tube through which urine and semen from the bladder and ejaculatory ducts travel. It usually measures between 16-20 cm in length. It is generally divided into three parts from proximal to distal:\\n• prostatic (blue in the image)\\n• membranous (green)\\n• spongy or penile (red).\\n\\nPrepubic angle\\n\\nEjaculatory duct\\n\\nInfrapubic angle\\n\\nTeachMeAnatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Penile\",\"e\":\"The penile urethra is the longest part of the urethra, measuring about 15 cm in length. It is the first site to encounter a catheter, but does not often cause difficulties unless there are strictures present requiring dilatation under local or general anaesthetic using metal dilators.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Internal urethral sphincter\",\"e\":\"The internal urethral sphincter is an involuntary sphincter that controls the bladder neck and the prostatic urethra above the openings of the ejaculatory ducts.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prostatic\",\"e\":\"The prostatic urethra is about 3 cm in length and is lined by transitional epithelium. It is the widest and most distensible part of the man urethra hence should not produce any resistance during catheterisation in a patient with a normal prostate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Verumontanum\",\"e\":\"The prostatic urethra runs through the prostate gland, dividing the prostate into a left and right lateral lobe. The paired ejaculatory duct perforates the prostate and opens into the middle of the prostatic urethra at the verumontanum. Note that the vermontanum is not part of the urethra, but is the collective name for the ductile entrances into the prostatic urethra.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"A 38-year-old man is involved in a high-speed road traffic accident (RTA) involving a car and a lorry during which he sustained a crush injury affecting his right hemi-pelvis. He is left with long-term sensory deficit affecting the majority of his right leg. A magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan confirms the clinical findings that damage has been sustained to the\\n\\nWith regards to the sacral plexus, which one of the following statements is true?\",\"lead\":\"With regards to the sacral plexus, which one of the following statements is true?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The spinal nerves L4-S4 form the basis of the sacral plexus\",\"e\":\"The sacral plexus is derived from the anterior rami of the L4-$4 spinal nerves. At each sacral vertebral level, paired spinal nerves leave the spinal cord via the anterior sacral foramina. These are joined by the lumbosacral trunk (formed by the anterior rami of the L4-L5 spinal nerves) to form the sacral plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is joined by the lumbosacral trunk posterior to piriformis\",\"e\":\"The sacral nerves are joined by the lumbosacral trunk anterior to the piriformis, which is made up of the 4th and 5th lumbar roots. This descends into the pelvis to meet the sacral roots as they emerge from the spinal cord.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"it is formed from the anterior division of the ist sacral nerve and posterior divisions of the 2nd and 3rd sacral nerves\",\"e\":\"The sacral plexus is formed by the lumbosacral trunk and the anterior divisions of the sacral spinal nerves S1, S2, S3 and S4\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh is a branch\",\"e\":\"The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh is a branch of the lumbar plexus arising from the dorsal branches of the second and third lumbar nerves, not the sacral plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The inferior gluteal nerve passes through the greater sciatic foramen above piriformis\",\"e\":\"The inferior gluteal nerve, posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh and pudendal nerves pass through the greater sciatic foramen below piriformis. It is responsible for innervation of gluteus maximus and has no sensory function. The following nerves pass through the greater sciatic foramen:\\n• ABOVE piriformis - superior gluteal nerve\\n• BELOW piriformis - inferior gluteal nerve, posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh and pudendal nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man presents to the Urgent Care Centre with a 3-month history of a palpable lump in his right groin. He is concerned this might be cancerous. On examination, there is a palpable smooth lump in the femoral triangle.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true of the femoral triangle?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true of the femoral triangle?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The floor or the femoral triangle is formed by the pectines, lopsoas and adductor magnus muscles\",\"e\":\"The femoral triangle has a roof and a floor:\\n• roof of the femoral triangle: fascia lata\\n• floor of the femoral triangle: pectineus, iliopsoas and adductor longus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The lateral border of the femoral triangle is formed by the medial border of the inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"There are three borders of the femoral triangle: superior, medial and lateral (shown in the image below):\\n• superior - inguinal ligament\\n• medial - medial border of adductor longus\\n• lateral - medial border of sartorius.\\n\\nInguinal Ligament\\n\\nSartorius\\n\\nAdductor Longus\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The genitofemoral and ilioinguinal nerve are contents of the femoral triangle\",\"e\":\"The contents of the femoral trianglet do not include the ilioinguinal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The femoral artery, vein and nerve are contained within a fascial compartment called the femoral sheath\",\"e\":\"The femoral sheath contains the femoral cana, femoral vein and femoral artery (from medial to lateral). It does not contain the femoral nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Clinically, a palpable lump found within the femoral triangle with no systemic symptoms in a woman patient is most likely to be a femoral hernia\",\"e\":\"Femoral hernias are palpable lumps/ bulges found within the femoral triangle and are more common in women. They are differentiated from inguinal hernias by being found below and lateral to the pubic tubercle. They are caused by herniation of abdominal contents through the femoral canal (shown in purple in the image below). The management is surgical in nature. Other differential diagnoses of a femoral hernia include: inguinal hernia, saphena varix, lymphadenopathy, lipoma, femoral aneurysm, sarcoma, ectopic testes and obturator hernia.\\n\\nFemoral nerve Femoral canal\\n\\nGreat saphenous vein Femoral sheath\\n\\ntrachnicanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"A 73-year-old man with symptomatic benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) has not had any improvement following a year of medical therapy. Due to the large size of his prostate gland, he is listed for an elective Millin's open prostatectomy. Which one of the following statements is true regarding the prostate gland?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding the prostate gland?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lymph drainage is to the deep inguinal lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The lymphatic drainage of the prostate primarily drains to the obturator and internal iliac lymphatic channels. There is also lymphatic communication with the external iliac, presacral and para-aortic nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Histologically, the prostate gland is divided into four zones\",\"e\":\"The prostate gland is histologically divided into three zones:\\n• central zone\\n• peripheral zone\\n• transitional zone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is commonly attributed to hyperplastic of prostatic tissue which occurs primarily in the peripheral zone of the prostate\",\"e\":\"The peripheral zone is commonly associated with malignant cells resulting in prostate carcinoma, whereas the transitional zone is where BPH occurs:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is supplied by a branch of the external iliac arteries\",\"e\":\"The arterial supply of the prostate gland is derived from the prostatic arteries, which originate from the internal iliac arteries, not the external iliac arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Venous drainage of the prostate gland is via the prostatic venous plexus, which drains into the internal iliac veins\",\"e\":\"The prostatic venous plexus drains the prostate gland. This plexus drains into the internal iliac veins and also has connections to the internal vertebral venous plexus and the Batson's venous plexus.\\n\\n_ Fibromuscula\\n\\nneck Peripheral толо\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":54,\"stem\":\"The lumbosacral plexus is formed by the ventral rami of T12 - S3, and gives off several peripheral nerve branches,\\n\\nWhich branch originates from L2-L4?\",\"lead\":\"Which branch originates from L2-L4?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The sciatic nerve. C-\",\"e\":\"The sciatic nerve originates from nerve roots L4 to S3. It is the largest nerve in the body and innervates the muscles in the posterior compartment of the thigh and the hamstring portion of adductor magnus. Its major branches are the\\n• tibial nerve: L4-S3\\n• common peroneal nerve: L4-S2.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The genitofemoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The genitofemoral nerve is formed by the branches of the anterior rami of L1 and L2 spinal nerves of the lumbar plexus. It divides into the genital and femoral branches just above the inguinal ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve arises from posterior divisions of L2-L4 roots of the lumbar plexus. It emerges from the lateral border of the psoas muscle to descend between the iliacus and psoas muscles, and sends a motor branch to iliacus before passing under the inguinal ligament. Its course below the inguinal ligament is shown below - it terminates as the cutaneous saphenous nerve.\\n\\nFemoral Marve Femoral artery\\n\\nAnterior cutaneous branches\\n\\nSaphenous nerve (branch of femoral)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The superior gluteal nerve\",\"e\":\"The superior gluteal nerve arises from nerve roots L4-S1. It supplies branches to the gluteus medius and minimus and terminates by innervating the tensor fasciae latae muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The inferior gluteal nerve\",\"e\":\"The inferior gluteal nerve arises from the posterior divisions of L5-S2 nerve roots: It supplies gluteus maximus. A posterior approach to the hip in surgical procedures may result in damage to the inferior gluteal nerve either by dissection of the nerve or by stretching and retraction of the nerve\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old man is brought to the Emergency Department having been stabbed multiple times. His injuries are extensive and he is in hypovolaemic shock. The Anaesthetic Registrar asks you to perform venepuncture via a femoral stab. The femoral artery is the main artery of the lower limb. Which branch of the femoral artery supplies the femoral neck and may result in avascular necrosis of the femoral head if damaged?\",\"lead\":\"Which branch of the femoral artery supplies the femoral neck and may result in avascular necrosis of the femoral head if damaged?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Deep circumflex iliac artery\",\"e\":\"The deep circumflex iliac artery arises from the external iliac artery. It is responsible for arterial supply of the iliacus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Profunda femoris artery To\",\"e\":\"The profunda femoris artery (deep femoral artery - shown in green below) arises from the posterolateral aspect of the common femoral artery (after it branches, the common femoral artery becomes simply the femoral artery). Its main branches are the medial and lateral circumflex arteries (shown in yellow), which supply the femoral head via retinacular arteries that travel in the joint capsule. If damaged, ischaemia and avascular necrosis of the femoral head may result\\n\\nMedial circumflex femoral artery\\n\\nLateral circumflex femoral artery Femoral artery \\\"Profunda femoris artery\\n\\nAdductor hiatus\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anterior branch of the obturator artery\",\"e\":\"This branch supplies the pectineus, obturator externus, adductor muscles and gracilis. It is not involved in supplying the femoral head or neck\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior branch of the obturator artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior branch of the obturator artery supplies some of the deep gluteal muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inferior epigastric artery\",\"e\":\"The inferior epigastric artery arises from the external iliac artery and primarily supplies the anterior abdominal wall. It is not involved in supplying the femur. The surface marking of the femoral artery is the mid-inguinal point [half-way between pubic symphysis and anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)], which lies just medial to the position of the deep inguinal ring. which is at the midpoint of the inguinal ligament (half-way between pubic tubercle and ASIS). The femoral artery is subcutaneous in the femoral triangle, separated from skin by the fascia lata.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":60,\"stem\":\"During total hip arthroplasty via the posterior approach in the gluteal region you are asked to locate the sciatic nerve and describe its course. Which one of the following statements is true with regards to the sciatic nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true with regards to the sciatic nerve?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The sciatic nerve originates from nerve roots L3-S4\",\"e\":\"The sciatic nerve is derived from the lumbosacral plexus. The nerve roots of the sciatic nerve are L4-S3, not L3-S4.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"As it descends through the gluteal region, the sciatic nerve crosses the anterior surface of the obturator internus, superior and inferior gemellus and quadratus femoris muscles\",\"e\":\"The sciatic nerve crosses the posterior surface of the obturator internus, superior and inferior gemellus and quadratus femoris muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The sciatic nerve is the largest nerve in the body, often described as being 'band-like' and measuring approximately 2 cm wide\",\"e\":\"The sciatic nerve is indeed the largest nerve in the body and with a width of about 2 cm. it has a 'band-like' appearance. Its course is as follows:\\n• it travels from the pelvis into the gluteal region through the greater sciatic foramen\\n• travels inferiorly to the piriformis muscle (although this is variable and the nerve can pass through piriformis or occasionally above piriformis)\\n• enters posterior thigh, passing deep to the long head of the biceps femoris\\n• bifurcates at the apex of the popliteal fossa into: tibial nerve and common fibular (peroneal) nerves.\\n\\nThe sciatic nerve is a major nerve of the lower limb. It is derived from the lumbosacral plexus. It is shown emerging from beneath piriformis in the image below.\\n\\nPiriformis\\n\\nSciatic nerve Long head of the biceps femoris\\n\\nCommon fibular nerve Tibial nerve\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The sciatic nerve bifurcates into the tibial and common fibular (peroneal) nerves at the medial aspect half-way through the popliteal fossa\",\"e\":\"The bifurcation of the sciatic nerve marks an important anatomical landmark and occurs at the apex of the popliteal fossa. Here it becomes the tibial and common fibular (peroneal) nerves. It is worth noting that although the point of bifurcation is described as the apex of the popliteal fossa it too is variable and can occur anywhere throughout the posterior thigh.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The sciatic nerve enters the posterior thigh by passing superficial to the long head of the biceps femoris\",\"e\":\"The sciatic nerve passes deep to the long head of the biceps femoris, not superficially.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old woman presents to her GP with a long history of menorrhaga, lethargy and dysmenorrhea. Blood tests confirm she is anaemic. An ultrasound scan confirms the presence of uterine fibroids and the GP starts her on medication to improve her symptoms.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding the female reproductive tract?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding the female reproductive tract?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The bood supply to the uterus is via the ovarian artery\",\"e\":\"The uterine artery which is a branch of the internal iliac artery supplies the uterus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The venous drainage of the uterus is via the accessory vaginal veins\",\"e\":\"The uterus is drained by the uterine vein which forms the uterine venous plexus alongside the cervix. The plexus then drains into the internal iliac veins.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lymph from the ovaries drains into the iliac and sacral lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"Lymphatic drainage of the ovaries is via the para-aortic nodes. Lymphatic drainage of the uterus is via the iliac, sacral, aortic and inguinal lymph nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Anatomically. the cervix is divided into two regions, the endocervix (endocervical canal) and the ectocervix. The endocervix is lined by columnar epithelial cells\",\"e\":\"The cervix is composed of two regions:\\n• Endocervix - proximal part of the cervix lined by simple columnar epithelium. The endocervical canal is contained within the internal os and the external os.\\n• Ectocervix - distal part of the cervix which starts at the external os and projects into the vagina. It is lined by stratified squamous non-keratinised epithelium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The nerve supply to the lower vagina is primarily from the sympathetic nervous system\",\"e\":\"The nerve supply to the lower vagina is primarily from somatic nerves. The pudendal nerve gives rise to sensory nerve fibres of the lower vagina whereas the sacral nerve roots give rise to pain fibres.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old woman presents with pain and paraesthesia of the left buttock. Which nerve accompanies the piriformis muscle as it exits the pelvis, and is involved in 'piriformis syndrome\\\"?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve accompanies the piriformis muscle as it exits the pelvis, and is involved in 'piriformis syndrome\\\"?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve arises from the posterior divisions of L2-L4 roots of the lumbar plexus and provides motor supply to the anterior compartment of the thigh and sensory supply to the hip, anterior and medial thigh, knee and leg. It is not closely related to the piriformis\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior gluteal nerve\",\"e\":\"The inferior gluteal nerve is closely related to the sciatic nerve to which it is medial and posterior as it exits the pelvis inferior to the piriformis muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pudendal nerve\",\"e\":\"The pudendal nerve arises from the anterior division of the ventral rami of S2-S4. It emerges from the pelvis and courses through the gluteal region through the greater sciatic foramen, below the piriformis muscle. It is not however involved in 'piriformis syndrome'.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"sciatic nerve\",\"e\":\"Piriformis syndrome is a entrapment neuropathy of the sciatic nerve resulting in radicular pain radiating into the buttocks. Paraesthesia may also result from prolonged or more severe entrapment. The sciatic nerve itself emerges from beneath piriformis (although occasionally through or even from above it), as seen in the image\\n\\nPiriformis\\n\\nSciatic nerve Long head of the biceps femoris\\n\\nCommon fibular nerve Tibial nerve\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior gluteal nerve\",\"e\":\"The superior gluteal nerve is formed from the L4 - S1 nerve roots and is responsible for the motor supply to the gluteus medius and minimus. It exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, passing over the piriformis muscle. ==-\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"An 89-year-old woman patient is admitted following a low-impact fall at home. Imaging confirms a mid-shaft fracture of the femur secondary to osteoporosis. In mid-shaft femoral fractures, the proximal fragment becomes abducted and externally rotated due to the action of which muscle inserting at the greater trochanter?\",\"lead\":\"In mid-shaft femoral fractures, the proximal fragment becomes abducted and externally rotated due to the action of which muscle inserting at the greater trochanter?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gluteus medius\",\"e\":\"The gluteus medius is the major hip abductor and inserts into the posterolateral surface of the greater trochanter of the femur. It is supplied by the superior gluteal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Obturator externis\",\"e\":\"The obturator externus laterally rotates the thigh at the hip joint and inserts at the greater trochanter. However. it is not responsible for abduction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Obturator internis\",\"e\":\"Obturator internus is responsible for the lateral rotation of the extended hip joint and abduction of the flexed hip. It is innervated by the nerve to obturator internus and inserts into the medial surface of the greater trochanter\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Piriformis\",\"e\":\"The piriformis muscle laterally rotates the extended hip joint and inserted into the medial side of the superior border of the greater trochanter.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Quadratus femoris\",\"e\":\"Quadratus femoris is a lateral hip rotator as well as an adductor. It does not play a part in abduction of the hip\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":70,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old man requires fixation of a pelvic fracture via the posterior approach. During the procedure, an anatomical structure is noticed, which the consultant refers to as 'structure X. Passing through it are the superior and inferior gluteal vessels and nerves, the sciatic nerve, the pudendal nerve, the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh, and the nerve to obturator internus. Identify structure X.\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Greater sciatic foramen\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Infrapiriform foramen\",\"e\":\"The infrapiriform foramen contains the\\n• inferior gluteal vessels\\n• internal pudendal vessels\\n• nerves of the sacral plexus.\\n• inferior gluteal nerve\\n• sciatic nerve\\n• pudendal nerve\\n• posterior femoral cutaneous nerve\\n• nerve to obturator internus\\n• nerve to quadratus femoris.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lesser sciatic foramen\",\"e\":\"The lesser sciatic foramen is the space between the sacrospinous and sacrotuberous ligaments. It contains: the pudendal nerve; the nerve and tendon to obturator internus, and the internal pudendal vessels The pudendal nerve and the internal pudendal vessels exit the pelvis via the greater sciatic foramen and enter the perineum through the lesser sciatic foramen. Similarly, the nerve to the obturator internus leaves the pelvis via the greater sciatic foramen and soon re-enters the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen.\\n\\nThe obturator foramen is a large ovoid aperture located at the anterior aspect of both sides of the pelvis covered by a membrane. the obturator membrane. At its superior margin, the membrane is devoid forming the obturator canal, which allows the obturator nerve, artery and vein to exit the pelvis and enter the medial compartment of the thigh.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Obturator foramen\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Suprapiriform foramen\",\"e\":\"The suprapiriform foramen contains the superior gluteal vessels and nerve only.\"}]},{\"t\":56,\"p\":73,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman presents to her general practice surgery. On questioning, she is troubled by urinary incontinence, which occurs on increasing intra-abdominal pressure. She has previously had three normal vaginal deliveries. She is also found to have a body mass index (BMI) of 30. You diagnose her with stress urinary incontinence and recommend a bladder diary as well as three months of pelvic floor exercises. Which one of the following statements are correct regarding the pelvic floor?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements are correct regarding the pelvic floor?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The levator ani muscles are innervated by branches of the genitofemoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The branches of the pudendal nerve innervate the three muscles which make up the levator ani, not the genitofemoral nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The levator ani muscle is composed of the pubococygeus, puborectalis and ilio coccygeus muscles\",\"e\":\"The levator ani is a large sheet of muscles made up of three paired muscles, which are the pubococcygeus, puborectalis and iliococcygeus muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The puborectalis muscle contracts during defecation\",\"e\":\"The puborectalis muscle is key in maintaining faecal continence. It relaxes during defaecation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The strength of the peivic floor muscles is not affected by obesity, number of vaginal deliveries and family history of pelvic floor dysfunction\",\"e\":\"The pelvic floor plays a major role in supporting the pelvic viscera. Factors that can damage the pelvic floor include\\n• gynaecological surgery/colorectal surgery\\n• number of vaginal deliveries\\n• overweight/ obesity\\n• family history of muscular/connective tissue disorders.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The pelvic floor is made up of three components: levator ani muscles, sacrococcygeus muscle and surrounding fascia\",\"e\":\"The three components of the pelvic floor are: levator ani muscles, coccyges muscle and fascia coverings of the muscles.\"}]},{\"t\":57,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old professional cyclist complains of scrotal and penile paraesthesia and an unusual sensation during ejaculation. The affected nerve arises from which roots?\",\"lead\":\"The affected nerve arises from which roots?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"L2-L4\",\"e\":\"L2-L4 is the origins of the femoral and obturator nerves which wouldn't be affected by cycling.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"L3-L5\",\"e\":\"The above symptoms are due to entrapment of the pudendal nerve which arises from S2-4.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"S1-S2\",\"e\":\"S1-52 is the origins of the piriformis nerve and entrapment of this nerve which wouldn't cause the above symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"S2-54\",\"e\":\"The pudendal nerve is being trapped and it arises from S2-4.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"3-S5\",\"e\":\"The pudendal nerve arises from S2-54 rather than S3-$5\\n\\nRegular cyclists are at risk of pudendal nerve entrapment (also known as Alcock canal syndrome). The pudendal nerve arises at the S2-S4 roots and supplies sensory and somatic fibres to the external genitalia and the urinary and anal sphincters. Other than the symptoms described in the scenario above, pathology of the nerve can lead to urinary and faecal incontinence.\"}]},{\"t\":57,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old man presents with severe perianal swelling and discomfort. A magnetic resonance imaging scan of his pelvis reveals an abscess in the ischiorectal fossa. Which muscle lies superior and medial to his abscess?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscle lies superior and medial to his abscess?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bulbospongiosus\",\"e\":\"Bulbospongiosus is not related to the ischiorectal fossa:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Levator ani\",\"e\":\"The ischiorectal fossa is a prism-shaped potential space adjacent with its inferior aspect formed by perineal skin. Levator ani lies medial and superior to the fossa\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Obturator internus\",\"e\":\"Obturator internus is found lateral to the ischiorectal fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spinter ani internus\",\"e\":\"The sphincter ani internus is found medial to the ischiorectal fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transversus perinei superficialis\",\"e\":\"Transversus perinei superficialis is anterior to the ischiorectal fossa.\\n\\nThe ischiorectal fossa is a prism-shaped potential space adjacent with its inferior aspect formed by perineal skin. Levator ani lies medial and superior to the fossa, which is also bordered by gluteus maximus (posterior), ischial tuberosity and obturator internus (lateral) and transversus perinea superficialis (anterior).\"}]},{\"t\":57,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"You are incising and draining a perianal abscess and the consultant asks you about the anal canal and its anatomical relations.\\n\\nHow would you best describe the canal?\",\"lead\":\"How would you best describe the canal?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The canal lies above the levator ani muscle\",\"e\":\"The levator ani forms part of the deep external anal sphincter and the canal lies below this muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The canal has a longitudinal muscular coat\",\"e\":\"The anal canal has no longitudinal muscular coat.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The canal has a lymphatic drainage via the inguinal lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"Lymph from the lower anal canal drains via the superficial inguinal nodes. The levator ani forms part of the deep external anal sphincter and the canal lies below this muscle. The anal canal has no longitudinal muscular coat. Lymph from the lower anal canal drains via the superficial inguinal nodes. The external anal sphincter is innervated by the inferior rectal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4), while the internal sphincter receives a sympathetic supply from the pelvic plexus. The upper anal canal is thrown into vertical folds called anal columns, seen below in the image. Above the dentate line, which is visible in the image as a wavy line at the level of the internal anal sphincter, the canal is lined by columnar epithelium, below this level this changes to squamous epithelium.\\n\\nAnal canal\\n\\nExternal anal Internal anal sphincter sphincter\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The canal has an external sphincter innervated by sympathetic fibres from the pelvic plexus\",\"e\":\"The external anal sphincter is innervated by the inferior rectal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4), the internal sphincter receives a sympathetic supply from the pelvic plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The canal is lined by columnar epithelium below the dentate line\",\"e\":\"Above the dentate line the canal is lined by columnar epithelium, below this level this changes to squamous epithelium.\"}]},{\"t\":57,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old man is admitted for an elective abdominoperineal (AP) resection for rectal carcinoma. What relationship does the rectum have to the peritoneum?\",\"lead\":\"What relationship does the rectum have to the peritoneum?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Peritoneum covers the posterior and lateral surfaces of the rectum in its superior two-thirds and just posteriorly in its inferior third\",\"e\":\"There is no peritoneal coverage on the rectum's posterior surface, nor in its inferior third.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Peritoneum covers the anterior and lateral surfaces of the rectum in the upper third and anteriorly in its middle third\",\"e\":\"The rectum is a retroperitoneal structure and therefore has at least one surface that is not covered by peritoneum. The rectum sits behind and below the peritoneal cavity with its upper third covered by peritoneum on the anterior and lateral surfaces, and the middle third only on the anterior surface. There is no peritoneum on the rectum's posterior surface or its inferior third\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Peritoneum covers the anterior and lateral surface of the rectum in the upper two-. thirds and the anterior wall in its lower third\",\"e\":\"There is no peritoneum covering the rectum's inferior third\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Peritoneum covers the posterior and lateral surface of the rectum in its upper third and the posterior wall only in its middle third\",\"e\":\"There is no peritoneal coverage on the rectum's posterior surface.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The rectum is intraperitoneal\",\"e\":\"The rectum is a retroperitoneal structure.\\n\\n3760\"}]},{\"t\":57,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old presents with a testicular lump on the left. The lump is around 1cm in the mid-testes, non-painful and firm What is the lymphatic drainage of the testes?\",\"lead\":\"The lump is around 1cm in the mid-testes, non-painful and firm What is the lymphatic drainage of the testes?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Drains to the pararectal lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The lymioh drainage follows the blood supply, which comes directly from the aorta, unlike the skin of the scrotum, which drains into inguinal nodes. The drainage does not pass posteriorly to rectal lymph drainage.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Drains to the prostatic lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"Although the urethra passes through the prostate, the lymph drainage of the testes passes to the para-aortic nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Drains to both para-aortic and inguinal lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The skin of the scrotum drains into the inguinal lymphatics whereas the testes themselves drain into the para-aortic lymph. If a malignancy invades both these structures, then it is possible for both lymphatics to be involved.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Drains to the inguinal lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The scrotum lymph drains into the inguinal nodes and follows its cutaneous blood suppy. The testes lymph follows the blood supply to the para-aortic lymph nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Drains to the para-aortic lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"During development, the testes descend downwards and drag with them the blood supply and lymphatic drainage. This means malignancy that spreads to local lymph nodes will be present in para-aortic nodes, relatively concealed compared with inguinal lymphadenopathy.\"}]},{\"t\":57,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old young man presents to A&E after slipping off a 'slack-line' with trauma to his perineum.\\n\\nWhat is the nerve innervation of the testis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the nerve innervation of the testis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ts\",\"e\":\"T& innervates the lower rib margin.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"TiO\",\"e\":\"The testes share T10 innervation with the ureter and the umbilicus, which is why testicular pain can often be felt in the abdomen and ureteric colic is sometimes accompanied by perception of pain in the testes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerve innervates the abdominal viscera and is involved in the parasympathetic system.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"L2=5\",\"e\":\"L2-5 provide innervation to the thigh and leg.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"2-4\",\"e\":\"S2-4 innervate the posterior leg, sitting area and penis/labia.\"}]},{\"t\":57,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 10-year-old boy is admitted with acute testicular pain and a diagnosis of torsion is made. He is listed for emergency scrotal exploration to preserve his future fertility.\\n\\nWhat is the correct order of layers one would pass through when making an incision through the scrotum to the testes?\",\"lead\":\"What is the correct order of layers one would pass through when making an incision through the scrotum to the testes?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Skin, dartos fascia (a continuation of Scarpa's fascia), external spermatic fascia, cremaster muscle and fascia, internal spermatic fascia, parietal layer of tunica vaginalis, visceral layer of tunica vaginalis, tunica albuginea of testis\",\"e\":\"This is correct - the layers of the abdominal wall extend to the scrotum as the testes descend during development.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"external spermatic rascia, cremaster muscle and rascia, internal spermatic rascia, tunica albuginea of testis\",\"e\":\"The external and internal spermatic fascia are derived from external oblique and transversalis fascia layers and, therefore, are situated superficial to the tunica vaginalis layers.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Skin, external spermatic fascia, cremaster muscle and fascia, internal spermatic fascia, dartos fascia, parietal layer of tunica vaginalis, visceral layer of tunica vaginalis, tunica albuginea of testis\",\"e\":\"Dartos fascia is a continuation of Colles fascia and is located between the skin/subcutaneous layers and the deeper muscle layers.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Skin, dartos fascia, external spermatic fascia, cremaster muscle and fascia, internal spermatic fascia, tunica albuginea of testis. parietal layer of tunica vaginalis, visceral layer of tunica vaginalis\",\"e\":\"The tunica albuginea is the white layer surrounding the testes themselves and therefore is the last layer encountered\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Skin, dartos fascia, external spermatic rascia, cremaster muscle and rascia, internal spermatic fascia, visceral layer of tunica vaginalis, parietal layer of tunica vaginalis. tunica albuginea of testis\",\"e\":\"The parietal layer is always situated on the outside of the visceral layer and therefore this answer is incorrect.\"}]},{\"t\":57,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman is referred by the A&E SHO with an irreducible rectal prolapse. The transverse perineal muscles lie where?\",\"lead\":\"The transverse perineal muscles lie where?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Posterior to the anus\",\"e\":\"The transverse perineal muscles lie anterior to the anus and posterior to the urethra, passing from the ischial tuberosities through a central tendinous portion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the anus and anterior to the urethra\",\"e\":\"The ischiocavernosus muscles pass anterior to both structures whereas the transverse perineal muscles lie between these structures.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Between the urethra and the anus\",\"e\":\"The transverse perineal muscles do indeed pass between the two structures into a central tendinous portion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lateral to the urethra\",\"e\":\"Lateral to the urethra are the bulbospongiosus muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Both anterior and posterior to the anus\",\"e\":\"The levator ani muscles lie lateral to the anus, both anteriorly and posteriorly, whereas the transverse perineal muscles form a slip of muscle between the rectum and the urethra.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a right hemiarthroplasty for a patient with a neck of femur fracture. Your consultant is asking you about the nerve supply of the lower limb Which one of the following is true regarding the lumbosacral plexus?\",\"lead\":\"Your consultant is asking you about the nerve supply of the lower limb Which one of the following is true regarding the lumbosacral plexus?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The posterior rami supply the adductor muscles\",\"e\":\"The adductor muscles are supplied by the L2-L4 roots of the lumbar plexus. They are supplied by anterior divisions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The lumbosacral trunk lies anterior to the piriformis muscle\",\"e\":\"The lumbosacral trunk (L4/L5) passes anterior to the ala of the sacrum, under cover of the common iliac vessels, and joins the S1 ventral ramus anterior (deep) to the piriformis muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The lumbosacral trunk passes over the common iliac vessels\",\"e\":\"The lumbosacral trunk passes under the common iliac vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The parasympathetic nerves originate from S4/S5 spinal segments\",\"e\":\"The splanchnic nerves also known as the nervierigentes originate from S2, 53 and often S4\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The sympathetic trunk sends grey rami to only S1-S5.\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic grey rami connect with all spinal nerve roots.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old woman presents with neurogenic claudication and bilateral buttock pain which worsens with prolonged standing and improves with sitting. Which structure of the lumbar spinal canal is most likely to be responsible for her spinal stenosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure of the lumbar spinal canal is most likely to be responsible for her spinal stenosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Normal facet joints\",\"e\":\"Normal facet joints would have no role in spinal stenosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Normal intervertebral disc anteriorly\",\"e\":\"A normal intervertebral disc would not cause spinal stenosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Synovial facet cysts posteriorly\",\"e\":\"Synovial facet cysts are found posterior to the spinal canal.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The ligamentum flavum posteriorly\",\"e\":\"The ligamentum flavum can thicken and hypertrophy, narrowing the spinal canal causing spinal stenosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The posterior longitudinal ligament posteriorly\",\"e\":\"The posterior longitudinal ligament lies anterior to spinal canal.\\n\\nThe spinal canal is anterior to the ligamentum flavum but posterior to the intervertebral vertebral discs and posterior longitudinal ligament. The spinal cord ends at L1.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old woman on home O_ for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) has sustained a neck of femur fracture. The anaesthetist has decided a spinal anaesthetic will be safer.\\n\\nWhat vertebral level does the spinal cord and at in adults?\",\"lead\":\"What vertebral level does the spinal cord and at in adults?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"E1=t2\",\"e\":\"The spinal cord typically terminates at L1-L2 in adults.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"L3-L4\",\"e\":\"In neonates and infants the spinal cord ends at levels L3.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"1- $2\",\"e\":\"The spinal dura mater ends at S2.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"tF=TS8\",\"e\":\"T8 is where the inferior vena cava (IVC) passes through the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Til. - Ti2\",\"e\":\"The aorta passes through the diaphragm at T12.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"One of your patients has a CT scan, which confirms a spinal tumour at the level of the bifurcation of the trachea. An image-guided biopsy is to be performed Which spinal level corresponds to the bifurcation of the trachea?\",\"lead\":\"An image-guided biopsy is to be performed Which spinal level corresponds to the bifurcation of the trachea?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lower border C7 vertebra\",\"e\":\"The lower border of C7 vertebrae is too high for the bifurcation of the trachea\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Upper border T2 vertebra\",\"e\":\"The upper border of the T2 vertebrae is too high for the bifurcation of the trachea.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lower border T2 vertebra\",\"e\":\"The trachea bifurcates lower than the lower border of the T2 vertebrae.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Upper border T3 vertebra\",\"e\":\"The trachea bifurcates lower than the upper border of the T3 vertebrae.\\n\\nThe manubriosternal joint (Angle of Louis) acts as a surface landmark for the tracheal bifurcation at T4. The cricoid cartilage is at the C6 level. The thyroid isthmus normally lies over the second and third tracheal rings and hence is a safe place for an emergency surgical airway. The common carotid artery bifurcates into the external and internal carotid arteries the upper border of the thyroid cartilage (C4).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lower border T4 vertebra\",\"e\":\"The trachea bifurcation is at the level of the lower border of the T4 vertebrae.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"You perform a peripheral neurological examination and detect several abnormalities. You think the lesion is likely to be affecting the spinothalamic tract. Which one of the following types of nerve fibre does the spinothalamic tract carry?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following types of nerve fibre does the spinothalamic tract carry?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Descending motor fibres\",\"e\":\"The spinothalamic tract doesn't contain any descending motor fibres.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"First order fibres carrying light touch and proprioception\",\"e\":\"Spinothalamic tract contains second order fibres carrying pain, light touch and temperature sensation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"First order fibres carrying pain and temperature sensation\",\"e\":\"It is the second order fibres carrying pain and temperature sensation that are contained in the spinothalamic tract.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Second order fibres carrying light touch and proprioception\",\"e\":\"Spinothalamic tract does contain second order fibres but they carry pain and temperature sensation rather than vibration and proprioception. The spinothalamic tract conveys pain, temperature, crude touch and pressure sensations from one side of the body to the contralateral side of the brain. Vibration and joint position sense (proprioception) are conveyed via the posterior column. The first neurone of the spinothalamic tract synapses in the dorsal horn; the next neurone crosses to the contralateral side of the spinal cord and synapse in the thalamus; the third neurone arises in the thalamus to pass to the cortex\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Second order fibres carrying pain and temperature sensation\",\"e\":\"The spinothalamic tract does contain the second order fibres that carry pain and temperature sensation.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old scaffolder falls from a height of ten metres and complains of back pain. On examination, he is unable to flex his knee, but can extend it following passive flexion. At which of the following vertebral levels has there been an injury?\",\"lead\":\"At which of the following vertebral levels has there been an injury?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"L1\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"L3\",\"e\":\"The knee extensors are controlled by L2, L3 and L4 (femoral nerve). Therefore the lesion must be below this level.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"L5\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"S2\",\"e\":\"If the lesion was as low as 52 he would have been able to flex the knee.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T12\",\"e\":\"If the lesion was at T12 he wouldn't be able to extend his knee either. The quadriceps (knee extensors) comprise rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis and vastus intermedius. These muscles are innervated by fibres originating from the posterior division of ventral rami of the femoral nerve (L2, 3, 4). The hamstrings (long head of biceps femoris, semitendinosus and semimembranosus) are supplied by the tibial component of the sciatic nerve (L5, S1), except the short head of biceps femoris, which is supplied by the common peroneal branch. The hamstrings flex the knee joint.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man is admitted to A&E after a fall from a first-floor roof. He has a CT scan, which demonstrates transection of the spine at the level of T11. What is the lowest point at which you would expect him to have normal sensation?\",\"lead\":\"What is the lowest point at which you would expect him to have normal sensation?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Nipples\",\"e\":\"The nipples are innervated by T4\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Costal margin\",\"e\":\"The costal margin is innervated by T7-8 and there is marked overlap of the skin innervation across the thorax:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Above the umbilicus!\",\"e\":\"The periumbilical area is innervated by T10 and, as with all dermatomes, there is significant overlap between adjacent zones. Although he will probably have sensation here, it is unlikely to be the lowest area.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Periumbilical\",\"e\":\"The periumbilical region is innervated by T10 and therefore probably the lowest area that will not be affected by T1l transection. Remember that the spinal cord itself finishes below this level in the adult, at around L1-2.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Upper thighs\",\"e\":\"The upper lower limbs are innervated by L1-2 with some overlap with lower thoracic dermatomes. Given the transection at T11, it is very unlikely that he will have any sensation at this level.\\n\\nIt is very important to know the dermatomes, especially the key, relatively consistent landmarks associated with them. Neurological function is an important although often neglected area of clinical examination due to its perceived complexity, but a good basic knowledge makes it easier.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old woman undergoes a knee arthroscopy under regional epidural anaesthesia. What is the location of the internal venous plexus of the spine?\",\"lead\":\"What is the location of the internal venous plexus of the spine?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Around the outside of cach vertebra\",\"e\":\"The valveless, internal, vertebral venous plexus sits within the vertebral canal, not outside it.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Between the bone and the extradural fat\",\"e\":\"The plexus does sit within the spinal canal but not outside the fat; it is instead within the fat.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Within the extradural fat\",\"e\":\"The extradural, or epidural, fat is the location for introducing epidural anaesthesia and where the epidural catheter should be sited. Within this area lies a complex varied plexus of veins without valves.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Between the dura and arachnoid mater:\",\"e\":\"This is the location of a potential space but, in reality, there is little space within it\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"In the subarachnoid space\",\"e\":\"This is the location of the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) rather than where the venous plexus is located\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old retired office worker is seen in outpatients with back pain on rotation.\\n\\nWhich vertebrae have a predominately coronal orientation of their articular facets?\",\"lead\":\"Which vertebrae have a predominately coronal orientation of their articular facets?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cervical\",\"e\":\"The cervical articular facets are relatively horizontal\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Thoracic\",\"e\":\"The vertical positioning, along with the coronal orientation of the thoracic vertebral articular facets, allows a large degree of rotation in this region of the spine.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lumbar\",\"e\":\"Although lumbar vertebral articular facets are vertical, they are positioned in a more parasagittal plane, which allows lateral and forward flexion/extension.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sacral\",\"e\":\"The sacrum is almost completely fused in adults, so it has little articulation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"All of the above\",\"e\":\"There are key variations in the vertebral regions and it is important to be able to identify each vertebra by its distinct features and its purpose.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man is admitted for a laminectomy of L4. The procedure is straightforward and the patient's symptoms have abated when they are reviewed in the follow up outpatient Clinic.\\n\\nWhere are the laminae of each vertebra located?\",\"lead\":\"Where are the laminae of each vertebra located?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the vertebral body between the transverse processes and the vertebral body\",\"e\":\"The arch of the vertebrae, which protects the spinal cord and its associated structures, lies posterior to the vertebral bodies.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Posterior to the transverse processes, between them and the spinous process\",\"e\":\"The laminae lie on both sides of the vertebral arch, between the transverse processes and the spinal process. The pedicles complete the arch and are between the transverse processes and the body.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the transverse processes, between them and the vertebral body\",\"e\":\"Anterior to the transverse processes sit the pedicles of the vertebral arches. There are two pedicles in each arch, and also two laminae.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior to the vertebral body between it and the transverse process\",\"e\":\"The pedicles sit anterior to the transverse processes, whereas the laminae sit posteriorly to the transverse processes to complete the arch of each vertebra.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Directly between the vertebral body and the spinous process\",\"e\":\"The arch of each vertebra is made from pedicles that attach to the transverse processes; these then, in turn, attach to the laminae and the central and posterior spinous processes. The body and the spinous processes are therefore not directly attached\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A rugby player is admitted with a suspected cervical injury. A CT scan reveals a possible fracture of the dens, which is known to be weaker than the transverse ligament. Where is the dens located?\",\"lead\":\"Where is the dens located?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"in the posterior half of the spinal canal, with the transverse ligament anterior to it\",\"e\":\"The dens is essentially a superior extension of the vertebral body of the C2 vertebra, so it is an anterior structure in relation to the spinal cord\\n\\nThe spinal cord sits in the posterior portion of the canal; although the transverse ligament is posterior to the dens, it is between the cord and the dens.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"In the anterior half of the spinal canal, with the transverse ligament posterior to it\",\"e\":\"The dens is anterior in the spinal canal and anterior to the spinal cord. To protect the spinal cord, an exceptionally strong transverse ligament traverses the space between the dens and the cord\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In the posterior half of the spinal canal with the transverse ligament posterior to it\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"In the anterior half of the spinal canal with the transverse ligament anterior to it\",\"e\":\"The transverse ligament protects the cord from the dens by being between the two structures, posterior to the dens:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"In the centre of the spinal canal with the transverse ligament posterior to it\",\"e\":\"The dens takes up a third of the anteroposterior space of the spinal canal and the cord another third. The remaining third, or two-sixths, are either side of the cord to prevent compression on movement. The dens does not sit in the centre of the spinal canal.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"You are a surgical senior house officer (SHO) helping a group of medical students revise spinal anatomy for their finals. You describe a triangular, sheet-like structure that supports the head and is attached from the skull to the spinous processes of vertebrae down to C7.\\n\\nWhich one of these is most likely to be the structure described?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is most likely to be the structure described?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ligamentum flavum\",\"e\":\"This structure (plural ligamenta flava) passes between the laminae of adjacent vertebrae on each side and are thin broad ligaments forming part of the posterior surface of the vertebral canal. They are primarily formed from elastic tissue fibres.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ligamentum nuchae\",\"e\":\"The ligamentum nuchae is the structure described here - a triangular, sheet-like structure in the median sagittal plane. The base of the triangle is attached from the external occipital protuberance to the foramen magnum, and the apex to the tip of the spinous process of C7.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"supraspinous ligament\",\"e\":\"This connects the tips of the vertebral spinous processes from C7 to the sacrum. This would have been an understandable response to the question as the ligament becomes known as the ligamentum nuchae above the level of C7. However, ligamentum nuchae is a more accurate answer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tectorial membrane of the atlanto-axial joint\",\"e\":\"The tectorial membrane is within the vertebral canal and is the upper part of the posterior longitudinal ligament connecting C2 to the base of the skull. It does not extend to C7. This is not to be confused with the tectorial membrane of the inner ear.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior longitudinal ligament\",\"e\":\"This lines the anterior surfaces of the vertebral canal and is attached to the vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs. The upper part of the posterior longitudinal membrane is known as the tectorial membrane. It does not attach to the spinous processes of the spine.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"A 24-year-old man is brought into the Emergency Department via ambulance after a high-speed road traffic collision. His neck is secured using triple immobilisation as per advanced trauma life support (ATLS) protocol. His cervical spine X-rays are brought up, to examine the spinal column for any fractures. Which structure in the cervical spine is characterised by a prominent odontoid process known as the dens?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure in the cervical spine is characterised by a prominent odontoid process known as the dens?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"Explanation a\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cl\",\"e\":\"Vertebra C1 is also known as the atlas and articulates with the head. It is distinguished by the fact that it lacks a vertebral body - the vertebral body of Ci fuses onto the body of C2 during development to become the dens of C2.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The atlas\",\"e\":\"The atlas is an alternative name for the C1 vertebra.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Alar ligaments\",\"e\":\"The alar ligaments are strong attachments that connect the dens to the medial surfaces of the occipital condyle. The dens is not formed from them however.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"c2\",\"e\":\"Vertebra C2 is also known as the axis, and is characterised by a prominent odontoid process called the dens, which acts as a pivot allowing the atlas and a person's head to rotate on the axis. It is best assessed on X-ray using an anteroposterior (AP) open mouth view.\\n\\nVertebra C1 is also known as the atlas and articulates with the head. It is distinguished by the fact that it lacks a vertebral body - the vertebral body of C1 fuses onto the body of C2 during development to become the dens of C2.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Atlanto-occipital joint\",\"e\":\"The atlanto-occipital joint allows the head to nod. It is a synovial joint, which is the articulation between the atlas and the occipital bone. It is not directly related to the dens Atlanto-occipital dislocation is a serious injury in which there is a ligamentous separation of the spinal column from the skull. Most cases are fatal.\\n\\n3917\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with signs of meningitis and he is now being assessed for a lumbar puncture At which of the following points does the spinal cord terminate in most adult patients?\",\"lead\":\"A 23-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with signs of meningitis and he is now being assessed for a lumbar puncture At which of the following points does the spinal cord terminate in most adult patients?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"L1/L2\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"L2/L3\",\"e\":\"Although the spinal cord can and as low as the disc between L2 and L3, this is less common than at L1/L2.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"L3/L4\",\"e\":\"L3/L4 is below the level at which the spinal cord terminates, and so is often used as a safe location to perform lumbar punctures. In neonates the spinal cord generally ends at L3.\\n\\nL4/L5 is below the level at which the spinal cord terminates and is used as a safe point at which to insert a needle for lumbar puncture. In neonates the spinal cord can extend as low as L5, but generally ends at L3.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"L4/L5\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"L5/S1\",\"e\":\"This is below the point at which the spinal cord terminates in both adults and neonates.\"}]},{\"t\":58,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a hemithyroidectomy. As the consultant separates the strap muscles of the neck to expose the thyroid, she points out the hyoid bone, to which they are attached. She asks you about the relation of the cervical column to the hyoid bone. Which vertebral level is most closely related to the hyoid bone?\",\"lead\":\"Which vertebral level is most closely related to the hyoid bone?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"C1\",\"e\":\"C1 is above the hyoid bone. It is more closely related to the base of the nose and the hard palate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"C2\",\"e\":\"In terms of anatomical landmarks, the teeth are closely related to C2\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"cS\",\"e\":\"c3* is related to the mandible and the hyoid bone, so is the correct answer here. The strap muscles, or infrahyoid muscles either originate from or insert onto the hyoid bone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"ca\",\"e\":\"C4 is more closely related to the upper aspect of the thyroid cartilage (the Adam's apple). which lies between C4 and C5. This C4 level helps to remember that embryologically the thyroid cartilage is a 4th arch derivative. The cricoid cartilage is a 6th arch derivative, and is related to C6.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"C5\",\"e\":\"C5 is more closely related to the lower aspect of the thyroid cartilage.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"You have performed a liver biopsy and shortly after the procedure the patient develops pain\\n\\non the tip of his right shoulder. They are concerned they may have injured the shoulder\\n\\nduring the procedure.\\n\\nWhich nerve is most likely to be responsible for his pain?\\n\\nA Right phrenic nerve\\n\\nAxillary nerve DRIA C Right vagus\\n\\nD Right sympathetic trunk\\n\\nIntercostobrachial nerve E\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Right phrenic nerve\",\"e\":\"The diaphragm is innervated centrally by the left and right phrenic nerves, and peripherally by the inferior intercostal nerves. The phrenic nerves are supplied by the ventral rami of the C5, 4 and 5 nerve roots, carrying both motor and sensory information to and from the diaphragm. They both travel anterior to the hila of the lungs, and over the pericardium - shown in yellow below. These nerve roots also supply cutaneous innervation over the shoulder region, hence irritation of the pleura or peritoneum of the diaphragm causes referred pain to the tip of the shoulder. More inferiorly, diaphragmatic irritation is referred directly to the chest wall via the inferior intercostal nerves. Phrenic nerve supply = C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive.\\n\\nLeft phrenic Pericardium nerve\\n\\nRight dome of the diaphragm\\n\\nteachmeanatomy041\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Axillary nerve\",\"e\":\"The axillary nerve supplies sensation to the upper lateral arm over the deltoid, but does not innervate the diaphragm and therefore is not responsible for the referred pain felt.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Right vagus\",\"e\":\"The vagus is implicated in referred pain, but not in diaphragmatic referred pain, which is mediated by the phrenic nerves. The vagus nerves travel posteriorly to the hila of the lungs in the mediastinum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right sympathetic trunk\",\"e\":\"Diaphragmatic irritation is conveyed back along the phrenic nerve and referred to the cutaneous supply of the nerve roots - C3/4/5. The sympathetic trunk is formed from the T1- L2 spinal segments, with the preganglionic sympathetic neurons travelling with the anterior rami of the T1-L2 spinal nerves and reaching the sympathetic chain via white rami communicantes. Certain organs, such as the viscera of the thorax or abdomen, receive their sympathetic innervation via splanchnic nerves which are derived from these sympathetic fibres in the sympathetic chain, and visceral sensation (eg pain) is conducted back along them via visceral afferent fibres. This is why cardiac pain is referred to the left arm - the heart receives its sympathetic innervation from the T1-T4/5 roots via the cardiac plexus, and these cutaneously supply the chest, shoulder and arm. The visceral pain is referred back along the visceral afferent nerves to the cutaneous dermatome of that spinal root - for cardiac pain, the T1-4 dermatomes.\\n\\nIntercostobrachial nerve E\\n\\nThe intercostobrachial nerve supplies cutaneous sensation to the upper medial arm. It is not responsible for the referred pain described here.\\n\\nThe phrenic nerve on both sides originates from the ventral rami of the third to fifth cervical nerves. It passes inferiorly down the neck to the lateral border of the scalenus anterior, then it passes medially across the border of scalenus anterior parallel to the internal jugular vein that lies inferomedially. The right phrenic nerve pierces the diaphragm in its tendinous portion just slightly laterally to the inferior vena caval foramen. It then forms three branches on the inferior surface of the diaphragm: anterior, lateral and posterior. These ramify out in a radial manner from the point of perforation to supply all but the periphery of the muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Intercostobrachial nerve\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old man who has suffered a myocardial infarction is being reviewed. Occlusion of the posterior descending coronary artery is suspected In this case, which region of myocardium would you expect to be most affected?\",\"lead\":\"Occlusion of the posterior descending coronary artery is suspected In this case, which region of myocardium would you expect to be most affected?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The right atrium\",\"e\":\"The right atrium is supplied by the right coronary artery. Although the right coronary artery gives off the posterior descending artery in around 85% of people (a right-dominant system), occlusion of the posterior descending coronary artery alone would not affect the right atrium.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The right ventricle alone.\",\"e\":\"Occlusion of the posterior descending coronary artery would indeed affect blood supply to the right ventricle (which is supplied by a combination of the posterior descending coronary artery, right marginal and anterior descending artery). However, it would also affect supply to the posterior part of the interventricular septum and the left ventricle. This answer is therefore incorrect.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The anterior septum\",\"e\":\"The anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum is supplied by the anterior descending artery (also known as anterior interventricular) - a branch of the left coronary artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The anterior left ventricular wall\",\"e\":\"The anterior left ventricle is supplied by the anterior descending artery, along with the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum.\\n\\nThe coronary system consists of left and right coronary arteries, which arise immediately above the aortic valve. They are unique in that they fill during diastole, when not occluded by valve cusps and when not squeezed by myocardial contraction. The right coronary artery arises from the right coronary sinus, giving off branches supplying the right atrium and right ventricle. It then continues as the posterior descending coronary artery, which supplies the posterior portion of the interventricular septum and the posterior left ventricular wall. The left coronary artery divides into the left anterior descending (LAD) and circumflex arteries. The LAD runs in the anterior interventricular groove and supplies the anterior septum and the anterior left ventricular wall. The left circumflex artery gives off branches that supply the left atrium and left ventricle. The sinus node is supplied by the right coronary artery in around 60% of people, the arteriovenous (AV) node in around 90%\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The posterior one-third of the interventricular septum and the right and left ventricles\",\"e\":\"The posterior descending coronary artery supplies the posterior one-third of the interventricular septum and the right and left ventricles (coloured grey in the image). The posterior descending coronary artery most commonly originates from the right coronary artery (in around 85% of individuals) - a right-dominant system. It may also arise from the left coronary artery (a left-dominant system), or as a variation of the two. The right coronary artery supplies the SA node in around 60% of people. The right coronary artery arises from the right aortic sinus and travels beneath the right atrial appendage, giving off the right marginal artery, before terminating posteriorly as the posterior descending artery (in around 85% of individuals). The left coronary artery arises from the left coronary cusp and divides into the anterior descending and circumflex branches, with the circumflex giving off the left marginal. The distribution of blood supply to the myocardium is broken down in anterior and posterior views in the images below.\\n\\n......\\n\\nsuckipater your Ritht Coronary Artery Staples the right unrum and the right ventricle centretema\\n\\n®6045\\n\\n© wach tous\\n\\n=\\n\\nand venerele $ Right Coronary Artery |\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"You are asked to see a 45-year-old man who is haemodynamically compromised and plan to insert a right subclavian line. He has a body mass index (BMI) of 38.\\n\\nWhere is the correct position for central venous cannulation?\\n\\nA 3 cm under the mid-point of the clavicle and 0.5 cm laterally\\n\\n1-2 cm below and lateral to the junction between the medial one-third and lateral two-thirds of the clavicle\\n\\nc 2.5 cm under the mid-point of the clavicle and 2 cm laterally\\n\\nD 0.5 cm under the mid-point of the clavicle and 1cm laterally\\n\\n1cm above the mid-point of the clavicle and 1cm medially\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"3 cm under the mid-point of the clavicle and 0.5 cm laterally\",\"e\":\"This is too low an approach that risks piercing the pleura and causing a pneumothorax.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"1-2 cm below and lateral to the junction between the medial one-third and lateral two-thirds of the clavicle\",\"e\":\"Although there are various landmarks used for approaches to the subclavian vein, a commonly used landmark for infra-clavicular subclavian vein cannulation is 1-2 cm inferior\\n\\nand lateral to the clavicular transition point (which is approximately the junction of the medial one-third and lateral two-thirds of the clavicle), with the needle directed towards the suprasternal notch. It is also worth bearing in mind that this procedure is normally performed under ultrasound guidance. Risks include pneumothorax, chylothorax con the left due to damage to the thoracic duct), nerve injury, and arterial puncture. If subclavian vein cannulation is attempted and fails, it is not advised to attempt to place a subclavian line on the opposite side due to the risk of inducing bilateral pneumothoraces. If there is unilateral lung pathology it is usually best to place the subclavian line in the ipsilateral side to avoid the risk of a pneumothorax in the good lung.\\n\\n3 cm under the mid-point of the clavicle and 0.5 cm laterally\\n\\nThis is too low an approach that risks piercing the pleura and causing a pneumothorax.\\n\\nCl2.5 cm under the mid-point of the clavicle and 2 cm laterally\\n\\nThis approach is a very low and lateral approach, and risks causing a pneumothorax.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"2.5 cm under the mid-point of the clavicle and 2 cm laterally\",\"e\":\"This approach is a very low and lateral appre a risks Causing &@ pneumothorax.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"0.5 cm under the mid-point of the clavicle and 1 cm laterally\",\"e\":\"This approach is very close to the clavicle and would make manoeuvring the needle difficult.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"1 cm above the mid-point of the clavicle and 1 cm medially\",\"e\":\"Although a supra-clavicular approach to subclavian vein cannulation is possible, the landmarks would be 1 cm superior and lateral (not medial) to the junction of the lateral aspect of the sternocleidomastoid muscle and the clavicle.\\n\\nIn obese patients, the standard position for right subclavian central venous cannulation is 2 cm under the mid-point of the clavicle and 1 cm laterally. In thin patients the standard position for insertion is 1 cm under the mid-point of the clavicle and 0.5 cm laterally. Jugular vein cannulation is now the preferred choice for central venous catheterisation, as insertion under ultrasound guidance is associated with a much lower rate of complications than subclavian insertion. The major hazard of the subclavian approach is arterial puncture, as the artery lies close to the vein.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a routine pre-operative chest X-ray. An unfamiliar abnormality is visible, and you are unsure what this is so await the radiologist report prior to proceeding with elective surgery. Where would you visualise an azygos lobe on an antero-posterior (A-P) chest X-ray?\",\"lead\":\"Where would you visualise an azygos lobe on an antero-posterior (A-P) chest X-ray?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left lower zone\",\"e\":\"The azygos lobe, if present, is found in the right upper zone.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left middle zone\",\"e\":\"The azygos lobe, if present, is found in the right upper zone rather than the left middle zone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"ORL Left upper zone\",\"e\":\"The azygos lobe, if present, is found in the right upper zone, not the left.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right upper zone\",\"e\":\"The azygos lobe is the commonest accessory lobe, seen in around 1% of people and around 0.4% of chest radiographs. It is an embryological variant, where the azygos vein causes a deep impression in the right upper lobe during development. The lobe is seen superior to the right hilum, separated from the rest of the lung by a groove containing the azygos vein. It is not a true lobe however, as it does not have its own separate bronchus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right lower zone\",\"e\":\"The azygos lobe, if present, is found in the right upper zone, not the lower zone. An azygos lobe is seen in about 0.5% of routine chest X-rays and is a normal variant. It is seen as a 'reverse comma sign' behind the med5znd of the right clavicle.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old previously fit and well man dies suddenly after two separate syncopal episodes. The family are very distressed as no cause of death is obvious. At post mortem examination, which one of the following congenital cardiac defects that ould be associated with his sudden death would you expect to find?\",\"lead\":\"At post mortem examination, which one of the following congenital cardiac defects that ould be associated with his sudden death would you expect to find?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dextrocardia with situs inversus!\",\"e\":\"Although dextrocardia and situs inversus can be associated with other abnormalities such as Kartagener syndrome (bronchiectasis, sinusitis, and situs inversus), it is not commonly associated with sudden cardiac death\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AAN Patent foramen ovale\",\"e\":\"Patent foramen ovale does not cause cardiovascular effects, as atrial pressures are similar between the left and right. It can however lead to paradoxical embolic phenomena such as stroke/TIA. These paradoxical emboli can very rarely cause myocardial infarction (MI) (and therefore potentially sudden death, but would not be associated with syncopal episodes as described.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bicuspid aortic valve\",\"e\":\"Aortic stenosis is associated with 'drop attacks' such as described here, as the stenosed valve causes a fixed limitation on maximal cardiac output which is then unable to maintain cerebral perfusion with peripheral vasodilation. This is also why anaesthesia in patients with severe aortic stenosis is more risky. The classic triad for aortic stenosis is heart failure, angina, and syncope. Bicuspid aortic valves are prone to early calcification, causing aortic stenosis that presents in middle-age rather than in the elderly. They are also associated with sudden death. Bear in mind that the commonest congenital causes of sudden cardiac death are arrhythmogenic conditions such as a long QT interval. Brugada or catecholaminergic polymorphic VT (CPVT).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ventricular septal defect\",\"e\":\"Small ventricular septal defects are usually of no significance and close early in childhood. If large, they lead to shunting of blood from the left to the right side of the heart due to the left-right pressure gradient. If untreated this will eventually progress to Eisenmenger's syndrome (particularly with pulmonary outflow obstruction), in which right-sided heart pressures become higher than left-sided pressures and deoxygenated blood is shunted from right to left. They are not commonly associated with sudden cardiac death.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Single coronary artery\",\"e\":\"Single coronary arteries are extraordinarily rare, and would not be associated with drop attacks as described.\\n\\nWhile isolated dextrocardia can be associated with severe cardiac anomalies, dextrocardia with situs inversus has a low incidence of accompanying defects and the heart functions normally. Isolated patent foramen ovale is of no haemodynamic significance. The relatively common (1-2% of people) bicuspid aortic valve leads to accelerated calcific stenosis, which is associated with syncope and sudden death. Small ventricular septal defects are insignificant, whereas large defects lead to massive left to right shunts. A single coronary artery is not common, but does occur. When present, there is no clinical consequence unless disease (such as atheroma) affects the vessel\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old passenger is involved in a high speed collision with a tree. You suspect a deceleration injury to the aorta Which feature on chest X-ray would most reliably support your suspicion?\",\"lead\":\"You suspect a deceleration injury to the aorta Which feature on chest X-ray would most reliably support your suspicion?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Enhancement of the aortic knuckle\",\"e\":\"Obliteration, not enhancement, of the sortie BT is a sign of aortic injury.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Obscuration of the aortopulmonary window\",\"e\":\"The aortopulmonary (AP) window is an area identified on AP chest radiographs, and can be abnormal with thoracic aortic aneurysms, but is not particularly useful in the identification of aortic injury in the trauma patient.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Presence of a pleural cap\",\"e\":\"An ‘apical pleural cap’, or blood above the apex of the left lung, is a sign of blunt aortic injury, but is less common than a widened mediastinum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Elevation of the left main bronchus\",\"e\":\"Depression, not elevation. of the left main bronchus is an indicator of aortic injury. Depression of the right main bronchus may also be seen in combination with deviation of the trachea to the right.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Widened mediastinum\",\"e\":\"A widened mediastinum should arouse suspicion of aortic injury, and is a sensitive indicator. However, it is non-specific, and aortic injury can occur without evidence of mediastinal widening.\\n\\nChest radiographs are the initial screening investigation performed for the trauma patient with blunt thoracic injuries, however the sensitivity and specificity for detection of aortic injuries is variable and suspicious clinical or radiological findings warrant a chest CT.\\n\\nVarious signs on chest X-ray can arouse suspicion of a blunt aortic injury:\\n• widened mediastinum\\n• obliteration of the aortic knuckle\\n• depression of the left main bronchus\\n• large left-sided haemothorax\\n• pleural cap (blood above the left lung apex)\\n• depression of right bronchus.\\n\\nAl Enhancement of the aortic knuckle\\n\\nobliteration, not enhancement, of the aortic knuckle is a sign of aortic injury.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"You review an elderly patient who has become febrile and hypoxic 2 days post general anaesthetic for a Hartmann's procedure. You hear crepitations in the right lower zone posteriorly and suspect post-operative pneumonia. Which one of the following chest X-ray appearances is most likely?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following chest X-ray appearances is most likely?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Blunting of the costophrenic angles\",\"e\":\"Blunting of the costophrenic angle is seen when fluid is present in the pleural space. This is in contrast to bronchopneumonia, which is characterised by airspace opacification/consolidation. Bear in mind however that parapneumonic pleural effusions or empyemas can occur in conjunction with infection, which would cause blunting of the costophrenic angle, in the trauma patient, blunting of the costophrenic angle should be considered a haemothorax until proven otherwise.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An indistinct right hemidiaphragm\",\"e\":\"This is the most likely answer, given the recent history of general anaesthetic and crepitations in the right lower zone. Lower lobe consolidation typically causes the diaphragmatic borders to become indistinct, in contrast to middle lobe consolidation which causes the right heart border to become indistinct, and lingular consolidation which involves the left heart border.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Kerley B lines\",\"e\":\"Kerley B linest are one of the signs of pulmonary oedema, caused by oedema of the septae between the lung lobules.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Indistinct right heart border\",\"e\":\"This would typically occur with right middle lobe consolidation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Indistinct left heart border\",\"e\":\"This would typically occur with lingular consolidation.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"An obese patient with profound shock requires emergency IV access. The anaesthetic team are able to place a central line, after some difficulty. in the right subclavian vein. Which is the least likely structure to be damaged?\",\"lead\":\"Which is the least likely structure to be damaged?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SPI Thoracic duct\",\"e\":\"Remember the thoracic duct is on the right side of the thorax only up to the level of the sternal angle, where it crosses over to the left. The thoracic duct enters the posterior mediastinum along with the aorta as the continuation of the cisterna chyli. It ascends on the right side between the aorta and the azygos vein until the T4-T5 level (at the manubriosternal angle) where it crosses to the left, draining eventually in to the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. A right-sided subclavian line would not be at risk of damaging the thoracic duct, but a left-sided might, with the subsequent complication of chylothorax.\\n\\nB Brachial plexus\\n\\nThe divisions of the brachial plexus lie beneath the clavicle, and are therefore potentially at risk of damage in subclavian line insertion. Other neural structures at risk include the phrenic\\n\\nnerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Brachial plexus\",\"e\":\"The divisions of the brachial plexus lie beneath the clavicle, and are therefore potentially at risk of damage in subclavian line insertion. Other neural structures at risk include the phrenic nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Subclavian vein\",\"e\":\"The subclavian vein is the target structure for line insertion, and is therefore certainly at risk of damage from the procedure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The subclavian artery lies posterior to the subclavian vein and is therefore potentially at risk.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lung apex\",\"e\":\"Subclavian vein C\\n\\nThe subclavian vein is the target structure for line insertion, and is therefore certainly at risk of damage from the procedure.\\n\\nSubclavian artery D\\n\\nThe subclavian artery lies posterior to the subclavian vein and is therefore potentially at risk.\\n\\nE Lung apex\\n\\nThe apices of the lungs extend 2.5 cm above the medial end of the clavicle, and the pleura lies immediately posterior to the subclavian vein and artery. Pneumothorax is a recognised complication of subclavian line insertion, and is more likely in left-sided placement as the left apex often extends up above the first rib.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"In a post-operative immunosuppressed transplant patient, some small vesicles have formed on the chest wall, in a linear patch, approximately level with his xiphoid process. Which dermatome is most likely to be affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which dermatome is most likely to be affected?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"Explanation o\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"C4\",\"e\":\"The C4 dermatome corresponds to the lower neck/upper shoulder region.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"T4\",\"e\":\"A, c4\\n\\nThe C4 dermatome corresponds to the lower neck/upper shoulder region.\\n\\nB TA\\n\\nThe nipple is at the level of the T4 dermatome. As the lesions are below the nipple, they\\n\\nmust involve 4 more inferior dérmatome.\\n\\nD TIO\\n\\nThe T10 dermatome Is at the level of the umbilicus, which is lower than the lesions indicated\\n\\nhere.\\n\\nE LI\\n\\nThe L1 dermatome its in the region of the inguinal ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"T7\",\"e\":\"The T7 dermatome is around the level of the xiphoid process. The dermatomes are shown on the image below.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"T10\",\"e\":\"The T10 dermatome is at the level of the umbilicus, which is lower than the lesions indicated here.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"L1\",\"e\":\"The L1 dermatome is in the region of the inguinal ligament.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"A pre-operative patient is on B-blockers. The anaesthetic team are happy for them to be continued during the procedure. Where in the thoracic cage are the Br-adrenoceptors concentrated?\",\"lead\":\"Where in the thoracic cage are the Br-adrenoceptors concentrated?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cardiac ventricles\",\"e\":\"B-Adrenoceptors are present in the heart and are both chronotropic and ionotropic. Bz- Adrenoceptors are present in blood vessels, caus ng vasodilation; in gut smooth muscle causing relaxation; in bronchial smooth muscle causing relaxation; and in the bladder wall causing relaxation\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Pulmonary trunk\",\"e\":\"The pulmonary trunk does not contain Br-adrenoceptors.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Aortic arch\",\"e\":\"The aortic arch (along with the carotid sinus) contains baroreceptors, which mediate arterial tone and heart rate and contractility to maintain arterial pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lungs\",\"e\":\"Bronchial walls contain Bz-adrenoceptors, which cause smooth muscle relaxation when stimulated\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Carotid sinus\",\"e\":\"The carotid sinus (along with the aortic arch) contains baroreceptors, which mediate arterial tone and heart rate and contractility to maintain arterial pressure. They are also in the neck. not the thorax.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"A 62-year-old ex-smoker nas been diagnosed with non-small-cell lung carcinoma by endobronchial biopsy. At standard mediastinoscopy, what is the least likely lymph node station to be sampled?\",\"lead\":\"At standard mediastinoscopy, what is the least likely lymph node station to be sampled?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SPI Paratracheal nodes\",\"e\":\"Paratracheal nodes are accessible through mediastinoscopy and are commonly sampled.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Subcarinal nodes\",\"e\":\"Anterior subcarinal nodes are accessible for sampling through mediastinoscopy, but can be technically challenging.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tracheobronchial nodes\",\"e\":\"These (such as the subcarinal group) are accessible through mediastinoscopy at the distal end of the tunnel created\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Aortopulmonary nodes\",\"e\":\"Aortopulmonary (subaortic) nodes are difficult to access via mediastinoscopy as the aorta is 'in the way' of the advancing mediastinoscope. They are accessed via thoracoscopy [such as video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS)] rather than mediastinoscopy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pretracheal nodes\",\"e\":\"The pretracheal space is entered as part of the mediastinoscopy procedure, and these nodes are therefore available for sampling.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"You are required to insert a chest drain in a patient with penetrating trauma. CT imaging has demonstrated a large haemothorax: Which anatomical landmark is least useful to you?\",\"lead\":\"CT imaging has demonstrated a large haemothorax: Which anatomical landmark is least useful to you?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lateral border of tismus dorsi,?\",\"e\":\"This forms the posterior border of the ‘safe triangle’ for insertion of chest drains.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AAMI The mid-clavicular line\",\"e\":\"The normal placement for a chest drain is in the 5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line, just above the 6th rib. The boundaries of the 'safe triangle' for insertion are:\\n• posteriorly - the lateral border of latissimus dorsi\\n• anteriorly - the lateral border of pectoralis major\\n• inferiorly - 5th intercostal space (at the level of the nipple in men, or the\\n\\ninframammary crease in women)\\n• superiorly - base of axilla.\\n\\nAlthough it is possible to place a chest drain in the second intercostal space, mid-clavicular line, this is not the usual location. Therefore of the options listed, the mid-clavicular line would be LEAST useful in terms of chest tube placement and is therefore is the correct\\n\\nanswer.\\n\\nAll Lateral border of latissimus dorsi\\n\\nThis forms the posterior border of the safe triangle' for insertion of chest drains.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The sixth rib\",\"e\":\"The sixth rib\\n\\nThe chest drain should be placed just above the sixth rib, in the fifth intercostal space, mid-axillary line. This avoids potential damage to the neurovascular bundle, which runs below the inferior border of the rib.\\n\\nD The mid-axillary line\\n\\nThe mid-axillary line is one of the key landmarks for chest drain placement - usually in the 5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line.\\n\\nInferolateral border of pectoralis major\\n\\nThe lateral border of pectoralis major forms the anterior border of the 'safe triangle'\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The mid-axillary line\",\"e\":\"The mid-axillary line is one of the key landmarks for chest drain placement - usually in the 5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inferolateral border of pectoralis major\",\"e\":\"The lateral border of pectoralis major forms the anterior border of the ‘safe triangle’.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"A young man is undergoing bronchoscopy for suspected inhaled peanut. You see the foreign body impacted and are successfully able to remove it. Where is the most likely site for the peanut?\",\"lead\":\"Where is the most likely site for the peanut?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Right middle lobe bronchus\",\"e\":\"Although inhaled objects do normally enter the right main bronchus, they usually end up in the right lower lobe rather than the right middle lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left inferior lobe bronchus\",\"e\":\"Although the left lower lobe is a possibility for an inhaled foreign body, they usually lodge in the right lower lobe as the right main bronchus is shorter, wider and angulated more vertically than the left.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Right superior lobe bronchus\",\"e\":\"This would require the object to travel back upwards once it had entered the right main bronchus, and would therefore be highly unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Left superior lobe bronchus\",\"e\":\"This would be a highly unlikely place for a foreign body as it would have to travel back upwards into the left superior lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right lower lobe bronchus\",\"e\":\"The right main bronchus is angled downwards in comparison with the left, which is more horizontal. It is also wider and shorter than the left, and is therefore the most common site for inhaled foreign objects to lodge. The right lower lobe bronchus is a direct continuation of the right main bronchus as it runs inferiorly, and is therefore the most likely final destination of inhaled objects.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with a right bundle branch block due to blockage in the atrioventricular nodal artery. Part of the right bundle branch of the atrioventricular bundle is carried by which structure?\",\"lead\":\"Part of the right bundle branch of the atrioventricular bundle is carried by which structure?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SPI Pectinate muscles\",\"e\":\"The pectinate muscles (musculi pectinati) are the name given to the muscular ridges in the anterior right atrium. They do not play a specific part in the conducting system of the heart, other than transmitting sinoatrial (SA) nod@07qulses towards the arteriovenous (AV) node.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anterior papillary muscle of the left ventricle\",\"e\":\"Anterior papillary muscle of the left ventricle\\n\\nThe papillary muscles are attached to the cusps of the mitral and tricuspid valves by the chordae tendineae. They do not play a part in the conducting system of the heart. Do not get confused with the anterior papillary muscle of the RIGHT ventricle, which receives the moderator band from the interventricular septum - part of the conducting system of the\\n\\nheart that carries the right bundle branch.\\n\\nD Crista terminalis\\n\\nThe crista terminalis demarcates the border between the primordial atrium and the sinus\\n\\nvenarum. It is seen as a vertical line running between the superior and inferior vena cava in the internal aspect of the right atrium. On the external aspect of the atrium is the sulcus terminalis, which corresponds to the crista terminalis. The sinoatrial (SA) node is located in the upper aspect of the crista terminalis.\\n\\nE Chordae tendineae\\n\\nThe chordae tendineae anchor the papillary muscles to the leaflets of the mitral and tricuspid valves. They do not play a role in the conducting system of the heart.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Moderator band (septomarginal trabecula)\",\"e\":\"The moderator band runs from the interventricular septum to the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle. It carries the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle. The conducting system of the heart is shown schematically below.\\n\\nAtrioventricular node Sinoatrial node Bundle of His / Purkinje fibres\\n\\nteachmoanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Crista terminalis\",\"e\":\"The crista terminalis demarcates the border between the primordial atrium and the sinus venarum. It is seen as a vertical line running between the superior and inferior vena cava in the internal aspect of the right atrium. On the external aspect of the atrium is the sulcus terminalis, which corresponds to the crista terminalis. The sinoatrial (SA) node is located in the upper aspect of the crista terminalis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Chordae tendineae\",\"e\":\"The chordae tendineae anchor the papillary muscles to the leaflets of the mitral and tricuspid valves. They do not play a role in the conducting system of the heart.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A Senior House Officer (SHO) has been asked to aspirate some pleural fluid for culture and sensitivity from the left pleural space of a 65-year-old man who has postpneumonic effusion. If the SHO wants to aspirate the fluid with the patient sitting up in bed, where would the fluid tend to accumulate?\",\"lead\":\"If the SHO wants to aspirate the fluid with the patient sitting up in bed, where would the fluid tend to accumulate?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"is\\\"i Costodiaphragmatic recess\",\"e\":\"The costodiaphragmatic recess is the most inferior recess between the lungs and pleura when upright. It is the area where the parietal pleura attached to the thoracic wall reflects on to the dome of the diaphragm. Remember that the pleura extends approximately two rib spaces below the lungs. The lungs are located at the 6th rib in the mid-clavicular line, 8th rib in the mid-axillary line, and the 10th rib posteriorly. The pleura extends further, to the 8th rib enteriorly, 10th rib laterally and 12th rib posteriorly.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Costomediastinal recess\",\"e\":\"This is behind the sternum, and would in any case be difficult to access via thoracocentesis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"ARLI Cupola\",\"e\":\"The cupola, or dome of the pleura, rises above the medial one-third of the clavicle by approximately 2.5 cm. Fluid would not collect here.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hilar reflection\",\"e\":\"The hilar reflections are where the parietal pleura and visceral pleura are continuous. It is not a site for collection of pleural fluid and would be very difficult to access even with image guidance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Middle mediastinum\",\"e\":\"The middle mediastinum contains the heart and great vessels, and is the site for pericardiocentesis in the event of a pericar 60 8 0sion.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"A victim of anterior chest stabbing received a wound In a structure that is in close proximity\\n\\nto where the first rib articulates with the sternum. They are currently haemodynamically\\n\\nstable.\\n\\nWhich of the following structures is most likely to be injured?\\n\\nAy Nipple\\n\\nB Root of the lung\\n\\nCc Sternal angle\\n\\nD Sternoclavicular joint\\n\\nE Xiphoid process\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Nipple\",\"e\":\"The nipple is at the level of the T4 dermatome, approximately at the fourth intercostal space in the midclavicular line and the fifth intercostal space in the mid-axillary line (although this is variable in women.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Root of the lung\",\"e\":\"The lung hila sit either side of the heart in the mediastinum. The lung hila are at approximately the 3rd and 4th costal cartilages, corresponding to the T5/T6 vertebrae posteriorly.\\n\\nC Sternal angle\\n\\nThe first rib articulates with the manubrium directly below the sternoclavicular joint, and the second rib articulates with the manubrium and sternum at the level of the manubriosternal\\n\\nangle.\\n\\nXiphoid process E\\n\\nThe xiphoid process articulates with the sternum inferiorly\\n\\n378\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sternal angle\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sternoclavicular joint\",\"e\":\"This is correct. The clavicle articulates with the manubrium superolaterally on each side. The sternoclavicular joint is an atypical synovial joint with an intervening cartilaginous disc dividing it into two separate cavities - as can be seen in the figure below.\\n\\nClavicle\\n\\nArticular disk\\n\\nRib 1\\n\\nManubrium...tsy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Xiphoid process\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man was stabbed in the right supraclavicular fossa. The knife punctured the portion of the parietal pleura that extends above the first rib. What is the name for this portion of the parietal pleura?\",\"lead\":\"What is the name for this portion of the parietal pleura?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SPı Costodiaphragmatic recess\",\"e\":\"The costodiaphragmatic recess is the area where the parietal pleura attached to the thoracic wall reflects on to the dome of the diaphragm. Remember that the pleura extends approximately two rib spaces below the lungs. The lungs are located at the 6th rib in the mid-clavicular line, 8th rib in the mid-axillary line, and the 10th rib posteriorly. The pleura extends further, to the ath rib anteriorly, 10th rib laterally and 12th rib posteriorly\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"NAMA Costomediastinal recess\",\"e\":\"The costomediastinal recess is located behind the sternum, where the costal pleura and mediastinal pleura are in contact.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Costocervical recess\",\"e\":\"This is not a defined space in normal pleural anatomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cupola\",\"e\":\"The cervical pleura is also known as the dome or cupola. It is important to know that the pleura extends approximately 2.5 cm above the medial one-third of the clavicle, higher on the left. Pneumothorax is a potential complicating factor in neck stabbings.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Endothoracic fascia\",\"e\":\"The endothoracic fascia is between the chest wed 8g the parietal pleura.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"During pericardiectomy. sudden bleeding was noticed due to accidental injury to a major vascular structure in the pericardium. The surgeon inserted his left index finger through the transverse pericardial sinus, pulled forward on the two large vessels lying ventral to his finger and compressed these vessels with his thumb to control bleeding. Which vessels were these?\",\"lead\":\"Which vessels were these?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary trunk and brachiocephalic trunk\",\"e\":\"The transverse sinus lies posterior to the aorta and pulmonary trunk, but the brachiocephalic trunk branches from the aorta more superiorly, and is not covered by pericardium.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary trunk and aorta T\",\"e\":\"The transverse pericardial sinus lies posterior to the aorta and pulmonary trunk, and anterior to the superior vena cava (SVC). It can be used in cardiac surgery, as seen below, to interrupt flow from the heart and enable coronary bypass. The oblique pericardial sinus, by contrast, is a blind-ending sinus posterior to the heart (below the transverse sinus) that ends between the left and right pulmonary veins.\\n\\nTRANSVERSE SINUS SUPERIOR VENA AORTA CAVA\\n\\nPULMONARY teachmeantat TRUNK\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary trunk and superior vena cava:\",\"e\":\"The pulmonary trunk lies anterior to the transverse sinus, but the SVC lies posterior to it.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superior vena cava and aorta\",\"e\":\"The SVC enters the right atrium from above and posterior, to the right of the aorta. It lies posterior to a finger inserted in the transverse sinus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior vena cava and right pulmonary artery\",\"e\":\"The SVC lies posterior to the transverse sinus. The right pulmonary artery is posterior to the svc.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"A patient with a cystic swelling in his left chest underwent a computer tomography-guided biopsy. The radiologist inserted the biopsy needle into the ninth intercostal space along the mid-axillary line to aspirate the swelling and obtain tissue for histological diagnosis. The swelling is most likely to be in which space?\",\"lead\":\"The swelling is most likely to be in which space?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cardiac notch\",\"e\":\"The cardiac notch is an indentation within the anteromedial border of the left lung, separating the lingula from the superior lobe of the left lung.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Costodiaphragmatic recess\",\"e\":\"The costodiaphragmatic recess is between the 8th and 10th intercostal spaces in the mid-axillary line. Remember that the pleura extend approximately two rib spaces below the lungs. The lungs are located at the 6th rib in the mid-clavicular line, 8th rib in the mid-axillary line, and the 10th rib posteriorly. The pleura extend further, to the 8th rib anteriorly, 10th rib laterally and 12th rib posteriorly.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Costomediastinal recess\",\"e\":\"The costomediastinal recesses are behind the sternum, formed by the reflection of the pleura from the chest wall over the heart and towards the hila of the lungs.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cupola\",\"e\":\"The cervical pleura (or the dome, or cupola) extends approximately 2.5 cm above the medial one-third of the clavicle, higher on the left.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Oblique pericardial sinus\",\"e\":\"The oblique pericardial sinus lies between the pulmonary veins behind the heart, and is a blind ending sinus formed by the reflection of parietal and visceral pericardium.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":54,\"stem\":\"A patient with a malignant mesothelioma is to undergo pleuropneumonectomy, which valves removal of the entire pleura and lung on the affected side. Which one of the following layers provides a natural cleavage plane for surgical separation of the costal pleura from the thoracic wall?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following layers provides a natural cleavage plane for surgical separation of the costal pleura from the thoracic wall?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Deep fascia\",\"e\":\"Deep fascia of the chest wall lies external to the ribs, enveloping muscle groups. It is not related to the pleura.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Endothoracic fascia\",\"e\":\"Endothoracic fascia lies between the inner aspect of the chest wall and the parietal pleura, and it is this layer that provides a cleavage plane for the parietal pleura in pleurectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Parietal pleura\",\"e\":\"The parietal pleura lines the inner aspect of the chest, and includes the costal pleura as well as the diaphragmatic pleura and mediastinal pleura. It is in contrast to the visceral pleura, which lines the lung itself.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Visceral pleura\",\"e\":\"The visceral pleura is the serous membrane that covers the lung, in contrast to the parietal pleura, which lines the inside of the chest cavity and mediastinum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transversus thoracis muscle fascia\",\"e\":\"The transversus thoracis fascia is associated with the transversus thoracis muscle only, and does not play a role with the pleura.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man with pseudobulbar palsy, lying supine in bed, aspirates one of his tablets into his ungs while swallowing. The tablet would most likely be found in which one of the following bronchopulmonary segments?\",\"lead\":\"The tablet would most likely be found in which one of the following bronchopulmonary segments?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior segmental bronchus of the right superior lobe\",\"e\":\"The anterior bronchus of the right upper lobe would be branching off superiorly if the patient is lying down, and therefore would not be a common site for the inhaled tablet.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Medial segmental bronchus of the right middle lobe\",\"e\":\"The medial segmental bronchus of the right middle lobe is a possible location for the inhaled tablet, however in the supine position it would be oriented more superiorly. Foreign objects would be more likely to fall in to the superior segmental bronchus of the right inferior lobe due to its posterior orientation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Superior segmental bronchus of the right inferior lobe\",\"e\":\"The right main bronchus is more vertical than the left, and is the usual site where aspirated foreign bodies end up. As this patient has aspirated the tablet while lying supine, it is likely to fall in to the superior segmental bronchus of the right inferior lobe, which branches posteriorly.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Medial basal segmental bronchus of the left inferior lobe\",\"e\":\"The left main bronchus is a less probable destination for inhaled objects as it is more horizontal than the right.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inferior segmental bronchus of the lingular\",\"e\":\"The left main bronchus (the lingular branches are from the left upper main bronchus) is a less probable destination for inhaled objects 6094nore horizontal than the right.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man was shot in the chest. The bullet punctured a vessel that courses across the mediastinum in an almost horizontal fashion. Which one of the following vessels was most likely injured?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following vessels was most likely injured?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The left subclavian artery courses superolaterally from the aortic arch towards the left arm, not horizontally. 6096\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"1AM. Left subclavian vein\",\"e\":\"The left subclavian vein runs outside of the mediastinum to join with the left internal jugular to form the left brachiocephalic vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left brachiocephalic vein\",\"e\":\"The left and right brachiocephalic veins join together on the right side of the mediastinum to form the SVC. The left brachiocephalic therefore has to run horizontally across the mediastinum to reach the right side.\\n\\nInternal jugular vein\\n\\nCommon carotid artery\\n\\nSubclavian vein Superior Brachiocephalic vein vona cava teachmoanatomy\\n\\nA Left subclavian artery\\n\\nThe left subclavian artery courses superolaterally from the aortic arch towards the left arm, not horizontally.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Left internal jugular vein\",\"e\":\"The left IJV runs vertically to join with the left subclavian vein, forming the left brachiocephalic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Left common carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The left common carotid artery runs up vertically from the aortic arch to the left side of the neck.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"While performing a surgical procedure in the mid-region of the thorax the surgeon accidentally injured an important structure that lies immediately anterior to the thoracic duct. Which one of the following structures was most likely to be injured?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures was most likely to be injured?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1St. Aorta\",\"e\":\"The aorta lies to the left of the thoracic duct in the thorax.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Azygos vein\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein lies to the right side of the thoracic duct in the thorax It is formed by the superior lumbar veins, ascends into the thorax and is joined by the haemiazygos and accessory haemiazygos veins from the left side.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oesophagus\",\"e\":\"The thoracic duct runs in a groove between the aorta (on the left and the azygos vein (on the right). It lies immediately posterior to the oesophagus:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The superior vena cava (SVC) is formed by the left and right brachiocephalic veins on the right side of the superior mediastinum, above the point at which the thoracic duct has crossed to the left side.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trachea\",\"e\":\"The trachea bifurcates at the sternal angle in to the two main bronchi. It is anterior to the desophagus, and the thoracic duct is more p0g69g behind the oesophagus.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"The sinus venosus is the large quadrangular cavity located between the two vena cava vessels in the embryonic human heart. In the adult heart the sinus venous gives rise to which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"In the adult heart the sinus venous gives rise to which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aortic vestibule of the left ventricle\",\"e\":\"The aortic vestibule of the left ventricle develops from the bulbus cordis:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"1AM. Coronary sinus\",\"e\":\"During development, the heart tube begins looping around day 23 - as shown in the stages below. The sinus venosus gives rise to the smooth part of the right atrium, the sinus venarum (as opposed to the auricle, which develops from the primitive atrium) and the coronary sinus. Aortic arch arteries Truncus\\n• arteriosus Right Truncus arteriosus atrium Bulbus cordis Left atrium Ventricle Ventricle Ventricle Atrium Atrium Sinus Sinus venosus venosus 23 days 35 days 24 days\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fossa ovalis\",\"e\":\"Fossa ovalis\\n\\nThe fossa ovalis is the site of the foramen ovale in the embryo. It is formed by the closure of the foramen ovale, through fusion of the septum primum and septum secundum.\\n\\nD Trabeculated part of the right ventricle\\n\\nThe trabeculated part of the right ventricle is formed from the primitive ventricle.\\n\\nTrabeculated portion of the right atrium E\\n\\nThe trabeculated portion of the right atrium is formed from the primitive atrium.\\n\\n785\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Trapeculated part of the right ventricle\",\"e\":\"The trabeculated part of the right ventricle is formed from the primitive ventricle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trabeculated portion of the right atrium\",\"e\":\"The trabeculated portion of the right atrium is formed from the primitive atrium.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a congenital heart defect operation. The consultant has questions about the embryological development of structures that they are dissecting. Which one of the following statements regarding the development of the human heart is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding the development of the human heart is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"In the second week, the endocardial tubes begin to fuse to form a single tube\",\"e\":\"The endocardial tubes fuse during the third week of development.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The septum primum appears in the eighth week\",\"e\":\"The septum primum appears in the fourth week.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The heart begins to beat in the fourth week\",\"e\":\"The heart is the first organ in the embryo to begin functioning, and it begins to beat in the fourth week of development. The primordium of the heart forms in the cardiogenic plate located at the cranial end of the embryo. Angiogenic cell clusters, which lie in a horseshoe-shaped configuration in the plate, coalesce to form two endocardial tubes. These tubes are then forced into the thoracic region due to cephalic and lateral foldings, where they fuse together forming a single endocardial tube during the third week. The heart begins to beat in the fourth week at about the same time that the septum primum appears and the bulboventricular loop is formed (shown in the images between days 24 and 35). From the fourth week onwards, septa begin to grow in the atria, ventricle and bulbus cordis to form right and left atria, right and left ventricles and the two great vessels - the pulmonary artery and the aorta. By the end of the eighth week, partitioning is completed and the fetal heart has formed.\\n\\nAortic arch Truncus arteries arteriosus Right Truncus arteriosus atrium Bulbus cordis\\n• Left atrium Ventricle Ventricle Ventricle Atrium Atrium Sinus Sinus venosus venosus 35 days 23 days 24 days\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The primordium of the heart forms in the cardiogenic plate located at the caudal end of the embryo\",\"e\":\"The primordium of the heart forms in the cardiogenic plate located at the cranial end of the embryo, not the caudal end.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The bulboventricular loop is formed in the sixth week\",\"e\":\"The bulboventricular loop forms in the fourth week.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following organs is a derivative of the endoderm?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following organs is a derivative of the endoderm?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PAS Adrenal medulla\",\"e\":\"The adrenal cortex is derived from mesoderm, and the medulla from ectoderm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Dermis of the skin\",\"e\":\"The dermis of the skin forms from mesoderm.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Epithelial part of the tympanic cavity\",\"e\":\"Endoderm is one of the three embryonic germ-cell layers ‹the others being ectoderm and mesoderm). It forms the epithelium of the gastrointestinal tract, respiratory system, auditory system, and urinary system.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gonads\",\"e\":\"The gonads develop broadly from intermediate mesoderm.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lens\",\"e\":\"The lens of the eye is ectodermal in origin, from the lens placode.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man presents with severe chest pain following a bout of prolonged vomiting. CT reveals oesophageal perforation with associated mediastinitis. Which artery supplies the upper one-third of the oesophagus?\",\"lead\":\"Which artery supplies the upper one-third of the oesophagus?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ascending pharyngeal artery\",\"e\":\"The ascending pharyngeal artery does not supply the desophagus, it supplies a variety of structures around the base of the skull.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior thyroid artery\",\"e\":\"The oesophagus is divided into three with relation to its blood supply. The upper third is supplied by the inferior thyroid artery (highlighted below); the middle third is supplied by direct branches from the descending thoracic aorta (oesophageal arteries); finally, the distal third is supplied by ascending branches from the left gastric artery.\\n\\nInferior thyroid artary\\n\\nDesophagus\\n\\nAzygous Thoracic aorta and voin besophageal branches\\n\\n*! Anatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lingual artery\",\"e\":\"The lingual artery supplies the tongue and floor of the mouth, not the oesophagus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Deso phageal artery.\",\"e\":\"The oesophageal arteries (plural) supply the middle third of the cesophagus, not the upper third.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior thyroid artery\",\"e\":\"The superior thyroid artery does not supply the oesophagus.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"You are asked to assist your consultant with a pulmonary resection. During the procedure she begins to dissect tissue posterior to the right main bronchus. Which vessel is at risk of injury?\",\"lead\":\"Which vessel is at risk of injury?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SPI Azygos vein\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein ascends up the right side of the midline and behind the right main bronchus. The fact that it is at risk can be appreciated when you see its location in the image below. It curves forward over the right main bronchus to join the superior vena cava (SVC).\\n\\nArch of Superior aorta vena cava\\n\\nAccessory hemiazygos vein Azygos vein\\n\\nHemiazygos vein\\n\\nTeachMeAnatomy\\n\\nB Hemiazygos vein\\n\\nThe hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygous veins arise from the left side of the thorax and cross over to the right to join with the azygos vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"1AM. Hemiazygos vein\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Superior vena cava Q\",\"e\":\"The superior vena cava (SVC) forms from the left and right brachiocephalic veins but passes anteriorly to the right main bronchus and right pulmonary artery to enter the right atrium. Remember the azygos vein ascends posteriorly to the right main bronchus and arches over it to join the SVC.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thoracic aorta\",\"e\":\"The thoracic aorta forms at the aortic root and ascends upwards and towards the left before descending on the left side of the midline.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thoracic duct\",\"e\":\"The thoracic duct ascends on the right side of the thorax between the azygos vein and the thoracic aorta. It crosses over to the left at the T4/5 level, which is the same level as the bifurcation of the trachea.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old woman smoker presents with acute severe chest pain and ST elevation on the electrocardiogram (ECG). Troponin levels are raised and suggestive of myocardial infarction. She undergoes primary coronary angioplasty and is noted to have an occlusion within the vessel running through the right coronary sulcus. Which vessel has been occluded?\",\"lead\":\"Which vessel has been occluded?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"MS I Conus artery\",\"e\":\"The conus artery is a small early branch from the right coronary artery with variable anatomy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left circumflex artery\",\"e\":\"The left circumflex branches from the left coronary artery after it emerges from beneath the left atrial appendage, and travels posteriorly in the left atrioventricular groove (left coronary sulcus)\\n\\nLeft coronary artery C\\n\\nThe left coronary artery branches from the aorta at the left posterior coronary cusp, and runs a short distance between the pulmonary trunk and the left atrial appendage (left auricle) before branching into the left anterior descending and the left circumflex arteries.\\n\\nD Posterior descending artery\\n\\nThe posterior descending artery runs on the postero-inferior surface of the heart, usually as a branch of the right coronary artery\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left coronary artery\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior descending artery\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right coronary artery\",\"e\":\"The heart is shown below in an anterior view. The right coronary artery branches from the corta at the anterior coronary cusp and travels antero-inferiorly in the right atrioventricular groove (otherwise known as the coronary sulcus).\\n\\nLeft coronary artery\\n\\nRight coronary Left marginal artery artery\\n\\nLeft anterior descending artery\\n\\nRight marginal teachmoanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"You are asked by your consultant to perform a median sternotomy under supervision. During the manoeuvre you are asked to describe which vascular structures are positioned on the same anatomical plane as the manubriosternal joint What is the correct answer?\",\"lead\":\"During the manoeuvre you are asked to describe which vascular structures are positioned on the same anatomical plane as the manubriosternal joint What is the correct answer?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aortic arch and junction of the azygos vein and hemiazygos vein\",\"e\":\"The hemiazygos vein crosses from the left side to anastomose with the azygos vein around the T8 level, not T4/5.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Aortic arch and junction of the azygos vein and superior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The level of the manubriosternal angle (angle of Louis) corresponds to the T4/5 vertebral level, otherwise known as the transthoracic plane. The image below shows the mediastinum. with the transthoracic plane drawn on in yellow. At this level, the aortic arch begins and ends, and the azygos vein joins the superior vena cava (SVC) by arching over the right main bronchus. At this level, the trachea also bifurcates and the thoracic duct moves from the right to the left.\\n\\nPulmonary Superior trunk vera cava\\n\\nLevel of sternal angle\\n\\nBorder of pericardium Ascending aorta\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Descending thoracic aorta and junction of the azygos and superior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The T4/5 level is the level of the aortic arch, not the descending thoracic aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hemiazygos vein\",\"e\":\"The hemiazygos vein is on the left side of the thorax, and crosses over to join the azygos vein on the right around the T8 level.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Origin of the brachiocephalic artery\",\"e\":\"The brachiocephalic artery is the second branch of the aorta (the first being the coronary arteries), and therefore originates just above the T4/5 level\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman presents with pain in the right hand which is associated with a tingling sensation and weakness. After further investigation she is diagnosed with thoracic outlet syndrome The affected neurovascular bundle runs through which muscles?\",\"lead\":\"After further investigation she is diagnosed with thoracic outlet syndrome The affected neurovascular bundle runs through which muscles?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Digastric\",\"e\":\"The digastric muscle is in the anterior neck. It has two bellies - the posterior supplied by the facial nerve, and the anterior supplied by the nerve to mylohyoid, It does not transmit the roots of the brachial plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Platysme\",\"e\":\"Platysma is the most superficial of the neck/facial muscles and does not transmit the brachial plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Scalene\",\"e\":\"The neurovascular bundle referred to is the brachial plexus, the roots of which emerge from the intervertebral foraminae of the C5-T1 vertebrae and pass between the anterior and middle scalene muscles. The brachial plexus is highlighted in the image below in yellow and the roots can be seen emerging from between them.\\n\\nScalenus medius Scalenus anterior\\n\\nBrachial plexus Subclavian artery\\n\\nfe: hM: Anatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sternocle domastoid\",\"e\":\"The brachial plexus does not pass through SCM.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sterothyroid\",\"e\":\"Platysma B\\n\\nPlatysma is the most superficial of the neck/facial muscles and does not transmit the brachial plexus.\\n\\nSternocleidomastoid D\\n\\nThe brachial plexus does not pass through sCM.\\n\\nSternothyroid E\\n\\nSternothyroid is one of the strap muscles of the anterior neck, and does not convey any part of the brachial plexus.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing aCT thorax in the lung MDT. The tumour has distorted the anatomy so the radiologist points to a landmark The aortic arch and the junction of the azygos vein with the superior vena cava are positioned on the same plane as which spinal column structure?\",\"lead\":\"The tumour has distorted the anatomy so the radiologist points to a landmark The aortic arch and the junction of the azygos vein with the superior vena cava are positioned on the same plane as which spinal column structure?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"T2 vertebra\",\"e\":\"This is too superior - these structures are at the T4/5 level. It corresponds to the superior border of the scapula.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"T3 vertebra\",\"e\":\"This is too superior - these structures are at the T4/5 level.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"T2/3 intervertebral disc\",\"e\":\"T3 vertebra\\n\\nThis is too superior - these structures are at the T4/5 level.\\n\\nc T2/3 intervertebral disc\\n\\nThis is too superior - these structures are at the T4/5 level. It corresponds to the level of the\\n\\nsuprasternal notch.\\n\\nD T3/4 intervertebral disc\\n\\nThis is too superior - these structures are at the T4/5 level.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"T3/4 intervertebral disc\",\"e\":\"This is too superior - these structures are at the T4/5 level.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T4/5 intervertebral disc\",\"e\":\"The T4/5 disc is the level of the transthoracic plane (in yellow below), which corresponds to angle (angle of -ouis). At this level is: the aortic arch; the the level of the manubriosternal junction of the azygos vein and the SVC; the second costal cartilage; the tracheal bifurcation, and the decussation of the thoracic duct from right to left.\\n\\nBrachiocephalic artery aL common carotid artery L& R L subclavian artery brachiocephalic veins € Arch of SVC aorte Level of sternal angle Lute\\n\\nEdge of / pericardium\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"A 49-year-old man undergoes coronary angioplasty for an ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI). During the procedure the patient is noted to have an occlusion within the short artery that passes between the left atrial appendage and the pulmonary trunk. Which vessel has been occluded?\",\"lead\":\"Which vessel has been occluded?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Conus artery\",\"e\":\"The conus artery is a small early branch from the right coronary artery with variable anatomy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left circumflex artery\",\"e\":\"The left circumflex branches from the left coronary artery after it emerges from beneath the left atrial appendage, and travels posteriorly in the atrioventricular groove.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left coronary artery\",\"e\":\"The left coronary artery branches from the aorta at the left coronary cusp, and runs a short distance between the pulmonary trunk and the left atrial appendage (left auricle). In the short segment, it is also referred to as the left main stem. It then gives off the left anterior descending and branches in to the left circumflex and left marginal arteries. The heart is shown from the front below.\\n\\nLeft coronary artery Right coronary Left marginal artery artery\\n\\nLeft anterior descending artery\\n\\nRight marginal artery teachmoanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior descending artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior descending artery runs on the postero-inferior surface of the heart, usually as a branch of the right coronary artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right coronary artery\",\"e\":\"The right coronary artery branches from the aorta at the right coronary cusp and travels antero-inferiorly in the right atrioventricular groove.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":92,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman presents with pain in the right hand which is associated with a tingling sensation and weakness. After further investigation she is diagnosed with thoracic outlet syndrome. The affected neurovascular bundle runs between scalenus anterior and medius. Where do these muscles insert?\",\"lead\":\"Where do these muscles insert?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Body of the sternum\",\"e\":\"The scalene muscles do not insert on to the sternum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Clavicle\",\"e\":\"The scalene muscles insert on to the first rib, not the clavicle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"First rib\",\"e\":\"The anterior scalene runs from the transverse processes of C3-C6 to insert on to the scalene tubercle of the first rib (it is highlighted in green in the image). The middle scalene originates from the transverse processes of C5-C7 to insert on to the first rib more posteriorly. They serve as the anterior and posterior borders of the nerve roots of the brachial plexus as they emerge from the intervertebral foraminae of the C5-T1 vertebrae.\\n\\nScalenus medius Scalenus anterior\\n\\nBrachial plexus Subclavian artery\\n\\nRoads Anatoniv\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Manubrium\",\"e\":\"The scalene muscles insert on to the first rib, not the manubrium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Second rib\",\"e\":\"Clavicle B\\n\\nThe scalene muscles insert on to the first rib, not the clavicle.\\n\\nManubrium D\\n\\nThe scalene muscles insert on to the first rib, not the manubrium.\\n\\nSecond rib E\\n\\nThe posterior scalene muscle attaches to the second rib, but the nerve roots of the brachial plexus emerge between the anterior and middle scalene muscles, which insert on to the first rib.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":95,\"stem\":\"A male jockey is thrown from his horse and sustains a spinal cord injury. He presents with difficulty breathing and ultrasound scan reveals a right hemidiaphragmatic paralysis. From which spinal roots does the affected nerve originate?\",\"lead\":\"From which spinal roots does the affected nerve originate?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"C2-3\",\"e\":\"C3 supplies the diaphragm, but C2 does not.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"C2-4\",\"e\":\"The diaphragm is supplied by C3-5, not C2-4.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"C3-5\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerves are supplied by the C3, C4 and C5 spinal nerves - remember C3/4/5 keep the diaphragm alive.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"C6-8\",\"e\":\"Nerve roots C6-8 supply the brachial plexus not the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"C7-8\",\"e\":\"C7 and C8 spinal roots innervate the See eine not the diaphragm\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":97,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old, tall man presents with sudden onset shortness of breath. He is noted to have absent breath sounds in the left hemi-thorax. A chest X-ray confirms a spontaneous simple left pneumothorax and chest drain is inserted. When inserting an intercostal drain in the fifth intercostal space, which one of the following structures is normally breached by the chest tube?\",\"lead\":\"When inserting an intercostal drain in the fifth intercostal space, which one of the following structures is normally breached by the chest tube?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Parietal pleura\",\"e\":\"The parietal pleura is the pleura that adheres to the inner chest wall, and is pierced by the chest drain to allow access to the pleural space. Remember that the visceral pleura (covering the lungs) and the parietal pleura (covering the inner chest cavity) together form the pleural space. An intercostal drain is typically inserted in the fifth intercostal space, anterior to the mid-axillary line. After piercing the skin, fat and intercostal muscles, the chest drain passes through the parietal pleura in to the pleural space.\\n\\nPectoralis major muscle l\\n\\nThe pectoralis major muscle arises from the clavicle and the sternum/upper six costal cartilages, and inserts into the bicipital groove of the humerus. It forms the anterior border of the axilla along with pec minor, and is not pierced in chest drain insertion, which is typically through the 'safe triangle' of insertion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pectoralis major muscle:\",\"e\":\"The pectoralis major muscle arises from the clavicle and the sternum/upper six costal cartilages, and inserts into the bicipital groove of the humerus. It forms the anterior border of the axilla along with pec minor, and is not pierced in chest drain insertion, which is typically through the ‘safe triangle’ of insertion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pectoralis minor muscle\",\"e\":\"Pectoralis minor forms part of the anterior wall of the axilla and is not pierced in a chest drain insertion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pericardium\",\"e\":\"The pericardium, for obvious reasons, should not be pierced when inserting a chest drain! It is pierced during pericardiocentesis for a pericardial effusion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Visceral pleura\",\"e\":\"Pectoralis minor muscle\\n\\nPectoralis minor forms part of the anterior wall of the axilla and is not pierced in a chest\\n\\ndrain insertion.\\n\\nD Pericardium\\n\\nThe pericardium, for obvious reasons, should not be pierced when inserting a chest drain! It is pierced during pericardiocentesis for a pericardial effusion.\\n\\nE Visceral pleura\\n\\nThe visceral pleura forms the inner border of the pleural space, being adherent to the lung. A chest drain should be inserted into the pleural space and should not therefore pierce the\\n\\nvisceral pleura.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":100,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old smoker undergoes a resection of the right middle lung lobe for squamous-cell lung cancer. The lesion is adherent to the mediastinum but is removed successfully. A post-operative chest X-ray reveals a raised right hemidiaphragm. There is no evidence of a pneumothorax. Which one of the following structures is most likely to have been damaged?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures is most likely to have been damaged?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The liver capsule and parenchyma\",\"e\":\"Liver capsule damage would be unlikely to lead to a raised hemidiaphragm, as it would also require direct diaphragmatic injury (as the liver lies beneath the diaphragm). A phrenic nerve injury is much more likely. However, subcapsular haematomas of the liver can lead to the appearance of a raised hemidiaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The phrenic nerve\",\"e\":\"Phrenic nerve injury leads to diaphragmatic palsy. This presents as a raised hemidiaphragm. A raised hemidiaphragm may also be caused by a subphrenic abscess or more rarely by a subcapsular haematoma of the liver. The phrenic nerves originate from the C3/4/5 nerve roots and travel over the anterior surface of the anterior scalene muscles in the neck, anterior to the lung hila and over the pericardium - they are highlighted in yellow below.\\n\\nLeft phrenic Pericardium. nervo\\n\\nRight dome of the diaphragm\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The right hemidiaphragm\",\"e\":\"C The right hemidiaphragm\\n\\nInjury to the diaphragm itself does not lead to a raised hemidiaphragm - this is caused by a phrenic nerve palsy. Direct diaphragmatic trauma, if severe, can lead to herniation of the bowel into the chest cavity - on the right this is less likely due to the presence of the liver.\\n\\nThe thoracic duct D\\n\\nThoracic duct injury during mediastinal/lung surgery can lead to chylothorax, not a raised hemidiaphragm.\\n\\nThe vagus nerve E\\n\\nThe vagus nerves run posterior to the hila of the lungs. Injury leads to a variety of symptoms depending on severity (based around reduced parasympathetic tone to the viscera), but does not lead to diaphragmatic palsy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The thoracic duct\",\"e\":\"Thoracic duct injury during mediastinal/lung surgery can lead to chylothorax, not a raised hemidiaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerves run posterior to the hila of the lungs. Injury leads to a variety of symptoms depending on severity (based around reduced parasympathetic tone to the viscera), but does not lead to diaphragmatic palsy.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":103,\"stem\":\"A 20-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a traumatic pneumothorax. He is obviously tachypnoeic but maintains adequate oxygen saturation. Which one of the following is the least likely to contribute to his respiratory effort?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the least likely to contribute to his respiratory effort?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"contraction of the intercostal muscles\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect as contraction of the intercostal muscles is one of the methods of increasing respiratory effort.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Descent of the hemidiaphragms\",\"e\":\"Hemidiaphragm descent (ie increased contraction of the daphragms) is one of the methods of increasing respiratory effort, as in the case above.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"An increase in the vertical dimension of the chest\",\"e\":\"ease in vertical dimension of the chest (ie increased air inspiration) is a method of increasing respiratory effort.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The long thoracic nerve of Bell (supplying the serratus anterior)\",\"e\":\"The serratus anterior is one of the accessory muscles of respiration and is recruited to increase the respiratory effort.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Upward,/forward movement of the first rib\",\"e\":\"The first rib does not move during respiration, and therefore does not contribute to respiratory effort.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":105,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old man is found to have a suspicious lesion in the right lower lung lobe on a CT scan. He undergoes bronchoscopy to obtain a histological sample. Which one of the following statements best describes the anatomy of the lungs?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements best describes the anatomy of the lungs?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The right lung has ten bronchopulmonary segments\",\"e\":\"The trachea branches into the left and right main bronchi at the carina, with the left dividing into upper and lower, and the right dividing into upper, middle and lower bronchi. These in turn branch into segmental bronchi - the right lung has ten bronchopulmonary segments. Each of these segments are supplied by a bronchus and by a pulmonary artery, and are therefore distinct and resectable.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The left lung has three bronchial openings\",\"e\":\"The left lung has two bronchial openings that supply air for gas exchange - one for the upper, and one for the lower lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The right lung has three pulmonary veins\",\"e\":\"The left and right lungs both have two pulmonary veins each that return blood to the left atrium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The right lung has two lobes\",\"e\":\"The left lung has two lobes - upper and lower. The right lung has the third, middle lobe, and therefore also has a transverse fissure. The lingual is part of the upper lobe of the left lung that sits anterior to the heart - it is not a distinct lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The left lung parenchyma receives its blood supply from the left pulmonary artery\",\"e\":\"The left and right pulmonary arteries supply deoxygenated blood at low pressure from the right ventricle to the lungs for gas exchange. The lungs themselves are supplied by bronchial arteries that branch from the descending thor6 f43rta.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"A 6-year-old boy accidentally aspirates a small coin. A chest X-ray shows this to be lodged in the right main bronchus. Which one of the following descriptions of the main bronchial airways is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following descriptions of the main bronchial airways is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aspiration pneumonitis is more common in the right lower lobe than the left\",\"e\":\"The right main bronchus is more vertically oriented and shorter than the left - as a result, it is the most likely destination for inhaled foreign material\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The azygous vein crosses the left main bronchus\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein ascends posterior to the right main bronchus and arches anteriorly over it to join with the superior vena cava (SVC).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The left bronchus is shorter than the right\",\"e\":\"The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and oriented more horizontally than the right. making the right the commonest site of inhaled foreign bodies.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The left main bronchus divides before entering the lung\",\"e\":\"The left main bronchus does not give off any branches before entering the lung parenchyma. The right, however, gives off the branch before entering the lung.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The right lung has eight bronchopulmonary segments\",\"e\":\"The right lung has ten bronchopulmonary segments, compared with eight in the left lung.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":109,\"stem\":\"A 43-year-old woman undergoes parathyroid surgery. Immediately following the surgery, she; noted to have developed a hoarse voice. Which one of the following is the most accurate description of the recurrent laryngeal nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most accurate description of the recurrent laryngeal nerve?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It lies deep to the inferior thyroid artery\",\"e\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerve is intimately related to the inferior thyroid artery, and may pass under, over or through branches of the artery. This answer indicates that the nerve runs only deep to the artery, and is therefore incoref 47\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It runs between the oesophagus and trachea in the neck\",\"e\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerves run in the trachea-oesophageal grooves, left and right. between the oesophagus and trachea. The mediastinum is drawn in schematic sagittal view below - the left recurrent laryngeal nerve can be seen branching from the vagus and ascending between the oesophagus and trachea.\\n\\nThanks. niet Supertr mediastinum. rategie mediastinum Posterior HEART\\n• *: mediastinum Anterior Phrenic n. Middie 'mediastinum\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It should be retracted during tracheostomy to avoid damage\",\"e\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerve should not be retracted due to the risk of neuropraxia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It supplies the cricothyroid muscle\",\"e\":\"Cricothyroid is antero-inferior to the thyroid cartilage, and is therefore supplied by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve rather than the recurrent laryngeal nerve. It is the only laryngeal muscle that is not supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It supplies the mucous surface of the vocal cords\",\"e\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies sensation below the vocal cords, and the superior laryngeal nerve supplies the mucosa of, and above, the vocal cords.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":112,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old woman undergoes an emergency thoracotomy following a gunshot wound to the left side of the chest. On exposing the left main bronchus, which one of the following structures may be encountered on its anterior surface?\",\"lead\":\"On exposing the left main bronchus, which one of the following structures may be encountered on its anterior surface?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aorta\",\"e\":\"The descending thoracic aorta lies posterior 6156 left main bronchus\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Azygos vein\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein lies posterior to the right main bronchus, and arches over it anteriorly to join the superior vena cava (SVC).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Phrenic nerve\",\"e\":\"Both the left and right phrenic nerves run anterior to the hila of the left and right lungs respectively - shown in yellow below. This situation is in contrast with the vagus nerves, which run posterior to the hila.\\n\\nLeft phrenic Pericardium - nerve\\n\\nRight dome of the diaphragm\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\\n\\nA Aorta\\n\\nThe descending thoracic aorta lies posterior to the left main bronchus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Recurrent laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"Azygos vein B\\n\\nThe azygos vein lies posterior to the right main bronchus, and arches over it anteriorly to join the superior vena cava (SVC).\\n\\nD Recurrent laryngeal nerve\\n\\nThe vagus nerve on the left travels anterior to the arch of the aorta (which is superior to the left main bronchus) and gives off the recurrent laryngeal nerve, which curves back beneath the aortic arch and in to the neck.\\n\\nE Vagus nerve\\n\\nThe left and right vagus nerves run posteriorly to the hila of the lungs, in contrast with the phrenic nerves, which run anteriorly to the hila.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"The left and right vagus nerves run posteriorly to the hila of the lungs, in contrast with the phrenic nerves, which run anteriorly to the hila.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":115,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old man undergoes coronary angioplasty for unstable angina. He suffers an iatrogenic rupture of the right coronary artery at the time of angioplasty Which one of the following statements is true regarding the right coronary artery?\",\"lead\":\"He suffers an iatrogenic rupture of the right coronary artery at the time of angioplasty Which one of the following statements is true regarding the right coronary artery?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"it typically supplies the left atrium\",\"e\":\"The right coronary artery typically supplies the right atrium, most of the right ventricle, inferior (diaphragmatic) portion of the left ventricle, sinoatrial (SA) and arteriovenous (AV) nodes, and posterior one-third of the interventricular septum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It overlies the right atrial appendage\",\"e\":\"The right coronary artery runs from the right aortic sinus underneath the right atrial appendage to curve around posterior-laterally.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies posterior to the infundibulum of the right ventricle\",\"e\":\"The infundibulum of the right ventricle is the outflow tract as it forms in to the pulmonary trunk. The right coronary artery arises from the right aortic sinus and runs beneath the right atrial appendage, then posterio-laterally to the inferior surface of the heart, and does not therefore run posterior to the infundibulum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It originates in the posterior aortic sinus\",\"e\":\"The posterior aortic sinus/cusp is also known as the non-coronary cusp as it does not give rise to a coronary artery\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It supplies the sinoatrial node\",\"e\":\"The right coronary artery usually supplies the sinoatrial node (in around 60% of people) via the sinoatrial nodal branch, which branches from the right coronary arteries near to its origin. The distribution of the coronary artery supply from an anterior and posterior view is shown below. latrogenic coronary artery rupture may require emergency placement of a pericardial drain to avoid pericardial tamponade.\\n\\nspelleright retail bet i BIcht Coronary Artary haples the it serial\\n\\nteacher\\n\\nRusht Coronary Artery Supplier the right onio ond Hoft Ventricle\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":118,\"stem\":\"An 82-year-old man undergoes insertion of central line through the right internal jugular vein. The wire used during the procedure damages the right atrium. Which one of the following is true regarding the right atrium?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding the right atrium?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It contains the sinoatrial node\",\"e\":\"The right atrium forms the right border of the heart, lies anterior to the left atrium and so its posterior wall is the interatrial septum. The sinoatrial node lies near the opening of the superior vena cava, in the upper part of the crista terminalis (shown in the image below which is of the right atrium opened up from an anterior view).\\n\\nRight Superior vena cava auricle orifice Right ventricle\\n\\nCrista terminalis\\n\\nFossa ovalis\\n\\nInferior vena cava Coronary sinus orifice orifice Anatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It forms the inferior border of the heart\",\"e\":\"When viewed anteriorly the right atrium forms the right heart border, not the inferior border which is formed by the right ventricle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It has an anterior wall formed by the interatrial septum\",\"e\":\"The posterior, not anterior, wall of the right atrium is formed by the interatrial septum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It has the coronary sinus opening above the septal cusp of the mitral valve\",\"e\":\"The coronary sinus (responsible for the venous drainage of the myocardium) opens into the atrium above both the opening of the inferior vena cava and the septal cusp of the tricuspid valve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It lies posterior to the left atrium\",\"e\":\"The right atrium is anterior to the left atrium.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":121,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man is shot in the chest. He is diagnosed with a pericardial tamponade. He undergoes an emergency thoracotomy and creation of a pericardial window by excising a segment of pericardium to relieve the tamponade. Which one of the following can occur as result of an iatrogenic injury during the pericardial incision?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following can occur as result of an iatrogenic injury during the pericardial incision?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Damage to the parasympathetic supply to the abdomen\",\"e\":\"This would involve damage to the vagus nerve which would not be affected during an emergency thoracotomy and creation of a pericardial window.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Elevation of the left hemidiaphragm\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerves (C3-C5) supply motor innervation to the diaphragm. They run vertically on the external surface of the cardiac pericardium, as seen below in the image. An incision over the pericardium in emergency situations could lead to a phrenic nerve damage and paralysis of the ipsilateral hemidiaphragm.\\n\\nLeft phrenic Pericardium nerve\\n\\nRight dome of the diaphragm\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"ORL Hiatal hernia\",\"e\":\"A hiatus hernia is a chronic problem resulting in oesophageal reflux and heartburn due to herniation of the cardia herniating through the diaphragm\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Paralysis of all intercostals muscles on the left hemithorax\",\"e\":\"The intercostal nerves from the thoracic spinal nerves supply the intercostal muscles and therefore would not be affected during an emergency thoracotomy due to their anatomical course.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulsion diverticulum of the oesophagus\",\"e\":\"hese occur as a result of increased intraluminal pressure. They are uncommon and related o motility disorders of the oesophagus.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":124,\"stem\":\"An 82-year-old man develops hilar lymphadenopathy secondary to a hamatological malignancy. This affects predominantly the superior aspect of the right main bronchus. Which one of the following structures is most likely to be compressed?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures is most likely to be compressed?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pr The azygos vein\",\"e\":\"The azygos system in the posterior mediastinum can be seen in the image below. The azygos vein is at first posterior and then arches over the superior aspect of the right main bronchus to join the superior vena cava (SVC), and as such is most likely to be affected by hilar lymphadenopathy affecting the superior aspect of the right main bronchus.\\n\\nArch of Superior aorta vena cava\\n\\nAccessory hemiazygos vein Azygos vein\\n\\nHemiazygos vein\\n\\nTeachMeAnatomy\\n\\nB The hemiazygos vein\\n\\nThe hemiazygos vein runs to the left of the thoracic vertebral column and so would not be compressed by lymphadenopathy at the rightiezin bronchus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The hemiazygos vein\",\"e\":\"The hemiazygos vein runs to the left of the thoracic vertebral column and so would not be compressed by lymphadenopathy at the ricpweg” bronchus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The right phrenic nerve\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve is anterior to the bronchus and therefore is less likely to be compressed.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The right recurrent laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The right recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around the right subclavian artery superior to the right main bronchus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The right vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerve lies just posterior to the right main bronchus and is unlikely to be affected.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":127,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man undergoes carotid endarterectomy having previously suffered a stroke. During the procedure he sustains an iatrogenic injury to his left vagus nerve. Which one of the following statements about the vagus nerve is the most accurate?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about the vagus nerve is the most accurate?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It enters the abdomen at the level of T10\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerve arises from the medulla and exits the skull through the jugular foramen. It travels in the carotid sheath between the internal jugular vein and the common carotid artery in the neck. in the chest it lies posterior to the hilum of the lung. while the phrenic nerve travels anterior to the hilum. The left vagus travels on the anterior aspect of the cesophagus, while the right one travels on the posterior side. Both left and right nerves enter the abdominal cavity at the level of T10 (osophageal hiatus).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It exits the skull through the foramen ovale\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerve exits through the jugular foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies anterior to the hilum of the lung\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve lies anterior to the hilum while the vagus lies posterior.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It originates from the pons\",\"e\":\"I he vagus nerve originates from the medulla, not the pons.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It travels just lateral to the oesophagus\",\"e\":\"The left vagus travels on the anterior aspect of the oesophagus, while the right one travels on the posterior side.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":129,\"stem\":\"A 38-year-old man is diagnosed with a sliding hiatus hernia.\\n\\nWhich pair of structures travels through the same diaphragmatic aperture as the hernia?\\n\\nA Azygos vein and inferior vena cava\\n\\nBAAzygos vein and right phrenic nerve\\n\\nC Hemiazygous vein and oesophagus\\n\\nVagus nerve and azygous vein D\\n\\nVagus nerve and oesophagus\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Azygos vein and inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein passes posterior to the diaphragm through the aortic opening, while the inferior vena cava (IVC) passes through the central tendon.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Azygos vein and right phrenic nerve\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein passes posterior to the diaphragm through the aortic opening, while the phrenic nerve pieces the diaphragm through the central tendon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hemiazygous vein and oesophagus\",\"e\":\"The hemiazygos vein runs superiorly along the left side of the vertebral column and passes through the left crus of the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve and azygous vein\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerve passes through the right crus of the diaphragm along with the oesophagus. however, the azygos vein passes through the aortic opening.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve and oesophagus\",\"e\":\"The sliding hernia occurs as a result of the stomach and oesophagus moving cranially through the oesophageal opening of the diaphragm into the thoracic cavity. Three sets of structures pierce the diaphragm at three vertebral levels\\n• T8 = Right phrenic nerve and inferior vena cava (IVC); these pierce the diaphragm through the central tendon (caval opening).\\n• T10 = Vagus nerve and oesophagus; these pierce the diaphragm through its right crus (oesophageal opening).\\n• T12 = Azygos vein, thoracic duct and abdominal aorta; these pass posterior to the diaphragm through the aortic opening.\\n\\nThese diaphragmatic openings are shown from below in the image: The vagus nerves accompany the oesophagus through the oesophageal hiatus.\\n\\nSternal Central tendon attachment\\n\\nCaval Oesophageal hiatus hiatus\\n\\nAortic hiatus\\n\\nVertebral attachment ©RAA®167\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":132,\"stem\":\"A. 73-year-old man is found to have a thoracic aortic aneurysm affecting the ascending aorta and aortic arch. The aneurysm is expanding in size, leading to aortic regurgitation. Which one of the following best describes the anatomy of the normal thoracic aortic arch?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describes the anatomy of the normal thoracic aortic arch?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It arches below the manubriosternal joint\",\"e\":\"The arch is entirely superior to the manubriosternal joint. The ascending aorta arises from the left ventricle at the level of the manubriosternal joint\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It arches directly over the left pulmonary artery\",\"e\":\"It arches over the right pulmonary artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It gives rise to the coronary arteries\",\"e\":\"The coronary arteries are the first branches of the ascending aorta and not branches of the aortic arch. The aortic arch gives rise to the brachiocephalic artery. the left common carotid and the left subclavian artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is covered by pleura\",\"e\":\"The arch is symmetrically covered by the pleura from both sides, which meet in the midline behind the manubriosternal joint.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It lies anterior to the brachiocephalic veins\",\"e\":\"The apex of the arch, which gives attachment to the pretracheal fascia, lies posteroinferior to the left brachiocephalic vein.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":134,\"stem\":\"A 19-year-old man is brought into the Emergency Department following a stabbing. On examination a wound is seen in the left supraclavicular fossa, but he is otherwise haemodynamically stable. A chest X-ray shows the left hemidiaphragm is elevated and there is concern that the phrenic nerve may be affected Which one of the following descriptions best describe the route by which the phrenic nerve enters the chest?\",\"lead\":\"A chest X-ray shows the left hemidiaphragm is elevated and there is concern that the phrenic nerve may be affected Which one of the following descriptions best describe the route by which the phrenic nerve enters the chest?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior to subclavian artery and posterior to the subclavian vein. Medial to the lower border of scalenus anterior (one of the scalene muscles)\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve runs over the anterior surface of scalenus anterior and enters the chest by descending from the medial lower border of the scalenus anterior muscle, between the artery. This can be seen in the upper part of the image below, where the subclavian vein and\\n\\nphrenic nerves are highlighted in yellow. The autonomic fibres in the phrenic nerve are sympathetic and pass from the superior (C1-C4) and middle (C5/C6) sympathetic cervical ganglia as grey rami into the C3-C5 roots of the phrenic nerve, and innervate blood vessels in the diaphragm. The nerve lies on the fibrous pericardium and is sensory to the mediastinal and diaphragmatic pleura, and also to the diaphragmatic peritoneum.\\n\\nLoft phrenic Pericardium nerve\\n\\nRight dome of the diaphragm\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the subclavian artery and posterior to the subclavian vein. Lateral to the lower border of scalenus anterior\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve is medial, not lateral, to the lower border of the scalenus anterior.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the subclavian vein and artery. Medial to the lower border of scalenus anterior\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve is anterior to the subclavian artery but posterior to the subclavian vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior to subclavian artery and vein. Medial to the lower border of scalenus anterior\",\"e\":\"It is posterior to the subclavian vein but anterior to the artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior to subclavian vein and artery. Lateral to the lower border of scalenus anterior\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve is anterior to the subclavian artery and posterior to the vein. It is also medial to the lower border of the scalenus anterior.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":137,\"stem\":\"A 62-year-old man with alcoholic liver disease is deteriorating in the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) following insertion of a Sengstaken- Blakemore tube for bleeding oesophageal varices. His chest X-ray shows a pneumomediastinum and he is thought to have perforated his besophagus. Vhich one of the following structures make an indentation on the left lung, but not the right lung?\",\"lead\":\"Vhich one of the following structures make an indentation on the left lung, but not the right lung?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Azygos vein\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein arches oven the right root of the lung.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Uesophagus\",\"e\":\"The oesophagus grooves the left lung above the arch of the aorta and below the hilum, however there is also a vertical groove for he Size oe\\\" on the right lung.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"Inferior vena cava\\n\\nThe inferior vena cava leaves an impression on the right lung anterioinferiorly from the root of the lung.\\n\\nDe Superior vena cava\\n\\nImpressions on the mediastinal surface of the right lung include the trachea, vagus, superior vena cava, right atrium and subclavian artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superior vena cava\",\"e\":\"Impressions on the mediastinal surface of the right lung include the trachea, vagus, superior vena cava, right atrium and subclavian artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Aortic arch\",\"e\":\"There is a deep groove superiorly for the arch of the aorta - the impression of the arch and the descending thoracic aorta can be seen in the medial surface of the left lung below.\\n\\nRight Left Superior lobe\\n\\nInferior lobe\\n\\nMiddle lobe\\n\\nHilum Horizontal — fissure\\n\\nOblique fissure\\n\\nA Azygos vein\\n\\nThe azygos vein arches oven the right root of the lung.\\n\\nOesophagus B\\n\\nThe oesophagus grooves the left lung above the arch of the aorta and below the hilum, however there is also a vertical groove for the oesophagus on the right lung.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":140,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man is rushed into the Emergency department following an explcsion at a factory. He has severe facial burns and requires a needle cricothyroidotomy following a failed intubation. Following stabilisation, it is decided to perform a tracheostomy Which one of the following landmarks represents the origin of the trachea?\",\"lead\":\"Following stabilisation, it is decided to perform a tracheostomy Which one of the following landmarks represents the origin of the trachea?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cricoid cartilage\",\"e\":\"The trachea commences at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage (highlighted in yellow in the image). It is palpable in the jugular notch, bifurcates in the transthoracic plane and is innervated by the recurrent laryngeal nerves. The left main bronchus bifurcates inside the left lung and is not as vertical as the right main bronchus.\\n\\nEpiglottis Thyroid cartilage Arytenoid cartilages Cricoid cartilage Corniculate cartilages\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyoid bone:\",\"e\":\"The hyoid bone is a horseshoe-shaped bone that is held in place by various muscles and ligaments, it has no other bony articulations. It is superior to the trachea and is found anterior to the pharynx in the midline of the neck.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Jugular notch\",\"e\":\"The trachea is palpable in the jugular (or suprasternal) notch and originates from the lower border of the cricoid cartilage.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Laryngeal prominence\",\"e\":\"Jugular notch C\\n\\nThe trachea is palpable in the jugular (or suprasternal) notch and originates from the lower border of the cricoid cartilage.\\n\\nLaryngeal prominence D\\n\\nThe laryngeal prominence; colloquially known as the Adam's apple, is most anterior part of the thyroid cartilage which provides structure to the larynx.\\n\\nSternal angle E\\n\\nThe sternal angle is formed by the manubriosternal joint, deep to this angle is the carina of the trachea.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sternal angle\",\"e\":\"The sternal angle is formed by the manubriosternal joint, deep to this angle is the carina of the trachea.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":143,\"stem\":\"Following a difficult left cervical rib excision, a 33-year-old woman is noted to have straw coloured fluid in her drain and the diagnosis of a chyle leak is made secondary to a thoracic duct injury Which one of the following best describes the vertebral level through which the thoracic duct passes through the diaphragm?\",\"lead\":\"Following a difficult left cervical rib excision, a 33-year-old woman is noted to have straw coloured fluid in her drain and the diagnosis of a chyle leak is made secondary to a thoracic duct injury Which one of the following best describes the vertebral level through which the thoracic duct passes through the diaphragm?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"T4\",\"e\":\"The diaphragm is situated between T8-T12, therefore T4 is too high.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"T6\",\"e\":\"The diaphragm is situated lower than T6.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"TS\",\"e\":\"B\\n\\nThe diaphragm is situated lower than T6.\\n\\nC T8\\n\\nThe right phrenic nerve and the inferior vena cava pierce the diaphragm through the central tendon (caval opening) at T8.\\n\\nD T10\\n\\nThe vagus nerve and oesophagus pierce the diaphragm through its right crus (oesophageal opening) at the level of T10.\\n\\n370\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"TIO\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerve and oesophagus pierce the diaphragm through its right crus (oesophageal opening) at the level of T10.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"TIi2\",\"e\":\"The thoracic duct crosses the diaphragm at the aortic hiatus (T12) and ascends the superior and posterior mediastinum between the descending thoracic aorta (to its left) and the azygos vein (to its right). The thoracic duct ascends anterior to the posterior intercostal vessels and has several valves. At the thoracic inlet, it lies to the left of the oesophagus and arches forward over the dome of the left pleura, draining into the left brachiocephalic vein. The right bronchomediastinal trunk drains into the right subclavian vein.\\n\\nSternal Central tendon attachment\\n\\nCaval hiatus Oesophageal hiatus\\n\\nAortic hiatus\\n\\nVertebral attachment\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":146,\"stem\":\"Following a very difficult right thoracic outlet syndrome decompression in a 35-year-old woman there is some respiratory difficulty and concern the right phrenic nerve may be injured. A chest X-ray shows elevation of the right hemidiaphragm. Which one of these descriptions most accurately describes the muscle relations of the phrenic nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these descriptions most accurately describes the muscle relations of the phrenic nerve?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior to scalenus anterior\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve arises from C3, C4 and C5 deep to the scalenus anterior and medius muscles, and runs on scalenus anterior, over the anterior part of the dome of the pleura, to enter the mediastinum posterior to the subclavian vein. Here the right phrenic nerve spirals forward to lie on the SVC, right atrium and inferior vena cava (IVC), and traverses the diaphragm via the caval orifice. Both phrenic nerves are shown below in the image.\\n\\nLoft phrenic Pericardium nerve\\n\\nRight dome of the diaphragm\\n\\nroachmoanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anterior to scalenus medius\",\"e\":\"While the phrenic nerve is anterior to the scalenus medius - it is more closely related to the scalenus anterior where it runs along the anterior surface of the muscle\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anterior to trapezius V\",\"e\":\"Anterior to trapezius C\\n\\nThe scalene muscles are anterior to the trapezius so the phrenic nerve is anterior to the trapezius. However, it is more correct to state that the phrenic nerve is anterior to the scalene anterior as the anatomical relationship is closer.\\n\\nPosterior to scalenus anterior D\\n\\nThe phrenic nerve runs along the anterior surface of the scalenus anterior.\\n\\nPosterior to scalenus medius E\\n\\nThe scalenus medius is posterior to the scalenus anterior therefore this answer is incorrect.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior to scalenus anterior\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve runs along the anterior surface of the scalenus anterior.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior to scalenus medius\",\"e\":\"The scalenus medius is posterior to the scalenus anterior therefore this answer is incorrect.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":149,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old woman is being considered for a thoracoscopic sympathectomy for refractory hyperhidrosis affecting both axillae and hands. She has failed to respond to conservative and medical therapy to date. Which one of the following descriptions most accurately describes how the sympathetic trunk passes from the abdomen to the thorax?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following descriptions most accurately describes how the sympathetic trunk passes from the abdomen to the thorax?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adjacent to the inferior vena cava and right phrenic nerve\",\"e\":\"These structures run through the caval opening in the diaphragm, which is anterior to the vertebral column and therefore would not contain the sympathetic trunk.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Adjacent to the oesophagus within the right crus of the diaphragm\",\"e\":\"It is the vagus nerve, not the sympathetic trunk, that runs adjacent to the osophagus and passes through the diaphragm within the right crus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Posterior to the lateral arcuate ligament over quadratus lumborum\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic trunk is posterior to the medial arcuate ligament and it passes over the psoas major not the quadratus lumborum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior to the medial arcuate ligament over psoas major\",\"e\":\"The thoracic sympathetic chain lies on the heads of the ribs, anterior to the posterior intercostal vessels, immediately under cover of the pleura, with the splanchnic nerves passing from the chain medially and anteriorly over the vertebral bodies. The thoracic sympathetic chain receives white rami from all the intercostal nerves, and passes into the abdomen under the medial arcuate ligament of the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Within the vertebral canal\",\"e\":\"The spinal cord, not the sympathetic chain, runs in the vertebral canal and is covered by the meningeal layers and bathed in cerebrospina6187\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":151,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old heavy smoker presents to his GP with a drooping left eyelid and dryness affecting the left side of his face. His GP notes he has a left-sided ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis, and a diagnosis of Horner syndrome is made. A computed tomography (CT) scan shows a Pancoast tumour invading the stellate ganglion. Which one of the following descriptions best describes the relationships of the stellate ganglion?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following descriptions best describes the relationships of the stellate ganglion?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the neck of the first rite\",\"e\":\"In around 80% of people the inferior cervical ganglion fuses with the first thoracic ganglion to form the stellate ganglion (otherwise known as the cervicothoracic ganglion). The stellate ganglion lies at the level of C7, in front of the neck of the first rib.\\n\\n¿ Internal carotid artery and nerve plexus\\n\\nSuperior cervical ganglion\\n\\nMiddle cervical ganglion\\n\\nInferior cervical C8 ganglion\\n\\nlunchbie Anatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the scalene tubercle\",\"e\":\"The stellate ganglion is posterior to the scalene tubercle where the scalenus anterior inserts. A groove is found on the first rib anterior to the scalene tubercle for the subclavian vein. The subclavian artery runs behind the scalenus 6789r muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lateral to the angle of the first rib\",\"e\":\"The stellate ganglion is medial to the angle of the first rib, not lateral.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior to the neck of the first rib\",\"e\":\"The neck of the first rib is lateral of the vertebral column, therefore the stellate ganglion is anterior to the neck of the first rib.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The stellate ganglion is posterior to the subclavian artery and anterior to the scalenus medius.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":154,\"stem\":\"An 81-year-old man presents with progressive dysphagia and weight loss. An oesophago-\\n\\ngastroduodenoscopy (OGD) reveals a tumour proximal to the gastro-oesophageal junction and histology confirms this is an oesophageal adenocarcinoma.\\n\\nWhich one of the following best describes where the oesophagus passes through the diaphragm?\\n\\nA Posterior to the medial arcuate ligament\\n\\nPosterior to the lateral arcuate ligament\\n\\nC Through the central tendon\\n\\nD Through the muscular sling of the left crus\\n\\nThrough the muscular sling of the right crus E\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Posterior to the medial arcuate ligament\",\"e\":\"The oesophagus passes anterior to the medial arcuate ligament. The arcuate ligament forms at the base of the diaphragm where the left and right crura join at the 12th vertebrae.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Posterior to the lateral arcuate ligament\",\"e\":\"Again, the oesophagus passes anterior to the arcuate ligaments.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Through the central tendon\",\"e\":\"It passes to the left of the central tendon, not through it.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Through the muscular sling of the left crus\",\"e\":\"It is through the muscular sling of the right, not left crus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Through the muscular sling of the right crus\",\"e\":\"The oesophagus is formed at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage. It is crossed anteriorly by the left main bronchus, lies behind the left atrium and passes through the muscular part of the diaphragm to the left of the central tendon, through the oesophageal hiatus (labelled below), which is formed by the muscular sling of the right crus. It is innervated, in part, by the recurrent laryngeal nerve, not the phrenic nerve. Central Sternal tendon attachment\\n\\nCaval Gesophageal hiatus hiatus\\n\\n- Aortic hiatus\\n\\nVertebral attachment\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":157,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man is undergoing decompression of thoracic outlet syndrome. During this procedure, the thoracic duct is thought to be injured and a drain is placed. Into where does the thoracic duct normally drain?\",\"lead\":\"Into where does the thoracic duct normally drain?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"IS P. Azygos vein\",\"e\":\"The bronchial veins drain into the azygos/hemiazygos system.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left brachiocephalic vein\",\"e\":\"The left brachiocephalic vein (labelled 'brachiocephalic vein' below) drains blood from: the cervical vertebrae via both vertebral veins; the thyroid gland by the inferior thyroid veins; the first left intercostal space via the left superior intercostal veins; and all the anterior intercostal spaces by the anterior intercostal veins draining into the internal thoracic veins.\\n\\nThe thoracic duct enters the vein at its commencement behind the left sternoclavicular joint.\\n\\nInternal jugular vein\\n\\nCommon carotid artery\\n\\nSubclavian vein Superior Brachiocephalic vein vona cava țeachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left external jugular vein\",\"e\":\"The left external jugular vein drains into the left brachiocephalic vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right brachiocephalic vein\",\"e\":\"The thoracic duct drains into the left, not the right. brachiocephalic vein.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The superior vena cava drains into the right atrium and receives venous biood from the left brachiocephalic vein into which the thoracic duct drains.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":160,\"stem\":\"During a difficult oesophagectomy in a 55-year-old man there is concern the thoracic duct may have been injured in the mediastinum. Which one of the following descriptions best describes the route of the thoracic duct within the mediastinum in relation to the oesophagus?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following descriptions best describes the route of the thoracic duct within the mediastinum in relation to the oesophagus?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It passes anterior and to the left of the oesophagus at T5 level\",\"e\":\"It passes posterior, not anterior, to the desophagus at the level of T5.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It passes anterior and to the right of the thoracic duct at T5 level\",\"e\":\"The question refers to the route of the thoracic duct in relation to the osophagus, not the desophagus. in relation to the thoracic duct.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It passes posterior and to the left of the oesophagus at T5 level\",\"e\":\"The thoracic duct begins below the diaphragm as the cisterna chyli and then ascends through the aortic opening in the diaphragm, to the right of the descending aorta. It passes behind the oesophagus and then to the left of the oesophagus at the T5 level. It then runs upwards on the left side of the oesophagus into the neck. Here it crosses the subclavian artery to enter the left brachiocephalic vein. At the root of the neck, the thoracic duct receives the left jugular, subclavian and bronchomediastinal lymph trunks, although they may occasionally drain directly into the adjacent large vessels. The thoracic duct therefore conveys all the lymph from the lower limbs, pelvic cavity, abdominal cavity, left side of the thorax, head and neck and the left arm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It passes posterior and to the left of the oesophagus at T10 level\",\"e\":\"It passes posterior and to the left of the osophagus at the T5 level, not the T10.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It passes posterior and to the right of the thoracic duct at T5 level\",\"e\":\"Again, the question asks about the relationship of the thoracic duct to the oesophagus and not the other way around.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":162,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old man is admitted with a tearing chest pain radiating through to his back and he has unequal radial pulses on examination. He remains haemodynamically stable and a computed tomography (CT) angiogram shows a type-A thoracic aneurysm with dissection.\\n\\nWhich one of the following vessels normally arises from the aortic arch?\\n\\nA Left subclavian artery\\n\\nLeft vertebral artery B\\n\\nRight internal mammary artery\\n\\nD Right common carotid artery\\n\\nRight subclavian artery E\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The arch of the aorta commences from the manubriosternal joint and passes backwards over\\n\\nthe left bronchus to reach the body of the T4 vertebra just to the left of the midline. The\\n\\narch is crossed on its left side by the phrenic and vagus nerves as they pass downwards in\\n\\nfront of and behind the lung root, respectively. The left subclavian, left common carotid and the brachiocephalic trunk (coloured below) arise from the arch of the aorta.\\n• Arch of aorta\\n\\nSubclavian arteries\\n• I Common carotid arteries\\n• Brachiocephalic trunk\\n\\nteachmeanatomy The #1 Applied Muman Anatomy Site on the Web.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left vertebral artery\",\"e\":\"The left vertebral artery comes off the left subclavian artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Right internal mammary artery\",\"e\":\"The right internal mammary artery comes off the right subclavian artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right common carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The right common carotid arises from the brachiocephalic trunk.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The right subclavian artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":165,\"stem\":\"Following complex surgery for a bypass graft from the right subclavian artery to the brachial artery, a patient is noted to have a hoarse voice post-operatively Which one of the following structures is most likely to have been damaged?\",\"lead\":\"Following complex surgery for a bypass graft from the right subclavian artery to the brachial artery, a patient is noted to have a hoarse voice post-operatively Which one of the following structures is most likely to have been damaged?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Brachial plexus\",\"e\":\"The brachial plexus is closely related to the subclavian artery, however, damage to the plexus would probably result in neurological signs and symptoms in the right arm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Accessory nerve\",\"e\":\"The accessory nerve is the 11th cranial nerve and innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Phrenic nerve 2-\",\"e\":\"Damage to the phrenic nerve leads to a raised hemi-diaphragm and potentially some respiratory distress depending on their pre-morbid function.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Recurrent laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The subclavian artery is closely related to the brachial plexus, the phrenic and vagus nerves (and recurrent laryngeal nerve on the right side only). The stellate ganglion is deeper within the neck (anterior to the neck of the first rib) and the thoracic duct lies on the left side. The approach to the subclavian artery may be supraclavicular and involve removal or division of the clavicle, dissection of the scalenus anterior, pectoralis major and pectoralis minor muscles. The hoarse voice is suggestive of an injury to the right recurrent laryngeal nerve. The left recurrent laryngeal nerve is given off from the left vagus nerve at the level of the aortic arch and curves behind (as shown below), running in the trachea-oesophageal groove to reach the larynx. The right recurrent laryngeal nerve curves around the right subclavian artery.\\n\\nтиресіся laryngesi nerve\\n\\nlarynx\\n\\nleft recurrent laryngeal nerve\\n\\naortic arch\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the vagus nerve is likely to result in wide ranging autonomic symptoms - the hoarse voice is indicative of a recurrent laryngeal nerve injury, this nerve is a branch of the vagus nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":168,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old woman complains of a long history of pain and paraesthesiae along the ulnar border of her left arm and forearm precipitated by placing her upper limb in certain positions. She reports episodes of dropping items in her left hand recently and also episodes of left arm swelling. On examination wasting of the small muscles of the left hand is noted. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"visi Carpal tunnel syndrome\",\"e\":\"Carpal tunnel syndrome is compression of the median nerve through the carpal tunnel.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cubital tunnel syndrome\",\"e\":\"This results in compression of the ulnar nerve through the cubital tunnel at the level of the medial epicondyle\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"DR - Erb's palsy\",\"e\":\"Erb's palsy affects C5/6 and is most commonly caused by shoulder dystocia during labour.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Klumpke's paralysis\",\"e\":\"Klumpke's paralysis is a palsy affecting the lower trunk of the brachial plexus. It results in paralysis of the intrinsic hand muscles and numbness in the C8/T1 dermatome. Changes with movement and swelling of the arm would no62:0fen.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thoracic outlet syndrome\",\"e\":\"Cervical rib is present in about 0.5% of the population, of which 60% are symptomatic. Symptoms due to the presence of a cervical rib depend on the structure it is compressing. Neurological symptoms are the most common presentation, usually compression of the C8 and T1 nerve roots, which causes pain and paraesthesiae on the ulnar aspect of the arm and forearm and wasting of the small muscles of the hand. Vascular changes are seen less often. The arm can become swollen as a result of venous compression. Compression of the subclavian artery can lead to thrombus formation, emboli, ischaemic changes, and even gangrene.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":170,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old male smoker presents with central chest pain radiating to his neck. His electrocardiogram (ECG) shows ST elevation in chest leads V5 and V6. Coronary angiography shows occlusion of the circumflex artery and, following successful angiography. this can be seen to give rise to the posterior interventricular branch. In approximately what proportion of people does the posterior descending artery not arise from the right coronary artery?\",\"lead\":\"In approximately what proportion of people does the posterior descending artery not arise from the right coronary artery?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1%\",\"e\":\"The number is higher than this - 15% of patients have a posterior descending artery that does not arise from the right coronary artery alone.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"20%\",\"e\":\"In around 15% of patients, the posterior descending (posterior interventricular) artery does not arise from the right coronary artery, instead arising directly from the left coronary artery (in around 10%) or via a co-dominant system where it arises from both (in around 20%). The interventricular septum receives its blood supply from the descending branches from the left coronary (LCA) and right coronary arteries (RCA). The RCA travels in the coronary groove to reach the posterior surface of the heart where it anastomoses with the circumflex branch of LCA.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"40%\",\"e\":\"Around 30% of patients have a left-dominant or co-dominant system in the heart.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"60%\",\"e\":\"30% of patients do not have a right-dominant system.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"90%\",\"e\":\"70% of patients have a right-dominant system where the posterior descending artery arises from the right coronary artery.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":172,\"stem\":\"Following excision of a left cervical rib a 28-year-old woman is found to have a milky, white fluid in her drain.\\n\\nWhat structure is most likely to have been damaged?\\n\\nCisterna chyli A\\n\\nHemiazygos vein B\\n\\nLeft subclavian artery C\\n\\nDo Left subclavian vein\\n\\nThoracic duct\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cisterna chyli\",\"e\":\"The cisterna chyli is a small sac within the abdomen that is a dilation of the lymphatic system as it receives tributaries from below. It usually lies to the right of the aorta at L1 next to the right crus of the diaphragm, and from it arises the thoracic duct.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hemiazygos vein\",\"e\":\"The hemiazygos vein carries venous blood in the lower left side of the thorax, being formed beneath the diaphragm at the junction of the left ascending lumber and subcostal veins. Injury would not result in the symptoms described.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The left subclavian artery arises directly from the aortic arch and ascends behind the sternoclavicular joint and above the first rib, between the anterior and middle scalene muscles. Injury would be possible in the surgery described but would result in brisk arterial bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Left subclavian vein\",\"e\":\"The left subclavian vein drains blood from the upper limb and external head and neck. It is formed at the lateral border of the first rib and terminates as the left brachiocephalic vein when it joins with the left internal jugular. It is located anterior to the anterior scalene muscle. Injury would result in brisk venous bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thoracic duct\",\"e\":\"The thoracic duct is formed at the upper end of the cisterna chyli and travels on the right side of the aorta between the aorta and azygos vein before crossing over to the left side around the T4/5 level. After crossing to the left, it continues anterior to the subclavian artery and behind the carotid sheath, before terminating at the junction of the left subclavian vein and internal jugular vein. Injury results in chylothorax. It carries lymph from the lower limbs, abdominal and pelvic regions, left thorax, left head and neck as well as the left arm.\\n\\nA Cisterna chyli\\n\\nThe cisterna chyli is a small sac within the abdomen that is a dilation of the lymphatic system as it receives tributaries from below. It usually lies to the right of the aorta at L1 next to the right crus of the diaphragm, and from it arises the thoracic duct.\\n\\nHemiazygos vein\\n\\nThe hemiazygos vein carries venous blood in the lower left side of the thorax, being formed beneath the diaphragm at the junction of the left ascending lumbar and subcostal veins. Injury would not result in the symptoms described.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":175,\"stem\":\"A 76-year-old man who is a long-term smoker presents with a headache and facial swelling., he reports episodes of haemoptysis and significant weight loss. On further questioning Examination reveals a plethoric face and neck with distended veins on his upper chest and neck. A computed tomography (CT) scan reveals a bronchial tumour causing superior vena cava (SVC) obstruction. At what level does the SVC enter the right atrium?\",\"lead\":\"At what level does the SVC enter the right atrium?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"At the level the azygos vein drains into it\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein drains in to the SVC after arching over the right main bronchus from posteriorly at the level of T4-5.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Behind the first costal cartilage\",\"e\":\"The first right costal cartilage is the landmark for the formation of the SVC by the union of the left and right brachiocephalic veins.\\n\\nLevel of sternal angle D\\n\\nThe sternal angle is a landmark for many structures in the thorax, such as the aortic arch, the junction of the azygos vein and SVC, bifurcation of the trachea, looping of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve around the aortic arch.\\n\\nE Vertebral level T8\\n\\nT8 is the level at which the inferior vena cava (IVC) passes through the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Behind the third costal cartilage\",\"e\":\"The SVC enters the right atrium behind the right third costal cartilage. This can be seen in the image where the transthoracic plane (a line drawn through the angle of Louis and therefore the second costal cartilage and T4/5 level) is highlighted in yellow. The SVC can be seen entering the right atrium below this.\\n\\nPulmonary Superior trunk vena cava\\n\\nLevel of sternal angle\\n\\nBorder of Ascending pericardium aorta\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Level of sternal angle\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vertebral level T8\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":178,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man undergoes an oesophagectomy for oesophageal adenocarcinoma. Dissection around the intrathoracic oesophagus is very difficult, with significant bleeding Which one of the following is a posterior left relation of the intrathoracic oesophagus?\",\"lead\":\"Dissection around the intrathoracic oesophagus is very difficult, with significant bleeding Which one of the following is a posterior left relation of the intrathoracic oesophagus?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SPI Aortic arch\",\"e\":\"Arch of Superior aorta vena cava\\n\\nAccessory hemiazygos vein Azygos vein\\n\\nHemiazygos vein\\n\\nTeachMeAnatomy\\n\\nA Aortic arch\\n\\nThe aortic arch is anterior to the oesophagus initially, and as the arch descends it becomes the descending thoracic aorta, which is to the left of the oesophagus initially, then posterior to the oesophagus at the T10 level where the oesophagus passes through the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"1AM Azygos vein:\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein is to the right of the aorta in the thorax, not posterior and left.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left common carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The left common carotid artery arises directly from the aortic arch and ascends upwards towards the neck within the carotid sheath. It is an anterior-left relation to the oesophagus in the cervical region.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hemiazygos vein Vi\",\"e\":\"The hemiazygos vein (shown in yellow in the image) is formed beneath the diaphragm at the junction of the left ascending lumbar and subcostal veins. It travels up in to the thorax through the left crus of the diaphragm and ascends on the left side to around the T9 level, where it crosses over to join the azygos vein on the right, travelling behind the cesophagus when it does so. Within the thorax the relations of the oesophagus are:\\n• anterior:\\n• left common carotid artery\\n• trachea\\n• left main bronchus\\n• pericardium\\n• posterior:\\n• thoracic vertebrae\\n• thoracic duct\\n• hemiazygos vein o the descending aorta below\\n• on the left:\\n• left subclavian artery\\n• aortic arch o left vagus nerve and its recurrent laryngeal branch\\n• thoracic duct\\n• left pleura\\n• on the right:\\n• right pleura\\n• azygos vein.\\n\\nis TT AS ime \\\\ rn on at oi & } ia * DN Arch of Superior ame ~ a slat vena cava a aA Za ars [eS wh Kf (g8S)\\\\< an Accessory aa EN hemiazygos vein acs ~ SS Azygos vein Ks Bh LN LS ot Hemiazygos vein ra ~~\\n\\n3 a) nZ; abe. ‘ Sie i co f] ~ P<; 4(C hMeAnaton\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right common carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The right common carotid artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk and ascends within the carotid sheath into the neck as an antero-lateral relation of the oesophagus in the cervical region. The right common carotid is therefore right in relation to the oesophagus, not left.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":182,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old giri is brought to the Emergency Department following a horse riding accident. She is very distressed and in a lot of pain. From the history the horse fell backwards and landed on the left side of her chest. On examination there is extensive bruising over the left chest and it can be seen to move in when she inspires. Auscultation reveals bilateral air entry and her trachea is central. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"AMS Chylothorax\",\"e\":\"Chylothorax occurs when chyle leaks from the thoracic duct in to the pleural space. It commonly occurs after injury to the thoracic duct, eg in oesophagectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"LA Flail chest\",\"e\":\"A flail chest occurs when three or more contiguous ribs are fractured anteriorly and posteriorly, leaving a discrete disconnected segment of the thoracic wall that lacks bony continuity with the rest of the thoracic cage. Paradoxical respiration, such as in the case described, occurs due to changes in intrathoracic pressure. Flail chest classically results rom high energy blunt trauma, as in high-speed road traffic accidents. The definitive management of flail chest is to re-expand the affected (contused or collapsed) lung, ensure adequate oxygenation, provide sufficient pain relief and judicious fluid resuscitation. Some patients may benefit from a short period of intubation and positive pressure ventilation. Surgical stabilisation of the chest is an option but is rarely necessary in the management of flail chest.\\n\\nChylothorax occurs when chyle leaks from the thoracic duct in to the pleural space. It commonly occurs after injury to the thoracic duct, eg in oesophagectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemothorax\",\"e\":\"Haemothorax is blood in the pleural cavity. Although a differential in trauma, it would cause reduced air entry and reduced chest movement rather than paradoxical breathing. Any pleural effusion in the context of trauma should be considered a haemothorax until proved otherwise.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"Pneumothorax\\n\\nAlthough a pneumothorax and haemothorax can occur in combination with a flail chest, a pneumothorax would lead to decreased breath sounds on the affected side and hyper-\\n\\nresonance to percussion. Tension pneumothorax would also present with tracheal deviation\\n\\nand hypotension.\\n\\nE Tension pneumothorax\\n\\nA tension pneumothorax would present with reduced air entry and reduced chest wall movement on the affected side, as well as hyper-resonance to percussion. The trachea would be deviated away from the pneumothorax and the patient would probably be hypotensive due to impaired venous return. Immediate management of a tension pneumothorax is needle decompression through the second intercostal space in the mid-clavicular line, followed by definitive management, which is a chest drain.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tension pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"A tension pneumothorax would present with reduced air entry and reduced chest wall movement on the affected side, as well as hyper-resonance to percussion. The trachea would be deviated away from the pneumothorax and the patient would probably be hypotensive due to impaired venous return. Immediate management of a tension pneumothorax is needle decompression through the second intercostal space in the mid-clavicular line, followed by definitive management, which is a chest drain.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":185,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman is noted to have a left phrenic nerve injury after excision of a left cervical rib. On chest X-ray she has a raised left sided hemidiaphragm. Which one of these descriptions best describes the relation of the phrenic nerve in this area?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these descriptions best describes the relation of the phrenic nerve in this area?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior to scalenus anterior\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerves run over the anterior of the scalenus anterior muscle - this can be seen in the image below, they are highlighted in yellow. The phrenic nerves then run anterior to both lung hila (in contrast to the wagus nerves that run posterior to the hila) It arises from the C3- 5 nerve roots and supplies the left and right dome of the diaphragm.\\n\\nLeft phrenic Pericardium nerve\\n\\nRight dome of the diaphragm\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Between the scalenus anterior and scalenus medius\",\"e\":\"The brachial plexus roots exit between scalenus medius and anterior, and the subclavian artery runs inferiorly between the two and o6223e first.rib.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medial to scalenus anterior\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve runs over the anterior surface of scalenus anterior, not the medial surface.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior to scalenus medius\",\"e\":\"The first rib is the posterior relation of the scalenus medius muscle. The scalenus posterior muscle inserts onto the second rib.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior to scalenus posterior\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve runs anterior to the anterior scalene, not posterior to the posterior scalene.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":188,\"stem\":\"Following a difficult cervical rib excision, the surgeon is concerned about a possible injury to the pleura. The patient reports ipsilateral chest pain worse on inspiration, is noted to have reduced breath sounds and the trachea remains central. There is hyper-resonance evident on percussion of the ipsilateral chest wall. Which one of the following complications is most likely from the history?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following complications is most likely from the history?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"M'S. Chylothorax\",\"e\":\"A chylothorax would lead to similar signs to a pleural effusion - stony dullness to percussion and decreased breath sounds. It occurs due to damage to the thoracic duct and leak of chyle in to the pleural space, and is a recognised complication of desophagectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemothorax\",\"e\":\"A haemothorax would lead to similar signs to a pleural effusion - stony dullness to percussion and decreased breath sounds.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pleural effusion\",\"e\":\"A pleural effusion would present with dullness to percussion (usually referred to as ‘stony dullness’) and reduced breath sounds.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"The signs of hyper-resonance to percussion, reduced air entry and pleuritic pain all point to a pneumothorax. Fluid rather than air in the pleural space leads to dullness to percussion alongside reduced air entry. The pleura is a serous membrane that invests the lung itself (visceral pleura) and lines the cavity containing the lung (parietal pleura). These two parts of the membrane are continuous at the lung hilum and enclose a small space occupied by about 5-10 ml of fluid produced mainly by the parietal layer. Due to the elasticity of the lungs, intrapleural pressure is usually negative during quiet inspiration and expiration, but during forced expiration contraction of expiratory muscles raises the pressures in the pleural spaces and within the lungs and bronchi.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tension pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"The trachea is reported as central, which points away from a tension pneumothorax. The signs of reduced air entry and hyper-resonance to percussion would be present in both however. With a tension pneumothorax one would also expect the patient to have circulatory compromise as the increased pleural pressure impedes venous return leading to hypotension.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":191,\"stem\":\"Following a difficult tracheostomy, the surgeon is concerned about the nerve supply to the trachea being damaged.\\n\\nWhich one of the following nerves supplies the trachea?\\n\\nA Phrenic nerve\\n\\nGlossopharyngeal nerve\\n\\nC Recurrent laryngeal nerve\\n\\nSuperior laryngeal nerve\\n\\nE External laryngeal nerve\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"Explanation c-\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Phrenic nerve\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerves originate from the C3, 4 and 5 nerve roots and supply the left and right domes of the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glossopharyngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"Phrenic nerve\\n\\nThe phrenic nerves originate from the C3, 4 and 5 nerve roots and supply the lett and right domes of the diaphragm.\\n\\nGlossopharyngeal nerve B\\n\\nThe glossopharyngeal nerve supplies sensation to the oropharynx. The vagus nerve supplies sensation to the laryngopharynx via the internal laryngeal nerve above the cords, and the recurrent laryngeal nerve below.\\n\\nSuperior laryngeal nerve D\\n\\nThe superior laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid muscle via its external branch.\\n\\nExternal laryngeal nerve\\n\\nThe external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Recurrent laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerve (shown in purple below) supplies all the intrinsic muscles of the larynx, except for the cricothyroid, which is supplied by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. The recurrent laryngeal nerve also supplies sensation to the trachea below the vocal cords. On the left it forms as the vagus nerve passes over the aortic arch and runs underneath the arch to run up to the larynx in the trachea oesophageal groove.\\n\\n/ SUDOTOr nerve\\n\\nlarynx\\n\\nle't recurter _ laryngeal nerve\\n\\naortic arch\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superior laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The superior laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid muscle via its external branch.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"External laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid muscle.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":194,\"stem\":\"A 66-year-old man undergoes a left lung lobectomy for a tumour originating from the lingula. Which lobe of the left lung contains the lingula?\",\"lead\":\"Which lobe of the left lung contains the lingula?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SPI Lower lobe\",\"e\":\"The lower (or inferior) lobe of the left lung does not give rise to the lingula.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Middle lobe\",\"e\":\"The left lung, unlike the right, does not have a middle lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"ARLI Upper lobe:\",\"e\":\"The lingula is a branch of the left upper lobe. The left lung is divided into upper and lower lobes by a long deep oblique fissure. The upper lobe has a wide cardiac notch on its anterior border. The anteroinferior part of the upper lobe also has a small tongue-like projection called the lingula.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Apical lobe\",\"e\":\"There is no such lobe in the lungs, it is termed the upper lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Basal lobe\",\"e\":\"There is no such lobe in the lungs, it is termed the lower lobe.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":196,\"stem\":\"An 87-year-old woman recovering from a stroke is noted to be increasingly short of breath and confused. On examination, anteriorly her chest is clear but posteriorly harsh bronchial breathing is detected in the lower and mid-zone bilaterally. Pneumonia is diagnosed. Which of the following is correct regarding the anatomy of the lungs?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is correct regarding the anatomy of the lungs?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The right lung has two lobes separated by oblique fissure\",\"e\":\"The right lung has three lobes, not two - the upper, middle and lower lobes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The right lung has three lobes separated by oblique and horizontal fissures\",\"e\":\"The right lung (the medial border of which is shown below) has three lobes - the upper and middle are separated by the horizontal fissure, and the midde and lower by the oblique fissure: Lett Right Superior lobe Inferior lobe Middle lode Hilum - Horizontal fissure Obliquo fissuro\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Regarding the lung roots, the pulmonary artery lies superiorly and the pulmonary vein lies inferiorly while the bronchus lies anteriorly\",\"e\":\"Within the lung hila, the pulmonary artery lies superiorly, the pulmonary veins inferiorly, and the bronchi posteriorly (not anteriorly).\\n\\nThe anterior margin of the left lung is straight, unlike that of the right lung\\n\\nle anterior border of the left lung has a lateral deviation at the 4th to 6th costal cartilages to accommodate the heart.\\n\\nThe lung is composed of approximately 300 000 alveoli each around 0.3 mm in diameter\\n\\nThe human lung is composed of approximately 300 million alveoli each around 0.3 mm in\\n\\ndiameter.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The anterior margin of the left lung is straight, unlike that of the right lung\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The lung is composed of approximately 300 000 alveoli each around 0.3 mm in diameter\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":199,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old man is rushed into A&E after being stabbed in the chest following an assault. The knife is still in place and he is taken to theatre for urgent surgical exploration. The knife is found to have penetrated the anterior wall of the right atrium. Which one of the following structures are present within the anterior wall of the right atrium?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures are present within the anterior wall of the right atrium?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Conus arteriosus\",\"e\":\"The conus arteriosus is located in the right ventricle: Otherwise known as the infundibulum, it forms the outflow tract of the right ventricle leading to the pulmonary valve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Coronary sinus\",\"e\":\"Coronary sinus B\\n\\nThe coronary sinus lies in the atrioventricular groove on the postero-inferior aspect of the heart. It opens in to the posterior aspect of the right atrium next to the inferior vena cava (IVC).\\n\\nD Fossa ovalis\\n\\nThe fossa ovalis lies in the atrial septum, which is on the left aspect of the right atrium. It is formed by the closure of the foramen ovale.\\n\\nE Sinus venarum\\n\\nThe sinus venarum is the posterior (smooth) part of the right atrium. It joins the rough\\n\\nanterior part at the crista terminalis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Musculi pectinati\",\"e\":\"Musculi pectinati are located in the anterior part and the auricle of the right atrium (the embryological remnant of the true atrium). The posterior wall of the right atrium is smooth, and is derived from the sinus venarum. The right atrium itself forms the right border of the heart between the superior vena cava and IV. The right atrium can be seen opened from the front below - the auricle and its musculi pectinati within can be seen highlighted in red\\n\\nRight Superior vena cava auricle orifice Right ventricle\\n\\nCrista terminalis\\n\\nFossa ovalis\\n\\nInferlor vena cava Coronary sinus orifice orifice Anatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fossa ovalis\",\"e\":\"The fossa ovalis lies in the atrial septum, which is on the left aspect of the right atrium. It is formed by the closure of the foramen ovale.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sinus venarum\",\"e\":\"The sinus venarum is the posterior (smooth) part of the right atrium. It joins the rough anterior part at the crista terminalis.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":202,\"stem\":\"A 39-year-old woman undergoes a transthoracic echo following the recent onset of heart failure with cyanosis: This confirms an atrial septal defect (ASD) and it is thought she has Eisenmenger syndrome. The foramen ovale normally closes at birth to form the oval fossa. Which one of the following best describes the location of the oval fossa (fossa ovalis) within the right atrium?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describes the location of the oval fossa (fossa ovalis) within the right atrium?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterolateral wall\",\"e\":\"The fossa ovalis lies on the posteromedial wegagg tre anterolateral wall\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anteromedial wall\",\"e\":\"Anteromedial wall B\\n\\nThe anteromedial wall is the location of the opening to the right auricle superiorly. The fossa ovalis lies on the medial wall, but more posteriorly in the anatomical position.\\n\\nInferior aspect\\n\\nThe opening of the inferior vena cava (IVC) lies on the inferior aspect of the right atrium, with the tricuspid valve anteriorly.\\n\\nD Posterolateral wall\\n\\nThe posterolateral wall is the location of the crista terminalis (and therefore the SA node).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"DRY Inferior aspect\",\"e\":\"The opening of the inferior vena cava (IVC) lies on the inferior aspect of the right atrium, with the tricuspid valve anteriorly.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterolateral wall\",\"e\":\"The posterolateral! wall is the location of the crista terminalis (and therefore the SA node).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posteromedial wall\",\"e\":\"The fossa ovalis (highlighted in green in the image) is found on the interatrial septum, which forms the posteromedial wall of the right atrium. It is formed by the closure of the foramen ovale at birth, when the septum primum and septum secundum fuse.\\n\\nRight Superior vena cava auricle orifice Right ventricle\\n\\nCrista terminalis\\n\\nFossa ovalis\\n\\nInferlor vena cava Coronary sinus orifice orifice Anatomy\\n\\nA Anterolateral wall\\n\\nThe fossa ovalis lies on the posteromedial web240 the anterolateral wall.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":205,\"stem\":\"During a coronary angiography a tight stenosis is noted at the proximal aspect of the left coronary artery. Occlusion of which one of the following branches of this artery is the most severe?\",\"lead\":\"Occlusion of which one of the following branches of this artery is the most severe?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pr Anterior interventricular\",\"e\":\"The anterior interventricular (anterior descending) artery supplies the anterior and lateral left ventricle and the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. Occlusion leads to widespread ST elevation and left ventricular infarction with a high mortality - it is also known as the widowmaker' artery.\\n\\nhouses if out wal-ai, sir-lias Richt Coronary Artery Supplies the right atrium th Man InelAnent and the right ventricle. the tvern\\n\\nSopping more amaT\\n\\nRight Coronary Artery Supplies the right otrium and night watrice\\n\\nFosterior interventricular Artery Sugpies the right ond left ventacles and the interventricular septum\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"NAMI Atrioventricular nodal\",\"e\":\"The atrioventricular (AV) nodal artery is usually a branch of the right coronary artery, not the left.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"ARLI Circumflex\",\"e\":\"The circumflex artery travels in the atrioventricular groove and supplies the lateral and posterior aspects of the left ventricle..\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Marginal\",\"e\":\"The left circumflex artery gives off a number of marginal arteries, also known as obtuse marginal arteries (named by number eg OM1) that supply the lateral aspect of the left ventricle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior interventricular\",\"e\":\"The posterior interventricular artery can be a branch of the left or right coronary arteries (known as cardiac dominance), It is usually a branch of the right coronary artery, not the left It supplies the posterior third of the interventricular septum and the diaphragmatic/inferior aspect of the left ventricle.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":208,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man with severe aortic stenosis undergoes coronary artery bypass grafting for coronary artery disease while having an aortic valve replacement. Their exercise tolerance after the immediate post-operative period is markedly better. Which one of the following is a commonly used arterial conduit?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a commonly used arterial conduit?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1St. Inferior epigastric artery\",\"e\":\"The inferior epigastric artery arises from the external iliac artery as its last branch before becoming the femoral artery underneath the inguinal ligament. It anastomoses with the superior epigastric artery around the umbilicus, and is therefore not a common candidate for bypass grafting.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left internal mammary artery\",\"e\":\"The internal mammary arteries are possible donor arteries for coronary artery bypass grafting, typically with good long-term results. The aortic valve itself is shown below, unfolded from the interior with the openings for the coronary arteries above the right and left cusps of the valve respectively.\\n\\nAortic Left coronary sinus artery\\n\\nRight coronary artery Lunule\\n\\nRight Nodule cusp\\n\\nA Inferior epigastric artery\\n\\nThe inferior epigastric artery arises from the external iliac artery as its last branch before becoming the femoral artery underneath the inguinal ligament. It anastomoses with the superior epigastric artery around the umbilicus, and is therefore not a common candidate for bypass grafting.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Short saphenous vein\",\"e\":\"It is the long saphenous vein, rather than the short, that is a commonly used conduit for bypass grafting.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gastroduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"Short saphenous vein\\n\\nIt is the long saphenous vein, rather than the short, that is a commonly used conduit for bypass grafting.\\n\\nDo Gastroduodenal artery\\n\\nThe gastroepiploic artery (rather than the gastroduodenal) artery can be used for coronary artery bypass grafting, but is less common than other conduits such as the long saphenous vein or the internal mammary arteries.\\n\\nE Ulnar artery\\n\\nThe radial artery (rather than the ulnar artery) is a common conduit used for bypass grafting, as it can be sacrificed if there is good collateral circulation to the hand via the ulnar artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulnar artery\",\"e\":\"The radial artery (rather than the ulnar artery) is a common conduit used for bypass grafting, as it can be sacrificed if there is good collateral circulation to the hand via the ulnar artery.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":211,\"stem\":\"During angiography a 56-year-old diabetic patient is noted to have stenosis of the posterior interventricular artery Which one of the following most often gives rise to this vessel?\",\"lead\":\"During angiography a 56-year-old diabetic patient is noted to have stenosis of the posterior interventricular artery Which one of the following most often gives rise to this vessel?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior interventricular artery\",\"e\":\"This is a branch of the left coronary artery. It supplies the antero-lateral left ventricle and the as the anterior anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. It is otherwise known descending artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AAN Circumflex artery\",\"e\":\"The circumflex artery can give rise to the posterior interventricular (posterior descending) artery in a left-dominant system, but it more commonly arises from the right coronary artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left coronary artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior descending artery most commonly arises from the right, not the left coronary artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Marginal branch of the right coronary artery\",\"e\":\"The right marginal artery arises from the right coronary artery and supplies the right entricle, but it is a terminal branch. It does not give rise to the posterior descending artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right coronary artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior descending artery most commonly arises from the right coronary artery - this is termed a right dominant system. It travels on the postero-inferior aspect of the heart, supplying the posterior one-third of the interventricular septum and the inferior part of the left ventricle.\\n\\nRight Coronary Artary Supplies the night atrium and the nght ventrate\\n\\nteachmoanatomy\\n\\nRight Coronary Artery Suppres the right at-ur le Man Inal Art ond right ventrele lates the lot ent\\n\\nposterior Interventricular Artery Supplies the mohit ond felt rontricles and the interventricular septum\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":214,\"stem\":\"A 63-year-old long-term smoker presents with haemoptysis and weight loss. A chest X-ray shows a tumour and bronchoscopy reveals it to be at the carina. At what level is the carina?\",\"lead\":\"At what level is the carina?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Level with xiphoid process\",\"e\":\"The xiphoid process is at the level of the T10 vertebra.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Level with the inferior angle of the scapula\",\"e\":\"The angle of the scapula is at the T7 vertebral level in the anatomical position.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sternal angle of Louis\",\"e\":\"The sternal angle (or the angle of Louis) at the level of T4/5, marks an important plane in the thorax - the transthoracic plane (shown in ye low below) - which divides the superior and inferior mediastinum. On this level are the carina, the start and end of the aortic arch, the point at which the azygos vein joins the superior vena cava (SVC), the level at which the thoracic duct moves from right to left, and the level at which the left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops around the aortic arch.\\n\\nPulmonary Superior trunk vena cava\\n\\nLevel of sternal angle\\n\\nBorder of pericardium Ascending aorta\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Suprasternal notch\",\"e\":\"The suprasternal notch is at the level of T2/3.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transpyloric plane\",\"e\":\"The transpyloric plane is at the jevel of L1.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":217,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old male who is involved in a road traffic accident whilst driving his motorbike has a pneumothorax clinically which is confirmed on imaging. It is decided he requires a chest drain Which of the following best describes a safe location for insertion?\",\"lead\":\"It is decided he requires a chest drain Which of the following best describes a safe location for insertion?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Posterior to the mid-axillary line, 4\\\" intercostal space below 4h rib\",\"e\":\"Although this is within the ‘safe triangle,’ the typical site is in the 5th intercostal space, not the 4th. Additionally, drains are inserted above the rib below to avoid damage to the neurovascular bundle that runs on the inferior aspect of each rib.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Posterior to the mid-axillary line, 4m intercostal space above 5t rib\",\"e\":\"Chest drains are typically inserted in the 5th intercostal space, not the 4th.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the mid-axillary line, 4* intercostal space below 4h rib\",\"e\":\"Anterior to the mid-axillary line, 4t intercostal space below 4th rib\\n\\nChest drains are typically inserted in the 5th intercostal space, not the 4th. Chest drains should not be inserted underneath a rib to avoid damage to the intercostal neurovascular\\n\\nbundle.\\n\\nEznd intercostal space, midclavicular line\\n\\nThis is the site for needle decompression of a tension pneumothorax - it is not used for chest\\n\\ndrain insertion, however a chest drain will need to be inserted in the safe triangle following needle decompression.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the mid-axillary line, 5th intercostal space above 6h rib\",\"e\":\"The 'safe triangle' for chest drain insertion is described as a triangle formed by the anterior border of latissimus dorsi, the lateral border of pectoralis major, with the base at the 6th rib and the apex below the axilla. Typically, a chest drain is inserted in the 5th intercostal space, at the mid-axillary line or just anterior, entering just above the 6th rib to avoid damaging the intercostal neurovascular bundle which runs on the inferior aspect of the 5th rib. Once placed, the chest drain should be pointed upward in the pleural cavity if a pneumothorax is being drained, and downward if fluid eg a pleural effusion is being drained\\n\\nA Posterior to the mid-axillary line, 4th intercostal space below 4th rib\\n\\nAlthough this is within the 'safe triangle,' the typical site is in the 5th intercostal space, not he 4th. Additionally, drains are inserted above the rib below to avoid damage to the neurovascular bundle that runs on the inferior aspect of each rib.\\n\\nB Posterior to the mid-axillary line, 4th intercostal space above 5th rib\\n\\nChest drains are typically inserted in the 5th intercostal space, not the 4th.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line\",\"e\":\"This is the site for needle decompression of a tension pneumothorax - it is not used for chest drain insertion, however a chest drain will need to be inserted in the safe triangle following needle decompression.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":220,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old morbidly obese diabetic is referred with episodes of chest tightness on minimal exertion. His exercise electrocardiogram (ECG) shows ST depression, and an angiogram demonstrates left main stem stenosis Which one of the following best describes the classical anatomy of the left coronary artery?\",\"lead\":\"His exercise electrocardiogram (ECG) shows ST depression, and an angiogram demonstrates left main stem stenosis Which one of the following best describes the classical anatomy of the left coronary artery?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Divides into the circumflex and arteriovenous (AV) nodal branch\",\"e\":\"The left coronary artery divides shortly after formation into the left anterior descending (left interventricular and left circumflex arteries 6258\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Divides into the circumflex and left anterior descending artery\",\"e\":\"The left coronary artery arises from the left coronary cusp of the aortic valve (the left aortic sinus) and travels laterally underneath the left auricle, where it divides into the left ircumflex artery and the left anterior descending artery, with the circumflex artery also giving off the left marginal artery (LMA) - this is shown in schematic form below. Right Posterior Anterior Surface left Posterior Surface Surface\\n\\nAorta _ rA\\n• -\\n\\n:\\n•\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Divides into the circumflex and sinoatrial nodal branch\",\"e\":\"The left coronary artery divides in to the left anterior descending and left circumflex arteries. The SA nodal branch is usually given off from the right coronary artery (in around 60% of cases), not the left.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Divides into the posterior descending artery and anterior descending artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior descending artery is usually a branch of the right coronary artery rather than the left (although in some cases it can arise from the left circumflex artery in what is termed a left-dominant system). The left anterior descending artery (left interventricular) does arise from the left coronary artery shortly after it forms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Divides into the posterior descending artery and marginal artery\",\"e\":\"The posterior descending is usually a branch of the right coronary artery, not the left. The marginal artery (arteries) are given off as branches from the circumflex artery.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":223,\"stem\":\"A 51-year-old woman is rushed in following a road traffic accident. The patient was haemodynamically stable and a right-sided haemothorax was noted. A CT scan showed a contained haematoma of the azygos vein. At what level does the azygos vein enter the superior vena cava (SVC)?\",\"lead\":\"At what level does the azygos vein enter the superior vena cava (SVC)?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"T2\",\"e\":\"T2 is the level of the suprasternal notch, whereas the azygos vein joins the SVC at the T4/5 level.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"T4/5\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein arches over the right main bronchus from behind and drains directly in to the SVC at the T4/5 level (the transthoracic plane), which approximates to the manubriosternal angle. This level also marks the carina, the start and end of the aortic arch, the point at which the azygos vein joins the SVC, the level at which the thoracic duct moves from right to left, and the level at which the left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops around the aortic arch. The mediastinum is shown below from behind - the azygos vein can be seen ascending on the right side of the mediastinum and arching over the right lung hilum from behind to join the SVC. The azygos vein itself is on the right side, and receives blood from the posterior intercostal veins and segmental veins of the abdomen, It passes through the diaphragm via the aortic opening at T12 and ascends on the right side of the vertebral bodies posterior to the oesophagus. It terminates by arching over the hilum of the right lung to enter the SVC at the T4/5 level. On the left a variable pattern exists, which usually involves an accessory hemiazygos vein (superiorly) and hemiazygos vein (inferiorly)\\n\\nInferior thyroid artery\\n\\nOesophagus\\n\\nAzygous Thoracic aorta and vein besophogeal branches\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"T6\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein drains into the SVC at the T4/5 level, not the T6.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"T8\",\"e\":\"T8 is the level at which the inferior vena cava (IVC) travels through the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T10\",\"e\":\"T10 is the level at which the oesophagus travels through the diaphragm.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":226,\"stem\":\"A CT2 in cardiothoracic surgery performs his first midline sternotomy. During the procedure heavy bleeding is noted. Which one of the following structures is most likely to be damaged?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures is most likely to be damaged?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Descending aorta\",\"e\":\"The descending aorta lies posteriorly on the left side of the thorax. It should certainly not be a source of bleeding in a midline sternotom 6264\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The inferior vena cava enters the thorax through the diaphragm at the level of T8 vertebra and opens into the base of the right atrium. It is not contained within the mediastinum and so not affected during a midline sternotomy incision.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Internal thoracic artery\",\"e\":\"The internal thoracic (or internal mammary) arteries run 2 cm laterally to the lateral edge of the sternal border and are therefore unaffected by a midline sternotomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right brachiocephalic vein\",\"e\":\"A midline sternotomy is performed to gain access to the mediastinum and the heart. Structures at risk include the brachiocephalic artery, the left common carotid artery, the left subclavian artery, both brachiocephalic veins (shown in the image below), trachea, oesophagus, phrenic nerves, both vagi, thoracic duct, left recurrent laryngeal nerve and thymus in children.\\n\\nInternal jugular vein\\n\\nCommon carotid artery\\n\\nSubclavian vein Superior Brachiocephalic vein vona cava feschmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior epigastric artery\",\"e\":\"Inferior vena cava B\\n\\nThe inferior vena cava enters the thorax through the diaphragm at the level of T8 vertebra\\n\\nand opens into the base of the right atrium. It is not contained within the mediastinum and so not affected during a midline sternotomy incision.\\n\\nInternal thoracic artery\\n\\nThe internal thoracic (or internal mammary) arteries run 2 cm laterally to the lateral edge of the sternal border and are therefore unaffected by a midline sternotomy.\\n\\nE Superior epigastric artery\\n\\nThe superior epigastric artery is formed as one of the terminal branches of the internal thoracic artery, It supplies the anterior abdominal wall, anastomosing with the inferior epigastric around the umbilicus. It is therefore not affected in a midline sternotomy.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":229,\"stem\":\"A 76-year-old man is undergoing an oesophagectomy for adenocarcinoma of the distal oesophagus thought to be secondary to long standing GORD. Which part of the diaphragm does the oesophagus pass through?\",\"lead\":\"Which part of the diaphragm does the oesophagus pass through?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SP Central tendon\",\"e\":\"The central tendon is the location for the caval opening, through which passes the inferior vena cava (IVC) (and the right phrenic nerve). This allows the IVC to remain patent during respiration.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AAMI Left crus\",\"e\":\"The left crus arises from L1 and L2, compared with the right that arises from L1-L3. The oesophageal hiatus is formed by the right crus of the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Median arcuate ligament\",\"e\":\"The median arcuate ligament lies behind the diaphragm at T12. Underneath it passes the aorta, thoracic duct, and typically the azygos vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Peripheral muscular part\",\"e\":\"The left phrenic nerve pierces the peripheral muscular part of the diaphragm on the left side to supply it.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right crus\",\"e\":\"The oesophageal hiatus is at TIO behind the seventh costal cartilage, It is formed by the medial fibres of the right crus of the diaphragm as they erch across to the left side of the midline (as can be seen in the image). They form a tubular envelope around the cesophagus with an elliptical opening. In addition to the oesophagus, it also transmits both vagus nerves and ocesophegea! branches of the left gastric artery\\n\\nCentral Sternal a —: Seem, ast ° te. a - “Do cot _f/ gy sophageal hiatus A 4 a Oe /} - s — \\\"N\\\\ hiatus Fai, aie EN sarc = a La ‘ Wess. _~ ~To Ai ge Bs. ol M Pe =.) Bee, i v ‘ \\\"4.\\n\\nVertebral attachment © werearesnatorry\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":232,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man being worked up for an open abdominal aortic aneurysm repair undergoes a routine chest X-ray, which shows a widened mediastinum. Further imaging confirms a thoracic aneurysm affecting the aortic arch Which one of the following branches normally arises from the aortic arch?\",\"lead\":\"Further imaging confirms a thoracic aneurysm affecting the aortic arch Which one of the following branches normally arises from the aortic arch?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left internal mammary artery\",\"e\":\"The left and right internal mammary arteries (also known as internal thoracics) arise from the first part of the left and right subclavian arteries respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The left subclavian artery arises directly from the arch of the aorta. The coronary arteries are the first branches of the aorta, followed by the innominate artery (also known as the brachiocephalic trunk), then the left common carotid and left subclavian. The arch of the aorta commences from the manubriosternal joint and passes backwards over the left bronchus to reach the body of T4 vertebra just to the left of the midline. The arch is crossed on its left side by the phrenic and vagus nerves as they pass downwards in front of (phrenic), and behind (vagus) the hilum of the lung.\\n• Arch of aorta\\n• Subclavian arteries I Common carotid arteries\\n• Brachiocephalic trunk\\n\\n+ descending aorta\\n\\n.. -i\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Right common carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The right common carotid artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk rather than the aorta itself.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The right subclavian artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk rather than directly from the aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right vertebral artery\",\"e\":\"The vertebral! arteries (left and right) arise from the left and right subclavian arteries respectively.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":235,\"stem\":\"You are asked to assess a patient with a swollen leg and respiratory distress. On closer examination you note they are breathing heavily in the bed and suspect a pulmonary embolism may be present.\\n\\nRegarding the muscles of respiration, which one of the following statements is true?\\n\\nQuiet inspiration is predominantly due to the action of the diaphragm\\n\\nThe scalene muscles play an important role in quiet expiration\\n\\nC Active inspiration is caused by the internal intercostal muscles\\n\\nInternal oblique muscles alone are important in active expiration D\\n\\nThe external intercostal muscles pull the ribs medially and inferiorly during active expiration\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Quiet inspiration is predominantly due to the action of the diaphragm\",\"e\":\"The diaphragm is active in both normal and increased respiration. In normal quiet breathing the diaphragm itself is the major active muscle, whereas the accessory muscles of respiration are recruited during increased work of breathing\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The scalene muscles play an important role in quiet expiration\",\"e\":\"The scalene muscles are accessory muscles of respiration that act to raise the first rib, and are therefore recruited during increased work of breathing.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Active inspiration is caused by the internal intercostal muscles\",\"e\":\"The external intercostal are active during forced inspiration, and the internal intercostals during forced expiration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Internal oblique muscles alone are important in active expiration\",\"e\":\"The internal oblique muscles act during forced expiration, but do not act alone - there are other muscles that assist such as the latissimus dorsi and the anterior abdominal wall muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The external intercostal muscles pull the ribs medially and inferiorly during active expiration\",\"e\":\"The external intercostals raise the ribs during inspiration, as they are aligned so that their fibres run infero-medially. Their contraction therefore raises the ribs and expands the ribcage. This action is in contrast to the internal intercostals, which are active during expiration.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":237,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old diabetic man presents with central chest pain radiating to his neck. His ECG shows he has developed complete heart block thought to be secondary to ischaemia affecting the atrioventricular (AV) node. In what proportion of people is the AV node supplied by the right coronary artery?\",\"lead\":\"In what proportion of people is the AV node supplied by the right coronary artery?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1%\",\"e\":\"The right coronary artery supplies the AV node in 85-90% of people, not 1%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"25%\",\"e\":\"This is too low - the RCA supplies the AV node in 85-90% of people\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"50%\",\"e\":\"The right coronary artery supplies the AV nodé in around 85-90% of people, via the AV\\n\\nnodal branch.\\n\\nA 1%\\n\\nThe right coronary artery supplies the AV node in 85-90% of people, not 1%.\\n\\nB 25%\\n\\nThis is too low - the RCA supplies the AV node in 85-90% of people.\\n\\nC 50%\\n\\nDo not get the AV nodal supply confused with the SA nodal supply. The RCA supplies the AV\\n\\nnode in 85-90% of people, and the SA node in around 60% of people.\\n\\nD /O%\\n\\nThe AV node Is supplied by the RCA In 85-90% of people.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"70%\",\"e\":\"The AV node is supplied by the RCA in 85-90% of people.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"90%\",\"e\":\"The right coronary artery supplies the AV node in around 85-90% of people, via the AV nodal branch.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":239,\"stem\":\"A patient requires an axillary artery bypass for severe stenosis. The consultant is able to anticipate the branches present based on a nearby structure. The axillary artery is divided into three parts by which muscle?\",\"lead\":\"The axillary artery is divided into three parts by which muscle?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Teres major\",\"e\":\"The inferior border of the teres major muscle is the division between the axillary and brachial arteries, it does not divide the axillary artery\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Teres minor\",\"e\":\"Teres minor is one of the rotator cuff muscles, attaching to the greater tuberosity. It does not divide up the axillary artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"ARLI Pectoralis major\",\"e\":\"It is the pectoralis minor, not major, that divides up the axillary artery into three parts.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"The axillary artery is divided up into three parts by the pectoralis minor muscle - the first part before the pectoralis minor, the second part behind the pectoralis minor and the third part distal to the pectoralis minor. This can be seen in the image with the axillary artery in green passing beneath the cut edge of pectoralis minor at the upper aspect. Conveniently there are one, two and three branches from the first, second and third parts.+\\n\\nAxillary artery\\n\\nAnterior circumflex - Subscapular artery artery\\n\\nBrachial Posterior circumflex artery teachmeanatomy artery fine:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Latissimus dorsi\",\"e\":\"Latissimus dorsi does not divide up the axillary artery. 478\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":242,\"stem\":\"While performing a radical mastectomy, the surgeon injures the long thoracic nerve. Which one of the following muscles will be affected due to injury to this nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following muscles will be affected due to injury to this nerve?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior scalene\",\"e\":\"The anterior scalene muscle is innervated by the C5 and C6 nerve roots directly.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Middle scalene\",\"e\":\"The middle scalene is innervated by the C3-C8 nerve roots.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Serratus anterior\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve (of Bell) arises from the C5, 6 and 7 nerve roots. It runs over the anterior aspect of serratus anterior, innervating it. Serratus anterior arises from the upper 8 ribs and inserts on to the medial borderon the thoracic aspect of the scapula, holding it against the rib cage - it is shown in blue from the anterior aspect below. It is at risk of injury in radical breast surgery, causing paralysis of serratus anterior and in turn winging of the\\n\\nscapula.\\n\\nPectoralis minor Internal intercostals\\n\\nExternal Serratus intercostals anterior\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Subscapularis\",\"e\":\"Subscapularis is innervated by the upper and lower subscapular nerves.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Teres major\",\"e\":\"Teres major is innervated by the lower subscapular nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":245,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old woman presents to clinic with a winged right scapula. She has previously undergone a right mastectomy and level 3 axillary clearance. Regarding the serratus anterior muscle, which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding the serratus anterior muscle, which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Has most of its fibres inserted into the superior angle of the scapula\",\"e\":\"The serratus anterior arises from the upper 8 ribs and inserts into the entire medial border of the scapula.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AMI Rotates the scapula\",\"e\":\"The serratus anterior has a large origin from the upper 8 ribs - it is shown in blue in the image below. It inserts into the entire medial border of the scapula. Contraction of the upper or lower aspect of the muscles therefore rotates the scapula\\n\\nPectoralis minor Internal intercostals\\n\\nExternal Serratus intercostals anterior\\n\\nAnatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Retracts the scapula\",\"e\":\"Serratus anterior, when contracting, protracts the scapula rather than retracts it, and holds it against the rib cage.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Receives its nerve supply from the upper trunk of the brachial plexus\",\"e\":\"The serratus anterior muscle is innervated by the long thoracic nerve (of Bell), which arises from the C5, C6 and C7 nerve roots directly.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The long thoracic nerve is protected under fascia which covers the muscle and is therefore not vulnerable to damage at this point\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve runs down over the anterior surface of the muscle making it vulnerable to injury in breast and axillary surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":248,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old limping patient has attended your vascular clinic with progressive pain in both buttocks and thighs at rest that now wakes him up at night. He is also complaining of impotence. Despite best medical management his pain is worsening and he has been booked for an axillo bi-femoral bypass. Which of the following is most accurate in relation to the axillary artery?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is most accurate in relation to the axillary artery?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The axillary artery begins at the medial border of the first rib as a continuation of the subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The axillary artery begins at the lateral border of the first rib as a continuation of the subclavian artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The second part of the axillary artery is posterior to the posterior cord of the brachial plexus\",\"e\":\"The second part of the axillary artery is behind the pectoralis minor muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The axillary artery is divided into three parts by the pectoralis minor muscle\",\"e\":\"The axillary artery begins at the lateral border of the first rib as the continuation of the subclavian artery, and ends at the inferior border of the teres major. It is indeed divided up in to three parts by pec minor, as can be seen in the image below with the axillary artery in green and the cut edge of pec minort overlying it from above:\\n• from the lateral border of the first rib to the medial border of the pectoralis minor\\n• behind the pectoralis minor\\n• from the lateral aspect of the pectoralis minor to the inferior border of teres major.\\n\\nThe first part has one branch (superior thoracic), the second part gives off two branches (thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic), and the third part gives off three branches (subscapular, anterior circumflex humeral, and posterior circumflex humeral).\\n\\nAxillary artery\\n\\nAnterior circumflex Subscapular artery artery\\n\\nBrachial Posterior circumflex artery teachmeanatomy artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The first part of the axillary artery gives off the thoracoacromial artery\",\"e\":\"The first part of the axillary artery has one branch - the superior thoracic artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The lateral thoracic artery branches from the first part of the axillary artery\",\"e\":\"The lateral thoracic artery is a branch of the second part of the axillary artery, behind the pectoralis minor.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":251,\"stem\":\"A patient has had recurrent attacks of shingles. They are concerned they are having a new presentation. Itching sensation from the skin immediately over the base of the spine of your scapula is mediated through which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Itching sensation from the skin immediately over the base of the spine of your scapula is mediated through which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Accessory nerve\",\"e\":\"The spinal accessory nerve supplies motor function to the sternocleidomastoid muscle and trapezius. It does not have a sensory component.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Dorsal primary ramus of T2\",\"e\":\"Dorsal and ventral primary rami are the first branches from spinal nerves. Dorsal rami provide sensory innervation to the skin over the back and give motor innervation to the true back muscles; ventral rami supply sensory innervation to the skin over the limbs and the skin over the ventral side of the trunk, as well as motor innervation to the skeletal muscles of the neck, trunk and extremities. The dorsal and ventral roots of spinal nerves are not directly responsible for any sensory innervation to the skin. Dorsal and ventral rootlets emerge from the spinal cord to form the dorsal and ventral roots. The ventral roots contain efferent motor fibres to skeletal muscles, while the dorsal roots contain afferent sensory fibres. These roots combine to form the spinal nerve, which then gives off the primary rami.\\n\\nAccessory nerve\\n\\nThe spinal accessory nerve supplies motor function to the sternocleidomastoid muscle and\\n\\ntrapezius. It does not have a sensory component.\\n\\nDorsal root of T2\\n\\nThe dorsal roots themselves do not supply sensation to the skin, it is the primary rami that branch from the roots that supply sensation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Dorsal root of T2\",\"e\":\"The dorsal roots themselves do not supply sensation to the skin, it is the primary rami that branch from the roots that supply sensation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ventral primary ramus of T2\",\"e\":\"Ventral primary ramus of T2 D\\n\\nVentral primary rami from spinal nerve roots are responsible for sensation to the limbs. ventral trunk and motor innervation to all except the true back muscles.\\n\\nE Ventral root of T2\\n\\nThe ventral root, as with the dorsal root, does not supply sensation directly - it is the primary rami that branch from the nerve roots that give sensory supply to the skin. Additionally, it is the dorsal primary rami that supply skin over the back.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ventral root of T2\",\"e\":\"The ventral root, as with the dorsal root, does not supply sensation directly - it is the primary rami that branch from the nerve roots that give sensory supply to the skin. Additionally, it is the dorsal primary rami that supply skin over the back.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":254,\"stem\":\"A patient sees you in the upper limb clinic several months after a motor cycle accident. On careful examination you think there may be impairment of the dorsal scapular nerve. Which one of the following effects is most likely to be seen if the right dorsal scapular nerve is injured near its origin?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following effects is most likely to be seen if the right dorsal scapular nerve is injured near its origin?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Skin of the upper back on the right side would be numb\",\"e\":\"Skin over the back is innervated directly from dorsal primary rami, not the dorsal scapular nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The point of the right shoulder would droop\",\"e\":\"Shoulder elevation is maintained by the trapezius muscle, which is innervated by the spinal accessory nerve, and the deltoid muscle, which is innervated by the axillary nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Scapular retraction on the right would be weakened\",\"e\":\"The dorsal scapular nerve is a motor nerve from the C5 nerve root directly that innervates the rhomboids and levator scapulas. These muscles help to retract and elevate the scapula, so these movements would be weakened following that damage.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Extension of the right arm would be weakened\",\"e\":\"Shoulder extension is chiefly via the latissimus dorsi, innervated by the thoracodorsal nerve, and also via the teres minor (axillary nerve) and the teres major (lower subscapular nerve).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inability to adduct the right arm\",\"e\":\"The chief arm adductor is the latissimus dord 292rvated by the thoracodorsal nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":256,\"stem\":\"The superficial cervical artery (superficial branch of the transverse cervical artery) is severed in a road traffic accident. The patient has a significant haematoma which is managed with just compression.\\n\\nÓRLAAMSPA Which muscle would be affected the most?\\n\\nLevator scapulae\\n\\nRhomboid major B\\n\\nRhomboid minor\\n\\nSupraspinatus D\\n\\nLatissimus dorsi\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Levator scapulae\",\"e\":\"Levator scapulae is supplied by the dorsal scapular artery. This is usually the deep branch of the transverse cervical artery, but occasionally branches directly from the second or third part of the subclavian artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Rhomboid major\",\"e\":\"Rhomboid major is supplied by the dorsal scapular artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Rhomboid minor\",\"e\":\"Rhomboid minor is also supplied by the dorsal scapular artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Supraspinatus\",\"e\":\"The suprascapular artery is a branch of the thyrocervical trunk in the neck. At first, it passes downward and laterally across the anterior scalene muscle and phrenic nerve, being covered by the sternocleidomastoid muscle; it then crosses the subclavian artery and the brachial plexus, running behind and parallel with the clavicle and the subclavius muscle and beneath the inferior belly of the omohyoid to the superior border of the scapula It passes over the superior transverse scapular ligament. The artery then enters the supraspinous fossa of the scapula. It travels close to the bone, running between the scapula and the supraspinatus muscle, to which it supplies branches.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Latissimus dorsi\",\"e\":\"The latissimus dorsi is supplied by the thor 6294al artery.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":258,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in theatre performing a sternotomy and the subclavian artery is accidently damaged near its origin. Your foundation year doctor asks if you can simply tie it off and ligate the artery to stop the bleeding. What is the most appropriate answer?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate answer?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It depends on which side. The artery can be sacrificed on the left but not the right\",\"e\":\"Although the artery arises differently on the left and right (from the brachiocephalic trunk on the right and directly from the aorta on the left), the supply is the same. Ligation should be nonetheless reserved for patients who are unstable, and have multiple life-threatening injuries, extensive shoulder trauma or infected or ruptured aneurysms.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"No, the subclavian artery supplies the thyroid, breast and upper lobe of the lung\",\"e\":\"e subclavian artery does not supply the upper lobe of the lung. The branches of the subclavian artery are the vertebral, internal mar29%y. thyrocervical and costocervical trunks:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"No, the subclavian artery supplies the thyroid gland, breast, rectus abdominus, brainstem and diaphragm\",\"e\":\"The subclavian artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk on the right, and directly from the aorta on the left. It supplies:\\n• the thyroid gland via the inferior thyroid artery, a branch of the thyrocervical trunk the breast, rectus abdominus and diaphragm via the internal thoracic artery the brainstem, cerebellum and posterior cerebral circulation via the vertebral arteries\\n• the upper limb and muscles of the back.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Unless pre-operative angiography has been performed to verity the anatomy. in 50% of the population the subclavian supplies the brain stem and in these people it cannot be sacrificed\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect as the vertebral arteries arise from the subclavian arteries, which form the posterior cerebral circulation and supply the brainstem and cerebellum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The subclavian is a relatively minor artery and can be sacrificed with impunity ¢zar\",\"e\":\"This is clearly incorrect.\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":261,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman undergoes breast reconstruction using a pedicled latissimus dorsi muscle transfer What is the blood supply of this muscle?\",\"lead\":\"A 45-year-old woman undergoes breast reconstruction using a pedicled latissimus dorsi muscle transfer What is the blood supply of this muscle?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Axillary artery\",\"e\":\"Although the main blood supply to the latissimus dorsi is derived from the axillary artery. it is the thoracodorsal artery that actually supplies the latissimus dorsi (a branch of the subscapular artery that branches from the third part of the axillary artery\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Dorsal scapular artery\",\"e\":\"The dorsal scapular artery is a branch of the transverse cervical artery (or occasionally directly from the axillary artery that runs with the dorsal scapular nerve and supplies the levator scapulas and the rhomboids.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Scapular artery\",\"e\":\"There is no scapular artery, do not get confused with the dorsal scapular or subscapular arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thoracoacromial artery\",\"e\":\"The thoracoacromial artery is a branch from the second part of the axillary artery, supplying the pectoralis major, pectoralis minor, and pa299he deltoid muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thoracodorsal artery\",\"e\":\"The main blood supply to the latissimus dorsi is via the thoracodorsal artery, a branch of the subscapular artery (which branches from the third part of the axillary artery)\"}]},{\"t\":59,\"p\":263,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old diabetic with hypertension and a long history of smoking is due to undergo a CABG for triple vessel disease. They are noted to have an ejection systolic murmur on auscultation of the praecordium, therefore an aortic valve replacement will happen at the same time. Regarding the aortic valve, which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding the aortic valve, which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"visi It always has three leaflets\",\"e\":\"The aortic valve, along with the tricuspid and pulmonary valves, consists of three leaflets. However, a proportion of patients (1-2%) are born with a congenitally bicuspid valve, which predisposes them to calcification, aortic stenosis and regurgitation in later life. Unicuspid and quadricuspid valves are also possible.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The right coronary artery arises from the posterior aortic sinus\",\"e\":\"The right coronary artery arises from the right aortic sinus, and the left coronary artery from the left. The posterior sinus has no artery arising from it, and is therefore referred to as the non-coronary sinus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The SA node lies between the right and non-coronary cusps of the aortic valve\",\"e\":\"The atrioventricular node (not the SA node) lies approximately between the right and non-coronary cusps of the aortic valve at the apex of the triangle of Koch.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The pulmonary valve is posterior to the aortic valve\",\"e\":\"The aortic valve is in the centre of the heart when viewed from above, with the pulmonary valve anterior, the mitral valve to the left and the tricuspid valve to the right.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The pulmonary valve is anterior to the aortic valve\",\"e\":\"This is correct - the aortic valve lies in the centre of the heart when viewed from above, with the pulmonary valve lying anteriorly, the mitral valve to the left, and the tricuspid valve to the right.\\n\\nPosterior Tricuspid vahn Bicuspid (mitral)....- valo\\n\\nRight Lott cide of side of homet heart\\n\\nAorte valo Putoriary valve\\n\\nAnterior\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A patient is undergoing carpal tunnel release after they have been troubled with carpal tunnel syndrome for a prolonged period. When you see them in clinic their symptoms are completely settled and the muscle bulk of the hand is improved Which one of the following muscles in the hand is supplied by the median nerve?\",\"lead\":\"When you see them in clinic their symptoms are completely settled and the muscle bulk of the hand is improved Which one of the following muscles in the hand is supplied by the median nerve?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Radial two interossei\",\"e\":\"The interossei are all supplied by the ulnar nerve [which supplies all intrinsic muscles of the hand except the LOAF (lumbricals 1 and 2, opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis and flexor pollicis brevis) muscles]. Do not get the interossei confused with the lateral (radial). two lumbricals that are indeed supplied by the median nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Abductor pollicis brevis\",\"e\":\"The abductor pollicis brevis is one of the four LOAF muscles supplied by the median nerve in the hand (the others are the lateral two lumbricals, opponens pollicis and flexor pollicis brevis). It lies superficially ano radially in the thenar eminence (as shown in the image) and is used to test median nerve function in the hand via the median nerve's recurrent motor branch. Recall that abduction of the thumb is bringing it away from the palm.\\n\\nFlexor pollicis brevis\\n\\nAbductor pollicis brevis\\n\\nOpponens pollicis\\n\\ntoachmanstony\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ulnar two lumbricales\",\"e\":\"The median nerve innervates the radial (lateral in the anatomical position) two lumbricals - the ulnar two lumbricals are innervated by the ulnar nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Adductor pollicis V\",\"e\":\"Adductor pollicis is innervated by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8. T1). It is a fan-shaped deep muscle within the palm with two heads (oblique and transverse) that inserts into the medial base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Extensor pollicis\",\"e\":\"The extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) originates in the extensor compartment of the forearm and is supplied by the radial nerve (posterior interosseous branch). The extensor pollicis longus (EPL) tendon forms the upper/ulnar border of the anatomical snuffbox, and EPB forms the lower/radial border along with the abductor pollicis longus tendon. The median nerve supplies four muscles in the hand.+ Cutaneously, the median nerve supplies the palmar surface of the thumb, index, middle and half of the ring finger, and the dorsal surface of the tip of the thumb, index, middle, and half of the ring finger.\\n\\nThe ulnar nerve supplies all the interossei and the rest of the hand muscles.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"An anaesthetist performs a successful block of the median nerve at the elbow.\\n\\nWhich neurological sign is likely to be present on examination?\",\"lead\":\"Which neurological sign is likely to be present on examination?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inability to flex the fingers\",\"e\":\"Although the median nerve innervates the entire flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS) muscle. it only innervates the radial half of the flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) muscle - the ulnar half is innervated by the unar nerve. Therefore, although finger flexion would be impaired, the patient would still be able to flex their ring and little fingers due to the action of the ulnar half of FDP\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An obvious wrist drop deformity\",\"e\":\"Wrist extension is via the extensor carpi radialis longis muscle (supplied by the radial nerve directly), and the extensor carpi radialis brevis and extensor carpi uinaris muscles (supplied by the posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve). A wrist drop is classically seen with a radial nerve injury, and therefore would not be seen with median nerve anaesthesia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The palm of the hand is totally anaesthetised\",\"e\":\"Although the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve supplies sensation to the radial half of the palm, the ulnar half of the palm is supplied by the palmar cutaneous branch of the ulnar nerve, and would therefore be unaffected.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inability to abduct and adduct the fingers\",\"e\":\"The four dorsal and three palmar interossei which are responsible for abduction and adduction of the fingers are supplied entirely by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8-T1). therefore they would be unaffected here.\\n\\nThe median nerve supplies all the muscles in the anterior compartment of the forearm, apart from the flexor carpi ulnaris and the ulnar half of the flexor digitorum profundus (both of which are supplied by the ulnar nerve). Therefore, with median nerve anaesthesia the little finger and ring fingers can still be flexed (due to the action of the ulnar half of FDP): The radial nerve supplies the wrist extensors (ECRL, ECRB and ECU) - hence no wrist drop. The ulnar nerve supplies the palmar and dorsal skin of the ulnar side of the hand, hence no anaesthesia there, and it also supplies the interossei muscles of the hand, which effect abduction and adduction of the fingers. The median nerve is usually tested in the hand by asking the patient to abduct the thumb against resistance from the anatomical position, testing for the action of abductor pollicus brevis - one of the thenar muscles supplied by the recurrent motor branch of the median nerve. The anterior interosseous branch of the median nerve can be tested with flexion at the thumb interphalangeal (IPJ).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Loss of sensation over thenar eminence\",\"e\":\"The median nerve supplies sensation over the radial half of the palm via the palmar cutaneous branch (shown in blue below - the image shows median nerve sensory distribution in the hand), which is given off before the median nerve enters the carpal tunnel, and palmar sensation is therefore spared in carpal tunnel syndrome. However, if the nerve is anaesthetised at the elbow then sensation over the thenar eminence will be affected.\\n\\nPalmar cutaneous branch PASTE Digital cutaneous branch\\n\\nPalm Dorsuminatomy\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old man, who is a keen bodybuilder, presents with a short history of left upper limb discomfort and difficulty in moving his shoulder. On examination he is noticed to have winging of the left scapula. There is no wasting of the shoulder girdle muscles. With stabilisation of the scapula, he has a full range of movement and is able to elevate the shoulder. Sensory testing is normal, as are upper limb reflexes. What is the likely anatomical origin of his problem?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely anatomical origin of his problem?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"C3,4 nerve root\",\"e\":\"The C3 and C4 nerve roots do not normally contribute to the brachial plexus (NB that C4 can occasionally contribute in a pre-fixed brachial plexus)\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Long thoracic nerve\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve (of Bell) originates from the C5, C6 and C7 nerve roots. It runs on the superficial aspect of serratus anterior, innervating it. It is at risk of damage, particularly in axillary or breast surgery, but also from wounds to the chest wall eg from a blade. As serratus anterior inserts into the medial under-surface of the scapula (that is, the surface closest to the chest wail), damage to the long thoracic nerve leads to winging of the ipsilateral scapula when the arms are outstretched and pressed up against a wall. Serratus anterior can be seen in blue in the image below of the thorax from the anterior aspect. The brachial plexus is also shown in schematic view.\\n\\nPectoralis minor Internal intercostals\\n\\nExternal Intercostals serratu: antero\\n\\nBranches Cords Divisions Trunks, CS Musculocutaneous Lateral Superior\\n\\n- C6 Axillary\\n\\nPosterior Median Middie C7\\n\\nRadial\\n\\nUlnar Medial Inferior\\n\\nteachineanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diffuse left brachial plexus injury\",\"e\":\"A diffuse brachial plexus injury would be expected to produce more symptoms than simply difficulty in moving the shoulder - for example weakness of elbow flexion or extension.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spinal accessory nerve (cranial nerve XI)\",\"e\":\"The spinal accessory nerve innervates sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. The patient is noted to be able to elevate the shoulder in the question, indicating trapezius (and the spinal accessory nerve) is intact. Trapezius acts in combination with serratus anterior to rotate the scapula (and therefore the glenoid cavity) with arm abduction, but incidence of scapular winging due to trapezius paralysis is much rarer than long thoracic injury.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"C5,6 nerve root\",\"e\":\"The C5 and C6 myotomes are responsible for arm abduction at the shoulder (C5 via deltoid). and elbow flexion (C6 via biceps, brachialis and coracobrachialis). There is no mention of a deficit in elbow flexion in the question, and he has a full range of movement with stabilisation of the scapula. Damage to these nerve roots would not lead to winging of the scapula as C7 remains intact (the long thoracic nerve derives from C5-C7). Remembering the brachial plexus:\\n\\n3:1:0:3:5:5\\n\\n3 branches from roots (LSD)\\n\\nLong thoracic nerve Nerve to Subclavius\\n\\nDorsal scapular nerve\\n\\n1 from trunk\\n\\nSuprascapular nerve\\n\\no from divisions\\n\\n3 branches from lateral cord (LML)\\n\\nLateral pectoral nerve\\n\\nMusculocutaneous nerve\\n\\nMusculocutaneous nerve\\n\\nLateral root of the median nerve. Five branches from medial cord (Miss Mary Makes Me Unhappy): Medial pectoral nerve Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm\\n\\nMedial cutaneous nerve of arm Medial root of median nerve\\n\\nUlnar nerve. Five branches from posterior cord (2 STAR): 2 Subscapular nerves (upper/lower)\\n\\nThoracodorsal nerve\\n\\nAxillary nerve\\n\\nRadial nerve:\\n\\nVarious muscles attach the scapula (shoulder blade) to the chest wall and help maintain normal scapular control. These muscles include trapezius, levator scapulas, rhomboids major, rhomboids minor, pectoralis minor and serratus anterior. The latissimus dorsi has a small attachment at the base of the scapula but does not significantly contribute to scapular stability. Of these muscles, the serratus anterior and the trapezius are the most important. A winging scapula is nearly always associated with partial or complete paralysis of either of these muscies. Weakness or paralysis of the serratus anterior, secondary to palsy of the long thoracic nerve, is the commonest cause of winging. The long thoracic nerve (from the C5-C7 roots) is thin, fragile and runs an anatomical course in the neck and upper thorax that makes it susceptible to damage by compression or trauma Commoner causes include: surgery (eg radical mastectomy, lymph node biopsy from axilla); stretch injury during sports (as in this case), viral/post-infectious (brachial neuritis); and other causes of neuropathy (vascular, toxic etc.).\\n\\nAccessory nerve (X) damage can also produce scapular winging via weakness of trapezius, but this would be milder, is rarer, and would be expected to be associated with weakness of shoulder elevation, which this patient does not have. The other options listed would tend to be associated with other symptoms and signs. Nerve conduction studies and electromyography would help confirm the diagnosis.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"You are undertaking an exploration of hand wound under anaesthetic. On closer inspection it is obvious that the median nerve has been transected.\\n\\nA knife wound at the wrist affecting the median nerve only will cause which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"A knife wound at the wrist affecting the median nerve only will cause which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The long flexors will be affected causing wasting the front of the forearm on the long run\",\"e\":\"The long flexors, innervated by the median nerve (the entire FDS and the radial half of FDP). arise in the forearm and are innervated by the median nerve (for FDS), or its anterior interosseous branch (for radial half of FDP), there. Damage to the median nerve at the wrist would not cause wasting of these muscles:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Flexor pollicis longus will be affected\",\"e\":\"Flexor pollicus longus is innervated by the anterior interosseous branch of the median nerve in the forearm, and therefore would not be denervated by a wound at the wrist\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The hand is held typically with the index finger straight 'pointing finger\",\"e\":\"This is referring to the 'hand of benediction', when the patient, with high median nerve damage, tries to make a fist and is unable to flex the distal interphalangeal joint (DIPJ) or proximal interphalangeal joint (PIPJ) of the index or middle fingers due to paralysis of both FDS, and lateral half of FDP. This would not occur with damage at the wrist as FDS and FDP innervation would remain intact.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Abduction of the thumb is affected\",\"e\":\"Abductor pollicis brevis is one of the four LOAF muscles* (it is shown in blue below) and is innervated by the recurrent motor branch of the median nerve which is given off after the carpal tunnel. It would therefore be affected with a knife wound at the wrist, as would the other thenar muscles and the lateral two lumbricals. IS PASTEST Flexor pollicis brevis\\n\\nAbductor pollicis brevis\\n\\nOpponens pollicis\\n\\nteactumeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Loss of sensation over the ulnar aspect of ring finger\",\"e\":\"Sensation over the ulnar aspect of the hand including the little finger is provided by the ulnar nerve, which has not been damaged in this question. NB damage to the median nerves at the wrist may also damage the palmar cutaneous branch, which is given off before it enters the carpal tunnel and provides sensation to the radial aspect of the palm.\\n\\nAbduction of the thumb is the only answer consistent with a low (ie at the wrist) lesion of the median nerve. The long flexors of the forearm (FDS and radial half of FDP), or thumb (FPL) would only be affected with a median nerve lesion higher up in the forearm - eg at the elbow. Damage to the median nerve at the wrist would affect sensation in the radial 3 ½ fingers, but may not lead to loss of sensation over the radial palm as the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve is given off before the carpal tunnel. In terms of motor function, median nerve damage at the wrist would lead to weakness of the four LOAF muscles supplied by the median nerve in the hand - the lateral two lumbricals, opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis, and flexor pollicis brevis.\\n\\nMedian and ulnar nerves - Some common features Each supplies half of flexor digitorum profundus: each supplies 2 lumbricals, each has a palmar cutaneous nerve, each supplies an eminence group of muscles (ulnar: hypothenar, median: thenar); each enters the forearm through two heads (ulnar: heads off flexor carpi ulnaris; median: heads of pronator teres) Each has no branches in the upper arm, each makes two fingers claw when cut at the wrist, each supplies a palmaris (median: palmaris longus; ulnar: palmaris brevis).\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a mastectomy with a new consultant supervisor. They ask you about basic anatomy relating to the procedure. In the breast, the connective tissue bands that extend from the deep layer of superficial fascia to the dermis are called what?\",\"lead\":\"In the breast, the connective tissue bands that extend from the deep layer of superficial fascia to the dermis are called what?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fibrous bands of Halstead\",\"e\":\"The fibrous bands of Halstead do not exist. Halstead was a pioneer of breast surgery. developing what became known as the Halstead procedure (radical mastectomy).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Tubercles of Montgomery\",\"e\":\"Montgomery's tubercles are the sebaceous glands that surround the nipple in the areola.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Retromammary bursay\",\"e\":\"The retromammary bursa is a potential space that lies between the pectoral fascia and the breast.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ligaments of Cooper\",\"e\":\"The suspensory ligaments of Cooper run from the deep fascia overlying the pectoral muscles, between the lobules and lobes of the mammary gland, and firmly attach the breast to the overlying dermis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Poupart's ligament\",\"e\":\"Poupart's ligament (singular) is another name for the inguinal ligament, after François Poupart.\\n\\nTaking a history of breast pathology - LMNOP:\\n\\nLump (ask about all the features of the lump)\\n\\nMammary changes\\n\\nNipple discharge/changes Obvious risk factors, eg previous breast cancer\\n\\nPain.\\n\\nThe suspensory ligaments of the breast (ligaments of Cooper) are fibrous bands of connective tissue that extend from the deep layer of fascia covering the pectoralis major muscle, interdigitate between the lobules and lobes of the breast, and insert into the dermis. These ligaments provide shape and support to the breast tissue, and are responsible for the 'peau d'orange' appearance associated with lymphoedema of malignancy - the ligaments remain firmly attached to the dermis despite the surrounding oedema of the skin. Tubercles of Montgomery are small accessory glands on the areola. The retromammary bursa is the posterior aspect of the breast between it and the pectoralis fascia. Poupart's ligament is the inguinal ligament.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"In an adult woman, which of the following statements accurately describes the anatomical location of the base of the breast?\",\"lead\":\"In an adult woman, which of the following statements accurately describes the anatomical location of the base of the breast?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The inferior lateral one-third lies on the Pectoralis major\",\"e\":\"Two-thirds of the breast base lies over the pectoralis major muscle, but the inferior lateral one-third lies on the serratus anterior and the external oblique muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Two-thirds lies over the Serratus anterior\",\"e\":\"Two-thirds of the breast overlies the pectoralis major muscle, and approximately one-third overlies the serratus anterior muscle and external oblique muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lies between the 2nd and 6* rib vertically and between the lateral border of the sternum and the mid-axillary line horizontally\",\"e\":\"Although breast shape can change significantly with age, the base of the breast is roughly circular and extends from the second to the 6* rib overlying the pectoralis major muscle by two-thirds, and extending over the serratus anterior laterally by one-third. The breast extends towards the axilla along the inferior aspect of pectoralis major as the axillary process, or tail (of Spence), which contains a significant amount of glandular tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lies between the 15 and 4m rib vertically\",\"e\":\"The base of the breast is described as lying between the lateral border of the sternum and the mid-axillary line horizontally, and between the second and sixth ribs vertically.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lies between the lateral edge of the sternum and the anterior-axillary line horizontally.\",\"e\":\"Horizontally, the base of the breast is described as lying between the lateral edge of the sternum and the mid-axillary line*\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in the hand theatre. The consultant wants to ensure you are safe to operate and asks you about the course of some of the important structures in the forearm Which one of the following structures runs under the brachioradialis, emerging distally on its medial side, and enters the palm by piercing the first dorsal interosseous muscle?\",\"lead\":\"The consultant wants to ensure you are safe to operate and asks you about the course of some of the important structures in the forearm Which one of the following structures runs under the brachioradialis, emerging distally on its medial side, and enters the palm by piercing the first dorsal interosseous muscle?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ulnar artery\",\"e\":\"The ulnar artery lies under the pronator teres and flexor digitorum superficialis muscles in the medial forearm. It enters the hand by passing above the flexor retinaculum through Guyon's canal, along with the ulnar nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Radial artery\",\"e\":\"The radial artery passes under the brachioradialis, reaching the wrist where its pulsations are felt against the radius on the palmar surface, before winding laterally around radius and crossing the floor of the anatomical snuffbox to enter the paim between the two heads of the first dorsal interosseous muscle.\\n\\nDeep Palmar Arch Radial Artery Brachial Artery\\n\\nUlnar Artery\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Median nerve\",\"e\":\"The median nerve enters the forearm beneath the biceps aponeurosis, medial to the brachial artery, by piercing the two heads of pronator teres. It runs in the plane between the flexor digitorum profundus and superficialis muscles and enters the palm through the carpal tunnel.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Profunda brachii artery\",\"e\":\"The profunda brachii artery is given off in the arm rather than the forearm (just distal to the lower border of teres major), and accompanies the radial nerve in the spiral groove of the humerus. It terminates in the anastomoses around the elbow joint.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Radial nerve\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve supplies the posterior extensor compartment of the forearm. It divides anterior to the lateral epicondyle of the humerus in to the superficial branch (which runs beneath brachioradialis), and the deep posterior interosseous branch which enters the forearm between the two heads of supinator and runs between the superficial and deep extensors. Some features of brachioradialis - BrachioRadialis:\\n\\nBeer-Raising muscle (flexes elbow with forearm in neutral)\\n\\nBreaks the Rule in that it is a flexor But supplied by the Radial nerve\\n\\nBehind it is the Radial nerve in the cubital fossa\\n\\nAttaches to the Bottom of the Radius (distal aspect). Branches of the axillary artery - Send The Lord to Say A Prayer:\\n\\nFrom proximal to distal:\\n\\nSuperior thoracic\\n\\nFrom proximal to distal:\\n\\nSuperior thoracic Thoracoacromial\\n\\nLateral thoracic\\n\\nSubscapular\\n\\nAnterior circumflex humeral\\n\\nPosterior circumflex humeral.\\n\\nThis question describes the course of the radial artery that starts at the level of the neck of the radius lying on the tendon of the biceps. It passes under the brachioradialis as described reaching the wrist where its pulsations are felt against the radius on the palmar surface, before winding laterally around radius and crossing the floor of the anatomical snuffbox to enter the palm between the two heads of the first dorsal interosseous muscle. It terminates as the deep palmar arch supplying the hand. The ulnar artery on the other hand passes inferiorly and medially in the anterior compartment of the forearm. It runs laterally to the ulnar nerve and the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon, and enters the hand superficial to the flexor retinaculum through Guyon's canal. It terminates in the hand as the superficial palmar arch.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"Which of the following structures passes through the canal of Guyon?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following structures passes through the canal of Guyon?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ulnar nerve\",\"e\":\"The uinar nerve enters the hand superficial to the flexor retinaculum in Guyon's canal, which is lateral to the pisiform bone. Within the canal, the unar nerve lies medial to the ulnar artery. The uinar nerve is shown in its full course highlighted in yellow in the image below.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Median nerve\",\"e\":\"The median nerve enters the palm beneath the flexor retinaculum, in the carpal tunnel.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Radial nerve\",\"e\":\"The deep branch of the radial nerve (the posterior interosseous nerve) does not enter the hand. The superficial branch runs beneath brachioradialis and then pierces the deep fascia distally and laterally to supply the skin of the first dorsal webspace.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Axillary nerve\",\"e\":\"The axillary nerve is a branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus and exits the axilla through the quadrangular space to supply deltoid and teres minor:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sural nerve\",\"e\":\"The sural nerve is a sensory nerve that is formed from both the tibial and common peroneal nerves. It supplies the skin of the posterolateral leg and lateral foot. Median and ulnar nerves - Some common features:\\n• Each supplies half of flexor digitorum profundus\\n• Each supplies two lumbricals\\n• Each has a palmar cutaneous nerve\\n• Each supplies an eminence group of muscles* (ulnar: hypothenar; median: thenar)\\n• Each enters forearm through two heads (ulnar: heads of flexor carpi ulnaris; median: heads of pronator teres)\\n• Each has no branches in the upper arm\\n• Each makes two fingers claw when cut at the wrist\\n• Each supplies a palmaris (median: palmaris longus; ulnar: palmaris brevis).\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"A patient has sustained a knife wound on the dorsum of their hand and they are unable to extend ail of their digits normally. You see them the following day on the emergency hand list and perform a tendon repair.\\n\\nThe first (most radial) extensor compartment at the wrist contains which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The first (most radial) extensor compartment at the wrist contains which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Extensor Carpi Radialis Longus and Extensor Carpi Radialis Brevis\",\"e\":\"The extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL) and extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB) tendons lie within the second compartment\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Extensor Pollicis Longus\",\"e\":\"The extensor pollicis longus (EPL) tendon lies within the third compartment.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Extensor Pollicis Brevis and Abductor Pollicis Longus\",\"e\":\"The first compartment contains the tendons of abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB), both of which form the radial border of the anatomical snuffbox.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Extensor Carpi Ulnaris\",\"e\":\"The extensor carpi ulnaris (ECU) tendon lies within the sixth and final compartment.\\n\\nThe extensor digiti minimi (EDM) tendon lies within the fifth compartment\\n\\nThere are six extensor compartments in the extensor retinaculum on the dorsum of the wrist. They contain in order, from radial to ulnar: yst - Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus 2n0 - Extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi radialis brevis 3rd - Extensor pollicis longus 4th - Extensor digitorum and extensor indicis 5th - Extensor digiti minimi\\n\\n6th - Extensor carpi ulnaris\\n\\nThe second and third compartments are separated by the dorsal tubercle of the radius, otherwise known as Lister's tubercle\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Extensor Digiti Minimi\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"A patient suffers with carpal tunnel syndrome that is not improving despite conservative management. You see them on an elective hand list and the consultant allows you to perform a carpal tunnel release\\n\\nWhich of the structures below is not contained within the carpal tunnel at the wrist?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the structures below is not contained within the carpal tunnel at the wrist?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Flexor digitorum Superficialis muscle tendons\",\"e\":\"The four flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS) tendons make up four of the ten structures that run within the carpal tunnel, along with the four flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) tendons, the median nerve and the flexor pollicis longus (FPL) tendon.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Palmaris longus tendon\",\"e\":\"Palmaris longus does not run through the carpal tunnel - it runs superficial to the flexor retinaculum and inserts into it and the palmar aponeurosis. It is absent in around 15% of people. The carpal tunnel is shown in section below with the 4 tendons of FDP, 4 tendons of FDS. FPL tendon and median nerve all highlighted.\\n\\nFlexor retinaculum\\n\\nMedian nerve\\n\\nFlexor pollicis Flexor digitorum longus superficialis Flexor digitorum profundus teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Median nerve\",\"e\":\"The median nerve runs within the carpal tunnel under the flexor retinaculum, hence the median nerve symptoms that occur with carpal tunnel syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Flexor digitorum profundus tendons\",\"e\":\"The four FDP tendons pass underneath the flexor retinaculum in the carpal tunnel along with the four FDS tendons, the FPL tendon and the median nerve\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Flexor pollicis longus tendon\",\"e\":\"The FPL tendon also passes through the carpal tunnelt along with the four FDS tendons, the four FDP tendons and the median nerve\\n\\nThere are ten structures that run within the carpal tunnel:\\n• Four flexor digitorum superficialis tendons\\n• Four flexor digitorum profundus tendons\\n• The flexor pollicis longus tendon\\n• The median nerve.\\n\\nPalmaris longus lies anterior/superficial to the flexor retinaculum, inserting into both it and the palmar fascia. The flexor carpi radialis tendon passes in its own canal in the lateral part of the flexor retinaculum, therefore lies outside of the carpal tunnel.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"Which of the following tendons runs within the glenohumeral joint capsule?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following tendons runs within the glenohumeral joint capsule?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Short head of biceps\",\"e\":\"The short head of biceps attaches to the coracoid process of the scapula and therefore does not run within the glenohumeral joint.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"25 ' Pectoralis major\",\"e\":\"Pectoralis major originates from the clavicle, sternum and costal cartilages, inserting into the lateral aspect of the bicipital (intertubercular) groove of the humerus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Long head of biceps\",\"e\":\"The tendon of the long head of biceps runs within the bicipital groovet and in the capsule of the glenohumeral joint to insert on to the supraglenoid tubercle. It can be seen in the image below running in the bicipital groove of the humerus. The long head can therefore be easily seen during shoulder arthroscopy and used for orientation\\n\\nCoracold process\\n\\nBiceps brachil (Short head) Biceps brachil (long head)\\n\\nCoracobrachialis\\n\\nBrachialis\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"Pectoralis minor originates from the 3rd to 5th ribs and inserts on to the coracoid process of the scapula.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Latissimus Dorsi\",\"e\":\"Latissimus dorsi originates from the spinous process of T6-T12, the thoracolumbar fascia and iliac crests. It inserts into the middle of the bicipital (intertubercular) groove of the humerus.\\n\\nThe tendon of the long head of biceps is the only tendon to pass through the glenohumeral joint and is invested in a synovial sleeve. The tendon is clearly visible during arthroscopy of the shoulder, and is therefore often used to orientate the camera.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"A patient has a fall onto his shoulder and is subsequently unable to internally rotate the shoulder against resistance. Other movements are relatively well preserved. X-rays taken show no bony injury Which of the following muscles is likely to be injured?\",\"lead\":\"X-rays taken show no bony injury Which of the following muscles is likely to be injured?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Supraspinatus\",\"e\":\"Supraspinatus initiates abduction of the shoulder, it does not perform internal rotation\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Infraspinatus\",\"e\":\"Infraspinatus is a lateral/external rotator of the arm and is therefore not affected here\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Teres Minor\",\"e\":\"Teres minor is a lateral/external rotator of the arm and is therefore not the cause of the deficit\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Deltoid\",\"e\":\"Deltoid is a strong abductor of the arm. The anterior and posterior parts of the muscle can assist in internal and external rotation respectively. but damage to deltoid or the axillary nerve which innervates it would lead to loss of abduction rather than internal rotation.\\n\\nSubscapularis is one of the four rotator cuff muscles, and the primary internal rotator of the shoulder joint. It arises from the costal surface of the scapula (the subscapular fossa), and inserts into the lesser tubercle. Pectoralis major (medial and lateral pectoral nerves) and latissimus dorsi (thoracodorsal nerve) also contribute to internal rotation so there is not a complete loss of internal rotation if only subscapularis has been injured.\\n\\nSupraspinatus initiates abduction and assists deltoid in abducting the shoulder joint. Teres minor along with infraspinatus externally rotate the shoulder. Deltoid abducts the shoulder.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Subscapularis\",\"e\":\"The subscapularis muscle (shown in green on the anterior or thoracic aspect of the scapula) is the primary medial/internal rotator* of the arm at the glenohumeral joint, as it inserts anteriorly on to the lesser tubercle. It is innervated by the upper and lower subscapular nerves, both branches from the posterior cord\\n\\nSubscapularis | Supraspinatus infraspinatus Teres minor\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"A motorcyclist fell from his bike. On arrival to the Emergency Department, it was noticed that extension of the shoulder was severely limited. Which nerve is he most likely to have damaged?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve is he most likely to have damaged?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Long thoracic nerve\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve (of Bell) innervates serratus anterior. Damage to this nerve leads to winging of the scapula, and can limit abduction as serratus anterior helps to rotate the glenoid cavity superiorly to ease abduction of the humerus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Medial pectoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The medial pectoral nerve innervates pectoralis minor and major. Although unlikely to be damaged in isolation, it would lead to loss of power in adduction and internal rotation of the humerus (via weakness of pectoralis major).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"suprascapular nerve\",\"e\":\"The suprascapular nerve innervates supraspinatus and infraspinatus. Damage to this nerve would cause inability to initiate abduction (supraspinatus) and weakness of external rotation (infraspinatus), as well as gleno-humeral instability (both are rotator cuff muscles).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thoracodorsal nerve\",\"e\":\"It is the only nerve mentioned that is involved with extension of the shoulder. The thoracodorsal nerve innervates latissimus dorsi (in yellow below), which extends and internally rotates the humerus, as well as being a powerful adductor of the shoulder. The thoracodorsal nerve, a branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus, arises between the upper and lower subscapular nerves: It derives its fibres from C6, C7 and C8. It follows the course of the subscapular artery, along the posterior wall of the axilla to the apex of the latissimus dorsi. The latissimus dorsi is responsible for adduction, extension and internal rotation of the humerus, and in the scenario described it is the affected muscle due to injury to the thoracodorsal nerve. The latissimus dorsi is a key muscle for climbing, as it powerfully adducts and depresses the humerus, enabling the trunk to be brought up to the arm, for example in pull-ups\\n\\nLevator Trapezius scapulae\\n\\nRhomboid minor Rhomboid major\\n\\nLatissimus dorsi\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Upper subscapular nerve\",\"e\":\"The upper (and lower) subscapular nerves innervate subscapularis, which internally rotates the humerus. The lower subscapular nerve also innervates Teres Major, which internally rotates and adducts the arm.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A young boy fell from his skateboard, twisted his forearm and sprained his annular ligament. Which of the following is true regarding the annular ligament in the forearm?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true regarding the annular ligament in the forearm?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Encircles the head of the radius\",\"e\":\"The annular ligament is an important feature in enabling pronation and supination of the forearm. It encircles the head of the radius, as shown in the image, holding the head of the radius in the radial notch of the proximal ulna, forming the proximal radioulnar joint. The annular ligament forms about four-fifths of the osteofibrous ring and is attached to the anterior and posterior margins of the radial notch. A few of its lower fibres continues around below the radial notch, forming a complete fibrous ring. A thickened band that extends from the inferior border of the annular ligament below the radial notch to the neck of the radius is known as the 'quadrate ligament. The superficial lateral surface of the annular ligament is strengthened by the radial collateral ligament of the elbow, and affords origin to part of the supinator muscle. Its deep surface is smooth and lined by synovial membrane, which is continuous with that of the elbow joint. Subluxation of the radial head due to a tear in the distal part of the annular ligament is responsible for a 'pulled elbow' (or nursemaid's elbow). particularly common in younger children.\\n\\nHumerus\\n\\nJoint Capsule\\n\\nAnular ligament\\n\\nUlna - Radius\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Encircles the head of the ulna\",\"e\":\"The annular ligament encircles the head of the radius, not the ulna.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Encircles the styloid process of the radius\",\"e\":\"The annular ligament encircles the head of the radius at the proximal radioulnar joint. The styloid process of the radius is on the lateral distal radius at the wrist.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Goes from the olecranon fossa to the olecranon process\",\"e\":\"The olecranon fossa is found on the posterior aspect of the humerus, enabling the olecranon process of the ulna to sit within it when the elbow is in full extension. The annular ligament is anterior to the humerus, encircling the head of the radius.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spans the space between the ulna and radius\",\"e\":\"The annular ligament arises from the anterior aspect of the radial notch of the ulna, and encircles the head of the radius, inserting back into the posterior aspect of the radial notch and blending with the radial collateral ligament. It does not arise from the humerus.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A young gymnast sustains an injury to a muscle that attaches to the coracoid process of the scapula. Which one of the following is a muscle that attaches to the coracoid process of the scapula?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a muscle that attaches to the coracoid process of the scapula?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"The pectoralis minor (in red below) originates from the 3rd to 5th ribs and inserts onto the coracoid process of the scapula, acting to stabilise the scapula, or elevate the ribs as an accessory muscle of respiration when the humerus is stabilised. It is innervated by the medial pectoral nerve.\\n\\nPectoralis minor Internal intercostals\\n\\nExternal intercostals serratus anterior\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Serratus anterior\",\"e\":\"The serratus anterior originates from the 1st to 8th ribs and inserts into the anterior medial border of the scapula, helping to stabilise the scapula against the thoracic wall. Paralysis of the long thoracic nerve causes scapular winging.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pectoralis major\",\"e\":\"The pectoralis major originates from the sternum, clavicle and 1st-6th costal cartilages. It inserts in to the lateral bicipital groove.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Latissimus dorsi\",\"e\":\"The latissimus dorsi* originates from a broad area over the lower back, including the spinous processes of T6 to T12, iliac crest and thoracolumbar fascia. It inserts in to the floor of the bicipital groove between pectoralis major (laterally) and teres major (medially).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supraspinatus\",\"e\":\"Supraspinatus s one of the rotator cuff muscles, originating from the supraspinous fossa of the scapula and inserting superiorly in to the greater tubercle of the humerus.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A rugby player is involved in a high tackle and sustains a complete spinal cord injury at the C8 level. They have severe disabilities and are brought in with spinal immobilisation as part of a trauma call. What is likely to be seen in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is likely to be seen in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The brachialis muscle will be paralysed\",\"e\":\"Brachialis is innervated by the musculocutaneous nerve (C5-C7), the terminal branch of the lateral cord, and is supplied by the C5 and C6 nerve roots. It would therefore be unaffected in a C8 level injury\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The muscles innervated by the radial nerve that would most likely be affected would be the most proximal ones\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve is supplied by all the spinal roots of the brachial plexus (C5-T1), however the general principle in the arm is that the more distal muscles receive the lower spinal roots. Triceps, for example, is innervated by the radial nerve via the C6, C7 and C8 roots, therefore a lesion at C8 would produce minimal weakness. Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis however, are innervated chiefly by C8 (with some contribution from C7). therefore a C& lesion would produce marked weakness in these muscies.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The hypothenar muscles would be completely paralysed\",\"e\":\"The hypothenar muscles are innervated by the ulnar nerve via C8 and T1. They comprise flexor digiti minimi (brevis), abductor digiti minimi and opponens digiti minimi in a mirror of the thenar muscles.. A complete lesion at C8 would therefore lead to a complete paralysis of the intrinsic muscles of the hand, including the hypothenar muscles. Remember that a complete spinal lesion will lead to no demonstrable motor function in the spinal roots below the level of injury. Here, a C8 level injury will lead to weakness at C8 and below. Recall also that the same spinal nerve roots can contribute to multiple peripheral nerves - eg the median nerve and the radial nerve both carry C5-T1 roots.\\n\\nFlexor digiti minimi brevis\\n\\ni Opponens digiti minimi Abductor digiti minimi\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Innervation to the deltoid muscle would be affected\",\"e\":\"The deltoid is innervated by the spinal root C5 (and a small amount of C6) via the axillary nerve and therefore would be unaffected in a C8 level injury.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The patient would be unable to flex their elbow joint\",\"e\":\"Elbow flexion is chiefly the C6 myotome (with some C5 contribution), and therefore would be unaffected in a C& level injury: Remembering reflex roots:\\n• 1,2 - buckle my shoe 3,4 - kick the door 5,6 - pick up sticks 7,8 - shut the gate\\n• S1, 2 = ankle jerk\\n• L3, 4 = knee jerk\\n• C5, 6 = biceps and brachioradialis\\n• C7, 8 = triceps.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"A patient sustains a kitchen knife wound to the dorsum of their wrist. They are unable to extend all of their digits normally. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the extensor retinaculum of the wrist?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the extensor retinaculum of the wrist?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is a direct extension of the axillary fascia\",\"e\":\"The extensor retinaculum is not related to the axillary fascia. The axillary fascia is continuous with the brachial fascia of the arm, and continues directly into the arm as the axillary sheath, enclosing the axillary artery, vein, and the brachial plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The median nerve runs deep to it\",\"e\":\"The median nerve runs through the carpal tunnel on the palmar (volar) aspect of the wrist. The extensor retinaculum is on the dorsal aspect of the wrist\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The tendon of the brachioradialis muscle runs through it\",\"e\":\"The brachioradialis muscle inserts on to the distal radius, proximal to the styloid process. It does not therefore pass under the extensor retinaculum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It contains a compartment for the thenar muscles\",\"e\":\"The thenar muscles are all on the palmar (volar) aspect of the hand, and all originate from the carpal and metacarpal bones. They therefore are not involved with the extensor retinaculum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It prevents the tendons of the posterior compartment of the forearm from 'bowstringing' when the hand is extended at the wrist\",\"e\":\"Both the flexor and extensor retinacula are important in preventing bowstringing of the long tendons attaching to the fingers and thumb. The extensor retinaculum is shown below (labelled by its other name, the dorsal carpal ligament). The tendons running beneath it are separated into discrete compartments. In order, they contain, from radial to ulnar:\\n• Ist - Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus\\n• 2nd - Extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi radialis brevis\\n• 3rd - Extensor pollicis longus\\n• 4th - Extensor digitorum and extensor indicis\\n• 5th - Extensor digiti minimi\\n• 6th - Extensor carpi ulnaris\\n\\nThe second and third compartments are separated by the dorsal tubercle of the radius. otherwise known as Lister's tubercle.\\n\\nExtensor pollicis longus\\n\\nExtensor pollicis brevis\\n\\nAbductor pollicis longus\\n\\nteachmsanatorsy._.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"The nursing staff are unable to take routine bloods from a patient. They ask you to do it on their behalf so that the patient's operation will not be further delayed To draw some blood from a patient's median cubital vein the needle should be inserted into which one of the following locations?\",\"lead\":\"They ask you to do it on their behalf so that the patient's operation will not be further delayed To draw some blood from a patient's median cubital vein the needle should be inserted into which one of the following locations?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Posterior aspect of the knee\",\"e\":\"The median cubital vein joins the basilic and cephalic veins obliquely across the antecubital fossa of the arm, not the knee\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral triangle\",\"e\":\"The femoral triangle contains the femoral vein, which is occasionally used for blood sampling, particularly in emergency situations: The order of structures in the femoral triangle is, from lateral to medial: femoral nerve, femoral artery, femoral vein. The median cubital vein joins the basilic and cephalic veins obliquely across the antecubital fossa of the arm.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Dorsal surface of the hand\",\"e\":\"The dorsum of the hand contains many and varied superficial veins in the dorsal venous network. The median cubital vein joins the basilic and cephalic veins obliquely across the antecubital fossa of the arm, not the hand.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Anterior aspect of the elbow\",\"e\":\"The median cubital vein joins the basilic and cephalic veins obliquely across the antecubital fossa of the arm, and is commoniy used for blood sampling. The median nerve and brachial artery are the deep relations of the median cubital vein and can potentially be damaged during venous access. Borders of the antecubital fossa+ are highlighted in the image below: Superior border: Imaginary line between the epicondyles\\n\\nMedial border: - Lateral border: Lateral border of the Medial border of the pronator teres brachioradialis\\n\\ncoachmoanatony..\\n• Superior: line between the two epicondyles of the humerus\\n• Lateral/radial: medial border of brachioradialis\\n• Medial/ulnar: lateral border of pronator teres\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Deltopectoral triangle\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein runs in the deltopectoral groove between the deltoid and pectoralis major muscles in the anterior upper arm, before piercing the clavipectoral fascia in the clavipectoral triangle to join with the axillary vein.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"What sort of a synovial joint is the elbow?\",\"lead\":\"What sort of a synovial joint is the elbow?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ellipsoid\",\"e\":\"The thumb carpometacarpal joint is an example of a saddle joint, in that it permits movement in two planes - the elbow only permits movement in one plane.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hinge\",\"e\":\"The elbow joint consists of three joints: between the ulna and the humerus (trochlea and trochlear notch), between the radius and humerus (radial head and capitulum/capitellum); and finally between the radius and ulna (proximal radioulnar joint), it allows movement only in one plane. Remember that supination and pronation of the forearm occur at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints: Osteology of the distal humerus and proximal ulna are shown below in the images. The trochlear notch of the ulna articulates with the trochlea of the humerus.\\n\\nCoronaid fosso Olecranon fossa\\n\\n- Positi Lateral epicondyle\\n\\nMedial Captitulum Trochica epicondyle\\n\\nAnterior: Lateral:\\n• Olecranon\\n• Coronoid Process\\n• Trochlear Notch Radial Notch\\n\\n©teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ball and socket\",\"e\":\"Bail and socket joints allow movement in several axes, such as the hip joint.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pivot\",\"e\":\"Pivot joints, such as between the C1 (atlas) and C2 (axis) vertebrae, allow only rotational movement.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Bicondylar\",\"e\":\"Bicondylar joints allow flexion and extension, as well as abduction and adduction, such as at the metacarpophalangeal joints of the hand\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"A patient was involved in a road traffic accident and has injured a nerve that resulted in loss of action of the muscle inserting on to the inferior facet of the greater tubercle of the humerus.\\n\\nWhich nerve is damaged?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve is damaged?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Radial\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve (C5 - T1) innervates the three heads of triceps brachil and anconeus in the arm, and the extensor muscles in the forearm. It provides sensory supply to the posterior forearm and to the dorsal lateral palm and 3½ digits. The long head of triceps inserts into the olecranon process of the ulna, not the lesser tubercle of the humerus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Long thoracic\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve (of Bell) innervates the serratus anterior, which inserts onto the medial anterior border of the scapula. Injury leads to winging of the scapula.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Median\",\"e\":\"The median nerve (C6 - T1) innervates the anterior flexor compartment of the forearm and the LOAF muscles of the hand (lateral two lumbricals, opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis and flexor pollicis brevis). It does not innervate any muscles of the shoulder region.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Axillary nerve\",\"e\":\"Teres minor originates from the lateral border of the scapula and inserts into the inferior facet of the greater tubercle. It is shown in yellow below, along with the other rotator cuff muscles. It is innervated by the axillary nerve (C5 C6) which is a continuation of the posterior cord from the brachial plexus. It innervates teres minor and deltoid muscles. One way to remember this is that a child (minor) needs help (an auxiliary). A patient with an axillary nerve transection would also have anaesthesia in the 'regimental badge area' - the skin over the lower deltoid. This is because the axillary nerve gives rise to the upper lateral cutaneous nerve of arm, which gives sensory supply to this area.\\n\\nSubscapularis ] Supraspinatus I Infraspinatus i Teres minse\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lower subscapular nerve\",\"e\":\"The lower subscapular nerve (C5 C6) branches from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus to supply teres major (which inserts into the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus) and the lower part of subscapularis (inserts into lesser tubercle of humerus).\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"A patient suffers a brachial plexus injury. Clinically you think that the suprascapular nerve is damaged Which one of the following statements regarding this nerve is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Clinically you think that the suprascapular nerve is damaged Which one of the following statements regarding this nerve is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"it courses superior to the suprascapular ligament en route to the supraspinatus muscle\",\"e\":\"The suprascapular nerve runs beneath the suprascapular ligament (otherwise known as the superior transverse scapular ligament), which crosses the suprascapular notch. The suprascapular artery conversely runs above the ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It contains nerve fibres from C5 and C6 spinal cord segments\",\"e\":\"The suprascapular nerve carries fibres from the C5 and C6 nerve roots, and goes on to innervate the supraspinatus and infraspinatus muscles. It runs laterally across the posterior triangle of the neck, deep to trapezius and omohyoid, passing below the superior transverse scapular ligament (in contrast with the suprascapular artery, which runs above it), supplying supraspinatus. It then curves around the lateral border of the spine of the scapula to supply infraspinatus in the infraspinous fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It innervates the teres minor muscle\",\"e\":\"The teres minor muscle is innervated by the axillary nerve, one of the terminal branches of the posterior cord.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It provides cutaneous innervation to the posterolateral surface of the shoulder\",\"e\":\"The suprascapular nerve does not provide any cutaneous innervation directly. but does send sensory branches to the glenohumeral joint. Skin over the posterolateral shoulder is innervated by the superior lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm, the cutaneous branch of the axillary nerve (which can be tested at the regimental patch area.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It branches from the middle trunk of the brachial plexus\",\"e\":\"The suprascapular nerve branches from the upper trunk of the plexus, receiving fibres from C5 and C6.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"The scaphoid bone is the most commonly injured carpal bone following a fall on the out stretched hand (FOOSH)\\n\\nWhere does the scaphoid major blood supply come from?\",\"lead\":\"Where does the scaphoid major blood supply come from?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Does not have arterial blood supply as it is 100% covered with cartilage\",\"e\":\"The scaphoid receives arterial blood from the radial artery via its branches. Supply to the proximal pole is in a retrograde fashion, and the proximal pole is therefore at risk of avascular necrosis after fracture.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The dorsal carpal branch of the radial artery\",\"e\":\"The carpal bones are shown below in various colours, with the scaphoid highlighted in red. Blood supply to the scaphoid is chiefly via the dorsal carpal branch of the radial artery, with some contribution from the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery on the palmar surface. Both of these arteries supply the distal pole and then supply the proximal pole in a retrograde fashion through interosseous perforators. When the scaphoid fractures through the waist, the fracture line severs the blood supply to the proximal fragment, and avascular necrosis of the proximal pole is therefore a common complication. Tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox may be the only clinical sign of the fracture before radiological evidence develops (often around 2 weeks later).\\n\\nScaphoid Lunate Triquetrum Pisiform Trapezium Trapezoid Capitate Hamate teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The superficial palmer arch of the ulnar artery\",\"e\":\"The superficial palmar arch is the direct continuation of the ulnar artery, and is formed distal to the carpal bones. It does not supply the scaphoid, which derives its blood supply chiefly from the radial artery and its branches.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The ulnar artery\",\"e\":\"The ulnar artery enters the hand through Guyon's canal, just lateral to the pisiform bone. This is on the medial aspect of the wrist, with the scaphoid being located on the lateral aspect. The scaphoid derives its blood supply chiefly from the radial artery and its branches.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Through the attachment to the adductor pollicis muscle\",\"e\":\"The two heads of the adductor pollicis muscle originate from the second and third metacarpals, inserting together on to the base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb, with the radial artery travelling between the two heads to form the deep palmar arch. The scaphoid is not attached to the adductor pollicis muscle, and does not derive its blood supply from the it.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old boy falls on an outstretched hand and sustains a fracture to the scaphoid bone of his right wrist. Which bone articulates with the anterolateral surface of the scaphoid?\",\"lead\":\"Which bone articulates with the anterolateral surface of the scaphoid?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hamate\",\"e\":\"The hamate is on the medial (ulnar) aspect of the carpus, whereas the scaphoid is on the lateral (radial) aspect. They do not articulate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lunate\",\"e\":\"The lunate does articulate with the scaphoid, but on its medial side, not anterolateral\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Trapezium\",\"e\":\"The scaphoid articulates with the trapezium on its anterolateral aspect, as can be seen in the image below.\\n\\nScaphoid Lunate\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Trapezoid\",\"e\":\"The scaphoid articulates with the trapezoid bone on its anterior surface (not anterolateral). as part of the scapho-trapezio-trapezoidal (STT) joint complex.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Triquetral\",\"e\":\"The triquetrum is medial in the proximal row of carpal bonest and as such does not articulate with the scaphoid.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old woman undergoes bilateral prophylactic breast mastectomies for a strong positive family history of breast carcinoma. She comes to clinic for consideration of breast reconstruction.\\n\\nWhich of the following concerning anatomy of the breast is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following concerning anatomy of the breast is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Approximately 50% of the lymph drains laterally to the parasternal lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"Usually over 75% of lymph from the breast drains to the axillary lymph nodes. the remaining 25% drains mostly to the internal thoracic (parasternal) nodes and contralateral breast, but also to the inferior phrenic lymph nodes for the inferior quadrants, and supraclavicular/inferior deep cervical for upper quadrants.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The breast is supplied by the lateral and anterior cutaneous branches of the ist to the 6th intercostal nerves\",\"e\":\"The cutaneous supply to the breast itself varies by position - anterior branches of the 1st to 6th intercostal nerves medially, and laterally by lateral branches of the 2nd to 7th intercostal nerves.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The chief venous drainage is to the subclavian vein\",\"e\":\"Chief venous drainage of the breast is to the axillary vein, not the subclavian vein directly. There is also some drainage to the internal thoracic veins bilaterally.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The majority of the lymph drainage is to the axillary lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"Usually over three-quarters of lymph from the breast drains to the axillary lymph nodes. initially to the pectoral nodes, then onwards to the other groups as shown in the image. The remainder drains mostly to the internal thoracic chain (parasternal) nodes, and the contralateral breast, but also to the inferior phrenic lymph nodes for the inferior quadrants, and the supraclavicular/inferior deep cervical for upper quadrants\\n\\nLateral\\n\\nApical\\n\\nCentral\\n\\nPosterior -Pectoral\\n\\nPectoral\\n\\nInAnatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Two-thirds of the breast covers the serratus anterior muscle\",\"e\":\"Two-thirds of the breast cover the pectoralis major muscle whereas one-third covers the serratus anterior muscle.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant in theatre with a patient who has fallen through his greenhouse and lacerated his dominant wrist on the central palmar aspect. Several tendons are clearly visible. Concerning the muscles of the forearm, which one of the following tendons is most vulnerable to damage?\",\"lead\":\"Concerning the muscles of the forearm, which one of the following tendons is most vulnerable to damage?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"S\\\" Flexor carpi radialis (FCR)\",\"e\":\"The FCR is one of the five superficial muscles of the flexor compartment of the forearm - shown in green below. It arises from the medial epicondyle and inserts radial to the carpal tunnel (not through it), onto the second metacarpal. It is radial to the palmaris longus tendon and, along with it, would be at risk from a palmar wrist laceration. The median nerve is reliably located between the FCR tendon and palmaris longus.\\n\\nFlexor carpi radialis Pronator teres\\n\\nPalmaris longus\\n\\nFlexor carpi ulnaris\\n\\nLeft Forearm\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Flexor pollicis longus\",\"e\":\"Flexor pollicis longus is a deep muscle of the flexor compartment of the forearm, innervated by the anterior interosseous branch of the median nerve. Its tendon runs beneath the flexor retinaculum in the carpal tunnel on the radial side, making it relatively well protected from a palmar aspect laceration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Abductor pollicis longus!\",\"e\":\"The abductor pollicis longus tendon forms the radial-most border of the anatomical snuffbox on the dorsal aspect of the wrist (along with extensor pollicis brevis), and is therefore not at risk in a palmar laceration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Supinator\",\"e\":\"Supinator is a deep muscle of the posterior compartment of the forearm, arising from the lateral epicondyle of the humerus and lateral ulna, inserting into the proximal one-third of the radius. It is not at risk in a palmar laceration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU)\",\"e\":\"The FCU inserts in to the hook of the hamate and 5* metacarpal (MCP) via the pisiform bone, which sits within the FCU tendon. As it sits on the very ulnar-most aspect of the wrist it is less at risk than the FCR tendon from a central laceration.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"An 88-year-old man with known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) presents with acute shortening of breath with low saturations. An arterial blood gas is attempted using his radial artery. Which one of the following concerning the radial artery is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following concerning the radial artery is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It begins opposite the head of the radius\",\"e\":\"The brachial artery bifurcates into the ulnar and radial arteries opposite the neck of the radius (rather than the head), beneath the bicipital aponeurosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It crosses the floor of the anatomical snuffbox\",\"e\":\"The radial artery crosses the floor of the anatomical snuffbox (where it can be palpated). before piercing the first dorsal interosseous muscle to form the deep palmar arch. It gives off its dorsal carpal branch in the proximal part of the snuffbox. The course of the radial artery in the forearm is shown below.\\n\\nDeep Palmar Arch Radial Artery Brachial Artery\\n\\nUlnar Artery\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It ends by forming the majority of the superficial palmar arch in conjunction with the ulnar artery\",\"e\":\"The superficial palmar arch is the direct continuation of the ulnar artery, not the radial. The radial arch terminates as the deep palmar arch.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is the larger of the two terminal branches of the brachial artery\",\"e\":\"The ulnar artery is the larger of the two terminal branches of the brachial artery (the radial is the smaller branch).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The radial recurrent artery arises from the medial side of the radial artery\",\"e\":\"The radial recurrent artery arises from the lateral (radial) aspect of the radial artery and anastomoses with the radial collateral artery (from the profunda brachii).\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant in a skin sparing mastectomy in a 67-year-old woman with ductal carcinoma in situ of her right breast. During the procedure she quizzes you on your understanding of breast anatomy. Which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The breast extends from the lateral margin of the sternum to the mid-axillary line\",\"e\":\"The base of the breast extends from the lateral sternal border to the mid-axillary line horizontally, and from the second to the sixth ribs vertically.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The breast extends from the 2nd to the 5th rib\",\"e\":\"The base of the breast is from the second to the sixth rib vertically, and from the lateral sternal border to the mid-axillary line.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The greater part of the gland lies in the deep fascia\",\"e\":\"The majority of the breast lies on the deep fascia, and is bounded posteriorly by the pectoral fascia (for two-thirds), and the fascia of serratus anterior (for the other third):\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The mammary gland consists of 5-10 lobes\",\"e\":\"The mammary gland has around 15-20 lobules, supported and divided by Cooper's ligaments.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The mammary gland is attached to the skin by the pectineal ligaments\",\"e\":\"The pectineal ligament (or the inguinal ligament of Cooper) is in the pelvis and is not to be confused with Cooper's ligaments in the breast. The suspensory ligaments of Cooper (Cooper's ligaments) support the breast gland and run through the gland from the clavicle and the clavipectoral fascia to the dermis.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":88,\"stem\":\"Your consultant is undertaking a proximal row carpectomy in a 54-year-old man with unremitting pain secondary to arthritis. Which one of the following bones is contained within the proximal row?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following bones is contained within the proximal row?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Capitate\",\"e\":\"The capitate forms the third of the distal row of carpal bones (from lateral to medial), and is the largest bone in the carpus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hamate\",\"e\":\"The hamate is one of the distal row of carpal bones, which are (from lateral to medial) trapezium, trapezoid, capitate and hamate. The hamate has a process, the hook, on the palmar aspect that forms one of the attachments of the flexor retinaculum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Scaphoid\",\"e\":\"The proximal row of carpal bones comprises (from lateral to medial) the scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum and pisiform. The scaphoid is the largest of the proximal row of carpal bones, and articulates proximally with the radius. The tubercle of the scaphoid forms one of the attachments for the flexor retinaculum, as do the hook of the hamate, trapezium, and pisiform.\\n\\nScaphoid Lunate\\n\\nTriquetrum Pisiform Trapezium Trapezoid Capitate\\n\\nHamate teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Trapezium\",\"e\":\"The trapezium is the most radial of the distal row of carpal bones, sitting immediately beneath the first metacarpal on the radial aspect (remember the trapezium is at the base of the thumb). The crest of the trapezium forms one of the radial attachments for the flexor\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trapezoid\",\"e\":\"The trapezoid is the second of the distal row of the carpal bones (from lateral to medial).\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":91,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old woman with a history of deliberate self-harm (DSH) presents to the Emergency Department having cut her wrist on the palmar aspect. Her hand is being examined to decipher if she has divided her median nerve. Paralysis of which muscle indicates division of the median nerve at this level?\",\"lead\":\"Paralysis of which muscle indicates division of the median nerve at this level?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adductor digiti minimi\",\"e\":\"Abductor digiti minimi is the uppermost muscle in the hypothenar eminence, and is supplied by the deep (motor) branch of the ulnar nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Extensor pollicis brevis\",\"e\":\"Extensor pollicis brevis arises from the posterior compartment of the forearm, and inserts on to the base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb. It is innervated by the posterior interosseous nerve (the motor branch of the radial nerve in the forearm).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Flexor policis brevis 1\",\"e\":\"Flexor pollicis brevis is one of the four musclest innervated by the median nerve in the hand. the others being the lateral (radial) two Lumbricals, Opponens pollicis and Abductor pollicis brevis. It lies on the unar aspect of the thenar eminence, as demonstrated in the image below, and would be tested by asking the patient to flex the thumb while palpating the muscle in the thenar eminence (remembering that FPL also contributes to thumb flexion and is innervated by the anterior interosseous branch of the median nerve in the forearm - the median nerve is more commonly tested via the action of abductor pollicis brevis)\\n\\nFlexor pollicis brevis\\n\\nAbductor pollicis brevis\\n\\nOpponens pollicis\\n\\nmonatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The dorsal interossei\",\"e\":\"All the interossei (palmar and dorsal) are innervated by the ulnar nerve, via its deep motor branch.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The ulnar two lumbricals\",\"e\":\"The ulnar (medial) two lumbricals are innervated by the ulnar nerve, in contrast to the lateral (radial) two which are innervated by the median nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":94,\"stem\":\"You are at a pre-assessment clinic for a breast mastectomy list. A patient is due to have a mastectomy, you deem her fit to go ahead for the procedure. Which one of the following regarding breast anatomy is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regarding breast anatomy is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cooper ligaments connect the ducts of the breast to the pectoralis fascia\",\"e\":\"The suspensory ligaments of Cooper (otherwise known as Cooper's ligaments) run from the skin, not the ducts, to the deep fascia behind the breast\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"it overlies the pectoralis major and serratus anterior only\",\"e\":\"The breast classically overlies the pectoralis major muscle by two-thirds, and the serratus anterior by one-third. Overall, the breast lies over pectoralis major, serratus anterior, external oblique, and rectus abdominis fascia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The breast is a modified sweat gland\",\"e\":\"The breast is considered to be a modified apocrine sweat gland (in contrast with eccrine sweat glands).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The breast is supplied with blood by the medial thoracic artery and the internal thoracic artery\",\"e\":\"The blood supply to the breast is chiefly via the internal mammary (internal thoracic) artery from the subclavian, and the lateral thoracic artery from the axillary. It also receives blood from the pectoral branch of the thoracoacromial, and the second to sixth intercostal vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"There are four major lactiferous ducts, each draining one quadrant\",\"e\":\"There are 15-20 lobules in the breast, each of which is drained by a lactiferous duct. All of the ducts converge and open independently at the nipple.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":96,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant on an axillary lymph node clearance for a patient with malignant melanoma with a positive sentinel node biopsy. During the procedure, your consultant quizzes you on the pertinent anatomy of the axillary artery Which of the following is correct regarding the axillary artery?\",\"lead\":\"During the procedure, your consultant quizzes you on the pertinent anatomy of the axillary artery Which of the following is correct regarding the axillary artery?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gives off no branches in its first part\",\"e\":\"The axillary artery gives off the superior thoracic artery from its first part.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is divided into 3 parts relative to pectoralis major\",\"e\":\"The axillary artery is divided into three parts in relation to the pectoralis minor, not the major\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is encompassed by the first digitation of serratus anterior\",\"e\":\"The axillary artery runs anterior to the upper digitations of serratus anterior.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"is invested in a fascial sheath\",\"e\":\"The axillary artery is contained within the axillary sheath, along with the axillary vein and the cords of the brachial plexus. The axillary sheath is a continuation of the prevertebral fascia of the neck. The axillary artery is shown in the image below in green. The axillary artery is divided into three parts\\n• first part - lateral first rib to medial border of pectoralis minor\\n• second part - behind pectoralis minor\\n• third part - lateral border of pectoralis minor to the inferior border of the teres major.\\n\\nThe first part gives off the Superior thoracic artery only, the second part gives off the Thoracoacromial and Lateral thoracic arteries, and the third part gives off the Subscapular, and the Anterior and Posterior circumflex humeral arteries.\\n\\nAxillary artery\\n\\nAnterior circumflex Subscapular artery artery\\n\\nBrachial Posterior circumflex artery C toachmoanatomy artery\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is the continuation of the external carotid\",\"e\":\"The axillary artery is a continuation of the subclavian artery. It begins at the lateral border of the first rib, and ends at the inferior border of teres major where it becomes the brachial artery.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":99,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old is involved in a stabbing. On surgical exploration the musculocutaneous nerve has been divided. They have significant neurological deficit in the affected upper limb. Which one of the following is correct regarding this nerve?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding this nerve?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arises from the medial cord of the brachial plexus\",\"e\":\"The musculocutaneous nerve is the continuation of the lateral cord of the brachial plexus, not the medial.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Becomes the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm (lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve)\",\"e\":\"The musculocutaneous nerve arises from the lateral cord of the brachial plexus. It is motor to the flexor compartment of the arm, supplying brachialis, biceps brachii and coracobrachialis. It then continues as its terminal branch - the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm - supplying sensation to the anterolateral forearm.\\n\\nMusculocutaneous nerve\\n\\nLateral cutaneous nervo\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Supplies skin on the medial aspect of the arm\",\"e\":\"The musculocutaneus nerve supplies sensation to the lateral forearm as its terminal branch. the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm. Sensation to the medial arm is supplied by the medial cutaneous nerve of the arm, a direct branch from the medial cord of the brachial plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Supplies skin over the shoulder\",\"e\":\"Sensation over the shoulder is from the axillary nerve laterally (via its cutaneous branch, the superior lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm, which is tested in the regimental badge area. and from the supraclavicular nerves superiorly (from the C3 and C4 nerve roots)\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supplies triceps muscle\",\"e\":\"Triceps is supplied by the radial nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"A 19-year-old accidentally cuts her lateral forearm on a bread knife. On surgical exploration she has divided her cephalic vein.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding the cephalic vein?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding the cephalic vein?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Drains into the subclavian vein C..\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein runs through the deltopectoral groove and drains to the axillary vein by piercing the clavipectoral fascia\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Forms in the anatomical snuff box\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein (shown below in the image) arises from the radial side of the dorsal venous network of the hand, classically in the floor of the anatomical snuffbox.\\n\\nCephalic vein\\n\\nBasilic Median vein cubital vein\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has a tributary called the basilic vein\",\"e\":\"The basilic vein is a separate entity, forming at the medial aspect of the dorsal venous network of the hand and coursing up the medial aspect of the arm. It pierces the brachial fascia around one-third up the arm and drains directly to the vene comitantes of the brachial artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"is medial to the distal biceps tendon\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein is lateral to the biceps tendon at the elbow - it runs up the lateral aspect of the forearm and arm.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Runs between deltoid and pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein runs in the deltopectoral groove before draining in to the axillary vein. The deltopectoral groove is between pectoralis major and deltoid (not pectoralis minor).\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":105,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old woman has been admitted for a mastectomy and sentinel node procedure for a large area of ductal carcinoma in situ detected on routine mammography. You have been asked to mark out the borders of the breast\\n\\nWhich of the following statements is accurate regarding the breast?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements is accurate regarding the breast?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Contains a maximum of 15 main lactiferous ducts that drain separately at the nipple\",\"e\":\"The breast comprises 15-20 lobules, each of which has a separate lactiferous duct which then confluence to open at the nipple.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is mainly supplied by the lateral thoracic artery and internal mammary artery\",\"e\":\"The blood supply to the breast is chiefly from the lateral thoracic artery (from the axillary> and the internal thoracic also known as the internal mammary artery (from the subclavian). it also receives a contribution from the pectoral branch of the thoracoacromial artery, and the second to sixth intercostal arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In this age group is best visualised by a combination of crainiocaudal and true mediolateral mammograms\",\"e\":\"The standard views taken on a mammogram are cranio-caudal (CC) and mediolateral oblique (MLO) (not truly mediolateral)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is a modified sebaceous gland\",\"e\":\"The breast is considered to be a modified apocrine sweat gland\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Predominantly drains to the internal thoracic nodes\",\"e\":\"Most (> 75%) of the lymph from the breast drains to the axillary lymph nodes. Lymph from the medial aspect of the breast drains to the parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes or across to the opposite breast. Lymph from the inferior aspect of the breast drains to the inferior phrenic nodes\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old woman has presented to the breast clinic with severe right-sided breast pain localised to the upper-outer quadrant. She is also concerned that the right breast is a cup size bigger than the left and she has trouble finding well-fitting bras.\\n\\nWhen considering the development and anatomy of the female breast, which of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"When considering the development and anatomy of the female breast, which of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Drains 60% of its lymph via the axillary lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The majority (>75%) of lymph from the breast drains to the axillary lymph nodes. Lymph from the inferior aspect drains to the inferior phrenic nodes, and lymph from the medial breast drains to the internal thoracic (parasternal) nodes, or to the contralateral breast.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Differences in breast size in the resting state are mainly due to differences in glandular content\",\"e\":\"Breast size in the non-lactating breast is mainly due to fat content rather than glandular content. Breast size increases during pregnancy due to expansion of glandular tissue\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has a nipple which reliable sits in the T3 dermatome\",\"e\":\"The nipple is a marker for the T4 dermatome, not T3.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Has a fairly reliable base irrespective of cup size\",\"e\":\"The base of the breast is from the lateral sternum to the mid-axillary line horizontally, and from the second to sixth rib vertically\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Has an arterial supply derived mainly from the subclavian artery and its branches\",\"e\":\"The blood supply to the breast is chiefly from the lateral thoracic artery (from the axillary). and the internal thoracic (from the subclavian). It also receives a contribution from the pectoral branch of the thoracoacromial artery, and the second to sixth intercostal arteries.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":109,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old woman presents to the breast clinic with a large mass in the left breast and pathologically enlarged lymph nodes in the left axilla. Mammograms and ultrasounds of both the breast and the axillary lesions are highly suggestive of breast cancer with core biopsies of both showing grade 2 ductal carcinoma. She has consented to have a mastectomy and level 3 axillary node clearance.\\n\\nRegarding axillary surgery which one of the following statements best represents normal anatomy?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding axillary surgery which one of the following statements best represents normal anatomy?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"An anaesthetic patch on the medial aspect of the upper arm is a recognised complication due to division of a branch of the lateral branch of the third intercostal nerve\",\"e\":\"Numbness over the medial arm is a recognised complication of axillary surgery, but results from division of the intercostobrachial nerve, which is the lateral cutaneous branch of the second intercostal nerve - T2, rather than the third intercostal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inadvertent division of the thoracodorsal nerve may lead to a winged scapula\",\"e\":\"Winging of the scapula results from division of the long thoracic nerve (of Bell), which innervates serratus anterior.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Level II nodes are those lying lateral to pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"Level 1 nodes lie lateral to the pectoralis minor, level ll lie behind and level III medial to pectoralis minor.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Level il nodes are those lying medial to the pectoralis minor muscle\",\"e\":\"Level Il nodes lie behind the pectoralis minor muscle, not medial to it\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The medial wall of the axilla is partly formed by serratus anterior\",\"e\":\"The medial wall of the axilla is formed by serratus anterior, with ribs 1-4 and the intercostal muscles underlying it. The axilla is a pyramidal space that provides a conduit for neurovascular structures passing from the neck to the arm. The boundaries of the axilla are shown in the image below in axial section:\\n• medially: serratus anterior, with ribs 1-4 and their intercostal muscles underlying\\n• superiorly/the apex: first rib, clavicle and superior scapula, which together form the cervico-axillary canal.\\n• inferiorly: skin, subcutaneous tissue, and axillary fascia\\n• laterally: bicipital groove (intertubercular sulcus)\\n• anteriorly: pectoralis major and minor\\n• posteriorly: latissimus dorsi, teres major and subscapularis, as well as the humerus and scapula.\\n\\nThe floor of the axilla is the axillary fascia. The clavipectoral fascia arises from the clavicle and encloses the pectoralis minor before fusing with the floor of the axilla.\\n\\nAnterior Border Pectoralis major and minor\\n\\nMedial Border Serratus anterior and thoracic wall Lateral Border Ister tubecular sulcus\\n\\nPosterior Border Scupuloris, teres major, fotissimus dorsi\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":112,\"stem\":\"You are performing a level 3 axillary dissection for a woman with node-positive breast cancer. Your consultant is supervising. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding axillary anatomy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding axillary anatomy?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The clavipectoral fascia is pierced by the basilic vein\",\"e\":\"The basilic vein is formed at the unar aspect of the dorsal venous network of the hand, and ascends up the medial aspect of the arm to pierce the brachial fascia (not the clavipectoral fascia) and joins with the venae comitantes of the brachial artery. It is the cephalic vein that pierces the clavipectoral fascia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The pedicle to latissimus dorsi is an anterior axillary structure\",\"e\":\"Latissimus dorsi forms part of the posterior wall of the axilla, not anterior. Its pedicle (the thoracodorsal artery) arises posteriorly from the subscapular artery (a branch of the axillary artery).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Division of the nerve to serratus anterior may lead to winging of the scapula\",\"e\":\"Division of the long thoracic nerve (of Bell), which supplies the serratus anterior muscle, leads to winging of the scapula. It is particularly at risk during axillary dissection. The boundaries of the axilla are shown below in axial section:\\n\\nAnterior Border Pectoralis major and minor\\n\\nMedial Border Serratus anterior and thoracic wall Lateral Border Intertubecular sulcus\\n\\nPosterior Border Scupuluris, teres major, Satissimus dorsi\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Rotter's nodes lie between the chest wall and serratus anterior\",\"e\":\"Rotter's nodes (interpectoral nodes) lie between the pectoralis minor and major muscles. They are not always present\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The clavipectoral fascia is overlain by the T4 dermatome\",\"e\":\"The region of the clavipectoral fascia is supplied by the supraclavicular nerves from the C3 and C4 dermatomes. The T4 dermatome is the level of the nipple.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":115,\"stem\":\"Which muscle initiates abduction of the shoulder?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscle initiates abduction of the shoulder?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Deltoid\",\"e\":\"The deltoid muscle is responsible for abduction of the shoulder between 15° and around 90°. After 90° scapula rotation is responsible, mainly via the trapezius muscle. It does not initiate abduction of the shoulder - this is performed by the supraspinatus muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Infraspinatus\",\"e\":\"The infraspinatus muscle is responsible for external rotation of the shoulder, not abduction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Subscapularis\",\"e\":\"The subscapularis muscle is responsible for internal rotation of the shoulder - it is the only muscle of the rotator cuff that attaches to the lesser tuberosity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Supraspinatus\",\"e\":\"The supraspinatus muscle is responsible for initiation of abduction of the shoulder, between 0° and 15°. After 15°, it acts to hold the head of the humerus in the glenoid fossa. In tears of the supraspinatus tendon, patients often initiate abduction by leaning over to the affected side, allowing the deltoid muscle to take over. The muscles of the rotator cuff* are shown in the image below, with the supraspinatus in red.\\n\\nSubscapularis\\n• supraspinatus I fnfraspinatus Teres minor\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trapezius\",\"e\":\"The trapezius muscle acts to elevate and rotate the scapula, it does not abduct the shoulder.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":118,\"stem\":\"A. 24-year-old man is admitted to the vascular ward following an open exploration of a laceration to the ulnar aspect of his wrist. During the operation the basilic vein was tied off Which nerve accompanies this vein in the forearm?\",\"lead\":\"During the operation the basilic vein was tied off Which nerve accompanies this vein in the forearm?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior interosseous\",\"e\":\"The anterior interosseous nerve is the deep branch of the median nerve. It forms as the median nerve enters between the two heads of the pronator teres muscle. It does not accompany the basilic vein\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Median\",\"e\":\"The median nerve lies between the flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS) and flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) muscles in the forearm. It does not accompany the basilic vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medial cutaneous\",\"e\":\"The medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm accompanies the basilic vein, and the lateral cutaneous nerve follows the cephalic vein. The basilic vein forms at the ulnar aspect of the dorsum of the hand and continues up the medial aspect of the arm to join with the venae comitantes of the brachial artery in the mid-arm to form the axillary vein. The cephalic and basilic veins are shown in the image below, connected by the median cubital vein in the antecubital fossa.\\n\\nCephalic vein\\n\\nMedian Basilic vein cubital vein\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Radial\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve continues into the forearm as the posterior interosseous nerve (motor) and the superficial branch of the radial nerve (cutaneous). The superficial branch is on the radial side of the forearm, whereas the basilic vein is on the ulnar aspect.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulnar\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve accompanies the ulnar artery in Guyon's canal. It lies deep to the flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU) muscle in the forearm, whereas the basilic vein is in the superficial fascia of the forearm.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":121,\"stem\":\"You are working in the transplant team and have been asked to assist your consultant with the formation of an arteriovenous fistula for the purpose of haemodialysis. Your consultant has chosen to fashion a brachiocephalic fistula in the patient's non-dominant antecubital fossa\\n\\nIn the cubital fossa, which is the most common anatomical arrangement from medial to lateral of the median nerve, biceps tendon and brachial artery?\",\"lead\":\"In the cubital fossa, which is the most common anatomical arrangement from medial to lateral of the median nerve, biceps tendon and brachial artery?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Brachial artery - biceps tendon - median nerve\",\"e\":\"The median nerve is the most medial of the structures in the antecubital fossa\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Biceps tendon - brachial artery - median nerve\",\"e\":\"The biceps tendon is more lateral than the brachial artery and the median nerve\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Brachial artery - median nerve - biceps tendon\",\"e\":\"The brachial artery is between the median nerve (most medial) and the biceps tendon.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Median nerve - biceps tendon - brachial artery\",\"e\":\"The biceps tendon is lateral to the brachial artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Median nerve - brachial artery - biceps tendon\",\"e\":\"The (ante) cubital fossa is a triangular area formed by a line between the two epicondyles of the humerus, the lateral border of the pronator teres muscle and the medial border of brachioradialis: The roof is formed by antebrachial fascia, reinforced by the bicipital aponeurosis. Its contents are shown in the image below - from lateral to medial:\\n• the radial nerve, beneath brachioradialis laterally\\n• the biceps tendon\\n• the brachial artery\\n• the median nerve.\\n\\nMedian nerve Biceps tendon Brachial artery\\n\\nRadial nerve\\n\\n© tachmoznatomy\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":124,\"stem\":\"A patient requires an open reduction and internal fixation of a proximal humerus fracture. A humeral nail is not appropriate in this case so a PHILOS plate is utilised. Which vein is encountered on the anterior deltopectoral approach to the shoulder joint?\",\"lead\":\"Which vein is encountered on the anterior deltopectoral approach to the shoulder joint?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Basilic vein\",\"e\":\"The basilic vein originates at the ulnar aspect of the dorsum of the hand. It ascends up the medial aspect of the arm and pierces the deep fascia of the arm to join with the venae comitantes of the brachial artery to form the axillary vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cephalic vein\",\"e\":\"In the deltopectoral approach an incision is made following the line of the deltopectoral groove. The deltopectoral fascia is encountered first and the cephalic vein is surrounded in a layer of fat and is used to identify the interval. The cephalic vein begins at the radial aspect of the dorsum of the hand in the anatomical snuffbox and ascends up the lateral aspect of the arm. It continues up the lateral aspect and arches over to run in the deltopectoral groove where it pierces the clavipectoral fascia to join with the axillary vein. Its course can be seen in the image below. It gives off a branch (the median cubital vein) in the cubital fossa which is commonly used for venepuncture.\\n\\nCephalic vein\\n\\nMedian Basilic vein cubital vein\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cubital vein\",\"e\":\"The cubital vein runs across the antecubital fossa - it is commonly used for blood sampling:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Venae comitantes of the brachial artery\",\"e\":\"The venae comitantes of the brachial artery run with it along its length. When joined by the basilic vein, they form the axillary vein. They do not run in the deltopectoral groove.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"None of the above\",\"e\":\"It is the cephalic vein that runs up the lateral aspect of the arm and runs in the deltopectoral groove. It pierces the deltopectoral fascia to join with the axillary vein.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":127,\"stem\":\"You are leading the trauma team in Accident and Emergency and are dealing with a 42-year-old man who has been stabbed in the left groin. You ask your house officer to establish intravenous access, which she does with a large bore cannula in the patient's cephalic vein. Which one of the following statements most accurately describes the course and anatomy of the cephalic vein?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements most accurately describes the course and anatomy of the cephalic vein?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Forms in the anatomical snuftok\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein begins at the radial aspect of the dorsum of the hand in the anatomical snuffbox and ascends up the lateral aspect of the arm. It continues up the lateral aspect and arches over to run in the deltopectoral groove where it pierces the clavipectoral fascia to join with the axillary vein. The cephalic and basilic veins are shown in the image below.\\n\\nCephalic vein\\n\\nBasilic Median vein cubital vein\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is deep to the cutaneous nerve of the forearm\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein runs alongside the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm, rather than deep to it.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Joins the brachial artery at the elbow\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein does not join the brachial artery. it joins the axillary vein in the deltopectoral groove:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is medial to the common biceps tendon\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein is lateral to the common biceps tendon in the antecubital fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Forms the axillary vein by joining the basilic vein\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein and basilic vein do not join directly. The cephalic vein joins the axillary vein in the deltopectoral groove, and the basilic vein joins the venae comitantes of the brachial artery in the mid-arm.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":130,\"stem\":\"The radial artery crosses the floor of the anatomical snuffbox What are the palmar and dorsal boundaries of the anatomical snuffbox?\",\"lead\":\"The radial artery crosses the floor of the anatomical snuffbox What are the palmar and dorsal boundaries of the anatomical snuffbox?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Palmar: flexor pollicis longus - dorsal: extensor pollicis longus\",\"e\":\"The flexor pollicis longus does not form part of the anatomical snuffbox, it passes through the carpal tunnel on the palmar aspect of the wrist.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Palmar: abductor pollicis brevis and extensor pollicis longus - dorsal: abductor pollicis longus\",\"e\":\"There is no such tendon as the abductor pollicis brevis (this muscle is part of the thenar eminence), only the abductor pollicis longus. Additionally, the extensor pollicis longus forms the dorsal boundary not the palmar, and the abductor pollicis longus forms part of the palmar boundary not the dorsal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Palmar: abductor pollicis brevis and extensor pollicis brevis - dorsal: abductor pollicis longus\",\"e\":\"There is no such tendon as the abductor pollicis brevis, only the abductor pollicis longus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Palmar: extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus - dorsal: extensor pollicis longus\",\"e\":\"The dorsal border of the anatomical snuffbox is the extensor pollicis longus tendon. The palmar border is formed by both the abductor pollicis longus and the extensor pollicis brevis tendons, as shown in the image. Proximally the border is formed by the radial styloid process. The floor is formed by the trapezium and scaphoid. As it leaves the cubital region, the radial artery passes medial to the biceps tendon, and superficial to the insertion of the pronator teres muscle. At the wrist, the artery passes across the floor of the anatomical snuffbox over the trapezium, and enters the hand between the two heads of the first dorsal interosseous and the oblique and transverse heads of the adductor pollicis muscles continuing as the deep palmar arch. The arch is completed medially by a branch of the ulnar artery and allows for anastomosis between these two arteries.\\n\\nExtensor pollicis longus\\n\\nExtensor pollicis brevis\\n\\n- Abductor pollicis longus\\n\\n© teachmeanators\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Palmar: extensor pollicis longus and abductor pollicis longus - dorsal: extensor pollicis brevis\",\"e\":\"The extensor pollicis brevis forms the palmar boundary along with the abductor pollicis longus, and the extensor pollicis longus forms the dorsal boundary.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":133,\"stem\":\"A patient is undergoing an upper limb vascular bypass procedure. The consultant is able to anticipate the various branches from the main vessels.\\n\\nFrom which of the following do the thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries normally branch off?\",\"lead\":\"From which of the following do the thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries normally branch off?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries are branches of the second part of the axillary artery, not the subclavian.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The first part of the axillary artery\",\"e\":\"The thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries branch from the second part of the axillary artery, not the first.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The second part of the axillary artery\",\"e\":\"The axiliary artery commences at the lateral border of the first rib as a continuation of the subclavian artery. The axillary artery becomes the brachial artery at the inferior border of teres major - the axillary artery is labelled in green in the image and the brachial artery in red. The axillary artery can be divided into three parts by pectoralis minor. In its first part, it gives off the superior thoracic artery; in the second part, the thoraco-acromial and lateral thoracic arteries, and in the third part, the subscapular, anterior and posterior circumflex\\n\\nAxillary artery\\n\\nAnterior circumflex Subscapular artery artery\\n\\nBrachial Posterior circumflex artery teachmeanatomy artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The third part of the axillary artery\",\"e\":\"The thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries branch from the second part of the axillary artery, not the third.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The proximal brachial artery\",\"e\":\"The axillary artery becomes the brachial artery at the inferior border of teres major. The thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries branch from the second part of the axillary artery, not from the brachial artery.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":136,\"stem\":\"A patient has new onset AF and has presented with a swollen right upper limb. You diagnose clinically a DVT and USS confirms the presence of a thrombus in the axillary vein. The axillary vein is normally formed by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The axillary vein is normally formed by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The union of the cephalic and vena comitantes of the brachial artery\",\"e\":\"The axillary vein is formed by the union of the basilic vein and the venae comitantes of the brachial artery at the inferior border of teres major. The cephalic vein runs up the lateral aspect of the arm and pierces the clavipectoral fascia to join the axillary vein at the superior aspect of pectoralis minor.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The union of the cephalic and basilic veins\",\"e\":\"The cephalic and basilic veins do not unite directly (although they do communicate via the median cubital vein).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is solely the continuation of the cephalic vein\",\"e\":\"The axillary vein is formed by the union of the basilic vein and the venae comitantes of the brachial artery. The cephalic vein joins the axillary vein directly.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is solely the continuation of the basilic vein\",\"e\":\"The axillary vein is formed by the union of the basilic vein and the venae comitantes of the brachial artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The union of the basilic vein and the vena comitantes of the brachial artery\",\"e\":\"The axillary vein is formed by the union of the basilic vein (which runs up the medial/ulnar aspect of the forearm and arm) and the venae comitantes of the brachial artery at the inferior border of teres major. The cephalic vein by contrast is formed at the radial aspect of the dorsal venous network of the hand, running over the anatomical snuffbox and then up the lateral/radial aspect to joins the axillary vein directly. The superficial venous network of the upper limb is shown below.\\n\\nCephalic vein\\n\\nMedian Basilic vein cubital vein\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":139,\"stem\":\"A patient has winging of the scapula after a difficult axillary clearance operation for breast cancer. You suspect a long thoracic nerve injury. Which part of the brachial plexus does the long thoracic nerve branch from?\",\"lead\":\"Which part of the brachial plexus does the long thoracic nerve branch from?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"From the superior trunk\",\"e\":\"The superior trunk gives off the suprascapular nerve, and the nerve to subclavius. The long thoracic nerve is formed directly from the C5, C6 and C7 roots.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"From all three posterior divisions\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve is formed directly from the C5. C6 and C7 roots: The posterior divisions all unite to form the posterior cord, which gives off the upper and lower subscapular nerves, and thoracodorsal nerve and the axillary nerve before terminating as the radial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"From the medial and lateral cords\",\"e\":\"The medial and lateral cords form the median nerve, with the lateral cord terminating as the musculocutaneous nerve and the medial cord terminating as the ulnar nerve. They do not contribute to the long thoracic nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"From the c8 and T1 roots\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve is formed from the C5, C6 and C7 roots, not the C8 and T1 roots. The C8 and T1 roots contribute to the median and unar nerves, supplying the intrinsic muscles of the hand (via the ulnar nerve), and the thenar muscles and lateral two lumbricals (via the median nerve).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"From the C5. C6 and C7 roots\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve (of Bell) is formed directly from the C5, C6 and C7 roots. It travels posteriorly to the C8 and T1 roots, and superficially over the serratus anterior muscle in the medial axilla where it is vulnerable to damage during axillary dissection. Damage to the long thoracic nerve leads to winging of the scapula. The brachial plexus with its main branches is shown in schematic in the image below. The three posterior divisions form the posterior cord. The medial and lateral cords form the median nerve. The C8 and T1 anterior division continues as the medial cord.\\n\\nBranches Cords Divisions Trunks Route,C5 TOPPIN TE\\n\\nMusculocutaneous Lateral Superior.\\n\\nAxillary\\n\\nPosterior Middle Median -- C7\\n\\nRadial _ C8 Inferior Medial Ulnar\\n\\n© teachineanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":142,\"stem\":\"A patient is undergoing a carpal tunnel release. The consultant is happy to let you do the procedure if you are able to answer a question on the median nerve and a structure you should be carefully checking for. Which one of the following sentences best describes the entry of the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve into the hand?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following sentences best describes the entry of the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve into the hand?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The nerve enters the palm through Guyon's canal\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve and unar artery enter the hand through Guyon's canal, which is a space superficial to the flexor retinaculum, between the pisiform bone (which sits within the flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU) tendon) and the hook of the hamate. The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve is given off before it enters the carpal tunnel, and runs superficial to the flexor retinaculum to provide sensation to the palm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The nerve enters the palm through the carpal tunnel\",\"e\":\"The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve usually branches off proximal to the carpal tunnel. and travels superficial to its roof (the flexor retinaculum) to supply sensation to the palm.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The nerve enters the palm superficial to the flexor retinaculum\",\"e\":\"The palmar cutaneous branch is given off proximal to the carpal tunnel (an average of 4 cm proximal to the distal wrist crease) and travels superficial to the flexor retinaculum to supply sensation to the palm. It usually remains as one distinct branch as it passes in to the palm, usually underlying the thenar crease, but may branch proximal to the distal wrist crease. It is important in carpal tunnel surgery, as the incision must be planned to avoid its transection, which would lead to absence of palmar sensation on the radial side. The fact that the palmar cutaneous branch does not travel through the carpal tunnel explains why palmar symptoms are usually absent in carpal tunnel syndrome, as sensation to the fingers is provided via the common digital nerves which branch directly from the median nerve after it emerges from the carpal tunnel, rather than the palmar cutaneous branch which supplies the palm only This is shown in the image below, with the palmar cutaneous branch supply highlighted in\\n\\nPalmar cutaneous branch Digital cutaneous branch\\n\\nPalm Dorsum atomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The nerve branches off proximal to the the wrist and runs along the dorsum of the hand\",\"e\":\"The palmar cutaneous branch, as the name suggests, supplies sensation to the palmar aspect of the hand, not the dorsum. It does not therefore run on the dorsum of the hand\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The nerve runs over the radial styloid into the anatomical snuffbox\",\"e\":\"It is the superficial branch of the radial nerve (not the median) that runs through the anatomical snuffbox to supply the radial side of the dorsum of the hand\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":145,\"stem\":\"A patient with an ulnar nerve palsy is exhibiting the unar paradox. You ask the patient to come to an orthopaedic teaching day due to the interesting clinical sign. Which muscles does the ulnar nerve supply in the forearm?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscles does the ulnar nerve supply in the forearm?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Flexor and extensor carpi ulnaris\",\"e\":\"In the forearm, the ulnar nerve supplies flexor carpi ulnaris, and the ulnar (medial) ½ of flexor digitorum profundus only. Extensor carpi ulnaris is a posterior compartment muscle, arising from the lateral epicondyle of the humerus and inserting in to the dorsal fifth metacarpal. It is therefore supplied by the radial nerve via its deep posterior interosseous branch.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Flexor carpi ulnaris and the unar half the flexor digitorum profundus muscles\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve is a terminal branch of the medial cord of the brachial plexus. It has no branches in the arm, and enters the forearm posterior to the medial epicondyle of the humerus, between the two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris. It runs between FCU and flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) muscle to the ulnar aspect of the wrist, where it enters the hand through Guyon's canal, on the superficial ulnar aspect of the wrist between the pisiform bone and the hook of the hamate. In the forearm it supplies only flexor carpi ulnaris and the ulnar (medial) half of flexor digitorum profundus. The superficial muscles of the forearm (including FCU) are shown below.\\n\\nFlexor carpi radialis Pronator teres\\n\\nPalmaris longus\\n\\nFlexor carpi uinaris\\n\\nLeft Forearm:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus\",\"e\":\"Flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus are supplied by the deep branch of the median nerve - the anterior interosseous nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"All the interosseous muscles\",\"e\":\"All the interosse are supplied by the ulnar nerve, via its deep motor branch. They are not muscles of the forearm\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The ulnar nerve does not supply any forearm muscles\",\"e\":\"In the forearm it supplies flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU) and the ulnar (medial) half of the flexor digitorum profundus (FDP). It does not supply any muscles in the upper arm, however.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":148,\"stem\":\"Which muscle is used to descriptively subdivide the axillary artery into three parts?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscle is used to descriptively subdivide the axillary artery into three parts?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Teres major\",\"e\":\"The axillary artery becomes the brachial artery at the inferior border of teres major, but it is pectoralis minor that is used to subdivide the axillary artery in to three parts anatomically.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Teres minor\",\"e\":\"Teres minor is one of the rotator cuff muscles and is not therefore directly related to the axillary artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pectoralis major\",\"e\":\"It is pectoralis minor that is used to divide the axillary artery in to three parts, not pectoralis major.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"Pectoralis minor arises from the third to fifth ribs and inserts on to the coracoid process of the scapula. It overlies the axillary artery, dividing it in to three parts anatomically: proximal to the muscle, beneath it, and distal to it. The axillary artery can be seen in green in the image below, with the cut edge of pectoralis minor seen overlying it from above. These three parts* each have one, two and three branches respectively.\\n\\nAxillary artery\\n\\nAnterior circumflex Subscapular artery artery\\n\\nBrachial Posterior circumflex artery toachmoanatomy artery\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Latissimus dorsi\",\"e\":\"It is pectoralis minor that is used as a landmark to divide up the axiliary artery rather than latissimus dorsi, which inserts in to the floor of the bicipital groove of the humerus and is therefore posterior to the axillary artery.\\n\\nThe axillary artery begins at the lateral border of the first rib as the continuation of the subclavian artery, and ends at the inferior border of the teres major. For descriptive purposes the axillary artery is divided into three parts by the pectoralis minor. The first part has one branch (superior thoracic), the second part gives off two branches (thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic), and the third part gives off three branches (subscapular, anterior circumflex humeral, and posterior circumflex humeral). The lateral thoracic artery is larger in women, and it supplies the lateral part of the mammary gland.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":151,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old man has been referred to your orthopaedic clinic with pain and tingling affecting the ring and little fingers of both hands. He is a keen cyclist and has just come back from a month long cycling holiday. You suspect compression of the ulnar nerve and advise him to rest, hoping for a non-surgical resolution. How would you describe the course of the affected nerve?\",\"lead\":\"How would you describe the course of the affected nerve?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"After giving off branches to the flexor carpi ulnaris it lies deep to flexor Digitorumprofundus\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve runs in the forearm between the flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU) and flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) muscles, supplying all of FCU and the ulnar (medial) half of FDP. It is therefore superficial to FDP rather than deep to it.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It enters the forearm by passing between the two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve enters the forearm by winding around the posterior aspect of the medial epicondyle of the humerus, and travelling between the two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris in a fibro-osseous channel known as the cubital tunnel. The two heads of FCU arise from the medial epicondyle of the humerus, and the olecranon/posterior ulna respectively. These bony landmarks (medial epicondyle and olecranon) form the medial and lateral borders of the cubital tunnel, with the roof formed by the cubital tunnel retinaculum, and the floor by the capsule of the elbow joint. The path of the ulnar nerve in the arm and forearm is shown in the image below. The cubital tunnel is an important site of entrapment of the ulnar nerve, with cubital tunnel syndrome being the second most common nerve entrapment of the upper limb after carpal tunnel syndrome. Symptoms include pain, paraesthesia, and numbness in the ring and little fingers as well as weakness of the hand, and chronic compression can lead to clawing.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"This nerve is a terminal branch of the medial cord of the brachial plexus (C5, 6, 7)\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve is the terminal branch of the medial cord of the brachial plexus, but the nerve roots of the medial cord are C8 and T1, not C5. C6 and C7 (these are the nerve roots of the long thoracic nerve of Bell).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The nerve wraps around the posterior aspect of the lateral epicondyle at the level of the elbow\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve wraps around the posterior aspect of the medial epicondyle of the humerus, not the lateral epicondyle. It is the radial nerve that passes anterior to the lateral epicondyle, between brachialis and brachioradialis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"This nerve enters the hand deep to the flexor retinaculum with the median nerve\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve enters the hand through Guyon's canal, which is a space superficial to the flexor retinaculum on the ulnar, palmar, aspect of the wrist, between the pisiform bone and the hook of the hamate.\\n\\nThe ulnar nerve is a terminal branch of the medial cord of the brachial plexus (C8 and T1 nerve roots): At the elbow it passes around the posterior aspect of the medial epicondyle and in the forearm lies on the anterior aspect of flexor digitorum profundus (FDP.). It has no branches in the arm but in the forearm it supplies flexor carpi uinaris and the medial half of the FDP, and the majority of the small muscles in the hand. It enters the hand superficial to the flexor retinaculum unlike the median nerve which passes deep. In this case compression has occurred at Guyon's canal (between the pisiform and hamate bones of the carpus).\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":155,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old ex-professional rugby player presents to your orthopaedic clinic with a painful arc of rotation in his right shoulder. It is increasingly affecting his life and he finds it difficult to brush his hair and reach for plates in his cupboard. You suspect damage to his rotator cuff mechanism is the root of his problems. How would you describe the rotator cuff mechanism?\",\"lead\":\"How would you describe the rotator cuff mechanism?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The supraspinatus muscle forms part of the rotator cuff and is supplied by the axillary nerve\",\"e\":\"The supraspinatus does indeed form part of the rotator cuff mechanism, arising from the supraspinous fossa of the scapula and inserting on to the greater tubercle of the humerus most superiorly. It is however innervated by the suprascapular nerve, not the axiliary nerve (which innervates deltoid and teres minor).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The teres major forms part of the rotator cuff\",\"e\":\"Teres major arises from the angle of the scapula and inserts in to the medial edge of the bicipital groove of the humerus. It therefore is not one of the rotator cuff muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The musculature of the rotator cuff attach from the clavicle to the head of the humerus\",\"e\":\"The muscles of the rotator cuff all arise from the scapula (not the clavicle), and insert on to the head of the humerus - subscapularis on to the lesser tubercle, and supraspinatus, infraspinatus, and teres minor on to the greater tubercle\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The nerve supply of the rotator cuff musculature arises from C7, 8 and Ti\",\"e\":\"The rotator cuff muscles are all supplied by more proximal nerve roots within the brachial plexus. Supra- and infraspinatus are supplied by the suprascapular nerve (C4, C5, C6); subscapularis by the upper and lower subscapular nerves (C5, C6, C7); and teres minor by the axillary nerve (C5, C6). The rotator cuff* consists of the supraspinatus (suprascapular n. C5), infraspinatus (suprascapular n. C5, 6), teres minor (axillary nerve C5), and subscapularis (upper and lower subscapular nerves C5, 6). All muscles of the rotator cuff mechanism attach from the scapula to the head of the humerus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The infraspinatus muscle forms part of the rotator cuff musculature and is supplied by the suprascapular nerve\",\"e\":\"Both supraspinatus and infraspinatus are supplied by the suprascapular nerve (C5, C6) which is a branch from the upper trunk of the brachial plexus. The suprascapular nerve runs through the suprascapular notch, below the transverse scapular ligament, supplying both muscles. Supraspinatus arises from the supraspinous fossa and inserts on to the greater tubercle of the humerus most superiorly (as seen in the image). Infraspinatus arises from the infraspinous fossa of the scapula and also inserts on to the greater tubercle of the humerus. in between supraspinatus and teres minor.\\n\\nSubscapularis\\n• Supraspinatus\\n• Infraspinatus\\n• Tererminor\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":158,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has been referred to your surgical clinic with symptoms of progressive pins and needles affecting the lateral three and a half fingers of her dominant hand. It is so severe now that she cannot hold drinks and is kept awake at night because of the pain. You suspect a median nerve neuropathy. How would you describe the course of the affected nerve?\",\"lead\":\"How would you describe the course of the affected nerve?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It enters the hand through the canal of Guyon\",\"e\":\"It is the ulnar nerve, not the median nerve, that enters the hand through Guyon's canal. The median nerve enters the hand through the carpal tunnel, beneath the flexor retinaculum, and it is compression of the nerve within this bony canal (formed by the carpal bones and the flexor retinaculum) that causes the symptoms of carpal tunnel syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It supplies only two thenar muscles and the ist and 2nd lumbricals\",\"e\":\"The median nerve* supplies all three thenar muscles (flexor pollicis brevis, abductor pollicis brevis and opponens pollicis) as well as the lateral (radial) two lumbricals.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It enters the wrist deep to the extensor retinaculum\",\"e\":\"The median nerve enters the hand deep to the flexor retinaculum, not the extensor retinaculum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It enters the hand between the tendons of the flexor pollicis longis and flexor digitorum superficialis\",\"e\":\"The median nerve enters the hand through the carpal tunnel, where it sits between then flexor pollicis longus and the most radial flexor digitorum superficialis tendon. the flexor carpi radialis tendon is superior to the flexor retinaculum. Bear in mind that the distal wrist crease marks the proximal aspect of the flexor retinaculum. The path of the median nerve in the arm and forearm is shown in the image.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The medial root of the median nerve is the continuation of the lateral cord of the brachial plexus\",\"e\":\"The medial root of the median nerve is a continuation of the medial cord, not the lateral cord. Remember that the median nerve is formed by the union of contributions from both the lateral and medial cords, whereas the posterior cord goes on to form the axillary and radial nerves.\\n\\nThe median nerve enters the hand through the carpal tunnel which is deep to the flexor retinaculum. It supplies motor fibres to the three thenar muscles and to the first and second lumbricals. Median nerve injury frequently occurs just proximal to the flexor retinaculum - the common site of wrist lacerations in deliberate self harm. The lateral root of the median nerve is the continuation of the lateral cord of the brachial plexus; the lateral root is joined by the medial root of the median nerve to form the median nerve\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":161,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man presents to your orthopedic clinic with a clinically ruptured proximal biceps brachii sustained during a 'snatch' in weightlifting. There is an obvious 'Popeye' deformity. How would you describe the biceps brachii muscle?\",\"lead\":\"How would you describe the biceps brachii muscle?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is a powerful supinator of the forearm\",\"e\":\"The biceps brachii muscie has two heads - the short head arising from the coracoid process of the scapula, and the long head arising from the supraglenoid tubercle - these two heads are shown in the image. The tendon of the long head travels through the capsule of the glenohumeral joint and the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus and is therefore at risk of rupture. Its tendon inserts distally on to the radial tuberosity, making it a powerful supinator of the forearm, particularly when the elbow is flexed. The tendon also gives off the bicipital aponeurosis distally. which forms a sheet of fascia that covers the antecubital fossa.\\n\\nCoracold process\\n\\nBiceps brachil (Short head) Biceps brachii (long head)\\n\\nCoracobrachialis\\n\\nBrachialis\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It lies lateral to the brachialis muscle in the arm\",\"e\":\"Biceps brachii lies superficially to brachialis in the arm, not laterally to it.\\n\\nBiceps brachii lies superficially to brachialis in the arm, not laterally to it\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is the anatomically most 'constant' muscle in the human body\",\"e\":\"Although its name suggests that biceps brachi has two heads, it is not uncommon to find a third head arising from the superior aspect of brachialis. It is therefore not a particularly 'constant' muscle\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is supplied by branches of the musculocutaneous nerve (C 7, 8)\",\"e\":\"Biceps is indeed supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve (as are all the muscles in the anterior compartment of the arm), but the nerve roots of the musculocutaneous nerve are C5, C6 and C7, not C7 and C8.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The two heads arise from a common origin on the scapula\",\"e\":\"The two heads of biceps arise from different parts of the scapula: the long head from the supraglenoid tubercle, and the short head from the coracoid process.\\n\\nThere are three muscles in the anterior compartment of the arm (biceps brachii, brachialis. and coracobrachialis) which are all supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7 origin) The brachialis lies posterior to the biceps brachii muscle. The long head of the biceps brachii is attached to the supraglenoid tubercle of the scapula, and the short head to the tip of the coracoid process of the scapula. As the muscle passes down it forms a common belly and inserts as two tendons, the first into the radial tuberosity and the second into the ulnar part of the antebrachial fascia as the bicipital aponeurosis. Biceps is a powerful supinator of the forearm and flexor at the elbow. Anatomically it is one of the most variable muscies in the human body by origin, insertion and number of muscle bellies.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":164,\"stem\":\"You have been asked to place a large bore intravenous cannula into a trauma patient in A&E. He appears relatively 'shut down' peripherally but you manage to secure intravenous access in his right antecubital fossa How would you describe the antecubital fossa?\",\"lead\":\"He appears relatively 'shut down' peripherally but you manage to secure intravenous access in his right antecubital fossa How would you describe the antecubital fossa?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is bounded medially by the brachioradialis\",\"e\":\"Brachioradialis forms the lateral (radial) border of the antecubital fossa, not the medial border.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is bounded laterally by the pronator teres\",\"e\":\"Pronator teres forms the medial border of the antecubital fossa, not the lateral border.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It contains the terminal branches of the brachial artery\",\"e\":\"The brachial artery divides in to its two terminal branches (the smaller radial artery and the larger ulnar artery) in the distal aspect of the antecubital fossa, opposite the neck of the radius. The cubital fossat is described as having the following borders, as shown in the image:\\n• Proximally: a line connecting the medial and lateral epicondyles of the humerus\\n• Medially: lateral border of the pronator teres\\n• Laterally: medial border of brachioradialis\\n• Roof: Bicipital aponeurosis, antebrachial and brachial fascia, subcutaneous fat and skin\\n• Floor: Brachialis and supinator muscles.\\n\\nSuperior border: Imaginary line between the epicondyles\\n\\nMedial border:\\n• Lateral border: Lateral border of the pronator teres brachioradialis\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The brachial artery runs on the lateral side of the biceps brachii muscle\",\"e\":\"The brachial artery runs medial to the biceps brachii muscle in the arm, and remains medial to the biceps tendon in the antecubital fossa. The brachial artery is between the biceps tendon (laterally) and the median nerve (medially) in the antecubital fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The bicipitalaponeurosis forms the floor of the cubital fossa\",\"e\":\"The bicipital aponeurosis is given off from the biceps tendon, and forms a sheet of fascia which forms the roof (not the floor) of the antecubital fossa, along with antebrachial and brachial fascia, subcutaneous fat and skin. The cubital fossa is bounded medially by the pronator teres and laterally by the brachioradialis. The roof of the fossa is formed by deep fascia and includes the bicipital aponeurosis; the floor is formed by the brachialis and supinator muscles of the arm and forearm. The fossa contains the median nerve, brachial artery, tendon of biceps, radial nerve and its posterior interosseus branch from medial to lateral.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":167,\"stem\":\"You have been asked to assist your consultant in theatre for a vascular case. A 23-year-old man has fallen through a reinforced glass window and has severed his brachial artery at the cubital fossa. A primary anastomosis with a vein patch is attempted. How would you describe the course of the affected artery?\",\"lead\":\"How would you describe the course of the affected artery?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is the continuation of the axillary artery\",\"e\":\"The brachial artery is the direct continuation of the axillary artery, beginning at the inferior border of teres major. It gives off the profunda brachii (deep artery of the arm) proximally. which accompanies the radial nerve in the spiral groove of the humerus - it can be seen winding behind the humerus in the image. The brachial artery continues anterior to brachialis and medial to biceps brachii, where it can be palpated against the humerus on the medial arm. It terminates in the cubital fossa opposite the neck of the radius as the radial and ulnar arteries.\\n\\nProfunda Brachil\\n\\nBrachial Artery\\n\\nUlnar Radial Artery Artery teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It begins at the inferior border of the teres minor\",\"e\":\"The brachial artery begins at the inferior border of teres major, not teres minor.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It usually ends opposite the head of the radius where it divides into the radial and ulnar arteries\",\"e\":\"The brachial artery ends in the cubital fossa opposite the neck of the radius (not the head) by dividing in to its two terminal branches beneath the bicipital aponeurosis - the smaller radial artery and larger ulnar artery. Within the cubital fossa, the brachial artery is between the biceps tendon (laterally) and the median nerve (medially).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It crosses the median nerve anteriorly in the middle of the arm\",\"e\":\"The median nerve is lateral to the brachial artery proximally, and the median nerve crosses anteriorly over the brachial artery in the middle of the arm to become medial to the artery in the cubital fossa. The brachial artery does not cross over the nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The nutrient humeral artery has the most superior origin of any of its branches\",\"e\":\"The most superior branch of the brachial artery is the profunda brachii. The nutrient humeral artery (where present) arises inferior to it from the lateral aspect of the brachial artery.\\n\\nThe brachial artery begins at the inferior border of the teres major as the continuation of the axillary artery, and ends opposite the neck of the radius. During its initial course it runs with the median nerve, which crosses anterior to the artery in the middle of the arm. The brachial artery gives rise to many unnamed branches mainly on the lateral side, the named branches are the profunda brachii (the largest branch with the most superior origin), the nutrient humeral artery, and the ulnar collateral arteries.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":170,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old golfer presents to the orthopaedic clinic with a painful right shoulder on abduction. Following appropriate investigation, a diagnosis of rotator cuff syndrome is\\n\\nWhich of the following statements is true regarding the rotator cuff mechanism?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements is true regarding the rotator cuff mechanism?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Supraspinatus aids in adduction of the shoulder joint, supplied by the suprascapular\",\"e\":\"Supraspinatus initiates ABduction of the arm, not ADduction. It is indeed supplied by the suprascapular nerve (C4-C6), along with infraspinatus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Subscapularis arises from the medial 2/3 of the costal surface of the scapula and the intermuscular septa. It acts to stabilise the shoulder\",\"e\":\"Subscapularis (shown in green below in the image of the shoulder) arises from the subscapular fossa on the costal surface of the scapula and inserts to the lesser tubercle of the humerus (the only rotator cuff muscle to do so - the others insert on to the greater tubercle). It is innervated by the upper and lower subscapular nerves (C5. C6. C7). It acts to internally rotate and adduct the arm, and along with the other three rotator cuff muscies, stabilises the humerus in the glenoid cavity.\\n\\nSubscapularis\\n• supraspinatus Infraspinatus l Teres minor\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The deltoid muscle forms the lateral rotator cuff and forms the bulk of the lateral shoulder girdle\",\"e\":\"The deltoid is not one of the four rotator cuff muscles. It arises from the lateral third of the clavicle, acromion and scapular spine, and inserts on to the deltoid tuberosity of the humerus. It is innervated by the axillary nerve (hence deltoid wasting with an axillary nerve palsy)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The Long head of biceps brachii forms part of the rotator cuff mechanism\",\"e\":\"The long head of biceps travels up through the bicipital groove and through the glenohumeral joint, inserting on to the supraglenoid tubercle. It does not form part of the rotator cuff.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Teres major forms an important part of the superior rotator cutt as an adductor and medial rotator of the humerus\",\"e\":\"The teres major does not form part of the rotator cuff (be careful not to get confuse it with the teres minor, which does), It arises from the infero-lateral scapula below the teres minor, and inserts onto the medial aspect of the bicipital groove of the humerus. It is innervated by the lower subscapular nerve\\n\\nThe rotator cufft is the sheath of conjoint tendons that run over the top of the shoulder capsule and insert into the greater tuberosity of the humerus. The tendons that make up the rotator cuff are those of the short muscles of the shoulder - supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis. The cuff is covered anteriorly by the coracoacromial arch with the subacromial bursa in between. The main role of the cuff is to stabilise the humeral head within the glenoid. Deltoid, teres major and biceps brachii muscles play no role in the formation of the rotator cuff.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":173,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old man has been brought in to the Accident and Emergency Department following a road traffic accident. As part of advanced trauma life support (ATLS) management you have been asked to secure large-bore intravenous access Which one of the following is correct regarding the venous drainage of the upper limb?\",\"lead\":\"As part of advanced trauma life support (ATLS) management you have been asked to secure large-bore intravenous access Which one of the following is correct regarding the venous drainage of the upper limb?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The basilic vein is a deep structure throughout most of the upper limb and therefore not easily suited to venepuncture\",\"e\":\"The basilic vein arises on the ulnar aspect of the dorsum of the hand as a continuation of the dorsal venous network. It travels up the medial aspect of the arm and pierces the brachial fascial in the mid-arm to join with the venae comitantes of the brachial artery, forming the axillary vein. It is superficial throughout its course until it pierces the deep fascia and is therefore suitable for venepuncture\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The basilic vein is accompanied by the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm which can be damaged during venepuncture\",\"e\":\"The basilic vein is accompanied by the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm along its course, until the nerve branches into anterior and posterior divisions. The basilic vein is used as a landmark when identifying the nerve for anaesthesia. The lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm runs with the cephalic vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The basilic vein pierces the deep fascia in the arm halfway between the elbow and the axilla\",\"e\":\"The basilic vein is a continuation of the ulnar side of the dorsal venous arch in the hand. It lies medially to the biceps tendon in the cubital fossa and is medial to the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm in the arm it. It communicates with the cephalic vein via the median cubital vein. The basilic vein pierces the deep fascia of the arm (brachial fascia) approximately half way up the arm - this is illustrated nicely in the image below. It runs with the brachial artery for a short distance before joining with the venae comitantes of the brachial artery at the inferior border of teres major to form the axillary vein.\\n\\nCephalic vein\\n\\nBasilic Median vein cubital vein\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The cephalic vein joins the basilic vein at the elbow via the lateral cubital vein\",\"e\":\"The cephalic and basilic veins are usually connected at the anterior elbow by the median cubital vein (not the lateral cubital vein, which does not exist).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The cephalic vein joins the basilic vein to form the axillary vein at the lower border of teres major\",\"e\":\"The axillary vein is formed by the union of the basilic vein and the venae comitantes of the brachial artery at the inferior border of the teres major. The cephalic vein continues up the lateral aspect of the arm and along the delto-pectoral groove to pierce the clavipectoral fascia within the clavipectoral triangle, and joins with the axillary vein directly.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":176,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old man is referred to a shoulder clinic because of pain he has noted in his right shoulder while performing DIY at home. On examination he is noted to have pain on abduction between 60° and 120°, however the extremes of movement are pain-free. He is able to actively initiate abduction of his shoulder. Which of the following muscles initiate shoulder abduction?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following muscles initiate shoulder abduction?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Deltoid\",\"e\":\"Deltoid is a strong abductor of the arm, but does not initiate abduction - this role is performed by supraspinatus. The anterior aspect of deltoid acts as a flexor and internal rotator, and the posterior part as an extensor and external rotator. Deltoid is innervated by the axillary nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Infraspinatus\",\"e\":\"Infraspinatus is one of the four rotator cuff muscles, acting to externally rotate the arm, not abduct it.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Subscapularis\",\"e\":\"Subscapularis is one of the four rotator cuff muscles, inserting anteriorly on to the lesser tubercle of the humerus. It is therefore an internal rotator of the arm, not an abductor.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"supraspinatus\",\"e\":\"Supraspinatus (shown in red below) initiates abduction of the humerus at the glenohumeral joint (the first 10-15°). In supraspinatus tendon rupture, patients usually initiate abduction by leaning to the affected side to allow gravity to abduct the arm enough for deltoid to take over.\\n\\nSubscapularis Supraspinatus Infraspinatus Teres minor\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Teres minor\",\"e\":\"Teres minor is one of the four rotator cuff muscles, arising from the lateral scapula and inserting most inferiorly on to the greater tubercle. It is an external rotator of the humerus and not an abductor. It is innervated by the axillary nerve.\\n\\nIn painful-arc syndrome, there is pain on abduction between 60° and 120° (the middle third of the arc), but the extremes of movements are painless. The initial phase of abduction is lost in patients with a complete tear of the supraspinatus tendon (rotator cuff* tear). The patient is unable to initiate abduction of the shoulder because the supraspinatus causes the first 10- 15° of abduction followed by the deltoid which helps to 90° of abduction.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":179,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old falls from his motorbike at 70 mph. He has significant pain and bruising in the left posterior triangle of his neck. He cannot extend his left elbow or wrist. A magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan confirms an injury to the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. In addition to the radial nerve, which one of the following nerves is a branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus?\",\"lead\":\"In addition to the radial nerve, which one of the following nerves is a branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Axillary nerve\",\"e\":\"This is correct. The posterior cord of the brachial plexus is formed from the posterior divisions of all three trunks. It gives off the upper and lower subscapular nerves and the Thoracodorsal nerve, before terminating as the radial and axillary nerves. The axillary nerve winds around the surgical neck of the humerus (where it is vulnerable to damage in fractures), and supplies teres minor and deltoid. The medial cord of the plexus gives off the medial cutaneous nerve of the arm and forearm, medial pectoral nerve, and the medial part of the median nerve, before terminating as the ulnar nerve. The lateral cord gives off the lateral pectoral nerve, and the lateral part of the median nerve, before terminating as the musculocutaneous nerve. The brachial plexus is shown below in schematic with the minor branches removed.\\n\\nBranches Cords Divisions Trunks Brats\\n\\nMusculocutaneous Lateral Superior\\n\\nAxillary\\n\\nMiddle Median Posterior. -C7\\n\\nRadial\\n\\nUlnar Medial Inferior\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Median nerve\",\"e\":\"The median nerve is formed by a union of the medial and lateral cords of the brachial plexus; not the posterior cord\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Musculocutaneous nerve\",\"e\":\"The musculocutaneous nerve is the terminal branch of the lateral cord of the brachial plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Long thoracic nerve\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve is formed directly from the C5, C6 and C7 roots, not from the cords.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulnar nerve\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve is the terminal branch of the medial cord of the plexus.\\n\\nThe branches of the posterior cord are the upper subscapular, thoracodorsal nerve, lower subscapular, axillary and radial nerves. Through the axillary nerve, the posterior cord supplies the deltoid. The musculocutaneous nerve leaves the lateral cord to supply the coracobrachialis.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":182,\"stem\":\"A 18-year-old man is taken straight to theatre from the Emergency Department after sustaining an injury to his right wrist from a circular saw, as there is concern his hand has been devascularised. It is noted the radial artery has been transected, along with the median nerve and several flexor tendons.\\n\\nWhich one of the following muscles is most likely to be affected by a median nerve injury?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following muscles is most likely to be affected by a median nerve injury?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abductor Bolicts brevis (APE S\",\"e\":\"The abductor pollicis brevis is one of the three thenar muscies (the others being the opponens pollicis and flexor pollicis brevis - shown below in the image). These three muscles, along with the lateral (radial) two lumbricals, are supplied by the median nerve*. with all other intrinsic muscles of the hand being supplied by the ulnar nerve. The motor supply to the thenar eminence is via the recurrent motor branch of the median nerve, which is given off immediately after the nerve has passed through the carpal tunnel. Complete injury to the median nerve at the wrist would lead to paralysis of the above muscles. alongside loss of sensation over the palmar aspect of the first (radial) three and a half digits. and the dorsal tips of the same. Remember that the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve is given off proximal to the flexor retinaculum, and so palmar sensation might be spared if the injury is distal to that branch\\n\\nFlexor pollicis brevis\\n\\nAbductor pollicis brevis\\n\\nOpponens pollicis\\n\\nLUARRAMIEAILLOMV\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Abductor pollicis longus (APL)\",\"e\":\"Abductor pollicis longus is a posterior compartment muscle of the forearm and is therefore supplied by the radial nerve, via its posterior interosseous branch.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Adductor pollicis\",\"e\":\"Adductor pollicus is supplied by the ulnar nerve (via its deep motor branch), like all the intrinsic muscles of the hand with the exception of the Lumbricals 1 and 2, Opponens pollicis, Abductor pollicis brevis and Flexor pollicis brevis muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Dorsal interossei\",\"e\":\"All the palmar and dorsal interossei are supplied by the deep branch of the unar nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Palmar interossei\",\"e\":\"All the palmar and dorsal interossei are supplied by the deep branch of the unar nerve and are therefore unaffected with a median nerve lesion.\\n\\nThe median nerve is most commonly injured at the wrist. This leads to wasting of the thenar eminence especially abductor pollicis brevis. There is loss of sensation over the radial three and a half fingers and radial side of the palm (ie if the palmar cutaneous branch of the medial nerve is involved) but the only autonomous areas of median nerve supply are over the pulp pads of the index and middle fingers.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":185,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old woman is admitted after falling while on a night out. An X-ray shows an angulated midshaft humerus fracture and on examination she is noted to have a wrist drop and altered sensation over the ist web space dorsally Which one of the following nerves are in direct contact with the humerus?\",\"lead\":\"An X-ray shows an angulated midshaft humerus fracture and on examination she is noted to have a wrist drop and altered sensation over the ist web space dorsally Which one of the following nerves are in direct contact with the humerus?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Median nerve\",\"e\":\"The median nerve arises from the medial and lateral cords and travels with the brachial artery down to the cubital fossa (first lateral to it, then medial to it in the cubital fossa). It does not come in to direct contact with the humerus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Musculocutaneous nerve\",\"e\":\"The musculocutaneous nerve is the terminal branch of the lateral cord. It pierces the coracobrachialis, and runs between the biceps and brachialis before terminating as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm. It does not therefore come in to direct contact with the humerus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Posterior interosseous nerve\",\"e\":\"The posterior interosseous nerve is the deep branch of the radial nerve in the posterior forearm (the superficial branch being purely sensory) It arises as the radial nerve bifurcates in to its superficial branch and deep branch proximal to supinator. It goes on to pierce supinator and run in the plane between the superficial and deep extensors in the posterior compartment of the forearm. It does hot come in to contact with the humerus, although the radial nerve that forms it proximally does.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Radial nerve\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve arises as one of the two terminal branches of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus (the other being the axillary), and travels with the profunda brachii artery in the spiral groove of the humerus: It is therefore in direct contact with the humerus and is at risk in mid-shaft humeral fractures. This can be seen in the image below of the arm from the posterior aspect. The other nerves that are in direct contact with the humerus are: the axillary nerve at the level of the surgical neck (where it is also at risk from humeral neck fractures), and the ulnar nerve, posterior to the medial epicondyle of the humerus where it travels between the two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU) in the cubital tunnel.\\n\\nRadial nerve (in the radial groove)\\n\\nDeep branch of the radial nerve\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superficial radial nerve\",\"e\":\"The superficial branch of the radial nerve arises proximal to supinator and travels beneath brachioradialis to supply sensation to the radial dorsum of the hand (tested in the first dorsal weo space).\\n\\nThere are three nerves that come into close contact with the humerus: the radial nerve contacts the humerus in the spiral groove; the axillary at the surgical neck; and the ulnar at the medial epicondyle.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":188,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old man falls in a bar under the influence of alcohol and sustains a deep laceration over his medial epicondyle. There is no fracture on X-ray, but it is noted he has altered sensation over the ulnar border of his hand and in theatre it is found that the ulnar nerve has been transected. Which one of the following will be seen after complete transection of the ulnar nerve at the elbow?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following will be seen after complete transection of the ulnar nerve at the elbow?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Claw hand\",\"e\":\"In the forearm, the ulnar nerve supplies flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU) and the ulnar half of FDP (to the little and ring fingers), whereas in the hand it supplies all the intrinsic muscles with the exception of the lumbricals 1 and 2, opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis and flexor pollicis brevis muscles*. When the unar nerve is transected at the wrist, power is lost to the lumbricals of the little and ring finger, as well as the other intrinsic muscles of the hand leading to unopposed extension at those MCPJs (remember the lumbricals flex the MCPJS and extend the IPJs). If the lesion is at the level of the wrist, the power to the ulnar half of FDP is preserved, and the IPJs are therefore flexed, leading to the claw hand appearance of extended MCPJs and flexed IPJs in the ring and little fingers. If, as in this case, the nerve is transected at the elbow, power to the ulnar half of FDP is also lost, leading to less flexion at the IPJs of the ring and little fingers and therefore a less deformed appearance, but a less functional hand - the ulnar paradox.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Loss of abduction of the thumb\",\"e\":\"Thumb abduction occurs through the action of abductor pollicis longus (radial nerve, posterior interosseous branch), and abductor pollicis brevis (median nerve). Uinar nerve injury does not therefore affect thumb abduction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Loss of adduction of the fingers\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve supplies all the dorsal and palmar interossei in the hand, via its deep motor branch. Loss of the dorsal interossei leads to loss of finger abduction, and loss of palmar interossei leads to loss of adduction.\\n\\nb) Palmar Interossei a) Dorsal Interossei\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Loss of flexion of the 5th proximal interphalangeal joint\",\"e\":\"Flexion at the proximal interphalangeal joint (PIPJ) of the fingers occurs through the action of flexor digitorum superficialis, which is innervated entirely by the median nerve and inserts to either side of the middle phalanx of the fingers. The flexor digitorum profundus inserts to the base of the distal phalanx, and therefore causes flexion at the distal interphalangeal joint (DIPJ). It is innervated by the median nerve on its radial half (index and middle fingers), and the ulnar nerve on its medial half (ring and little fingers). Loss of the unar nerve at the elbow would mean the loss of power in FDP to the little and ring fingers, but FDS would remain intact and therefore flexion at the proximal interphalangeal joint (PIPJ) would also remain intact.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wrist drop\",\"e\":\"A wrist drop occurs with injury to the radial nerve (which supplies all the extensors of the fingers and wrist), not the ulnar nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":192,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man who has fallen whilst rock climbing is brought in with pain, bruising and swelling to his left arm. An X-ray confirms an angulated midshaft humerus fracture, and there is concern that the radial nerve could be injured. Injury to the radial nerve in the radial (spiral) groove of the humerus causes loss of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Injury to the radial nerve in the radial (spiral) groove of the humerus causes loss of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abduction of the thumb\",\"e\":\"Although the radial nerve innervates abductor pollicis longus, the abductor pollicis brevis is innervated by the median nerve and therefore thumb abduction is weakened but not lost with a radial nerve injury.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Elbow extension\",\"e\":\"Elbow extension is mediated by the triceps, which is innervated by the radial nerve. However, the branches to the long and medial heads of triceps arise high in the arm (proximal to the spiral groove), and therefore the triceps is not completely paralysed with a mid-shaft humeral fracture. The branch to the lateral head of triceps arises within the spiral groove.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pronation\",\"e\":\"Pronation occurs largely through the action of the pronator teres, which is innervated by the median nerve, and pronator quadratus, and also innervated by the median nerve via its anterior interosseous branch.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Supination\",\"e\":\"Supination occurs chiefly through the action of two muscles: biceps brachii, (innervated by the musculocutaneous nerve) and supinator (which is innervated by the radial nerve. posterior interosseous branch). Although the supinator would be paralysed in this type of injury, supination would not be lost as the musculocutaneous nerve remains intact.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cutaneous sensation over the dorsal surface of the first web space\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve divides into the deep motor (posterior interosseous) and superficial sensory branches just proximally to the supinator at the lateral epicondyle. The superficial branch travels beneath the brachioradialis to supply sensation to the radial dorsum of the hand, tested at the first dorsal web space (shown in red in the image). Sensation here would therefore be lost with a radial nerve lesion in the arm.\\n\\nLower lateral cutaneous nerve of arm a Posterior cutaneous nerve of arm\\n• Posterior cutaneous nerve of forearm\\n• Superficial branch\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":195,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man presents having sustained a deep laceration over the first dorsal web space of his left hand with a circular saw while attempting DIY at home. On examination it is noted he is unable to extend his index finger at the interphalangeal joints.\\n\\nWhich one of the following muscles contributes to extension of the fingers at the interphalangeal joints?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following muscles contributes to extension of the fingers at the interphalangeal joints?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Extensor Carpi radialis brevis (ECRB)\",\"e\":\"ECRB arises from the lateral epicondyle of the humerus (common extensor origin) and inserts in to the base of the third metacarpal on its dorsal surface. It extends and abducts the hand at the wrist, and therefore does not contribute to finger extension. It is innervated by the posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Extensor Carpi radialis longus (ECRL)\",\"e\":\"ECRL arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus (higher than ECRB), and is therefore innervated by the radial nerve proper before it divides into its deep and superficial branches at the proximal aspect of supinator. It inserts into the dorsal aspect of the second metacarpal, extending and abducting the wrist. It does not contribute to finger extension.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Extensor hallucis longus (EHL)\",\"e\":\"Extensor hallucis longus is a muscle in the anterior compartment of the leg, innervated by the deep fibular nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lumbricals\",\"e\":\"The lumbricals arise from the FDR tendons, and insert into the lateral side of the extensor expansion of the fingers. The radial two are unipennate, and the medial two bipennate, as seen in the image. Their action is to flex the MCPJs and extend the IPJs in the fingers. They have a split innervation - the radial (lateral) two being innervated by the median nerve, and the ulnar (medial) two being innervated by the ulnar nerve. The lumbricals and interossei act together to flex the MCPJs and extend the IPJs of the fingers.\\n\\nIII (bipennate) (unipennate)\\n\\n(unipennate)\\n\\n(bipennate) eachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Opponens pollicis\",\"e\":\"The opponens pollicis is one of the musclest in the hand that are innervated by the median nerve. It acts to oppose the thumb, but does not contribute to finger extension.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":198,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old medical secretary has sustained a radial head fracture after falling while out jogging. Following surgical fixation of this she is noted to be unable to extend her fingers and it is thought the posterior interosseous nerve (PIN) has been injured.\\n\\nWhich one of the following descriptions best describes the route by which the PIN exits the antecubital fossa?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following descriptions best describes the route by which the PIN exits the antecubital fossa?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Between brachioradialis and brachialis\",\"e\":\"It s the radial nerve itself, not its posterior interosseous branch, that runs between brachioradialis and brachialis proximal to the lateral epicondyle. The radial nerve travels in the spiral groove of the humerus posteriorly from medial to lateral, and pierces the lateral intermuscular septum to become anterior to the lateral epicondyle at the elbow. It travels between brachioradialis and brachialis before branching in to its superficial and deep (posterior interosseous) branches proximal to supinator.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Between the two heads of Flexor Carpi Ulnaris (FCU)\",\"e\":\"It s the ulnar nerve, not the PIN, that enters the forearm posterior to the medial epicondyle between the two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Between the two heads of Pronator teres (PT)\",\"e\":\"It s the median nerve that enters the forearm between the two heads of pronator teres, not the posterior interosseous branch of the radial.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Between the two heads of the Supinator muscle\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve enters the forearm anterior to the lateral epicondyle between brachialis and brachioradialis. Proximal to supinator, it divides into the superficial branch (purely sensory). and the deep branch - the posterior interosseous nerve (PIN). The PIN travels between the two heads of supinator to reach the posterior compartment of the forearm, where it runs between the superficial and deep groups of extensor muscles, supplying them all. The boundaries of the antecubital fossa are:\\n• proximally - a line between the two epicondyles of the humerus\\n• laterally - brachioradialis and the other extensor muscles\\n• medially - pronator teres and the other flexor muscles\\n• roof - skin, subcutaneous fascia, bicipital aponeurosis and brachial/antebrachial fascia\\n• floor - brachialis and supinator.\\n\\nDeep branch of the radial nerve\\n\\nPosterior Interosseous Supinator nerve\\n\\nPosterior Interosseous artery Recurrent interosseous artery\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Between the median nerve and tendon of the biceps muscle\",\"e\":\"It s the brachial artery that lies between the biceps tendon (laterally) and the median nerve (medially).\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":201,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old man is brought in as a trauma call after falling from his motorbike onto his right shoulder. On examination of his right upper limb it is noted he cannot abduct his shoulder or flex his elbow. His forearm is held in a pronated position. He is noted to have an absent biceps tendon reflex.\\n\\nWhich one of the following parts of the brachial plexus mediate this reflex?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following parts of the brachial plexus mediate this reflex?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lower trunk\",\"e\":\"The lower trunk is comprised of the C8 and T1 roots: Damage to the lower trunk results in Klumpke's palsy, with or without a Horner's's syndrome. C8 and T1 contribute to both the ulnar and median nerves, and therefore power is lost to all the small muscles of the hand, including the lumbricals and interossei, which leads to hyperextension at the MOPJs and flexion at the IPJs. The biceps reflex is comprised of the C5 and C6 root values, and therefore is not lost with a lower trunk injury.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Medial cord\",\"e\":\"The medial cord is formed by the anterior division of the lower trunk, which contains C8 and T1. The biceps reflex is comprised of C5 and C6 root values, and therefore it is not lost with a medial cord injury.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Middle trunk\",\"e\":\"The middle trunk receives only the C7 root. The biceps reflex is comprised of c5 and C6 root values, and therefore it is not lost with a middle trunk injury.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior cord\",\"e\":\"The posterior cord receives the posterior divisions of all three trunks, and goes on to form the radial and axillary nerves as its terminal branches. The biceps reflex is mediated by the C5 and C6 roots which are carried by the upper trunk before being conveyed to the biceps muscle by the lateral cord and then the musculocutaneous nerve. Injury to the posterior cord would not therefore affect the biceps tendon reflex.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Upper trunk\",\"e\":\"The biceps reflex is mediated by the C5 and C6 roots, which travel via the upper trunk, then the lateral cord and the musculocutaneous nerve to reach the biceps muscle. Each C5 and C6 spinal nerve is formed from dorsal (sensory) and ventral (motor) roots, mediating the afferent and efferent parts of the reflex arc respectively. Injury to the upper trunk would therefore result in loss of the biceps reflex. Injury to the upper trunk or superior roots is otherwise known as Erb's palsy (or Erb-Duchenne's palsy). Here, the arm is held in the characteristic 'waiter's tip' position: adducted, internally rotated and pronated with an extended elbow. The brachial plexus is shown in schematic view below, and the upper trunk can be seen forming from the C5 and C6 roots,\\n\\nBranches Cords Divisions Trunks Prat: / C5 Musculocutaneous Lateral Superior\\n\\nAxillary\\n\\nPOSTeriOr Middle Median -C7\\n\\nRadial\\n\\nUlnar Medial Inferior\\n\\n© teachune anatomy.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":204,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old obese woman presents with intermittent episodes of altered sensation in her right hand which mostly occur at night. The altered sensation occurs in palmar aspect of the hand over the radial three and a half digits. She is thought to have a diagnosis of carpal tunnel syndrome.\\n\\nIn addition to the median nerve, the carpal tunnel also contains the flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS) tendons, flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) tendons, and which other?\",\"lead\":\"In addition to the median nerve, the carpal tunnel also contains the flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS) tendons, flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) tendons, and which other?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)\",\"e\":\"Abductor pollicis brevis is one of the thenar muscles, supplied by the median nerve. It arises from the flexor retinaculum and carpal bones, inserting on to the lateral aspect of the proximal phalanx of the thumb. Its tendon does not therefore pass through the carpal tunnel.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Abductor pollicis longus (APL)\",\"e\":\"The APL tendon forms the inferior, radial-most border of the anatomical snuffbox, along with the extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) tendon. It does not pass through the carpal tunnel.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Extensor pollicis longus\",\"e\":\"The extensor pollicis longus (EL) tendon forms the superior, unar most border of the anatomical snuffbox. It does not pass through the carpal tunnel.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Flexor pollicis longus\",\"e\":\"The carpal tunnel contains ten structures: 4 × FDP tendons, 4 x FDS tendons, the FPL tendon and the median nerve - these can be seen in the image below in section. As the median nerve passes through the tight tunnel, it is liable to compression. This manifests as paraesthesia over the palmar thumb, index, middle and radial half of the ring finger. Note the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve, which supplies sensation to the palm is given off proximally to the carpal tunnel and is therefore usually spared in carpal tunnel syndrome. Long-term compression of the median nerve in the carpal tunnel leads to wasting of the thenar muscles. The ulnar nerve and artery do not pass through the carpal tunnel. Instead they pass superficial to the flexor retinaculum on the ulnar palmar aspect of the wrist through Guyon's canal, between the pisiform bone and the hook of the hamate. The flexor retinaculum forms the roof of the carpal tunnel, and is attached to the tubercle of the scaphoid and crest of the trapezium on the radial side, and the hook of the hamate and pisiform bones on the ulnar side.\\n\\nFlexor retinaculum\\n\\nMedian nerve\\n\\n1 Flexor pollicis longus Flexor digitorum profundus teachmeanatomy\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pronator quadratus\",\"e\":\"The pronator quadratus lies over the distal interosseous membrane between the ulna and the radius. It is a broad flat muscle and therefore does not have a tendon in the same way as the long flexor muscles. it is innervated by the deep branch of the median nerve - the anterior interosseous nerve.\\n\\nThe pronator quadratus lies over the distal interosseous membrane between the ulna and the radius. It is a broad flat muscle and therefore does not have a tendon in the same way as the long flexor muscles. It is innervated by the deep branch of the median nerve - the anterior interosseous nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":60,\"p\":207,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man is seen in the clinic with a suspected scaphoid fracture. Your consultant quizzes you on the anatomy of the carpal bones Which one of the following lies in the distal carpal row?\",\"lead\":\"Your consultant quizzes you on the anatomy of the carpal bones Which one of the following lies in the distal carpal row?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hamate\",\"e\":\"The hamate is one of the four bones that form the distal row of carpal bones* - from radial (lateral) to ulnar (medial): the trapezium, trapezoid, capitate and hamate\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lunate\",\"e\":\"The lunate forms part of the proximal row of carpal bones, which are (from radial/lateral to ulnar/medial): scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pisiform\",\"e\":\"The pisiform forms part of the proximal row of carpal bones, which are (from radial/lateral to ulnar/medial): scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform. It is considered a sesamoid bone as it sits within the tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU) on the ulnar side of the wrist, where it can be palpated.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"1 Monoras\",\"e\":\"The scaphoid forms part of the proximal row of carpal bones, which are (from radial/lateral to ulnar/medial): scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform. The scaphoid is vulnerable to avascular necrosis of the proximal pole when fractured, as its blood supply is in a retrograde fashion from distal to proximal.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Triquetrum\",\"e\":\"The triquetrum forms part of the proximal row of carpal bones, which are (from radial/lateral to ulnar/medial): scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform.\\n\\nThe proximal row of carpal bones from lateral to medial are scaphoid, lunate, triquetral, and pisiform. The distal row of carpal bones from lateral to medial are trapezium, trapezoid capitate, and hamate.\"}]},{\"t\":61,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man is stabbed in the abdomen with a long knife from the front. He is hypotensive on arrival. Which part of the abdomen must be injured to result in inferior vena cava (IVC) trauma?\",\"lead\":\"Which part of the abdomen must be injured to result in inferior vena cava (IVC) trauma?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The central tendon of the diaphragm at T12 level\",\"e\":\"The central tendon of the diaphragm is an aponeurosis within the diaphragm. The descending aorta passes through the diaphragm at the level of T12. The inferior vena cava passes through this structure in the 'caval opening', however this is at the T8 level.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The diaphragm at T10 level\",\"e\":\"At the level of T10 the oesophagus passes through the diaphragm in the oesophageal hiatus:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The free edge of the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The lesser omentum's free edge forms the anterior boundary of the epiploic foramen (otherwise known as the mental foramen or foramen of Winslow). This foramen communicates between the lesser and greater sacs. The free edge of the lesser momentum folds over, and between these layers is found the common bile duct, hepatic artery and the portal vein - these structures would be at risk if the free edge of the lesser omentum was lacerated.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The left paravertebral space\",\"e\":\"The thoracic paravertebral space runs T1-T12 - its medial boundary is formed by the thoracic vertebrae and discs - at this point the vertebral foramina are also found with their corresponding nerve roots. posteriorly the ribs and transverse processes of the respective vertebrae as well as the superior costotransverse ligament, and anterolaterally the parietal pleural with the intercostal membranes a little more lateral to this. Within the space are intercostal vesseis, the sympathetic chain and spinal nerves. Clinically this allows for a paravertebral block; in penetrating trauma these same structures would, of course, be at risk.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The posterior wall of the epiploic foramen\",\"e\":\"The IVC commences opposite the L5 vertebra. It is on the right side of the aorta, upwards beyond the aortic opening of the diaphragm and extends to the central tendon of the diaphragm, which it pierces at the level of T8. The IVC lies behind the portal vein near the pancreas and bile duct, and forms the posterior wall of the epiploic foramen of Winslow. Apertures in the diaphragm:\\n\\nCome Enter the Abdomen:\\n• T8 inferior vena Cava (caval foramen)\\n• T10 oEsophagus (oesophageal hiatus)\\n• T12 Aorta (aortic hiatus).\\n\\nEach of the three holes have three structures passing through them:\\n• Caval foramen - Inferior vena cava, right phrenic nerve, lymph nodes\\n• oEsophageal hiatus - Oesophagus, vagal trunks, left gastric vessels\\n• Aortic hiatus - Aorta, thoracic duct, azygous vein\"}]},{\"t\":61,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"Knowledge of surface anatomy allows for the placement of optimal surgical incisions. The transpyloric plane is a key landmark At which one of the following sites is the transpyloric plane found?\",\"lead\":\"The transpyloric plane is a key landmark At which one of the following sites is the transpyloric plane found?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It crosses the right costal margin at the tip of the tenth costal cartilage, the surface\",\"e\":\"The transpyloric plane does indeed lie at the same level as the ga l-bladder fundus, however, the correct surface marking for this is the tip of the ninth rather than tenth costal cartilage.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Defines the level at which the coeliac axis leaves the aorta\",\"e\":\"The coeliac axis leaves the aorta at T12, this is not in alignment with the transpyloric plane.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"is the plane where the portal vein is formed\",\"e\":\"The transpyloric plane is a convenient way to relate anatomical structures. It is an imaginary transverse plane with a surface marking mid-way between the jugular notch and the pubic symphysis. The important anatomical relations of this site include - the origin of the superior mesenteric artery, the pancreatic neck, the first part of the duodenum, the pylorus, the renal hila, the duodenojejunal flexure, the gall-bladder fundus, the origin of the hepatic portal vein. the transverse mesocolon and the splenic hilum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lies at the level of the second lumbar vertebra\",\"e\":\"L2 is a surface marker for the pancreatic head and gall-bladder neck, these lie below the transpyloric plane.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lies mid-way between the manubrium sterni and the pubic symphysis\",\"e\":\"The correct surface marking for the transpyloric plane is the halfway between the jugular or suprasternal notch (rather than the manubrium sterni itself) and the pubic symphysis.\"}]},{\"t\":61,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 74-year-old man undergoes an emergency repair of a ruptured infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm. Two days later he develops signs of systemic sepsis and returns to theatre, where an infarcted section of ileum is excised\\n\\nThe origin of the superior mesenteric artery is at which vertebral level?\",\"lead\":\"The origin of the superior mesenteric artery is at which vertebral level?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1\",\"e\":\"L1 is the correct level for the origin of the superior mesenteric artery. This level also corresponds to the level of the transpyloric plane: this is an imaginary transverse plane with a surface marking mid-way between the suprasternal notch and the pubic symphysis. The important anatomical relations of this site include - the origin of the superior mesenteric artery, the pancreatic neck, the first part of the duodenum, the pylorus, the renal hila, the duodenojejunal flexure, the gall-bladder fundus, the origin of the hepatic portal vein and the right and left flexures of the colon.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"L2\",\"e\":\"L2 corresponds with the pancreatic head and ampulla of Vater. The superior mesenteric artery, however, originates at L1.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"TMH\",\"e\":\"This level corresponds with the upper liver and spleen.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"TiO\",\"e\":\"T10 is an important surface marker for the point at which the desophagus passes through the diaphragm and forms to oesophageal-gastric junction\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ti2\",\"e\":\"T12 is the site at which the aorta* passes through the diaphragm\"}]},{\"t\":61,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old man is undergoing an emergency open inguinal hernia repair for a strangulated inguinal hernia.\\n\\nRegarding the anatomy of the inguinal region, which of the following is correct regarding the mid-point of the inguinal ligament?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding the anatomy of the inguinal region, which of the following is correct regarding the mid-point of the inguinal ligament?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It forms the surface marking of the femoral pulse\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It lies at a point halfway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the symphysis pubis\",\"e\":\"As above, this surface anatomy describes the mid-inguinal point and is the point that the femoral artery can be best palpated. This point is also approximately one centimetre medial to the deep inguinal ring, the surface marking for which is the mid-point of the inguinal ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies proximal to the deep inguinal ring\",\"e\":\"The deep inguinal ring is the opening to the inguinal canal. The mid-point of the inguinal ligament lies at a point halfway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic tubercle, and is the surface landmark of the deep inguinal ring\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It lies closer to the midline on the left than the right\",\"e\":\"There is no anatomical difference between the left and right sides in this instance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It lies laterally to the mid-inguinal point\",\"e\":\"The mid-point of the inguinal ligament lies at a point halfway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic tubercle, and is the surface landmark of the deep inguinal ring. It Is distinct from and lies laterally to, the mid-inguinal point, which lies halfway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the symphysis pubis and is the surface marking of the femoral pulse. When considering the anatomy of the inguinal region it is vital to remember this distinction. This can often lead to confusion, but the two are independent structures.\\n\\nThe femoral pulse is best palpated at the mid-inguinal point, this is halfway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis\"}]},{\"t\":61,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"You admit an 84-year-old man to the acute surgical ward with a 24 hour history of colicky abdominal pain, abdominal distension and vomiting. On examination he has an irreducible lump in his right groin. Which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Direct hernias arise lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels\",\"e\":\"Direct hernias are a direction protrusion of abdominal viscera through the posterior wall of the inguinal canal. This normally occurs in 'Hesselbach's triangle', the borders of which are formed by the inferior epigastric artery laterally. the lateral border of the rectus sheath medially and the inguinal ligament inferiorly\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Indirect hernias arise medial to the inferior epigastric vessels\",\"e\":\"Indirect hernias are a protrusion of abdominal viscera through the inguinal canal via the deep inguinal ring, which is lateral to the inferior epigastric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Posterior border of the inguinal canal is the external oblique muscle\",\"e\":\"The posterior border is formed by the conjoint tendon and transversalis fascia. While the external oblique aponeurosis forms the anterior border and superior border, the muscle itself does not form any boundary.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The mid-inguinal point refers to the midpoint of the inguinal canal\",\"e\":\"The mid-inguinal point is the surface marking for the femoral artery pulse. It is half way between the symphysis pubis and the anterior superior iliac spine. It does not correspond to the mid-point of the inguinal ligament, which is found half way between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic tubercle.\\n\\nInguinal hernias are common, with a peak incidence in the 6th decade of life. The inguinal canal lies between the superficial and deep inguinal rings. In men, the inguinal canal contains the spermatic cord and ilioinguinal nerve. In women, it contains the round ligament and ilioinguinal nerve. The boundaries of the inguinal canal are as follows:\\n• Anterior border - external oblique aponeurosis\\n• Posterior border - transversalis fascia, conjoint tendon (medial third of canal)\\n• Inferior border - inguinal ligament\\n• Superior border - external oblique aponeurosis, musculoaponeurotic arches of internal oblique and transverse abdominal, transversalis fascia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The inferior border of the inguinal canal comprises the inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"The floor of the inguinal canal is comprised of the inguinal ligament. This ligament is a continuation of the external oblique aponeurosis and runs between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic tubercle\"}]},{\"t\":62,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man is brought to the Emergency Department after being assaulted. He has considerable facial swelling with reduced sensation in the distribution of his left infraorbital nerve. CT reveals nasal bones fractures on the ascending process of the maxilla, lacrimal bone and extending into the orbital rim. The orbital floor is otherwise intact.\\n\\nWhat is the likely injury?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely injury?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Base of skull fracture\",\"e\":\"A base of skull fracture typically involves the temporal bone, occipital bone, sphenoid bone or ethmoid bone. If suspected, a CT head scan is indicated. Clinical signs include Battle's sign, raccoon/panda eyes, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) rhino/otorrhoea, haemotympanum, and cranial nerve palsy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Le Fort I fracture\",\"e\":\"This is a horizontal maxillary fracture including the alveolar ridge, inferior maxillary sinus and lateral nasal fractures. It results in a 'floating palate' - it separates the teeth from the upper face\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Le Fort II fracture\",\"e\":\"This is a fracture through the posterior alveolar ridge, lateral maxillary sinus, inferior orbital rim and nasal bones. It results in a floating maxilla.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Le Fort III fracture\",\"e\":\"Le fort Ill injuries describe a complex fracture that involves the nasofrontal suture, orbital wall, zygomatic arch, zygomaticofrontal suture and maxilla-frontal suture: It results in a\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tripod fracture\",\"e\":\"A 'tripod' fracture often involves the orbital floor as well as the lateral wall of the maxillary sinus, and a zygomatic arch fracture. A fluid level in the maxillary sinus represents blood and often directs you to the area concerned\"}]},{\"t\":62,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man presents with significant facial trauma following a road traffic collision (RTC) with proptosis of the right eye. A computed tomography (CT) scan demonstrates a fracture across the superior orbital fissure with associated haematoma.\\n\\nWhich structure is most likely to be affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure is most likely to be affected?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The zygomatic nerve\",\"e\":\"This is a branch of the maxillary nerve (V2), it enters the orbit via the inferior orbital fissure carrying sensory fibres and parasympathetics.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The ophthalmic artery\",\"e\":\"This artery runs through the optic canal inferior laterally to the optic nerve, not through the superior orbital fissure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The zygomatic branch of the facial nerve\",\"e\":\"Not to be confused with the zygomatic nerve - this nerve runs across the zygomatic bone to the lateral angle of the orbit supplying motor control to the orbicularis oculi.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The optic nerve\",\"e\":\"The optic nerve passes out of the orbit through the optic canal, not the superior orbital fissure\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The frontal nerve\",\"e\":\"The superior orbital fissure lies at the apex of the orbit bounded by the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid bone. A number of structures* pass through from superior to inferior; lacrimal nerve (V1), frontal nerve (VT), superior ophthalmic vein, trochlear nerve, superior part of oculomotor nerve, nasociliary nerve (VT), inferior part of oculomotor nerve, abducens nerve, a branch of the inferior ophthalmic vein.\"}]},{\"t\":62,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man taking warfarin experiences a sudden onset of headache with visual disturbance. A computed tomography (CT) scan demonstrates a left occipital lobe intracerebral haematoma. What visual finding would you expect to find on examination?\",\"lead\":\"What visual finding would you expect to find on examination?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bitemporal hemianopia\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Central scotoma\",\"e\":\"A central scotoma describes visual loss corresponding with the point of fixation most commonly caused by a lesion between the optic chiasm and the retina.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left homonymous hemianopia\",\"e\":\"A left homonymous hemianopia would result from a right occipital lobe lesion/infarction.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A macular sparing left homonymous hemianopia\",\"e\":\"Occipital lobe stroke typically results in a homonymous hemianopia contralateral to the side of the lesion. Macular or central field sparing can occur if the occipital pole remains intact through dual blood supply from a branch of the middle cerebral artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right homonymous hemianopia\",\"e\":\"A left-sided occipital haemorrhage will result in a right-sided homonymous hemianopia. There may or may not be macular sparing, depending if the occipital pole is spared or not.\\n\\nBitemporal hemianopia is caused by a compressive lesion at the optic chiasma, typically either a pituitary adenoma or a craniopharyngioma.\"}]},{\"t\":62,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"You are reading a radiology report before the head and neck cancer MDT. A lesion is described as being on the 'True vocal cords' on an MRI of the neck.\\n\\nThe true vocal cords are:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abducted by the lateral cricoarytenoid muscles\",\"e\":\"The cords are adducted by the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle, not abducted.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Abducted by the posterior cricoarytenoid muscles\",\"e\":\"The true vocal folds have a stratified squamous epithelium, innervated by the recurrent laryngeal branch (CN X), and are formed by the vocal ligament (the free edge of the quadrangular membrane forms the false vocal cord). The cords are adducted by the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle, abducted by the posterior cricoarytenoid and tensed by tilting the thyroid cartilage downwards and forwards by contracting the cricothyroid muscle. All the laryngeal muscles are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve except for cricothyroid, which is supplied by the external laryngeal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Formed by the lower free edge of the quadrangular membranes\",\"e\":\"They are are formed by the vocal ligament (the free edge of the quadrangular membrane forms the false vocal cord).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Innervated by sensory fibres of the internal laryngeal nerves\",\"e\":\"Above the vocal cords. the larynx is sensorily innervated by the internal laryngeal nerve (CN\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lined by respiratory epithelium\",\"e\":\"The vocal cords are not lined by respiratory epithelium\"}]},{\"t\":63,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A male pelvis differs greatly from that of a woman. The chief reason for this is so that women undergo childbirth.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements concerning the pelvis is most correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements concerning the pelvis is most correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The male pelvis has a larger superior pelvic aperture than the female pelvis\",\"e\":\"The female pelvis has larger superior and inferior pelvic aperture compared with the male pelvis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The obturator foramina are round in the female\",\"e\":\"The obturator foramina are oval in females and round in males.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The male sacrum is less curved than that of the female\",\"e\":\"The male sacrum is more curved, while the female sacrum is less curved. This ensures a wider birth canal.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The ischial tuberosities are farther apart in the female pelvis\",\"e\":\"The ischial tuberosities are farther apart in the female pelvis because of a wider pubic arch.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The male pelvis is shallower than that of the female\",\"e\":\"The female pelvis is wider and shallower than the male pelvis.\\n\\nThe general structure of the male pelvis is heavier and thicker than the female pelvis and has more prominent bone markings. The female pelvis is wider, shallower, and has both a larger superior and inferior pelvic aperture. The ischial tuberosities are farther apart in the female pelvis because of the wider pubic arch, and the sacrum is less curved in the female pelvis. In addition to this the obturator foramina are round in the male and oval in the female. The adaptations to the female pelvis compared with the male largely serve to ensure a wide and safe birth canal.\"}]},{\"t\":63,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 37-year-old man presents with colicky right loin pain and is noted to have microscopic haematuria. A plain kidney-ureter-bladder radiograph reveals a dense opacity at the level of the peivic brim. Which structure lies posterior to the ureter at this point?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure lies posterior to the ureter at this point?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Appendix\",\"e\":\"The appendix is usually attached to the caecum and located in the right iliac fossa with a surface anatomical marker of McBurney's point - this is one-third of the way from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus. There is significant variability in the position/exact anatomy of the appendix, however, it is intraperitoneal while the ureters are retroperitoneal in their course.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bifurcation of the common iliac artery\",\"e\":\"The ureter descends towards the pelvis anterior to the psoas major muscle but posterior to the gonadal artery. It then crosses anterior to the bifurcation of the common illac artery at the level of the pelvic brim before descending posteroinferiorly along the lateral pelvic side\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Testicular artery\",\"e\":\"The testicular arteries arise from the aorta inferior to the renal arteries. Their initial course is retroperitoneal overlying psoas major. Eventually they cross obliquely anterior to the ureters to reach the inguinal ring.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vas deferens\",\"e\":\"Further in its course the ureter passes beneath the vas deferens or the uterine vessels (gender dependent).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vesicoureteric junction\",\"e\":\"The ureter enters the urinary bladder at the vesicoureteric junction, as the name implies. This occurs below the level of the pelvic brim.\"}]},{\"t\":63,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old male presents with macroscopic haematuria and is found to have a transitional cell carcinoma in the posterior wall of the bladder. Staging investigations suggest tumour invasion into a retrocystic structure. Which structure has been invaded?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure has been invaded?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior portion of prostate gland\",\"e\":\"The prostate gland is inferior to the bladder.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bulbospongiosus\",\"e\":\"The bulbospongiosus is not directly in contact with the bladder:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Corpus spongiosum\",\"e\":\"The corpus spongiosum is not directly in contact with the bladder.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Perineal body\",\"e\":\"The perineal body is a midline perineal structure that is inferior to the bladder.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Seminal vesicles\",\"e\":\"In males, the seminal vesicles lie in the rectovesical pouch, posterior to the posterior wall of the bladder. They are at risk of local invasion from posterior wall bladder tumours.\"}]},{\"t\":63,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old man presents with a unilateral testicular swelling and is diagnosed with a teratoma. He undergoes orchidectomy and makes a full recovery During his regular follow-up. a computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen and pelvis reveals lymph nodes of more than 1 cm in diameter.\\n\\nWhich lymph nodes are likely to be enlarged?\",\"lead\":\"Which lymph nodes are likely to be enlarged?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"External iliac lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"Lymph from the prostate and glans in men, and the cervix, upper vagina and clitoris in women drains here. The external iliac nodes also drain the fundus of the bladder, the inferior abdominal wall and the adductor region of the thigh\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inguinal lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The scrotum drains to the inguinal nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Internal illac lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"These receive lymphatic from the pelvic viscera and superior half of the rectum. They do not receive lymph drainage from the testis (or from the ovaries in women).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Para-aortic lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The testes develop in the embryological abdomen, and so lymphatic drainage follows the route of the gonadal vesseis to arrive at the para-aortic lymph nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sacral lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"These receive lymph drainage from the rectum and posterior wall of the pelvis.\"}]},{\"t\":64,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"During a difficult vaginal delivery, the senior house officer (SHO) on call was asked to come and make an episiotomy. She made a midline episiotomy and in doing so cut too far through the perineal body into the structure immediately posterior. Which one of the following structures did she cut?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures did she cut?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"External anal sphincter\",\"e\":\"An episiotomy is a perineal incision used in labour. The episiotomy has the effect of enlarging the distal birth canal - often the purpose is to prevent trauma to and damage of the perineal structures in breech or forceps delivery, in which there is a risk of this occurring. A midline episiotomy is made from the posterior vagina through the perineal body with a risk of damage to the external anal sphincter if extended too far. The rectum can also be damaged with this procedure. Most episiotomies are, however, posterolateral rather than midline - this procedure is safer and avoids the risk of damage to the external anal sphincter and rectum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bulbospongiosus muscle Ni-\",\"e\":\"The bulbospongiosus muscle, ischiocavernosus muscle and sphincter urethrae are anterior to the area that is cut during an episiotomy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ischiocavernosus muscle\",\"e\":\"The bulbospongiosus muscle, ischiocavernosus muscle and sphincter urethrae are anterior to the area that is cut during an episiotomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sphincter urethrae\",\"e\":\"The bulbospongiosus muscle, ischiocavernosus muscle and sphincter urethrae are anterior to the area that is cut during an episiotomy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sacrospinous ligament\",\"e\":\"The sacrospinous ligament extends from the sacrum to the ischial spine - it is deep to the perineum and should not be involved in this procedure.\"}]},{\"t\":64,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A young man developed a boil on his scrotum. He presents to the acute urology service and is started on intravenous antibiotics. Which one of the following lymph nodes are most likely to enlarge in this patient due to lymphatic spread of infection?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following lymph nodes are most likely to enlarge in this patient due to lymphatic spread of infection?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Internal iliac nodes\",\"e\":\"The internal iliac nodes drain the pelvis and gluteal region\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sacral nodes\",\"e\":\"The sacral nodes drain the prostate gland and posterior pelvic wall.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Superficial inguinal nodes\",\"e\":\"The perineum and the external genitalia, including the scrotum and labia majora, drain to the superficial inguinal lymph nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Para-aortic nodes\",\"e\":\"In men the testes drain to the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes, not the superficial inguinal lymph nodes. The lymphatic vessels for the testes travel in the spermatic cord and drain the testes into the para-aortic nodes. In women, the ovaries drain to these nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"External illac nodes\",\"e\":\"The external iliac nodes drain the lower limb.\"}]},{\"t\":64,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 38-year-old woman was seen in the outpatient clinic complaining of a boil located on her labia majora. She is admitted under gynaecology for incision and drainage of the lesion. Lymphatic spread of the infection would most likely enlarge which nodes?\",\"lead\":\"Lymphatic spread of the infection would most likely enlarge which nodes?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Para-aortic\",\"e\":\"The internal pelvic organs, including ovaries, drain to the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes. In men the testes drain to the para-aortic nodes, not the superficial inguinal lymph nodes. The lymphatic vessels for the testes travel in the spermatic cord and drain the testes into the lumbar nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sacral\",\"e\":\"The sacral nodes drain the prostate gland and posterior pelvic wall.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"External iliac\",\"e\":\"The external iliac nodes drain the lower limb.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superficial inguinal\",\"e\":\"The perineum and the external genitalia, including the scrotum and labia majora, drain to the superficial inguinal lymph nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Internal iliac\",\"e\":\"The internal iliac nodes drain the pelvis and gluteal region. =791\"}]},{\"t\":64,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"In a Urology multidisciplinary team (MDT) meeting, a pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of a 45-year-old man with HIV. presenting with acute or chronic urinary retention is discussed. It shows an irregular mass encasing the urethra within the deep perineal pouch\\n\\nIn relation to the deep perineal pouch, which is the most likely structure/tissue that the mass will invade?\",\"lead\":\"In relation to the deep perineal pouch, which is the most likely structure/tissue that the mass will invade?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The bulbourethral glands\",\"e\":\"In men, the deep perineal pouch contains the membranous urethra, sphincter urethra, bulbourethral glands, deep transverse perineal muscles, internal pudendal vessels and dorsal nerves of the penis. The identified mass must therefore be within the membranous urethra, and of the available options the bulbourethral glands are the most likely structures to be invaded as they lie on each side of the membranous urethra.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The internal urethral sphiricter\",\"e\":\"The internal urethral sphincter is located at the inferior pole of the urinary bladder, not within the deep perineal pouch.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The penile urethra\",\"e\":\"The membranous urethra runs within the deep perineal pouch, the penile urethra runs within the penis. The bulb of the penis itself lies in the superficial perineal pouch.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The pudendal nerve\",\"e\":\"The pudendal nerve provides sensation to the perineum and external genitalia - it runs in the pudendal canal, not the deep perineal pouch.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The superficial perineal fascia\",\"e\":\"The superficial perineal fascia (Colles' fascia) forms the inferior boundary for the superficial perineal pouch. 1395\"}]},{\"t\":65,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old woman presents to the A&E department complaining of isolated neck pain, without neurology, following a fall from standing. A lateral X-ray of the neck reveals a retropharyngeal haematoma. The doctor in A&E suspects an odontoid peg fracture. What is the next most appropriate investigation?\",\"lead\":\"What is the next most appropriate investigation?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A computed tomography (CT) scan of the cervical spine\",\"e\":\"In current practice a CT scan is the gold standard. Many hospitals include C-spine CT as standard with any head CT request, particularly in elderly patients. The odontoid peg (dens) is an upward projection from the axis (C2). The peg has an articular facet at its front and forms part of a joint with the anterior arch of the atlas. The atlanto-axial joint (C1-C2) allows rotation. It is a non-weight-bearing joint. It is supported by a number of ligaments - the alar ligaments (from the side of the peg to the occipital condyle), the apical ligament (from the tip of C2 to the anterior margin of the foramen magnum), and the transverse ligament (which arches across the atlas to maintain the position of the peg).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"ACT scan of the whole spine\",\"e\":\"Given the low energy mechanism of injury and in the absence of other symptoms, there is no requirement to image the whole spine and this would expose the patient to unnecessary radiation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan of the neck\",\"e\":\"MRI is used to look for ligamentous injury, and also for cord abnormalities, and could be considered after a CT scan.\\n\\nThis is the standard imaging technique used in plain imaging to look for possible peg fractures. However, with significant clinical/radiological concern, a CT scan is the investigation of choice. In a number of units, especially in the elderly, due to the availability of CT scanning, this has largely replaced plain radiographs as the initial diagnostic tool.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An 'open mouth' view X-ray\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"No further imaging is required, a retropharyngeal haematoma is diagnostic of an odontoid peg fracture\",\"e\":\"A retropharyngeal haematoma is not specific for a PEG fracture and can occur in trauma to the neck.\"}]},{\"t\":65,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man is rushed into the resuscitation unit after being stabbed in the back while on a night out. The injury is shown on MRi to involve a right hemisection of his spinal cord. If the spinothalamic tract is transected in the injury, what deficit will it cause below the lesion?\",\"lead\":\"If the spinothalamic tract is transected in the injury, what deficit will it cause below the lesion?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Loss of fine touch and proprioception on the right side\",\"e\":\"Transection of the dorsal columns on the right-hand side would cause this loss, but the question asks specifically about the spinothalamic tract.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Loss of fine touch and proprioception on the left side\",\"e\":\"Dorsal columns carry ipsilateral fine touch and proprioception, a left-sided injury would therefore be required for this loss.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Loss of movement on the right side\",\"e\":\"Motor pathways are ipsilateral, carried in the lateral motor column of the spine. However, this question asks specifically about the spinothalamic tract.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Loss of pain and temperature on the right side\",\"e\":\"Pain and temperature are carried in the spinothalamic tract, these tracts carry contralateral sensation - a left-sided injury would be required to cause this injury.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Loss of pain and temperature on the left side\",\"e\":\"Pain and temperature are carried in the spinothalamic tract; these tracts carry contralateral sensation - a right-sided injury to the spinothalamic tract would therefore cause this sensory loss. The spinothalamic tract conveys pain, temperature, crude touch and firm pressure sensations from one side of the body to the opposite side of the brain. The first neurone of the spinothalamic tract synapses in the posterior horn; the next neurone crosses to the contralateral side of the spinal cord and synapse in the thalamus, after ascending through the cord and brainstem; the third neurone arises in the thalamus to pass to the cortex. Axons from the cervical region synapse medially while axons from the lumbar region synapse laterally. As the fibres decussate in the spinal cord, a lesion of the spinothalamic tract at the level of the spinal cord would lead to loss of pain sensations on the contralateral side, beginning one level below the level of the lesion.\"}]},{\"t\":65,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old builder presents with back and severe left-leg pain radiating to his foot after lifting a heavy box. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) demonstrates an L5/S1 paracentral intervertebral disc bulge. With these observations and findings, which one of the following is most likely to be correct?\",\"lead\":\"With these observations and findings, which one of the following is most likely to be correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"An absent ankle jerk means that the disc should be removed\",\"e\":\"An absent ankle jerk implies some nerve root involvement, but on its own does not demand surgical intervention.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Numbness on the sole of the foot may be found on examination\",\"e\":\"Paracentral intervertebral disc bulges usually impinge the transiting root. In this case it would be the S1 root, which can result in plantar flexion weakness, numbness on the sole of the foot in the S1 dermatome and an absent ankle jerk reflex.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pain radiating to the buttocks would be due to nerve root involvement\",\"e\":\"Nerve root involvement is suggested by pain radiating below the knee. It is worse when the nerve is stretched, as with the sciatic stretch test, or when intra-abdominal pressure is raised, as with straining at stool, coughing or sneezing.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The exiting, rather than the transiting, nerve root is most likely to be compressed at this level\",\"e\":\"Paracentral disc bulges are the most common in the lumbar region and usually impinge on the transiting rather than the exiting nerve root. In this instance the S1 root would most likely be affected.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urinary retention would never be secondary to pain and bedrest\",\"e\":\"Urinary retention is a worrying sign that can be consistent with cauda equina syndrome but is not infrequently secondary to pain, analgesic medication and bedrest. Before this can be assumed, a full neurological examination must be performed, and if there are concerns regarding a cauda equina syndrome then further investigations (eg lumbar-sacral MRI) must be carried out.\"}]},{\"t\":65,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old man who had suffered long-standing back pain presents to the Emergency Department with a sudden deterioration and pain radiating to the lateral malleolus. He is referred to the spinal surgeons. What is the most likely cause of these symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of these symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A prolapsed intervertebral disc at the L4/5 interspace\",\"e\":\"A prolapsed L4-L5 disc presses on the L5 spinal nerve. The L5 dermatome lies over the anterior shin and dorsum of the foot.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A prolapsed intervertebral disc at the L5/S1 interspace\",\"e\":\"A prolapsed L5-S1 disc presses on the S1 spinal nerve (the L5 nerve passes above the prolapsed disc in the intervertebral foramen and so escapes damage). The S1 dermatome lies over the lateral malleolus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A prolapsed intervertebral disc compressing the cauda equina\",\"e\":\"The symptoms of cauda equina syndrome are classically urinary retention followed by overflow incontinence, bowel incontinence, bilateral sciatica and saddle anaesthesia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A prolapsed intervertebral disc compressing the sacral segments of the cord\",\"e\":\"The cauda equina starts at the level of vertebra L1/2 as the spinal cord terminates. The symptoms in this patient are consistent with S1 root compression (the S1 dermatome overlies the lateral malleolus). At the likely level of prolapse, the canal contains the cauda equina and not the spinal cord.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Collapse of a vertebral body secondary to osteoporosis with subsequent compression of the spinal canal\",\"e\":\"Osteoporosis can cause painful collapse of vertebral bodies but rarely causes neurological symptoms and most often affects older women.\"}]},{\"t\":66,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman with history of high-grade fever, gradual-onset loss of consciousness and a petechial rash is suspected of having bacterial meningitis. As part of the diagnostic procedure, a lumbar puncture is to be performed. To avoid injury to the spinal cord and nerves you must insert the spinal needle just below the spine of the fourth lumbar vertebra.\\n\\nWhich anatomical landmark would you use to identify the spine of the fourth lumbar vertebra?\",\"lead\":\"Which anatomical landmark would you use to identify the spine of the fourth lumbar vertebra?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1'5. Pubic symphysis\",\"e\":\"This lies at the midline of the perineum, and is below the level of the lumbar spine.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"lac crest\",\"e\":\"The anatomical landmark used to identify L4 is the top of the iliac crest. The line connecting the top of the two iliac crests, the supracristal line, passes through the spinous process of the L4 vertebra. Therefore, by finding the tops of the iliac crests, you should be able to identify L4.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Xiphoid process\",\"e\":\"This is at the level of T9.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"iliac tuberosity\",\"e\":\"The iliac tubercle is on the iliac crest but inferior to the supracristal line and lies at the level of L5.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Umbilicus\",\"e\":\"The umbilicus lies at L3/4.\\n\\nThe fourth lumbar vertebra (L4) is a relatively safe level for performing a lumbar puncture. Since the conus medullaris is at the inferior border of L1 or the superior border of L2, it should be safe to insert a needle at L4\"}]},{\"t\":67,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 2-month-old baby is admitted with recurrent episodes of projectile vomiting. The mother states that the vomit reaches several metres distance. The child is listless and dehydrated The Paediatric Registrar suspects pyloric stenosis and has taken an arterial blood gas.\\n\\nWhich acid-base disturbance listed below would you classically find associated with this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which acid-base disturbance listed below would you classically find associated with this condition?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Low serum p(CO2)\",\"e\":\"Respiratory compensation will attempt to correct for metabolic alkalosis by hypoventilation leading to an increase in serum p(CO2).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperchloraemia\",\"e\":\"Gastric juice is high in hydrochloric acid leading to loss of Cl-in this condition.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemia\",\"e\":\"Gastric juice is also high in potassium, containing 10 mmol/l. In addition to this, secondary hyperaldosteronism will occur due to hypovolaemia leading to conservation of Na+ at the expense of K+, further depleting serum potassium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"Gastric juice is high in hydrochloric acid leading to loss of H+ (and therefore alkalosis) in this\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Metabolic alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Gastric juice is high in hydrochloric acid leading to loss of H+ (and therefore alkalosis) in this condition\"}]},{\"t\":67,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 3-week-old baby exhibits projectile vomiting shortly after feeding and failure to thrive. On examination an olive-shaped mass is palpable in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen. A clinical diagnosis of late pyloric stenosis is made. What biochemical laboratory features would support the diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What biochemical laboratory features would support the diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemia, metabolic alkalosis, low urinary pH\",\"e\":\"Gastric juice is high in hydrochloric acid and potassium. Severe vomiting in this context leads to loss of H+ (and therefore alkalosis, loss of chloride ions and hypokalaemia The excess loss of Cl depletes extracellular chloride and. with the luminal loss of hydrogen ions, the net result is metabolic alkalosis. The kidney then tries to maintain blood pH by excreting alkaline urine. HCO3 is excreted with Nat and K+ until the overall volume deficit triggers an expansion of the extracellular volume rather than maintenance of pH. Na+ is reabsorbed, but K+ is lost via an aldosterone mediated mechanism and this leads to excretion of Hions, thus resulting in a 'paradoxical aciduria' in the alkalotic patient.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemia, metabolic acidosis, high urinary pH\",\"e\":\"Severe vomiting leads to loss of K+ and H+, and therefore alkalosis and hypokalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemia, metabolic acidosis, high urinary pH\",\"e\":\"Severe vomiting in this context leads to loss of Hr (and therefore alkalosis).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemia, metabolic alkalosis, low urinary pH\",\"e\":\"Severe vomiting of gastric contents results in hypokalaemia\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemia, metabolic alkalosis, high urinary pH\",\"e\":\"Dehydration stimulates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. It appears that the defence of extracellular fluid volume takes precedence over acid-base homeostasis and further sodium reabsorption in the kidney occurs in exchange for hydrogen ions (perpetuating the alkalosis) and potassium ions (leading to further potassium depletion) to maintain intravascular volume. This explains the apparently paradoxical finding of acidic urine in patients with pyloric stenosis.\"}]},{\"t\":67,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 5-week-old boy is brought to the Emergency Department by his mother with projectile vomiting. Following investigation a diagnosis of pyloric stenosis is made. What biochemical abnormality would likely be seen on the admission blood tests?\",\"lead\":\"What biochemical abnormality would likely be seen on the admission blood tests?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemic metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"Gastric juice is high in hydrochloric acid and potassium. Severe vomiting in this context leads to loss of H+ (and therefore alkalosis) and hypokalaemia\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemic metabolic alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Gastric juice is high in potassium and severe vomiting results in hypokalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemic metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"Loss of hypochloric acid results in a metabolic alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Gastric juice is high in hydrochloric acid and potassium. Severe vomiting in this context leads to loss of Ht (and therefore alkalosis) and hypokalaemia. Secondary hyperaldosteronism will occur due to hypovolaemia leading to conservation of Na+ at the expense of K-, further depleting serum potassium. The severe vomiting caused by pyloric stenosis classically causes a hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis. It is important this physiological derangement is corrected before surgery as this is known to improve outcomes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hyponatraemia and hypercalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Secondary hyperaldosteronism will occur due to hypovolaemia leading to conservation of Na+. In the early stages, hyponatraemia can occur due to loss of Na in vomitus, but sodium is typically normal or raised. Hypercalcaemia is not a feature in this context.\"}]},{\"t\":67,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 6-week-old boy is admitted to the surgical unit with a suspected pyloric stenosis. He has been vomiting profusely and appears clinically hypovolaemic.\\n\\nWhat is the likely biochemical picture?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely biochemical picture?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Metabolic acidosis, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia\",\"e\":\"Pyloric stenosis effectively causes a progressive post-gastric obstruction. This results in the gastric contents being vomited; with the child losing water as well as electrolytes, metabolic alkalosis occurs.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Metabolic alkalosis, hyperkalaemia, hyponatraemia\",\"e\":\"The child is often dehydrated and has low levels of potassium.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Metabolic acidosis, hyperkalaemia, hypernatraemia\",\"e\":\"The condition causes dehydration with loss of both sodium and potassium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Metabolic alkalosis, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia\",\"e\":\"Stomach secretions contain high concentrations of hydrochloric acid, loss of which causes patients to have a hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis, along with wasting of other key electrolytes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Normal blood pH, hyperkalaemia, hypernatraemia\",\"e\":\"Although a child may present early and have little biochemical disturbance, it is unlikely that he or she will have excess potassium and sodium.\"}]},{\"t\":67,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A 10-year-old girl is admitted with an acute abdomen. She is peripherally cool and tachycardic with a normal blood pressure. You decide to administer intravenous (IV) fluids. What is the most appropriate regimen?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate regimen?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hartmann's solution 20 ml/kg centrally, then reassess\",\"e\":\"Although the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidelines do suggest 20 ml/kg as a sensible volume for a fluid challenge in a patient who is unwell, this should not be given centrally unless there is already central access. Multiluminal central lines have significant complications and flow rates are lower through them than through wide-bore peripheral cannulas.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hartmann's solution 20 ml/kg peripherally, then reassess\",\"e\":\"The NICE (National Institute for Health and Care Excellence) suggests 20 ml/kg of a solution containing 131-154 mmol/l of sodium over 10 min through an IV route. The safest way of giving IV fluids in this instance is peripherally. If a child needs further multiple boluses then expert help should be sought.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Colloid solution 20 ml/kg peripherally, then reassess\",\"e\":\"Tetrastarches and colloids should ideally be avoided for multiple reasons in children. including their lack of electrolytes and risk of allergy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hartmann's solution 50 ml/kg peripherally, then reassess\",\"e\":\"Although after an initial bolus the child may need further fluids, 50 ml/kg would be an unnecessarily large volume in the first instance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Any easily available fluid 20ml/kg orally, then reassess\",\"e\":\"In a patient for whom surgery may be planned, oral intake is not sensible because it may delay safe anaesthesia by 2 hours. Moreover, its movement into the intravascular space is delayed. It is, however, important to remember that patients should be kept nil by mouth for only an appropriate and limited time, unless there is a clear indication:\"}]},{\"t\":67,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A neonate has undergone an emergency laparotomy for bowel obstruction caused by a congenital stenosis within the jejunum. She is being closely monitored post-operatively on the neonatal intensive care unit.\\n\\nWhat would be a normal urine output for her?\",\"lead\":\"What would be a normal urine output for her?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"0.3 ml/kg/h\",\"e\":\"This is much too low for a neonate, child or adult\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"0.5 ml/kg/h\",\"e\":\"0.5 ml/kg is a normal urine output for an adult.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"1.ml/kg/h\",\"e\":\"In children over 2 years old, urine output should be >1 ml/kg/h\\n\\nThis would be an acceptable urine output for a child over the age of two years, when the aim is >1 ml/kg/h. For children under the age of 2 years, it should be >2 ml/kg/h.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"15m/kg/.2\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"2 ml/kg/h\",\"e\":\"In children under the age of 2 years, a normal urine output should be >2 ml/kg/h.\"}]},{\"t\":67,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 7-year-old girl who weighs 22 kg is referred to the surgical team with abdominal pain. The girl's mother explains she has had profuse vomiting and diarrhoea for three days. On examination, she appears severely dehydrated and has a generally tender, non-peritonitic abdomen.\\n\\nWhen giving a fluid bolus to this patient, how much should be prescribed?\",\"lead\":\"When giving a fluid bolus to this patient, how much should be prescribed?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"R'S' 170 ml\",\"e\":\"Fluid bolus should be calculated as 20ml/kg. In this case: 20 × 22 = 440 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"250 ml\",\"e\":\"In an adult with a cardiac history or an elderly patient when giving a fluid bolus it is advised to give a smaller volume of 250 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"360 ml\",\"e\":\"This young girl is severely dehydrated and needs an immediate fluid bolus of 20 ml/kg. in her case 440 ml. She may require further boluses depending on her initial response.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"440 ml\",\"e\":\"In paediatrics, fluid boluses are 20 ml/kg. This child weighs 22 kg: 20 x 22 = 440 ml to be given STAT.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"500 ml\",\"e\":\"In a healthy young adult, a standard fluid bolus would be 500 ml. In children, fluid bolus should be calculated based on their weight.\"}]},{\"t\":67,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"An 8-year-old girl was a restrained passenger in the backseat of a car travelling at 70 miles per hour that has collided with the vehicle in front. She is brought into the Emergency Department as a trauma call. The emergency crew were unable to get intravenous access but have intraosseous access. Which one of these is correct regarding infusion through this modality?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is correct regarding infusion through this modality?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Intraosseous access can only be used if bone marrow is aspirated\",\"e\":\"Bone marrow aspiration can assist in determining that the intraosseous needle is correctly placed. However, if no bone marrow is aspirated, saline flushes easily and without evidence of swelling then it can be used.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intraosseous infusion is an acceptable option tor additional access in a patient with established vascular access\",\"e\":\"Intraosseous infusion should be limited to emergency situations oniy due to the risk of complications including infection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"if the child were to arrest and need adrenaline through the intraosseous access the dose normally given intravenously should be reduced\",\"e\":\"In a cardiac arrest the doses for intravenous and intraosseous access of adrenaline are the same\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Infusion should be discontinued if there is swelling in the soft tissue around the site\",\"e\":\"Swelling in the soft tissue around the intraosseous access would indicate it is not correctly positioned and therefore infusion should be stopped and new access obtained.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Intraosseous access is not useful in emergency resuscitation\",\"e\":\"Fluid and medications given through intraosseous access will reach the bloodstream quickly and without loss from absorption. In an emergency setting it may be the only available option and can be life-saving\"}]},{\"t\":67,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"An 8-year-old boy is awaiting surgery for an appendicectomy. You are asked to prescribe maintenance fluids and specifically the rate at which you want fluid to be delivered Which one of the following is the most appropriate fluid delivery rate?\",\"lead\":\"You are asked to prescribe maintenance fluids and specifically the rate at which you want fluid to be delivered Which one of the following is the most appropriate fluid delivery rate?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"58 ml/h\",\"e\":\"The weight must be estimated to calculate the volume of fluid to give: (age + 4) × 2 = 24 kg. The maintenance fluid rate for this patient is therefore: (100 × 10) + (50 × 10) + (20 × 4) = 1580 ml/24 h = 65.8 ml/h\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"60 ml/h\",\"e\":\"Maintenance fluid in children is given as 100 ml/kg for first 10 kg. 50 ml/kg for next 10 kg and 20ml/kg for further kg. Estimated weight can also be calculated for this patient: (age + 4) ×\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"72 ml/h\",\"e\":\"Weight must first be estimated as: (age + 4) × 2 = 24 kg. Then maintenance fluid would be 100 ml/kg for 10 kg, 50 ml/kg for next 10 kg then 20 ml/kg. For this patient 1580 ml over 24\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"74 ml/h\",\"e\":\"Maintenance fluid rate in children is determined by weight. Over 24 h 1500 ml for 20 kg and then an additional 20 ml/kg for every further kg. No weight has been given in this question and it must therefore be estimated: (age + 4) × 2 = 24 kg. Total fluid over 24 h would therefore be 1580 ml and an hourly rate can then be calculated.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"66 ml/h\",\"e\":\"Without a given weight it can be estimated using the formula: weight = (age + 4) x 2. For an 8-year-old the estimated weight is 24 kg. Over a 24-h period 100 ml/kg should be given for the first 10 kg, 50 ml/kg for the next 10 kg and 20ml/kg for the remaining. In this case (100 x 10) + (50 × 10; + (4 × 20) = 1580 ml over 24 h. Houriy rate of 65-66 ml.\"}]},{\"t\":68,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old girl fell off her scooter, sustaining a head injury. She was not wearing a helmet Her mum says she lost consciousness for 10 minutes and vomited several times. On arrival in the Emergency Department, she is drowsy and cannot remember the events preceding the accident. She also reports neck pain and is tender in the C5-C7 region. What is the most appropriate investigation in her management?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate investigation in her management?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Computed tomography (CT) head and cervical spine\",\"e\":\"Imaging is required in this case. The patient meets more than one of the indications stated in the NICE guidance for CT brain imaging within 1h. CT cervical spine imaging in conscious patients is requested only if the patient complains of pain (or-specific tenderness on examination) or there is an abnormality on plain C spine X-rays or restricted active movements.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"CT head\",\"e\":\"While a head CT is indicated to investigate for traumatic brain injury, CT cervical spine imaging is also required\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"No images required\",\"e\":\"Imaging is required in this case. The patient meets more than one of the indications stated in the NICE guidance for CT brain imaging within 1 h\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Skeletal survey\",\"e\":\"Skull X-rays and skeletal survey are considered if non-accidental injury (NAI) is suspected, but are not first-line in the investigation of traumatic brain injury.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Skull X-rays\",\"e\":\"Skull X-rays and skeletal survey are considered if non-accidental injury (NAI) is suspected, but are not first-line in the investigation of traumatic brain injury.\"}]},{\"t\":68,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"An 8-year-old boy undergoes a laparotomy after a small bowel perforation following a cycling accident. He is recovering in HDU post-operatively and initially is stable. Which of the following best describes the initial physiological response to surgery?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following best describes the initial physiological response to surgery?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Reduction in cardiac output, reduced oxygen consumption and reduced glucose tolerance\",\"e\":\"Cuthbertson's original description of the two phases of response to injury describes an initial slowing down (ebb), followed by speeding up (flow), of most of the metabolic pathways. The initial 'ebb' phase after either trauma or surgery lasts 2-3 days and consists of a slowing down of the intrinsic metabolic activity. The cardiac output reduces, basal metabolic rate slows and glucose tolerance decreases. The 'flow' phase that follows for anything from days to weeks after the initial 'ebb' reverses these processes. Muscle catabolism and a negative nitrogen balance will also occur during this second phase.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased cardiac output, increased oxygen consumption and increased glucose tolerance\",\"e\":\"Initially, cardiac output and oxygen consumption reduce. These pathways speed up again after the initial lebb' phase.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased cardiac output, reduced basal metabolic rate and increased glucose tolerance\",\"e\":\"The cardiac output reduces initially then speeds up after this initial slowing. It is correct that the basal metabolic rate decreases.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduction in cardiac output, increased basal metabolic rate and increased oxygen consumption\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output does indeed slow, along with most metabolic pathways. Oxygen consumption also reduces along with the metabolic rate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Static cardiac output, increased basal metabolic rate and reduced glucose tolerance\",\"e\":\"The initial response to surgery or trauma is a reduction in cardiac output along with a reduced basal metabolic rate and reduced glucose tolerance.\"}]},{\"t\":68,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"An 11-year-old boy presents with a short history of sudden onset left testicular pain and has a scrotal exploration. Which one of these is correct regarding the stress response in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is correct regarding the stress response in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Curlisol levels will be elevated 24 h post-operatively\",\"e\":\"In the paediatric population, stress hormones such as cortisol are elevated for a much shorter duration. Unlike adults, at 24 h these levels would have returned to their pre-operative level.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"There will be a decrease in glucagon levels\",\"e\":\"As part of the stress response there would be an increased secretion of stress hormones including glucagon in an effort to utilise protein, fat and carbohydrate for energy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"There will be an increase in catecholamine levels immediately post-operatively\",\"e\":\"Catecholamine levels are significantly elevated during surgery as part of the stress response and will remain so immediately post-operatively. Over the following approximately six hours these levels will return to normal in a paediatric patient.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"There will be a decrease in gluconeogenesis\",\"e\":\"As part of the stress response there is an increase in gluconeogenesis to provide energy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The intraoperative cortisol level will be lower than an adult undergoing the same procedure\",\"e\":\"Overall, children have much higher intraoperative levels of cortisol and catecholamines than adults. Unlike adults, these levels quickly return to the pre-operative level.\"}]},{\"t\":68,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 10-year-old girl requires assessment following an open appendicectomy. The nurse is concerned about her observations: respiratory rate 22/min, saturations 99% on air, heart rate 110/min, blood pressure 90/47 mmHg and temperature 37°C. The patient is comfortable, and is mildly tender around the operative site. What is the best next step in management of this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the best next step in management of this patient?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Reassure\",\"e\":\"For a child of 10 years old, all of the observations are within normal range. In comparison to adults, children have a higher normal respiratory rate and heart rate with a lower blood pressure. With normal observations and a review of the patient in keeping with a normal post-operative course it would be appropriate to reassure and to continue to observe.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Deliver high flow oxygen\",\"e\":\"High flow oxygen is not necessary in this case. She has normal saturations and respiratory rate and at present she has not developed sepsis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Start intravenous antibiotics\",\"e\":\"Some patients require further doses of intravenous antibiotics after an appendicectomy. a decision typically made at the time of surgery and in cases of a perforated appendix.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Start oral antibiotics\",\"e\":\"There is no evidence at present that this patient requires any antibiotics. All of the observations are within normal range and after uncomplicated appendicitis ongoing antibiotics are rarely required\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prepare to take the patient back to theatre\",\"e\":\"At present there is no indication this patient needs to return to theatre. The observations are within normal range for a child of 10 years and clinically there does not appear to be any concerning features:\"}]},{\"t\":68,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 2-year-old boy comes into the Emergency Department with bleeding after a circumcision performed in the community. Which one of these is a correct compensatory mechanism?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is a correct compensatory mechanism?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased aldosterone secretion\",\"e\":\"Increased aldosterone secretion is a compensatory mechanism. It causes retention of sodium and water to increase circulating volume.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) secretion\",\"e\":\"ACTH is secreted by the pituitary and acts on the adrenal cortex to stimulate secretion of aldosterone and cortisol. Aldosterone secretion will increase circulating volume by sodium and water retention.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decreased antidiuretic hormone (ADH) secretion\",\"e\":\"ADH is released by the posterior pituitary and causes increased water reabsorption in the distal tubule to increase circulating volume. ADH increases as a compensatory mechanism.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased catecholamine secretion\",\"e\":\"There will be increased catecholamine secretion causing vasoconstriction and tachycardia to maintain blood pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased capillary circulation\",\"e\":\"Catecholamine secretion causes vasoconstriction to maintain blood flow to vital organs and results in decreased capillary microcirculation.\"}]},{\"t\":68,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A 7-year-old child with generalised abdominal pain is referred to the surgical team and found to be severely dehydrated. In children, what is the cardiac output most dependent on?\",\"lead\":\"In children, what is the cardiac output most dependent on?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Stroke volume. C-\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output is a product of heart rate and stroke volume. Stroke volume is dependent on preload, myocardial contractility and afterload. Children have limited ability to alter these and heart rate is therefore the most important factor in determining cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Heart rate\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume. Unlike adults, children have a limited ability to increase their cardiac output by increasing stroke volume due to a higher proportion of fibrous tissue. Paediatric cardiac output is. therefore, significantly dependent on heart rate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Preload\",\"e\":\"Preload is one of the determinants of stroke volume, which in turn determines cardiac output. Preload is determined by cardiac compliance and filing pressure. Children have less cardiac compliance than adults due to the increased proportion of fibrous tissue and it is therefore not an important factor in increasing cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Myocardial contractility\",\"e\":\"Compared with adults, children have a higher proportion of fibrous tissue and the myocardium has an insignificant role in increasing cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Afterload\",\"e\":\"Stroke volume and heart rate are the two determinants of cardiac output. Stroke volume is dependent on preload, myocardial contractility and afterload. Afterload is equivalent to systemic vascular resistance. Children have a limited ability to alter their stroke volume and heart rate is the main determinant of cardiac output.\"}]},{\"t\":68,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 9-year-old boy who weighs 30 kg is having a circumcision. He has a brisk bleed during the procedure and loses approximately 200 ml of blood.\\n\\nNormal value\\n\\n7.35-7.45 pH\\n\\np= (02) 10.5-13:5 kPa\\n\\np (COz) 4.6-6.0 kPa\\n\\n24-30 mmol/l HCO:-\\n\\n0.5-2.2 mmol/i Lactate\\n\\nWhat blood gas findings would be most in keeping with this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What blood gas findings would be most in keeping with this patient?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"7.42, pa (02) 12 kPa, pa(CO2) 5.3 kPa, HCO; 28 mmol/l, lactate 1.4 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"Paediatric blood volume is approximately 80 ml/kg. This boy weighs 30 kg. Therefore, his blood volume will be approximately 2400 ml. 200 ml is <10% of his blood volume and therefore this is a relatively small bleed and, at this stage, the blood gas would be normal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"7.33, p,(O2) 10.9 kPa, p,(CO:) 4.8 kPa, HCO;- 20 mmol/l, lactate 2.5 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"This blood gas represents a metabolic acidosis. This will happen with significant blood loss as a result of anaerobic respiration, but would be a late sign. It would not be expected in this child who has had a bleed equating to <10% of his blood volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"7.37, pa(Oz) 11.1 kPa, Pa(CO2) 3.8 kPa, HCO3* 16 mmol/l, lactate 2.1 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"This blood gas picture is suggestive of a metabolic acidosis which has full respiratory compensation. It would not be expected in acute blood loss but may develop later.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"7.47, pa(02) 10.8 kPa, p,(CO2) 5.1 kPa, HCO; 33 mmol/l, lactate 0.9 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"This blood gas demonstrates a metabolic alkalosis, which is not a typical picture with blood loss that is more likely to result in metabolic acidosis. Causes of metabolic alkalosis include prolonged vomiting.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"7.31. P.(02) 11.6 kPa, Pa(CO2) 7.0 kPa, HCO; 26 mmol/l, lactate 1.6 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"This blood gas represents a respiratory acidosis. which typically occurs in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). With a massive acute blood loss, a metabolic acidosis picture would be expected. However, this patient has lost 200 ml of an estimated 2400 ml blood volume (80 ml/kg) and at this stage the blood gas is most likely to be normal\"}]},{\"t\":68,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 12-year-old boy has had a circumcision and is recovering in the day care unit. He needs to be assessed as the staff nurse is concerned that he is bleeding. What would be the earliest sign of massive blood loss in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What would be the earliest sign of massive blood loss in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased systolic blood pressure\",\"e\":\"Decreased blood pressure is a very late sign of massive blood loss both in the paediatric and adult population.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lactic acidosis:\",\"e\":\"Lactic acidosis will occur as a late sign of massive blood loss due to increasing anaerobic metabolism.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tachycardia\",\"e\":\"Blood loss will be detected by stretch receptors in the atria and arterial baroreceptors in the aorta and carotid artery. Increased catecholam ne release causes vasoconstriction and tachycardia one of the earliest signs of blood loss.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased urine output\",\"e\":\"Urine output in this 12-year-old boy should be >1 ml/kg/h. Decreased urine output is a late sign of hypovolaemic shock\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Fever\",\"e\":\"Fever is not associated with massive blood loss.\"}]},{\"t\":69,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 2-year-old boy is admitted for an inguinal hernia repair. He is due to receive his immunisations. Which one of the following is a relative contraindication to immunisation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a relative contraindication to immunisation?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anaphylaxis after a previous dose\",\"e\":\"This is an absolute contraindication. Anaphylaxis is a life-threatening condition and the relative risks of recurrence far outweigh the benefits of immunisation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Temperature >38.5 °C\",\"e\":\"It is sensible to delay an immunisation if the child has a fever. However, it is only a relative contraindication and exceptional circumstances may mean that immunisation is sensible.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A minor cough\",\"e\":\"Children have coughs on a regular basis and a minor cough in an otherwise well child is not a contraindication. If accompanied by a fever or respiratory distress, it would be sensible to delay it.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Egg allergy and measles, mumps and rubella (MMR)\",\"e\":\"Flu vaccine is egg based and should be avoided if anaphylaxis has occurred, but mild intolerances are not a reason to avoid an immunisation. MMR is not contraindicated in egg allergy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Immunodeficiency and live vaccines\",\"e\":\"Live vaccines depend on the host's immunity to work. If a child is immunosuppressed, then there is significant concern that the live vaccine could result in active disease. Therefore it is an absolute contraindication.\\n\\nAlthough immunisations are the mainstay of paediatricians and GPs, it is important to know when they should be avoided. Many children who have long hospital stays miss their normal immunisations unnecessarily and anxiety around allergies and immunisations is rife.\"}]},{\"t\":69,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 6-year-old girl weighing 25 kg is admitted with vague abdominal pain, which moves to the right iliac fossa. She is febrile at 39.5 °C and had evidence of peritonitis. A diagnosis of appendicitis is made and she is scheduled for theatre\\n\\nWhat would be an appropriate antibiotic plan?\",\"lead\":\"What would be an appropriate antibiotic plan?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hold antibiotics until a definitive diagnosis is made in surgery\",\"e\":\"Although historically some clinicians have been reluctant to give antibiotics early in the context of abdominal pain in children, this patient clearly has intra-abdominal sepsis, and the most likely diagnosis of appendicitis has already been made. Any delay in treatment with antibiotics will only increase the risk to the patient.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Give routine prophylactic antibiotics on induction as per local guidelines\",\"e\":\"In some instances of a very unclear diagnosis of appendicitis, this could be considered because the risks of administering antibiotics blindly may expose a patient to risk. Antibiotics on induction, part of the World Health Organization (WHO) checklist, has been shown to improve outcomes. In this case, she is clearly unwell with abdominal sepsis so delay in treatment with antibiotics will worsen her probable outcome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Normal adult dose according to local policy because she is adult size\",\"e\":\"Although some antibiotics are given in similar doses to large children as to adults, the girl in this case is just 25 kg and an unnecessarily high dose would expose the child to higher chances of toxicity. Moreover, local guidelines on choice of antibiotic vary between adults and children.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Perform blood cultures and treat with empirical antibiotics for intra-abdominal sepsis\",\"e\":\"This girl obviously has intra-abdominal sepsis and delay in treatment risks increasing both morbidity and mortality. Blood cultures may be useful to guide treatment at a later date, but can take several days to come back. In modern NHS hospitals there is often a delay to theatre due to the pressures on CEPOD (Confidential Enquiry into Patient Outcome and Death), so in general, once the decision to operate for appendicitis has been made. antibiotics should be started to control sepsis. In doubtful cases of patients with right iliac fossa pain undergoing observation, antibiotics should be held, because their administration can reduce local inflammation and distort the clinical picture, making a diagnosis more difficult; of course if these patients become septic then they merit antibiotic therapy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Perform blood cultures and hold antibiotics until intraoperative swabs can be taken\",\"e\":\"As with other options, delay in treatment with antibiotics in a patient with obvious signs of intra-abdominal sepsis will only increase the risk. Intraoperative swabs can be taken but should not be prioritised over active treatment.\"}]},{\"t\":69,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 10 kg, 4-year-old girl is brought in by her mother after a 2-day history of worsening abdominal pain. She has obvious signs of peritonitis, and is febrile and tachycardic. Her mother, not knowing the dose of paracetamol, tells you she has given her 'dozens of normal paracetamol' over the past 12 hours, last dose reliably being 2 hours ago. What is the most appropriate management of her possible paracetamol overdose?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate management of her possible paracetamol overdose?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Give activated charcoal and acetylcysteine immediately, along with taking serum paracetamol level 4 hours after the last dose\",\"e\":\"This girl has very probably had an overdose of paracetamol (>150 mg/kg) which risks hepatotoxicity. Her presentation is delayed (>8 hours) and so should be treated immediately with acetylcysteine. Activated charcoal has no place when the ingestion is more than 1 hour ago.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Give acetylcysteine and take serum paracetamol levels\",\"e\":\"Given that ingestion is more than 8 hours ago, immediate treatment with acetylcysteine is indicated. Measurement of paracetamol levels is sensible although this may not change treatment. Accidental paracetamol overdose is a common problem. Understanding the treatment algorithm, and its timings. is important. Acetylcysteine should be given in all patients whose serum concentration of paracetamol is above the treatment line (graph can be found in the British National Formulary) or when the presentation is delayed (>8 hours). Education for the family is also very important, along with further questioning on possible other medications that have been taken, such as aspirin, In this case there may also be safeguarding issues that need to be explored.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Take serum paracetamol levels and give acetylcysteine depending on the result\",\"e\":\"She has a delayed presentation so paracetamol levels are not particularly useful. Delaying acetylcysteine treatment will only increase the risk of hepatotoxicity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Take serum paracetamol levels, give activated charcoal and give acetylcysteine depending on the result\",\"e\":\"Activated charcoal has no place when ingestion is over an hour ago and it is also unpleasant and messy for the patient. Waiting for the result of paracetamol levels to come back will only delay treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Admit for serial paracetamol levels\",\"e\":\"Serial paracetamol levels will only delay treatment further in this patient with a delayed presentation. She should take acetylcysteine immediately.\"}]},{\"t\":69,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A previously healthy, 60-kg, 16-year-old boy is admitted through A&E with a perianal abscess. He requires pain relief, and you decide on paracetamol in the first instance. What is the correct oral dosing regimen?\",\"lead\":\"What is the correct oral dosing regimen?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"30-60 mg every 8 hours as required\",\"e\":\"This dose is appropriate for children aged 1-2 months.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"240 mg every 4-6 hours\",\"e\":\"This dose is appropriate for children aged 4-5 years.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"480-500 mg 4- to 6-hourly, maximum of four doses a day\",\"e\":\"This dose is appropriate for children aged 10-11 years.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"0.5 g to 1 g 4- to 6-hourly, maximum four doses a day\",\"e\":\"This is correct, and is the same as the adult dose. The intravenous (IV) dose depends. however, on bodyweight, and adults under 50 kg should have only 15 mg/kg per dose, which for a 50-kg patient is 750 mg. It is important to have a grasp of sensible dosing of common analgesics. Safe prescribing in children is very important and best practice is to check every prescription in the British National Formulary.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Paracetamol is contraindicated in children\",\"e\":\"Paracetamol is a relatively safe first-line analgesic that can be used from a very young age. Caution should be taken in children with co-morbidities, but it is rarely contraindicated\"}]},{\"t\":69,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"You are performing a scrotal exploration on an 8-year-old boy with a weight of 24 kg. At the end of the operation the scrub nurse asks you how much 1% lidocaine you would like?\",\"lead\":\"At the end of the operation the scrub nurse asks you how much 1% lidocaine you would like?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"5 ml\",\"e\":\"1% lidocaine has a maximum dose of 3 mg/kg; 24 × 3 = 72.5 ml would be the equivalent of 50 mg 1% lidocaine and he can have more to provide effective analgesia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"7 ml\",\"e\":\"The maximum dose of 1% lidocaine is 3 mg/kg. For this boy this is 72 mg. As there is 10 mg/ml the boy should be given no more than 72 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"10 ml\",\"e\":\"10 ml is more than the recommended dose which could result in local anaesthetic toxicity causing cardiac arrhythmias; 3 mg/kg of 1% lidocaine means he should have a maximum dose of 72 mg. 7.2 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"12ml\",\"e\":\"Local anaesthetic dose is based on weight and each agent has maximum allowed doses. For 1% lidocaine the maximum dose is 3 mg/kg, 72 mg for this boy. which would equate to 72 ml of 1% lidocaine.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"15 ml\",\"e\":\"15 ml of 1% lidocaine would be the equivalent of 150 mg. Over double the allowed dose (3 mg/kg) for his weight of 24 kg. 1n1=\"}]},{\"t\":69,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 10-year-old boy with a past medical history of asthma is referred to the surgical team with right iliac fossa discomfort. He is suspected of having appendicitis. He has not taken any analgesia today and is feeling mildly uncomfortable What analgesia should be prescribed first?\",\"lead\":\"He has not taken any analgesia today and is feeling mildly uncomfortable What analgesia should be prescribed first?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Paracetamol\",\"e\":\"Paracetamol is a suitable first line choice for children with mild to moderate pain. This is the first step in the World Health Organization (WHO) prescribing ladder for pain.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intravenous morphine\",\"e\":\"Morphine is an effective perioperative pain killer, but starting with paracetamol is more appropriate and is in keeping with the WHO prescribing guidance:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fentanyl\",\"e\":\"Fentanyl is a potent opioid and, like morphine, is not the first step in prescribing for analgesia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Aspirin\",\"e\":\"Aspirin interferes with platelet function and can increases the risk of bleeding. Aspirin is not licensed for use in people under 16 years of age because of the risk of Reye's syndrome, which can cause significant neurological decline.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Codeine\",\"e\":\"Codeine is restricted in children under the age of 12 and should only be used in older children if first line measures (paracetamol and ibuprofen) fail.\"}]},{\"t\":69,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 6-year-old giri has had an umbilical hernia repair. Post-operatively she has one large vomit and is given an anti-emetic. Shortly after administration she begins to what appears to be an acute dystonic reaction. From the following options, which anti-emetic was most likely prescribed?\",\"lead\":\"From the following options, which anti-emetic was most likely prescribed?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyoscine hydrobromide\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cyclizine\",\"e\":\"Cyclizine is an anti-emetic with anticholinergic and antihistamine actions. It is not associated with acute dystonic events.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Dexamethasone\",\"e\":\"Dexamethasone is a potent glucocorticoid that is used perioperatively for anti-emesis. However, side-effects include immunosuppression, poor wound healing and hyperglycaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"V ondansetron\",\"e\":\"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist licensed for use as an anti-emetic in children and, although there are no guidelines for post-operative nausea and vomiting, is useful in the management of vomiting in children. Ondansetron can however induce acute dystonia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Metoclopramide\",\"e\":\"Metoclopramide is a dopamine receptor antagonist. It should not be used in children and young adults due to the high incidence of dystonia and extrapyramidal side-effects.\\n\\nHyoscine is an antimuscarinic used as an anti-emetic typically in motion sickness tablets. It would not be a first line choice for post-operative nausea and vomiting.\"}]},{\"t\":69,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A 10-year-old boy who weighs 30 kg has suspected appendicitis and is awaiting surgery. There is an emergency laparotomy currently in theatre and this will be at least another 2 h He has recently had paracetamol and ibuprofen but his pain remains poorly controlled. You decide to prescribe intravenous morphine.\\n\\nAccording to the British National Formulary (BNF) Children Guidance, what starting dose would you prescribe?\",\"lead\":\"According to the British National Formulary (BNF) Children Guidance, what starting dose would you prescribe?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"11mg\",\"e\":\"This boy has poorly controlled pain and you want to try and get on top of it quickly as it can cause a lot of psychological distress. A 1 mg dose would be safe but a typical starting dose is higher than that at 0.1 mg/kg.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"3mg\",\"e\":\"2 mg would be the maximum starting dose for a child of 20 kg. more can be given to this child. A dose of 0.1 mg/kg is used as the starting dose which for him will be 3 mg.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"2mg\",\"e\":\"Based on the boy's weight 4 mg is too much to give as a starting dose, which should be 0.1 mg/kg.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"4mg\",\"e\":\"A starting dose for a child of 10 years would be 0.1 mg/kg of intravenous morphine. In a child that weighs 30 kg he should be given an initial dose of 3 mg.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"5mg\",\"e\":\"In a child that weighs just 30 kg, 5 mg of intravenous morphine would be too much and they would be at risk of opiate overdose. The starting dose is 0.1 mg/kg\"}]},{\"t\":70,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"The hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis is responsible for neuroendocrine regulation of metabolism. It utilises a negative feedback loop to maintain homeostasis. How does hypothermia cause a rise in circulating plasma thyroxine?\",\"lead\":\"How does hypothermia cause a rise in circulating plasma thyroxine?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased production of thyroxine by the thyroid gland\",\"e\":\"While there is increased production of thyroxine from the thyroid gland, hypothermia is detected by the hypothalamus first, which causes a change in the hypothalamus-pituitary-thyroid axis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A decrease in renal excretion of thyroxine\",\"e\":\"Renal excretion does not influence the rise of plasma thyroxine in the context of hypothermia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Release of thyrotrophin releasing hormone from the hypothalamus\",\"e\":\"The hypothalamus is the control centre for thermoregulation. Hypothermia will result in an increase in the secretion of thyrotropin releasing hormone from the hypothalamus, resulting in an increase in the secretion of thyroid stimulating hormone from the anterior pituitary gland which acts directly on the thyroid gland to increase the secretion of thyroxine.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Release of thyroid stimulating hormone from the anterior pituitary gland\",\"e\":\"While thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH) is released from the anterior pituitary, hypothermia is detected by the hypothalamus first, which causes a change in the hypothalamus-pituitary-thyroid axis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"An increase in iodine absorption from the intestines\",\"e\":\"lodine absorption from the intestine is not a major influence in the rise of plasma thyroxine in the context of hypothermia.\"}]},{\"t\":70,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 4-year-old child suffers an isolated closed fracture of the tibia and fibula after falling off a sledge in the snow. He has a central body temperature of 32.5 °C on arrival and is cold in his peripheries What would be an appropriate way to warm this child?\",\"lead\":\"5 °C on arrival and is cold in his peripheries What would be an appropriate way to warm this child?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Warming blanket and warmed intravenous fluids.\",\"e\":\"This patient has mild hypothermia. Overall this case should be managed by a trauma team and attention paid to ruling out other life-threatening injuries. However, the question does state that there are no other injuries. Many trauma patients have been immobile in a cold environment for many minutes, if not hours, so hypothermia is common. Mild hypothermia, where the core temperature is between 32 and 35 °C, should be treated proactively by simple methods such as using a Bair Hugger system and warming any IV fluids the patient receives. This is often enough to raise the core temperature steadily\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bilateral chest drain with warming fluid lavage\",\"e\":\"This intervention carries with t the added risk of chest drain insertions, would probably require sedation in a 4-year-old patient and is therefore not a sensible first-line intervention.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Initiation of cardiopulmonary bypass\",\"e\":\"This patient has mild hypothermia and, although cardiopulmonary bypass can raise and lower the patient's core temperature rapidly, it requires cannulation either centrally in the chest or in a major peripheral vessel with administration of high-dose heparin. It is not a first-line intervention.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intraperitoneal lavage of warm fluids\",\"e\":\"This patient has mild hypothermia and should be treated with minimally invasive methods. Intraperitoneal lavage is useful to warm the core temperature rapidly, but risks further harm.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"ECMO circuit\",\"e\":\"This patient has mild hypothermia and therefore less invasive methods should be used in the first instance. Although ECMO (extracorporeal membrane oxygenation) circuits can be run without heparin, this often risks clotting problems. The other methods of warming can be used in more extreme situations but the risk-benefit ratio should always be balanced Invasive methods that raise the temperature more quickly will be of little benefit and risk major complications.\"}]},{\"t\":70,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 9-year-old boy, who presented with localised testicular pain and no abdominal signs, is undergoing a scrotal exploration for possible testicular torsion. Before the first incision his temperature is noted to be 40 °C. What is the most important diagnosis to consider?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most important diagnosis to consider?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Sepsis from a perforated appendicitis\",\"e\":\"Although appendicitis can sometimes present with scrotal pain, this would be very unusual in the context of a normal abdominal exam in a boy aged 9.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Malignant hyperthermia\",\"e\":\"Malignant hyperthermia is caused by exposure to suxamethonium and many inhaled anaesthetic agents. It carries with it a mortality rate of up to 30%. Intravenous (IV) dantrolene, along with cooling methods, are the treatments of choice.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Spurious reading\",\"e\":\"Although most methods of measuring temperatures are open to error, this would be a dangerous conclusion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transfusion reaction\",\"e\":\"There is no reason why this patient would have had a transfusion at the start of what is a routine case.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyrotoxicosis\",\"e\":\"This should be considered but is a less likely diagnosis in a patient on induction. More often thyrotoxicosis presents itself after surgery\"}]},{\"t\":70,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 5-year-old girl is taken to the operating room for a laparoscopic appendicectomy: The anaesthetist administers inhaled halothane and shortly afterwards her end-tidal CO_ rises, she becomes tachycardic and a diagnosis of malignant hyperthermia (MH) is made What is the most common pattern of inheritance of MH?\",\"lead\":\"A 5-year-old girl is taken to the operating room for a laparoscopic appendicectomy: The anaesthetist administers inhaled halothane and shortly afterwards her end-tidal CO_ rises, she becomes tachycardic and a diagnosis of malignant hyperthermia (MH) is made What is the most common pattern of inheritance of MH?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Autosomal recessive with full penetrance\",\"e\":\"MH is passed on in a dominant manner through both the maternal and the paternal lines. Numerous mutations have been identified as causing the syndrome, which is triggered by many inhaled anaesthetic agents, suxamethonium and other agents.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Autosomal recessive with variable penetrance\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. MH is passed on as an autosomal dominant condition\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Autosomal dominant with full penetrance\",\"e\":\"The pattern is autosomal dominant, but not with full penetrance.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Autosomal dominant with variable penetrance\",\"e\":\"MH is passed on in an autosomal dominant manner but with variable penetrance. A family history of anaesthetic problems is an important question to ask when planning surgery Mortality from this disorder is high, although it has improved with the modern use of dantrolene.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is not inherited\",\"e\":\"Although MH can be caused by spontaneous mutations it is an inherited condition.\"}]},{\"t\":70,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"An 8-year-old giri presents with right iliac fossa pain and a fever. An ultrasound is suggestive of appendicitis and she has an open appendectomy. What is the main mechanism of heat loss during surgery?\",\"lead\":\"What is the main mechanism of heat loss during surgery?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Conduction\",\"e\":\"Conduction is when heat is lost by the transfer of energy to adjacent molecules. It occurs by direct contact with an object of a lower temperature. It is a mode of heat loss during surgery contributing to approximately 5% of heat loss.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Convection\",\"e\":\"Convection describes the loss of heat to air immediately surrounding the body and contributes up to 30% of heat loss during surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Evaporation V\",\"e\":\"Energy in the form of heat is used during the vaporisation of water. Water evaporates from the body even when not sweating. At very high environmental temperature evaporation is an important source of heat loss. This is not usually the case in an operating theatre and therefore it contributes approximately 15% to heat loss.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Osmosis\",\"e\":\"Osmosis is the movement of molecules from a less to a more concentrated environment and is not used to describe heat loss.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Radiation\",\"e\":\"Radiation is the transfer of heat through electromagnetic waves and heat loss through this occurs all the time. Radiation is the major source of heat loss in surgical patients contributing up to 40% of heat loss.\"}]},{\"t\":70,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A 6-year-old boy is having an elective umbilical hernia repair. Intraoperatively his temperature is noted to be 32°C. Which one of these responses to hypothermia is likely to occur in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these responses to hypothermia is likely to occur in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased platelet activity\",\"e\":\"Temperatures below 35°C can cause mild platelet dysfunction and below 33°C can also affect the clotting cascade resulting in an increased bleeding risk.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Metabolic acidosis is typically found in hypothermia and has been attributed to respiratory depression resulting in inadequate tissue perfusion causing a buildup of anaerobic products of respiration such as lactate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tachypnoea\",\"e\":\"Hypothermia causes respiratory depression.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased cardiac output\",\"e\":\"Hypothermia results in bradycardia due to decreased depolarisation of cardiac pacemaker cells. Cardiac output = heart rate x stroke volume. There is a decreased cardiac output with hypothermia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased duration of action of anaesthetic drugs\",\"e\":\"There is decreased drug metabolism in hypothermia resulting in an increased duration of action of drugs.\"}]},{\"t\":70,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A newborn baby girl with gastroschisis is being closely monitored on the neonatal intensive care unit. Which one of these mechanisms is important with regards to conserving body temperature in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these mechanisms is important with regards to conserving body temperature in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Shivering\",\"e\":\"Shivering can double heat production but does not occur in newborn babies. Instead they have an abundance of brown fat that contains many mitochondria to generate ATP production and heat.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Noradrenergic sympathetic stimulation\",\"e\":\"Noradrenergic sympathetic stimulation will cause vasoconstriction to the extremities and vasodilation to the trunk to reduce heat loss.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sweating\",\"e\":\"The question asks about mechanisms of heat conservation, sweating causes heat loss by evaporation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Extension of limbs\",\"e\":\"Neonates will flex their extremities to decrease the surface area available for heat loss.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cutaneous vasodilatation\",\"e\":\"Cutaneous vasodilatation would result in further heat loss. Alpha 1 receptors cause cutaneous vasoconstriction to reduce heat loss by radiation and convection\"}]},{\"t\":70,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"Children under a general anaesthetic are at risk of hypothermia. Which one of these is correct regarding thermoregulation in children?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is correct regarding thermoregulation in children?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Brown fat is required for thermogenesis in the newborn and is located around the scapulas, mediastinum, kidneys and adrenal glands\",\"e\":\"Brown fat metabolism is required for thermogenesis and comprises 2-6% of neonatal body weight.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Small surface area to weight ratio increases heat loss\",\"e\":\"Babies and infants have a large surface area to weight loss and are more at risk of developing hypothermia as a result.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Infants have a large amount of subcutaneous fat, which helps insulate\",\"e\":\"Babies and infants have minimal subcutaneous fat. Brown fat is important for thermogenesis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"During anaesthesia heat loss is mostly through evaporation\",\"e\":\"Heat loss is predominately through radiation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Preterm infants are less vulnerable to heat losses\",\"e\":\"The preterm infant is very vulnerable to heat loss owing to their thin skin.\"}]},{\"t\":70,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A newborn baby born with duodenal atresia undergoes emergency surgery. Post-operatively he becomes hypothermic. Which one of these options is an effect of hypothermia in the neonate?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these options is an effect of hypothermia in the neonate?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased noradrenaline release\",\"e\":\"Hypothermia in the neonate is associated with an increased noradrenaline release, which causes thermogenesis using brown fat.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased serum lactate\",\"e\":\"As a result of anaerobic metabolism there is an increased serum lactate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyperglycaemia\",\"e\":\"In hypothermia there is increased glucose metabolism for heat production that results in hypoglycaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The pituitary gland will detect and control temperature\",\"e\":\"Thermoregulation is controlled by the hypothalamus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased peripheral vascular resistance\",\"e\":\"Activation of the sympathetic nervous system and release of noradrenaline increases peripheral vascular resistance as a result of vasoconstriction.\"}]},{\"t\":71,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with right-sided abdominal pain and vomiting. A presumed diagnosis of appendicitis is made and she is prepared for a diagnostic laparoscopy. This subsequently reveals an inflamed Meckel's diverticulum.\\n\\nWhich one of the following most correctly describes a Meckel's diverticulum?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following most correctly describes a Meckel's diverticulum?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"May contain heterotopic gastric mucosa\",\"e\":\"A Meckel's diverticulum is the most common congenital abnormality of the small intestine. affecting 2% of the population. It is a true diverticulum, caused by failure of the vitelline duct to obliterate. The vitelline duct during development connects the yolk sac to the embryonic gut. It is typically lined by ileal mucosa but in half the cases it is lined by ectopic mucosa, most commonly gastric mucosa. Most patients are asymptomatic, but can present with signs of rectal bleeding (more commonly in children) and bowel obstruction. Diagnosis is with a technetium-99m-pertechnetate scintiscan. This radioactive isotope is absorbed by gastric mucosa and therefore in the presence of a Meckel's diverticulum appears as an ectopic hot spot. Definitive treatment is excision of the diverticulum and its adjacent ileal segment. Meckel's diverticulum, the rule of 2s:\\n• occurs in 2% of the population\\n• 2:1 male female ratio\\n• approximately 2 inches long\\n• found 2 feet from the ileocaecal vaive (and up to 100 cm)\\n• 1 in 2 will contain ectopic tissue: gastric or pancreatic\\n• only 2% are symptomatic\\n• important cause of rectal bleeding in the under 2s.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Classically presents in middle-age with rectal bleeding\",\"e\":\"A Meckel's diverticulum in adults is either asymptomatic or presents with inflammation and signs of bowel obstruction It more typically present with rectal bleeding or intussusception in childhood, most commonly in children under the age of 2 years. It is important to consider this option in the differential of painless rectal bleeding in children.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is equally distributed between men and women\",\"e\":\"There is a 2:1 ratio of men:women affected.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"May be a false diverticulum\",\"e\":\"Meckel's diverticulum is a true diverticulum,\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"When present within a hernia is termed a Richter's hernia\",\"e\":\"When a Meckel's diverticulum is present with a femoral hernia it is called a Littre's hernia. A Richter's hernia is a hernia in which only the intestinal wall protrudes through a defect in the abdominal wall.\"}]},{\"t\":71,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"You are performing an appendicectomy and find a Meckel's diverticulum. Your consultant asks you what this is, and what causes it. How do you respond?\",\"lead\":\"How do you respond?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is a false diverticulum and a remnant of the vitello-intestinal duct\",\"e\":\"Meckel's diverticulum is a true diverticulum (an outpouching of a hollow organ in the body), because it contains all layers of the ileal wall including the muscularis propria and connective tissue. False diverticula involve only the mucosa and submucosa. A Meckel's diverticulum is the remnant of the vitello-intestinal duct.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"it is a false diverticulum and a urachal remnant\",\"e\":\"A Meckel's diverticulum is a true diverticulum and a remnant of the vitello-intestinal duct (vitelline duct). The urachus is a remnant of the allantois.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is a true diverticulum and remnant of the allantois\",\"e\":\"A Meckel's diverticulum is a true diverticulum and a remnant of the vitello-intestinal duct (vitelline duct). The remnant of the allantois is called an urachus, connecting the fetal bladder to the yolk sac and so removing waste products.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is a true diverticulum and a remnant of the vitello-intestinal duct\",\"e\":\"A Meckel's diverticulum* is a true diverticulum, containing all the layers of the ileal wall, and affecting 2% of the population. it is the remnant of the vitelline duct, which during development connects the yolk sac to the embryonic gut. Failure of the vitelline (vitello-intestinal) duct to obliterate leads to a Meckel's diverticulum.\\n\\nA Meckel's diverticulum is commonly an incidental finding at laparoscopy, but should be removed if it is symptomatic, contains heterotropic mucosa leading to ulceration and per rectal (PR) bleeding or if it is long.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is a true diverticulum and a urachal remnant\",\"e\":\"A Meckel's diverticulum is a true diverticulum and a remnant of the vitello-intestinal duct (vitelline duct). The urachus is a remnant of the allantois and connects the fetal bladder to the yolk sac, aiding the removal of waste products.\"}]},{\"t\":71,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 16-year-old male adolescent presents with right iliac fossa pain and vomiting. He is taken to theatre with a diagnosis of suspected acute appendicitis. During the procedure, his appendix is noted to be normal, but there is evidence of an inflamed Meckel's diverticulum on the antimesenteric ileal border\\n\\nSuch diverticulae are remnants of which embryological structure?\",\"lead\":\"Such diverticulae are remnants of which embryological structure?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cloaca\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The cloaca is an embryonic structure that gives rise to the urogenital sinus anteriorly and the anorectal canal posteriorly. The former will form the larger part of the bladder, the urethra and genital tubercle, whereas the latter will form the rectum and anal canal. A persistent cloaca describes a congenital condition in which the urethra, vagina and rectum empty into one common channel.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gubernaculum\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The gubernaculum is a fibrous ligament, connecting the lower pole of the testes to the scrotum on each side. As it matures, it retracts the ipsilateral testis into the scrotum. Failure of maturation of the gubernaculum can lead to undescended testes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mesonephric duct\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The mesonephric or Wolffian ducts are the embryological precursors of some of the male reproductive structures. Under the influence of testosterone they form the epididymis, vas deferens and seminal vesicles. In women, the mesonephric ducts regress, as there is no testosterone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Paramesonephric duct\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The paramesonephric duct or Müllerian ducts are the embryological precursors of the female reproductive system, giving rise to the uterus, fallopian tubes. cervix and the upper two-thirds of the vagina. In men, the Müllerian ducts regress due to the influence of the anti-Müllerian hormone.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vitelline duct\",\"e\":\"This is the correct answer. The scenario describes a case of a Meckel's diverticulum+, the most common congenital abnormality of the small intestine. It is caused by failure of the vitelline duct to obliterate. This is connecting the volk sac to the embryonic gut, carrying nutrients until the 7th week of gestation. Meckel's diverticulum is a true diverticulum, typically lined by ileal mucosa, however in half the cases it is lined by ectopic mucosa, most commonly gastric. It is found up to 100 cm from the ileocaecal valve on the antimesenteric border of the ileum and is usually up to 2 inches long. Patients are most commonly asymptomatic, but can present with painless rectal bleeding, signs of obstruction or in cases of inflammation it can resemble acute appendicitis with right iliac fossa pain. Definitive treatment is with excision of the diverticulum and its adjacent ileal segment.\"}]},{\"t\":71,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old male adolescent presents with right iliac fossa pain, fever and vomiting. He is assumed to have acute appendicitis, but during laparotomy he is noted to have an inflamed diverticulum on the antimesenteric border of the distal ileum. Meckel's diverticulum is a remnant of which embryological structure?\",\"lead\":\"Meckel's diverticulum is a remnant of which embryological structure?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cloaca\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The cloaca is an embryonic structure that gives rise to the urogenital sinus anteriorly and the anorectal canal posteriorly. The former will form the larger part of the bladder, the urethra and genital tubercle, whereas the latter will form the rectum and anal canal. A persistent cloaca describes a congenital condition where the urethra, vagina and rectum empty into one common channel.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Müllerian duct\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The Müllerian or paramesonephric ducts are they embryological precursors of the female reproductive system, giving rise to the uterus, fallopian tubes, cervix and upper two-thirds of the vagina. In men, the Müllerian ducts regress due to the influence of the anti- Müllerian hormone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Urachus\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The urachus is a fibrous remnant of the allantois, connecting the fetal bladder to the yolk sac and thus removing waste products. The urachus will become the median umbilical ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vitelline duct\",\"e\":\"This is the correct answer. The scenario describes a case of a Meckel's diverticulum+, the most common congenital abnormality of the small intestine. It is caused by failure of the vitelline duct to obliterate. This is connecting the yolk sac to the embryonic midgut, carrying nutrients until the 7th week gestation. Meckel's diverticulum is a true diverticulum, typically lined by ileal mucosa, however in half the cases it is lined by ectopic mucosa, most commonly gastric or pancreatic. It is found up to 100 cm from the ileocaecal valve on the antimesenteric border of the ileum and is usually up to 2 inches long. Patients are most commorily asymptomatic, but can present with painless rectal bleeding, signs of obstruction or in cases of inflammation it can resemble acute appendicitis with Right iliac fossa pain. During laparoscopy, if the appendix appears normal it is important to examine the ileum to rule out a Meckel's diverticulum. Definitive treatment is with excision of the diverticulum and its adjacent ileal segment.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wolffian duct\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The Wolffian or mesonephric ducts are the embryological precursors of some of the man reproductive structures. Under the influence of testosterone they form the epididymis, vas deferens and seminal vesicles. In the female the mesonephric ducts regress, as there is no testosterone.\"}]},{\"t\":71,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"You are examining a newborn baby who has an abdominal wall defect larger than 5 cm in diameter with a sac protruding. The umbilical cord arises from the apex of the sac. Within the sac are the intestines, liver, spleen and bladder. What is the most probable associated anomaly?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most probable associated anomaly?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fibular hemimelia\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Fibular hemimelia, and in general musculoskeletal abnormalities, may be associated with omphalocele, however these are not as common as cardiac defects which may be associated in 50% of the cases.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"is vi- Hydrocephalus\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Hydrocephalus is not commonly associated with omphalocele.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Spina bifida\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Central nervous system abnormalities occur more commonly with exomphalos minor.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tetralogy of Fallot\",\"e\":\"This is the correct answer. This patient has omphalocele (exomphalos major, >4 cm defect) a congenital defect of the abdominal wall affecting 1 in 2500 live births. During normal development of the abdominal viscera, by the 6th week of embryonic development, the rapid growth of the liver and the intestines causes the midgut to herniate through the umbilical ring. By the 10th week, the abdominal cavity has grown enough to accommodate the organs and the midgut returns into the abdomen, with rotation and fixation of the duodenum and colon. In exomphalos (omphalocoele) there is failure of the return of the midgut in the abdominal cavity and the intestine remains herniating into the umbilical cord. The herniating sac can contain bowel and liver and is surrounded by a peritoneal membrane. Exomphalos major is associated with a >4 cm abdominal wall defect, whereas exomphalos minor is associated with a <4 cm defect. Exomphalos major is associated with congenital heart defects (such as tetralogy of Fallot) in 50% of cases, limb abnormalities, ectopia cordis, bladder exstrophy. Exomphalos minor is less commonly associated with associated anomalies. Diagnosis is via routine antenatal ultrasound and can be suspected with a raised maternal serum a-fetoprotein..\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wilms tumour\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Wilms' tumour is more commonly-associated with exomphalos minor.\"}]},{\"t\":71,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A neonatal patient is born with malrotation of the midgut, where failure of rotation has occurred: Normally this is 270 degrees anticlockwise (when viewed from the front). During normal development, what action does the hindgut make inside the developing fetus?\",\"lead\":\"During normal development, what action does the hindgut make inside the developing fetus?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hinges on its mesocolon to the left\",\"e\":\"The midgut and hindgut are in continuity. The midgut rotates around the superior mesenteric artery (SMA). The hindgut moves in a more simplistic way during development; as the midgut returns to the abdominal cavity, it in turn moves to the left on the hinge of its mesocolon. As a result of this movement, the vessels that supply it are anterior to other posterior abdominal wall constituents.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hinges on its mesocolon to the right\",\"e\":\"It moves to the left. Note that the hindgut starts two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hinges on its mesocolon upwards\",\"e\":\"It moves to the left. The midgut and hindgut are in continuity. The midgut rotates around the superior mesenteric artery (SMA). The midgut extends proximally from halfway along the second part of the duodenum, finishing distally two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hinges on its mesocolon downwards\",\"e\":\"It moves to the left, hence the descending colon and sigmoid are on that side.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Does not move from its central position\",\"e\":\"The entirety of the bowel undertakes movement during embryological development Knowledge of this helps surgeons to understand where to find the avascular congenital planes of dissection.\"}]},{\"t\":72,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man is brought to the Emergency Department after being assaulted. He has considerable facial swelling with reduced sensation in the distribution of his left infraorbital nerve. CT reveals nasal bones fractures on the ascending process of the maxilla, lacrimal bone and extending into the orbital rim. The orbital floor is otherwise intact. What is the likely injury?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely injury?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Base of skull fracture\",\"e\":\"A base of skull fracture typically involves the temporal bone, occipital bone, sphenoid bone or ethmoid bone. If suspected, a CT head scan is indicated Clinical signs include Battle's sign, raccoon/panda eyes, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) rhino/otorrhoea, haemotympanum, and cranial nerve palsy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Le fort I fracture\",\"e\":\"This is a horizontal maxillary fracture including the alveolar ridge, inferior maxillary sinus and lateral nasal fractures. It results in a 'floating palate' - it separates the teeth from the upper face\\n\\nLe fort Ill injuries describe a complex fracture that involves the nasofrontal suture, orbital wall, zygomatic arch, zygomaticofrontal suture and maxilla-frontal suture. It results in a 'floating face'\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Le fort II fracture\",\"e\":\"This is a fracture through the posterior alveolar ridge, lateral maxillary sinus, inferior orbital rim and nasal bones. It results in a floating maxilla.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Le fort IIl fracture\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tripod fracture\",\"e\":\"A 'tripod' fracture often involves the orbital floor as well as the lateral wall of the maxillary sinus, and a zygomatic arch fracture. A fluid level in the maxillary sinus represents blood and often directs you to the area concerned.\"}]},{\"t\":72,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man presents with significant facial trauma following a road traffic collision (RTC) with proptosis of the right eye: A computed tomography (CT) scan demonstrates a fracture across the superior orbital fissure with associated haematoma. Which structure is most likely to be affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure is most likely to be affected?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The zygomatic nerve\",\"e\":\"This is a branch of the maxillary nerve (V2). it enters the orbit via the inferior orbital fissure carrying sensory fibres and parasympathetics.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The ophthalmic artery\",\"e\":\"This artery runs through the optic canal inferior laterally to the optic nerve, not through the superior orbital fissure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The zygomatic branch of the facial nerve\",\"e\":\"Not to be confused with the zygomatic nerve - this nerve runs across the zygomatic bone to the lateral angle of the orbit supplying motor control to the orbicularis oculi\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The optic nerve\",\"e\":\"The optic nerve passes out of the orbit through the optic canal, not the superior orbital fissure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The frontal nerve\",\"e\":\"The superior orbital fissure lies at the apex of the orbit bounded by the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid bone. A number of structures* pass through from superior to inferior; lacrimal nerve (V1), frontal nerve (Vi. superior ophthalmic vein, trochlear nerve, superior part of oculomotor nerve, nasociliary nerve (V1), inferior part of oculomotor nerve, abducens nerve, a branch of the inferior ophthalmic vein.\"}]},{\"t\":72,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man taking warfarin experiences a sudden onset of headache with visual disturbance. A computed tomography (CT) scan demonstrates a left occipital lobe intracerebral haematoma.\\n\\nWhat visual finding would you expect to find on examination?\",\"lead\":\"What visual finding would you expect to find on examination?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bitemporal hemianopia\",\"e\":\"Bitemporal hemianopia is caused by a compressive lesion at the optic chiasma, typically either a pituitary adenoma or a craniopharyngioma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"M Central scotoma\",\"e\":\"A central scotoma describes visual loss corresponding with the point of fixation most commonly caused by a lesion between the optic chiasm and the retina.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left homonymous hemianopia\",\"e\":\"A left homonymous hemianopia would result from a right occipital lobe lesion/infarction.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A macular sparing left homonymous hemianopia\",\"e\":\"Occipital lobe stroke typically results in a homonymous hemianopia contralateral to the side of the lesion. Macular or central field sparing can occur if the occipital pole remains intact through dual blood supply from a branch of the middle cerebral artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right homonymous hemianopia\",\"e\":\"A left-sided occipital haemorrhage will result in a right-sided homonymous hemianopia. There may or may not be macular sparing, depending if the occipital pole is spared or not.\"}]},{\"t\":72,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"You are reading a radiology report before the head and neck cancer MDT. A lesion is described as being on the 'True vocal cords' on an MRI of the neck The true vocal cords are:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abducted by the lateral cricoarytenoid muscles\",\"e\":\"The cords are adducted by the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle, not abducted.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Abducted by the posterior cricoarytenoid muscles\",\"e\":\"The true vocal folds have a stratified squamous epithelium, innervated by the recurrent laryngeal branch (CN X), and are formed by the vocal ligament (the free edge of the quadrangular membrane forms the false vocal cord). The cords are adducted by the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle, abducted by the posterior cricoarytenoid and tensed by tilting the thyroid cartilage downwards and forwards by contracting the cricothyroid muscle. All the laryngeal muscles are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve except for cricothyroid, which is supplied by the external laryngeal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Formed by the lower free edge of the quadrangular membranes\",\"e\":\"They are are formed by the vocal ligament (the free edge of the quadrangular membrane forms the false vocal cord).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Innervated by sensory fibres of the internal laryngeal nerves\",\"e\":\"Above the vocal cords, the larynx is sensorily innervated by the internal laryngeal nerve (CN\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lined by respiratory epithelium\",\"e\":\"The vocal cords are not lined by respiratory epithelium\"}]},{\"t\":73,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 3-day-old boy undergoes urological surgery. During antenatal imaging, there was evidence of bilateral hydronephrosis. What is the most likely primary pathology?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely primary pathology?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior urethral valves\",\"e\":\"Anterior urethral valves are rare congenital anomalies in the form of diverticula that typically occur when there is a defect in the corpus spongiosum. This leaves a thin walled urethra that balloons out during voiding. Symptoms mimic those of a posterior urethral valve, but anterior urethral valves are rarer. They can present with recurrent urinary tract infections, dribbling, or a weak stream. Diagnosis is with voiding studies showing a dilatation in the urethra. Ultrasound scan shows evidence of hydronephrosis, incomplete voiding, distended bladder. Imaging studies can show evidence of hydronephrosis. They are found in children and can lead to lower urinary tract obstruction. They are treated with endoscopic or open ablation of the urethral diverticulum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bladder exstrophy\",\"e\":\"Bladder exstrophy does not present with hydronephrosis. This is a rare, anterior midline defect of the infraumbilical abdominal wall affecting the pelvis, abdominal muscles, urinary tract and external genitalia. It presents as incomplete closure of the lower abdominal wall at the midline and therefore protrusion of the bladder through the defect. Typical examination findings are low set umbilicus, a red patch of mucosa freely draining urine, wide pubic symphysis, open urethra (men), absent mons pubis and bifid clitoris (women). Treatment is with staged operations:\\n• after birth (best within 72 h): operation to close the bladder and pelvis\\n• 2 years: genitoplasty (repair of epispadias and penis)\\n• 4-5 years: bladder neck repair to achieve continence.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glandular hypospadia\",\"e\":\"Granular hypospadias describes a congenital defect where the urethral opening is found near the head of the penis (the glans) on the inferior side of the penis, rather than at the tip of the penis. In hypospadias, there is incomplete fusion of the urethral folds: It is commonly associated with undescended testis. Diagnosis is by its characteristic appearance of the penis with a displaced urethral opening, incomplete foreskin. In mild cases it can be asymptomatic. When symptomatic it can cause difficulty in urination, abnormal stream or curvature of the penis. It is not associated with bilateral hydronephrosis. It is corrected surgically.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior urethral valves\",\"e\":\"Posterior urethral valves are a form of congenital obstruction secondary to malformation of the posterior urethra. They occur in 1 in 5000 live births. In the posterior urethra, valves occur by one of four proposed mechanisms: hypertrophy of the urethral mucosal folds, abnormal development of the Wolffian duct, fusion of the vermontanum or persistence of the urogenital membrane (a structure that normally vanishes). All the above cause obstruction. They are usually diagnosed antenatally by evidence of bilateral hydronephrosis, thickened bladder wall and bladder diverticula. It is the most common cause of bladder outlet obstruction in male newborns. After birth there is bladder distention, dribbling urine or complete retention. Management of the newborn is with a urethral catheter to relieve the obstruction followed by vesicotomy or valve ablation. Patients may require a cutaneous ureterostomy to ensure urine drainage..\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vesicoureteric reflux\",\"e\":\"Vesicoureteric reflux is retrograde flow of urine from the bladder into the kidneys that can lead to infection, scarring and kidney damage. Primary vesicoureteric reflux is present at birth and is caused by a defect in the valve that normally prevents backflow of urine. It is associated with recurrent urine infections and pyelonephritis leading to kidney damage. Treatment is aimed at preventing kidney damage. If severe, it can lead to hydronephrosis. Diagnosis is via ultrasound of the kidneys and bladder as well as a voiding cystourethrography.\\n\\nEven though both anterior and posterior urethral valves can cause bilateral hydronephrosis. posterior urethral valves are the most common form of male newborn obstruction.\"}]},{\"t\":73,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 12-year-old boy presents with acute testicular pain and a 'blue dot' on the scrotum. He is taken to theatre for an exploration to rule out a testicular torsion. Which one embryological remnant is affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which one embryological remnant is affected?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gartner's duct.C-\",\"e\":\"The Gartner's duct is derived from the remnants of the mesonephric ducts in the women. It persists between the layer of the broad ligament and the wall of the vagina. It can give rise to a cyst.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hydatid of Morgagni\",\"e\":\"The hydatid of Morgagni, also called the appendix testis, is the remnant of the Müllerian duct found in 90% of men. It is on the upper pole of the testis, adjacent to the tunica vaginalis. It has no function but carries the risk of torsion presenting with unilateral testicular pain. Diagnostically, patients present with a tender 'blue dot' discoloration of the testis. Torsion of the hydatid of Morgagni does not affect testicular function, but may require surgical excision for symptomatic relief.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mechels diverticulum Y Meckel's diverticulum\",\"e\":\"A Meckel's diverticulum is a true diverticulum, which is the remnant of the vitelline duct. present in 2% of the population. It is found about 2 feet from the ileocaecal valve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urachal cyst\",\"e\":\"The urachus is a remnant of the allantois. It involutes before birth and remains a fibrous cord attaching the umbilicus to the bladder. A urachal cyst is the result of closure in both ends of the tubes with a central open portion on the anterior abdominal wall that is fluid filled.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urachal sinus\",\"e\":\"A urachal sinus is non-communicating dilatation of the urachus on the superior and, towards the umbilicus. This can either present congenitally or be as a result of an infected urachal cyst draining to the surface of the abdomen. They can present with periumbilical tenderness, granulation tissue or pus at the umbilicus.\"}]},{\"t\":73,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"You are in the paediatric surgery outpatient clinic: In between cases your consultant discusses relevant embryology with you about the next case. Which one of the following is derived from the mesonephric ducts?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is derived from the mesonephric ducts?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Prostate\",\"e\":\"In the male the prostate forms from the urogenital sinus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fallopian tubes\",\"e\":\"Fallopian tubes, the uterus, the cervix, upper part of the vagina and the vaginal wall in women develop from the Müllerian ducts also known as paramesonephric ducts (as the lie laterally to the mesonephric ducts). The parames onephric ducts regress in men, due to secretion of anti-Müllerian hormone, and have no major contribution.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Uterus\",\"e\":\"Fallopian tubes; the uterus, the cervix, the upper part of the vagina and the vaginal wall in women develop from the Müllerian ducts, also known as paramesonephric ducts (as they lie laterally to the mesonephric ducts). The parames onephric ducts regress in men, due to secretion of anti-Mullerian hormone, and have no major contribution.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vagina\",\"e\":\"Fallopian tubes, the uterus, the cervix and the upper part of the vagina and the vaginal wall in women develop from the Müllerian ducts, also known as paramesonephric ducts (as they lie laterally to the mesonephric ducts). The paramesonephric ducts regress in men, due to the secretion of anti-Müllerian hormone, and have no major contribution:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vas deferens\",\"e\":\"In men the mesonephric ducts give rise to the tubular system of the genito-urinary system, ie epididymis, vas deferens, ureter, renal pelvis, calyces, collecting tubules, seminal vesicle and ejaculatory duct. In women they only contribute to the tubular system of the urinary system leading to the formation of the ureter, renal pelvis, calyces and collecting tubules.\"}]},{\"t\":73,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 2-day-old child is diagnosed with cryptorchidism. The left testicle has not descended into the scrotum and therefore there is only one testicle palpable in the scrotum on examination. Which one embryological structure is associated with this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one embryological structure is associated with this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Allantois\",\"e\":\"The allantois forms as an evagination of the hindgut and becomes the urachus, connecting the fetal bladder to the yolk sac and aids in the removal of waste products from the fetus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Genital tubercle\",\"e\":\"As the genital tubercle develops around the fourth week of gestation and will give rise to the clitoris in the women and the penis in the men.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gubernaculum\",\"e\":\"The gubernaculum is a ligament connecting the lower pole of the testes to the scrotum. Early in the fetal period the testes are in the lumbar area and have to travel into the future scrotum. Migration happens in two phases: the first from the abdomen to the internal inguinal ring and the second through the inguinal canal and into the scrotum. The gubernaculum thickens and shortens, anchoring the testes in the first phase. In the second phase the muscular part of the gubernaculum, responding to androgens, grows and differentiates, migrating the testes through the inguinal region by traction. In patients with undescended testis the gubernaculum fails to undergo the changes required to shorten and thicken sufficiently impairs the migration of the testist to the scrotum. The gubernaculum in patients with crypto-orchidism is less collagenous and more fibrous.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Müllerian duct\",\"e\":\"The Müllerian ducts (or paramesonephric ducts) give rise to the female reproductive system. They will lead to the uterus, fallopian tubes, cervix and upper part of the vagina. They originate as invaginations of the coelomic epithelium on the anterolateral surface of the urogenital ridge.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wolffian duct\",\"e\":\"The Wolffian ducts (also called mesonephric ducts) give rise to the male urogenital organs including the epididymis, seminal vesicles and vas deferens. In the women the Wolffian ducts degenerate in the absence of testosterone.\"}]},{\"t\":73,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 38-year-old woman undergoes an elective hysterectomy. She has intractable menorrhagia that has been unresponsive to all other management options. From which embryological structure does the uterus develop?\",\"lead\":\"From which embryological structure does the uterus develop?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Allantois\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The allantois forms as an evagination of the hindgut and becomes the urachus, connecting the fetal bladder to the yolk sac and aids in the removal of waste products from the fetal bladder\\n\\nThis is incorrect. The gubernaculum is a ligament that connects the lower pole of the testes to the scrotum and aids their descent as it shortens and matures, from the abdomen into the scrotum, by traction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"W'\\\" Gubernaculum\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Müllerian duct\",\"e\":\"This is the correct answer. The fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix, upper part of the vagina and vaginal walls in the female are derived from the Müllerian ducts, also known as paramesonephric ducts (lying lateral to the mesonephric/Wolffian ducts), which are of mesodermal origin. The Müllerian ducts originate as invaginations of the coelomic epithelium on the anterolateral surface of the urogenital ridge. They regress in men, due to the action of anti-Müllerian hormone secreted from 8 weeks from the Sertoli cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Wolffian duct\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The Wolffian ducts (mesonephric ducts) connect the primitive kidney (mesonephros) to the cloaca and develops, under the influence of testosterone, into the male reproductive organs. It gives rise to the epididymis, vas deferens and the seminal vesicles. In women, the structures regress in the absence of testosterone, but may remain as a remnant on the lateral wall of the vagina giving rise to a Gartner's cyst/duct. In both genders, the Wolffian ducts. will form the trigone of the bladder.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Processus vaginalis\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The processus vaginalis is present in the developing fetus from 12 weeks of gestation onwards. It is an outpouching of parietal peritoneum which precedes the testes in their descent with the gubernaculum, through the deep inguinal ring. Once descent of the testes is completed, the processus vaginalis closes. A patent processus vaginalis increases the risk of an indirect inguinal hernia.\"}]},{\"t\":73,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 6-year-old male child presents with hypospadias and undergoes reconstructive surgery During the surgery your consultant asks you about disorders of sexual development The male urethra develops from which embryological structure?\",\"lead\":\"A 6-year-old male child presents with hypospadias and undergoes reconstructive surgery During the surgery your consultant asks you about disorders of sexual development The male urethra develops from which embryological structure?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gubernaculum\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The gubernaculum is a ligament connecting the lower part of the testes to the scrotum. As it matures, it shortens and thickens, pulling the testes from their abdominal position to their final position within the scrotum by traction. Failure of this maturation of the gubernaculum is associated with undescended testis. There is no association between the gubernaculum and hypospadias.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Müllerian duct\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The Müllerian duct is of mesodermal origin and is the embryological structure that will become the female reproductive system. In men, it regresses under the influence of anti-Müllerian hormone\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cloaca\",\"e\":\"This is the correct answer. During the 5-7th week of embryonic development, the cloaca will divide into the urogenital sinus (anterior) and the anal canal (posterior). The former will form the larger part of the bladder, the urethra and genital tubercle, whereas the latter will form the rectum and anal canal. Hypospadias, is a birth defect presenting in about 1 in 300 male live births. It occurs due to incomplete fusion of the urethral folds leading to a displaced urethral meatus (opening), not at the tip of the penis, but somewhere along the inferior of the shaft of the penis. There are two types of hypospadias\\n• Anterior or Distal = The meatus is found on or near the glans. This is the most common type, also referred to as glanular hypospadias.\\n• Posterior or Proximal = The meatus is found near or within the scrotum.\\n\\nDiagnosis is clinical with a displaced urethral opening and incomplete foreskin. Symptoms include: difficult urination, abnormal stream, or curvature of the penis. It is corrected surgically. It is commonly associated with undescended testes. Diagnosis is by its characteristic appearance of the penis with a displaced urethral opening, incomplete foreskin. In mild cases it can be asymptomatic. When symptomatic it can cause difficulty in urination, abnormal stream or curvature of the penis. It is not associated with bilateral hydronephrosis. Minor abnormalities do not require treatment. In symptomatic patients the treatment is surgical, by distal urethral repositioning and glanuloplasty.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sinovaginal bulbs\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The sinovaginal bulbs are of endodermal origin. The form is as paired outgrowths of the urogenital sinus (the ventral part of the cloaca). As they develop the pair will fuse to form the lower part of the vagina in the developing female fetus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wolffian duct\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. The Wolffian ducts (mesonephric ducts) connect the primitive kidney (mesonephros) to the cloaca and gives rise to the man reproductive organs. This process occurs under the influence of testosterone, It give rise to the epididymis. vas deferens and the seminal vesicles. In women, the structures regress in the absence of testosterone.\"}]},{\"t\":74,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A newborn is referred from a neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) after it was noticed that he has a proximal hypospadias. He is considered for reconstructive surgery. With what other abnormalities is this developmental abnormality associated?\",\"lead\":\"With what other abnormalities is this developmental abnormality associated?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Undescended testicle\",\"e\":\"Hypospadias is, like a lot of congenital problems, associated with many other development disorders. Roughly 5-10% have concurrent undescended testicles\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cleft lip/palate\",\"e\":\"Similar to many developmental problems, hypospadias is linked with many other disorders. including cleft lip and palate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"congenital heart disease?\",\"e\":\"Similar to many developmental problems, hypospadias is linked with many other disorders of development, including congenital heart disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inguinal hernia\",\"e\":\"Similar to many developmental problems, hypospadias is linked with many other disorders of development, including inguinal hernias.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"All of the above\",\"e\":\"Similar to most developmental disorders, hypospadias is linked with many other problems Mild, distal hypospadias carries with it a lower rate of concurrent disorder whereas proximal hypospadias has a higher rate. It is important to investigate children appropriately for other abnormalities.\"}]},{\"t\":74,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A. 4-month-old infant is seen with a left-sided undescended testicle, just palpable in the inguinal region. It had not been previously noticed. When should intervention be considered in patients with an undescended testicle?\",\"lead\":\"When should intervention be considered in patients with an undescended testicle?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Immediately after birth. C\",\"e\":\"Many undescended testicles will eventually descend, so immediate surgical intervention would expose a lot of children to unnecessary risk.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"0-3 months\",\"e\":\"When an undescended testicle is found on examination at birth, routine referral for surgical intervention at this early stage would lead to over-treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"6-12 months\",\"e\":\"An undescended testicle is the most common disorder of development. It is best managed conservatively in the first instance because many undescended testicles will descend within the first 6 months. After this point few will descend.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"12-24 months\",\"e\":\"Evidence suggests that earlier surgery is best (3-6 months ideal, 6-12 months acceptable) to reduce the associated risks of an undescended testicle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"2-4 years\",\"e\":\"Evidence suggests that earlier surgery is best (3-6 months ideal, 6-12 months acceptable) to reduce the associated risks of an undescended testicle.\\n\\nCryptorchidism is the most common developmental disorder of the genital tract in boys. The presentation is varied and can be unilateral or bilateral and of varying severity. The abnormally located testicle can be anywhere from its embryological origin to the scrotum. Intermittent retraction may be interpreted as an undescended testicle. Referrals should be made in plenty of time to plan surgery, so referral before 6 months, with a plan to admit, re-examine and operate, would be sensible. Older patients should be referred straight away.\"}]},{\"t\":74,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A paediatric registrar is asked to attend the labour ward after the midwives are unsure whether a newly delivered infant is male or female. The registrar is also unsure and sends blood tests to analyse the baby's genetics further Which one of the following is most accurate with regard to normal reproductive development?\",\"lead\":\"The registrar is also unsure and sends blood tests to analyse the baby's genetics further Which one of the following is most accurate with regard to normal reproductive development?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The round ligament of the uterus is derived from the gubernaculum, which itself is derived from the ectoderm\",\"e\":\"The round ligament of the uterus is derived from the gubernaculum. However, this is derived from the mesoderm of the embryo.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The round ligament of the uterus is derived from the gubernaculum, which itself is derived from the mesoderm\",\"e\":\"The round ligament of the uterus in the woman corresponds to the gubernaculum testis in the man, and both develop from the gubernaculum, which is derived from the mesoderm of the embryo.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The female ovaries and male testes are derived from the embryonic endoderm\",\"e\":\"The gonads are derived from the mesoderm, not the endoderm. From the seventh week of gestation male and female systems become more distinguishable under differing hormonal influences.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The labia majora of the remale genitalia corresponds developmentally with the male scrotum, and they both originate from the endoderm layer\",\"e\":\"The labia majora and male scrotum develop from the labioscrotal folds. These develop from the ectoderm layer as paired swellings on either side of the embryonic cloacal membrane. In the man they fuse to form the scrotum with the fusion line known as the scrotal raphe. In the woman they do not fuse, except for the portion that becomes the frenulum of the labia majora.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The separate male and female genitalia are distinguishable and differentiated from week 5 of gestation\",\"e\":\"It is only from around week 9 that the genitalia are distinguishable: They are similar until week 7 and fully developed by week 9.\"}]},{\"t\":74,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old woman presents to her GP after taking a pregnancy test. She thinks she is six weeks pregnant and asks if she can get a scan at this stage to see whether the fetus is male or female. Her GP explains that at this stage the fetus is developmentally neither male nor female\\n\\nWhich one of the following is most accurate concerning the embryological development of gender differentiation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most accurate concerning the embryological development of gender differentiation?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Testosterone induces male development, and it is produced by the interstitial cells of Leydig\",\"e\":\"By week 8 of development the interstitial cells of Leydig develop. These produce testosterone, which is responsible for the induction of gender differentiation in the male fetus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Testosterone induces male development, and it is produced by the seminiferous tubules\",\"e\":\"Male development is induced by testosterone, but it is produced by the interstitial cells of Leydig. The seminiferous tubules develop from the seminiferous cords.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Testosterone induces male development, and it is produced by the Sertoli cells\",\"e\":\"Testosterone induces male development, but it is produced from the interstitial cells of Leydig. The Sertoli cells produce Müllerian Inhibiting Factor (MIF). This factor suppresses the development of the paramesonephric duct.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The presence of testis determining factor (TDF) in the female causes gonads to develop into ovaries\",\"e\":\"It is the absence of TDF in the female that causes the gonads to develop into ovaries.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The embryonic urogenital folds form the labia majora in women, and the ventral aspect of the penis in men\",\"e\":\"The embryonic urogenital folds form the labia minora in women, and the ventral aspect of the penis in men. The labia majora are formed from the labioscrotal swellings and are correspondent in men with the scrotum.\"}]},{\"t\":74,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 31-year-old woman presents to the fertility clinic with her husband. Her menstrual cycle is normal, but the couple have suffered several early pregnancy losses and are seeking further help. Further investigations show a congenital anatomical abnormality in the uterus, which is due to incomplete or partial fusion of the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts.\\n\\nWhich developmental abnormality is this most likely to represent?\",\"lead\":\"Which developmental abnormality is this most likely to represent?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypospadias\",\"e\":\"This is an abnormality in the male penis that occurs after a failure of the urethral folds to fuse. This results in a urethral opening on the inferior aspect of the penis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Unicornuate uterus\",\"e\":\"This is a congenital abnormality in which the uterus has a single horn. It occurs when only one paramesonephric duct fails to develop, but not with partial fusion. It is associated with miscarriage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bicornuate uterus\",\"e\":\"Bicornuate uterus is often discovered incidentally It occurs when there is incomplete or partial fusion of the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts, and results in a uterus with two separate horns. It is associated with recurrent pregnancy loss and cervical incompetence.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Millerian agénesis\",\"e\":\"This is a congenital abnormality in which the Müllerian ducts fail to develop. resulting in an absent uterus and fallopian tubes. Patients with the condition do not menstruate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cryptorchidism\",\"e\":\"This describes the undescended testes in men and can lead to infertility if both are undescended\"}]},{\"t\":74,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old woman presents with pain, erythema and swelling of the labia. She was initially referred to General Surgery as a perianal abscess but the surgical registrar diagnoses an infected Bartholin's cyst and refers her to Gynaecology. Which one of the following is true regarding the development of Bartholin's glands?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding the development of Bartholin's glands?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bartholin's glands are the embryological equivalent to the seminal vesicles in men\",\"e\":\"Bartholin's glands are the equivalent of the bulbourethral glands in men, also known as Cowper's glands.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The Bartholin's glands lie on either side of the urethral opening\",\"e\":\"The Bartholin's glands lie on either side of the vaginal opening and secrete mucus to lubricate the vagina.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The Bartholin's glands are located on the anterior wall of the vagina and are the embryological equivalent to the prostate gland\",\"e\":\"This statement would be true of the Skene's glands.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Bartholin's glands are the embryological equivalent to the bulbourethral glands in men\",\"e\":\"The Bartholin glands are also known as the greater vestibular glands. They are homologous to the bulbourethral glands in men.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Bartholin's glands are embryological outgrowths of the paramesonephric ducts\",\"e\":\"Bartholin's glands are formed from outgrowths of the urogenital sinus in the fetus. Their main function is lubrication. LARA\"}]},{\"t\":74,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 71-year-old man presents with difficulty in urination that is secondary to urethral stricture. Further investigations reveal that the stricture is secondary to adenocarcinoma of the bulbourethral glands Which one of the following is most accurate regarding the development of the bulbourethral glands?\",\"lead\":\"Further investigations reveal that the stricture is secondary to adenocarcinoma of the bulbourethral glands Which one of the following is most accurate regarding the development of the bulbourethral glands?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are common to both men and women\",\"e\":\"The bulbourethral glands are found only in men. Their equivalent in women are the Bartholin's glands, found on either side of the vaginal opening\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are endocrine glands found inferior to the prostate\",\"e\":\"The bulbourethral glands are exocrine glands, because their main function is to secrete lubrication to the spongy urethra. They also produce prostate-specific antigen (PSA). They are found inferior to the prostate and posterolaterally to the membranous urethra.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The bulbourethral glands are also known as Skene's glands\",\"e\":\"The bulbourethral glands are also known as Cowper's glands, after the English surgeon Will am Cowper who first described them in the 17th century. Skene's glands are found at the anterior wall of the vagina.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The bulbourethral glands develop during the 6th to 8th weeks of fetal development\",\"e\":\"The bulbourethral glands form during the 10th to 12th week of gestation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The bulbourethral glands are formed from the urogenital sinus\",\"e\":\"These glands do arise from the urogenital sinus, which is the ventral part of the embryonic cloaca. It is divided into upper, pelvic and phallic sections. Also arising from the urogenital sinus in men are the urethra, bladder (except trigone) and the prostate gland.\"}]},{\"t\":75,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old young man is seen in A&E after sustaining a dog bite to his lower right back You notice a tuft of hair over the lower back and consider spina bifida occulta. What percentage of children are born with this lack of fusion of the L5 or S1 neural arch?\",\"lead\":\"What percentage of children are born with this lack of fusion of the L5 or S1 neural arch?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1%\",\"e\":\"Up to around 15% of children are born with this developmental abnormality.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"3%\",\"e\":\"It is higher than 3%.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"12%\",\"e\":\"The true value is less than this.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"50%\",\"e\":\"Approximately 10 - 15% children are born with this developmental abnormality. The bony defect rarely carries with it any concurrent back problems and can be identified by a commonly found tuft of hair at this location. More severe forms of failure of neural arch fusion, such as spina bifida cystica, are far more rare and come with significant associated paralysıs, and bowel and bladder problems.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"75%\",\"e\":\"This lack of fusion is not this common.\"}]},{\"t\":75,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man is admitted with the worst headache he has ever had and a possible subarachnoid haemorrhage is considered. A CT scan is not diagnostic so a lumbar puncture is attempted.\\n\\nWhere does the subarachnoid space and in a fully developed adult?\",\"lead\":\"Where does the subarachnoid space and in a fully developed adult?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"T10-12\",\"e\":\"At this level the spinal cord sits within the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) in the dura:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"L1-2\",\"e\":\"At this level in adults the spinal cord becomes the cauda equina, which then continues further down.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"L3-4\",\"e\":\"At this level the cord has invariably ended in adults, whereas in children it may remain. The cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) space containing the cauda remains.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"L4-5\",\"e\":\"At this level the cord has ended and the cauda equina remains within the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). This location is often used for lumbar puncture.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"ai\",\"e\":\"Although the spinal cord in an adult finishes in the upper lumbar vertebrae, the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) within the dura passes all the way down to the sacral vertebrae. The exact termination is variable within the sacrum.\"}]},{\"t\":75,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"An infant is born with a sac-like fleshy protrusion from his lower spine. The paediatrician on call is alerted and examines the baby. She informs the parents that part of the spinal cord is protruding into this sac, and that it will need to be corrected surgically Which disorder of neural tube development is this most likely to be?\",\"lead\":\"She informs the parents that part of the spinal cord is protruding into this sac, and that it will need to be corrected surgically Which disorder of neural tube development is this most likely to be?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Spina bifida occulta\",\"e\":\"This is the mildest form of spina bifida, which rarely causes complications. Spina bifida is a congenital malformation in which the vertebral arch of the spinal column is absent or partially formed. In spina bifida occulta there is failure of the fusion of the vertebral bodies without exposing neural tissue. Skin overlying the abnormality may appear normal, or be identified by several overlying features including hair, dimpling, skin sinus and Mongolian blue spot.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Myelomeningocele\",\"e\":\"This is a form of spinal bifida in which a neural tube defect causes an opening in the vertebral column, exposing the spinal cord and meninges which protrude through the opening. It is generally seen as a fleshy protrusion and can cause paralysis, absence of sensation and bladder/bowel symptoms. As with other neural tube defects, it results from failed neural tube pore closure by 28 days during embryological development. Treatment is surgical and the brain should be imaged to assess for a Chiari 2 malformation (also known as a Arnold-Chiari malformation) and hydrocephalus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Meningocele\",\"e\":\"Also known as meningeal cyst, this is a cleft in the spinal column with herniation of the meninges, but not the spinal cord as in myelomeningocele.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Encephalocele\",\"e\":\"This is a herniation of the brain and/or meninges through a skull defect, and so does not occur in the lower spine.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Syringomyelia\",\"e\":\"This refers to dilatation and fluid accumulation in the spinal cord. It is often present in cases of myelomeningocele due the association with Chiari 2 malformation (also known as an Arnold-Chiari malformation).\"}]},{\"t\":75,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old primigravida attends her GP after taking a pregnancy test, which was positive. She is advised to take folic acid to decrease the likelihood of neural tube defects.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is most accurate with regard to embryological neural tube closure?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most accurate with regard to embryological neural tube closure?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The neural plate appears at around day 10 post-conception\",\"e\":\"The neural plate appears around the beginning of the third week. The lateral edges of the plate become elevated, forming neural folds that come together to fuse the neural tube.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Neural tube fusion begins in the caudal region and extends cranially\",\"e\":\"Neural tube fusion begins in the cervical region and extends in the caudal direction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The neural plate is composed of thickened endoderm\",\"e\":\"The neural plate is composed of thickened ectoderm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The cranial neuropore closes around day 25 post-conception\",\"e\":\"The cranial neuropore closes before the caudal aspect at around 25 days. The caudal neuropore closes around two days after this.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The neural plate is composed of thickened mesoderm\",\"e\":\"The central nervous system (CNS) first appears as a plate composed of thickened ectoderm, not mesoderm\"}]},{\"t\":75,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old pregnant woman attends for a second trimester fetal ultrasound scan. This shows polyhydramnios and the brain is not seen. The sonographer is concerned and contacts the obstetric consultant for a second opinion.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is the most accurate?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is the most accurate?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Folic acid supplementation is recommended for pregnant mothers to decrease the occurrence of neural tube defects. It should be commenced once conception has occurred\",\"e\":\"Folic acid supplementation is recommended for women planning a pregnancy to decrease neural tube defect occurrence. However, it should be commenced before conception, and continued for 12 weeks. Those felt to have a higher risk of neural tube defects (family history) should take a higher dose.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"This clinical scenario is likely to represent anencephaly, which occurs from failure of the caudal neuropore to close around day 25\",\"e\":\"This scenario is likely to represent anencephaly, but it occurs when the cranial neuropore fails to close around day 25\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anencephaly results from the failure of the cranial neuropore to close around day 27\",\"e\":\"Anencephaly results from the failure of the cranial neuropore to close around day 25. Spina bifida results from the failure of the caudal neuropore to close, which occurs several days later at day 27.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"This clinical scenario is likely to represent holoprosencephaly, which occurs when the prosencephalon fails to develop into two hemispheres\",\"e\":\"Holoprosencephaly is an abnormality that occurs when the prosencephalon fails to develop into two hemispheres, but does not lead to absence of the brain as in this clinical scenario.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"This clinical scenario is likely to represent anencephaly, which occurs from failure of the cranial neuropore to close around day 25\",\"e\":\"This scenario is likely to represent anencephaly, which occurs when the cranial neuropore fails to close around day 25. It is a severe defect in which the developing forebrain and parts of the brainstem are exposed in utero, and fail to develop or are destroyed.\"}]},{\"t\":75,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"An infant is born at full term and is about to be discharged home. During his pre-discharge baby check, the midwife notices a hairy area at the base of the spine. Which disorder is this most likely to represent?\",\"lead\":\"Which disorder is this most likely to represent?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Myelomeningocele\",\"e\":\"This is a form of spinal bifida, which is more severe than spina bifida occulta. It causes an opening in the vertebral column, exposing the spinal cord and meninges which protrude through the opening, It is generally seen as a fleshy protrusion, which is not what is described here.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mongolian blue spot\",\"e\":\"A Mongolian blue spot overlying the spine can be a sign of spina bifida, but this is not what is described here.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Meningocele\",\"e\":\"Meningocele is a neural tube defect that causes a cleft in the spinal column with herniation of the meninges, but not the spinal cord as in myelomeningocele.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Haemangioma\",\"e\":\"Haemangiomas are benign vascular proliferations causing an unsightly tumour appearance on the skin. They are common in infancy but generally undergo gradual involution. This is not what is described in this clinical scenario\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spina bifida occulta\",\"e\":\"Spina bifida occulta is the mildest form of spina bifida, which rarely causes complications. Spina bifida is a congenital malformation in which the vertebral arch of the spina column is absent or partially formed. in spina bifida occulta there is failure of the fusion of the vertebral bodies without exposing neural tissue. Often patients are unaware of the malformation as there are no outward signs. Sometimes however there are signs overlying the area of the abnormality such as a tuft of hair, skin sinus, Mongolian blue spot or dimpling.\"}]},{\"t\":75,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 31-year-old pregnant woman attends a fetal ultrasound. The sonographer tells her that the scan assesses any developmental abnormalities, including neural tube defects Which one of the following is the strongest risk factor for developing a neural tube defect?\",\"lead\":\"The sonographer tells her that the scan assesses any developmental abnormalities, including neural tube defects Which one of the following is the strongest risk factor for developing a neural tube defect?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Folic acid supplementation. Cr\",\"e\":\"It is folate deficiency that is associated with neural tube defects. Folic acid supplementation is recommended before conception, and should be taken up to 12 weeks of pregnancy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Edward's syndrome (Trisomy 18)\",\"e\":\"Syndromes associated with neural tube defects include Edward's syndrome (Trisomy 18). Patau's syndrome (Trisomy 13), and Meckel-Gruber and Roberts\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Maternal hypothermia,\",\"e\":\"Maternal hyperthermia is associated with neural tube defects, occurring because of a febrile illness or other sources such as hot tub or sauna use.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Maternal underweight\",\"e\":\"Although maternal low weight is associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes, it is maternal obesity that is associated with a two-fold increase in neural tube defects\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Metformin\",\"e\":\"There have been some reported associations with nitrosatable drugs such as metformin and paroxetine, but there is a lack of strong evidence to support this.\"}]},{\"t\":76,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 2-day-old male newborn presents with regurgitation of saliva, choking after feeds and drooling. A diagnosis of oesophageal atresia without fistula is made following further investigation. Which fetal abnormality would you have expected to see during antenatal ultrasound screening?\",\"lead\":\"Which fetal abnormality would you have expected to see during antenatal ultrasound screening?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aneuploidy\",\"e\":\"Aneuploidy is defined as any variation in the number of chromosomes in a cell that is not an exact multiple of the haploid number. This can represent one extra/less chromosome or part of a chromosome. Examples of aneuploidy include Down's syndrome (trisomy 21) and Turners syndrome (45XO). To diagnose aneuploidy antenatally you need to carry out an invasive procedure such as amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling and carry out karyotyping. Features associated with specific aneuploidies can be detected during antenatal ultrasound screening but aneuploidy as such cannot be seen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Oligohydramnios\",\"e\":\"Oligohydramnios is defined as diminished amniotic fluid. This is detected during routine antenatal ultrasound scans and can be due to the following:\\n• placental causes: placental abruption, twin to twin transfusion syndrome\\n• fetal factors: renal agenesis, chromosomal/congenital factors, IUGR, premature rupture of membranes, intrauterine fetal death\\n• maternal factors: uteroplacental insufficiency, HTN, pre-eclampsia, chronic hypoxia\\n• drug induced: NSAIDs, angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors\\n• idiopathic\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Polyhydramnios\",\"e\":\"Oesophageal atresia is a congenital disorder occurring in 1 in 2500 live births. It describes a failure of the cesophagus to form as a continuous tube but rather forms as a blind ended pouch. It is part of the VACTERL syndrome, and can be-associated with a tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF). It arises in the 4th week of gestation when the oesophagus and trachea normally separate. A fetus with oesophageal atresia cannot swallow amniotic fluid effectively, especially in the absence of TEF. Swallowing reduces the amount of amniotic fluid present and with impaired swallowing polyhydramnios develops in almost all mothers carrying foetuses with oesophageal atresia without TEF. Symptoms of oesophageal atresia in the neonate include: cyanosis when feeding, drooling, poor feeding, and gagging on attempted feeding. Polyhydramnios is a condition defined as an excessive amount of amniotic fluid around the fetus. This can be detected during antenatal ultrasound screening and is also-associated with the following conditions: duodenal atresia, cardiovascular defects, neural tube defects, renal disorders, trisomies, maternal diabetes, fetal anaemia and congenital infections such as parvovirus/toxoplasmosis/rubella/CMV.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Subchorionic haemorrhage\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Subchorionic haemorrhage is defined as a collection of blood between the uterine wall and chorionic membrane, which can be concealed or revealed with bleeding from the cervix. It is detected via routine antenatal ultrasound scanning and is-associated with increased risk of miscarriage, pre-term labour, placental abruption and stillbirth.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Anencephaly\",\"e\":\"Anencephaly is described as a gross malformation of the brain and cranial vault with reduced or absent cerebrum and cerebellum, affecting 1 in 10 000 pregnancies. It is part of the neural tube defects, and is secondary to failure of neural tube closure in the 3rd to 4th week of development. It can be detected antenatally via an ultrasound (US) scan or an elevated maternal serum a-fetoprotein. It is non-compatible with life, given the severe abnormalities, even if born alive death in unavoidable. Folic acid is used pre-conception and antenatally to reduce the incidence of neural tube defects. Anencephaly is associated with other congenital malformations most commonly spina bifida, cleft lip/palate, gastroschisis and genitourinary anomalies. It is not associated with oesophageal atresia.\"}]},{\"t\":76,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 1-day-old newborn is noted to be in respiratory distress. Imaging reveals herniation of abdominal contents through the posterior aspect of the left hemidiaphragm. Which type of hernia does the child have?\",\"lead\":\"Which type of hernia does the child have?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amyand's hernia\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Amyand's hernia describes a condition in which the appendixis found in an inguinal hernia sac. It occurs in 1% of inguinal hernias and most commonly on the right side. It is usually discovered intra-operatively.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bochdalek's hernia\",\"e\":\"This is the correct answer. A Bochdalek hernia* is the most common form of congenital diaphragmatic hernia, occurring posterolaterally and usually on the left-hand side. It results from a defect in the posterior attachment of the diaphragm during its formation (8th week gestation). It is thought to be due to failure of the pleuroperitoneal membrane to close or failure of the muscle cells to migrate, making the diaphragm vulnerable and allowing protrusion of abdominal viscera into the thoracic cavity. This causes pulmonary hypoplasia and pulmonary hypertension. Congenital diaphragmatic hernias occur at a frequency of 1 in 2500 live births and may be more common in men. Infants affected present with cyanosis and respiratory distress usually in the first hour of life. Examination findings include:\\n• barrel chest\\n• scaphoid-shaped abdomen\\n• respiratory distress (cyanosis, grunting, retractions)\\n• poor air entry on auscultation of the ipsilateral side, medial displacement of heart sounds.\\n\\nDiagnosis occurs with a chest X-ray. Initial management requires endotracheal intubation and ventilation to support the respiratory system and passing a nasogastric tube to decompress the abdominal contents and thus reduce compression of the lung. Definitive repair is via surgery, usually within the first week of life, with reduction of the abdominal viscera and repair of the diaphragmatic defect. The other type of congenital diaphragmatic hernia is the Morgagni hernia that occurs anteriorly and is less severe. Use the '5 Bs' mnemonic to remember the differences between the two: Bochdalek hernias are Bad, Big. occur in Babies and are found at the Back of the chest (posterior).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hiatus hernia\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. A hiatus hernia, can be congenital or acquired and describes the protrusion of a portion of the stomach through the oesophageal hiatus. It only requires treatment if severe and patients can be asymptomatic or show signs of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Littre's hernia\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Littre's hernia is a hernia with a Meckel's diverticulum in the sac. They most commonly present in the inguinal region but can be found in femoral or umbilical hernias as well. They are more common in men and are usually right-sided. This is treated by hernia repair and excision of the Meckel's and adjacent ileum in the same procedure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Morgagni's hernia\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Morgagni's hernia is the second and less common form of congenital diaphragmatic hernia, that occurs through the foramen of Morgagni in the anterior of the chest, is right-sided and small. Most patients remain asymptomatic.\"}]},{\"t\":76,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A full-term baby boy is born with incomplete fusion of the embryonic endocardial cushions in the heart. He has a murmur present during the neonatal baby check. The baby is most likely to have which one of the following conditions?\",\"lead\":\"The baby is most likely to have which one of the following conditions?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"An atrioventricular septal defect\",\"e\":\"This is the correct answer. The endocardial cushions are masses of mesenchymal tissue that form in the atrioventricular and conotruncal regions of the bulbus cordis, one dorsal and one ventral. These grow towards each other and fuse to form the atrial/ventricular septa, the atrioventricular canal and valves. The septae form between the 27th to 37th day of development. Failure of the endocardial cushions to fuse gives rise to atrioventricular septal defects.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Coarctation of the aorta\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Coarctation of the aorta is a condition of narrowing of the aorta that usually occurs at the site of the ductus arteriosus. This may occur in isolation or with a ventricular septal defect and or a bicuspid aortic valve. Coarctation of the aorta is due to a localised medial thickening causing a localised constriction of the aorta and causes increased left ventricular wall stress to generate the increased pressure required and ventricular hypertrophy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary stenosis\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Pulmonary artery stenosis is usually caused by valvar stenosis. This leads to right ventricular hypertrophy due to the increased wall stress and maintenance of high pressures to maintain flow. Sometimes the stenosis is so severe that it appears almost atretic, in which case the ductus arteriosus forms the only access to the pulmonary circulation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tetralogy of Fallot\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Tetralogy of Fallot arises at the conotruncal region, secondary to unequal division of the conus resulting from anterior displacement of the conotruncal septum. Displacement of the septum leads to four abnormalities:\\n• pulmonary stenosis (= narrow right ventricular outflow)\\n• ventricular-septal defect\\n• overriding aorta\\n• right ventricular hypertrophy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transposition of the great arteries\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Transposition of the great arteries, the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease in neonates, describes a condition with ventriculoarterial discordance where the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary arteries from the left. It arises from a defect in the bending of the cardiac loop (from days 23-28), causing a reversal in sites. It is associated with high mortality if remains untreated.\"}]},{\"t\":76,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 3-day-old baby is noted to be coughing and choking any time she is fed. Her parents note that she turns blue during these episodes, and frequently vomits. Further investigations reveal she has a trachea-oesophageal fistula. From which embryological structure does the trachea develop?\",\"lead\":\"From which embryological structure does the trachea develop?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"St Foregut ectoderm\",\"e\":\"Ectoderm does not develop into the primitive foregut. Rather the ectoderm forms the skin cells of the epidermis, the nervous system, adrenal medulla and tooth enamel among other parts of the adult body.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Foregut mesoderm\",\"e\":\"The mesoderm does not form the foregut. It develops into the skeletal and muscular systems, the dermis of the skin and the reproductive system (aside from the germ cells).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Foregut endoderm\",\"e\":\"During the 4* gestational week the respiratory diverticulum appears as a ventral outgrowth of the foregut endoderm. As it grows, longitudinal folds form to become trachea-desophageal ridges that progress to allow separation of the trachea and oesophagus. Failure of this process can lead to trachea-oesophageal fistulas, which are associated with the VACTERL group+\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Midgut endoderm\",\"e\":\"The endoderm does form the midgut, but the trachea forms from the ventral outgrowth of the foregut endoderm\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hindgut mesoderm\",\"e\":\"The hindgut is not formed from the mesoderm, but the endoderm. The trachea develops from the foregut and not the hindgut\"}]},{\"t\":76,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"é 32-vear-old man is rushed to the Emergency Department after a diving accident. He was recovered from the water in respiratory arrest and was ventilated at the scene. He undergoes CT imaging, which shows cervical spine fractures.\\n\\nFrom which group of cervical roots does innervation of the diaphragm occur?\",\"lead\":\"From which group of cervical roots does innervation of the diaphragm occur?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"C2\",\"e\":\"C1 and C2 do not contribute to the innervation of the diaphragm. Complete transection of the spinal cord at C1/C2 would be fatal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"C2. C3, C4\",\"e\":\"C2 does not contribute to the phrenic nerve, which innervates the diaphragm. C3 and C4 both contribute, along with C5, at the cervical plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"C3, C4\",\"e\":\"C3 and C4 ventral rami are a major component of the phrenic nerve that innervates the diaphragm, but this is not the most accurate answer as C5 is also a contributor.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"C3. C4, C5\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve innervates the diaphragm. The embryology of the phrenic nerve is key in understanding clinical pathology of the diaphragm. The key structure in the embryology of the diaphragm is the septum transversum. This is an aggregation of cranial mesenchyme which becomes prominent as the most superior part of the embryonic mesenchyme around day 22 of fetal development. During head folding it is folded under the heart. During fetal development the posterior aspect develops faster than the anterior, resulting in an effective descent of the anterior structures. As the septum transversum descends it carries with it the precursor of the phrenic nerve formed by the ventral rami of the C3. C4 and C5 spinal nerves. A patient who has a fractured C3 C5 will require mechanical ventilation. Remember, C3. 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"C4. C5\",\"e\":\"A patient who has fractured C4 and C5 will have some loss of diaphragm function, and may require mechanical ventilation. The patient will also suffer from quadriplegia, and loss of bladder/bowel function. Lesions of the spinal cord below C5 will not affect the function of the diaphragm.\"}]},{\"t\":76,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old man presents to his General Practitioner (GP) feeling generally unwell after SCUBA diving. He complains of fatigue, joint pain and pruritus. He is considered to have decompression sickness and the GP wonders if this was accentuated by an undiagnosed patent foramen ovale of the heart. Which of the following statements regarding the embryology of the cardiac foramen ovale is the most accurate?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements regarding the embryology of the cardiac foramen ovale is the most accurate?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"During fetal development a patent foramen ovale is required to facilitate blood flow from the left atrium to the right atrium\",\"e\":\"During fetal development a patent foramen ovale is required so that blood can flow from the right to the left atrium. This provides oxygenated blood to the systemic circulation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"During fetal development a patent foramen ovale is required to facilitate blood flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle\",\"e\":\"Blood flow is facilitated from the right atrium to the left atrium. The foramen ovale is replaced by the interatrial septum at birth.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"At birth, elevated pressure within the left atrium forces the septum primum and secundum together, closing the foramen ovale\",\"e\":\"Left atrial pressure rises at birth because of the increasing amount of blood returning to the heart from the pulmonary circulation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"At birth, elevated pressure within the right atrium forces the septum primum and secundum together, closing the foramen ovale\",\"e\":\"The right atrial pressure actually drops, which facilitates the closure of the foramen ovale. This occurs because oxygen filling the alveoli causes pulmonary arterioles to open, reducing right atrial pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"During fetal development, oxygenated blood from the pulmonary veins crosses the patent foramen ovale to supply the systemic circulation\",\"e\":\"Oxygenated blood enters the heart from the pulmonary veins after birth. During development in the womb oxygenated blood from the inferior vena cava (IVC) crosses the patent foramen ovale to supply the systemic circulation.\"}]},{\"t\":76,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A 4-month-oid baby is brought to the Paediatric Emergency Department after his parents have become increasingly worried about him struggling to breathe. The paediatrician is concerned about a cardiac problem, and requests an echocardiogram. This scan reveals a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA).\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding the fetal ductus arteriosus?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding the fetal ductus arteriosus?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"In the developing fetus, the ductus arteriosus facilitates oxygenated blood flow from the left to the right atrium\",\"e\":\"It is the foramen ovale in the developing fetal heart that facilitates oxygenated blood flow from the right to the left atrium.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"in the retal heart the ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary artery to the aortic arch\",\"e\":\"In utero, the ductus arteriosus allows oxygenated blood to pass from the pulmonary artery to the aorta, and then to the fetal circulation. Fetal blood is oxygenated from the placenta via the umbilical arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In normal development, the ductus arteriosus closes completely within 12-24 h of birth\",\"e\":\"In normal development the ductus arteriosus narrows within 12-24 h, but does not completely close until 2-3 weeks of life. PDA can cause presentations similar to the scenario in the question within the first year of life. If left untreated it can reverse from a left-right shift to a right-left shunt, also known as Eisenmenger's syndrome. This can progress to pulmonary hypertension.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"In normal development the ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum flavum after: birth\",\"e\":\"The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum within three weeks after birth.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"In the fetal heart the ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary veins to the aortic arch\",\"e\":\"In the fetal heart the ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary artery, not the pulmonary veins, to the aortic arch.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"You have passed your MRCS part A and have taken a well-deserved skiing holiday. During the first day, you become tired on the slopes easily. Which one of the following is a normal physiological response to strenuous exercise?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a normal physiological response to strenuous exercise?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A fall in blood pressure „Cr\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A left shift in the oxygen dissociation curve\",\"e\":\"There will be a right-sided shift in oxygen dissociation curve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A rise in mixed venous blood O2 saturation\",\"e\":\"There is approximately a two-fold increase in oxygen consumption, therefore the mixed venous blood O2 saturation falls to 50%\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A rise in pa(CO=)\",\"e\":\"p. (COz) is often lowered in very strenuous exercise. The increase in ventilation is probably due to a neural drive to the respiratory centre, together with a voluntary drive to contracting muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tachycardia\",\"e\":\"This is a normal physiological response to exercise. The relationship between heart rate and O2 consumption is linear.\\n\\nThere will be a rise in blood pressure in response to exercises. The stroke volume can increase twofold, and the heart rate threefold.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"As part of the preoperative preparation before thoracic surgery, a 70-year-old woman undergoes a cardiopulmonary exercise test (CPET). She is found to have a VOmax of 17 ml kg-1 min-:. Which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"An anaerobic threshold of 12 ml kg-' min-with evidence of myocardial ischaemia suggests that she should be admitted to the Intensive Therapy Unit (ITU) in the postoperative period\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect, an admission to High Dependency Unit (HDU) would be more appropriate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anaerobic Threshold is reached at 50% of VO 2max\",\"e\":\"Anaerobic threshold is reached at 50% of VO2max is correct.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cardiomyopathy is an absolute contraindication to CPET\",\"e\":\"This is only a relative contraindication.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"She has a very high risk for postoperative complications\",\"e\":\"Very high risk is a value of VO2 max<10 ml kg-min-!, this patient's VO2 max reading is 17 ml kg-l min-i\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The VOrmax only predicts cardiorespiratory complications\",\"e\":\"The VOmax only predicts cardiorespiratory complications is incorrect as it aids prediction of mortality and morbidity. Cardiopulmonary exercise testing is a preoperative investigation that has been shown to be a useful predictor of mortality and morbidity. It has also been used to triage patients to the appropriate level of postoperative care with success. in a study of pulmonary resection for cancer, A VOmax of >20 ml kg-min-'suggested no increased risk of complications or death. One reading of <15 ml kg-' min-'suggested an increased risk of perioperative complications; <10 ml kg: min-'implied very high risk of postoperative complications. Lactate anaerobic threshold is usually calculated from the V-slope method. If the anaerobic threshold <11 ml kg-' min-then patients should be triaged to the ITU. If it is a reading of >11 ml kg-' min-with evidence of myocardial ischaemia or VE/VO2 > 35 ml kg-1 min-'then admission to the HDU is more appropriate.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"You are the orthopaedic senior house officer (SHO) on call and are paged to the Accident and Emergency Resuscitation Unit for a 'trauma call'. A 30-weeks-pregnant woman has been invo ved in a serious road traffic accident.\\n\\nHow does the 'physiological hypervolaemia' of pregnancy have clinical significance in the management of the severely injured pregnant woman?\",\"lead\":\"How does the 'physiological hypervolaemia' of pregnancy have clinical significance in the management of the severely injured pregnant woman?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It complicates the management of closed head injury\",\"e\":\"Physiological hypervolaemia secondary to pregnancy will not complicate management of head injury\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It increases the volume of blood loss to produce signs of shock\",\"e\":\"In late pregnancy, maternal blood volume increases by 50%. Haematocrit decreases to 30- 35%. After haemorrhage, pregnant women may lose 30% of their blood volume before exhibiting signs of hypovolaemic shock. Blood will be diverted away from the gravid uterus to the mother's circulation, therefore the fetus will be the first to suffer from the haemorrhage. Fluid replacement must therefore be aggressive and early. It is recommended when possible that the pregnant woman be positioned on her left side to prevent compression of the inferior vena cava by the uterus\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It reduces the need for blood transfusion\",\"e\":\"The haemodilution of hypervolaemia of pregnancy results in lower haemoglobin concentration, but overall red cell mass can actually be increased. Due to the decreased haemoglobin concentration a given volume of blood loss will result in a decreased loss of red cells. However as the patient's haemoglobin concentration may already be lower than a non-pregnant patient's she may still more readily become symptomatic and require a transfusion. This answer is therefore possibly correct, depending on the patient, but it is not always correct and therefore not 'the most correct answer available from these options\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It increases the risk of pulmonary oedema\",\"e\":\"It does not increase the risk of pulmonary oedema.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It reduces the volume of crystalloid required for resuscitation\",\"e\":\"It increases the required volume of crystalloid for resuscitation.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A patient receives too many infusions after an operation resulting in a 20% increase in his blood volume. They have become exceedingly oedematous and begin to have difficulty in breathing What is the physiological process that is most likely to correct this abnormality?\",\"lead\":\"They have become exceedingly oedematous and begin to have difficulty in breathing What is the physiological process that is most likely to correct this abnormality?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Reduced activity of arterial pressure sensors\",\"e\":\"This will not be helpful to correct hypervolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased activity of renal sympathetic nerves\",\"e\":\"This will not be contributory to alleviate the effects of hypervolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Aldosterone release\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone release would lead to water retention and so further complicate the situation. It would be useful in hypovolaemia not hypervolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) release\",\"e\":\"The atria contain granulated cells that release peptides, ANP, in response to stretch. ANP has several functions - it relaxes the peripheral vasculature, reduces aldosterone secretion and increases glomerular filtration rate. This would counteract the deleterious effects of hypervolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Venous dilatation\",\"e\":\"Although part of the compensatory response to hypervolaemia will be venous dilatation, this will not actually correct the underlying problem. ANP (released by atrial stretch) is the most important control and its actions to both relax peripheral vasculature and directly impede the release of aldosterone and increase glomerular filtration rate means that ANP is most likely to correct the underlying fluid overload.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"You have asked the medical registrar to review a patient on the surgical ward. They have persistent tachycardia and have an abnormal ECG. Phase O of the cardiac action potential relates to which one of the following options?\",\"lead\":\"Phase O of the cardiac action potential relates to which one of the following options?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Rapid efflux of potassium\",\"e\":\"I here is efflux of potassium in phase s as the cell repolarises.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Rapid influx of calcium\",\"e\":\"There is slow influx of calcium in phase 2, which balances the efflux of potassium, giving rise to the plateau phase.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Influx of potassium\",\"e\":\"There is slow movement of potassium in phase 4 to maintain the resting potential\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Rapid influx of sodium\",\"e\":\"This is correct - a rapid influx of sodium occurs in phase O (depolarisation) of the cardiac action potential:\\n• phase O: rapid sodium influx\\n• phase 1: efflux of potassium\\n• phase 2: slow influx of calcium\\n• phase 3: efflux of potassium\\n• phase 4: sodium/calcium efflux, potassium influx.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"None of the above\",\"e\":\"Rapid influx of sodium relates to phase O of the cardiac action potential.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A patient is undergoing exercise testing to assess their fitness prior to elective surgery. A treadmill is utilised for this purpose and various parameters are monitored Which one of the following is most likely to increase during exercise?\",\"lead\":\"A treadmill is utilised for this purpose and various parameters are monitored Which one of the following is most likely to increase during exercise?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Peripheral vascular resistance (\",\"e\":\"The fall in total peripheral resistance, is caused by dilatation of the blood vessels within the exercising muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary vascular resistance\",\"e\":\"The pulmonary vessels undergo passive dilatation as more blood flows into the pulmonary circulation. As a result, pulmonary vascular resistance decreases.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Stroke volume\",\"e\":\"The increase in cardiac output is due to an increase in both heart rate and stroke volume.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Diastolic pressure\",\"e\":\"Diastolic pressure is incorrect, as it either stays the same or decreases slightly in exercise due to a fall in total peripheral resistance\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Venous compliance\",\"e\":\"Decrease in venous compliance is caused by sympathetic stimulation, helping to maintain venous return and therefore ventricular filling during diastole.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A patient with known heart failure undergoes an emergency laparotomy. Several days post-operatively, they have difficulty breathing and have coarse crackles on auscultation of the chest.\\n\\nIn normal physiology, which one of the following is cardiac output decreased?\",\"lead\":\"In normal physiology, which one of the following is cardiac output decreased?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"During stimulation of sympathetic nerves to the heart:\",\"e\":\"This would increase cardiac output due to a rise in heart rate and stroke volume.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"On cutting the vagus supply to the heart\",\"e\":\"This would increase cardiac output by an increase in heart rate, as the vagus nerve provides an inhibitory effect.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"By increasing the end diastolic volume of the heart\",\"e\":\"if the end diastolic volume of the heart (pre-load) is increased, under normal physiological circumstances, cardiac output is increased by the Frank-Starling mechanism. The exception is in the failing heart where cardiac output actually falls.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"As a result of decreased pressure within the carotid s nus\",\"e\":\"If the carotid sinus pressure is reduced, the baroreceptors become inactive and lose their inhibitory effect on the vasomotor centre in the brainstem. The result is activation of the sympathetic nervous system. This activation produces a rise in heart rate, stroke volume. mean systemic filling pressure and venous return, leading to an increase in cardiac output and return of the mean arterial blood pressure to its original value.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Upon assuming the upright position\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output falls when one stands up due to the pooling of blood in the venous side of the circulation, which has a large capacitance. Stepping out of a hot bath exacerbates this pooling effect because superficial cutaneous veins dilate in response to heat, increasing their capacitance further. Under normal circumstances, activation of the baroreceptor reflex compensates to some degree, preventing syncope. However, in the elderly, or in patients on antihypertensives, inadequate compensation from the baroreceptor reflex may result in a vasovagal syncope or orthostatic hypotension.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A patient with massive haemorrhage is brought into A&E Resus. A grey cannula is placed in the antecubital fossa. Which one of the following best describes flow through a vessel or lumen?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describes flow through a vessel or lumen?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is inversely proportional to the pressure head of flow\",\"e\":\"Flow is directly proportional to the pressure gradient along the vessel.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is inversely proportional to the radius\",\"e\":\"Flow through a vessel is directly proportional to the radius, Hagen-Poiseuille law states that it is proportional to the fourth power of the radius.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is directly proportional to the length of the tube\",\"e\":\"Flow through a vessel is inversely proportional to the length of a tube; the shorter the tube. the faster the flow.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is directly proportional to the viscosity of blood passing through it\",\"e\":\"Flow is indirectly proportional to the viscosity; the thicker (more viscous) the fluid, the slower the flow.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is directly proportional to the fourth power of radius\",\"e\":\"According to Hagen-Poiseuille law, the radius of the tube is the most important determinant of flow through a blood vessel. Doubling the radius of the tube will lead to a 16-fold increase in flow at a constant pressure gradient. Hagen-Poiseuille law states that the flow through a vessel is:\\n• directly proportional to the pressure head of flow\\n• directly proportional to the fourth power of radius\\n• inversely proportional to the viscosity\\n• inversely proportional to the length of the tube.\\n\\nThe radius of the tube is therefore the most important determinant of flow through a blood vessel So, doubling the radius of the tube will lead to a 16-fold increase in flow at a constant pressure gradient. The implications of this are several fold\\n• First, owing to the fourth power effect on resistance and flow, active changes in radius constitute an extremely powerful mechanism for regulating both the local blood flow to a tissue and central arterial pressure. The arterioles are the main resistance vessels of the circulation and their radius can be actively controlled by the tension of smooth muscle within its wall.\\n\\nSecond, in terms of intravenous fluid replacement in hospital, flow is greater through a peripheral cannula than through central lines. The reason is that peripheral lines are short and wide (and therefore of lower resistance and higher flow) compared to central lines, which are long and possess a narrow lumen. A peripheral line is therefore preferential to a central line when urgent fluid resuscitation, or blood, is required.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A patient is found to have deranged biochemistry. They are complaining of palpitations, so an ECG is performed urgently on the ward. The plateau phase of the cardiac action potential is due to which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The plateau phase of the cardiac action potential is due to which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Magnesium influx\",\"e\":\"Magnesium, of which most is intracellular, has an effect on the movement of other cellular ions eg calcium and potassium, but does not directly affect the membrane potential.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Potassium efflux\",\"e\":\"Potassium efflux occurs in phase 3 as the cell repolarises\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Calcium influx\",\"e\":\"Calcium influx opposes the efflux of potassium so causing the constant membrane potential seen in the plateau phase (phase 2). The plateau phase of the action potential in cardiac muscle (principally due to calcium influx) maintains the membrane at a depolarised potential for as long as 500 ms. The result is that the cell membrane is refractory to further stimulation, allowing the impulse that originates in the sino-atrial node to propagate throughout the entire myocardium just once, thereby preventing re-entry arrhythmias. Cardiac action potential:\\n• phase O: rapid sodium influx\\n• phase 1: small efflux of potassium\\n• phase 2: slow influx of calcium\\n• phase 3: efflux of potassium\\n• phase 4: sodium/calcium efflux, potassium influx.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Chloride efflux\",\"e\":\"There is a small amount of chloride efflux, along with potassium, during phase 1.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sodium influx\",\"e\":\"Rapid sodium influx occurs during phase 0 - depolarisation.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A patient with known cardiovascular disease needs an elective laparotomy for low colorectal cancer. They undergo a pre-operative echo to help establish their risk. The ejection fraction is defined as which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The ejection fraction is defined as which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The ratio of the end diastolic volume to stroke volume\",\"e\":\"Ejection fraction is the stroke volume divided by the end diastolic volume.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The ratio of stroke volume to end diastolic volume\",\"e\":\"Ejection fraction is the stroke volume divided by the end diastolic volume. The volume of blood in the ventricles at the end of diastole is known as the end diastolic volume. As the ventricles contract during systole, the volume of blood ejected per beat is known as the stroke volume. The remaining volume in each ventricle is known as the end systolic volume and acts as a reserve which can be utilised to increase stroke volume in exercise. The fraction of end diastolic volume that is ejected is called the ejection fraction - usually equal to about 60%. The ejection fraction is often used clinically as an indirect index of contractility.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"End diastolic volume minus end systolic volume\",\"e\":\"End diastolic volume minus end systolic volume is the stroke volume.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"End systolic volume divided by stroke volume\",\"e\":\"Ejection fraction is the stroke volume divided by the end diastolic volume, not end systolic volume divided by stroke volume.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"End diastolic volume multiplied by stroke volume\",\"e\":\"Ejection fraction is the stroke volume divided by the end diastolic volume.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"The cardiac output is shared throughout the body. Some organs require different volumes of blood at different times. Which one of the following organs has the greatest blood flow per 100 g of tissue?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following organs has the greatest blood flow per 100 g of tissue?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Brain:\",\"e\":\"The brain has a blood flow rate of 54.0 ml/100 g/min blood flow and therefore the kidneys, liver and heart have a higher blood flow per 100 g tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Heart\",\"e\":\"The heart has an 84.0 ml/100 g/min blood flow and therefore the kidneys have a higher rate of flow.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Skin\",\"e\":\"The skin has a 12.80 ml/100 g/min blood flow and therefore the brain, liver, heart and kidneys have a higher rate of flow.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Liver\",\"e\":\"The liver has a 57.7 ml/100 g/min blood flow and therefore the heart and kidneys have a higher flow.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Kidneys\",\"e\":\"The kidneys have 420.0 ml/100 g/min blood flow.\\n\\nBlood flow in ml/100g/min Organ\\n\\nKidneys 420.0\\n\\n84.0 Heart\\n\\n57.7 Liver\\n\\nBrain 54.0\\n\\n12.8 Skin\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A patient with cardiovascular disease is being assessed for their risk in planned elective surgery. They have been referred to a consultant cardiologist The cardiac index is equal to which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"They have been referred to a consultant cardiologist The cardiac index is equal to which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Blood pressure x heart rate\",\"e\":\"Stroke volume x heart rate = cardiac output. Blood pressure = cardiac output x peripheral resistance.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cardiac output/body surface area\",\"e\":\"Cardiac index = cardiac output/body surface area. The cardiac index is a measure that allows cardiac output to be compared between patients of differing size for direct comparison. It is measured in L/min/m?, and usually ranges from 2.5-4.0. Stroke volume x heart rate is equal to the cardiac output, and cardiac output x peripheral resistance equals the blood pressure. Cardiac output/height and blood pressure x heart rate are not useful measurements.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cardiac output/height N\",\"e\":\"Cardiac index = cardiac output/body surface area rather than height.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cardiac output x peripheral resistance\",\"e\":\"Blood pressure = cardiac output x peripheral resistance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stroke volume x heart rate\",\"e\":\"Stroke volume x heart rate = cardiac output.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A patient is septic on the ward, they are attempting to increase their cardiac output. They are tachycardic, short of breath and pyrexial. Increased venous return to the heart is most likely to be caused by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Increased venous return to the heart is most likely to be caused by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Deep inspiration\",\"e\":\"Negative intra-thoracic pressure generated during inspiration causes increased venous return to the heart. Blood returns to the heart from the lower limbs via the action of the calf muscle pumps, valves in the veins of the leg, and the effect of negative intra-thoracic pressure generated during inspiration. Anything causing the intra-thoracic pressure to become less negative will decrease the venous return to the right atrium. Tension pneumothorax, positive pressure ventilation and forced expiration all cause this effect, and therefore reduce the venous return. Although hypovolaemia may cause vasoconstriction in an attempt to increase venous return, it is unlikely to increase above normal levels.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Forced expiration\",\"e\":\"Inspiration rather than expiration leads to increased venous return to the heart.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypovolaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypovolaemia may cause vasoconstriction in an attempt to increase venous return, but is unlikely to increase above normal levels.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Positive pressure ventilation\",\"e\":\"This will increase the intra-thoracic pressure and so reduce venous return to the heart.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tension pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"This will reduce venous return to the heart by increasing the intra-thoracic pressure. 1575\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"A patient with a known allergy to penicillin is given augmentin. They immediately develop a rash, become hypotensive and have facial swelling The largest contribution to systemic vascular resistance (SVR) regulation is made by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"They immediately develop a rash, become hypotensive and have facial swelling The largest contribution to systemic vascular resistance (SVR) regulation is made by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aortic valve\",\"e\":\"The aortic valve and large arteries do not affect SVR; it is contraction and relaxation of the smooth muscle of arterioles that contribute the most to SVR.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Great arteries\",\"e\":\"The large arteries do not affect SVR; it is contraction and relaxation of the smooth muscle of arterioles that contribute the most to SVR.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Arterioles\",\"e\":\"The arterioles are the vessels that regulate blood fiow. They have abundant smooth muscle in their walls, and so are important in the maintenance of SVR.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"RY Venules\",\"e\":\"It is contraction and relaxation of the smooth muscle of arterioles that contribute most to SVR, not the venous system.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Great veins\",\"e\":\"It is contraction and relaxation of the smooth muscle of arterioles that contribute most to SVR. not the venous system.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"A patient goes to Pre-assessment Clinic prior to routine surgery. The electrocardiogram (ECG) of a patient shows a prolonged PR interval and they are diagnosed with heart block. Which one of the following options is the normal duration of a PR interval?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is the normal duration of a PR interval?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"0.001-0.0025\",\"e\":\"This PR is very short. A short PR may indicate accessory bundle that depolarises the ventricle early, such as seen in Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"0.12-0.20 $\",\"e\":\"The PR interval is measured from the beginning of the P-wave to the beginning of the QRS complex. It is usually 0.12-0.20 s.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"0.25-0.35\",\"e\":\"The normal PR interval is 0.12-0.20 s. A prolonged PR indicates a first-degree heart block.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"035-0459 V\",\"e\":\"This PR interval is prolonged and may indicate a heart block.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"0.45-0.50\",\"e\":\"This PR interval is prolonged as a normal PR interval is 0.12-0.20 s:572\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"A patient has deranged biochemistry on the Ward and an electrocardiogram (ECG) is obtained to see if there is any risk to the heart. The ECG of a patient shows a prolonged QT interval.\\n\\nWhich one of the following represents a normal QT interval?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following represents a normal QT interval?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"0.60.5\",\"e\":\"This is a prolonged QT interval. QT prolongation is associated with hypokalaemia, hypocalcaemia, hypothermia, drug treatment, head injury, and the congenital long QT syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AM 0.20\",\"e\":\"This QT interval is short. Causes of a shortened QT interval include hypercalcaemia, digoxin or the genetic short QT syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"0.30\",\"e\":\"Again, 0.30 s is a short QT interval as normal QT interval is approximately 0.40 s. However, it is important to note that the QT interval varies based on the heart rate and various correction factors have been developed to correct the QT interval for the heart rate (QTC).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"O40 5\",\"e\":\"The QT interval is measured from the beginning of the QRS complex to the end of the T-wave. A normal QT interval is about 0.40 s. As the QT interval is affected by heart rate, it is usually corrected to a rate of 60 bpm to enable comparison, and is written as QTc.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"O.50s\",\"e\":\"The QT interval is usually about 6.40 s. Therefore, this GT interval is prolonged. It would be useful to review the patient's prescriptions as causative agents include amiodarone, tricyclic antidepressants, erythromycin, ketoconazole and quinine.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"A patient with chest pain following laparotomy has ST depression on his electrocardiogram (ECG). They are urgently referred to the cardiologists How long does the ST segment ordinarily last?\",\"lead\":\"They are urgently referred to the cardiologists How long does the ST segment ordinarily last?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"0.025\",\"e\":\"This option (0.02 s) is too short. Upward or downward displacement (ST segment elevation or ST segment depression) may indicate coronary ischaemia or myocardial infarction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"0.04\",\"e\":\"The ST segment is typically 0.08 s. Elevation or depression are classically associated with myocardial ischaemia. However, downsloping changes may also occur in left ventricular hypertrophy and in digoxin therapy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"0.08\",\"e\":\"The ST segment connects the QRS complex and the T-wave. This segment ordinarily lasts about 0.08 s and is usually level with the PR segment.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"0.16\",\"e\":\"The normal ST segment often merges with the T wave and therefore, soporation is often difficult.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"08s\",\"e\":\"This ST segment is long as it is usually 0.08 s: A saddle-shaped widespread ST elevation may indicate pericarditis.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"A patient is brought in following a collapse. No cause is established and an ECG is taken.\\n\\nAn independence of the P waves and the QRS complexes of the ECG indicates which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"An independence of the P waves and the QRS complexes of the ECG indicates which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A conduction block in the left bundle-branch\",\"e\":\"A conduction block in the left bundle-branch is known as left bundle-branch block (LBBB). It is most commonly associated with coronary artery disease and hypertensive heart disease. LBBB may also occur in dilated cardiomyopathy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A failure of the atrioventricular (AV) node to conduct\",\"e\":\"Failure of the arteriovenous (AV) node to conduct is describing heart block. Heart block can occur at any level of the electrical conduction system of the heart. Blocks that occur within the sinoatrial (SA) node are described as SA nodal blocks. Blocks that occur within the AV node are described as AV nodal blocks. Blocks that occur below the AV node are known as infra-Hisian blocks (named after the bundle of His). Clinically speaking, most of the important heart blocks are AV nodal blocks and infra-Hisian blocks. Complete failure of the AV node to conduct results in a third-degree heart block, also known as complete heart block or third-degree AV block. It is a defect of the electrical system of the heart, in which the impulse generated in the SA node does not propagate to the ventricles. Because the impulse is blocked, an accessory pacemaker below the level of the block will typically activate the ventricles. This is known as an escape rhythm. As this accessory pacemaker activates independently of the impulse generated at the SA node, two independent rhythms can be noted on the ECG. The first will activate the atria and create the P-waves, typically with a regular P to P interval. The second will activate the ventricles and produce the QRS complex, typically with a regular R to R interval. The PR interval will be variable, as the hallmark of complete heart block is that there is no apparent relationship between P waves and QRS complexes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"An early repolarisation of ventricular fibres\",\"e\":\"Early repolarisation of the ventricular fibres can appear as J-waves and ST segment elevation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Depression of the sinoatrial node\",\"e\":\"The SA node is found in the right atrium and initiates atrial depolarisation, manifesting as the P wave on the ECG. Ischaemia of the inferior myocardial wall can lead to SA node ischaemia and result in sinus bradycardia\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Slowing of conduction at the atrioventricular node\",\"e\":\"Slowing of the AV node to conduct can lead to first-degree heart block whereby there will be a constant, prolonged PR interval on ECG. There will be an association between P wave and QRS complexes.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"An athlete is exercising and they decide to sprint. Their heart rate increases. If the end-diastolic ventricular volumes are increased (within physiological limits), which one of the following will happen?\",\"lead\":\"If the end-diastolic ventricular volumes are increased (within physiological limits), which one of the following will happen?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cardiac output would be decreased\",\"e\":\"An increase in right atrial pressure, caused by an increase in venous pressure, causes increased right ventricular end-diastolic volume, increased right ventricular end-diasto ic pressure and increased stroke volume.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The force of cardiac contraction would be decreased\",\"e\":\"The force of contraction will be increased in response to stretching of the myocardium as per Starling's law.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The output of the right ventricle would exceed the output of the left ventricle\",\"e\":\"The output of the left ventricle exceeds the output of the right ventricle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The stroke volume would be increased\",\"e\":\"Increased end-diastolic volumes results in increased end-diastolic stretching of the cardiac muscle. If the stretch is within physiological limits the heart muscle will respond by increasing its contractile force and therefore also cardiac output - this is known as Starling's Law of the Heart. A supraphysiological stretch, however, will decrease contractility and results in decreased cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Venous pressure would be decreased\",\"e\":\"Venous pressure would be increased leading to an increase in right atrial pressure.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"The primary function of the skin circulation is to help maintain body temperature. Blood vessels constrict to prevent heat loss and dilate to facilitate transfer of heat from the body core to the body surface. What percentage of cardiac output is received by skin?\",\"lead\":\"What percentage of cardiac output is received by skin?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"5%\",\"e\":\"The skin receives about 5% of the cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"12%\",\"e\":\"The skin receives approximately 5% of cardiac output. The differences in oxygen concentrations in the arteries and veins supplying the skin is small. This indicates that a large part of the blood flow is non-nutrient flow.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"20%\",\"e\":\"The skin receives approximately 5% of cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"50%\",\"e\":\"As above, the skin does not receive anything near this amount - however the overall percentage would increase when body temperature is too high.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"70%\",\"e\":\"As above, the skin does not receive this level of cardiac output.\\n\\n1520\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with cor pulmonale. They have massive right ventricular hypertrophy Pulmonary vascular resistance increases during which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"They have massive right ventricular hypertrophy Pulmonary vascular resistance increases during which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PI At high altitude\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary vascular resistance increases at high altitude. Pulmonary arterioles constrict in response to hypoxia (known as the Euler-Liljestrand mechanism), to re-direct blood flow to well ventilated lung regions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"During space flight\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary vascular resistance during space flight would, if anything, decrease as blood flow becomes more uniform.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"On exercise\",\"e\":\"With exercise, pulmonary arterial pressure tends to rise causing recruitment and distension of pulmonary vessels leading to a fall in pulmonary vascular resistance.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"On inspiring 100% oxygen\",\"e\":\"Inspiring 100% oxygen would also cause the pulmonary vascular resistance to drop.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"With aminophylline\",\"e\":\"Aminophylline is a bronchodilator and lowers pulmonary vascular resistance.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient who underwent a heart transplant 7 days ago. The patient has had a routine recovery, with no complications. When assessing their pulse, at rest, what would you normally expect to find?\",\"lead\":\"When assessing their pulse, at rest, what would you normally expect to find?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atrial fibrillation rate 100 bpm (\",\"e\":\"Atrial fibrillation (AF) is the most common susta ned arrhythmia. AF is caused by multiple re-entrant circuits firing'/activating in the atrial myocardium. It is manifest clinically as an irregularly irregular rhythm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sinus rhythm rate 70 bpm\",\"e\":\"This patient would not have a normal rate as the vagus nerve is not able to slow down the rate in a transplanted heart and therefore the patient will have sinus tachycardia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sinus bradycardia rate 50 bpm\",\"e\":\"This patient will be tachycardic rather than bradycardic as the vagus nerve is unable to slow down the heart rate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sinus tachycardia rate 100 bpm\",\"e\":\"In a denervated or transplanted heart the vagus nerves have been severed and therefore do not exert a 'braking' effect on the cardiac rate. This leads to an elevated resting heart rate, with a sinus tachycardia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supraventricular tachycardia rate 120 bpm\",\"e\":\"Supraventricular tachycardias (SVT) arise from either the atria or sinoatrial node eg sinus tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, atrial flutter or atrial tachycardia. Alternatively, SVT may arise from the AV node through atrioventricular re-entrant tachycardia or atrioventricular nodal re-entrant tachycardia.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":54,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient who has had a postoperative myocardial infarction. He is complaining of severe pain in the left femur, having undergone a left femoral nailing. What effect is the pain most likely to have on myocardial blood flow?\",\"lead\":\"What effect is the pain most likely to have on myocardial blood flow?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased\",\"e\":\"Myocardial blood flow is 250 ml/min at rest, represents 5% of the cardiac output and is dependent on arterial pressure. Flow is only seen in diastole. Pain and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) reduce myocardial blood flow.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased in diastole N\",\"e\":\"Pain reduces myocardial blood flow, which is seen in diastole.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased in systole T\",\"e\":\"Myocardial perfusion occurs during diastole.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased throughout cardiac cycle\",\"e\":\"Pain is known to reduce myocardial blood flow.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"No effect\",\"e\":\"Pain is associated with a reduction in myocardial blood flow. 1591\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a woman patient who is in the Intensive Care Unit, 1 day post-operation, following an emergency thoracotomy for a stab wound to the left side of the chest. The patient has a raised cardiac output. Which one of the following can cause an increase in cardiac output?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following can cause an increase in cardiac output?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Late pregnancy\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate. The cardiac output increases during pregnancy and reaches its maximal for the pregnancy by approximately 20 weeks' gestation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Long-term habitation at altitude\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output is increased only short-term when arriving at altitude.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Rapid dysrhythmias\",\"e\":\"Rapid dysrhythmias are associated with reduced stroke volume, which results in reduced cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Standing from a lying position\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output is decreased on standing from a lying position.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Starvation\",\"e\":\"Eating increases cardiac output, while starvation decreases it.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"You are assessing a patient in the Emergency Department and note that he has raised jugulovenous pressure. On the chest X-ray, the cardiac shadow looks markedly enlarged and you suspect a degree of left ventricular failure. What normally increases jugulovenous pressure (JVP)?\",\"lead\":\"What normally increases jugulovenous pressure (JVP)?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased intrathoracic pressure\",\"e\":\"Decreased intrathoracic pressure results in increased compliance of the intrathoracic vena cava (ie the vena cava will dilate due to the intrathoracic pressure change). This will result in decreased central venous pressure and decreased JVP.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypovolaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypovolaemia results in decreased central venous pressure (CVP) and therefore decreased JVP.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inspiration\",\"e\":\"In inspiration the intrathoracic pressure decreases, so the JVP would drop as above. The exception to this Kussmaul sign - a paradoxical rise in JVP with inspiration due to impaired right ventricular filling. For example, cardiac tamponade or restriction pericarditis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"supers\",\"e\":\"Standing up from a sitting/lying position results in reduced venous return, therefore a reduced CVP and a drop in JVP.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior vena cava (SVC) obstruction\",\"e\":\"Superior vena cava obstruction will result in obstruction of venous return from the jugular veins into the superior vena cava and ultimately the heart. This obstruction results in increased pressure above the obstruction, and a raised JVP, JVP is a way of estimating right atrial pressure by observing the atrial pressure waveform in the neck veins. There is a continuous column of blood from right atrium to the jugular vein, and the height of this therefore provides an estimate of pressure. JVP will be affected by changes in central venous pressure (CVP), or by obstructions to jugular venous flow into the superior vena cava.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old lifelong smoker with known heart failure is admitted to the acute medical ward with shortness of breath and peripheral oedema. Your consultant asks you to note his jugular venous pressure and proceeds to quiz you on 'a'. 'C' and 'v\\\" waves. Which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"wave refers to atrial contraction\",\"e\":\"The 'a' wave corresponds to atrial contraction and is seen just before the carotid artery pulse.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"wave refers to closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves\",\"e\":\"The c' wave occurs when the ventricles begin to contract and is caused by bulging of the atrioventricular (AV) valves backwards towards the atria.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"v wave refers to a full atrium\",\"e\":\"The 'v' wave represents tricuspid closure and atrial filling.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"v wave occurs just before the carotid pulse\",\"e\":\"It is the 'a' wave that occurs just before the carotid pulse, the 'v' wave represents tricuspid closure and atrial filling.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"wave is elevated in atrial fibrillation\",\"e\":\"The absence of 'a' waves is a feature of atrial fibrillation because there is no co-ordinated atrial contraction. Summary of JVP waveform (in correct time-sequence order):\\n• 'a' wave = corresponds with atrial contraction\\n• 'c' wave = corresponds with right ventricular contraction and the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium\\n• 'x' descent = corresponds with rapid atrial filling due to atrial relaxation\\n• 'v' wave = corresponds with atrial filling against a closed tricuspid vaive\\n• 'y' descent = corresponds with the opening of the tricuspid valve and rapid emptying of blood into the right ventricle.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old lifelong smoker is admitted to the Critical Care Unit (CCU) with a history of shortness of breath and chest pain. His troponin is elevated and his electrocardiogram (ECG) reflects ischaemic changes. A diagnosis of myocardial infarct is made Which one of the following is correct regarding the coronary circulation?\",\"lead\":\"A diagnosis of myocardial infarct is made Which one of the following is correct regarding the coronary circulation?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is approximately 500ml/min at rest\",\"e\":\"Myocardial blood flow is approximately 250 ml/min at rest (which represents 5% of the cardiac output).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Autoregulatory mechanisms are not found as part of the mechanism to control coronary blood flow\",\"e\":\"Autoregulatory mechanisms are very important in matching metabolic demand of cardiac myocytes to blood flow. Additionally, coronary vessels have both a- and B-adrenergic. receptors so can respond to sympathetic/parasympathetic stimulation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The right coronary artery typically supplies a third of the blood to the left ventricular muscle\",\"e\":\"The right coronary artery supplies a third of the blood to the left ventricular muscle - the inferior wall.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is dependent on venous pressure\",\"e\":\"During systole the open aortic valve leaflets lie against the coronary artery openings preventing blood flow. In diastole the pressure within the aorta ensures back flow into the now open coronary arteries - the blood flow into the coronary arteries is therefore dependent on the arterial pressure not venous.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Occurs during systole\",\"e\":\"in diastole the pressure within the aorta ensures back flow into the now open coronary arteries - the blood flow into the coronary arteries is therefore only possible in diastole.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"You are undertaking your first session at the gym with your new personal trainer who is monitoring your heart rate while you pedal on the exercise bike. As you pedal harder, the rate inevitably increases. Regarding heart rate, which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding heart rate, which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreases on inspiration\",\"e\":\"Inspiration increases heart rate and decreases blood pressure via stretch receptors in the lung and the respiratory centre in the medulla. This matches increased blood flow with the renewal of oxygen-rich air in the lungs.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increases with pressure on the eyeball\",\"e\":\"The oculocardiac reflex (Aschner-Dagnini reflex) is a decrease in pulse rate associated with compression of the eyeball.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decreases with sleep\",\"e\":\"Heart rate is determined by the electrical discharge of the sinoatrial (SA) node and is normally 60-80/min. The SA node spontaneously fires at 140/min and resting parasympathetic activity (vagal tone) reduces this to approximately 70/min. During sleep vagal tone is increased and thus the heart rate is reduced. Reference: Elsenbruch S, Harnish MJ and Orr WC. Heart rate variability during waking and sleep in healthy males and females. Sleep Journal 1999;22;1067-21.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreases after a meal\",\"e\":\"After a meal heart rate would be expected to increase. One possible explanation for this is a response to the increased cardiac output required due to the increased metabolic demand (and therefore blood flow) to the digestive tract.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreases with pressure on the sinoatrial (SA) node\",\"e\":\"Baroreceptors in the carotid and aortic sinuses produce a reflex response to hypertension by inhibiting the SA node and decreasing the heart rate. Clinically this can be utilised by carotid sinus massage as a first step in managing superventricular tachycardias.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"Carotid sinus baroreceptors are located in the carotid sinus at the bifurcation of the external and internal carotids. Direct pressure in the area increases vagal tone and has use in managing supraventricular tachycardias. These baroreceptors are stimulated by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"These baroreceptors are stimulated by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased arterial p(Co,\",\"e\":\"Carotid sinus baroreceptors respond to stretch, not chemical composition. The carotid body is a chemoreceptor sensitive to changes in arterial p(CO2) and arterial p(O2) as well as temperature and pH. The main feedback of this is to the cardiorespiratory centres of the brain.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fall in arterial o(COz)\",\"e\":\"The carotid sinus are not chemoreceptors, the carotid sinus are baroreceptors.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anaemia\",\"e\":\"Carotid sinus baroreceptors respond to stretch, not the chemical composition of the blood.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decrease in pulse pressure\",\"e\":\"Pulse pressure is the difference between systolic and diastolic blood pressures. The most important feedback is the total amount of stretch applied to the respective arterial wall - a decrease in pulse pressure could be combined with an increase or decrease in total stretch (ie higher or lower blood pressure) and the baroreceptors may or may not respond to this.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rise in mean arterial pressure\",\"e\":\"Baroreceptors are pressure receptors, they respond to alterations in the stretch of the carotid sinus. They are found in the adventitia of the blood vessels and wall of the heart. The arterial baroreceptors are located in the carotid sinus and aortic arch. When the arterial blood pressure drops, the arterial wall is subjected to less stretch, and the sensory nerves coming from the carotid sinus (sinus nerve) and from the aortic arch (depressor nerve) become less active and send fewer impulses. Upon receiving fewer impulses from the baroreceptors, the cardiovascular centres respond by exciting the sympathetic and inhibiting the parasympathetic nervous systems. This leads to an increase in heart rate and stroke volume, and generalised constriction of arterioles (not brain or heart) and veins. On the venous side, receptors are found in the wall of the left and right atria, at the entrances of the inferior vena cava and superior vena cava and in the pulmonary circulation.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":72,\"stem\":\"You are participating in your hospital's annual medics versus surgeons five-a-side footbail game. As you sprint for the ball, you notice your heart rate increasing. Which one of the following is most associated with increased cardiac output as a result of exercise?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most associated with increased cardiac output as a result of exercise?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Raised central venous pressure (CVP)\",\"e\":\"During vigorous exercise although venous return will be increased, the Starling mechanism of the heart ensures that venous return is matched with cardiac output so CVP should not change\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increase in end-systolic volume\",\"e\":\"In exercise, the stroke volume increases, with a decrease in end-systolic volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A small increase in end-diastolic volume\",\"e\":\"With the increase in heart rate, the end-diastolic volume may decrease slightly as both the absolute amount of time spent by the heart in diastole is decreased, as is the proportion of time spent in diastole compared with systole leaving less time for cardiac filling. Over time if the heart is unable to compensate for decreased cardiac filling time preload and end-diastolic volume can then increase, but this is not the most accurate answer to the question given it is unlikely to be the case in a healthy person exercising.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decrease in stroke volume\",\"e\":\"During exercise cardiac output will increase, to achieve this both stroke volume and heart rate will increase. Cardiac output = Heart rate x Stroke volume.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased myocardial contractility\",\"e\":\"Myocardial contractility will increase as a response to sympathetic stimulation - both by sympathetic innervation and due to circulating catecholamines. In addition, contractility increases when heart rate increases, known as the Bowditch effect\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"You are participating in your hospital's annual medics versus surgeons five-a-side football game. At the end of the match, your heart rate slowly recovers back to a resting rate. Which one of the following is associated with an increase in heart rate?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is associated with an increase in heart rate?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hyperthyroidism causes tachycardia, hypothyroidism results in bradycardia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intravenous adenosine\",\"e\":\"Adenosine results in bradycardia, clinically intravenous (IV) adenosine is used to terminate atrioventricular node-dependent tachycardias as its action is to reduce signal transduction through the arteriovenous (AV) node.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Verapamil\",\"e\":\"Verapamil is a calcium-channel blocker - its action on smooth muscle within vasculature results in smooth muscle relaxation and decreased blood pressure, its action on the AV node of the heart reduces the speed of signal transduction through the AV node and therefore slows the heart rate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Metronidazole\",\"e\":\"Metronidazole has no effect on the heart rate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Salbutamol\",\"e\":\"Salbutamol has B-adrenoceptor activity resulting in tachycardia.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old intravenous drug abuser is found unconscious at home lying on his left arm for an unknown period of time. He develops rhabdomyolysis, requires a surgical decompression and daily dialysis. A central line is inserted to monitor his intravascular fluid needs. Which one of the following is true of central venous pressure (CVP)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of central venous pressure (CVP)?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is affected by the Valsalva manoeuvre:\",\"e\":\"The Vaisalva manoeuvre raises CVP as the increased intrathoracic pressure impedes venous returns to the heart.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is reduced in right ventricular failure\",\"e\":\"The CVP reflects the pressure in the right atrium and so is elevated in right-sided heart failure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"is normal in septic shock\",\"e\":\"CVP is low in all types of shock either due to decreased circulating blood volume, decreased peripheral vascular resistance or both. The exception to this is cardiogenic shock in which poor cardiac function is the cause of shock, and CVP would be expected to rise.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Accurately reflects cardiac output\",\"e\":\"CVP does not directly reflect cardiac output. It can be clinically used as an indicator of intravascular fluid volume.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is raised in hypertension\",\"e\":\"arterial blood pressure and does not result in any change in CVP Hypertension is raised\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":79,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old obese man with known unstable angina is admitted to the coronary care unit following a myocardial infarction. Your consultant places a Swan-Ganz catheter. He asks what parameters the catheter can measure.\\n\\nWhich one of the following can be applied?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following can be applied?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Jugular venous pressure\",\"e\":\"A Swan-Ganz catheter is a balloon-tipped catheter that is floated through the right side of the heart into the pulmonary artery. It is therefore not in the correct place to measure jugular venous pressures.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary artery wedge pressure\",\"e\":\"A Swan-Ganz catheter is a balloon-tipped catheter that can be floated through the right side of the heart into the pulmonary artery+ It can be 'wedged' in the pulmonary artery when the balloon is inflated to measure the pulmonary artery wedge pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Venous return\",\"e\":\"Venous return cannot be directly measured, however, cardiac output can be calculated and (assuming a normally functioning heart) will approximate to venous return. The Swan-Ganz catheter tip comprises a thermistor, cardiac output can be calculated using the thermodilution principle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Left ventricular pressure\",\"e\":\"The measurement of pulmonary artery wedge pressure gives a good estimation of left atrial pressure, however it does not measure left ventricular pressures or aortic root pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Systemic vascular resistance\",\"e\":\"Systemic vascular resistance cannot be measured, however using the below equation it can be calculated using measurements from the catheter to determine cardiac output. Systemic vascular resistance = Blood pressure/Cardiac output\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":81,\"stem\":\"One of your patients on the Intensive Care Unit (ITU) has a Swan-Ganz catheter in situ. The patient's cardiac output has been calculated as 6 L/min.\\n\\nWhich one of the following factors increases cardiac output?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following factors increases cardiac output?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acidosis\",\"e\":\"Both acidosis and alkalosis decrease contractility, so decreasing stroke volume.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Histamine\",\"e\":\"Histamine causes reduced peripheral resistance (via vasodilatation and increased capillary permeability), resulting in increased cardiac output (see formula below). Systemic vascular resistance = ((mean arterial pressure - central venous pressure)/cardiac output) × 80.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Long-term acclimatisation at altitude\",\"e\":\"When high altitude is first attained, cardiac output increases to ensure adequate delivery of oxygen despite the lower partial pressure of oxygen in the environment. After this initial rise, cardiac output gradually returns to normal but haematocrit level increases to improve the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Rapid arrhythmias\",\"e\":\"At rapid heart rates the diastolic filling time is greatly reduced, so stroke volume and cardiac output decrease. Additionally, depending on the arrhythmia, disordered electrical activity may reduce the effectiveness of either atrial contractility and atrial output (eg in atrial fibrillation) so further reducing ventricular filling, or ventricular contractility so reducing ventricular output because of poor muscle pump function (rather than just alterations in filling).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Standing from a lying position\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output is decreased upon standing from a lying position as venous return to the heart is suddenly reduced. For all of these options it is vital to be aware of the following formula: Cardiac output = Heart rate x Stroke volume. So, increasing stroke volume or heart rate will result in increased cardiac output. As stroke volume is inextricably linked to venous return by Starling's law of the heart, an increase in venous return will also increase cardiac output.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":84,\"stem\":\"You are auscultating a patient's chest as part of their pre-operative assessment and their first heart sound is very prominent. Examination of the heart is otherwise normal. What does the first heart sound correspond to?\",\"lead\":\"What does the first heart sound correspond to?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The closure of the atrioventricular valves\",\"e\":\"The atrioventricular valves include the mitral valve and tricuspid valves. As the ventricles contract the pressure rises, when the pressure in the ventricle is higher than its respective atria this will force the atrioventricular valve closed\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The closure of the aortic valve\",\"e\":\"The second heart sound represents closure of the pulmonary and aortic valves - in the case of the aortic valve this occurs when the left ventricle relaxes and intraventricular pressure drops. When the pressure in the aorta exceeds the dropping intraventricular pressure the aortic valve will close.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The closure of the pulmonary valve\",\"e\":\"Closure of the pulmonary valve is part of the second heart sound.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The opening of the mitral and tricuspid valves\",\"e\":\"The mitral and tricuspid valves open in diastole when the relaxing ventricles' pressures have dropped below the pressure in the atria. The sound is not classically audible.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The opening of the pulmonary valve\",\"e\":\"The pulmonary valve opens in systole when pressure in the right ventricle exceeds the pressure in the pulmonary artery, It is not classically audible.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"You have a post-operative patient on the Intensive Care Unit who has had a Swan-Ganz catheter inserted peri-operatively. They steadily improve and become ready for the Ward after several days.\\n\\nWhat can directly be measured using the Swan-Ganz catheter?\",\"lead\":\"What can directly be measured using the Swan-Ganz catheter?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cardiac index\",\"e\":\"Cardiac index = cardiac output/body surface area. This formula allows comparison of cardiac function corrected for size of the individual. It cannot be directly measured by a Swan-Ganz catheter, however cardiac output can be derived using the Fick principle from Swan-Ganz measurements allowing calculation of cardiac index.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"End-tidal CO2:\",\"e\":\"This cannot be measured by a Swan-Ganz catheter.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"FiOz\",\"e\":\"This is the concentration of inspired oxygen. This cannot be measured by a Swan-Ganz catheter.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Left ventricular stroke work\",\"e\":\"Cardiac index and left ventricular stroke work can be derived from these measurements using the Fick principle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary artery occlusion pressure\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary artery+ occlusion (or wedge) pressure can be directly measured by Swan-Ganz catheterisation. A Swan-Ganz catheter has an inflatable balloon tip, this is floated into the pulmonary arterial system. The pressure measured by the catheter without the balloon tip inflated is the pulmonary artery pressure. When the balloon tip is inflated and wedged into a pulmonary artery branch the measurement is equivalent to the pulmonary vein pressure.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":88,\"stem\":\"One of your patients who underwent an abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) repair is on the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) and has a pulmonary artery occlusion pressure (PAOP) of 10 mmHg. They steadily begin to improve and become wardable after a week The PAOP most closely represents the pressure in which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"They steadily begin to improve and become wardable after a week The PAOP most closely represents the pressure in which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The aorta\",\"e\":\"This option is not correct.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The left atrium\",\"e\":\"When the pulmonary artery* is wedged and pressure measured on the venous side of the wedge, the pressure measured is equivalent to that in the pulmonary vein and therefore the left atrium.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The left ventricle\",\"e\":\"PAOP most closely represents the pressure in the left atrium. PAOP can be used to exclude cedema of cardiac origin. It may be useful in cardiogenic shock to allow more accurate fluid management. The pulmonary artery wedge pressure is elevated in cardiogenic shock.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The right atrium\",\"e\":\"PAOP most closely represents the left atrium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The right ventricle\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct response.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":90,\"stem\":\"You are treating a 20-year-old man, who weighs 70 kg. who has been stabbed in the right side of the chest; 1.5 L of blood has collected in the chest drain. He undergoes a resuscitative thoracotomy in the operating theatre. The normal adult blood volume is approximately what percentage of the body weight?\",\"lead\":\"The normal adult blood volume is approximately what percentage of the body weight?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"3%\",\"e\":\"The correct answer is 7%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"4%\",\"e\":\"The correct answer is higher than this.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"7%\",\"e\":\"Haemorrhage is defined as an acute loss of circulating blood. The normal adult blood volume is approximately 7% of body weight (a 70-kg man has a 5-litre circulating blood volume). and in children it is approximately 8-9% of their body weight. The blood volume of obese patients is estimated using their ideal body weight, as their true weight gives an overestimation of blood volume.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"9%\",\"e\":\"This is an incorrect response. Classes of haemorrhage in trauma: like a game of tennis (Love- 15-30-40)\\n\\n< 15% Up to 750 ml loss 0-15\\n\\n15-30% 15-30 800-1500 ml loss\\n\\n30-40 30-40% 1500-2000 ml loss\\n\\nGame over > 40% > 2000 ml loss\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"1%\",\"e\":\"The correct answer is less than this.*\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":93,\"stem\":\"An individual is exercising and their heart rate begins to rise. Starling's law of the heart describes the strength of the heart's systolic contraction. Which one of the following statements about Starling's law is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about Starling's law is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It describes how the cardiac output is matched to mean arterial pressure\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Starling's law describes how cardiac output is matched to venous return.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It explains how congestion in the left or right side of the heart is prevented\",\"e\":\"Starling described the intrinsic response of the heart to changes in right atrial and aortic pressure. With an increase in ventricular filling during diastole there is an increase in fibre length, which results in an increase in the force of contraction and stroke volume. This is an adaptation of stroke volume to venous return. As a consequence of Starling's law of the heart the outputs of the two ventricles are the same and so congestion is prevented in either the right or the left heart. An increase of venous return to the right heart will increase right ventricular stroke work by Starling's law of the heart and hence prevent right-sided congestion. The increase of right ventricular stroke work will therefore increase left atrial filling (assuming no changes to the pulmonary vascular circulation) and increase left ventricular end-diastolic volume and hence systolic stroke work to prevent left-sided congestion. All this assumes that the ventricles are operating on the 'ascending limb of the Starling curve, which is generally the case. With heart failure when the heart muscle pump is not functioning normally, or is overstretched and operating on the plateau or descending limb of the Starling curve. there is a risk of congestion as either or both ventricular outputs cannot match changes to preload.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It implies that the more the heart is filled during systole the greater the force of contraction during diastole\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect, the correct statement would be: It implies that the more the heart is filled during diastole, the greater the force of contraction during systole.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It implies that the more the heart is filled the lesser the volume of blood that will be ejected during systole\",\"e\":\"The opposite of this is true, it implies that the more the heart is filled the greater the volume of blood that will be ejected during systole.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It states that the energy or cardiac contraction is independent or the resting lengt of a cardiac muscle cell\",\"e\":\"It states that the energy of cardiac contraction is dependent on the resting length of the cardiac muscle cell.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":96,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department in haemorrhagic shock having sustained an open tibial fracture. He undergoes emergency external fixation on the trauma operating list. Which one of the following statements concerning blood volume is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements concerning blood volume is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is around 2.5 litres in a 70 kg man\",\"e\":\"Blood volume is around 5 litres in a typical 70 kg man.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is increased by diuretics\",\"e\":\"Diuretics decrease intravascular fluid volume, therefore they decrease total blood volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is reduced by renin\",\"e\":\"Renin is involved in the formation of angiotensin 2 and ultimately in the secretion of aldosterone. Aldosterone will increase the intravascular volume and so in turn the blood volume, through retention of sodium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It equals the volume of plasma plus the volume of red blood cells\",\"e\":\"Total blood volume = red cell volume + plasma volume.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Men have less blood volume than women\",\"e\":\"An average man has a higher blood volume than an average woman.* Classes of haemorrhage in trauma: like a game of tennis (Love-15-30-40).\\n\\nUp to 750 ml loss < 15% 0-15\\n\\n15-30 15-30% 800-1500 ml loss\\n\\n30-40 30-40% 1500-2000 ml loss\\n\\nGame over > 40% > 2000 ml loss\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":99,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old woman undergoes a transverse rectus abdominis myocutaneous (TRAM) breast reconstruction following mastectomy for breast cancer. The anaesthetist inserts a central venous pressure (CVP) line for monitoring. Which one of the following statements regarding CVP is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding CVP is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It indicates left ventricular filling pressure in left ventricular failure\",\"e\":\"CVP is not an indicator of left ventricular (LV) filling pressure:\\n• CVP is a measure of right atrial (filling) pressure as there is minimal vascular resistance between the internal jugular vein, or the inferior vena cava (IVC) and right atrium (and the right ventricle in diastole if the tricuspid valve is properly open).\\n• CVP under normal circumstances is an approximate measure of left atrial filling pressure because right atrial pressure is similar to left atrial pressure. However, left atrial pressure might be higher under some circumstances (eg mild LV failure). So CVP cannot be a reliable measure of left atrial pressure under all circumstances.\\n• If an actual pressure in the LA was required a wedge pressure would be an estimate (it is about 0.5-1 cmH20 greater, generally) using a Swan-Ganz catheter. This is done less and less and echocardiography now provides a surrogate approach.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"it is most useful when taken as a one off measurement\",\"e\":\"The zero measurement of CVP is taken at the level of the right atrium. This is somewhat arbitrary and so one-off measurements are not very useful unless there is a very low CVP indicating profound hypovolemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In normovolaemia after administration of a 200 ml bolus of colloid intravenously there will be a fall in CVP sustained for 10 min\",\"e\":\"It is more useful is to measure the changes in CVP occurring during and after the administration of a bolus of 200 ml of colloid intravenously, rather than a one-off reading:\\n• In hypovolemia, there will be only a very small transient change in CVP.\\n• In normovolaemia there will be a rise in CVP, which will be sustained for 10 minutes or so.\\n• In hypervolemia the rise in CVP will be sustained for longer.\\n\\nThis is because an administered colloid bolus will distribute itself in the circulation. As starting effective circulating volume is greater an administered colloid bolus will generate a more sustained rise of CVP as venous capacitance will be progressively attained.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It can be estimated by examining the internal jugular vein\",\"e\":\"Central* venous pressure (CVP) can be estimated by examining the height of the pulsation in the internal jugular vein. The height of the pulsation (in cm) should be compared with the angle of Louis as the patient sits at 45° to the horizontal.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is high in hypovolaemia\",\"e\":\"Central venous pressure (CVP) is a measure of right atrial filling pressure: In hypovolaemia the decreased circulating blood volume is low, therefore right atrial filling pressure is low and the CVP will correspondingly be low. A high CVP may indicate myocardial failure. Myocardial failure will result in less complete ejection and so end-diastolic volume will be increased - filling is compromised and so flow from the venous system through the atrium to the failed ventricle will be reduced as so blood will congest in the low pressure side of the circulation (veins and atrium) behind the failed ventricle.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"The lung has two orculations, the pulmonary circulation that perfuses the alveoli and the bronchial circulation that provides nutrients for the conducting airways: All vessels concerned with two circulations traverse the lung hila. What percentage of cardiac output is received by bronchial circulation?\",\"lead\":\"What percentage of cardiac output is received by bronchial circulation?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"2%\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"10%\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"20%\",\"e\":\"Bronchial arteries arise from branches of the aorta, intercostal, subclavian or internal mammary arteries. The bronchial arteries supply the tracheobronchial tree with both nutrients and Og. Vascular pressures in the bronchial circulation are similar to those in other systemic vascular beds.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"50%\",\"e\":\"About a third of the venous drainage from the bronchial circulation is via the azygos. haemiazygos and intercostales veins, which return bronchial venous blood to the right atrium. However, about two-thirds of bronchial capillary blood is thought to drain into anastomoses or communicating vessels that empty into the pulmonary veins. This vascular connection between the bronchial and pulmonary circulation is called the bronchopulmonary circulation. This communicating circulation adds a small volume of poorly oxygenated bronchial venous blood to the freshly oxygenated blood in the pulmonary vein.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"12%\",\"e\":\"Certain conditions, such as inflammatory conditions of the lung like chronic bronchitis, can result in this figure increasing to as high as 10%.\\n\\nThis is incorrect, the correct answer is 2%.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":105,\"stem\":\"Behind the carotid bifurcation lies the carotid body. The carotid sinus is separate to this and is usually located at the origin of the internal carotid artery. What stimulates the carotid body?\",\"lead\":\"What stimulates the carotid body?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A fall in arterial pa(COz) _ (I\",\"e\":\"The carotid body* (like the aortic body) is a peripheral chemoreceptor with an enormous blood flow: 2000 ml/100 g tissue per minute. It is stimulated chiefly by a drop in arterial Pa(Oz) or to a lesser degree a rise in arterial pa(CO2) or fall of pH. Stimulation of the carotid body results in a compensatory increase in ventilation. Partial pressure of oxygen is the primary stimulus of the carotid body.\\n\\nA rise in arterial pa(CO2) stimulates the carotid body\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A fall in arterial pa (02)\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A fall in blood pH\",\"e\":\"Changes in blood pH can also stimulate the carotid body. Oxygen partial pressure is, however, the main stimulus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A fall in metabolic ran\",\"e\":\"The carotid body does not directly measure metabolic rate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A fall in lactate level\",\"e\":\"A rise in lactate level will normally resuit in a fall of pH, which would stimulate the carotid body.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man presents with small bowel obstruction. His fluid management is proving difficult so you catheterise the patient and carefully monitor both their urine and nasogastric output each day.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true of extracellular fluid in comparison with intracellular fluid?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of extracellular fluid in comparison with intracellular fluid?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Greater volume\",\"e\":\"The intracellular component of body water accounts for about 40% of body weight, and the extracellular component 20%\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Higher chloride concentration.\",\"e\":\"Extracellular has higher sodium, chloride and bicarbonate levels when compared with intracellular fluid.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Higher potassium concentration\",\"e\":\"Intracellular fluid has higher concentrations of potassium, phosphate, protein and magnesium\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Higher protein concentration\",\"e\":\"More proteins are present intracellularly when compared with the extracellular space\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lower pH\",\"e\":\"This is not true, the two should be approximately the same. In health, the intracellular and extracellular pH are maintained at very constant levels: pH 7.40 = 0.05. 1035\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":109,\"stem\":\"Nitric oxide (NO) is an important cellular signalling molecule. It is involved in several pathological processes.\\n\\nWhich of the following occurs as a consequence of its release?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following occurs as a consequence of its release?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bronchial smooth muscle relaxation\",\"e\":\"Inhaled nitric oxide can be used in pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure, the relaxation of the bronchial smooth muscle results in lowered pulmonary vascular resistance. Nitric oxide is a molecule with broad and diverse effects. It was first described in 1980 as a product of endothelial cells that causes vasorelaxation. This product was called endothelium-derived relaxing factor (EDRF) and was eventually shown to be nitric oxide. Nitric oxide is known to be produced by many cell types and to exert a wide range of biological effects. The physiological actions of nitric oxide include relaxation of gastrointestinal smooth muscle and bronchial smooth muscle, maintenance of vascular integrity, and inhibition of smooth muscle migration and proliferation. Endothelial nitric oxide also plays a critical role in haemostasis, making an important contribution to the normal inhibition of platelet function. Inhibition of platelet adhesion is a property of nitric oxide Nitric oxide is also an important determinant of blood cell flow and is thought to be involved in immune and inflammatory responses via its production in macrophages. lymphocytes and neutrophils. Nitric oxide is synthesised by a family of enzymes, the nitric oxide synthetases.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastrointestinal smooth muscle contraction\",\"e\":\"Nitric oxide results in gastrointestinal smooth muscle relaxation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Platelet aggregation\",\"e\":\"Nitric oxide inhibits platelet aggregation\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Smooth muscle proliferation\",\"e\":\"Nitric oxide inhibits smooth muscle proliferation and migration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vasoconstriction of smooth muscle\",\"e\":\"Nitric oxide results in relaxation of gastrointestinal and bronchial smooth muscle, it is also a vasodilator.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":112,\"stem\":\"A patient with haemorrhagic shock arrives in A&E resus, and requires a fluid challenge. The nurse puts up 5% dextrose, which is the first bag they found. The consultant asks them for a different crystalloid for this patient.\\n\\nHow much of 1 litre of 5% dextrose infused intravenously will remain in the intravascular compartment?\",\"lead\":\"How much of 1 litre of 5% dextrose infused intravenously will remain in the intravascular compartment?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"250 ml\",\"e\":\"As previously stated, the volume is less.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"400 ml\",\"e\":\"The volume is less than this.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"100 ml\",\"e\":\"One-third of total body water is extracellular and two-thirds intracellular. Of the extracellular fluid volume, only one-quarter of extracellular fluid is intravascular and therefore only one-twelfth (one-third x one-quarter) of infused 5% dextrose will remain in the intravascular space. In comparison, one-quarter of 0.9% saline will remain in the intravascular space as it contains 154 mmol/l of Na+, which is similar to the concentration of Na+ found in the extracellular compartment.\\n\\n5% dextrose has no oncotic properties (the dextrose is absorbed) and therefore 1 litre of 5% dextrose will be distributed equally among the total body water. The amount remaining intravascularly then is entirely dependent on the total intravascular volume compared with the total body water volume.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"RY V 500 ml\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"333.33 ml\",\"e\":\"Dextrose does not stay in the intravascular space particularly well, therefore it is not a useful fluid for resuscitation of shocked patients.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":114,\"stem\":\"You are resuscitating a patient with sepsis. They are profoundly shocked and you think a fluid challenge will improve their physiological parameters. The infusion of 1 litre of which one of the following solutions will initially lead to the greatest rise in intravascular interstitial fluid volume?\",\"lead\":\"The infusion of 1 litre of which one of the following solutions will initially lead to the greatest rise in intravascular interstitial fluid volume?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gelatin colloid solution (eg Gelofusin® or Haemaccels)\",\"e\":\"Colloids may be natural (eg blood, human albumin and gelatins) or synthetic (eg dextrans) They comprise large branching molecules with molecular weights in excess of 30 000 which exert oncotic properties. Assuming intact capillary integrity, these large molecules are confined to the intravascular volume and the volume effects of colloid infusion are, at least initially, therefore confined to the plasma compartment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypertonic NaCl\",\"e\":\"Crystalloids such as NaCl solution, pass more readily from the plasma fluid compartment into the rest of the extracellular fluid compartment. Whereas the sodium content in 0.9% NaCi is approximately isotonic and confines the infused volume to the extracellular fluid compartment, hypertonic NaCI will have an even greater effect than normal NaCl, as hypertonic solutions will draw additional water from the intracellular fluid compartment by osmosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Normal (0.9 %) Naci\",\"e\":\"Crystalloids such as Nac! solution pass more readily from the plasma fluid compartment into the rest of the extracellular fluid compartment. The sodium content in 0.9% NaCl is approximately isotonic and in theory confines the entire fluid volume to the extracellular fluid compartment.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"5% dextrose solution\",\"e\":\"Dextrose is very quickly absorbed/metabolised, so a 5% dextrose solution rapidly acts like pure water and becomes distributed among the total body water volume as it has no oncotic properties.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pure water\",\"e\":\"Pure water will be immediately distributed among the total body water volume as it has no oncotic properties.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":117,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old trauma patient is brought in following a skiing accident. They are unresponsive on arrival with an obvious open fracture. They are profoundly hypothermic. Which ECG feature is most likely to be present?\",\"lead\":\"Which ECG feature is most likely to be present?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Reduced RR interval\",\"e\":\"A reduction in the RR interval is an ECG feature of hyperthermia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Reduced PR interval\",\"e\":\"Reduced PR interval may be found in Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome, junctional rhythms and Lown-Ganong-Levine syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tachycardia\",\"e\":\"Tachycardia, much like a reduction in the RR interval, is an ECG feature of hyperthermia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"u waves 2\",\"e\":\"The U-wave may be present on the ECG in hypokalaemia and is an additional upward peak that follows the T-wave.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"J waves\",\"e\":\"The J-wave may be present on the ECG in patients with hypothermia and is an additional upward peak immediately following the QRS complex.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":119,\"stem\":\"QQ Eee\\n\\nYou are reviewing a man with significant early arterial disease. The brachial artery response post-occlusion is severely diminished and confirms a diagnosis of endothelial dysfunction\\n\\nThe successful production of which compound is best linked to vascular smooth muscle cell relaxation?\",\"lead\":\"The successful production of which compound is best linked to vascular smooth muscle cell relaxation?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Angiotensin I!\",\"e\":\"Angiotensin Il is part of the body's response to hypovolaemia in the renin-angiotensin system - it is a vasoconstrictor.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Nitrous oxide\",\"e\":\"Nitrous oxide (not to be confused with nitric oxide - NO) has a chemical formula of NaO. It does have medical uses as an anaesthetic and analgesic, it is also known as laughing gas' - it is not primarily known as a vasoactive agent.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Endothelin\",\"e\":\"Endothelin i is a vasoconstrictive agent produced by endothelial cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Nitric oxide\",\"e\":\"Nitric oxide (NO) is a rapidly diffusible gas with a half-life of only a few seconds. It is formed in endothelial cells via the metabolism of arginine and catalysed by nitric oxide synthase (NOS). NO has immunological and inflammatory functions, and also anti-atherosclerotic, and anti-thrombotic effects. It also contributes to smooth muscle cell relaxation and the control of blood pressure. In this specific scenario impaired brachial artery relaxation after a period of occlusion is a sign of endothelial dysfunction and associated with increased cardiovascular risk. This, at least in part, is due to endothelial damage and reduced NO production; in turn, reduced NO production brings about an increased susceptibility to further endothelial damage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serotonin\",\"e\":\"Serotonin is a vasoconstrictive agent, it is released by platelets as part of the clot-forming response. Its vasoconstrictive effect improves haemostasis\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":121,\"stem\":\"You are prescribing a 2 unit red blood-cell transfusion for a patient. One of the medical students asks you about red blood cells.\\n\\nWhich of the following properties do these possess?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following properties do these possess?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are biconcave discs\",\"e\":\"Red blood cells (RBCs), erythrocytes, are biconcave discs of 7 microns in diameter. The shape allows a high surface area for diffusion (the surface area to volume ration of a biconcave disc is higher than, for example, a sphere).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They have a lifespan of 1 year\",\"e\":\"The average red-cell lifespan is approximately 120 days, after which they are removed from the circulation by phagocytosis in the liver, bone marrow and spleen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They have mitochondria. ™\",\"e\":\"They have no mitochondria, nor ability to metabolise aerobically.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They have nuclei\",\"e\":\"They have no nuclei, immature erythrocytes contain DNA but this is usually only found in the peripheral circulations in small amounts, they are called reticulocytes (<2% of total RBCs).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They metabolise aerobically\",\"e\":\"RBCs are only able to obtain energy through anaerobic metabolism of glucose.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":123,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman sustains a head injury and is brought in unconscious as a trauma call. CT imaging confirms a subdural haematoma Which one of the following statements is true regarding the cerebral blood flow (CBF)?\",\"lead\":\"CT imaging confirms a subdural haematoma Which one of the following statements is true regarding the cerebral blood flow (CBF)?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Accounts for about 15% of the cardiac output at rest\",\"e\":\"Cerebral blood flow accounts for about 15% of the cardiac output at rest, equivalent to around 700 ml/min. Autoregulatory mechanisms keep this relatively constant despite changes in blood pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"is decreased by hypercapnia\",\"e\":\"As part of the autoregulatory response of cerebral blood flow both hypercapnia and hypoxia will increase blood flow through cerebral vasodilation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is decreased by hypoxia\",\"e\":\"As part of the autoregulatory response of cerebral blood flow both hypercapnia and hypoxia will increase blood flow through cerebral vasodilation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is mainly controlled by sympathetic and parasympathetic activity\",\"e\":\"The main control of cerebral blood flow is via autoregulatory mechanisms, not by neuronal activity.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is decreased by isoflurane in general anaesthesia\",\"e\":\"Isoflurane increases CBF through cerebral vasodilation.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":125,\"stem\":\"The brain is the least tolerant of all the body organs to ischaemia. Interruption of cerebral blood flow for as little as five seconds may cause fainting (syncope), while even transient decreases in blood flow result in dizziness. What percentage of cardiac output is received by the brain?\",\"lead\":\"What percentage of cardiac output is received by the brain?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1%\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect, the correct answer is 15%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"15%\",\"e\":\"This is the correct answer. Cerebral blood flow is approximately 15% of resting cardiac output\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"35%\",\"e\":\"The degree of cardiac output is less than this\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"50%:\",\"e\":\"The brain comprises about 2% of the total body weight and receives 15% of the cardiac output at rest. This is equivalent to around 700 ml/min.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"75%\",\"e\":\"Cerebral blood flow is kept relatively constant despite changing perfusion pressures - this is important because the brain is encased in the rigid cranium and fluids (that comprise about 80% of all tissues) are incompressible. This means that the total volume within the cranial cavity must remain constant\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":127,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old man is admitted with a 24 h history of productive cough and fever. Observations are: temperature 39.1 °C, Sats 93% on room air, respiratory rate 25/min, heart rate 115/min, blood pressure 88/47 mmg. He is also drowsy, with confused speech. Glasgow Coma Score (GCS) is 13/15, E3 V4 M6. Which one of the following is most likely to increase cerebral blood flow in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most likely to increase cerebral blood flow in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A decrease in body temperature\",\"e\":\"Decreased body temperature results in decreased cerebral blood flow as well as decreased oxygen requirement.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An increase in p(O2)\",\"e\":\"A reduction in p(O2) would increase cerebral blood flow. This is through autoregulatory mechanisms that control the calibre of the arterioles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decrease in arterial p(CO2)\",\"e\":\"Increase in arterial p(CO2) would increase cerebral blood flow. This is through autoregulatory mechanisms that control the calibre of the arterioles.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Raised intracranial pressure\",\"e\":\"Raised intracranial pressure will reduce cerebral blood flow as the increased pressure within the fixed space of the cranial vault will result in compression of the vascular spaces and therefore increased vascular resistance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Noradrenaline\",\"e\":\"Noradrenaline would increase mean arterial blood pressure, in normal circumstances this would not be expected to alter cerebral blood flow. However, in a patient with very low blood pressure it may be of help. Cerebral perfusion pressure is calculated by the following: Mean arterial pressure - Intracranial pressure = Cerebral perfusion pressure. So, as intracranial pressure (ICP) is relatively constant (and in a patient with a stroke may even be abnormally high due to haemorrhage or oedema) if your mean arterial pressure drops your cerebral perfusion pressure will drop. At physiological ranges cerebral autoregulatory mechanisms should maintain cerebral blood flow approximately constant despite changes in perfusion pressure, however, should your perfusion pressure drop too far (or indeed become too high) autoregulatory mechanisms will fail. So, noradrenaline's action to raised mean arterial pressure (MAP) will allow cerebral blood flow to increase.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":130,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old man is admitted to the High Dependency Unit (HDU) with a history of sudden onset left-sided facial droop and limb weakness. A diagnosis of stroke is made. Which one of the following statements regarding cerebral blood flow is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding cerebral blood flow is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is supplied entirely by the carotid arterial system\",\"e\":\"Blood supply to the brain is by the internal carotid and vertebral arteries, which form the circle of Willis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is decreased as intracranial pressure increases\",\"e\":\"The brain lies with n the rigid skull. So an increase in the volume of the skull contents will increase the intracranial pressure, Cerebral perfusion pressure is proportional to the difference between mean arterial pressure and intracranial pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Averages approximately 1L per minute per 100 g of brain\",\"e\":\"Cerebral blood flow averages 50-100ml ml per min per g of brain. This is equivalent to approximately 700 ml/min or 15% of resting cardiac output on average.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is diminished with a loss of 10% of the circulating volume of blood in the normal young person\",\"e\":\"Cerebral blood flow is sustained in preference to other organs to which blood flow is diminished when there is loss of blood. Mean arterial pressure must decrease to less than 60 mmg before there is a decrease in cerebral blood flow. This does not occur until there is a loss of over 40% of the circulating volume in the normal individual (2 L in a 75 kg man).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increases at mean arterial pressures of less than 60 mmHg\",\"e\":\"At this pressure cerebral blood flow will become to drop.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":132,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman has a ruptured spleen following a RTA. She undergoes trauma laparotomy where a splenectomy is performed. The acute blood loss of 1.5 L leads to a decrease in which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"5 L leads to a decrease in which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The rate of oxygen extraction by the peripheral tissues\",\"e\":\"The rate of oxygen extraction by the peripheral tissues is increased in response to acute blood loss.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Renin secretion\",\"e\":\"Renin secretion is increased due to renal hypoperfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Platelet count\",\"e\":\"A reactive/secondary thrombocytosis is generated as a result of acute blood loss.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The cardiac output\",\"e\":\"A loss of circulating blood volume results in decreased central venous pressure and venous return. Cardiac output therefore also drops due to Starling's mechanism of the heart that ensures venous return matches cardiact output. Classes of haemorrhage in trauma: Like a game of tennis (Love-15-30-40):\\n\\n<15% Up to 750 ml loss 0-15\\n\\n15-30 15-30% 800-1500 ml loss\\n\\n30-40 30-40% 1500-2000 ml loss\\n\\nGame over >40% >2000 ml loss\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Coronary and cerebral blood flow due to sympathetic overactivity\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic activity has little role in the cerebral and coronary circulation, however, sympathetic activity elsewhere will cause widespread vasoconstriction in other organs (eg gastrointestinal tract, skeletal muscle, and renal circulation). This increase in peripheral vascular resistance preserves blood pressure and therefore coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure with the aim of keeping these two circulations relatively constant. As blood loss progresses this compensatory mechanism will eventually fail and blood flow will indeed drop, this will be in spite of rather than because of sympathetic activity though.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":135,\"stem\":\"A 53-year-old man undergoes coronary artery bypass grafting for left main stem atherosclerosis. During weaning from bypass he is noted to have a poor mean arterial pressure and requires cardiovascular support.\\n\\nWhich one of the following will act mainly to increase the coronary artery perfusion and increase systolic blood pressure?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following will act mainly to increase the coronary artery perfusion and increase systolic blood pressure?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Intra-aortic balloon pump\",\"e\":\"Aortic balloon pumps inflate during diastole, increasing coronary artery perfusion which only occurs in diastole. In addition, it deflates in systole increasing forwards blood flow by reducing afterload through a vacuum effect. The effect is to increase cardiac output and systolic blood pressure. It is commonly used in post cardiothoracic surgery and in weaning patients from cardiopulmonary bypass after continued perioperative injury to myocardial tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Noradrenaline\",\"e\":\"Catecholamines (including adrenaline, noradrenaline and dopamine) are secreted from the adrenal medulla. Peripheral vasoconstriction is largely mediated by alpha-1-receptors, and maximally stimulated by noradrenaline rather than adrenaline, so increasing systolic blood pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary artery (Swan-Ganz) catheter\",\"e\":\"A Swan-Ganz catheter can be used to measure right atrial, pulmonary artery, and pulmonary capillary wedge pressures and to determine card ac output and oxygen saturations in the right heart chambers.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Dopamine\",\"e\":\"Dopamine is one of the catecholamines (including adrenaline, noradrenaline and dopamine) and is secreted from the adrenal medulla. It acts at low doses on D1 and D2 receptors, resulting in inotropic effects. At medium doses on b-receptors with further inotropic effects. At higher doses it acts on alpha receptors resulting in vasoconstriction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Glyceryl trinitriate\",\"e\":\"Glyceryl trinitrate is a potent vasodilator and would reduce systemic vascular resistance and systolic blood pressure\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":138,\"stem\":\"You are playing football in your annual medics versus surgeons match, which has gone into extra time. As you struggle to keep up the pace of the match, you stop to consider your body's response to exertion\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true of Starling's law of the heart?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of Starling's law of the heart?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"States that the energy or cardiac contraction is directly proportional to the resting length of the cardiac muscle fibre\",\"e\":\"Although the energy of cardiac contraction is dependent on the resting length of the cardiac muscle fibre, this relationship is not directly proportional. As you increase the resting length of the muscle fibre the energy gained per unit length increase begins to decrease and eventually further length gain will actually decrease the energy of cardiac contraction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Means that the less the heart is filled during diastole the greater the force of contraction during systole to ensure that cardiac output is maintained\",\"e\":\"The opposite of this is true - the more that the heart is filled during diastole, the greater the muscle fibre stretch and therefore the greater the force of contraction. This ensures that cardiac output is matched to venous return.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Means that stroke volume is reduced during exercise\",\"e\":\"This is not true, stroke volume would increase.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Describes how the cardiac output is matched to venous return\",\"e\":\"Increased venous return results in increased ventricular filling. With an increase in ventricular filling during diastole there is an increase in fibre length, which results in an increase in the force of contraction and stroke volume - cardiac output will therefore also increase. This is an adaptation of stroke volume to venous return.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Explains the reason that heart rate increases during exercise\",\"e\":\"Starling described the intrinsic response of the cardiac muscle fibre contractility to alterations in muscle fibre length. Although heart rate does increase it is not a part of the heart's intrinsic response in Starling's law of the heart.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":140,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man, with no past medical history, attends a workplace health assessment and has an electrocardiogram (ECG) taken. Calibration: speed = 25 mm/s; chest/limb = 10 mm/mV.\\n\\n11\\n\\nITT\\n\\n701 25 20/600 AREA: 10 0/NY 160-0-18-1008X CL P? 202-210,0m0/MY PHLO\\n\\nSource: jcecho.org What is the heart rate in this ECG?\\n\\nSource: jcecho.org\\n\\nWhat is the heart rate in this ECG?\",\"lead\":\"What is the heart rate in this ECG?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"40 bpm\",\"e\":\"On a standard-calibration ECG trace, each large square' represents 200 ms (or 0.2 s). The number of 'large squares' between R waves is important to note. By dividing 300 by the number of large squares, the number of beats per minute is found. In this case, 300/3 = 100 bpm. A heart rate of 40 bpm would mean 7.5 large squares between R waves and would represent bradycardia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"50 bpm iN\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"60 bpm\",\"e\":\"On a standard-calibration ECG trace, each 'large square' represents 200 ms (or 0.2 s). The number of 'large squares' between R waves is important to note. By dividing 300 by the number of large squares, the number of beats per minute is found. In this case, 300/3 = 100 opm. A heart rate of 60 bpm would mean five large squares between R waves.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"100 bpm\",\"e\":\"On a standard-calibration ECG trace, each 'large square' represents 200 ms (or 0.2 s). The number of 'large squares' between R waves is important to note. By dividing 300 bv the number of large squares, the number of beats per minute is found In this case, 300/3 = 100 opm. There are several methods for calculating the heart rate from an ECG. The 300 method as described in the answers here works well for regular rhythms, but for irregular traces, often the 6-s method is used - in 6s (30 big boxes), count the number of R waves and multiply this by 10.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"150 bpm\",\"e\":\"On a standard-calibration ECG trace, each 'large square' represents 200 ms (or 0.2 s). The number of 'large squares' between R waves is important to note. By dividing 300 by the number of large squares, the number of beats per minute is found In this case, 300/3 = 100 bpm. A heart rate of 50 bpm would mean six large squares between R waves and would also represent bradycardia. In athletes, however, this can be a normal finding\\n\\nOn a standard-calibration ECG trace, each 'large square' represents 200 ms (or 0.2 s). The number of 'large squares' between R waves is important to note. By dividing 300 by the number of large squares, the number of beats per minute is found, in this case, 300/3 = 100 bpm. A heart rate of 150 bpm would mean only two large squares between R waves. A heart rate of 150 bpm would represent tachycardia.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":144,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old woman is on the Post-operative. Ward after having a dynamic hip screw repair of a fractured neck of femur 3 days ago. She is now complaining of shortness of breath and chest pain on the ward. The following electrocardiogram (ECG) is taken: Calibration: speed = 25 mm/s; chest/limb =10 mm/mv.\\n\\nSource: Ecglibrary.com\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis indicated by this ECG?\\n\\nSource: Ecglibrary.com What is the most likely diagnosis indicated by this ECG?\",\"lead\":\"com What is the most likely diagnosis indicated by this ECG?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atrial fibrillation\",\"e\":\"Atrial fibrillation is characterised by an irregular rhythm, no P waves and tachycardia (raised heart rate). While this rhythm is fast at 150 bpm, it is regular and there are discernible P waves, seen best in the chest leads.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anterior myocardie infarction\",\"e\":\"Acute anterior myocardial infarction is characterised by ST elevation in leads V1-V6, with hyperacute (peaked) T waves in these leads. ST elevation may extend to leads 1 and aVL. There may be reciprocal ST depression in leads Ill and aVF. The broad QRS complex left bundle branch block W and M notching pattern in the anterior and lateral leads, respectively. can also indicate an anterior myocardial infarction. None of those changes are seen on this ECG\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inferior myocardial infarction\",\"e\":\"The inferior aspect of the heart refers to the part that lies on the diaphragm. Inferior myocardial infarction is characterised on the ST segment elevation in leads II, III and aVF, with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL. There are none of these changes on this ECG.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pericarditis\",\"e\":\"Pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardium. Pericarditis results in widespread saddle-shaped ST elevation in the limb and chest leads, as well as PR segment depression. Tachycardia is also common. While the heart rate on this ECG is fast at 150 bpm, there are no elevated or depressed segments.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary embolism\",\"e\":\"This rhythm is sinus - a QRS complex follows a P wave and precedes a T wave - and it is tachycardic at 150 bpm. Sinus tachycardia is the most common ECG finding associated with pulmonary embolism. However, more specifically. this trace shows low-amplitude deflections and an 'S1Q3T3' pattern, with a prominent S wave in lead I, a Q wave and an inverted T wave in lead III. There is also T wave inversion in leads VI-V3. These changes are associated with a large pulmonary embolus.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":148,\"stem\":\"An 80-year-old man with a previous history of a cerebrovascular accident (CVA) attends the Pre-assessment Clinic for an inguinal hernia repair. The following electrocardiogram (ECG) was taken:\\n\\nCalibration: speed = 25 mm/s; chest/limb = 10mm/m.V.\\n\\nhus\\n\\n\\\"AA':. -\\n\\nSource: ecgquest.net\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis on the ECG?\\n\\n-.....\\n\\nSource: ecgquest.net\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis on the ECG?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis on the ECG?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atrial flutter\",\"e\":\"Atrial flutter is a rhythm that features flutter waves at a rate of around 300 (240-360) bpm that make the baseline resemble a saw-tooth pattern. The ventricular rate is often half this rate (150 bpm). which is known as 2: 1 conduction. Although this trace has a ventricular rate of 150 bpm, the baseline shows the fibrillation of the atrial, rather than the rhythmic contraction seen in atrial flutter.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Atrial fibrillation\",\"e\":\"Atrial fibrillation is characterised by an irregularly irregular ventricular rhythm, without P waves. Instead, there is fine or coarse fibrillation of the iso-electric line. This trace has exactly these features and is therefore the correct answer.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left bundle branch block\",\"e\":\"Left bundle branch block is caused by a conduction abnormality of the left bundle of His. It results in a broad QRS complex. Left bundle branch block also results in the S wave 'W' and the R wave 'M' notching pattern in the anterior and lateral leads, respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right bundle branch block.\",\"e\":\"Right bundle branch block is caused by a conduction abnormality of the right bundle of His. It results in a broad QRS complex. Right bundle branch block also results the R wave 'M' and the S wave 'W' notching pattern in the anterior and lateral leads, respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sinus tachycardia\",\"e\":\"In order for a rhythm to be sinus, there needs to be a P wave, followed by a QRS complex and a T wave. In this trace, there are no real P waves. The rate is approximately 75 bpm. which means it is not tachycardia.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":151,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old man attends the Pre-assessment Clinic before an elective total hip replacement. He admits to having palpitations. The following electrocardiogram (ECG) is taken:\\n\\nCalibration: speed = 25 mm/s; chest/limb = 10 mm/mV.\\n\\nSource: ecgquest.net What is the most likely diagnosis as indicated by this ECG?\\n\\nSource: ecgquest.net\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis as indicated by this ECG?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis as indicated by this ECG?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atrial flutter\",\"e\":\"Atrial flutter is a supraventricular tachycardia caused by a re-entry circuit within the right atrium. This ECG trace is regular (unlike atrial fibrillation) and the isolectric line has the saw-tooth appearance typical of atrial flutter. This trace shows flutter waves of 300 bpm, and there is a 2:1 conduction ratio, leading to a ventricular rhythm of 150 bpm\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Atrial fibrillation\",\"e\":\"Atrial fibrillation is characterised by an irregularly irregular ventricular rhythm, without P waves. Instead there is fine or coarse fibrillation of the isolectric line. This ECG trace is regular and has the saw-tooth appearance of atrial flutter, with a 2: 1 conduction 'block'\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lert bundle branch block\",\"e\":\"Left bundle branch block is caused by a conduction abnormality of the left bundle of His: It results in a broad QRS complex. Left bundle branch block also results in the S wave 'W' and the R wave 'M' notching pattern in the anterior and lateral leads, respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right bundle branch block\",\"e\":\"Right bundle branch block is caused by a conduction abnormality of the right bundle of His. It results in a broad QRS complex. Right bundle branch block also results in the R wave 'M and the S wave 'W' notching pattern in the anterior and lateral leads, respectively. These features are not seen in this trace\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sinus arrhythmia\",\"e\":\"in order for a rhythm to be in sinus rhythm, there needs to be a P wave, followed by a QRS complex and a T wave. Sinus arrhythmia, often seen in the young, resembles a normal sinus rhythm, except that there is beat-to-beat variation in the rate with respiration. This trace is regular, with no beat-to-beat variation in the ventricular rate. The ventricular rate is 150 bpm, which stays the same throughout the ECG trace.\"}]},{\"t\":77,\"p\":155,\"stem\":\"A 33-year-old man who is a cyclist and running enthusiast attends a 'health MOT' offered by his gym. He has no medical history and reports no symptoms. The following electrocardiogram (ECG) is taken:\\n\\nCalibration: speed = 25 mm/s; chest/limb = 10 mm/mV.\\n\\nV4 V.1. aVR:\\n\\nV2. aVL V5\\n\\naVF: УЗ.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis as indicated by the ECG?\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis as indicated by the ECG?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis as indicated by the ECG?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atrial fibrillation\",\"e\":\"Atrial fibrillation is characterised by an irregular rhythm, no P waves and tachycardia. This rhythm is slow at 38 bpm; it is regular and there are discernible single P waves.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Complete heart block (third-degree atrioventricular block)\",\"e\":\"In complete heart block, there is complete absence of atrioventricular node conduction - none of the supraventricular impulses are conducted to the ventricles. This leads to a dissociation of atrial and ventricular contraction. P waves are not reliably followed by QRS complexes. The QRS complexes that occur are broad complexes. None of these features are seen on this trace.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"First-degree heart block\",\"e\":\"First-degree heart block is proiongation of the P-R interval. This means an interval of > 200 ms (one large square). In this trace, the P-R interval is 180 ms:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mobitz type I second-degree block\",\"e\":\"Also known as Wenckebach, this is progressive prolongation of the PR interval, concluding in a non-conducted P wave - a missing QRS complex or a 'dropped beat'. In Mobitz type I, the PR interval is longest immediately before the dropped beat and the PR interval is shortest immediately after the dropped beat. On this ECG trace, the PR interval remains constant and every P wave is conducted to produce a QRS complex.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sinus bradycardia\",\"e\":\"The heart rate on this ECG is 38 bpm. Bradycardia like this can be seen in athletes and is benign. The athlete's heart is larger and considerably stronger than that of a less active person. This means that at rest the heart rate can decrease to 50 bpm or less and still maintain an adequate cardiac output.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man is brought to the Emergency Department after a road traffic accident. His primary survey reveals a patent airway and bilateral equal air entry on both sides of his chest. His observations are respiratory rate 26 breaths/min, heart rate 120 beats/min, blood pressure 90/70 mmHg\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely response of the body?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely response of the body?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased insulin release\",\"e\":\"In the ebb phase of metabolic response to injury. insulin levels are suppressed.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased vasopressin release\",\"e\":\"This patient is in a state of shock, so vasopressin levels (ADH) will be increased to conserve body fluid volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decreased cortisol release\",\"e\":\"The hormonal response to injury includes release of corticosteroids (a stress hormone).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"increased circulating To and T\",\"e\":\"Te and Ta levels reduce in the state of shock and injury.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased growth hormone\",\"e\":\"Growth hormone increases blood glucose levels. Its levels are raised in the ebb phase to increase glucose concentration.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 70 kg patient is admitted with haemorrhagic shock. Their urine output drops with an output of approximately 20ml per hour. Which one of the following is a stimulus for renin release?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a stimulus for renin release?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Isoflurane-induced hypotensive anaesthesia\",\"e\":\"The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system is not activated in patients who are induced with isoflurane and hypotensive anaesthesia is maintained. This approach is widely used to prevent haemorrhage during surgery and has been reported as safe.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Propranolol\",\"e\":\"Beta-blockers cause renal vasodilatation and do not induce renin production. Moreover the activation of the sympathetic system is a stimulus for renin release hence b-blockers are expected to block the sympathetic system and release of renin in turn.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"High Kt levels\",\"e\":\"The renin-aldosterone system plays an important role in the regulation of arterial pressure and sodium and potassium balance. The effect of plasma potassium on renin secretion is independent of aldosterone. Hyperkalaemia inhibits renin secretion, but stimulates aldosterone. Hypokalaemia stimulates renin and suppresses aldosterone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Angiotensin Ih\",\"e\":\"The angiotensin system is activated by renin, however renin is supressed by angiotensin I| and aldosterone through a negative feedback system.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Nat levels\",\"e\":\"Delivery of blood to the macula densa with low Na levels induces the production of renin.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A patient has compartment syndrome following a tibial nail for a fracture. They develop excruciating pain on the Ward which is not managed despite high doses of morphine via the PCA.\\n\\nWhich one of the following hormones is secreted by the kidney in direct response to sympathetic nervous stimulation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following hormones is secreted by the kidney in direct response to sympathetic nervous stimulation?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1 5% Aldosterone\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Angiotensin 1\",\"e\":\"Renin acts to cleave angiotensin I from angiotensinogen produced in the liver. Angiotensin I is not produced by direct stimulation by the sympathetic system.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Angiotensin II\",\"e\":\"Angiotensin-converting enzyme is present in many tissues, especially the lungs, and converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II is a powerful vasoconstrictor, causing vasoconstriction of the renal arteries, as well as a positive inotropic effect on the heart. Its activation depends on angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Erythropoietin\",\"e\":\"Erythropoietin is released by the kidney in response to hypoxia and high levels of the products of red cell breakdown, and increases the rate of red cell production.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Renin\",\"e\":\"Renin is produced by the juxtaglomerular apparatus of the kidney in response to hypovolaemia, via three mechanisms:\\n• increased catecholamine levels secondary to sympathetic stimulation from arterial receptors\\n• direct effect of hyponatraemia on the juxtaglomerular apparatus\\n• reduction of renal perfusion pressure via afferent arteriolar baroreceptors.\\n\\nAldosterone, which is released via stimulation by angiotensin II, conserves Nat and H2O by acting on the distal renal tubule and collecting ducts. Direct stimulation by the sympathetic system does not release aldosterone.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman presents with recent mood changes, weight gain, thinning of skin and amenorrhea. Examination reveals truncal obesity and a moon face.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely histopathological change?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely histopathological change?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypertrophy of the zona glomerulosa\",\"e\":\"The zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex is involved in production of aldosterone. Conn's syndrome results in hypertrophy of the zona glomerulosa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypertrophy of the zona fasciculata\",\"e\":\"The clinical picture is suggestive of Cushing's syndrome which can result from hypertrophy of the adrenal gland. The part of the gland involved in excessive production of cortisol is the zona fasciculata.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypertrophy of the zona reticularis\",\"e\":\"This zone produces sex hormones. Excessive production would lead to excessive androgen hormones. resulting in hirsutism in women and precocious puberty in men.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Atrophy of the adrenal medulla\",\"e\":\"The adrenal medulla is involved in production of adrenaline and noradrenaline: Phaeochromocytoma originating from the medullary region can cause episodic hypertension and palpitations.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Atrophy of the zona reticularis\",\"e\":\"Atrophy of this zone will cause deficiency of sex hormones.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with bitemporal hemianopia. On imaging, they are found to have a pituitary tumour and are considered for resectional trans-sphenoidal surgery. Which of the following hormones is synthesised in the hypothalamus and secreted from the posterior pituitary?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following hormones is synthesised in the hypothalamus and secreted from the posterior pituitary?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anti diuretic hormone (ADH)\",\"e\":\"Vasopressin (ADH) and oxytocin are synthesised in the hypothalamic nuclei and pass down axons to the posterior pituitary where they are secreted into the systemic circulation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH)\",\"e\":\"ACTH is produced in the anterior pituitary. CRH is secreted by the hypothalamus and acts on the anterior pituitary to release ACTH. ACTH then acts on adrenal gland to produce cortisol.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Corticotrophin releasing hormone (CRH)\",\"e\":\"CRH is secreted by the hypothalamus in response to neural stimuli, and drains into the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal vessels to the anterior pituitary. This in turn leads to the release of ACTH from the anterior pituitary.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thyrotrophin releasing hormone (TRH)\",\"e\":\"TRH is secreted by the hypothalamus in response to neural stimuli, and drains into the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal vessels to the anterior pituitary. This in turn leads to release of TSH from the anterior pituitary.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)\",\"e\":\"TSH is produced in the anterior pituitary, TRH is secreted by the hypothalamus and acts on the anterior pituitary to release TSH. TSH then acts on thyroid gland to produce Ts and T4.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A patient has a papillary carcinoma of their thyroid gland. They are being worked up for thyroidectomy and their post-operative long-term hormone replacement regimen is being considered\\n\\nWhat is the half life of free tri-iodothyronine (Tz) in the blood?\",\"lead\":\"What is the half life of free tri-iodothyronine (Tz) in the blood?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"5 min\",\"e\":\"The half-life of parathyroid hormone is approximately 5 min. It is useful to measure during parathyroidectomy as a drop in its concentration can confirm the removal of all hyper-functioning parathyroid tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"60-90 min\",\"e\":\"Calcitonin has a half-life of 60-90 min. Calcitonin can be used therapeutically for the treatment of hypercalcaemia or osteoporosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"1 day\",\"e\":\"The half-life of Ta is only 1 day. Ta is four times more active than the more abundant Ta. In patients with hypothyroidism or post thyroidectomy, Levothyroxine Ta is given as a replacement therapy. T; is responsible for most of the functions, but due to short half-life daily more frequent dosing would be required. Moreover, in cases of stress, increased dosage may be needed on a daily basis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"1 week\",\"e\":\"The half-life of Ta is 5-7 days. In peripheral tissues it acts as a reservoir for Ts.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"1-2 months\",\"e\":\"The average biological half-life of 131 is variable depending upon the thyroid status of the patient. In euthyroid patients it is about 66 days, while in hyperthyroid patients is about 38 days. It is important to know the half-life of 151, as patients requiring radioiodine scan for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes need to stay away from children and pregnant women for at least 2 weeks.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A patient with chronic pancreatitis has high blood sugars. They are found to have a degree of pancreatic failure. Which one of the following inhibits the release of insulin?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following inhibits the release of insulin?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amino acids\",\"e\":\"Insulin release is stimulated by amino acids, some fatty acids, and the gut hormones secretin and cholecystokinin.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"B-adrenergic stimulation\",\"e\":\"B-Adrenergic stimulation increases insulin levels.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoglycaemia\",\"e\":\"Low plasma glucose inhibits insulin secretion by pancreatic beta cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased plasma glucose\",\"e\":\"Glucose is the most important stimulus to insulin, causing its release from secretory granules in the B-cells of the pancreas, and release of newly synthesised insulin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagal nerve stimulation\",\"e\":\"Vagal stimulation also increases insulin levels.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing an insulin-dependent diabetic patient the night before their surgery. You decide the safest method for glycaemic control while they are starved is an insulin sliding scale:\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely action of insulin?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely action of insulin?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inhibition of gluconeogenesis\",\"e\":\"Insulin affects many organs and intracellular pathways. Insulin is synthesised by the islet B-cells of the pancreas and is an anabolic hormone. It stimulates glucose storage but inhibits glucose production.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inhibition of protein synthesis\",\"e\":\"Insulin enhances protein synthesis and inhibits proteolysis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inhibition of potassium entry into cells\",\"e\":\"Insulin causes an intracellular shift of K+. This reduces serum levels of K+. but not total body K+. Hence insulin is given with dextrose solution for acute management of hyperkalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Stimulation of glucagon release\",\"e\":\"Insulin is released in response to high serum glucose concentrations. High glucose levels supress glucagon release. Hence insulin and glucagon act in antagonism.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased uptake of glucose by brain tissue\",\"e\":\"The uptake of glucose by the brain is independent of insulin.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with unexplained change in blood pressure. On further questioning, they complain of intermittent episodes of sweating, palpitations and anxiety Which one the following is a probable finding in a patient with phaeochromocytoma?\",\"lead\":\"On further questioning, they complain of intermittent episodes of sweating, palpitations and anxiety Which one the following is a probable finding in a patient with phaeochromocytoma?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"St::Glycosuria\",\"e\":\"Hepatic glucose production increases therefore giving rise to hyperglycaemia and glycosuria.\\n\\nExcessive sympathetic discharge causes increased metabolism leading to weight loss.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Weight gain\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bradycardia\",\"e\":\"Tachycardia and paroxysmal hypertension are features of high circulating levels of adrenaline.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Paroxysmal hypotension\",\"e\":\"Tachycardia and paroxysmal hypertension are features of high circulating levels of adrenaline.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Bronchospasm\",\"e\":\"Adrenaline causes bronchodilation, hence bronchospasm is not the feature of phaeochromocytoma.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A patient is noted to have a high calcium when they are admitted. They are given fairly high volumes of intravenous (IV) fluids, and this improves over several days. Which one of the following is the most likely role of parathyroid hormone in calcium homeostasis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely role of parathyroid hormone in calcium homeostasis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Calcium absorption from the gut\",\"e\":\"Parathyroid hormone increases bone resorption, calcium reabsorption and calcium absorption.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased bone mineralisation\",\"e\":\"Parathyroid hormone releases calcium from the bone to maintain blood calcium levels. Hyperparathyroidism can cause osteitis fibrosa cystica (OFC).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased urinary excretion of calcium\",\"e\":\"Parathyroid hormone causes increased calcium reabsorption in the kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased loss through faces\",\"e\":\"Parathyroid hormone increases calcium reabsorption from the gut hence decreasing faecal excretion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stimulation of calcitonin\",\"e\":\"Parathyroid hormone and calcitonin are antagonistic. Calcitonin levels are suppressed by low calcium concentration. Calcitonin is used in the treatment of hypercalcaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man is brought the Emergency Department in shock following a trauma. They are acutely unwell with obvious derangement of their vital signs. Which part of the adrenal gland is least likely to be stimulated?\",\"lead\":\"Which part of the adrenal gland is least likely to be stimulated?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Zona glomerulosa\",\"e\":\"The outermost layer, the zona glomerulosa, is the site for production of mineralocorticoids, mainly aldosterone. In a state of shock, the renin, angiotensin and aldosterone system is activated to conserve total body fluid\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Zona fasciculata\",\"e\":\"The zona fasciculata is the layer situated between the glomerulosa and reticularis. This layer is responsible for producing glucocorticoids, such as cortisol. Glucocorticoids are an important part of the stress response to trauma\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Zona reticularis\",\"e\":\"The zona reticularis is the innermost cortical layer; the zona reticularis produces androgens, mainly dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA). These are anabolic hormones and are supressed during the stress response.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Adrenal medulla\",\"e\":\"The adrenal gland consists of two parts: the medulla and the cortex. The medulla secretes adrenaline and noradrenaline in response to stress. This reaction is rapid and generates the early part of stress response.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Chromaffin cells\",\"e\":\"Chromaffin cells are the cells of the adrenal medulla that secrete adrenaline and noradrenaline in response to nervous stimulation. Hence these are stimulated in a state of shock\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A patient sustains a neck of femur fracture. Post-operatively, and once they have recovered from undergoing a dynamic hip screw, they are found to have low levels of vitamin D. They are therefore started on adcal-D3 in order to address this and prevent further fractures.\\n\\nWhich one of the following hormones is involved in physiological hydroxylation of vitamin D3?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following hormones is involved in physiological hydroxylation of vitamin D3?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PTH-related protein (PTH-B) L\",\"e\":\"PTH-rP is produced by some of the carcinomas of lung, breast, and kidney. It binds to and activates the parathyroid hormone (PTH) receptor mimicking actions of PTH. However, PTH-rP is also present in the normal population, where its normal function is not known\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Calcitonin\",\"e\":\"Calcitonin is released by the parafollicular (C-cells on the thyroid) and reduces circulating calcium by opposing the effects of parathyroid hormone. It is not involved with hydroxylation of vitamin Ds-\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cortisol\",\"e\":\"Hypercortisolism is associated with increased chances of bone fracture. Effect is due to inhibiting of differentiation and apoptosis of osteoblasts in the bone. There is no intermediary activation of vitamin D\\n\\nHyperthyroidism causes decreased bone mineral density. This is due to the direct effect of TSH on receptors in the bone. Activation of vitamin D is not the intermediary step.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"PTH\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old intravenous drug user (IVDU) is admitted with reduced consciousness, severe sepsis and a reduced blood pressure requiring ionotropic support. They steadily improve following optimisation on the ITU Which one of the following would be the most likely finding in the acute phase?\",\"lead\":\"They steadily improve following optimisation on the ITU Which one of the following would be the most likely finding in the acute phase?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased aldosterone levels\",\"e\":\"In a state of shock, aldosterone levels are increased.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased renin levels\",\"e\":\"Shock leads to increase level of renin, which in turn activates angiotensin and in turn aldosterone. Renin secretion is increased by any stimuli that decreases extracellular fluid volume, blood pressure or increase of sympathetic output. Therefore, stimuli that increase renin secretion include: sodium depletion, cardiac failure, dehydration, constriction of the renal artery, hypotension, haemorrhage and an upright posture. Renin is responsible for the conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme is responsible for the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin I| (predominantly in the lungs). Renin is produced by the juxtaglomerular cells, which, together with the macula densa. constitute the juxtaglomerular apparatus\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decreased conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin I\",\"e\":\"By the action of renin, conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I is stimulated. This in turn causes increased conversion of angiotensin | to angiotensin II. This stimulates release of aldosterone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased antidiuretic hormone (ADH) release\",\"e\":\"ADH is released in response to hypotension. It acts on the collecting tubules to increase reabsorption of water.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased urinary sodium concentration\",\"e\":\"Although urine osmolality is increased due to decreased production of urine, urinary sodium concentration falls. This is due to the effects of renin, angiotensin and the aldosterone system that cause reabsorption of sodium.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old woman presents with renal stones and is found to be hypercalcaemic. On further investigation, imaging confirms a parathyroid adenoma. Which one of the following is the action of parathyroid hormone?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the action of parathyroid hormone?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Conversion of vitamin D to 25-hydroxycholecalciferol in the kidney\",\"e\":\"Vitamin D is produced in the skin by sunlight and is ingested in the diet. It first undergoes 25-hydroxylation in the liver and then la-hydroxylation in the kidney to form the active 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (1,25-DHCC). This last stage is under the control of parathyroid hormone (PTH) and phosphorus. A rise in PTH or a fall in serum phosphate increases 1,25- DHCC synthesis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased bone resorption\",\"e\":\"Parathyroid hormone acts on the gut (increases dietary absorption of calcium), bones (increases resorption) and kidneys (activates vitamin D) to increase calcium levels.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased renal excretion of calcium\",\"e\":\"Parathyroid hormone increases calcium resorption from kidneys through the action of vitamin D.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduced bone resorption\",\"e\":\"Calcitonin acts to oppose parathyroid hormone, and it therefore causes increased renal excretion and reduces bone resorption.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduced gastrointestinal absorption of calcium\",\"e\":\"Gastrointestinal absorption of calcium is increased.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old woman has been noted at the diabetes clinic to have increasingly poor glycaemic control despite metformin, gliciazide and dietary advice. It is decided to commence insulin to improve her glycaemic control. Which of the following cells are insulin dependent for facilitated diffusion of glucose?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following cells are insulin dependent for facilitated diffusion of glucose?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Brain cells\",\"e\":\"Insulin does not affect transport of glucose into brain cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cardiac muscle cells\",\"e\":\"Glucose will not diffuse through a cell membrane against a concentration gradient. Insulin facilitates the diffusion of glucose across many cell membranes including cardiac muscle cells, skeletal muscle cells, some smooth muscle cells and adipose tissue cells:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Large bowel mucosa\",\"e\":\"Insulin does not affect transport of glucose across large intestinal mucosa.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal tubular epithelium\",\"e\":\"Insulin does not affect transport of glucose into renal tubular epithelium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Small bowel mucosa\",\"e\":\"Insulin does not affect transport of glucose across small intestinal mucosa.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A 79-year-woman presents to the pre-admission clinic with untreated hypertension. After discussion with the Cardiology Department you start her on lisinopril. Which one of the following is the most likely action of this drug?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely action of this drug?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased excretion of ammonium\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased excretion of hydrogen.\",\"e\":\"Lisinopril is an ACE Inhibitor and leads a reduction in angiotensin Il and aldosterone. Aldosterone causes excretion of H+. Antagonism by lisinopril will lead to retention of H-ions\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"increased excretion of potassium\",\"e\":\"Lisinopril is an ACE Inhibitor and leads a reduction in angiotensin Il and aldosterone. Aldosterone causes excretion of K°. Antagonism by lisinopril will lead to hyperkalaemia. ACE I reduce the efferent renal tubular arteriolar pressure and reduce GFR which can also lead to reduction in K° excretion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased excretion of magnesium\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone increases excretion of magnesium. Lisinopril reduces aldosterone secretion by preventing angiotensin conversion. Hence magnesium excretion will be reduced. This medicine can cause hyper-magnesia and magnesium stone formation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increases excretion of sodium\",\"e\":\"The primary effects of aldosterone on the renal system are to increase reabsorption of sodium and increase excretion of potassium and hydrogen ions. Aldosterone acts by increasing the number and activity of the active transport systems in the distal renal tubule to sodium and potassium. Lisinopril by blocking these actions will lead to increased sodium excretion. Drugs with the suffix 'pril' are angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors - another example is ramipril.\\n\\nLisinopril is an angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) Inhibitor and therefore has no action on ammonium.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old woman is seen by her general practitioner (GP) and describes a history of lethargy, reduced appetite but increased weight over the previous 6 months. Biochemistry reveals she has hypothyroidism and treatment is commenced with thyroxine. In normal physiology which one of the following thyroid hormones has the most potent effect on metabolism of peripheral tissues?\",\"lead\":\"In normal physiology which one of the following thyroid hormones has the most potent effect on metabolism of peripheral tissues?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Calcitonin\",\"e\":\"Calcitonin is involved in the control of plasma calcium\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Tetraiodothyronine (T4)\",\"e\":\"T4 is less active than T3. Its half-life is about 2 weeks. Its concentration is more than T3. Essentially it serves as a reservoir for 13 and in peripheral tissues it is converted into T3 by deiodinase enzymes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)\",\"e\":\"TSH is released from the anterior pituitary gland and acts on the thyroid to produce T3 and T4, which act on peripheral tissues and in turn have a negative feedback effect on TSH production\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thyrotrophin - releasing hormone (TRH)\",\"e\":\"TRH from the hypothalamus releases TSH from the anterior pituitary gland.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Triiodothyronine (T3)\",\"e\":\"T3 is around three to five times more active on metabolism than T4: It has wide ranging effects on metabolism. This includes increased oxygen consumption in every tissue in the body and stimulation of protein synthesis. It affects both the synthesis and degradation of lipids, the net effect being a decrease in lipid stores. By influencing the mechanisms by which cholesterol is eliminated from the body, it decreases plasma cholesterol levels. Owing to its stimulatory effect on metabolic processes, it will also have the effect of increasing demand for coenzymes and vitamins.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"A patient is found to have high levels of aldosterone on a blood test. They are referred to the Endocrine Service who undertake further investigations. Which one of the following is the effect of hyperaldosteronism?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the effect of hyperaldosteronism?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"Hyperaldosteronism causes alkalosis due to excretion of H+ ions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemia\",\"e\":\"Hyperaldosteronism causes hypokalaemia due to excretion of K+ ions:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypotension\",\"e\":\"Hyperaldosteronism causes hypertension due to sodium and water retention.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypermagnesaemia/\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone promotes magnesium excretion, thus hyperaldosteronism may lead to hypomagnesemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemia\",\"e\":\"Excess secretion of aldosterone occurs in primary hyperaldosteronism and Conn syndrome. These are characterised by hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis. Aldosterone is a steroid hormone produced in the zona glomeruli adrenal cortex. Its main effect is to promote water and sodium resorption in response to diminished circulating volume. A decrease in circulating volume is detected by diminished blood flow and hypoxia at the juxtaglomerular apparatus of the nephron. This results in the release of renin into the bloodstream. Renin catalyses the transformation of angiotensinogen into angiotensin 1 in the kidney. This angiotensin I is then converted into angiotensin Il as it is transported through the lungs, by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). This angiotensin Il stimulates the adrenal cortex to secrete the mineralocorticoid aldosterone.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A patient is found to have high levels of aldosterone. On further investigation, their renin levels are also increased.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the site of renin production?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the site of renin production?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Collecting ducts\",\"e\":\"This is the site of action of aldosterone.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Proximal convoluted tubule\",\"e\":\"This is the site of action of carbonic anhydrase inhibitor diuretics.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Loop of henle\",\"e\":\"This is a site of action of loop diuretics (Furosemide)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Juxtaglomerular apparatus\",\"e\":\"The juxtaglomerular apparatus is formed of specialised juxtaglomerular cells in the wall of afferent arterioles and macula densa of the distal convoluted ducts. Renin secretion is stimulated here by reduced renal perfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Liver\",\"e\":\"This is a site of production of angiotensinogen. Angiotensinogen I is catalysed by renin to form angiotensin I, which is turn is catalysed by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) to form angiotensin II. Angiotensin has several functions that aim to increase blood pressure and restore renal perfusion. It causes vasoconstriction and stimulates the adrenal cortex to produce aldosterone. Aldosterone promotes renal reabsorption of sodium and water from the distal convoluted tubules and collecting ducts.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"A man is being considered for transgender surgery. They have completed hormone therapy and there is no contraindication to proceeding to surgery Which one of the following is the most likely site for the production of testosterone in men?\",\"lead\":\"They have completed hormone therapy and there is no contraindication to proceeding to surgery Which one of the following is the most likely site for the production of testosterone in men?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Interstitial cells in the testes\",\"e\":\"Interstitial cells of the testes, also known as Leydig cells, secrete testosterone when stimulated by luteinising hormone.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sertoli cells in the testes\",\"e\":\"The main function of the Sertoli cells is to nourish the developing sperm cells through the stages of spermatogenesis. However during fetal life, anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) is secreted by Sertoli cells that determines the male sex of the fetus. During puberty, inhibin and activins are released that regulate FSH secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Vas deferens\",\"e\":\"The vas deferens are a pair of coiled tubules that carry sperm from seminiferous tubules to the ejaculatory ducts.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prostate gland\",\"e\":\"The prostate gland does not produce any hormones; however it is dependent on androgen hormones for its growth and function. It produces prostatic fluid that mixes with the spermatozoa to increase fluidity and easy ejaculation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Adrenal gland\",\"e\":\"The androgens secreted in physiologically significant amounts by adrenal cortex are dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) and androstenedione, not testosterone:822\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman is seen for follow-up six weeks after hemithyroidectomy. Her blood results are as follows:\\n\\nNormal Value Investigation Result\\n\\n2.1 mU/l 0.4-4.0 mU/l TSH\\n\\n5.6 pmol/l 3:54-5.44 pmol/l T3\\n\\n110 nmol/l 64.4-154 nmo|/l\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely mechanism of production of Tz in the thyroid gland?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely mechanism of production of Tz in the thyroid gland?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Delodination ofTen. I\",\"e\":\"This is a mechanism of conversion of Ta to Ts in peripheral tissues by the action of deiodinase. Almost 85% of the circulating Ta is produced by this mechanism.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Coupling of mono-iodotyrosine (MIT) and diodotyrosine (DIT)\",\"e\":\"The thyroid gland also produces small amounts of Ta directly It is produced by the combination of mono- and diiodotyrosine.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Coupling of di-diodotyrosine (DIT)\",\"e\":\"Ta is formed by this mechanism. The combination of two DIT, leads to the production of Ta by the action of peroxidase.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Coupling of two mono-iodotyrosine (MIT)\",\"e\":\"Peroxidase enzyme in the thyroid gland cannot combine two MITS. Dilodotyrosine is directly produced by the process of organification that involves the combination of the la molecule with tyrosine.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Combination of a- and B-subunits\",\"e\":\"This is the mechanism of production of TSH in the pituitary gland. TSH is released once stimulated by thyrotropin releasing hormone (TRH) by the hypothalamus.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"A man is referred with hypogonadotrophism. A pituitary tumour is thought responsible and they are considered for surgery.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely function of luteinizing hormone (LH) in men?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely function of luteinizing hormone (LH) in men?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Promotion of spermatogenesis\",\"e\":\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and testosterone are required for spermatogenesis (division of spermatogonia to form spermatids) and spermiogenesis (maturation of spermatids to mature sperm):\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Stimulation of testosterone secretion\",\"e\":\"LH hormone is produced by the anterior pituitary once stimulated by gonadotrophin releasing hormone (GnRH). In men LH acts on the interstitial cells of the testes (Leydig cells) to secrete testosterone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Promotion of adrenal androgen secretion\",\"e\":\"Adrenal androgen secretion is not affected by luteinising hormone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Stimulation of Sertoli cells to produce inhibin\",\"e\":\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) stimulates Sertoli cells to produce androgen-binding proteins and inhibin\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Promotion of spermiogenesis\",\"e\":\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and testosterone are required for spermiogenesis (maturation of spermatids to mature sperm). The role of LH is indirect as it stimulates testosterone synthesis.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old postmenopausal woman has been prescribed calcitonin. Last year, she suffered a neck of femur fracture and required a total hip replacement. Which one of the following is the most likely mechanism of calcitonin in the prevention of bone fractures?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely mechanism of calcitonin in the prevention of bone fractures?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased calcium absorption from the intestine\",\"e\":\"Calcium absorption from GIT is increased by activated vitamin Ds. Vitamin Da activation is PTH dependent.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased calcium reabsorption from the kidneys\",\"e\":\"Vitamin Ds increases the reabsorption of calcium from kidneys.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Activation of vitamin D\",\"e\":\"PTH causes activation of vitamin D.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inhibition of osteoclasts\",\"e\":\"Calcitonin is produced by the parafollicular cells of the thyroid gland and inhibits bone resorption by inhibiting osteoclasts. It can reduce calcium levels in states of hypercalcemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Suppression of parathyroid hormone (PTH)\",\"e\":\"PTH secretion is dependent on blood calcium levels. Calcitonin does counteract the effects of PTH by inhibiting osteoclastic activity, however it does not supress PTH directly. 324\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man presents with significant endocrine disturbances. They are referred to the surgical team for consideration of removal of a neuroendocrine tumour. Which one of the following is the site of production of dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the site of production of dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA)?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adrenal medulla\",\"e\":\"Adrenal medulla produces adrenaline and noradrenaline\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sertoli cells\",\"e\":\"Sertoli cells are part of tubular interstitium that constitutes blood-testis barrier. These are not involved in production of sex hormones.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Leydig cells\",\"e\":\"Testosterone is the major sex hormone and is synthesised in the testes from a steroid precursor by Leydig cells and released into bloodstream.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Testicular endothelial cells\",\"e\":\"Testicular endothelial cells (present with in the testicular vessels) do not produce testosterone. These contribute to the establishment of the blood-testis barrier.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Adrenal cortex\",\"e\":\"The adrenal cortex produces dehydroepiandrosterone which is a 'male' sex hormone and is only produced in very small amounts in women. However, it is not the same as testosterone nor is it a precursor or breakdown product of it.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":54,\"stem\":\"The female reproductive system depends heavily on hormones. Their manipulation can have therapeutic effects in obstetric and gynaecological conditions. Which one of the following is the most likely action of oxytocin?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely action of oxytocin?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Endometrial shedding...\",\"e\":\"In women during menstruation, endometrial shedding happens due to a drop in estradiol and progesterone levels. This drop in hormone levels signals the endometrium layer to shed, resulting in menstrual bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sperm maturation\",\"e\":\"Oxytocin is produced in the hypothalamo-pituitary system. In men it is released from the posterior pituitary gland at ejaculation and this stimulates contractions of the reproductive tract, helping sperm release. Sperm maturation is dependent on FSH and testosterone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Skeletal muscle contraction\",\"e\":\"Skeletal muscle contraction is a voluntary function and is not dependent on oxytocin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Uterine muscle contraction\",\"e\":\"Oxytocin has two known functions - milk ejection and contraction of myometrial cells of the pregnant uterus. The presence of increased oxytocin receptors in myometrial cells is responsible for the onset and progress of labour.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Intestinal smooth muscle contraction\",\"e\":\"There are no receptors for oxytocin in the intestines, hence it does not affect the contraction of smooth intestinal muscle.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) is a glycoprotein hormone synthesised and secreted by gonadotrophs in the anterior pituitary gland. It acts in different ways in men compared to women. Which one of the following is the most likely action of this in men?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely action of this in men?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antagonism of testosterone\",\"e\":\"FSH stimulates testicular growth and enhances the production of an androgen-binding protein by the Sertoli cells. This androgen-binding protein causes high local concentrations of testosterone near the sperm. This high concentration is essential for the development of normal spermatogenesis. Hence, action of FSH is supportive and not antagonistic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inhibits Sertoli cells\",\"e\":\"In men, FSH helps to maintain Sertoli cells (large cells in the seminiferous tubules that nourish the developing sperm). Sertoli cells cannot synthesise androgens but rely on the diffusion of androgens from interstitial cells of Leydig.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inhibits sperm maturation\",\"e\":\"FSH is necessary for spermatids to mature into sperm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Suppression of testosterone\",\"e\":\"There is no effect of FSH on production or suppression of testosterone. LH is responsible for production of testosterone by cells of Leydig.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stimulation of synthesis of androgen-binding protein\",\"e\":\"FSH and testosterone synergise to stimulate the synthesis of androgen binding protein (ABP). ABP keeps the levels of testosterone high in the tubular fluid.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old woman with suspected appendicitis is referred to the general surgeons. She has a positive pregnancy test on her urine and is referred to the gynaecology service with an ectopic pregnancy. Which one of the following would be expected if an antibody that neutralises the activity of human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) is administered for 7 days, 4 weeks after conception?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would be expected if an antibody that neutralises the activity of human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) is administered for 7 days, 4 weeks after conception?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Death of the embryo and its subsequent expulsion\",\"e\":\"hCG is a glycoprotein hormone, produced in pregnancy, which is made by the embryo soon after conception and later by the syncytiotrophoblast (part of the placenta). Its role is to prevent the disintegration of the corpus luteum of the ovary and thereby maintain progesterone production that is critical for a pregnancy in humans. hCG may have additional functions, for instance it is thought that it affects the immune tolerance of the pregnancy Early pregnancy testing generally is based on the detection or measurement of hCG Neutralisation of hCG activity will result in disintegration of the corpus luteum, with subsequent death and expulsion of embryo\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased secretion of pituitary luteinising hormone (LH)\",\"e\":\"Normally in pregnancy there is inhibition of secretion of pituitary LH, when the corpus luteum disintegrates there will be an increase in secretion of pituitary LH.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased secretion of estradiol\",\"e\":\"hCG increases secretion of estradiol, with neutralisation this will decrease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased secretion of progesterone\",\"e\":\"The role of hCG is to prevent the disintegration of the corpus luteum of the ovary and thereby maintain progesterone production that is critical for a pregnancy in humans. With neutralisation of hCG activity there would be a decrease in the secretion of progesterone.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased secretion of prolactin\",\"e\":\"Prolactin levels will increase in pregnancy, and therefore with neutralisation of hCG and loss of the corpus luteum prolactin levels decrease.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":60,\"stem\":\"A patient is found to be hypogonadotrophic. They undergo hormone testing and their testosterone levels are found to be low. Which one of the following is the site of production of a hormone that stimulates the release of testosterone from testis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the site of production of a hormone that stimulates the release of testosterone from testis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pituitary acidophils\",\"e\":\"The anterior pituitary contains five types of endocrine cells. These include somatotropes (GH); lactotropes (PRL); gonadotropes (LH and FSH), corticotropes (ACTH) and thyrotropes (TSH): Depending upon the histological appearance, these are classified as acidophils and basophils. Somatotropes and lactotropes are aç dophils while others are basophils.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pituitary basophils\",\"e\":\"In men, luteinising hormone (LH) acts upon the interstitial (Leydig) cells in the testes and is responsible for testosterone production that exerts intratesticular activity in terms of the spermatogenesis and endocrine activity as the 'man hormone'. LH is produced by gonadotropes, which are basophilic cells in the anterior pituitary gland.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Primary spermatocytes\",\"e\":\"These are produced by the action of FSH and testosterone. These do not produce LH or testosterone.\\n\\nThese are supporting cells in the testis and do not produce any hormone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"sertoli cells 2\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spermatogonia\",\"e\":\"These are produced as an intermediate stage during spermatogenesis. These are not involved in the production of LH. 1350\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old woman presents with a breast abscess. They are one week post-partum and are breastfeeding.\\n\\nWhich one of the following hormones prevents lactation during pregnancy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following hormones prevents lactation during pregnancy?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Growth hormone (GH). C.\",\"e\":\"GH is lactogenic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Human chorionic somatomammotrophin\",\"e\":\"Human placental lactogen is also known as human chorionic somatomammotropin. It is produced by the syncytiotrophoblast of the placenta and is homologous to normal GH Although called as placental lactogen, its role in lactation is not clear. Its major function is induction of resistance to insulin action in the mother.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Insulin\",\"e\":\"Longer duration of lactation is associated with improved insulin and glucose response among women with prior gestational diabetes mellitus. However, insulin in diabetic patients with pregnancy in not involved in inhibition of lactation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Progesterone\",\"e\":\"Progesterone inhibits lactation during pregnancy. The fall in serum progesterone with the passage of the placenta seems to be the important event in establishment of lactogenesis. Furthermore, progesterone administration inhibits casein and a-lactalbumin synthesis in vitro. Once lactogenesis is initiated, prolactin appears to be the key hormone in maintenance of milk synthesis (galactopoiesis) and secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyroxine\",\"e\":\"During pregnancy there is a two-fold to three-fold increase in thyroxine-binding globulin concentrations. It leads to an increase in total triiodothyronine and thyroxine concentrations. However, it does not cause inhibition of lactation in pregnancy.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old woman presents with a breast abscess. They are one week post-partum and are breastfeeding Which one of the following hormones produces contraction of the smooth muscle cells underlying the milk-producing alveolar cells?\",\"lead\":\"They are one week post-partum and are breastfeeding Which one of the following hormones produces contraction of the smooth muscle cells underlying the milk-producing alveolar cells?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"FSH\",\"e\":\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), a gonadotropin hormone secreted by the anterior pituitary in response to gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GRH) is secreted by the pituitary gland. FSH stimulates the development of ovarian follicles and is essential for reproduction in women.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Oestrogen\",\"e\":\"Oestrogen is a steroid hormone produced by the ovaries that is responsible for the development of secondary sexual characteristics, ovarian follicle maturation and breast tissue changes during puberty and pregnancy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oxytocin\",\"e\":\"Oxytocin is a hormone secreted by the posterior pituitary gland. The baby's suckling stimulates the afferent nerves that connect with the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei of the hypothalamus, which in turn stimulates pulsatile release of oxytocin from the posterior pituitary gland. Oxytocin binds to receptors on the breast myoepithelial cells surrounding the ducts leading to contraction of the smooth muscle and milk expulsion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Progesterone\",\"e\":\"Progesterone is a steroid hormone produced in the luteal phase of the menstrual cycle and during pregnancy. It causes the development of secretory epithelium and causes the start of menstruation once its concentration drops significantly. It is also known as the 'pregnancy hormone' due to the multiple roles it has during pregnancy some of which include thickening of the cervical mucus to prevent sperm and ascending infection to enter the uterine cavity, suppressing lactation and decreasing the contractility of the myometrium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prolactin\",\"e\":\"Prolactin, a hormone secreted by the anterior pituitary is a peptide hormone that stimulates the proliferation of the mammary glands and production of milk following the suckling stimulus.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old woman presents with abdominal pain post-partum, the patient underwent c-section. Imaging demonstrates a dilated bowel and you suspect an ileus. They are breastfeeding without difficulty. Which one of the following hormones may prevent the menstrual cycle during the early post-partum period in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following hormones may prevent the menstrual cycle during the early post-partum period in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH).\",\"e\":\"FSH is a gonadotrophin hormone released by the anterior pituitary that stimulates follicle maturation following ovulation if the released egg is not fertilised then menstruation occurs\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Oestrogen\",\"e\":\"Ostrogen production occurs in the ovaries, by the granulosa cells of the follicles, it is stimulated by FSH. Ostrogen is involved in the maturation of the ovarian follicles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oxytocin\",\"e\":\"Oxytocin is released by the posterior pituitary in response to suckling of the baby and is involved in the ejection of milk from the breast and also contraction of the uterus back to its original, pre-pregnancy size.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Progesterone\",\"e\":\"Progesterone is produced by the corpus luteum and the placenta and drops significantly after delivery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prolactin\",\"e\":\"In the post-partum period, during lactation, there are high levels of circulating prolactin hormone. The high circulating levels of prolactin act as an inhibitor for the release of gonadotrophins [FSH and luteinising hormone (LH)], which are necessary to stimulate follicle maturation and lead to ovulation and menstruation. This occurs in the early post-partum period. After about 40 days post-partum, the prolactin sensitivity drops significantly and gonadotrophins start increasing in concentration stimulating follicle maturation.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old woman undergoes a caesarean section because of fetal distress. On review, she complains of not opening her bowels. You suspect she has developed an ileus. Which one of the following is true regarding her prolactin levels?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding her prolactin levels?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Secretion is increased by dopamine\",\"e\":\"Secretion of prolactin from the anterior pituitary is inhibited by dopamine secretion from neurones in the hypothalamus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Secretion is under hypothalamic control\",\"e\":\"Prolactin secretion is from the lactotroph cells of the anterior pituitary gland under the control of the hypothalamus. The hypothalamic neurosecretory tuberoinfundibulum neurones of the arcuate nucleus in the hypothalamus secrete dopamine, which inhibits prolactin secretion+ from the anterior pituitary. It is the only pituitary hormone whose control is inhibitory. Ostrogens increase the growth of the lactotroph cell and also inhibit the release of dopamine, both of which lead to an increase in prolactin concentration during pregnancy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Secretion would be lower as she has undergone surgery\",\"e\":\"Prolactin secretion increases following surgery or trauma as a stress response.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Secretion would be lower than normal because of her pregnancy\",\"e\":\"As previously mentioned during pregnancy the high concentration of ostrogens stimulates the growth of the lactotroph cells of the anterior pituitary leading to an increase in prolactin secretion but also inhibits dopamine release, therefore lifting inhibition of and allowing secretion of prolactin in the serum. During pregnancy and the post-partum period pro actin stimulates enlargement of the breast mammary glands and milk production, while it inhibits gonadal activity, suppressing follicular stimulation and menstruation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Secretion would be reduced due to the stress response\",\"e\":\"Prolactin levels can be raised in various conditions, some of which include physical and emotional stress, exercise, following surgery, after a high protein meal. Pathological mechanisms leading to hyperprolactinaemia* include: a prolactin secreting tumour in the pituitary and treatment with dopamine receptor antagonists.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":72,\"stem\":\"A 77-year-old man is referred by his GP with a raised PSA and a craggy prostate on PR. He is subsequently diagnosed with prostate cancer and commenced on flutamide (antiandrogen). Which one of the following combinations most appropriately describe the physiological effects of androgens?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following combinations most appropriately describe the physiological effects of androgens?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"increased protein synthesis, increased spermatogenesis, increased erythropolesis\",\"e\":\"Androgens are steroid hormones, found in both men and women. The main androgen seen in men is testosterone. Other androgens include dihydrotestosterone and androstenedione. Androgens are involved in the development of the testes, act as paracrine hormones on the Sertoli cells leading to spermatogenesis. Additionally, androgens stimulate the development of secondary male sexual characteristics. They have anabolic effects by increasing protein synthesis and muscle mass. Androgens increase erythropoiesis by stimulating erythropoietin release.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased protein synthesis, increased spermatogenesis, reduced erythropoiesis\",\"e\":\"Androgens are steroids produced primarily by the testes but also by the adrenal glands. specifically the zona reticularis of the cortex, and to a lesser extent the ovaries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased protein synthesis, reduced spermatogenesis. reduced erythropoiesis\",\"e\":\"Androgens are anabolic hormones, increasing protein synthesis and are responsible for the increase in muscle mass seen in men during puberty:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduced protein synthesis, increased spermatogenesis, increased erythropoiesis\",\"e\":\"The androgens, along with the gonadotrophin hormones FSH and luteinising hormone (LH) are involved in the stimulation and maintenance of spermatogenesis in men\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduced protein synthesis, reduced spermatogenesis, reduced erythropoiesis\",\"e\":\"The androgens promote production of red blood cells by stimulating the production of erythropoietin, increasing the activity of the bone marrow and also enhancing incorporation of iron into the red blood cells during their maturation process.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"A 15-year-old boy is referred to an endocrine clinic as his GP has noted a lack of secondary sexual characteristics. In the clinic following further tests he is commenced on testosterone for delayed puberty.\\n\\nWhich of the following cell types is the main source of testosterone in men?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following cell types is the main source of testosterone in men?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adrenal cortex\",\"e\":\"The adrenal cortex, and more specifically the zona reticularis, is the site of production of steroid hormones in both men and women, including testosterone. The production of testosterone from the adrenal glands though is only small, with the main site of testosterone secretion being the testes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Adrenal medulla\",\"e\":\"The adrenal medulla is a site for catecholamine secretion and not steroid hormones.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anterior pituitary\",\"e\":\"The anterior pituitary is the site of production of the gonadotrophin hormones, follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinising hormone (LH). Luteinising hormone stimulates the production of testosterone from the Leydig cells in the testis and FSH, in addition to testosterone, are essential for spermatogenesis and sperm maturation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Leydig cells)\",\"e\":\"Leydig cells are found between the seminiferous tubules in the testis. These cells secrete testosterone when stimulated by luteinising hormone (LH) from the anterior pituitary. Testosterone promotes the development of secondary male sexual characteristics as well as spermatogenesis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sertoli cells\",\"e\":\"Sertoli cells are a part of the seminiferous tubules, the epithelial supporting cells, whose role is to provide nutrients necessary for spermatogenesis and also act as macrophages to phagocytose any excess spermatid cytoplasm.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":76,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old pregnant patient presents with abdominal pain. The obstetric team are concerned about appendicitis and want her to be taken to theatre. Which one of the following hormones stimulates respiration and causes the arterial p(CO2) to fall during pregnancy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following hormones stimulates respiration and causes the arterial p(CO2) to fall during pregnancy?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cortisol\",\"e\":\"Cortisol is required in the last trimester, between weeks 30-32 to stimulate the production of lung surfactant in the fetus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glucose\",\"e\":\"Glucose is not a hormone, it is a monosaccharide ie a simple sugar molecule made up of carbon, oxygen and hydrogen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Insulin\",\"e\":\"Insulin is a hormone produced by the B-cells in the islets of Langerhans of the pancreas, released in response to an increase in circulating glucose, for example following a meal. During pregnancy a relative insulin resistance can develop leading to the presentation of gestational diabetes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Destrogen Q\",\"e\":\"The increased levels of ostrogen during pregnancy promote the growth of lactotroph cells in the anterior pituitary and, therefore, increase the production of prolactin which is responsible for the breast changes seen in pregnancy and lactation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Progesterone\",\"e\":\"The main cause of the change in respiration seen in pregnancy is the high levels of circulating progesterone. This, increases the sensitivity of the respiratory centres of the hypothalamus to smail increases in carbon dioxide, A small rise in p(CO2) has the effect of a significant rise in minute ventilation and respiratory rate. This as a result leads to hyperventilation and a lower normal p(CO2).\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":78,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man is found to have incidental deranged serum calcium concentration on his admission biochemistry. It is not clear what is causing the abnormality. Which one of the following statements best describes the function of calcitonin?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements best describes the function of calcitonin?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increases plasma calcium levels\",\"e\":\"Parathyroid hormone, produced by the parathyroid glands in response to hypocalcaemia causes an increased in plasma calcium levels.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Promotes osteoclastic bone resorption\",\"e\":\"One of the ways that parathyroid hormone increases the level of circulating calcium is by activating osteoclasts, the cells responsible for bone resorption, and promoting bone digestion\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increases renal excretion of phosphate\",\"e\":\"Calcitonin, is a peptide hormone produced in the C-cells or parafollicular cells of the thyroid gland. The C-cells sense an increase in the concentration of calcium in the serum and then secrete calcitonin to bring about a reduction of calcium. Calcitonin inhibits the action of osteoclasts and therefore inhibits bone resorption and also inhibits the reabsorption of calcium and phosphate ions by the kidney, promoting their excretion in the urine. In long-term hypercalcaemic syndromes the parafollicular cells can hypertrophy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"is produced in the parathyroid glands\",\"e\":\"Calcitonin is produced by the C-cells of the thyroid gland.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Deficiency causes osteoporosis\",\"e\":\"A total thyroidectomy does not actually have any significant skeletal effect. Similarly in patients with a medullary thyroid carcinoma, in which there is an excess of calcitonin, no significant skeletal problems manifest\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"An 81-year-old, nursing-home, resident is admitted to hospital in an unconscious state. His blood sugar is measured as 1.5 mmol/l (normal range 3.5-5.5 mmol/l). He is administered glucagon. Which one of the following best describes one of the main actions of this hormone?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describes one of the main actions of this hormone?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased ketone body production from fatty acids\",\"e\":\"Glucagon increases the production of ketone bodies from fatty acids, a process called ketogenesis. Ketones can be used as an alternative energy source by the brain during periods of fasting and lack of glucose.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased lipogenesis in adipose tissue\",\"e\":\"Insulin, released when the glucose concentration in the serum is raised, is the hormone that stimulates lipogenesis, ie the process by which acetyl-CoA is used in the production of fatty acids which can be deposited in the peripheral tissues.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decreased givcogendlysis\",\"e\":\"Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis, ie the breakdown of glycogen in the liver to glucose.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased gluconeogenesis\",\"e\":\"Glucagon stimulates gluconeogenesis\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis\",\"e\":\"Glucagon, a peptide hormone secreted by the alpha-cells in the pancreatic islets of Langerhans, is released in response to a drop in circulating glucose It acts via three main mechanisms to increase the level of circulating glucose. These are:\\n• glycogenolysis, ie the breakdown of glycogen into constituent glucose molecules in the liver;\\n• gluconeogenesis, ie conversion of non-carbohydrate molecules to glucose, a process taking place again in the liver;\\n• lipolysis, promoting the breakdown of lipids and releasing fatty acids that can be used as an alternative source of energy during starvation.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"A 66-year-old man undergoes a major colorectal resection for bowel cancer. His nutritional status is concerning, so he is subsequently referred to a dietician post-operatively. Gluconeogenesis is best described as a process by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Gluconeogenesis is best described as a process by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Glucose is generated from carbohydrate precursors\",\"e\":\"Carbohydrate precursors are broken down into monosaccharide constituents such as glucose molecules by the action of enzymes in the saliva, stomach and intestine.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glucose is generated by the breakdown of glycogen stores\",\"e\":\"The breakdown of glycogen stores to generate glucose in known as glycogenolysis, it occurs in the liver and is stimulated by glucagon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glucose is generated from non-carbohydrate sources\",\"e\":\"Gluconeogenesis is the process by which glucose is generated from non-carbohydrate sources such as glycerol, lactate and amino acids. This process occurs during fasting and starvation. The body is able to maintain adequate amount of circulating glucose by breakdown of glycogen for the first 8-12 h of starvation. Following this, if the circulating glucose is not replenished by a meal, the body starts using other non-carbohydrate substrates to produce glucose which is essential for the central nervous system. It occurs mostly in the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Glucose is broken down to acetyl-CoA which enters the tri-carboxylic cycle\",\"e\":\"Glucose is broken down during glycolysis into two pyruvate (three carbon) molecules that enter Kreb's cycle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Glucagon is generated from carbohydrate precursors\",\"e\":\"Glucagon is a hormone produced by the alpha-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas. It is secreted during fasting in response to a drop of the serum concentration of glucose. It stimulates two processes in the liver: glycogenolysis, ie the breakdown of glycogen to glucose, and gluconeogenesis, ie the process by which substrates such as amino acids and lactate are converted into glucose.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"An 80-year-old woman is admitted with confusion and drowsiness. She lives alone and it is not clear what has caused the deterioration. Her serum glucose concentrations are found to be profoundly low, but improve rapidly following administration of IV dextrose. Which one of the following physiological responses occur in an acute hypoglycaemic episode?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following physiological responses occur in an acute hypoglycaemic episode?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A rise in serum insulin\",\"e\":\"Insulin* is secreted by the b-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas as a response of a raised serum glucose and not hypoglycaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An increase in liver glycogen\",\"e\":\"A brief or mild serum hypoglycaemia is sensed by the body and leads in the secretion of glucagon which will induce glycogenolysis and use of the glycogen stores in the liver leading to its decrease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A decrease in serum glucagon\",\"e\":\"Serum glucagon* is released by the a-cells of the islets of Langerhans as a response to hypoglycaemia, therefore the serum concentration should increase.\\n\\nKetone bodies are produced when the body starts using fatty acids as an energy source. This occurs after a long period of hypoglycaemia, or when there is not enough insulin in the body to enable the cells to uptake and use the circulating glucose.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A rise serum adrenaline\",\"e\":\"Hypoglycaemia* is defined as a blood sugar below the normal range (3.5-5.5 mmol/l). It most commonly occurs in diabetic patients who have taken their normal insulin dose when the carbohydrate intake was low. Other causes include an insulin secreting tumour of the pancreas (insulinoma), hypothyroidism, severe infections, liver failure. Brain metabolism is highly dependent on glucose as an energy source. Initial steps in brief or mild hypoglycaemia* include the release of glucagon to promote glycogenolysis, use of the glycogen stores in the liver and gluconeogenesis. Hypoglycaemia triggers the release of adrenaline from the adrenal glands, as a stress hormone Increased circulating adrenaline causes the symptoms of sweating, shaking, tachycardia and a feeling of hunger all of which alert the patient to act upon the symptoms and have a meal. Adrenaline also acts synergistically with glucagon in gluconeogenesis. If the hypoglycaemia is not resolved, the brain does not have adequate support and the patient can experience seizures and coma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A rise in serum ketone bodies\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"A 77-year-old woman sustains a neck of femur fracture. Following operative repair she is found to have osteoporosis and is started on bone strengthening therapy. What percentage of calcium is available for buffering changes in Ca+ balance in the body?\",\"lead\":\"What percentage of calcium is available for buffering changes in Ca+ balance in the body?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1%\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"10%\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"20%\",\"e\":\"Calcium homeostasis is dependent on factors such as the circulating level of parathyroid hormone, vitamin D levels, phosphate and magnesium concentration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"50%\",\"e\":\"40-50% of the total serum calcium is bound to proteins, most of it to albumin and a smaller portion of it to globulins.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"99%\",\"e\":\"10% of the total serum calcium is found complexed with ions such as citrate and bicarbonate. This fraction is known as the complexed fraction.\\n\\nAbout 99% of the total body calcium is in the skeleton, mainly in the form of hydroxyapatite, a crystal composed of calcium, phosphate and hydroxide.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":91,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old woman complains of easy fatigability and shortness of breath after abdominal aortic aneurysm repair. She is subsequently diagnosed with iron-deficiency anaemia and prescribed an oral iron preparation.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements about iron metabolism is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about iron metabolism is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ferritin is a plasma protein that transports iron in the blood\",\"e\":\"Ferritin is mainly an intracellular protein that binds to iron and stores it within the cells. Only small amounts are found in the serum where it acts as an iron carrier. Ferritin concentration has been shown to increase in response to stress and it is therefore considered an acute phase protein. Most of the iron in the plasma is bound to transferrin, which is an iron transporter protein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Heemosiderin is a product of haemoglobin degradation\",\"e\":\"Haemosiderin is an intracellular iron storing complex, composed of ferritin, denatured ferritin and other materials. The iron within these complexes is not readily available to supply iron when required. Haemosiderin is most commonly found within macrophages in areas where haemorrhage has occurred.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Iron is more efficiently absorbed in the ferrous state (Fe?+) than in the ferric state (Feš+)\",\"e\":\"The iron in the ferrous state (Fe 2+) is more efficiently absorbed than in the ferric state (Fest). In the physiological pH the iron is oxid sed to the ferric state (Fest) which is insoluble. Gastric acid secretions however, lower the ph and assist the absorption of iron in the ferrous state in the duodenum and first part of the jejunum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Most iron in the body is stored as haemosiderin\",\"e\":\"60-70% of the iron in the body is found within haemoglobin and myoglobin molecules. The remainder is stored intracellularly bound to ferritin with a smaller amount as haemosiderin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The gastrointestinal rate of iron absorption is extremely high\",\"e\":\"Iron absorption occurs in the duodenum and first part of the jejunum with only a small rate of absorption occurring. Only 3-6% of the ingested daily iron is actually absorbed because once the ferrous ion is oxidised to ferric it becomes insoluble.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":94,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man is due to be operated on the trauma list first in the morning for a closed displaced tibial fracture. He is kept nil by mouth from 02.00 the night before. What are the effects of fasting on the body?\",\"lead\":\"What are the effects of fasting on the body?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anabolism, gluconeogenesis, decreased glucagon production\",\"e\":\"Insulin is a hormone involved in anabolism, promoting lipogenesis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anabolism, gluconeogenesis, increased glucagon production\",\"e\":\"Other anabolic hormones include circulating androgens which increase the muscle mass.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Catabolism, gluconeogenesis, decreased glucagon production\",\"e\":\"During fasting, the serum glucose concentration is reduced and this hypoglycaemia is sensed by the body leading to production of glucagon from the pancreas which promotes gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Catabolism, gluconeogenesis, increased glucagon production\",\"e\":\"Fasting, drops the circulating glucose levels. This is sensed by the alpha-cells of the islets of Langerhans which secrete glucagon. Glucagon is a hormone that stimulates catabolism by promoting lipolysis and glycogenolysis, dissolving the glycogen stores in the liver to produce glucose. It also promotes gluconeogenesis, ie the use of non-carbohydrate molecules such as lactate, amino acids and glycerol to form glucose. Action of glucagon: Gluca has Gone to CAMP to bring out glucose, glucagon elevates glucose by cAMP involvement.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Catabolism, gluconeogenesis, increased insulin production\",\"e\":\"Insulin production during fasting is decreased and the counterpart hormone glucagon production is increased.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":96,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old man presents with suspected acromegaly due to a pituitary adenoma. He has been referred to the neurosurgical service for consideration of a trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy. Which one of the following hormones exerts the least effect on growth?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following hormones exerts the least effect on growth?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"St Growth hormone\",\"e\":\"Growth hormone stimulates growth by stimulating production of insulin-growth factor 1 from the liver. This is a hormone with anabolic effects similar to insulin, which promotes growth in muscle, bones and almost all tissues in the body\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Testosterone\",\"e\":\"Testosterone is an anabolic steroid hormone that leads to protein synthesis and increase in muscle bulk, seen mostly in men during puberty.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"T4\",\"e\":\"Thyroxine stimulates the anabolic effect of growth hormone and also increases its production by the pituitary.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Insulin\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vasopressin\",\"e\":\"Vasopressin, a so known as antidiuretic hormone, is a peptide hormone produced in the posterior pituitary gland and acts on the kidney by increasing the number of aquaporins in the membranes of tubules, leading to reabsorption of water and preventing its loss in the urine. It has no effect on growth:\\n\\nInsulin is an anabolic hormone that leads to lipid synthesis from glucose and storage of triglycerides in the liver and adipose tissues (lipogenesis) and the storage of glycogen in the liver and muscle (glycogenesis).\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old man is under investigation for suspected acromegaly. Growth hormone and glucagon are both polypeptide hormones.\\n\\nWhich of the following best describes the action of these two hormones?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following best describes the action of these two hormones?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decrease blood glucose concentration\",\"e\":\"Both glucagon and growth hormone increase serum glucose concentration.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increase blood glucose concentration\",\"e\":\"Glucagon is released by the pancreas in response to a drop in glucose concentration, to bring about an increase in glucose by mobilising and breaking down glycogen stores ie glycogenolysis and promotes utilisation of non-carbohydrate molecules to produce glucose. Growth hormone decreases the uptake of glucose by the cells and increases the glucose serum concentration. It also promotes lipolysis and gluconeogenesis in the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decrease gluconeogenesis\",\"e\":\"Glucagon and GH increase gluconeogenesis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increase glycogenolysis\",\"e\":\"Glycogenolysis is a function of glucagon only.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decrease lipolysis\",\"e\":\"Growth hormone increases lipolysis and mobilisation of the fatty acids from adipose tissue, promoting the use of fatty acids as an alternative energy source. Glucagon also stimulates lipolysis:\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":100,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old man is being investigated for possible acromegaly. Growth hormone is a 191-amino acid, single-chain polypeptide hormone that is synthesised, stored and secreted by the somatotroph cells within the lateral wings of the anterior pituitary gland, which stimulates growth and cell reproduction in humans\\n\\nSecretion of this hormone is increased by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Secretion of this hormone is increased by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cortisol\",\"e\":\"Circulating glucocorticoids, such as cortisol, inhibit growth hormone secretion. Another inhibitor of growth hormone is a carbohydrate-rich diet.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Exercise\",\"e\":\"Exercise, is one of the physiological stimulators of growth hormone secretion along with sleep, hypoglycaemia and circulating estradiol. A diet rich in protein is also considered a stimulator of growth hormone secretion. Growth hormone is secreted by the somatotroph cells in the anterior pituitary gland.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Free fatty acids\",\"e\":\"Free circulating fatty acids have been found to inhibit the release of growth hormone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hyperglycaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypoglycaemia is a stimulator of growth hormone secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Somatostatin\",\"e\":\"Somatostatin, produced by the periventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus, is an inhibitor of growth hormone secretion from the anterior pituitary.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man presents with sialolithiasis of the submandibular gland. The stone can be easily bimanually palpated, the duct is open and the stone is removed under local anaesthetic Concerning the salivary glands, which of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"The stone can be easily bimanually palpated, the duct is open and the stone is removed under local anaesthetic Concerning the salivary glands, which of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They secrete around 150 ml of saliva per day\",\"e\":\"Around 1.5 litres (1500 ml) of saliva is produced by the salivary glands every day.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They secrete saliva with a pH of 4-5\",\"e\":\"The saliva is usually at a neutral pH, around 7-8.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They secrete saliva which is hypertonic\",\"e\":\"They secrete saliva which is isotonic, composed mainly of water (98%) with a smaller amount of solutes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They are supplied by the parasympathetic nervous system\",\"e\":\"The salivary glands are supplied both by the sympathetic and the parasympathetic nervous system: The sympathetic nervous system promotes thicker saliva production, necessary for respiration. The parasympathetic nervous system on the other half promotes the secretion of thinner saliva, for facilitation of digestion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They secrete saliva containing trypsinogen\",\"e\":\"Saliva contains three main enzymes: amylase that is associated with the digestion of starch, kallikrein and lipase. Additionally, there are some antimicrobial agents in the saliva such as İysozyme, IgA and lactoferrin.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":104,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old woman has undergone thyroidectomy for cancer. She is placed on lifelong thyroxine replacement therapy. Which one of the following metabolic effects is most likely to be caused by this hormone?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following metabolic effects is most likely to be caused by this hormone?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased glycogenolysis in the liver\",\"e\":\"Thyroid hormone increases glycogenolysis in the liver, a similar action as glucagon.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased glucose absorption in the gut\",\"e\":\"Thyroid* hormones increase the level of circulating glucose, by a variety of mechanisms. They increase glucose absorption in the gastrointestinal system, promote glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. All these mechanisms result in increase in free serum glucose and hyperglycaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decreased lipolysis\",\"e\":\"Thyroid hormone stimulates the mobilisation of fat from adipose tissues and increased lipolysis to cause increased free serum fatty acids.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased expression of f-adrenergic receptors\",\"e\":\"Thyroid hormones cause increased expression of f-adrenergic receptors in skeletal and cardiac muscle leading to increased inotropic effect, with tachycardia and increased cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased oxygen uptake in the mitochondria\",\"e\":\"Thyroid hormones increase oxygen consumption and the rates of ATP hydrolysis in the mitochondria, resulting in increase in basal metabolic rate and heat production.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":106,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old woman is found to have an incidental adrenal adenoma. They undergo further investigation and a raised aldosterone is found Conn syndrome is suspected Which one of the following is the site of production of aldosterone?\",\"lead\":\"They undergo further investigation and a raised aldosterone is found Conn syndrome is suspected Which one of the following is the site of production of aldosterone?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Zona glomerulosa\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone is a steroid hormone that facilitates the reabsorption of sodium and water, and the excretion of potassium and hydrogen ions from the distal convoluted tubule and collecting ducts. It is secreted by zona glomerulosa of adrenal cortex\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Zona fasciculata\",\"e\":\"The zona fasciculata secretes glucocorticoids such as cortisol\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Zona reticularis\",\"e\":\"The zona reticularis secretes sex steroids\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Adrenal medulla\",\"e\":\"The adrenal medulla secretes adrenaline (70%) and noradrenaline (30%)\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Macula densa\",\"e\":\"Macula densa cells sense the concentration of sodium chloride in the distal convoluted tubule. if the sodium (Na) level is low, the macula densa cells release prostaglandins, which trigger juxtaglomerular cells lining the afferent arterioles to release renin into the bloodstream.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":108,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman presents with symptoms in keeping with severe hyperthyroidism. They have been referred to clinic due to a failure of medical treatment and consideration of thyroidectomy Which one of the following is the most important step in the production of thyroid hormones?\",\"lead\":\"They have been referred to clinic due to a failure of medical treatment and consideration of thyroidectomy Which one of the following is the most important step in the production of thyroid hormones?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Passive transport of iodine into the thyroid gland (Ta)\",\"e\":\"lodine is actively transported into the thyroid gland against a concentration gradient, the normal iodine concentration in the thyroid is 30 times greater than that in blood.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Synthesis of thyroglobulin by the follicular epithelial cells\",\"e\":\"Thyroglobulin is synthesised within the follicular epithelial cells and secreted into the follicular lumen. It is an important step in production of thyroid hormones and organification of iodine with tyrosine molecules produces Ta or T4. This selection depends upon the number of iodine molecules.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oxidation of iodine by lysosomal degradation\",\"e\":\"Oxidation of iodine is done by thyroid peroxidase.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Organification of iodine by lysosomal degradation\",\"e\":\"Thyroid peroxidase first generates l2 by oxidising iodine ions present in the follicular lumen. Thyroid peroxidase then 'organifies' the generated lz by covalently linking it with the tyrosine residues present in thyroglobulin. This step generates either a single or doubly iodinated species of tyrosine, termed 'monoiodotyrosine (MIT)' or 'di-iodotyrosine (DIT). respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Conversion of Ts into T4 by deiodinase\",\"e\":\"Deiodinase converts Te into Ta in peripheral tissues. Ta is metabolically active and has a shorter half-life.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":110,\"stem\":\"A 20-year-old woman suffers a neck of femur fracture requiring operative repair On investigation, she is found to be nutritionally depleted and is diagnosed with osteomalacia Which one of the following is the most likely mechanism of action of vitamin D?\",\"lead\":\"A 20-year-old woman suffers a neck of femur fracture requiring operative repair On investigation, she is found to be nutritionally depleted and is diagnosed with osteomalacia Which one of the following is the most likely mechanism of action of vitamin D?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased calcium excretion from the kidneys\",\"e\":\"Calcium absorption is increased in the kidneys.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Stimulation of parathyroid hormone (PTH) release from the parathyroid glands\",\"e\":\"Increasing levels of vitamin D inhibit the release of PTH from the parathyroid glands. This provides a negative feedback mechanism as PTH is required for hydroxylation of vitamin D in the kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased phosphate absorption from the intestines\",\"e\":\"Active vitamin D plays a crucial role in the homeostasis of calcium. Whether the source is from the skin or dietary. 25-hydroxyvitamin Ds and 1-a-hydroxylase hydroxylation is required in the liver and kidney respectively to convert vitamin D into its metabolically active form. Its main function in calcium homeostasis includes an increase in the absorption of both calcium and phosphate from the gut.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased calcium absorption from the intestines\",\"e\":\"Calcium absorption is increased in the intestine by the action of active vitamin D.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased bone mineralisation\",\"e\":\"By the action of vitamin D. bone mineralisation and stabilisation is promoted\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":112,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old boy is brought to his GP with a history of generalised lethargy and weight loss. Further questioning reveals polydipsia and polyuria. A random blood sugar is very high Which of the following cells of pancreas are most likely dysfunctional?\",\"lead\":\"A random blood sugar is very high Which of the following cells of pancreas are most likely dysfunctional?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alpha cells\",\"e\":\"Alpha cells produce glucagon. Glucagon raises blood glucose levels by stimulating the liver to convert its glycogen into glucose, however it is not responsible for type-l diabetes. Moreover, to cause hyperglycaemia these cells need to hypofunction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Beta cells\",\"e\":\"Beta cells produce insulin and amylin. Insulin lowers blood glucose levels by stimulating cel s to take up glucose out of the bloodstream. In type-l DM insulin production by beta cells is defective due to immune based damage to the islets of Langerhans beta cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Delta cells\",\"e\":\"Delta cells produce somatostatin. It is a hormone that suppresses the release of the other hormones made in the pancreas\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gamma cells\",\"e\":\"Gamma cells produce pancreatic polypeptide. Pancreatic polypeptide regulates both the endocrine and exocrine pancreatic secretions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Epsilon cells\",\"e\":\"Epsilon cells produce ghrelin and make up less than 1% of the total islet cells. Ghrelin is a protein that stimulates hunger\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":114,\"stem\":\"A 12-year-old boy is taken to his General Practitioner (GP) with a history of increasing lethargy and fatigue. On examination, he is noted to have increased skin pigmentation in his skin creases and buccal mucosa. His blood tests show hyperkalaemia and a mild acidosis. Which of the following tissue types is most likely atrophied?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following tissue types is most likely atrophied?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior pituitary gland\",\"e\":\"The child is most likely suffering from Addison's disease due to deficiency of glucorticoid and mineralocorticoid hormones. The deficiency can be primary or secondary. The patient has hyperpigmentation which means adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) levels are high, hence the anterior pituitary gland is not responsible, as it is likely to be producing more ACTH.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla\",\"e\":\"Chromaffin cells are specialised sympathetic post-ganglionic neurones that release a variety of hormones when stimulated, including adrenaline, noradrenaline, and dopamine.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Posterior pituitary gland\",\"e\":\"This patient has Addison's disease, which is primary adrenal insufficiency. The posterior pituitary is not involved in the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis which controls gluco and mineralocorticoid synthesis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex\",\"e\":\"This area produces cortisol and is commonly affected in Addison's disease. 90% of the cortex is destroyed before patients become symptomatic The Zona glomerulosa and fasculata are destroyed with sparing of the zona reticularis and medulla A deficiency of cortisol, as occurs in Addison's disease, can produce hyperkalaemia and mild acidosis. In primary cases adrenal cortex is defective while in secondary cases anterior pituitary does not produce enough ACTH) Pigmentation differentiates between the two as high ACTH has a partial function of melanin stimulating hormone\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Zona reticularis of the adrenal cortex\",\"e\":\"This is involved in production of sex hormones, which would not cause Addison's.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":117,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old woman has been referred to the endocrine surgeon after she was found to be hypercalcaemic by her General Practitioner (GP). In calcium metabolism and homeostasis, where is extracellular calcium concentration detected?\",\"lead\":\"In calcium metabolism and homeostasis, where is extracellular calcium concentration detected?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior pituitary gland\",\"e\":\"The anterior pituitary gland is involved in bodily homeostasis through its production of various hormones. The anterior pituitary hormone adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) can influence serum electrolytes via its influence on glucocorticoid production. However. serum calcium is not detected in the anterior pituitary gland.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Kidney\",\"e\":\"The kidneys act to increase or decrease serum calcium under the influence of parathyroid hormone (PTH) and calcitonin. PTH and calcitonin, respectively, increases and decreases calcium resorption from the kidney. However, serum calcium concentration is not detected in the kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Parathyroid glands\",\"e\":\"Calcium-sensing receptors expressed in the parathyroid glands are responsible for detecting extracellular calcium concentration. PTH secretion by the parathyroid chief cells is stimulated or inhibited in response.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Skin\",\"e\":\"The skin is involved in calcium metabolism as the site of 7-dehydrocholesterol synthesis and conversion to colecalciferol. These are important steps in the synthesis of active vitamin D However: serum calcium concentrations are not detected in the skin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Small intestine\",\"e\":\"Calcium is absorbed in the small intestine under the influence of calcitonin. However, serum calcium concentration is not detected in the small intestine.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":119,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old woman presents to the General Practitioner (GP) complaining of low mood, persistent tiredness and mild constipation. She has no past significant medical history of\\n\\nHer routine blood tests show:\\n\\nResult Investigation Normal value\\n\\nHaemoglobin (Hb) 12.5 g/dl 12.5-17.5 g/di\\n\\nWhite cell count (WCC) 9 × 109/1 4-11 × 10%/l\\n\\n201 × 109/1 150-400 × 109/1 Platelets (PLT)\\n\\nSodium (Nat) 137 mmol/l 135-145 mmol/l\\n\\n4.5 mmol/l 3.5-5.0 mmol/l Potassium (K+)\\n\\n90 umol/l 50-120 umol/l Creatinine\\n\\n7 mmol/l Urea 2.5-7.1 mmol/l 2.2-2.6 mmol/l 2.9 mmol/l Calcium\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\\n\\n2.5-7.1 mmol/l 7 mmol/l Urea\\n\\n2.2-2.6 mmol/l 2.9 mmol/l Calcium\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"DI Hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"Although hypothyroidism is associated with symptoms of tiredness and fatigue, it is not associated with hypercalcaemia. Electrolyte abnormalities are rarely associated with hypothyroidism but can include anaemia, hyperlipidaemia, dilutional hyponatraemia and raised creatinine.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoparathyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hypoparathyroidism is usually a result of surgery or radiotherapy on the thyroid or parathyroid glands. It is associated with hypocalcaemia, rather than hypercalcaemia. Reduced levels of parathyroid hormone (PTH) lead to less calcium resorption from the kidneys and bone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Primary hyperparathyroidism\",\"e\":\"This patient has symptoms consistent with hypercalcaemia and isolated hypercalcaemia, the most common finding in primary hyperparathyroidism, in her bloods. The majority (85%) of primary hyperparathyroidism cases are caused by a single PTH-producing adenoma. Excess PTH causes an increase in calcium resorption from the kidney and bone, leading to hyperparathyroidism.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Secondary hyperparathyroidism\",\"e\":\"Secondary hyperparathyroidism arises in the context of chronic kidney disease or vitamin D deficiency. Failure of the kidneys to produce vitamin D or reabsorb calcium leads to chronically low serum calcium. This results in hypertrophy of the parathyroid glands as they are stimulated to produce more PTH. Serum calcium remains low as the kidneys are not able to resorb calcium in response to PTH, and therefore PTH levels remain elevated.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tertiary hyperparathyroidism\",\"e\":\"Tertiary hyperparathyroidism occurs post-renal transplant after a long period of low calcium, with parathyroid gland stimulation and hypertrophy. The parathyroid glands become autonomous, no longer responding to the serum calcium negative feedback loop. This results in raised PTH and raised calcium levels. The patient in this question has no history of renal disease or transplant, and hence this is not the correct answer.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":123,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old man has been referred to the Urology Clinic with a 4-month history of urinary frequency. He reports drinking over 5 litres of water a day because he is thirsty; however, his lips and mucous membranes appear dry His routine bloods results show:\\n\\nResult Investigation Normal value\\n\\nHaemoglobin (Hb) 12.5-17.5 g/dl 14.5 g/dl\\n\\n4-11 × 109/1 White cell count (WCC) 6 × 109/1\\n\\n201 × 109/1 150-400 × 10/1 Platelets (PLT)\\n\\n150 mmol/l 135-145 mmol/l Sodium (Nat)\\n\\n3.5-5.0 mmol/l 4.0 mmol/l Potassium (K)\\n\\n120 umol/l Creatinine 50-120 umol/l\\n\\n2.5-7.1 mmol/l 7 mmol/l Urea\\n\\n2.2-2.6 mmol/l 2.2 mmol/l Calcium\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\\n\\n7 mmol/l 2.5-71 mmol/i Urea\\n\\n2.2 mmol/l 2.2-2.6 mmol/l Calcium\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bladder outlet obstruction\",\"e\":\"Bladder outlet obstruction can result in polyuria as patients are not able to completely void their bladder each time. Urethral stricture could be a cause in this age group; however, this does not explain this patient's thirst and signs of hypovolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Conn syndrome\",\"e\":\"Conn syndrome is also known as primary hyperaldosteronism and results in excess sodium resorption from the kidneys. However, water moves back into the circulation with sodium, and so hypernatraemia is rare, unless fluids are restricted by the patient\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diabetes insipidus\",\"e\":\"A new diagnosis of diabetes mellitus and diabetes insipidus can be mistaken for overactive bladder syndrome in the absence of a careful history. In central diabetes insipidus, failure of antidiuretic hormone (ADH). also known as vasopressin, production prevents resorption of water from the kidneys, whereas in nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, the kidneys are insensitive to the ADH produced. Excessive water is lost via the urinary tract, leading to polyuria and hypovolaemia. Thirst response is appropriately activated, resulting in polydipsia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Overactive bladder syndrome\",\"e\":\"Polyuria is a symptom of overactive bladder syndrome. However, patients with an overactive bladder often self-restrict their fluid intake in an attempt to reduce trips to the bathroom and they do not complain of excessive thirst. Overactive bladder syndrome is caused by detrusor muscie overactivity.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Psychogenic polydipsia\",\"e\":\"Psychogenic polydipsia is excessive intake of fluid in the absence of the physiological stimuli to drink. This is, in approximately 30% of cases, associated with dilutional hyponatraemia. It is not associated with hypernatraemia.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":127,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman is admitted with adhesive bowel obstruction. She has been vomiting and now has a nasogastric tube inserted and is on free drainage\\n\\nHer arterial blood gas shows\\n\\nNormal value Investigation Result\\n\\n7.35-7.45 7.55 pH\\n\\n4.7-6.0 kPa 6.1 kPa p(CO2)\\n\\n27 mEg/l 22-26 mEq/l HCO:\\n\\n0.5-2 mmol/l 2 mmol/l Lactate\\n\\nChloride 98-106 mmol/l 69 mmol/l\\n\\nSodium (Nat) 134 mmol/l 135-145 mmol/l\\n\\n3.5-5.0 mmol/l Potassium (K=) 3.5 mmol/l\\n\\n50-120 umol/l 98 umol/l Creatinine\\n\\n7 mmol/l 2.5-7.1 mmol/l Urea\\n\\nCalcium 2.2 mmol/l 2.2-2.6 mmol/l\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\\n\\n2.5-7.1 mmol/l Urea 7 mmol/l\\n\\nCalcium 2.2-2.6 mmol/i 2.2 mmol/l\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PI Acute kidney injury\",\"e\":\"Severe vomiting and third spacing in bowel obstruction can lead to intravascular depletion. This, in turn, can result in acute kidney injury. However, this patient does not have the raised urea or creatinine which would characterise acute kidney injury.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypervolaemic hypontremia?\",\"e\":\"Patients treated for bowel obstruction are nil by mouth, which could lead to excessive parenteral fluid administration and dilutional hyponatraemia. However, this patient is unlikely to have fluid overload if she is vomiting. The sodium level is also within normal range.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemic alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Gastrointestinal losses of potassium from diarrhoea, high-output stomas and fistulae can lead to hypokalaemia. Loss of extracellular potassium ions leads to hydrogen ions being exchanged into the intracellular compartment in an attempt to normalise potassium levels. This patient is alkalotic, but their potassium level is normal.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypochloraemic alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Vomiting and nasogastric drainage lead to a loss of hydrochloric acid from the stomach Loss of hydrogen ions leads to alkalosis and this patient is also hypochloraemic on the blood test.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lactic acidosis\",\"e\":\"Lactic acidosis can occur in patients with bowel obstruction, often if there is bowel ischaemia. Uncompensated lactic acidosis would result in a low pH, but that is not seen here. The patient has a raised pH, indicating an alkalosis.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":130,\"stem\":\"An 88-year-old woman is on the ward awaiting an emergency arthroplasty after a neck of femur fracture. She was found at home after being on the floor for 24 hours. On the ward, she goes into cardiac arrest Which electrolyte abnormality is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"On the ward, she goes into cardiac arrest Which electrolyte abnormality is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypercalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypercalcaemia is a cause of cardiac arrest. However, this patient has no apparent reason to have a raised serum calcium. In fact, this patient is at high risk of rhabdomyolysis, which causes an increase in intracellular calcium in the muscle cells, and therefore low serum calcium.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemia\",\"e\":\"Hyperkalaemia causes cardiac arrest by precipitating ventricular tachycardia. This patient is a risk of multiple factors that lead to hyperkalaemia. The long period on the floor can lead to rhabdomyolysis and renal failure. Her fluid intake would also be low, causing acute kidney injury. These factors increase serum potassium and can lead to ventricular tachycardia and cardiac arrest.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypernatraemia\",\"e\":\"This patient is at risk of hypernatraemia caused by hypovolaemia. Her inability to drink for the last 24 hours would cause intravascular fluid depletion and resorption of sodium in the kidney. However, hypovolaemic hypernatraemia is not likely to cause cardiac arrest.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemia\",\"e\":\"This patient is more at risk of hyperkalaemia than of hypokalaemia. This is because of possible age-related chronic kidney disease, acute kidney injury from an inability to eat or drink and possible rhabdomyolysıs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hyponatraemia\",\"e\":\"This patient is unlikely to have hyponatraemia. The most common causes of hyponatraemia are from diuretic treatment or dilution from excessive fluid administration. Other causes include syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH) and cardiogenic oedema. Hyponatraemia does not cause cardiac arrest.\"}]},{\"t\":78,\"p\":133,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old patient is kept nil by mouth overnight following a neck of femur fracture: In the morning of the surgery, they are found to be profoundly hypoglycaemic, and the anaesthetists are not happy to proceed until this corrected. What stimulates glycogenesis in the liver?\",\"lead\":\"What stimulates glycogenesis in the liver?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"c i Insulin\",\"e\":\"Insulin is produced by the pancreas in response to a high circulating glucose level. It acts to decrease this by promoting movement of glucose and potassium from the circulation to the intracellular compartments. It does this by inserting glucose transporters on the membranes of fat and muscle cells. It also has anabolic effects by promoting the synthesis and storage of glycogen in the liver and muscle, and with lipogenesis by the conversion of glucose to triglycerides, increasing fat storage in the liver and adipose tissues.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glucagon\",\"e\":\"Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis in the liver; the process by which the glycogen stores of the liver are broken down to glucose molecules. Glucagon increases intracellular cAMP levels.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cortisol\",\"e\":\"Cortisol counteracts the action of insulins and increases hepatic gluconeogenesis\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline 2V\",\"e\":\"In hypoglycaemic states adrenaline is released and acts synergistically with glucagon to promote glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyroxine)\",\"e\":\"High levels of thyroxine promote hyperglycaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A patient is noted to have a gastric ulcer and a positive urease breath test. They are started on Helicobacter pylori eradication therapy. The chief cells of the stomach produce which one of the following substances?\",\"lead\":\"The chief cells of the stomach produce which one of the following substances?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gastric acid\",\"e\":\"Gastric acid is produced by the parietal cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intrinsic factor\",\"e\":\"Intrinsic factor is also produced by the parietal cells of the stomach and is necessary for the absorption of vitamin Biz at the terminal ileum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pepsinogen\",\"e\":\"Pepsinogen is produced by chief cells in the stomach and is an inactive form of an enzyme. Once this is exposed to the acidic environment of the stomach it is converted to its active form, pepsin, which is involved in the breakdown of proteins to amino acids\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mucus\",\"e\":\"Mucus production in the stomach is the function of foveolar cells that line the gastric mucosa found in the gastric pits.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Somatostatin\",\"e\":\"Somatostatin is produced by D-cells in the pancreas.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with small bowel obstruction. On the initial AXR there were no obvious bowel loops seen, a subsequent CT scan demonstrates that the obstruction is high up. Which one of the following is produced by the duodenum?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is produced by the duodenum?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pepsin\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Secretin\",\"e\":\"Secretin is a hormone secreted from the S-cells in the duodenum in response to the arrival of acidic gastric chyme in the duodenum. Secretin influences the release of pancreatic enzymes by increasing the rate of bicarbonate synthesis.\\n\\nPepsin is the enzyme responsible for the breakdown of proteins and is produced in the inactive form of pepsinogen, by the chief cells of the stomach:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Amylase\",\"e\":\"Amylase is an enzyme involved in carbohydrate metabolism, produced in the pancreas and the salivary glands\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lipase\",\"e\":\"Lipase is produced in the pancreas and is involved in fat metabolism, breaking down triglycerides into monoglycerides and two fatty acids.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Elastase\",\"e\":\"Elastase is an enzyme produced in the acinar cells of the pancreas involved in the metabolism of proteins.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old man is 2 days post-operation, following a hemiarthroplasty for a fractured neck of the femur. A diagnosis of paralytic ileus is made. Which one of the following is correct regarding intestinal motility?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding intestinal motility?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is increased after eating due to the action of the sympathetic nervous system\",\"e\":\"Intestinal motility is increased after a meal due to the action of the parasympathetic nervous system.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is increased when acetylcholine (ACh) acts at nicotinic receptors\",\"e\":\"It is increased by the action of the parasympathetic system, with acetylcholine acting at the muscarinic receptors.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Occurs as a result of automatic depolarisation by cells in the wall of the bowel\",\"e\":\"Electrical activity in the gastrointestinal tract occurs in slow waves, generated and propagated by the interstitial cells of Cajal, relying on calcium current. These slow waves cause depolarisation of the cells in the wall of the bowel and create a peristaltic wave.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Peristalsis occurs only after a meal\",\"e\":\"Peristalsis occurs continuously and not just after a meal.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Segmentation efficiently moves the chyme along the intestine\",\"e\":\"Segmentation, ie contraction of isolated segments found at intervals along the intestine. efficiently mixes the chyme with mucus and hydrolytic enzymes. It is peristalsis that moves the chyme along the intestine.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old man is put on a proton pump inhibitor to suppress the symptoms of oesophagitis. They have ongoing symptoms when next seer in clinic, so ranitidine is added into their treatment plan.\\n\\nThe cell and membrane biology of the gastric acid pump has which one of the following features?\",\"lead\":\"The cell and membrane biology of the gastric acid pump has which one of the following features?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Histamine-stimulated acid production is independent of the proton pump\",\"e\":\"Histamine-stimulated acid production is dependent on the proton pump. Histamine binding to receptors on the basolateral membrane of parietal cells activates adenylate cyclase leading to a high intracellular cyclic AMP concentration and activation of protein kinase A. This process has the effect of recruiting more proton pumps (H/K+-ATPase) from the cytoplasm to the luminal (apical membrane of the parietal leading to increase acid production.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The proton is exchanged with magnesium ions\",\"e\":\"In the proton pump a proton is exchanged with a potassium ion (H+/K+-ATPase) at the expense of an ATP molecule\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Acetylcholine-stimulated acid production is independent of the proton pump\",\"e\":\"Acetylcholine-stimulated acid production is again dependent on the proton pump. Acetylcholine binds muscarinic (Ma) receptors on the basolateral membrane of the parietal cells, a G protein coupled receptor, and stimulates the secretion of gastric acid via the\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The proton pump spans the apical membrane of the gastric parietal cell\",\"e\":\"The proton pump. (H+/K+-ATPase) is found in the apical (luminal) membrane of gastric parietal cells and secretes protons into the lumen of the stomach in exchange for potassium ions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The proton pump spans the basolateral membrane of the gastric parietal cell\",\"e\":\"The proton pump (H+/K+-ATPase) is found in the apical (luminal) membrane of the parietal cell and not the basolateral membrane.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A patient is noted to have macrocytic anaemia. After subsequent investigation this is found to be pernicious anaemia.\\n\\nWhich of the following cells secretes intrinsic factor?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following cells secretes intrinsic factor?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Goblet cells\",\"e\":\"Goblet cells are found in the lining of the crypts of the small intestine and in the respiratory tract and produce mucus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Kupffer cells\",\"e\":\"Kupffer cells, a specialised macrophage group also known as stellate cells, are found in the liver. Their primary action is the recycling of old red blood cells. The red blood cells are broken down by phagocytic action.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Peptic cells\",\"e\":\"Peptic cells, also known as chief or zymogenic cells, produce the zymogen pepsinogen, which when in contact with the hydrochloric acid of the stomach is converted to the active enzyme pepsin, involved in the breakdown of proteins.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Chief cells\",\"e\":\"Chief cells, also known as peptic or zymogenic cells, produce the zymogen pepsinogen, which when in contact with the hydrochloric acid of the stomach is converted to the active enzyme pepsin, involved in the breakdown of proteins.\\n\\nGoblet cells are mucus-secreting cells, widely distributed throughout epithelial surfaces, but especially dense in the gastrointestinal and respiratory tracts. Kupffer cells have phagocytic properties and are found in the liver. They participate in the removal of ageing erythrocytes and other particulate debris. The gastric mucosa contains many cell subtypes, including acid-secreting cells (also known as parietal or oxyntic cells). pepsin secreting cells (also known as peptic, chief or zymogenic cells) and G-cells (gastrin-secreting cells). Peptic cells synthesise and secrete the proteolytic enzyme. pepsin. Parietal cells actively secrete hydrochloric acid into the gastric lumen, accounting for the acidic environment encountered in the stomach. However parietal cells are also involved in the secretion of the glycoprotein. intrinsic factor. Intrinsic factor plays a pivotal role in the absorption of vitamin B12 from the terminal ileum. Autoimmune damage to parietal cells leads to a lack of intrinsic factor and hydrochloric acid, leading to vitamin Biz deficiency and achlorhydria. This is known as pernicious anaemia. Pernicious anaemia is associated with a 3-fold increase in gastric cancer risk\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Parietal cells\",\"e\":\"Parietal cells of the stomach have a dual secretory function. They secrete hydrochloric acid. responsible for the acidic environment of the stomach, but also secrete intrinsic factor. Intrinsic factor is a glycoprotein essential for the absorption of vitamin Biz by the terminal ileum. In the absence of intrinsic factor, vitamin Biz deficiency, a condition called pernicious anaemia, occurs that trebles the patient's risk of developing gastric cancer.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old woman presents with sudden onset severe epigastric pain. On examination they have board-like rigidity of their anterior abdominal wall. An erect CR reveals air above the liver and they are taken to theatre for a perforated gastric ulcer.\\n\\nGastric acid secretion is stimulated by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Gastric acid secretion is stimulated by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Somatostatin\",\"e\":\"Somatostatin inhibits the release of gastric acid. Secretion of somatostatin is stimulated when the acid of the stomach drops too low. Somatostatin inhibits the release of gastric acid secretion both by having a direct effect on the parietal cells but also by inhibiting the release of histamine and gastrin, both of which stimulate gastric acid secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastrin\",\"e\":\"Gastrin is the main hormone that acts as a stimulant for gastric acid secretion during a meal Stimulation of gastrin release occurs in response to the presence of luminal amino acids in the stomach. Gastrin is released by antral G-cells found in the gastric pits. Gastrin binds the gastrin receptors on the parietal cells of the stomach, promoting acid secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Secretin\",\"e\":\"Secretin is a hormone secreted from the S-cells in the duodenum in response to the arrival of acidic gastric chyme in the duodenum. Secretin influences the release of pancreatic enzymes by increasing the rate of bicarbonate synthesis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The glossopharyngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"One-third of the total production of gastric acid secretin is stimulated via the cephalic phase in which the anticipation/smell/taste of food signals through the vagus nerve (cranial nerve X) and not the glossopharyngeal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cholecystokinin\",\"e\":\"Cholecystokinin is a hormone released from the I-cells of the duodenum in response to pancreatic chyme entering the small bowel. In the presence of fatty acids it induces contraction of the smooth muscle of the gall-bladder and relaxation of the sphincter of oddi. Additionally, it affects secretion of the pancreatic enzymes in response to a meal, by increasing the synthesis of bicarbonate.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man presents with profound jaundice. An ultrasound abdomen is obtained which shows a profoundly dilated biliary tree and a gallstone visible within the common bile duct. They undergo ERCP for stone clearance and their jaundice resolves Bile salt reuptake principally occurs in which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"They undergo ERCP for stone clearance and their jaundice resolves Bile salt reuptake principally occurs in which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Duodenum\",\"e\":\"Iron is absorbed in the duodenum, where there is also absorption of carbohydrates and amino acids.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AMI Jejunum\",\"e\":\"The jejunum is the site of absorption of calcium, carbohydrates and amino acids\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"ileum\",\"e\":\"About 95% of bile salts are actively reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and these are excreted again from the liver in a cycle called enterohepatic circulation: There is a very small amount of bile salt synthesis but the enterohepatic circulation ensures they are re-used. The entire pool of bile salts recycles about 6-8 times per day. Vitamin Big is also absorbed at the terminal ileum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Colon\",\"e\":\"The colon is where water and sodium is reabsorbed\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Caecum\",\"e\":\"There is sodium and potassium absorption in the caecum.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old woman suffers with dumping syndrome. She has undergone a gastric bypass procedure as part of a bariatric operation to treat her morbid obesity\\n\\nWhich of the following best describes the rate at which a liquid meal leaves the stomach?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following best describes the rate at which a liquid meal leaves the stomach?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Greater in the upright than in the supine position\",\"e\":\"Gastric emptying rate is accelerated when a patient lies supine rather than standing.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Proportional to the volume of stomach content\",\"e\":\"The rate of gastric emptying is influenced by two factors: volume of stomach contents and composition of stomach contents. In the absence of stomach distention there is little stimulation for gastric motility and therefore the rate of emptying is slower. Isotonic fluids are emptied at a maximal rate, hypertonic meals have a slower rate of gastric emptying.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Greater if the meal contains fat\",\"e\":\"Fat reaching the duodenum slows gastric emptying.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Slower if the meal is 5% glucose than if it is 50% glucose\",\"e\":\"The more hypertonic or nutrient-rich the meal is, the slower the rate of gastric emptying. Therefore with a meal of 5% glucose emptying will be faster than that of a meal containing 50% glucose.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Slower if vagotomy and drainage procedure (such as gastroenterostomy or pyloroplasty) has been performed\",\"e\":\"In patients who had a vagotomy and draining procedure (such as gastroenterostomy or pyloroplasty) performed, there is an increase in the rate of gastric emptying. in the postoperative period, gastric emptying starts becoming faster, and continues to increase until reaching a steady state at 2-3 months post-operation.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A patient is unwell on the ward, it is unclear as to the cause. When the nurse takes the patient's blood pressure their hand contracts into a claw which causes the patient a lot of discomfort. They are exhibiting Trosseau's sign and are treated for hypocalcaemia. Which of the following is most accurate regarding the absorption of calcium from the digestive tract?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is most accurate regarding the absorption of calcium from the digestive tract?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"it takes place in the proximal jejunum\",\"e\":\"Absorption of calcium occurs in the duodenum and jejunum, and is both active - under the regulation of calcitriol - and passive, through tight junctions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is prevented by the presence of smail amounts of phytic acid in the diet, even when an excess calcium is ingested\",\"e\":\"Phytic acid binds to calcium and forms the insoluble calcium phytate. Once all phytic acid is saturated and formed a complex with calcium, then the excess calcium remains free and can be absorbed by the intestine.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is facilitated by the presence of fat in food\",\"e\":\"Fatty acids form insoluble calcium salts and prevent its absorption by the intestines.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It can be reversed (calcium is secreted into bowel lumen) when plasma calcium concentration is raised by a calcium infusion\",\"e\":\"Calcium is not secreted in the bowel. The kidneys filter calcium ions and there is a net average loss of 5 mmol of calcium daily.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is about as rapid as that of sodium\",\"e\":\"Sodium absorption is much faster than that of calcium (up to 50 times faster).\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man eats a high protein meal as part of their recovery from major surgery. They are now able to meet their calorific needs so that total parenteral nutrition can be stopped Pepsinogen is secreted from which cells of the stomach?\",\"lead\":\"They are now able to meet their calorific needs so that total parenteral nutrition can be stopped Pepsinogen is secreted from which cells of the stomach?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Parietal cells\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chief cells\",\"e\":\"Chief cells are the cells that secrete pepsinogen. Pepsinogen is the inactive form of the enzyme pepsin. Once released into the stomach and exposed to the acidic environment pepsinogen is turned into pepsin, an enzyme involved in the digestion of proteins.\\n\\nParietal cells are involved in the secretion of gastric acid, in the form of hydrochloric acid. and intrinsic factor.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"G cells\",\"e\":\"G-cells in the stomach produce gastrin\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mucous cells!\",\"e\":\"Mucous cells secreted mucous.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Kupffer cells\",\"e\":\"Kupffer cells are found in the liver and are involved in the breakdown of red blood cells.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old man complains of intermittent right upper quadrant pain. On further history they find the pain is particularly bad after junk food - they are subsequently diagnosed with biliary colic. During digestion of a meal like this, which hormone causes contraction of the gall-bladder and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi?\",\"lead\":\"During digestion of a meal like this, which hormone causes contraction of the gall-bladder and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cholecystokinin\",\"e\":\"Cholecystokinin is a hormone released from the I-cells of the duodenum in response to pancreatic chyme entering the small bowel. In the presence of fatty acids it induces contraction of the smooth muscle of the gall-bladder and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi allowing release of bile and pancreatic enzymes in the second part of the duodenum. Additionally, it affects secretion of the pancreatic enzymes in response to a meal, by increasing the synthesis of bicarbonate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastrin\",\"e\":\"Gastrin is produced by the antral G-cells in the stomach, and is the main hormone involved in the stimulation of gastric acid. Gastrin binds the gastrin receptors on the parietal cells to promote secretion of gastric acid\\n\\nInsulin is an anabolic hormone released from the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans of the pancreas, involved in the regulation of serum glucose concentration. It is released when the serum glucose concentration is high and drives potassium and glucose into the cells, while inducing glycogenesis and inhibiting glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Insulin\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Secretin\",\"e\":\"Secretin is produced by the S-cells in the duodenum, in response to the arrival of acidic gastric chyme: It increases the secretion of pancreatic enzymes in the duodenum, acts synergistically with cholecystokinin and also inhibits the release of gastric acid from the stomach.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Somatostatin\",\"e\":\"Somatostatin is released from the D-cells found in the pancreas, stomach and intestine and acts to inhibit the release of insulin, glucagon, gastrin, histamine and gastric acid\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man eats a high protein meal as part of their recovery from major surgery. They are now able to meet their calorific needs so that total parenteral nutrition can be stopped. in the stomach, chief cells release pepsinogen. Pepsinogen is directly activated by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Pepsinogen is directly activated by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cholecystokinin\",\"e\":\"Cholecystokinin affects the secretion of pancreatic juice and causes the contraction of the gall-bladder and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi, to release bile and pancreatic enzymes into the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chymotrypsin\",\"e\":\"Chymotrypsin is a protease enzyme secreted from the pancreas, involved in protein digestion, but not involved in the activation of pepsinogen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastrin and pepsin\",\"e\":\"Pepsin in the active form of pepsinogen and is under the influence of an auto-catalytic process, where the presence of active pepsin stimulates secretion of more pepsin. Gastrin stimulates gastric acid secretion, which in turn activates pepsin. However gastrin is not a direct stimulator of pepsin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Trypsin and acid pH\",\"e\":\"Trypsin is another protease involved in the breakdown and digestion of protein, but it is not involved in the activation of pepsinogen. Conversely, acid pH activates pepsinogen by converting it to pepsin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Acid pH and pre-existing pepsin\",\"e\":\"Pepsinogen, the proenzyme of pepsin, is released in the stomach by chief cells and, once in contact with the hydrochloric acid in the stomach (causing a low pH), becomes activated into pepsin. Pepsin is a protease composed of 44 amino acids, an enzyme involved in the breakdown of proteins. Pepsin is under the influence of an auto-catalytic process, in which the presence of active pepsin stimulates the secretion of more pepsin.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old woman presents with chronic pancreatitis and describes pain after eating. She is subsequently started on CREON® which appears to improve their symptoms. Which of these statements regarding the cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which of these statements regarding the cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion is true?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Its main result is the production of bicarbonate\",\"e\":\"HCO: secretion is mainly affected by the hormones cholecystokinin and secretin involved. in the intestinal phase of pancreatic secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Its main result is the production of active anzymes\",\"e\":\"The cephalic phase affects the acinar cells to release inactive zymogens. Once these chemicals are released into the duodenum these are converted to active enzymes that digest food products in the small intestine. As the zymogens are the inactive precursors of the active enzymes, any alteration to the cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion will eventually impact on overall enzymatic secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It accounts for approximately 10% of the total pancreatic secretion\",\"e\":\"The cephalic phase of the pancreatic meal response accounts for about 20-25% of the total pancreatic exocrine secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A vagotomy will lead changes during this phase\",\"e\":\"The cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion involves the integration of sensory inputs, such as sight, smell and taste of food as well as the process of mastication and the presence of certain molecules binding on receptors in the mouth. It is under the control of the vagus nerve. Inputs are integrated in the brainstem and, specifically, the dorsal vagal complex. It is transmitted down to the pancreas via the vagus nerve through cholinergic fibres innervating the acinar cells. Stimulation of the acinar cells leads to pancreatic enzyme secretions. Therefore, a vagotomy would interrupt this circuit of information and reduce the pancreatic secretion from the cephalic phase.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It will result after administration of a sympathetic antagonist\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerve is the nerve involved in the stimulation of the cephalic phase, therefore the cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion is under the influence of the parasympathetic system. Administration of a sympathetic antagonist would not decrease the response from the cephalic phase.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"A patient has suspected irritable bowel syndrome. They have extensive investigations to rule out any pathology as they are struggling with their symptoms. They are found to have a deficiency of maitase Deficiency of maltase in the brush border of small intestine:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Does not prevent nydrolysis or maltose because it can be hydrolysed by pancreatic amylase\",\"e\":\"Maltase is a carbohydrase enzyme, secreted by the villi of the small intestine, involved in the digestion of carbohydrates from the diet. Specifically, it is involved in the hydrolysis of the disaccharide maltose, by breaking down the glycosidic bond, to two glucose molecules. Amylase, in contrast, is a carbohydrase produced by the pancreas and the salivary glands and is involved in hydrolysing polysaccharides into smaller carbohydrate molecules such as maltose.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increases rate of absorption of ingested glucose\",\"e\":\"Ingested starches are hydrolysed by amylase into small polysaccharides and disaccharides, such as maltose. Maltose is the substrate for the maltase enzyme and breaks down maltose in two glucose molecules. Therefore, maltase increases the amount of glucose from ingestion that is available for absorption in the small intestine and increasing the rate of absorption as well.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Results in lack of absorption of lactose\",\"e\":\"Maltase deficiency results in lack of absorption of maltose. It is a deficiency in lactase. the enzyme that hydrolyses lactose to glucose and galactose, that results in the lack of absorption of lactose.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Results in decreased frequency of stool\",\"e\":\"Maltase deficiency results in increased frequency of stool.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Results in increased passage of maltose in stool\",\"e\":\"Maltase deficiency will result in a reduction in the hydrolysis of maltose, therefore there is an increased passage of maltose in the stool.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"Gastrointestinal (Gl) resistance vessels are affected by their unique vascular properties, by the presence of vasodilator metabolites, by autonomic nerves and by a variety of circulating agents. Which one of the following circulating factors dilates these vessels?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following circulating factors dilates these vessels?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline\",\"e\":\"Adrenaline, noradrenaline and dopamine released from the adrenal medulla are potent vasoconstrictors of the gastrointestinal resistance vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Angiotensin II\",\"e\":\"Angiotensin Il is released as a consequence of the activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone pathway, following the release of renal renin, and causes vasoconstriction of the gastrointestinal resistance vessels\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Noradrenaline\",\"e\":\"Adrenaline, noradrenaline and dopamine that are released from the adrenal medulla are potent vasoconstrictors of the gastrointestinal resistance vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vasoactive intestinal peptide\",\"e\":\"The vasoactive intestinal peptide, is a 28 amino acid peptide hormone belonging to the glucagon/secretin family. It has various roles in the intestine including: causing vasodilation in the gastrointestinal resistance vessels, relaxation of enteric smooth muscle, inhibition of gastric acid secretion and stimulation of pancreatic juice and bile secretion. Other agents that cause gastrointestinal resistance vessel dilation include: gastrin, glucagon and cholecystokinin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vasopressin\",\"e\":\"Vasopressin, also known as antidiuretic hormone, is released from the posterior pituitary and has a vasoconstrictor effect on the gastrointestinal resistance vessels.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following factors reduces gastric blood flow?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following factors reduces gastric blood flow?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acetylcholine\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastrin\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Histamine\",\"e\":\"Histamine stimulates gastric acid secretion and increases gastric blood flow via direct vasodilator effect on the gastric mucosa arterioles and indirect through an increase in vasodilator metabolites.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vagal stimulation\",\"e\":\"Vagal stimulation, cholinergic agents, histamine and gastrin are all known to increase gastric acid production and have been found to increase gastric blood flow as well through an increase in vasodilator metabolites.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vasopressin\",\"e\":\"Vasopressin and secretin decrease gastric acid secretion and have been shown to decrease gastric blood flow. Additionally, noradrenaline decreases gastric blood flow by causing vasoconstriction of the gastric arterioles. Similarly, hypovolaemia and shock can decrease gastric blood flow. Maintaining a good gastric blood flow is necessary for protecting the gastric mucosa from damage from the acid gastric contents.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"Glucagon is secreted by the a-cells of the pancreatic islets. This subsequently leads to gluconeogenesis. Which one of the following is most likely to induce glucagon secretion?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most likely to induce glucagon secretion?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"High serum concentration of glucose\",\"e\":\"High serum concentration of glucose is a stimulator of insulin production by the b-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Low serum concentration of amino acids\",\"e\":\"A high serum concentration of amino acids stimulates the secretion of glucagon, which in turn stimulates gluconeogenesis in the liver, ie conversion of amino acids to glucose\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Low serum concentration of glucose\",\"e\":\"Glucagon* is a hormone released from the alpha-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas, in response to a low serum concentration of glucose. Glucagon acts or the liver to stimulate breakdown of the glycogen stores, a process called glycogenolysis, by increasing intracellular cyclic AMP levels. A high concentration of serum amino acids can also stimulate the release of glucagon.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Secretion of somatostatin by the pancreatic d-cells\",\"e\":\"Secretion * of somatostatin inhibits glucagon and insulin release.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Parasympathetic stimulation\",\"e\":\"Glucagon secretion is under the influence of sympathetic and parasympathetic systems Activation of both can stimulate glucagon secretion.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"The liver is the main site of glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. A patient has starved for several hours while waiting for surgery. Which one of the following hormones increases hepatic glycogenolysis and promotes gluconeogenesis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following hormones increases hepatic glycogenolysis and promotes gluconeogenesis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cholecystokinin\",\"e\":\"Cholecystokinin is a hormone released from the I-cells of the duodenum in response to pancreatic chyme entering the small bowel. In the presence of fatty acids it induces contraction of the smooth muscle of the gall-bladder and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi. Additionally, it affects secretion of the pancreatic enzymes in response to a meal, by increasing the synthesis of bicarbonate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glucagon\",\"e\":\"Glucagon * is a peptide hormone composed of 29 amino acids, released from the alpha-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas in response to a low serum concentration of glucose. Glucagon acts on the liver to stimulate breakdown of the glycogen stores, a process known as glycogenolysis, by increasing intracellular cyclic AMP levels. It also stimulates gluconeogenesis. Both of these processes have the effect of increasing circulating levels of glucose. It is thought that glucagon has a minor effect in enhancing lipolysis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Growth hormone\",\"e\":\"Growth hormone is an anabolic hormone promoting gluconeogenesis in the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Insulin\",\"e\":\"Insulin* is a peptide hormone released by the beta-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas in response to a rise in serum glucose. Its actions include: stimulation of glucose and potassium uptake by the cells, glycogen synthesis in the liver and muscle (glycogenesis). inhibition of glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, increased amino acid uptake, decreased lipolysis and proteolysis and increased lipid synthesis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Secretin\",\"e\":\"Secretin is released by the S-cells in the duodenum in response to entry of gastric chyme and influences the release of pancreatic enzymes by increasing the rate of bicarbonate synthesis.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"You are performing a laparoscopic appendicectomy and a medical student is holding the camera for you. They are unable to differentiate the small bowel from the large bowel. You give a short description of the normal function of the colon. Which one of the following best describes this?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describes this?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Absorption of sodium and water\",\"e\":\"Sodium and water absorption are the main functions of the colon. Sodium is actively absorbed with the aid of a basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase that creates an electrochemical gradient. Water absorption in the colon is via a passive mechanism mostly via enterocyte tight junctions, and is dependent on the absorption of sodium and other solutes, creating electrochemical gradients.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Absorption of bicarbonate\",\"e\":\"Bicarbonate reabsorption occurs predominantly in the proximal convoluted tubule of the kidney. In the jejunum however, its absorption assists in the active transport of sodium against electrochemical gradients.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Absorption of bile salts\",\"e\":\"Bile acids are absorbed in gastrointestinal tract both actively and passively. Active absorption occurs in the ileum. Passive forms of absorption can occur at any point in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract and includes: ionic, non-ionic and micellar diffusion mechanisms\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"secretion of sodium\",\"e\":\"In the colon sodium is reabsorbed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"secretion of chloride\",\"e\":\"Chloride is reabsorbed in the colon, along with sodium. 2062\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man suffers with upper abdominal pain after eating. Secretin is released into the circulation in response to low duodenal pH. Which one of the following is the best description with regard to the action of secretin?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the best description with regard to the action of secretin?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreases water and bicarbonate secretion from the duodenum\",\"e\":\"Secretin stimulates the release of bicarbonate in the duodenum, pancreatic and biliary ducts\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreases the release of bile from the gallbladder\",\"e\":\"Secretin stimulates the release of bile from the gall-bladder.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inhibits the contraction of the gallbladder\",\"e\":\"Secretin augments the action of cholecystokinin and therefore stimulates contraction of the gall-bladder.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduces insulin released from the pancreas\",\"e\":\"Secretin stimulates insulin release from the pancreas following glucose ingestion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stimulates the pancreas to secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid\",\"e\":\"Secretin is secreted by the S-cells in the duodenum in response to the arrival of gastric chyme, which is acidic, reducing the pH of the intestinal lumen. Secretin binds to G-coupled protein secretin receptors in various tissues to exert its effects. Secretin increases the release of bicarbonate from the duodenum and release of bile from the gall-bladder. It also stimulates pepsin secretion from the stomach and insulin release from the pancreas. Secretin inhibits gastrin release and gastric acid secretion.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"You are advising a patient about their diet, they are overweight and you want to reduce their risk for future planned abdominal surgery. Following the conversation a prospective medical student who has been shadowing you asks from which area fat is most predominantly absorbed\\n\\nWhat would be the most appropriate answer?\",\"lead\":\"What would be the most appropriate answer?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antrum\",\"e\":\"In the stomach part of lipid digestion takes place in the presence of lipase enzymes but absorption of fats does not occur here:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Colon\",\"e\":\"In the colon there is absorption of water and sodium but not lipid absorption.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Proximal small intestine\",\"e\":\"Fat is emulsified in the proximal small intestine (duodenum and jejunum) and free fatty acids are taken up into the enterocytes, via a protein-dependent active transport, in the form of fatty acids, glycerol and mono-or di-glycerides. Once in the enterocyte they reform triglycerides and are packaged with cholesterol into chylomicrons, which are transported outside the cells. Some short chain fatty acids are transported via diffusion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pylorus\",\"e\":\"In the stomach part of lipid digestion occurs in the presence of lipase enzymes, but the fat is not absorbed here.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Terminal Ileum\",\"e\":\"In the terminal ileum there is a significant amount fat absorption, however this absorption is greatest in the proximal small intestine.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old woman is due to undergo a subtotal colectomy, She is concerned about the physiological consequences of not having a colon post-operatively. The colon has a large absorptive capacity. Which one of the following is the most likely component actively absorbed by the colon?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely component actively absorbed by the colon?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amino acids\",\"e\":\"Amino acids are absorbed in the small intestine, with the majority of absorption occurring in the jejunum and the remainder in the ileum. They are absorbed by co-transport with Na through sodium dependent amino acid transporters.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bicarbonate\",\"e\":\"Bicarbonate reabsorption occurs predominantly in the proximal convoluted tubule of the kidney. In the jejunum however, its absorption assists in the active transport of sodium against electrochemical gradients.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bile salts\",\"e\":\"Bile acids are absorbed in gastrointestinal tract both actively and passively. Active absorption occurs in the ileum. Passive forms of absorption can occur at any point in the gastrointestinal (Gl) tract and includes: ionic, non-ionic and micellar diffusion mechanisms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sodium\",\"e\":\"Sodium is actively absorbed in the colon with the aid of a basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase that creates an electrochemical gradient.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Water\",\"e\":\"In total, 80% of water is absorbed by the small intestine. Water absorption in the colon is via a passive mechanism mostly via enterocyte tight junctions, and is dependent on the absorption of sodium and other solutes, creating electrochemical gradients.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":54,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man has been started on omeprazole due to dyspepsia. Gastric acid secretion is important for the pH of the stomach. Which one of the following statements about gastric acid is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about gastric acid is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Controlled by pepsin\",\"e\":\"Gastrin is the main hormone that acts as a stimulant for gastric acid secretion during a meal. Castrin release occurs in response to the presence of luminal amino acids in the stomach. Gastrin is released by G cells found in the gastric pits: Gastrin binds to the gastrin receptors on the parietal cells of the stomach promoting acid secretion. Other hormones stimulating acid secretion (HCI) by the parietal cells include: acetylcholine (secreted from postganglionic neurons) and histamine (released from enterochromaffin like cells). Pepsin is the active form of the enzyme called pepsinogen, produced by the Chief cells of the stomach. Once exposed to the acidic environment of the stomach, pepsinogen is converted to pepsin, which is involved in the breakdown of proteins to amino-acids.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"H cells secrete gastrin\",\"e\":\"Gastrin is secreted by G cells in the stomach, found in the gastric pits.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Passively secreted\",\"e\":\"Gastric acid is secreted actively. Binding of gastrin, acetylcholine and histamine to parietal cells of the stomach promotes an increase in intracellular CAMP. IPs, diacylglycerol and calcium. This in turn promotes fusion of vesicles containing H+/K+/ATPase molecules with the membrane of the parietal cells. The increase in the transmembranous receptors, promotes proton secretion and therefore acid secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Promoted by vagal nerve stimulation\",\"e\":\"Acetylcholine released by the enteric neurons in response to vagal nerve stimulation promotes the production of acid by the stomach\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sympathetic nervous system activates the release\",\"e\":\"It is parasympathetic nervous system activation, via the stimulation of the vagal nerve and release of acetylcholine, that promotes the production of gastric acid.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old heavy smoker undergoes radiotherapy for laryngeal cancer. As a result of this, his saliva production is reduced due to irradiation of the salivary glands. Which one of the following would be true regarding his saliva production?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would be true regarding his saliva production?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"He would previously have secreted >3000ml/day\",\"e\":\"Normal saliva production in adults varies from 1-2 litres per day. The secretion of saliva from the salivary glands is mediated by the autonomic nervous system. The submandibular glands contribute about three-quarters of the saliva, with one-quarter produced by the parotid gland. The other salivary glands only contribute a small amount of saliva to the total amount produced\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It should have the same pH as plasma\",\"e\":\"Plasma pH in a healthy adult is in the range of 7.35-7.45, whereas saliva pH is slightly acidic and ranges from 6.2-7.4. This is very important in preventing teeth minerals from dissolving.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It will contain a lower molar concentration of potassium than that found in plasma\",\"e\":\"The concentration of potassium in saliva is higher than that found in plasma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It will contain amylase\",\"e\":\"Human saliva is composed of water (99.5%) as well as electrolytes, epithelial cells, white blood cells, enzymes and antimicrobial agents. The enzymes include lipase and amylase. which commence the process of digestion of dietary fats and starches respectively in the mouth. Antimicrobial agents found in saliva include IgA antibodies and lysozyme.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It will produce hypertonic secretions\",\"e\":\"The salivary glands produce a hypotonic solution. Saliva is made in the acinar cells of the salivary glands. It is initially isotonic to plasma but as it moves down the salivary ducts its composition is changed, resulting to the end saliva being secreted with a lower concentration of NaCI and a higher concentration of KHCO, than plasma. The saliva secreted from the glands is therefore hypotonic.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old man with cystic fibrosis receives supplements of pancreatin to help with the digestion of starch, fat and protein. He has been reading articles online and is wondering if there are any hormonal therapies which may help his condition. Secretion of pancreatic juice is primarily under the control of which two hormones?\",\"lead\":\"Secretion of pancreatic juice is primarily under the control of which two hormones?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cholecystokinin and glucagon\",\"e\":\"Cholecystokinin and secretin control the secretion of pancreatic enzymes (pancreatic juice). Glucagon is released by the a-cells of the pancreas in response to a drop in glucose concentration in the blood and stimulates the liver to breakdown glycogen stores and release glucose (glycogenolysis), as well as activating hepatic gluconeogenesis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cholecystokinin and insulin\",\"e\":\"Cholecystokinin and secretin control the secretion of pancreatic enzymes (pancreatic juice):\\n\\nInsulin is released by the pancreatic b-cells and regulates carbohydrates fat and protein metabolism. It is an anabolic hormone released in response to a raised glucose concentration in the blood and works by increasing cellular uptake of glucose and the formation of the glycogen stores in the liver, a process known as glycogenesis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cholecystokinin and secretin\",\"e\":\"Secretion of pancreatic juice in the intestinal phase is regulated by the hormone pair of cholecystokinin and secretin. As the gastric chyme enters the small intestine, its acidity stimulates S cells and I cells in the duodenum to release secretin and cholecystokinin, respectively, into the blood. Secretin and cholecystokinin increase the synthesis of aqueous bicarbonate. This process in turns drives secretion of inactive pancreatic enzymes (pancreatic juice) in the gut lumen where they become activated and carry out digestive processes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Glucagon and insulin\",\"e\":\"Glucagon and insulin are produced by the a- and b-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas respectively and are the hormones that regulate glucose concentration in the\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Glucagon and secretin\",\"e\":\"Cholecystokinin and secretin control the secretion of pancreatic enzymes (pancreatic juice).\\n\\nGlucagon is released by the a-cells of the pancreas in response to a drop in glucose concentration in the blood and stimulates the liver to breakdown glycogen stores and release glucose, as well as activating hepatic gluconeogenesis.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man presents with frank jaundice and is found to have a gallstone obstructing the common bile duct. He undergoes ERCP and makes a prompt recovery without developing cholangitis.\\n\\nWhich one of the following stimulates the release of bile from the gallbladder?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following stimulates the release of bile from the gallbladder?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ADH\",\"e\":\"Anti-diuretic hormone (ADH) is a hormone involved in thereabsorption of sodium and water in the kidneys.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cholecystokinin\",\"e\":\"Bile salts arrive to the liver via the portal circulation and bile is produced in the liver. It is stored in the gallbladder until required. Once digested fatty acids arrive at the duodenum, the I cells are stimulated to release cholecystokinin. This hormone, triggers smooth muscle contraction in the gallbladder and smooth muscle relaxation at the sphincter of Oddi, both allowing for release of bile in the small bowel.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Parasympathetic nervous system\",\"e\":\"The parasympathetic nervous system affects gallbladder muscle tone and can influence the rate of bile secretion but to a lesser extent\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renin\",\"e\":\"Renin affects the conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin, but is not involves in bile secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Somatostatin\",\"e\":\"The output of bile acids is reduced by the action of somatostatin\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man with type | diabetes is admitted with suspected appendicitis for diagnostic laparoscopy. He is to be kept nil by mouth until the next morning Regarding glucose metabolism, what normally stimulates glycogenesis in the liver?\",\"lead\":\"He is to be kept nil by mouth until the next morning Regarding glucose metabolism, what normally stimulates glycogenesis in the liver?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Glucagon\",\"e\":\"Glucagon * is a hormone released from the a-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas in response to a drop in serum glucose. Glucagon acts on the liver to stimulate breakdown of the glycogen stores, a process called glycogenolysis, by increasing intracellular cyclic AMP levels.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Insulin\",\"e\":\"Insulin* is a peptide hormone released by the b-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreast in response to a raise in serum glucose. Its action include: stimulation of glucose and potassium uptake by the cells, glycogen synthesis in the liver and muscle (glycogenesis). inhibition of glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, increased amino acid uptake, decreased lipolysis and proteolysis and increased lipid synthesis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cortisol\",\"e\":\"In the fasting state, cortisol influences the liver to breakdown glycogen stores and commence gluconeogenesis to increase serum glucose concentration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline (epinephrine)\",\"e\":\"Adrenaline stimulates glycogenolysis by increasing intracellular calcium levels.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyroxine.)\",\"e\":\"Thyroxine stimulates carbohydrate metabolism, it acts in synergy with insulin for insulin-dependent glucose entry into cells but it also stimulates glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.\"}]},{\"t\":79,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"A 24-year-old woman comes to the Emergency Department with right iliac fossa and hip pain some 3 weeks after appendicectomy following rupture and subsequent peritonitis. She has deteriorated over the past week suffering swinging fevers, night sweats and worsening pain. On examination, she is pyrexial 37.9°C, BP is 105/70 mmHg. She is lying down with the right hip slightly flexed as she finds this most comfortable, and is very tender in the right iliac fossa.\\n\\nInvestigations are below:\\n\\nResult Normal value Investigation\\n\\n12.0 g/dl 11.5-15.5 g/dl Haemoglobin (Hb)\\n\\n4-11 x 109/1 11.3 × 109/1 White cell count (WCC)\\n\\n203 × 109/1 150-400 × 109/1 Platelets (PLT)\\n\\n137 mmol/l 135-145 mmol/l Sodium (Nat)\\n\\n4.5 mmol/l 3:5-5.0 mmol/l Potassium (K=)\\n\\n105 umol/l 50-120 umol/l Creatinine\\n\\n205 mg/l 0-10 mg/l C-reactive Protein (CRP)\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\\n\\nCreatinine 105 umol/l 50-120 umol/l\\n\\n0-10 mg/l C-reactive Protein (CRP) 205 mg/l\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adhesions\",\"e\":\"Adhesions is incorrect. The steadily worsening fevers and abdominal pain fit better with an abscess.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"iliopsoas bursitis\",\"e\":\"Iliopsoas bursitis is incorrect. Swinging fevers and raised inflammatory markers are not a feature of bursitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Psoas abscess\",\"e\":\"The history of ruptured appendix and peritonitis, coupled with the characteristic RIF pain and painful, flexed hip, raises the possibility of a psoas abscess. Whilst abdominal films may suggest loss of psoas abscess definition, CT scanning is the definitive investigation. Percutaneous drainage under ultrasound guidance is thought to be successful in up to 90% of cases:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Septic arthritis\",\"e\":\"Septic arthritis is incorrect. Whilst it would fit with the raised inflammatory markers and intermittent fevers, abdominal pain would be unexpected. In addition, given the recent appendicectomy, a psoas abscess is significantly more likely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Post operative hernia\",\"e\":\"Post-operative hernia, is incorrect. Fevers and raised inflammatory markers would not be expected with a post-operative hernia.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A patient suffers a duodenal ulcer perforation. In response to the massive peritoneal contamination they feel sick, tachycardic and like they may collapse. Stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system leads to which one of be following?\",\"lead\":\"Stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system leads to which one of be following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bronchial constriction\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation will cause bronchial relaxation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased sweating\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation will cause an increase in sweating.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased peristalsis\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation will cause a decrease in peristalsis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inhibition of lipolysis\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation will cause an increase in lipolysis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Peripheral vasoconstriction\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation will cause peripheral vasoconstriction by acting on alpha adrenergic receptors.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"The mother of a patient involved in a car accident comes to visit her son. She is told that he has passed away. She becomes sweaty, anxious and unsteady on her feet. Which of the following statements concerning the sympathetic nervous system is most accurate?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements concerning the sympathetic nervous system is most accurate?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cell bodies of the postganglionic neurones lie in the intermediolateral horn of the spinal cord\",\"e\":\"The preganglionic neurones have their cell bodies in the intermediolateral horn of the spinal cord\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Efferent neurones leave the spinal cord from all thoracic and lumbar segments\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic nerves originate in the spinal cord from segments T1-L2. They leave the spinal cord in the anterior roots and pass to the paired sympathetic chains\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Preganglionic neurones reach the adrenal medulla without synapsing\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic innervation to the adrenal medulla is unique in the regard that there is no synapse at ganglia between the spinal cord and organ. The 'preganglionic neurone innervates the Chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla (through acetylcholine), causing direct release of catecholamines into the bloodstream.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The sympathetic chain extends three quarters of the length of the vertebral column\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic chain extends the entire length of the vertebral column.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The vagus nerve carries postganglionic sympathetic neurones\",\"e\":\"There are no sympathetic fibres in the vagus nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the Intensive Care Unit with post-operative sepsis and feel they need inotropic support. They have had multiple fluid challenges; however, their blood pressure remains low.\\n\\nRegarding normal neurotransmitters in the body which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding normal neurotransmitters in the body which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"About a third of postganglionic sympathetic neurones secrete noradrenaline\",\"e\":\"Most postganglionic sympathetic neurones secrete adrenaline or noradrenaline. Exceptions to this is supply to the sweat glands (acetyl cho ine) and the adrenal medulla which has a direct supply from preganglionic fibres (through acetyl choline).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline and noradrenaline act on muscarinic and nicotinic receptors\",\"e\":\"Adrenaline and noradrenaline act primarily on adrenergic receptors (alpha or beta).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"All preganglionic neurones in the autonomic nervous system are cholinergic\",\"e\":\"Acetylcholine and noradrenaline are the two main neurotransmitters secreted in the autonomic nervous system. Neurones secreting acetylcholine are called cholinergic and those secreting noradrenaline are termed adrenergic. Both parasympathetic and sympathetic preganglionic neurones are cholinergic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Most postganglionic sympathetic neurones secrete acetylcholine\",\"e\":\"Most postganglionic sympathetic neurones are adrenergic and secrete adrenaline or noradrenaline. Almost all postganglionic parasympathetic neurones are cholinergic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The adrenal cortex secretes both adrenaline and noradrenaline in response to sympathetic stimulation\",\"e\":\"The adrenal medulla secretes both adrenaline and noradrenaline in response to sympathetic stimulation.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A patient is anaesthetised for their operation. You see the Anaesthetic team attach the patient to the ventilator for the duration of the operation Spontaneous respiration ceases after which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"You see the Anaesthetic team attach the patient to the ventilator for the duration of the operation Spontaneous respiration ceases after which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Transection of the brainstem superior to the pons\",\"e\":\"Since the cardiorespiratory centre in the brain is inferior to this point, the innervation pathway to the muscles required for respiration are intact. It is feasible that spontaneous respiration could continue in a transection superior to the pons.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Transection of the brainstem at the caudal end of the medulla\",\"e\":\"Rhythmic discharge of neurons in the medulla and pons (the cardiorespiratory centre) produces automatic respiration, and so transection of the brainstem below the medulla stops the respiration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bilateral vagotomy combined with transection of the brainstem at the superior border of pons\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transection of the spinal cord at the level of first thoracic segment\",\"e\":\"The respiratory muscles are supplied by C3-C5 segments (diaphragm) and thoracic segments for intercostal and flat muscles of the trunk. A transection at a thoracic level will not affect all of the muscles of respiration. Spontaneous respiration will still be possible.\\n\\nRhythmic discharge of neurons in the medulla and pons produces automatic respiration, and so transection of the brain stem below the medulla stops the respiration. The respiratory muscles are supplied by C3 - C5 segments (diaphragm) and thoracic segments for intercostal & flat muscles of the trunk.\\n\\n(C3,4,5 keep the diaphragm alive!)\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Bilateral vagotomy\",\"e\":\"Spontaneous respiration is not dependent on the vagus nerve. The respiratory muscles are supplied by C3-C5 segments (diaphragm) and thoracic segments for intercostal and flat muscles of the thorax\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A patient is having nerve studies performed for suspected carpal tunnel syndrome. The resting membrane potential of a neuronal cell body is -70 mV Opening potassium channels in the neuronal membrane will most probably cause which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The resting membrane potential of a neuronal cell body is -70 mV Opening potassium channels in the neuronal membrane will most probably cause which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Depolarisation to about -30 my\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Depolarisation to about +30 mV\",\"e\":\"A value of -30 mV is near the Nernst potential for Clions in smooth muscle cells, but not for K+ in neurones.\\n\\nA value of +30 mV is near the Nernst potential for Nations.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyperpolarisation to about -90 mV\",\"e\":\"Increasing the membranes conductance to potassium will result in K+ efflux and the membrane potential approaching the value dictated by the potassium equilibrium potential. which is about -90 mV for neurones.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Initiation of an action potential\",\"e\":\"Action potentials occur if the cell membrane is depolarised above threshold\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"No change in membrane potential\",\"e\":\"The membrane potential would remain unchanged only if the cell resting membrane potential is already at the Nernst potential of the ion channels that were opened.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"In an experiment a drug blocked the transmembrane proteins mainly responsible for the resting membrane potential of vascular smooth muscle cells Which one of the following transmembrane proteins was blocked by the drug?\",\"lead\":\"In an experiment a drug blocked the transmembrane proteins mainly responsible for the resting membrane potential of vascular smooth muscle cells Which one of the following transmembrane proteins was blocked by the drug?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cl channels.. C\",\"e\":\"The resting membrane potential of excitable cells is largely due to the selective permeability of the cell membrane to potassium ions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"K+ channels\",\"e\":\"The resting membrane potential of excitable cells is largely due to the selective permeability of the cell membrane to potassium ions. If the cell membrane were a perfect K+ electrode, the membrane potential would equal the equilibrium potential for K+ as predicted by the Nernst equation. In reality, the resting membrane potential is more positive because of small contributions by Nat channels, Ci-channels and non-selective cation channels.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Na+/K+ pump\",\"e\":\"The Nat/K+ pump generates the ion gradient across the cell membrane (ie, high intracellular K+, high extracellular Na+), but it is the back diffusion of K+ ions through K+ channels that are open at rest, which charges the cell membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Nat channels\",\"e\":\"The resting membrane potential of excitable cells is largely due to the selective permeability of the cell membrane to potassium ions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Non-selective cation channels\",\"e\":\"The resting membrane potential of excitable cells is largely due to the selective permeability of the cell membrane to potassium ions.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"You are rotating through neurosurgery and the consultant asks you some questions on basic neurophysiology. The signal passes through the neuromuscular junction via the neurotransmitter acetylcholine.\\n\\nAfter release from the skeletal muscle neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine does which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"After release from the skeletal muscle neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine does which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Activates presynaptic potassium channels\",\"e\":\"Acetylcholine does not affect presynaptic potassium channels.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Causes postsynaptic depolarisation\",\"e\":\"When acetylcholine is released into the synaptic trough it attaches to acetylcholine-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, causing them to open. The net effect of opening the channels is to allow large numbers of sodium ions to pour inside the fibre. This creates a local potential inside the fibre that initiates an action potential at the muscle membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Enters the sarcoplasmic reticulum\",\"e\":\"Acetylcholine is released first into the synaptic trough. It then acts on acetylcholine-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, causing them to open. It is then recycled into the presynaptic neurone. It does not enter the sarcoplasmic reticulum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"is triggered by acetylcholinesterase\",\"e\":\"Acetylcholinesterase acts within the synaptic trough to deactivate acetylcholine.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Suppresses noradrenaline secretion\",\"e\":\"Acetylcholine has no effect on noradrenaline secretion\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man is undergoing a resection of a suspected sebaceous cyst on his back. The procedure is performed under local anaesthetic, however when the initial incision is made he still feels some pain\\n\\nWhich of the following statements pertains to a resting neurone?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements pertains to a resting neurone?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is uncharged\",\"e\":\"In the resting state the neurones is positively charged externally and negatively charged internally\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is depolarised\",\"e\":\"Since the charge is negative on the inside and positive on the outside the net result is that the neurone is polarised\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is negatively charged externally\",\"e\":\"it is positively charged externally.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is positively charged externally\",\"e\":\"In its resting state, the inside of a neurone is rich in chloride ions and a lesser amount of potassium ions. The outside of the neurone is rich in sodium ions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is unable to conduct impulses\",\"e\":\"When stimulated, the permeability of the neurone changes, allowing sodium from the extracellular fluid to flood into the neurone and potassium to move out. This depolarisation causes the generation of an action potential.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes is suspected to have diabetic autonomic neuropathy. They are due to have a routine cholecystectomy, but you refer them back to their General Practitioner (GP) for better control of their diabetes before committing to surgery:\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a function of the sympathetic nervous system?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a function of the sympathetic nervous system?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Miosis\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation produces pupillary dilatation (mydriasis)\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Defecation\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation slows gut activity and increases sphincter contraction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bradycardia\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation causes an increase in heart rate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Erection\",\"e\":\"Parasympathetic† stimulation causes erection. Sympathetic simulation causes ejaculation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sweating\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic nervous sympathetic system prepares the body for flight or fight response. Sweating increases with sympathetic stimulation.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"You are discussing the MRCS exam with your consultant, who states that on the day of the exam your sympathetic nervous system will be stimulated. This is known as the fight or flight response' What does stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system result in?\",\"lead\":\"This is known as the fight or flight response' What does stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system result in?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Defaecation\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation slows gut activity and increases sphincter contraction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Erection of the penis\",\"e\":\"Parasympathetict stimulation causes erection: Sympathetic simulation causes ejaculation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pupillary constriction\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation produces pupillary dilatation (mydriasis).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Watery salivation\",\"e\":\"Watery salivation is increased by parasympathetic stimulation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tachypnea\",\"e\":\"Stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system results in the flight or fight response - including an increase in the rate of breathing (tachypnoea).\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man undergoes a lumbar puncture for suspected subarachnoid haemorrhage. A sample is easily obtained and analysed. Which of the following best describes normal cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following best describes normal cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is produced within arachnoid granulations\",\"e\":\"Most of the CSF is produced by the choroid plexus, which is situated in the lateral, third and fourth ventricles. CSF is absorbed directly into the cerebral venous sinuses through the arachnoid villi, or granulations, by a process known as mass or bulk flow.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Has an identical composition to that of plasma\",\"e\":\"The composition of CSF is different to plasma. Of importance to mention are the concentrations of K+, Catt +, bicarbonate, glucose and protein which are lower in CSF than in plasma. This is to prevent high concentrations of these electrolytes inadvertently exciting neurones present within the brain substance.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has a protein content that is approximately 0.5% that of plasma\",\"e\":\"The low protein content of the CSF prevents some proteins and amino acids acting as 'false neurotransmitters'\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Has a higher potassium content than that of plasma\",\"e\":\"CSF has a lower potassium content than that of plasma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"pH is heavily buffered\",\"e\":\"The CSF is more acidic than plasma because the pH of CSF plays a critical role in the regulation of pulmonary ventilation and cerebral blood flow.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man undergoes a laparotomy for a perforated duodenal ulcer. Post-operatively they are unable to take a deep breath due to severe discomfort.\\n\\nWhich of the following is true of pain?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true of pain?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is transmitted faster through C-fibres than through A-delta fibres\",\"e\":\"Unmyelinated C-fibres transmit more slowly than myelinated A-delta fibres.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pain impulse received in the dorsal horn can be modulated by other descending spinal inputs\",\"e\":\"Descending fibres modulate nociceptive inputs via the dorsal horn. It is a site of spinal integration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Opioids act on u receptors in the peripheral nerves\",\"e\":\"Opioids act on u receptors in the central nervous system and the gastrointestinal system. thus contributing to their side-effect of constipation\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Side effects of opioids can be reversed by neostigmine\",\"e\":\"Naloxone is the correct agent to reverse the effects of opioids.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Glycine is excitatory pain neurotransmitter\",\"e\":\"Glycine is an inhibitory neurotransmitter.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old diabetic and hypertensive woman suffered a stroke causing loss of sensation down the left side of her body. She is found to have carotid artery stenosis on the right side and has been referred for consideration of carotid endarterectomy. Which option describes the brain region affected by this stroke?\",\"lead\":\"Which option describes the brain region affected by this stroke?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Central sulcus\",\"e\":\"The central sulcus (also known as the Rolandic fissure) is one of the major sulci. It is a potential space and is not part of the brain parenchyma. It therefore has no function.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Occipital lobe\",\"e\":\"The occipital lobe is involved in visual processing. A stroke affecting this region would cause a contralateral hemianopia (visual field defect).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Precentral gyrus\",\"e\":\"The precentral gyrus is known as the primary motor cortex. It is responsible for voluntary movement. A stroke affecting this region would cause contralateral weakness\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Postcentral gyrus S\",\"e\":\"The primary somatic sensory (S1) cortex in the postcentral gyrus is the first and largest cortical receiving area for the analysis of information from somatosensory receptors. S1 cortex is necessary for the conscious awareness: (1) that a stimulus has occurred; (2) ot stimulus quality; (3) of stimulus location; (4) of stimulus amplitude; and (5) of stimulus duration. A stroke affecting this region would cause contralateral paraesthesia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sylvian fissure\",\"e\":\"The Sylvian fissure (also known as the lateral sulcus) is one of the major sulci. It is a potential space and is not part of the brain parenchyma. It therefore has no function\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old woman with long-standing atrial fibrillation suffered an embolic stroke that causes weakness down one side of her body. She is subsequently started on warfarin and rate control of her AF\\n\\nWhich option describes the brain region affected by this stroke?\",\"lead\":\"Which option describes the brain region affected by this stroke?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cerebellar vermis\",\"e\":\"The cerebellar vermis is a posterior, midline cerebellar structure. A stroke affecting this region would cause postural instability\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AM Occipital lobe\",\"e\":\"The occipital lobe is involved in visual processing. A stroke affecting this region would cause a contralateral hemianopia (visual field defect).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Precentral gyrus\",\"e\":\"The primary motor cortex is located in the dorsal part of the precentral gyrus and the anterior bank of the central sulcus. The primary motor cortex (or M1) works in association with premotor areas to plan and execute movements. M1 contains large neurons known as Betz cells, which send long axons down the spinal cord to synapse onto alpha-motor neurones, which connect to the muscles. A stroke affecting this region will result in contralateral weakness.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Postcentral gyrus\",\"e\":\"The postcentral gyrus is known as the primary somatosensory cortex. It is responsible for the conscious sensation of touch. A stroke affecting this region would cause contralateral paraesthesia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sylvian fissure\",\"e\":\"The Sylvian fissure (also known as the lateral sulcus) is one of the major sulci. it is a potential space and is not part of the brain parenchyma. It therefore has no function.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man suffered a complex open and comminuted tibial fracture. It has required several orthopaedic and plastics procedures to make the limb functional again. He is now being reviewed in the outpatient clinic with chronic neuropathic pain that is severely impacted on their quality of life.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct with regards to the pain pathways in normal individuals?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct with regards to the pain pathways in normal individuals?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Rain is relaved via the thalamus →\",\"e\":\"The thalamus serves as a relay station, receiving pain stimuli from ascending sensory tracts.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The left precentral gyrus receives pain stimuli from the right side of the body\",\"e\":\"The postcentral gyrus receives pain stimuli\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Proprioception is conveyed along with pain in the anterior spinothalamic tracts\",\"e\":\"Proprioception is conveyed in the dorsal columns.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spinal reflex activity cannot be modulated by descending spinal pathways\",\"e\":\"Spinal reflex activity can be modulated by the corticospinal tract. This explains why in an upper motor neurone lesion there is the development of 'spastic paresis in which there is increased tone due to uninhibited reflex loops.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neurones conveying thermal sensation rarely cross to the contralateral spinal cord\",\"e\":\"The lateral spinothalamic tract conveys temperature sensation. Most neurones of the spinothalamic tract decussate via the anterior white commissure to the contralateral side of the cord\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old woman with a lumbar disc prolapse presents to the neurosurgical outpatient clinic. No knee-jerk reflex on the affected side can be elicited, despite reinforcement. Which one of the following is correct regarding this reflex?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding this reflex?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The knee jerk is supplied by the L5 nerve root\",\"e\":\"The knee jerk is supplied by the L3 and L4 nerve roots.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Passive stretch or the intratusal muscle spindles when tapping the tendon results in ipsilateral contraction of the extensors of the knee\",\"e\":\"The knee-jerk reflex is the best-known example of a monosynaptic stretch reflex. Passive stretch of the intrafusal spindle fibres through tapping the tendon with a tendon hammer results in generation of afferent action potentials. These are transmitted via the dorsal roots of the spinal nerves to the ipsilateral ventral grey matter where the only synapse with the efferent neurone occurs. The action potential is transmitted through the ventral nerve roots via an alpha-motor neuron, which synapses with the motor endplate of the muscle fibres of the knee extensors.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Efferent stimuli are transmitted via the dorsal roots of the spinal nerves\",\"e\":\"Efferent stimuli are transmitted via the ventral roots of the spinal nerves:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The synapse between afferent and efferent neurons occurs in the brainstem\",\"e\":\"The synapse between afferent and efferent neurons occurs in the ventral grey matter of the spinal cord.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The afferent neuron also synapses with inhibitory interneurons that result in relaxation of the contralateral knee extensors\",\"e\":\"The afferent neuron also synapses with inhibitory interneurons that result in relaxation of the ipsilateral knee flexors.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man is consented for carpal tunnel decompression and you are reading the preoperative neurophysiology report. They are reviewed in the outpatient clinic and the decision is made to proceed to surgery to decompress the median nerve. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the role of neurons?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the role of neurons?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Have dendrites that transmit stimuli away from the cell body\",\"e\":\"Dendrites transmit stimuli towards the cell body\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Have axons that transmit stimuli towards the cell body\",\"e\":\"Axons transmit stimuli away from the cell body\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Transmit action potentials more rapidly if they are non-myelinated\",\"e\":\"Neurons transmit action potentials more rapidly if they are myelinated\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Are supported by glial cells in the central nervous system\",\"e\":\"Neurons are supported by glial cells in the central nervous systems. Examples of glial cells include: astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, microglia and ependymal celis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transmit impulses less rapidly if they are of a larger diameter\",\"e\":\"transmit more rapidly if the axons are of larger diameter. Neurones\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man has fallen out of a window and sustained severe spinal trauma. imaging confirms a spinal cord injury and it is suspected they have developed autonomic dysfunction.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding autonomic neurons?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding autonomic neurons?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They arise from the spinal cord only\",\"e\":\"The parasympathetic neurons are formed at the upper and lower extremities of the spinal cord, in the mid-brain and hindbrain (fibres are carried by cranial nerves Ill. VII. IX and X) but also in the sacral spinal cord (S2-S4).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"In the sympathetic nervous system the ganglia is most commonly near the target organ\",\"e\":\"The neurons of the sympathetic nervous system are formed throughout the thoracic and upper lumbar spine. The ganglia of the sympathetic nervous system are found in a chain either side of the spine. The ganglia of the parasympathetic nervous system tend to lie near the target organ (eg coeliac ganglion which supplies the foregut)\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They form in the mid-brain and the sacrum for the parasympathetic system\",\"e\":\"The parasympathetic neurons are formed at the upper and lower extremities of the spinal cord, in the mid-brain and hindbrain (fibres are carried by cranial nerves III, VII, IX and X) but also in the sacral spinal cord (S2-S4).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They have ganglia running in a chain alongside the spinal cord for the parasympathetic system\",\"e\":\"The ganglia of the parasympathetic nervous are closer or are within the end organ.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They have adrenaline as their major pre-ganglionic synaptic neurotransmitter\",\"e\":\"The neurotransmitter at the synapses in the ganglia of both the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems is acetylcholine. The synapses of the postganglionic fibres with the end organs in the sympathetic nervous system involve catecholaminergio neurotransmission\\n\\n2213\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old woman is being treated for a moderate blunt head injury, and is having her intracranial pressure (ICP) monitored. The ICP is normal. Between what mean arterial pressure values is cerebral blood flow auto-regulated?\",\"lead\":\"Between what mean arterial pressure values is cerebral blood flow auto-regulated?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"50-130 mmg\",\"e\":\"The correct answer is 50-160 mmHg.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"50-160 mmHg\",\"e\":\"In normal physiological conditions, the cerebral blood flow (CBF) is usually maintained by both pressure and chemical autoregulation. The pressure autoregulation is facilitated by reflex constriction or dilatation of the precapillary cerebral vasculature in response to mean arterial pressures between 50-160 mm Hg.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"50-200 mmHg\",\"e\":\"The correct answer is 50-160 mmHg\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"80-160 mmHg\",\"e\":\"The correct answer is 50-160 mmHg.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"80-200 mmHg\",\"e\":\"The correct answer is 50-160 mmHg.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"A 3-month-old infant is admitted to the paediatric ward with a persistent fever. As part of the workup, a lumbar puncture is performed. When the CSF is analysed in the lab, it is found to be normal. Which one of the following would you expect to be true with regards to this patient's CSF?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would you expect to be true with regards to this patient's CSF?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Contains less glucose than blood\",\"e\":\"When normal, the CSF contains less glucose than is contained in the blood.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Contains less glucose than nasal mucus\",\"e\":\"CSF contains more glucose than nasal mucus; a positive glucose dipstix test of clear nasal discharge may indicate a CSF leak although is not a reliable test. Beta-2 transferrin is more reliable.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Contains more lactic acid than blood\",\"e\":\"The CSF contains less lactic acid than is contained in the blood.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Contains more protein than blood\",\"e\":\"The CSF contains less protein than is contained in the blood.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Has a more alkalotic pH than blood\",\"e\":\"The pH of CSF is 7.33, whereas that of plasma is 7.4 due to the higher oCO in CSF than in plasma.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old man is brought into the Emergency Department with a 1-day history of feeling generally unwell and feverish. On further questioning it emerges he has photophobia and is increasingly drowsy. He is commenced on antibiotics and a lumbar puncture is arranged for possible meningitis. In a healthy individual what is the relationship between glucose and protein levels in the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) relative to plasma?\",\"lead\":\"In a healthy individual what is the relationship between glucose and protein levels in the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) relative to plasma?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CSF glucose equal, CF protein equal\",\"e\":\"CSF glucose and protein levels are both much lower than those of plasma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"CSF glucose equal, CSF protein lower\",\"e\":\"The CSF glucose level is much lower than, not equal to plasma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"CSF glucose lower, CSF protein equal\",\"e\":\"The CSF protein level is much lower than, not equal to plasma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"CSF glucose lower, CSF protein higher\",\"e\":\"The CSF protein level is much lower, not higher than plasma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"CSF glucose lower, CSF protein lower\",\"e\":\"CSF is formed mainly by the choroid plexus by an active secretory process. It circulates in the subarachnoid space and is absorbed into the circulation by the arachnoid villi. CSF glucose and protein levels are both much lower than those of plasma. Changes in these concentrations are helpful in detecting pathological processes such as tumour or infection, where the blood-brain barrier is breached.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man has presented with suspected autonomic neuropathy and wants to know what management options there are. Which one of the following statements regarding autonomic neurons is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding autonomic neurons is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Activity of autonomic ganglionic neurons is directly modulated by postganglionic neurons\",\"e\":\"There is no neuronal feedback from the postganglionic neuron to the preganglionic neuron.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Generally involve one neurone in the efferent pathway\",\"e\":\"All autonomic nerves are composed of two neurons, which synapse in the autonomic ganglia. The ganglia of the sympathetic nervous system are found in a chain either side of the spine. The ganglia of the parasympathetic nervous system tend to lie near the target organ (eg coeliac ganglion which supplies the foregut)\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Of the sympathetic variety are formed in the lower lumbar spine and the sacrum\",\"e\":\"The neurons of the sympathetic nervous system are formed throughout the thoracic and upper lumbar spine.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Parasympathetic cell bodies are located within the mid-brain and the sacral spinal cord\",\"e\":\"The parasympathetic neurons are formed at the upper and lower extremities of the spinal cord, in the mid-brain and hindbrain (fibres are carried by cranial nerves IlI, VII, IX and X). and the sacral spinal cord S2-4\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The neurotransmitter at the synapses in the ganglia of parasympathetic nerves is most commonly noradrenaline\",\"e\":\"The neurotransmitter at the synapses in the ganglia of both the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems is acetylcholine. The synapses of the postganglionic fibres with the end organs in the sympathetic nervous system involve catecholaminergic neurotransmission (adrenaline/noradrenaline)\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"The skin contains several sensory receptors which are located in the dermis and subcutaneous layers. Prior to closing a surgical wound you inject long acting local anaesthetic to ease the post-operative symptoms Which skin structure acts as a slowly adapting receptor to detect pressure, and low frequency vibration and is best evaluated by two-point discrimination?\",\"lead\":\"Prior to closing a surgical wound you inject long acting local anaesthetic to ease the post-operative symptoms Which skin structure acts as a slowly adapting receptor to detect pressure, and low frequency vibration and is best evaluated by two-point discrimination?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Free nerve endings\",\"e\":\"Free nerve endings receive sharp acute, and chronic slow pain sensation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Meissner's corpuscles\",\"e\":\"Meissner corpuscles are rapidly - adaptive receptors to light touch and vibration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Merkel's receptor\",\"e\":\"Merkel's skin receptors are slowly adapting skin receptors that detect pressure, texture, and low frequency vibration and can be appropriately evaluated by static two-point discrimination\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pacinian corpuscles\",\"e\":\"Pacinian ('lamellar') corpuscles are rapidly adapting and especially detect vibration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ruffini corpuscles\",\"e\":\"Ruffini corpuscles detect stretching of the skin.\"}]},{\"t\":80,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"You are performing an excision of a sebaceous cyst under local anaesthetic. As you put the needle in to the skin to inject the local anaesthetic the patient immediately complains of a sharp pain. Which one of these fibres are responsible for the immediate response?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these fibres are responsible for the immediate response?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aß fibres\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ao fibres\",\"e\":\"Aô fibres are small myelinated fibres with rapid conduction responsible for the rapid sharp localised pain\\n\\nAß fibres are large myelinated fibres that usually respond to light touch.\\n\\nAa fibres are important for proprioception, not pain transmission.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"B fibres\",\"e\":\"B fibres are not involved in pain transmission and are part of the autonomic nervous system.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"satires.\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"C fibres\",\"e\":\"C fibres are small unmyelinated fibres with a slow conduction speed. C fibre stimulation produce a slow dull pain.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"You take an electrocardiogram (ECG) on one of your inpatients who has developed palpitations and shortness of breath. The ECG shows a supraventricular tachycardia. You get the patient to expire forcibly against a closed glottis, what is this called?\",\"lead\":\"You get the patient to expire forcibly against a closed glottis, what is this called?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carotid sinus massage\",\"e\":\"Carotid sinus massage is a technique where pressure is applied to the carotid sinus and can be used to treat SVT and diagnose carotid sinus syncope. However, it carries a 1-2% risk of stroke.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The diving reflex\",\"e\":\"The (mammalian) diving reflex is a response to cold exposure to the face and breath-holding. It causes bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction. It can be used to treat SVT.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The Heimlich manoeuvre\",\"e\":\"The Heimlich manoeuvre is a technique to treat asphyxiation. The patient's upper abdomen is rapidly compressed from behind to cause forced exhalation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The Jendrassik manoeuvre\",\"e\":\"The Jendrassik manoeuvre involves clenching the teeth and pulling interlocked fingers against each other. It reinforces tendon reflexes. 15E4\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The Valsalva manoeuvre\",\"e\":\"The Valsalva manoeuvre is forced expiration against a closed glottis. When the intrathoracic pressure is increased as occurs in Valsalva's manoeuvre, there is resultant decrease in venous return to the right atrium. In the normal subject this results in a diminished pulse pressure and decreased cardiac output. When sustained this results in a mild tachycardic response and a rise in the diastolic BP\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"An asthmatic is not coping well on their current medication regimen. They undergo some basic investigations in the Respiratory Outpatient Department. What is the term for the volume of expired air at forced expiration following maximal inspiration?\",\"lead\":\"What is the term for the volume of expired air at forced expiration following maximal inspiration?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Forced vital capacity (AVO, CaT\",\"e\":\"FVC is the amount of air expelled during forced expiration following a maximal inspiration. it is reduced in restrictive disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Functional residual capacity (FRC)\",\"e\":\"FRC is the volume of gas left in the lung at the end of quiet respiration. It is equal to the expiratory reserve volume + residual volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Expiratory reserve volume (ERV)\",\"e\":\"ERV is the maximum volume of expired air from the end of normal expiration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Residual volume (RV)\",\"e\":\"RV is the volume of air remaining in the lungs after forced expiration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Total volume (TV)\",\"e\":\"TLC is the total volume of air in the lungs at the end of forced inspiration and includes the residual volume.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A patient acutely deteriorates on the Ward. The FY1 performs an ABG to try and delineate the cause and guide early treatment. Which one of the following shifts the oxygen dissociation curve to the left?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following shifts the oxygen dissociation curve to the left?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Raised Ht concentration\",\"e\":\"Raised H+ concentration ie acidosis shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Raised 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (2,3-DPG)\",\"e\":\"A raised 2,3-DPG shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased inspired oxygen concentration\",\"e\":\"The oxygen concentration in the blood does not shift the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased CO concentration\",\"e\":\"A decrease in COz concentration shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the left. This is a feature of the Bohr effect-in metabolically active tissue, the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation shifts to the right ie for a given partial pressure of 02. oxygen will more readily dissociate. This situation means more oxygen is delivered into the tissues to aid aerobic respiration. Active metabolic tissue will have a decreased pH (lactic acid production), increased temperature, increased 2,3-DPG levels and increased CO_ levels. The inverse of any of these parameters will shift the curve to the left\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Raised temperature\",\"e\":\"A rise in temperature will shift the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"An anaesthetist is giving you teaching for the MRCS. They show you an ABG with some deranged parameters. What would shift the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right?\",\"lead\":\"What would shift the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decrease in P.CO2 concentration:\",\"e\":\"A decrease in PaCOz (hypocapnia) shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the left.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decrease in temperature below 37°C\",\"e\":\"Decreasing temperature shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the left.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decrease in 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (2,3-DPG)\",\"e\":\"Decreasing 2,3-DPG concentration shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the left.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increase in serum sodium\",\"e\":\"Extreme hypernatraemia can be accompanied by metabolic alkalosis and can therefore resuit in a left shift of the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve. 1865\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"pH of 7.31\",\"e\":\"A pH of 7.31 indicates acidosis, which shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right. This effect is a feature of the Bohr effect in metabolically active tissue, the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation shifts to the right ie for a given partial pressure of O2, oxygen will more readily dissociate. This situation means that more oxygen is delivered into the tissues to aid aerobic respiration. Active metabolic tissue will have a decreased pH (lactic acid production), increased temperature, increased 2,3-DPG levels and increased CO_ levels. The inverse of any of these parameters will shift the curve to the left.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A patient is awaiting an elective laparoscopy. There is some concern their severe COPD will not be able to tolerate the pneumoperitoneum. They undergo respiratory function tests. Which one of the following is a normal reading for a 70 kg man?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a normal reading for a 70 kg man?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Peak expiratory flow of 376 l/min\",\"e\":\"Normal peak expiratory flow in an adult man is 520-700 l/min, depending on their age and height.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Total lung capacity of 3.5 litres\",\"e\":\"Total lung capacity ranges from 5-6.5 litres in an adult man. The total lung capacity is the sum of the vital capacity and the residual volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Functional residual capacity of 3.5 litres\",\"e\":\"Functional residual capacity (the volume at the end of normal expiration) is 2-3 litres in an adult man. The functional residual capacity is the sum between the residual volume and the expiratory reserve volume\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tidal volume of 250ml\",\"e\":\"Normal tidal volume is 300-500 mls. The tidal volume is the amount of air that can be inhaled or exhaled during one respiratory cycle. This depicts the function of the respiratory centres respiratory muscles, and the mechanics of the lungs and chest walls. The normal adult value is 10% of the vital capacity. thus approximately 300 - 500 mls. (6-8 ml/kg); thus number can increase to up to 50% of the vital capacity during exercise.\\n• 5:2\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inspiratory reserve volume of 3 litres\",\"e\":\"This value is normal for inspiratory reserve volume. This volume can be inhaled at the end of normal inspiration. The normal adult values range between 1900 and 3300 mls.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A long-term smoker has been denied an elective plastics graft procedure due to the high chance of failure. They give up smoking and return for reassessment. Which one of the following physiological characteristics relates to the lining of the respiratory tract?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following physiological characteristics relates to the lining of the respiratory tract?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"About 1 litre of mucus is secreted every day\",\"e\":\"The respiratory tract secretes about 10-100 ml of mucus a day (in health).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The cilia are under the control of a physiological motor, dynein\",\"e\":\"Dynein is a family of cytoskeletal motor proteins that control the beat of the cilia. It is absent in Kartagener's syndrome (primary ciliary dyskinesia).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The mucociliary escalator moves at 0.2 cm/min\",\"e\":\"The mucociliary elevator moves at approximately 1 mm/min in the peripheral airways. The flow rates in the trache can be much higher, ranging from 5-10 mm/min\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The bronchioles have cartilage in their wall\",\"e\":\"Unlike bronchi, bronchioles do not have any cartilage in their walls.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The bronchioles have diameters up to 5 mm\",\"e\":\"Bronchioles are up to 1 mm in diameter.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A patient becomes hypoxic on the Ward. As the first doctor to review them, you support their respiratory system with 15 L high-flow oxygen through a non-rebreathe mask while you gain more information Pulmonary gas exchange occurs under which one of the following physiological principles?\",\"lead\":\"As the first doctor to review them, you support their respiratory system with 15 L high-flow oxygen through a non-rebreathe mask while you gain more information Pulmonary gas exchange occurs under which one of the following physiological principles?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gas exchange can occur in the final seven branches of the bronchoalveolar tree\",\"e\":\"The final seven branches of the bronchoalveolar tree are the respiratory zone and gas exchange can occur here.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The first 12 branches of the bronchial tree are collectively known as the conducting zone\",\"e\":\"The conducting zone is made up of the first 16 branches. Its function is to warm and moisten air, filter air, and assist in phonation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The equilibration of gases takes about 2.5 s in the resting lung\",\"e\":\"This happens much quicker and takes 0.25 s.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Only about 0 15% of oxygen is carried in solution in the plasma\",\"e\":\"About 1.5-2% of 02 is dissolved in plasma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Carbon dioxide is less water-soluble than oxygen\",\"e\":\"COz is much more water soluble than oxygen.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A patient is high-risk for a cholecystectomy They undergo pre-operative lung function testing to assess their fitness for surgery.\\n\\nThe functional residual capacity:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is the sum of the tidal volume and residual volume\",\"e\":\"The functional residual capacity is the sum of the expiratory reserve volume and the residual volume.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is the sum of the inspiratory reserve volume, the expiratory reserve volume and the tidal volume\",\"e\":\"The functional residual capacity is the sum of the expiratory reserve volume and the residual volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Can be measured directly by spirometry\",\"e\":\"The residual volume (and therefore functional residual capacity and total lung capacity) cannot be measured directly by spirometry. They are measured by either whole body plethysmography, or by using the helium dilution or nitrogen wash-out techniques.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is equal to the sum of the residual volume and the expiratory reserve volume\",\"e\":\"This is the functional residual capacity. It is the total volume of air in the lungs at the end of normal expiration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is that volume of air that remains in the lung after forced expiration\",\"e\":\"Functional residual capacity is the total volume of air in the lungs at the end of normal expiration.\\n\\nSpirometry traces are easy to understand if you remember the following two rules: (1) There are four lung volumes and five capacities that you need to remember. (2) A capacity is made up of two or more lung volumes. The four lung volumes are: (1) Tidal volume = volume of air inspired or expired with each normal breath in quiet breathing, approximately 500 ml. (2) Residual volume = that volume of air that remains in the lung after forced expiration. (3) Inspiratory reserve volume = extra volume of air that can be inspired over and above the normal tidal volume\\n\\n(4) Expiratory reserve volume = extra volume of air that can be expired by forceful expiration after the end of a normal tidal expiration. The five lung capacities are (1) Functional residual capacity = that volume of air that remains in the lung at the end of quiet expiration. Equal to the sum of the residual volume and the expiratory reserve volume. (2) Inspiratory capacity = inspiratory reserve volume + tidal volume.\\n\\n(3) Expiratory capacity = expiratory reserve volume + tidal volume. (4) Vital capacity = inspiratory reserve volume + tidal volume + expiratory reserve volume (or total lung capacity - residual volume) (5) Total lung capacity = vital capacity + residual volume. The residual volume (and therefore functional residual capacity and total lung capacity) cannot be measured directly by spirometry. They are measured by either whole body plethysmography, or by using the helium dilution or nitrogen wash-out techniques:\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A patient with severe COPD has poor respiratory function. They are deemed unfit for a cholecystectomy, so the maximal definitive treatment for their gallstone pancreatitis will be an ERCP with sphincterotomy. Lung compliance:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is defined as the change in pressure per unit volume\",\"e\":\"This describes elastance. Compression is the change in volume per unit change in pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is synonymous with elastance\",\"e\":\"Elastance is the inverse of compliance.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is increased in emphysema\",\"e\":\"Compliance is the ease with which the lungs expand in relation to a change in the transmural pressure (the pressure difference between the alveoli and the intrapleural space). Lungs are generally more compliant when they are at lower volume ie they expand more easily when transmural pressure increases. If the transmural pressure is O (ie no difference between the alveoli and the intrapleural pressures), the lungs would collapse due to their property of elastic recoil. This is due to the presence of structural proteins such as elastin. Compliance is pathologically increased in emphysema. Loss of structural proteins that support the lungs means that a change in pressure elicits a larger change in volume than in non-emphysematous lungs. However, the airways collapse on exhalation as they are less well supported, causing hyperinflation and air trapping. In comparison, compliance decreases (ie 'stiffer' lungs) in disease states such as pulmonary fibrosis and pulmonary oedema.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is equal in inflation and deflation\",\"e\":\"Compliance changes depending on whether the lung is inflating or deflating. This is known as hysteresis. The lung volume at any given pressure during deflation is larger than during inflation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is reduced by the presence of surfactant\",\"e\":\"Surfactant is produced by type. Il pneumocytes and helps prevent alveolar collapse by lowering the surface tension between water molecules in the surface layer. In this way it helps to reduce the work of breathing (makes the lungs more compliant) and permits the lungs to be more easily inflated.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A patient is brought in with severe hypothermia. An ABG is taken on arrival to aid guidance of eariy management.\\n\\nWhat is the haemoglobin oxygen dissociation curve is shifted to the left by?\",\"lead\":\"What is the haemoglobin oxygen dissociation curve is shifted to the left by?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"An increase in pCO\",\"e\":\"An increase in pCO2 (hypercapnia) shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A fall in pH\",\"e\":\"A fall in pH (acidosis) shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A rise in temperature\",\"e\":\"Rise in temperature shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right. Hypothermia shifts the curve to the left\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An increase in 23-DPG\",\"e\":\"An increase in 2,3-DPG concentration shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Fetal haemoglobin\",\"e\":\"The oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve is sigmoidal. Haemoglobin becomes saturated at high partial pressures of oxygen. Haemoglobin's affinity for oxygen is high when oxygen tension is very high and also where it is very low. This produces the two plateaus of the curve. The steep section between the plateaus reflects a decreased affinity for oxygen, and in this range oxygen dissociates and is delivered to tissues when it is required Surrounding environmental factors affect haemoglobins' affinity for oxygen relative to its partial pressure. This is the Bohr effect in metabolically active tissue, the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation shifts to the right, ie for a given partial pressure of O2 oxygen will more readily dissociate. This situation means more oxygen is delivered into the tissues. Active metabolic tissue will have a decreased pH (lactic acid production), increased temperature, increased 2,3-DPG levels and increased CO2 levels. The inverse of any of these factors will shift the curve to the left. A shift of the oxygen dissociation curve to the left is characteristic of fetal haemoglobin. When compared with adult haemoglobin, it is composed of two alpha and two gamma chains, instead of the usual two alpha and two beta chains of adult haemoglobin. This arrangement assists in the transfer of oxygen across the placenta from the maternal to the fetal circulation. The corollary of this is that fetal tissue oxygen levels have to be low to permit the release of oxygen from the haemoglobin.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A patient with COPD is found to have a hypercapnia on an ABG. The team take some caution in prescribing oxygen for the patient. In what form is Carbon dioxide is principally transported in the blood?\",\"lead\":\"In what form is Carbon dioxide is principally transported in the blood?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CO2 physically dissolved in solution\",\"e\":\"This only accounts for 5-10% of CO2 transport.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Carboxyhaemoglobin\",\"e\":\"Carboxyhaemoglobin is haemoglobin bound to carbon monoxide.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bicarbonate\",\"e\":\"The majority of CO_ is transported as bicarbonate. CO, combines with water to form carbonic acid (HaCOs this is catalysed by carbonic anhydrase) which then dissociates to form a Htion and HCO=\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Carbaminohaemoglobin\",\"e\":\"Carbaminohaemoglobin is carbon dioxide bound to haemoglobin. Carbamino compounds only account for 5-10% of CO_ in blood\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Carbonic anhydrase\",\"e\":\"Carbonic anhydrase is an enzyme found in erythrocytes that catalyses the conversion of CO2 and water to carbonic acid which then dissociates into proton and bicarbonate.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"You are attached to the ITU on a 4-month rotation. The consultant asks you details about the respiratory system. In the respiratory system, which one of the following statements best describes the physiological shunt?\",\"lead\":\"In the respiratory system, which one of the following statements best describes the physiological shunt?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is greater than the anatomical shunt\",\"e\":\"A right-left shunt refers to oxygen-poor blood from the right heart that has entered the left heart without undergoing gas exchange in the alveolar capillaries (ie it is shunted away from sites of gas exchange). The physiological shunt is the sum of the anatomical shunt (blood passing from the right ventricle to the systemic circulation via normal anatomical pathways. eg the bronchial vessels, without passing through the pulmonary alveolar capillaries), and the element of pulmonary alveolar capillary blood that has passed through non-aerated or poorly aerated alveoli. Therefore the physiological shunt is always at least as great as or greater than the anatomical shunt.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"is not present in healthy adult\",\"e\":\"There is always a shunt even in a healthy adult due to normal anatomical shunting ie the return of blood via pathways which bypass pulmonary circulation eg bronchial veins and Thebesian veins\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Affects arterial carbon dioxide more than arterial oxygen tension\",\"e\":\"The difference in carbon dioxide tension between arterial and venous blood is small and much less than the difference in oxygen tension. When shunted blood mixes with blood which has passed through ventilated alveoli, the overall partial pressure of O2 in the mixed blood is an intermediary of the two. The greater the degree of shunting, the lower the overall 002 This does not affect COz as much, due the similar pCO levels in shunted blood and blood that has undergone gas exchange. Shunting therefore affects arterial oxygen tension more than carbon dioxide tension.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Has the same effect on respiratory gas exchange as does physiological dead space\",\"e\":\"Physiological dead space (aeration but poor perfusion; high V/Q ratio) results in higher arterial carbon dioxide tension as carbon dioxide is not removed via gas exchange. Shunting reduces arterial oxygen tension.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is abolished when the subject breathes pure oxygen\",\"e\":\"Breathing pure oxygen cannot correct for the normal anatomical shunt, which is always present.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"You are attached to Intensive Care Unit on rotation. The consultant on the Unit has some questions about physiology.\\n\\nWith regard to CO2 transported in the blood, most of the CO2 is:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dissolved in plasma. C-\",\"e\":\"This only accounts for 5-10% of CO, transport.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"In the form of carbamino compounds formed from plasma proteins\",\"e\":\"Carbamino compounds only account for 5-10% of COz in blood.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In the form of carbamino compounds formed from haemoglobin\",\"e\":\"Carbamino compounds only account for 5-10% of CO2 in blood. Carbon dioxide bound to haemoglobin forms carbaminohaemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Bound to Chloride\",\"e\":\"Carbon dioxide does not bind to chloride in blood.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"in the form of H60;\",\"e\":\"The majority of CO_ is transported as bicarbonate. CO2 combines with water to form carbonic acid (H¿COs). This acid is catalysed by carbonic anhydrase and then dissociates to form a Htion and HCO:\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A patient has been severely injured and lost a significant amoun: of blood. On the VBG, the lactate is noted to be raised, you hypothesise that this is due to ncreased anaerobic respiration at a cellular level.\\n\\nAt a given pOz, haemoglobin gives up oxygen more readily in the following situation:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased pCO:\",\"e\":\"Decreased pCO2 (hypocapnia) causes the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to shift to the left, meaning for that a given pOg oxygen dissociates less readily from haemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased levels of 2,3 DPG\",\"e\":\"Decreased levels of 2,3-DPG causes the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to shift to the left, meaning for that a given pOg. oxygen dissociates less readily from haemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Presence of fetal haemoglobin\",\"e\":\"When compared with adult haemoglobin, fetal haemoglobin is composed of two alpha and two gamma chains, instead of the usual two alpha and two beta chains of adult haemoglobin. This arrangement assists in the transfer of oxygen across the placenta from the maternal to the fetal circulation. The corollary of this is that fetal tissue oxygen levels have to be low to permit the release of oxygen from the haemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased pH\",\"e\":\"Increased pH ie alkalosis causes the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to shift to the left, meaning for that a given pOz, oxygen dissociates less readily from haemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased temperature\",\"e\":\"Increased temperature promotes a shift of the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right, whereby for a given pOs. oxygen dissociates more readily from haemoglobin.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"A patient is being ventilated on ITU. They slowly recover and are subsequently extubated then transferred to the surgical ward Anatomical dead space is decreased by which one option?\",\"lead\":\"They slowly recover and are subsequently extubated then transferred to the surgical ward Anatomical dead space is decreased by which one option?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline:\",\"e\":\"Adrenaline relaxes bronchial smooth muscle, making it a potent bronchodilator. This increases anatomical dead space.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Tracheostomy\",\"e\":\"Tracheostomy bypasses the anatomical dead space above the level of the tracheostomy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A subject standing as opposed to lying supine\",\"e\":\"This increases anatomical dead space.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increasing size of a subject\",\"e\":\"Anatomical dead space increases as the size of the subject increases.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increasing lung volume\",\"e\":\"Increasing lung volume (eg during inspiration) will increase anatomical dead space.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"The anaesthetic consultant is giving teaching on physiology. They have some direct questions for you to answer. Which one of the following is the most important direct stimulus to respiration?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most important direct stimulus to respiration?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased p(CO2) of the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)\",\"e\":\"CO_ can easily diffuse across the blood-brain barrier (unlike H+ and HCO; ions). This releases H+ ions. Central chemoreceptors respond to increased Ht ions (ie increased acidity) of CSF.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased H+ concentration of the CSF\",\"e\":\"Central chemoreceptors, located in the ventral medulla, are the most important stimulus for respiration. They respond to increased H° concentration of cerebral extracellular fluid (which is directly linked to CSF). H+/HCO, cannot easily cross the blood-brain barrier, but CO2 does so readily. This frees Ht ions, causing a low CSF pH and stimulates the central chemoreceptors to fire.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decreased arterial proa\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased arterial pH\",\"e\":\"Decreased arterial pH stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors in the aortic arch and carotid bodies lo fire, but these are less important in sumulaling respiration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased arterial p(CO2)\",\"e\":\"Decreased arterial p(O2) stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors in the aortic arch and carotid bodies to fire, but these are less important in stimulating respiration:\\n\\nChemoreceptors respond to increased arterial p(CO2). not decreased p(CO2).\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"A patient becomes acutely breathless on the Ward while recovering from a neck of femur fracture, they had a hemiarthroplasty done 2 days ago. Under normal conditions, O2 uptake by the lungs is limited to pulmonary perfusion. Which one of the following conditions would limit O, transfer from alveolar to pulmonary capillary blood?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following conditions would limit O, transfer from alveolar to pulmonary capillary blood?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Breathing hyperbaric gas mixture y:\",\"e\":\"A hyperbaric gas mixture (assuming at least the same concentration of O2 as air) would increase partial pressure of Og and increase rate of gas exchange.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chronic obstructive lung disease\",\"e\":\"Chronic obstructive lung disease does not affect rate of diffusion as the diffusion distance does not change. COPD does cause air trapping as there is airway collapse on expiration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased ventilatory rate\",\"e\":\"Increase in ventilatory rate will maintain a high O2 gradient to assist gas exchange.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mild exercise\",\"e\":\"Exercise increases tidal volume and respiratory rate, increasing pulmonary ventilation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary oedema\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary oedema decreases rate of diffusion. According to Fick's Law, rate of diffusion is proportional to surface area and concentration gradient and inversely proportional to diffusion distance. Pulmonary oedema increases the diffusion distance as there is more fluid in the extracellular space between the alveoli and the capillaries.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"Oxygen is carried in the blood in two forms. Most oxygen is carried combined with haemoglobin, but there is a very small amount dissolved in the plasma. How much oxygen is normally carried in the blood?\",\"lead\":\"How much oxygen is normally carried in the blood?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"2 ml oxygen/100 ml blood.C~\",\"e\":\"Normally 20 ml oxygen is carried per 100 ml blood.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"5 ml oxygen/100 ml blood\",\"e\":\"Normally 20 ml oxygen is carried per 100 ml blood. The haemoglobin in venous blood is about 75% saturated with O2 (15 ml Oz per 100 ml blood), therefore 5 ml Ozper 100 ml blood is the amount taken up by tissues in the capillary.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"10ml oxygen oxygen/100 ml blood\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"20 ml oxygen/100 ml blood\",\"e\":\"In a normal person each 100 ml of blood contains about 15 g of haemoglobin and each gram of haemoglobin can bind to 1.34 ml of oxygen when it is 100% saturated (15 × 1.34 = 20 ml 02/100 ml blood).\\n\\nNormally 20ml oxygen is carried per 100ml blood.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"30 ml oxygen/100 ml blood\",\"e\":\"Normally 20ml oxygen is carried per 100ml blood.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"The anaesthetic consultant is doing teaching on physiology for the MRCS. There are three ways by which carbon dioxide is transported in the blood. About 70% of the carbon dioxide is transported to the lungs:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"In the form of bicarbonate ions.\",\"e\":\"The majority of CO_ is transported as bicarbonate. CO2 combines with water to form carbonic acid (HaCO; this. is catalysed by carbonic anhydrase), which then dissociates to form a Htion and HCO:.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"In the form of carbonic acid\",\"e\":\"The majority of carbonic acid spontaneously dissociates into Hand HCO3 ions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In the form of carbaminohaemoglobin\",\"e\":\"Carbaminohaemoglobin is carbon dioxide bound to haemoglobin. Carbamino compounds only account for 5-10% of COz in the blood\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"In chemical combination with albumin\",\"e\":\"Carbon dioxide does not bind to albumin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"In the dissolved state in the water of the plasma and cells\",\"e\":\"This only accounts for 5-10% of CO2 transport.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"A patient becomes acutely short of breath following a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. On the ABG, their paO2 is noted to be 7 Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acts reflexively via the central nervous system\",\"e\":\"The mechanism of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction does not require central nervous system (CNS) input as it can occur in an isolated lung.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Improves matching of ventilation and blood flow in some lung diseases\",\"e\":\"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction results in decreased blood flow to poorly ventilated alveoli, improving matching of blood flow to ventilation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is not important in the perinatal period\",\"e\":\"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is important in the perinatal period. When the newborn baby makes the transition from placental to air breathing, it is important for pulmonary vascular resistance to fall precipitously within a few seconds. As a consequence, pulmonary blood flow dramatically increases from its value of only about 15% of the cardiac output in utero. The increase in pulmonary blood flow is assisted by closure of both the ductus arteriosus and the foramen ovale.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is irreversible \\\")\",\"e\":\"it is reversible unless it is long standing.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Requires a p(O2) of less than 40 mmHg in mixed venous blood\",\"e\":\"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction occurs independently of venous 02 content.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman was admitted with a large post-pneumonic pleural effusion. Nearly 2 litres of purulent fluid was drained from the pleural space following insertion of a chest drain What is the normal amount of pleural fluid?\",\"lead\":\"Nearly 2 litres of purulent fluid was drained from the pleural space following insertion of a chest drain What is the normal amount of pleural fluid?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"10 ml\",\"e\":\"The normal amount of pleural fluid is 2-10 ml. It acts as a lubricant to allow the visceral and parietal layers to move smoothly against each other during respiration.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"100 ml\",\"e\":\"The normal amount of pleural fluid is 2-10 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"250 ml\",\"e\":\"The normal amount of pleural fluid is 2-10 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"500 ml\",\"e\":\"The normal amount of pleural fluid is 2-10 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"1OSO mi\",\"e\":\"The normal amount of pleural fluid is 2-10 ml.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"The vessels of the pulmonary circulation are very compliant (easily distensible). Therefore, they can serve as a reservoir for the left ventricle. How much blood volume can the pulmonary vessels accommodate in an adult man under normal resting conditions?\",\"lead\":\"How much blood volume can the pulmonary vessels accommodate in an adult man under normal resting conditions?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"150 ml\",\"e\":\"The pulmonary vessels can accommodate up to 500 ml in an adult man under normal resting conditions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"250 ml\",\"e\":\"The pulmonary vessels can accommodate up to 500 ml in an adult man under normal resting conditions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"500 ml\",\"e\":\"The pulmonary vessels can accommodate up to 500 ml in an adult man under normal resting conditions. This means that cardiac output can be increased quickly before there is a match in the venous return, as the blood stored in the pulmonary vessels act as a reservoir for the left ventricle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"1000m 27\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"1500 ml\",\"e\":\"The pulmonary vessels can accommodate up to 500 ml in an adult man under normal resting conditions.\\n\\nThe pulmonary vessels can accommodate up to 500 ml in an adult man under normal resting conditions.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"Various lung intravascular and extravascular pressures influence pulmonary blood flow and its distribution in the lung. This will vary depending on the activity of an individual how unwell they may be and throughout the day. What is the normal mean pulmonary arterial pressure?\",\"lead\":\"What is the normal mean pulmonary arterial pressure?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"5 mmHg\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"30 mmHg\",\"e\":\"Normal mean pulmonary arterial pressure is 15 mmHg.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"15 mmHg\",\"e\":\"Normal mean pulmonary arterial pressure is 15 mmHg. This is the hydrostatic pressure generated by the right ventricle and is 1/6'h that of systemic arterial pressure generated by the left ventricle. The pulmonary circulation lacks high resistance arterioles and so the pressure drop between the pulmonary arteries and capillaries is minimised\\n\\nNormal mean pulmonary arterial pressure is 15 mmHg\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"50 mmHg\",\"e\":\"Normal mean pulmonary arterial pressure is 15 mmHg.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"90 mmHg\",\"e\":\"Normal mean pulmonary arterial pressure is 15 mmHg. 90 mmHg is the normal systemic mean arterial pressure.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A patient becomes acutely breathless on the Ward. On arrival, you note the nurses have placed an oxygen saturation probe on the patient's finger which is reading 80%. You immediately start high-flow oxygen. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding pulse oximetry?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding pulse oximetry?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gives a direct reflection of the p(O2)\",\"e\":\"This can only be measured by arterial blood gas sampling.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Shows a linear relationship with oxygen carriage\",\"e\":\"The relationship between oxygen carriage and pulse oximetry is a sigmoid curve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is inaccurate in the presence of high levels of carbon monoxide.\",\"e\":\"Carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin to form carboxyhaemoglobin. The presence of carboxyhaemoglobin leads to inaccuracy in the pulse oximeter, as it will read a falsely high oxygen saturation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Indicates adequate tissue oxygen delivery if readings are over 95%\",\"e\":\"Adequate oxygen delivery to tissues depends on overall haemoglobin content as well as cardiac output. For example, a patient in shock may have adequate O saturation, but oxygen delivery to tissues will be poor as tissue perfusion is inadequate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is the best means of indicating inadequate ventilation\",\"e\":\"The adequacy of ventilation is better reflected by the p(CO2). 1778\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"You receive pre-operation tests back on a patient regarding their respiratory status. The anaesthetist is happy that the patient proceed to a general anaesthetic. What is the functional residual capacity?\",\"lead\":\"What is the functional residual capacity?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Approximately 2,5 litres\",\"e\":\"The functional residual capacity is the total volume of air in the lungs at the end of passive expiration. It is the sum of the residual volume and the expiratory reserve volume and is approximately 2.5 litres in the average adult.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Approximately 60% of vital capacity\",\"e\":\"The functional residual capacity is less than 50% of the vital capacity.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased when supine.\",\"e\":\"Functional residual capacity is decreased when supine and increased when standing.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The inspiratory capacity plus the inspiratory reserve volume\",\"e\":\"The functional residual capacity is the sum of the residual volume and the expiratory reserve volume\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The tidal volume plus the expiratory reserve volume\",\"e\":\"The functional residual capacity is the sum of the residual volume and the expiratory reserve volume\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing pre-operative lung function tests for a 70 kg 21-year-old man. He has a history of asthma, but based on the investigations, the anaesthetist is happy to proceed. What would you expect the vital capacity to be?\",\"lead\":\"What would you expect the vital capacity to be?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"2000 ml\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity would be higher in this young patient. As the patient is a 70 kg man, vital capacity is approximately 70 ml/kg ie 70 × 70 = 4900 ml.\\n\\nVital capacity is the sum of the inspiratory reserve volume, the tidal volume and the expiratory reserve volume. A normal range would be between 3100 and 4900 mls.\\n\\nVital capacity would be slightly lower than this. As the patient is a 70 kg man, vital capacity is approximately 70 ml/kg ie 70 × 70 = 4900 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"3000ml\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity would be higher in this young patient. As the patient is a 70 kg man, vital capacity is approximately 70 ml/kg ie 70 × 70 = 4900 ml. In healthy individuals, the vital capacity can be approximated as 60-70 ml/kg, therefore, in this scenario, the number would be between 4200 and 4900 mls.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"3500 ml\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity would be higher in this young patient. As the patient is a 70 kg man, vital capacity is approximately 70 ml/kg ie 70 × 70 = 4900 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"4500 ml\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity would be higher in this young patient. As the patient is a 70 kg man, vital capacity is approximately 70 ml/kg ie 70 × 70 = 4900 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"IW 0009\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"You admit a patient known to have asthma who requires emergency surgery and you measure their peak expiratory flow rate (PER). You report your findings to the anaesthetist who is happy the patient proceeds to surgery. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the PER in adults?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the PER in adults?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It can be measured by a Wright's peak flow meter\",\"e\":\"A Wright's peak flow meter is used to measure PER and is a handy bedside test.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It increases with age\",\"e\":\"PER decreases with age.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In normal men is equal to or greater than 400 1/min\",\"e\":\"PER should be greater than 500 1/min in men and 400 1/min in women.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is unchanged by training\",\"e\":\"Regular exercise will improve PER.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is unchanged in obstructive lung disease\",\"e\":\"PEFR is decreased in obstructive lung disease: Collapse of small airways on expiration causes air trapping and decreases PER. This makes PER measurement clinically relevant particularly in the acutely asthmatic patient and can be used to assess severity of an asthma attack\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"You are treating a 70 kg man with a vital capacity of 3000 ml. He has asthma but, given this result, is deemed fit enough to proceed to a laparoscopic inguinal hernia repair. Which one of the following decreases vital capacity?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following decreases vital capacity?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"increased body surface area\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity is proportional to body surface area.\\n\\nMales have on average a 20-25% greater vital capacity.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Male gender\",\"e\":\"Males have on average & 20-25% greater vital capacity.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"AN Pain\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity is reduced by pain as it leads to a decrease in respiratory effort.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Standing\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity changes with posture. It increases with standing and decreases when supine.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Young age\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity decreases with advancing age. 19'0\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient who is in respiratory distress. On further examination, he is noted to be peripherally cyanosed. What would increase the release of oxygen from blood to the peripheral tissues?\",\"lead\":\"What would increase the release of oxygen from blood to the peripheral tissues?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased blood volume\",\"e\":\"Decreased blood volume will lead to a fall in haemoglobin and less oxygen delivery to tissues, but does not have an effect on the dissociation of oxygen from haemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"increased p(co: AN\",\"e\":\"Increased p(CO2) causes a Bohr shift of the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right. This means that, for a given partial pressure of oxygen, less oxygen is bound to haemoglobin and is released into the peripheral tissues.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased pH\",\"e\":\"Increased pH (alkalosis) will lead to decreased release of oxygen from blood ie it shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the left.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased osmotic pressure\",\"e\":\"Osmotic pressure does not affect the dissociation of oxygen from haemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased temperature\",\"e\":\"Decrease in temperature will lead to decreased release of oxygen from blood ie it shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the left.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"You have taken a patient's arterial blood gases, and they have a raised p(CO2). You refer them to the medical registrar for consideration of BiPaP Regarding carbon dioxide in arterial blood, which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"You refer them to the medical registrar for consideration of BiPaP Regarding carbon dioxide in arterial blood, which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It diffuses with difficulty into red blood cells\",\"e\":\"CO_ is readily soluble and diffuses into red cells easily:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It enters red cells by active transport\",\"e\":\"CO_ diffuses passively into red blood cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is carried predominantly as bicarbonate (HCO;-)\",\"e\":\"The majority of COs is transported as bicarbonate. CO2 combines with water to form carbonic acid (H2COs' this is catalysed by carbonic anhydrase) which then dissociates to form a Htion and HCO:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is less soluble than oxygen\",\"e\":\"COg is about 20 times more soluble than oxygen at equal partial pressures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is mainly dissolved in plasma\",\"e\":\"The majority of COg is in the form of bicarbonate ions. Only about 5% is dissolved in plasma.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man presents with increased lung compliance. The anaesthetists are unwilling to ventilate the patient and so the patient's surgical pathology is treated conservatively Which of the following may be responsible for this patient's presenting complaint?\",\"lead\":\"The anaesthetists are unwilling to ventilate the patient and so the patient's surgical pathology is treated conservatively Which of the following may be responsible for this patient's presenting complaint?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Emphysema. C-\",\"e\":\"Compliance is increased in emphysema. Loss of structural proteins that support the lungs means that a change in pressure elicits a larger change in volume than in non-emphysematous lungs. However, the airways collapse on exhalation as they are less well supported, causing hyperinflation and air trapping.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An increase in the elastic fibres in the lung parenchyma\",\"e\":\"A decrease in elastic fibres will increase compliance.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"An increase in the surface tension of the alveolar fluid\",\"e\":\"High surface tension will decrease lung compliance. Surfactant, produced by type Il pneumocytes, decreases the surface tension of alveolar fluid and increases compliance.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An open pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"An open pneumothorax will decrease lung compliance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary contusion\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary contusion decreases lung compliance.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A patient in the ICU demonstrates hypoxia secondary to severe pneumonia, and suffers a subsequent derangement in acid-base balance. This is noted and the settings on the ventilator are adjusted to compensate Which one of the following options is a consequence of the normal physiological response to hypoxia?\",\"lead\":\"This is noted and the settings on the ventilator are adjusted to compensate Which one of the following options is a consequence of the normal physiological response to hypoxia?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decrease in arterial blood pressure\",\"e\":\"Hypoxia does not stimulate a decrease in arterial blood pressure:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decrease in blood flow to skeletal muscles\",\"e\":\"Hypoxia will not cause a decrease in blood flow to skeletal muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Respiratory acidosis\",\"e\":\"Hypoxia will stimulate increased ventilation, which will result in respiratory alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Respiratory alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Peripheral chemoreceptors in the aortic arch and carotid bodies are sensitive to arterial oxygen levels. In hypoxia, they will stimulate the respiratory centres to increase ventilation, leading to a decreased p(CO2) and a subsequent respiratory alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Respiratory depression\",\"e\":\"Hypoxia leads to an increase in respiratory rate as peripheral chemoreceptors stimulate medullary respiratory centres.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"You have a patient who is hypoxic with a decreased respiratory rate. Following administration of naloxone they rapidly improve. What stimulates an increase in pulmonary ventilation?\",\"lead\":\"What stimulates an increase in pulmonary ventilation?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carotid body chemoreceptors (\",\"e\":\"Carotid body chemoreceptors respond to hypoxia to stimulate the respiratory centres in the medulla. This increases pulmonary ventilation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased arterial PO2\",\"e\":\"Increased arterial p(O2) does not stimulate increased pulmonary ventilation, hypoxia does.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decreased arterial PCo\",\"e\":\"Decreased arterial p(CO2) does not stimulate increased pulmonary ventilation. Hypercapnia leads to the stimulation of central chemoreceptors to increase ventilation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased pH\",\"e\":\"Alkalosis does not stimulate chemoreceptors, acidosis does.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reflex activation in the respiratory centre in the cerebellum\",\"e\":\"The respiratory centre is located in the medulla.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":73,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient 1 day following major abdominal surgery, who has been having respiratory difficulties and has been transferred to the intensive Care Unit (ICU) overnight.\\n\\nAssuming that the patient is in pain, and that there is no evidence of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), which respiratory physiological variable remains unaffected by post-operative pain?\",\"lead\":\"Assuming that the patient is in pain, and that there is no evidence of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), which respiratory physiological variable remains unaffected by post-operative pain?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The ventilation perfusion ration\",\"e\":\"Pain causes shallower breathing and causes V/Q mismatch as less air is reaching the alveoli that are being perfused.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Functional residual capacity\",\"e\":\"Functional residual capacity is decreased by pain.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lung compliance\",\"e\":\"Lung compliance should not be affected.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Minute ventilatory volume\",\"e\":\"Minute ventilatory volume (the product of tidal volume and respiratory rate) will decrease. Although respiratory rate may increase, tidal volume decreases significantly.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tidal volume\",\"e\":\"Tidal volume decreases as the patient takes shallower breaths when they are in pain\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"On review of a patient's pre-operative lung function tests, an increased functional residual capacity (FRC) is noted. They are referred to the respiratory physicians for optimisation prior to their elective procedure.\\n\\nWhich of the following can increase FRC?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following can increase FRC?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Emphysema\",\"e\":\"The FRC is the volume of air in the lungs at the end of passive expiration. Emphysematous lungs are more compliant, resulting in a greater FRC.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"VISA Fibrosing alveolitis\",\"e\":\"Fibrosing alveolitis (idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis) is a restrictive lung disease: FRC is reduced.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"General anaesthesia\",\"e\":\"General anaesthesia reduces FRC by approximately 20% due to loss of inspiratory muscle tone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Obesity\",\"e\":\"FRC is reduced in obesity.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Recent abdominal surgery\",\"e\":\"FRC is reduced following abdominal surgery due to postoperative pain causing splinting of the diaphragm\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"You are describing the Bohr effect to a group of medical students. You draw a diagram to illustrate, they have further questions for you. What displaces the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the left?\",\"lead\":\"What displaces the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the left?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute Anaemia\",\"e\":\"Anaemia will not affect the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A fall in 2,3-DPG\",\"e\":\"A fall in 2,3-DPG causes a shift in the oxygen-haemoglobin curve to the left. This means that for a given partial pressure of oxygen, more oxygen is bound to haemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"AN A fall in pH\",\"e\":\"A fall in pH ie acidosis will cause the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to shift to the\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An increase in pCO\",\"e\":\"An increase in p(CO2) will cause the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to shift to the right.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pyrexia\",\"e\":\"Pyrexia will cause the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to shift to the right.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":79,\"stem\":\"You are drawing the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve for the benefit of some medical students. You demonstrate that the curve can shift to the left or right depending on various parameters. With regards to this curve, what results in a decreased affinity for oxygen?\",\"lead\":\"With regards to this curve, what results in a decreased affinity for oxygen?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decrease in red cell 2,3-DPG\",\"e\":\"A fall in 2,3-DPG causes a shift in the oxygen-haemoglobin curve to the left. This means that for a given partial pressure of oxygen, more oxygen is bound to haemoglobin ie an increased affinity for oxygen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypocapnia\",\"e\":\"Hypocapnia causes a shift in the oxygen-haemoglobin curve to the left. This means that for a given partial pressure of oxygen, more oxygen is bound to haemoglobin ie an increased affinity for oxygen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Metabolic alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Metabolic alkalosis causes a shift in the oxygen-haemoglobin curve to the left. This means that for a given partial pressure of oxygen, more oxygen is bound to haemoglobin ie an increased affinity for oxygen.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pyrexia\",\"e\":\"Pyrexia causes a shift in the oxygen-haemoglobin curve to the right. This means that for a given partial pressure of oxygen, less oxygen is bound to haemoglobin ie a decreased affinity for oxygen.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Respiratory alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Respiratory alkalosis causes a shift in the oxygen-haemoglobin curve to the left. This means that for a given partial pressure of oxygen, more oxygen is bound to haemoglobin ie an increased affinity for oxygen.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":81,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing one of your patients lung function tests. The results are for a 25-year-old man, who is normally fit and well and weighs 70 kg What would be the expected normal vital capacity in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"The results are for a 25-year-old man, who is normally fit and well and weighs 70 kg What would be the expected normal vital capacity in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Vital capacity (3.5 I) = inspiratory reserve volume (3 I) + tidal volume (0.5 l)\",\"e\":\"This is the inspiratory capacity. Vital capacity is the sum of the inspiratory reserve volume. tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Vital capacity (45 l) = inspiratory reserve volume (3 l) + expiratory reserve volume (1.5 I)\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity is the sum of the inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Vital capacity (5 |) = inspiratory reserve volume (3 I) + tidal volume (0.5 I) + expiratory reserve volume (1.5 |)\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity is the sum of the inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume (this is also equal to the total lung capacity minus the residual volume).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vital capacity (6L) = Inspiratory reserve volume (3L) + tidal volume (0.5L) + expiratory reserve volume (1.5L) + residual volume (1L)\",\"e\":\"This is the total lung capacity. The vital capacity is the sum of the inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vital capacity (2L) = Tidal volume (0.5L) + expiratory reserve volume (1.5L)\",\"e\":\"This is Lhe expiratory capacity: Vital capacity is the sum of the inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume 1876\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"You are called to see one of your ward patients who is hyperventilating. They become confused and extremely anxious. What physiological effects may be caused?\",\"lead\":\"What physiological effects may be caused?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased blood flow to the brain\",\"e\":\"Hyperventilation causes hypocapnia, which causes cerebral vasoconstriction leading to decreased cerebral blood flow. This mechanism can be used therapeutically in hypocapnic ventilation to treat cerebral oedema.\\n\\nBasal metabolic rate increases during hyperventilation\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decrease in the basal metabolic rate\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased serum calcium levels\",\"e\":\"Hyperventilation causes hypocapnia. The loss of CO2 causes a respiratory alkalosis. As pH increases, calcium ions have a greater affinity for albumin binding, decreasing the level of free serum calcium. This is because Ht ions compete with calcium ions for albumin binding sites, and the loss of Ht results in more binding sites for calcium ions to occupy. This hypocalcaemia is what causes the perioral and digital paraesthesia seen in hyperventilating patients.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased COz levels\",\"e\":\"COz levels decrease in hyperventilation as it is 'blown off.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Respiratory acidosis\",\"e\":\"Hyperventilation causes hypocapnia. The loss of CO2 causes a respiratory alkalosis. 1933\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":85,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old life-long smoker is seen pre-operatively for excision of a squamous cell carcinoma. The lesion is on his face and he will require a general anaesthetic. Which one of the following is true of functional residual capacity (FRC)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of functional residual capacity (FRC)?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It can be measured via spirometry\",\"e\":\"The residual volume (and therefore functional residual capacity and total lung capacity) cannot be measured directly by spirometry. They are measured by either whole body plethysmography, or by using the helium dilution or nitrogen wash-out techniques.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is the tidal volume plus the expiratory reserve volume\",\"e\":\"FRC is the sum of the residual volume and the expiratory reserve volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is approximately 60% of vital capacity\",\"e\":\"FRC is less than 50% of the vital capacity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is increased when someone is supine\",\"e\":\"FRC decreases when someone is supine and increases on standing. 1890\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is approximately 2.5 litres in a healthy adult man\",\"e\":\"This is the average FRC. FRC is the sum of the residual volume and the expiratory reserve volume.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":87,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old man is admitted to the Accident and Emergency resuscitation unit having sustained 60% burns following a house fire. His carboxyhaemoglobin is 24%. Which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carboxyhaemoglobin level of 50% is manifested by headache and confusion\",\"e\":\"These are early symptoms of carbon monoxide poisoning. At a 50% carboxyhaemoglobin level the patient will experience hallucinations, ataxia, coma then death.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Carbon monoxide binds with the intracellular cytochrome system and produces abnormal cellular function\",\"e\":\"Carbon monoxide has a high affinity than oxygen for haem groups. It binds to the intracellular cytochrome system, causing cellular dysfunction\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oxygen has a greater affinity for haemoglobin than carbon monoxide\",\"e\":\"Carbon monoxide has a 240-fold greater affinity for haemoglobin than oxygen.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Oxyhaemomoglobin dissociates less readily than carboxyhaemoglabin\",\"e\":\"Carboxyhaemoglobin does not readily dissociate, unlike oxyhemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The fall in the partial pressure of arterial oxygen (pa(O2)) is directly proportional to a rise in carboxyhaemoglobin levels\",\"e\":\"The pa(0z) will remain normal. This is because only a small amount of CO is required to bind to haem groups to cause cellular dysfunction and CO poisoning. The amount needed to cause poisoning is low enough that it does not alter the amount of oxygen physically dissolved in the plasma and hence the PaO2. However, ABGs will usually reveal a metabolic acidosis in patients with serious poisoning.\"}]},{\"t\":81,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"A patient has been referred for lung function tests. There is some doubt they will be able to safely tolerate a pneumoperitoneum for their elective laparoscopic hernia repair.\\n\\nWhich is the correct calculation for normal lung volumes?\",\"lead\":\"Which is the correct calculation for normal lung volumes?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Vital capacity = expiratory reserve volume + inspiratory reserve volume\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity is the sum of the inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Vital capacity = expiratory reserve volume + tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume\",\"e\":\"This is the vital capacity. In women it is 50-60 ml/kg and in men 70 ml/kg.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Total lung capacity = vital capacity + functional reserve capacity\",\"e\":\"Total lung capacity is vital capacity + residual volume\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Total lung capacity = inspiratory reserve volume + residual volume\",\"e\":\"Total lung capacity is vital capacity + residual volume.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Functional residual capacity = residual volume + tidal volume\",\"e\":\"Functional residual capacity is residual volume + expiratory reserve volume.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A patient on the ward becomes oliguric post-operatively. Their urine output does not improve at all despite multiple fluid challenges and there is no hydronephrosis on renal USS. Concerning glomerular filtration, which one of the following is true of the proximal convoluted tubule?\",\"lead\":\"Concerning glomerular filtration, which one of the following is true of the proximal convoluted tubule?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Reabsorbs water by sodium secretion\",\"e\":\"It actively reabsorbs sodium rather than secreting it. The proximal tubule efficiently regulates the pH of the filtrate by exchanging hydrogen ions in the interstitium for bicarbonate ions in the filtrate; it is also responsible for excreting organic acids, such as creatinine and other bases, into the filtrate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Reabsorbs phosphate\",\"e\":\"It is the site of phosphate reabsorption under control of parathyroid hormone. In the proximal convoluted tubule PTH acts to decrease the resorption of phosphate. PTH reduces reabsorption of phosphate in the PCT, however, because it also enhances the uptake of phosphate from the intestine and bones into the blood, the responses to PTH cancel each other out, and the serum concentration of phosphate remains approximately the same.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increases the volume of reabsorbed fluid under aldosterone stimulation\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone acts primarily on the distal convoluted tubules.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Contains renin-secreting cells\",\"e\":\"Renin is secreted by the cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus (the granular cells). The juxtaglomerular apparatus is part of the nephron, adjacent to the glomerulus. It is found between the afferent arteriole and the distal convoluted tubule.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Receives most of its blood supply from the vasa recta\",\"e\":\"The vasa recta supply the loop of Henle.\\n\\nThe proximal convoluted tubule actively reabsorbs sodium. This sets up an osmotic gradient and water is drawn out of the tubule. It is the site of phosphate reabsorption under control of parathyroid hormone (note that calcium reabsorption occurs in the ascending loop of Henle, distal tubule, and collecting tubule). Aldosterone acts primarily on the distal convoluted tubules. Renin is secreted by the cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus, between the afferent arteriole and the distal convoluted tubules.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A patient with haemorrhagic shock has a low urine output. They have several fluid challenges and begin to pass urine. The glomerular filtration rate is increased by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The glomerular filtration rate is increased by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure\",\"e\":\"If the osmotic pressure is increased the estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) will decrease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Constriction of the glomerular afferent arterioles\",\"e\":\"This will decrease the hydrostatic pressure within the glomerulus and will decrease the filtration pressure:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Constriction of the glomerular efferent arterioles\",\"e\":\"Constriction of the glomerular efferent arterioles increases the hydrostatic pressure within the glomerulus and increasing the filtration pressure and filtration rate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Saline depletion\",\"e\":\"Sodium chloride (saline) depletion leads to a decreased eGFR\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor therapy\",\"e\":\"ACE inhibitors block the conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin II. Angiotensin I| causes constriction of the efferent arteriole to increase the hydrostatic pressure within the glomerulus increasing the filtration rate. Blocking this action causes a decrease in the filtration rate.\\n\\nConstriction of the glomerular efferent arterioles increases the hydrostatic pressure within the glomerulus and hence the filtration pressure.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with pituitary pathology. There is a concern the disease may be related to the posterior part of the gland.\\n\\nVasopressin acts by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Vasopressin acts by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Enhancing sodium reabsorption in the proximal tubule\",\"e\":\"The sodium reabsorption in the proximal tubule is not under the control of vasopressin.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increasing the permeability of collecting ducts to water\",\"e\":\"Vasopressin (otherwise known as ADH) acts upon the collecting ducts. It increases reabsorption of water by increasing the permeability of the collecting ducts to water.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increasing water reabsorption in the proximal tubule\",\"e\":\"Vasopressin acts upon the collecting duct by increasing its permeability.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Enhancing counter-current exchange\",\"e\":\"Vasopressin is responsible for increasing the permeability of the collecting ducts, thereby increasing water reabsorption.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreasing potassium excretion in the distal tubule\",\"e\":\"Vasopressin has no effect on the potassium concentration of the body.\\n\\nFine tuning of salt and water balance is achieved in the distal and collecting tubules under the influence of aldosterone and vasopressin\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A patient admitted with abdominal pain has chronic renal impairment, with a plasma creatinine level of 225 umol/l (normal range 50-120 umol/l) and the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) of 25 ml/min. Their baseline creatinine is 150 umol/l\\n\\nConcerning the renal function:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The GFR is the main factor determining the rate of urine production\",\"e\":\"The rate of urine production is determined by tubular function and not by GFR\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"GFR can be measured by para-aminchippuric acid (PAH)\",\"e\":\"Para-aminohippuric acid is used to measure renal blood flow and not GFR\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The normal GFR is 50mls/min\",\"e\":\"In the normal adult human the GFR (or normal renal clearance) averages 125 ml/min.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inulin clearance can be used to estimate GFR\",\"e\":\"A substance used to measure the GFR must be freely filtered at the glomerulus, not be secreted by the tubules, not be reabsorbed, not be metabolised or synthesized in the body. not alter the renal function/GFR, be non-toxic and soluble in plasma. Such a substance is the polyfructose molecule, inulin. However, it is too cumbersome to use in routine clinical practice. Instead, GFR is more commonly quantified by measuring the 24-hour urinary creatinine excretion\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A normal plasma creatinine implies normal renal function\",\"e\":\"GFR can decrease by as much as 50% before plasma creatinine rises beyond the normal range. Consequently, a normal creatinine does not necessarily imply normal renal function, although a raised creatinine does usually indicate impaired renal function.\\n\\nIn the normal adult human the GFR (or normal renal clearance) averages 125 ml/min, or 180 I/day. The entire plasma volume (about 3 litres) can therefore be filtered and processed by the kidney approximately 60 times each day. The rate of urine production in humans is dominated by tubular function and not by GFR. The GFR remains relatively constant through autoregulation. After 35 years of age, GFR falls at about 1 ml/min/year. By the age of 80, GFR has fallen to about 50% of its youthful level. GFR. can decrease by as much as 50% before plasma creatinine rises beyond the normal range. Consequently, a normal creatinine level does not necessarily imply normal renal function, although a raised creatinine level does usually indicate impaired renal function. A substance used to measure the GFR must be freely filtered at the glomerulus, not be secreted by the tubules, not be reabsorbed, not be metabolised or synthesized in the body, not alter the renal function/GFR, be non-toxic and soluble in plasma. Such a substance is the polyfructose molecule, inulin. However, it is too cumbersome to use in routine clinical practice. Instead, the GFR is more commonly quantified by measuring the 24-h urinary creatinine excretion. Para-aminohippuric acid is used to measure renal blood flow and not GFR\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"Renin is secreted in the kidney, this stimulates conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin 1. This is converted to angiotensin Il by angiotensin converting enzyme in the lungs. The angiotensin Il in turn stimulates release of aldosterone. Aldosterone causes which one of the following effects?\",\"lead\":\"Aldosterone causes which one of the following effects?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Na+, H2O loss and K+ conservation in the distal tubule\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone leads to water conservation and potassium loss in the distal convoluted tubule.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"H2O conservation and K+ loss in the distal tubule\",\"e\":\"At the distal convoluted tubule, aldosterone acts to increase the Nat/K permeability of the luminal surface of the cells and causes resorption of Na+ and H2O in exchange for K+. which is excreted into the urine. It also acts in the collecting ducts to secrete H+ ions, thereby regulating plasma HCO3 levels.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ht loss in the distal tubule\",\"e\":\"It promotes hydrogen loss in the collecting ducts rather than the distal convoluted tubule.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Na+, H2O loss and K-conservation in the ascending limb of the loop of Henle\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone acts upon the distal convoluted tubule.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Na+, H¿O conservation and K+ loss in the ascending limb of the loop of Henle\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone promotes sodium and water conservation and potassium loss from the distal convoluted tubule.\\n\\nAldosterone is a steroid hormone produced by the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex. Its release is stimulated mainly by the renin/ angiotensin system. Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) also causes aldosterone production, as do hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and hypovolaemia. It regulates Na-/ K+ and water balance. as well as playing a role acid base balance. At the distal tubule, it acts to increase the Nat/K+ permeability of the luminal surface of the cells, and causes resorption of Nat and H2O in exchange for K+, which is excreted into the urine. It also acts in the collecting ducts to secrete Hions, thereby regulating plasma HCO3-levels.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A patient is severely injured and loses a significant amount of blood. In response to the hypovolaemia, the posterior pituitary gland produces additional antidiuretic hormone (ADH). ADH release in response to dehydration causes which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"ADH release in response to dehydration causes which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased permeability of the collecting ducts to water\",\"e\":\"Antidiuretic hormone increases the permeability of the collecting ducts to water.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased urine osmolality\",\"e\":\"It increases urine osmolality by increasing the amount of water reabsorbed from the filtrate, making it more concentrated.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased Nat resorption in the ascending limb of the loop of Henle\",\"e\":\"Sodium reabsorption in the loop of Henle is not under the control of ADH.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased Nat resorption in the descending limb of the loop of Henle\",\"e\":\"In the descending limb of the loop of Henle, sodium and water are passively resorbed.\\n\\nADH is released by the posterior pituitary in response to dehydration, from stimulation of osmoreceptors adjacent to the supraoptic nucleus, as well as volume receptors in the aorta atria and great veins. Water absorption in the collecting ducts is independent of sodium concentration, and is under the control of ADH, which causes increased permeability of the ducts. Increased ADH levels will increase the osmolality of the urine via this method in the descending limb of the loop of Henle, sodium and water are passively resorbed, but the loop is highly permeable to water and has low permeability to ions, meaning the filtrate will be hypertonic with increased osmality. The ascending limb is impermeable to water, with active sodium resorption, producing a concentration gradient in the renal medulla, which is essential for the maintenance of water balance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased permeability of the collecting ducts to water\",\"e\":\"ADH increases the permeability of the collecting ducts to water.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient's routine biochemistry as they recover from a laparotomy. You note some abnormalities and are concerned they may have developed an acute kidney injury. For which one of the following substances would you expect the renal clearance to be the lowest, under normal conditions?\",\"lead\":\"For which one of the following substances would you expect the renal clearance to be the lowest, under normal conditions?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Urea\",\"e\":\"Urea is one of the waste products of protein metabolism and is actively filtered by the kidneys.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Creatinine\",\"e\":\"Creatinine is a waste product of protein metabolism and is actively filtered by the kidneys.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sodium\",\"e\":\"The body excretes excess sodium into the urine.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Glucose\",\"e\":\"Under normal conditions the renal clearance of glucose is zero, as glucose is completely reabsorbed in the renal tubules and not excreted. Glycosuria may be an indicator of glucose intolerance and diabetes mellitus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Water\",\"e\":\"The body excretes water to form urine.\\n\\nUnder normal conditions the renal clearance of glucose is zero, as glucose is completely reabsorbed in the renal tubules and not excreted.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted with intractable vomiting. They have markedly deranged biochemistry on their bloods in keeping with an acute kidney injury When a person is dehydrated, hypotonic fluid will be found in which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"They have markedly deranged biochemistry on their bloods in keeping with an acute kidney injury When a person is dehydrated, hypotonic fluid will be found in which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Glomerular filtrate\",\"e\":\"As the glomerulus forms a passive filtrate it has the same tonicity as the plasma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Proximal tubule\",\"e\":\"The fluid in the proximal tubule of the same tonicity as plasma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Distal end of the ascending loop of Henle\",\"e\":\"Fluid in the distal end of the ascending limb of loop of Henle is hypotonic regardless of the state of hydration because of the active reabsorption of sodium chloride but not water, which is impermeable in this tubular segment.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Late distal convoluted tubule\",\"e\":\"Similar to the collecting duct, in dehydration the fluid in the late distal convoluted tubule becomes more hypertonic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Collecting duct\",\"e\":\"In dehydration the fluid in the collecting duct becomes more and more hypertonic due to the action of antidiuretic hormone (ADH).\\n\\nFluid in the distal end of the ascending limb of loop of Henle is hypotonic regardless of the state of hydration because of the active reabsorption of sodium chloride but not water, which is impermeant in this tubular segment. in dehydration, the late distal convoluted tubule and the collecting duct become more and more hypertonic in the presence of antidiuretic hormone (ADH), which increases permeability of these tubular segments. The glomerular filtrate and proximal tubule have the same tonicity as plasma.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"The normal glomerular filtration rate (GFR) for both kidneys is 125 ml/min per 1.73 m2 of body surface area. It naturally declines over a person's lifetime GFR increases due to which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"It naturally declines over a person's lifetime GFR increases due to which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Afferent arteriolar constriction\",\"e\":\"Afferent arteriolar constriction results in decreased renal blood flow and decreased glomerular pressures, leading to decreased GFR\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors\",\"e\":\"ACE inhibitors cause dilatation of the efferent arteriole with resultant reduction in GFR\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Efferent arteriolar constriction\",\"e\":\"Efferent arteriolar constriction results in increased resistance to outflow from glomeruli. This results in increased pressure across the glomerular with resultant increased GFR\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reducing real blood flow\",\"e\":\"Reduced renal blood flow reduces GFR\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sympathetic stimulation\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic stimulation causes preferential afferent arteriolar constriction, which decreases renal blood flow and decreases glomerular pressure, resulting in decreased GFR Increased renal blood flow increases the GFR, while reduced renal blood flow reduces GFR Afferent arteriolar constriction results in decreased renal blood flow and decreased glomerular pressures, leading to decreased GFR. Efferent arteriolar constriction results in increased resistance to outflow from glomeruli. This results in increased glomerular pressure with resultant increased GFR. Sympathetic stimulation causes preferential afferent arteriolar constrict on, which decreases renal blood flow and decreases glomerular pressure, resulting in decreased GFR. Angiotensin Il induces contraction of mesangial cells, causing decreased glomerular capillary area with resultant reduction in GFR.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A patient has an acute kidney injury. Their creatinine and urea remains high for number of days before the kidneys begin to recover. A defect in which part of the renal tubule will affect absorption of amino acids and glucose the most?\",\"lead\":\"A defect in which part of the renal tubule will affect absorption of amino acids and glucose the most?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Collecting duct\",\"e\":\"The collecting duct is acted upon by ADH and this increases the permeability to water.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Collecting tubule\",\"e\":\"The collecting tubule plays no part on the reabsorption of amino acids and glucose.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Distal convoluted tubule\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone promotes sodium and water conservation and potassium loss from the distal convolute tubule.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Loop of Henle\",\"e\":\"Sodium and water are absorbed in the loop of Henle.\\n\\nGlucose, amino acids. inorganic phosphate and some other solutes are reabsorbed via secondary active transport in the proximal renal tubule through co-transport channels driven by the sodium gradient out of the nephron.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Proximal convoluted tubule\",\"e\":\"Glucose, amino acids, inorganic phosphate and some other solutes are reabsorbed via secondary active transport in the proximal renal tubule through co-transport channels driven by the sodium gradient out of the nephron.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"In an experiment the juxtaglomerular cells in the kidney were selectively destroyed. These juxtaglomerular cells have a key role to play in human physiology. This will result in loss of production of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This will result in loss of production of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aldosterone\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone is produced by the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex in the adrenal gland.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Angiotensin\",\"e\":\"Angiotensin is formed by the action of renin on angiotensinogen, which is produced in the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Erythropoietin\",\"e\":\"Erythropoietin is produced by interstitial fibroblasts.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renin\",\"e\":\"Renin is produced by the juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urodilatin\",\"e\":\"Urodilatin is produced in the distal renal tubules.\\n\\nIn the kidney, the juxtaglomerular cells (JG cells) are cells that synthesise, store and secrete the enzyme renin. They are specialised smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole that delivers blood to the glomerulus. In synthesising renin, they play a critical role in the renin-angiotensin system and so in renal autoregulation, the self-governance of the kidney. In appropriately stained slides, juxtaglomerular cells are distinguished by their granulated cytoplasm.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"A patient has a severe acute kidney injury and the phosphate concentration in their blood is noted to be increasing. Approximately 80% of phosphate is absorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule Phosphate reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"is due to active co-transport with calcium ions\",\"e\":\"Reabsorption of phosphate by renal tubular cells occurs via a carrier co-transport of phosphate and sodium\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"is due to active co-transport with chloride ions\",\"e\":\"It is due to an active co-transport with sodium, rather than chloride, ions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is due to passive diffusion down its electrochemical gradient\",\"e\":\"The reabsorption is not passive as it is driven by the Na+ gradient built by the Na+/K+-ATPase pump.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"is inhibited by calcitonin\",\"e\":\"Calcitonin has only minor effects on renal calcium and phosphate handling. It lowers serum calcium by suppressing bone osteoclasts, so shifting the balance in favour of calcium deposition in the bone\\n\\nRegulation of phosphate excretion is accomplished primarily by PTH, which inhibits phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule. At high PTH concentration, as much as 40% of filtered phosphate may be excreted. Reabsorption of phosphate by renal tubular cells occurs via a carrier co-transport of phosphate and sodium. This mechanism is similarly to the reabsorption of glucose and amino acids and is driven by the Na+ gradient built by the Na+/K+-ATPase at the basolateral membrane of the tubular epithelium. Co-transport with calcium or chloride does not occur. Reabsorption of phosphate occurs against its electrochemical gradient and therefore cannot be passive. Calcitonin has only minor effects on renal calcium and phosphate handling, It lowers serum calcium by suppressing bone osteoclasts, so shifting the balance in favour of calcium deposition in the bone.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"is inhibited by parathyroid hormone\",\"e\":\"Regulation of phosphate excretion is accomplished primarily by parathyroid hormone (PTH), which inhibits phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman is diagnosed as having diabetes insipidus. in this patient although vasopressin (ADH) significantly contributes to fluid and electrolyte balance, it does not appear to closely regulate blood volume in the long run Blood volume is maintained at near normal levels in this patient because:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Plasma oncotic pressure increases\",\"e\":\"This would increase blood volume but can only be maintained for a short amount of time.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Renal blood flow decreases 1 -\",\"e\":\"Decreased renal blood flow occurs as a result of extreme circumstances, such as hypovolaemia (eg haemorrhage, dehydration), distributive shock (eg sepsis) or intense sympathetic activity. It can only be maintained for a short time.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sympathetic reflexes decrease glomerular filtration\",\"e\":\"This would be a short-term response to an acute pathological circumstance such as haemorrhage.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The peripheral renin-angiotensin system is stimulated\",\"e\":\"The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) system is activated when hypotension is identified\\n\\nIn diabetes insipidus, appropriate water intake from thirst will adequately compensate for the potential excess volume loss. Only if access to appropriate intake is prevented will a large volume loss occur. All of the other changes listed would tend to maintain blood volume, but all are either short-term effects or the result of extreme stimuli, such as haemorrhage or intense sympathetic activity.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Water intake is appropriately adjusted\",\"e\":\"In diabetes insipidus, appropriate water intake from thirst will adequately compensate for the potential excess volume loss. Diabetes insipidus is a condition where large amounts of urine are produced due to the inability of the kidney to concentrate urine. The cause can either be cranial which is due to the lack of ADH produced by the posterior pituitary, or nephrogenic which is when there is adequate levels of ADH but the kidney does not respond to it. Cranial diabetes insipidus can be caused by brain trauma or a pituitary tumour among other things. Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus can be caused by acquired diseases such as amyloidosis, drug therapy such as lithium or hypercalcaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old man is admitted to the Emergency Department having sustained a gun shot tre surg abdomen. He has lost significant amount of blood and is being stabilised before surgery. Which one of the following regarding renal blood flow is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regarding renal blood flow is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It can be estimated by para-aminohiopuric acid (PAH) clearance\",\"e\":\"Renal plasma flow can be measured by infusing PAH and determining its urine and plasma concentrations. PAH is filtered by the glomeruli and secreted by the renal tubular cells, so its excretion ratio is high. The effective renal plasma flow is equivalent to the urine clearance of PAH. PAH is not used in routine practice, where surrogates such as haemodynamic parameters and lactate are more useful.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"it decreases in hypercarbia\",\"e\":\"Hypercarbia increases renal blood flow.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It increases in hypovolaemic shock\",\"e\":\"Hypovolaemic shock decreases renal blood flow as intravascular circulating volume is reduced\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal plasma flow is given by the Starlings equation\",\"e\":\"Starling's equation is the relationship between the oncotic and hydrostatic pressures and the movement of fluid across a membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The kidneys receive roughly 75% of cardiac outflow\",\"e\":\"At rest, the kidney receives 25% of the cardiac output, approximately 1.2 litres of blood/min\\n\\nAt rest, the kidney receives 25% of the cardiac output, approximately 1.2 litres of blood/mi. Renal plasma flow can be measured by infusing PAH and determining its urine and plasma concentrations. PAH is filtered by the glomeruli and secreted by the renal tubular cells, so its excretion ratio is high. The effective renal plasma flow is equivalent to the urine clearance of PAH. Blood flow in the renal cortex is much higher than in the medulla. The factors affecting renal blood flow include: blood volume (hypovolaemia reduces renal blood flow) and catecholamines (constrict renal vessels so reducing flow).\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"Your consultant has asked you to prepare a presentation for his 2nd year medical students on the anatomy and function of the loop of Henle. You draw the descending and ascending limb from memory and begin to label the diagram. Which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chloride is transported into the ascending limb\",\"e\":\"Sodium, potassium and chloride are all co-transported out of the thick segment of the ascending limb rather than into it.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Tubular sodium concentration increases as the descending limb travels downwards\",\"e\":\"The fluid in the descending loop of Henle becomes hypertonic as water moves into the hypertonic interstitium (sodium level increases as the descending limb travels downwards).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The descending limb is impermeable to urea\",\"e\":\"Urea can permeate through the descending limb of the loop of Henle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The low osmolality maintains the counter-current mechanism\",\"e\":\"In the ascending limb, the fluid in the tubules becomes more dilute and is hypotonic to plasma because of movement of sodium and chloride out of the tubular lumen. This forms the mechanism of the counter-current multiplier.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Water moves out of the ascending limb into the interstitium\",\"e\":\"The ascending loop is impermeable to water.\\n\\nThe descending loop of Henle is permeable to water but the ascending loop is impermeable. Sodium, potassium and chloride are co-transported out of the thick segment of the ascending limb. Therefore, a gradient exists whereby the fluid in the descending loop of Henle becomes hypertonic as water moves into the hypertonic interstitium (sodium level increases as the descending limb travels downwards). In the ascending limb, the fluid in the tubules becomes more dilute and is hypotonic to plasma because of movement of sodium and chloride out of the tubular lumen. This forms the mechanism of the counter-current\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old novice athlete partakes in his first 10 000 metre race. Along the way, he neglects to drink any fluids. Which one of the following occurs in dehydration?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following occurs in dehydration?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Approximately 15% of the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed in the proximal renal tubule\",\"e\":\"Seventy per cent of the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed in the proximal renal tubule.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fluid in the distal convoluted tubule is hypertonic with respect to plasma\",\"e\":\"The fluid is hypotonic compared with plasma in the distal convoluted tubule.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Interstitial fluid in the medulla of the kidney is hypotonic\",\"e\":\"The fluid in the medulla of the kidney is hypertonic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal blood flow will increase\",\"e\":\"Dehydration leads to decreased intravascular volume and so renal perfusion falls. Dehydration leads to splanchnic and visceral vasoconstriction, so renal blood flow falls. This causes a drop in urine output and a minimum obligatory urine output of 500ml/24 h may result. The secretion of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) leading to increased water reabsorption is another mechanism by which this occurs. The Loops of Henle of the juxtamedullary nephrons dip deeply into the medullary pyramids before draining into the distal convoluted tubules in the cortex. There is a graded increase in the osmolality at the tips of the papillae (around 1200 mOsm/l approximately equal to four times that of plasma).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urine output may decrease to around 600 ml/24\",\"e\":\"A fall in renal blood flow can cause a decrease in the urine output and it can be as low as 500 ml/24 h.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"Your consultant has asked you to prepare a presentation for his second-year medical students on the anatomy and function of the distal convoluted tubule. You produce a diagram of the kidney and begin to label it. The distal convoluted tubule is the site of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The distal convoluted tubule is the site of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aldosterone-regulated reabsorption of sodium\",\"e\":\"The reabsorption of sodium in this segment is regulated by aldosterone.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chloride in seton. N -IN\",\"e\":\"Chloride ions are absorbed along with sodium ions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Principal water reabsorption in the kidney\",\"e\":\"The principle water reabsorption in the kidney occurs in the proximal convoluted tubule.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sodium secretion\",\"e\":\"Sodium absorption occurs in the distal convoluted tubule rather than sodium secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The primary site of action of antidiuretic hormone (ADH)\",\"e\":\"ADH acts primarily upon the collecting ducts. The first part of the distal convoluted tubule is effectively an extension of the thick segment of the loop of Henle, and is therefore relatively impermeable to water. The reabsorption of sodium in this segment is regulated by aldosterone. Aldosterone also has the effect of increasing the secretion of potassium and hydrogen ions into the tubular lumen Approximately 5% of the filtered water is removed in this segment of the kidney. ADH acts predominantly on the collecting ducts to increase their permeability to water, so leading to water retention.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man is admitted to the Emergency Department having sustained a stab injury to his abdomen. He has lost a significant amount of blood and is being stabilised before surgery. Which one of the following regarding renal blood flow is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regarding renal blood flow is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Autoregulation is unimpeded by shock, sepsis and low cardiac states\",\"e\":\"Autoregulation is impaired in shock, sepsis and low cardiac output states.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Renal blood flow is higher in the medulla than in the cortex\",\"e\":\"Blood flow in the cortex is much higher than in the medulla.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Renal blood flow is unaffected by hypertension\",\"e\":\"In hypertensive patients there is a shift of autoregulation, so that they have a slightly reduced renal blood flow for a given blood pressure\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal blood flow is normally kept constant by autoregulation\",\"e\":\"At normal systemic blood pressure the renal vascular resistance adjusts the pressure in the renal arterioles so that renal blood flow remains fairly constant (autoregulation).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Renal blood flow may be increased by 50% in acute renal failure\",\"e\":\"In acute renal failure, renal blood flow is often decreased due to causes such as hypovolaemia.\\n\\nAt normal systemic blood pressure the renal vascular resistance adjusts the pressure in the renal arterioles so that renal blood flow remains fairly constant (autoregulation). In hypertensive patients there is a shift of autoregulation, so that they have a slightly reduced renal blood flow for a given blood pressure. Blood flow in the cortex is much higher than in the medulla. Autoregulation is impaired in shock, sepsis and low cardiac output states.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"You are on an Intensive Care Unit (ITU) ward round when the consultant asks you how best to measure renal function. The consultant gives you a list of examples. Which one of the following statements concerns an ideal substance for measuring glomerular filtration rate (GFR)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements concerns an ideal substance for measuring glomerular filtration rate (GFR)?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Creatinine is an ideal substance for measuring the GFR\",\"e\":\"Creatinine is not an ideal substance for measuring GFR as a small amount is secreted by the tubules. This means that slightly more creatinine is excreted in the urine than is filtered Baseline creatinine is also affected by an individual's muscle mass.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Insulin is an ideal substance for measuring the GFR\",\"e\":\"Inulin (not insulin), a polysaccharide molecule, meets these criteria for an ideal substance.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The substance should be secreted by the renal tubule\",\"e\":\"The substance should not be secreted by the renal tubules.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The substance may be reabsorbed by the renal tubule\",\"e\":\"The substance cannot be reabsorbed by the renal tubule as it will give a false filtration rate.\\n\\nAn ideal substance for measuring the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) should be freely filtered at the glomerulus and neither secreted nor reabsorbed by the tubules Inulin (not insulin), a polysaccharide molecule, meets these criteria for an ideal substance. It can be administered intravenously to patients to measure their GFR. Creatinine is not an ideal substance for measuring GFR as a small amount is secreted by the tubules. This means that slightly more creatinine is excreted in the urine than is filtered. However, the laboratory usually underestimates the plasma creatinine level and so these two errors cancel one another out. Therefore creatinine provides a useful clinical estimation of GFR.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The substance should be freely filtered by the glomerulus\",\"e\":\"An ideal substance for measuring the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) should be freely filtered at the glomerulus and neither secreted nor reabsorbed by the tubules.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"You are on an Intensive Care Unit (ITU) ward round when the consultant asks you how best to measure renal function. He gives you a list of substrates to choose from. Of the following, which one can be best used to measure glomerular filtration rate (GFR)?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following, which one can be best used to measure glomerular filtration rate (GFR)?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ADH\",\"e\":\"ADH acts upon the collecting ducts and cannot be used to estimate the GFR\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glucose\",\"e\":\"Glucose is reabsorbed and therefore cannot be used to estimate GFR.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inulin\",\"e\":\"An ideal substance for measuring the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) should be freely filtered at the glomerulus and neither secreted nor reabsorbed by the tubules. Inulin, a polysaccharide molecule, meets these criteria for an ideal substance.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Insulin\",\"e\":\"Insulin is not used to estimate the GFR\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Para-amino hippuric acid (PAH)\",\"e\":\"PAH is secreted by the tubules and cannot be used to estimate GER but can be used to assess renal perfusion. GFR can be measured using the relationship GFR = UV/P for any substrate that is freely filtered and not reabsorbed or secreted (UV is the amount of urine per minute and P is the concentration in plasma). Inulin fulfils those criteria. Clinically GFR is estimated using creatinine. Glucose is reabsorbed and PAH is secreted and therefore they are so not suitable for the measurement of GFR.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old woman is admitted to coronary care following a myocardial infarct. Her renal blood flow and renal perfusion pressure fall, causing a drop in urine output Which one of the following factors would be responsible for increasing renal blood flow and renal perfusion pressure?\",\"lead\":\"Her renal blood flow and renal perfusion pressure fall, causing a drop in urine output Which one of the following factors would be responsible for increasing renal blood flow and renal perfusion pressure?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Angiotensinogen\",\"e\":\"Angiotensinogen is a precursor of angiotensin I that is activated by renin. It has no effect on the renal blood flow in this form.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"IT Antidiuretic hormone\",\"e\":\"ADH has no effect on the renal blood flow or perfusion. It can reduce urine output by increasing water reabsorption in the collecting ducts.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Endothelin\",\"e\":\"Endothelin is a potent vasoconstrictor secreted by endothelial cells of arterioles, which decreases the renal blood flow.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Insulin\",\"e\":\"Insulin has no effect on the renal blood flow.\\n\\nThe renal blood flow and renal perfusion are controlled by several factors including hormones and the sympathetic nervous system. Atrial natriuretic peptide causes dilatation of the afferent arterioles and increases the renal blood flow and the glomerular filtration rate. Prostaglandins (PGE2 and PG|2) increase the renal blood flow and the glomerular filtration rate. Angiotensin I| (not angiotensinogen) are produced systemically, and within the kidney, primarily constricts the efferent arterioles to maintain glomerular filtration rate. Adenosine triphosphate selectively vasoconstricts the afferent arteriole and so reduces the renal blood flow. Endothelin is a potent vasoconstrictor secreted by endothelial cells of arterioles, which decreases the renal blood flow. Glucocorticoids dilate the afferent arterioles and increase the renal blood flow and the glomerular filtration rate. Likewise, nitric oxide also causes vasodilatation and increases the renal blood flow\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prostaglandins\",\"e\":\"Prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) increase the renal blood flow and the glomerular filtration rate:\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old woman who has recently been diagnosed with polycystic kidneys is referred to a Renal Clinic as it is noted her glomerular filtration rate is quite low. They recommend she is put on the waiting list for a renal transplant with haemodialysis to commence in the interim.\\n\\nWhat is the normal glomerular filtration rate approximately in a normal individual?\",\"lead\":\"What is the normal glomerular filtration rate approximately in a normal individual?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"12 ml/min\",\"e\":\"in the normal individual the glomerular filtration rate is kept fairly constant at 120 ml/min. The ultrafiltrate is essentially plasma without the proteins (these are not able to cross the basement membrane of the glomerulus). The GFR is governed by the rate of glomerular blood flow, which is governed by the tone of BOTH the afferent and efferent arterioles. This is kept constant over a range of blood pressures. Glucose is able to cross the glomerular basement membrane into the ultrafiltrate. In normal individuals it is then actively reabsorbed. The resorption mechanism has a maximal rate. If the glucose concentration of the serum, and consequently the ultrafiltrate is too high, then the mechanism is overwhelmed and glycosuria occurs (as occurs in diabetes mellitus).\\n\\nThis would be 10% of a normal individual.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"24 ml/min\",\"e\":\"This is 20% of a normal individual's filtration rate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"64 ml/min\",\"e\":\"64 ml/min is lower than average.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"120 m/min?-\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"240 ml/min\",\"e\":\"A normal individual has a rate of 120 ml/min.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman with suspected meningitis presents in shock and is admitted to ITU. She has a central line placed within the subclavian vein and she has a urinary catheter. Her urine output tails off in the proceeding 24 h to 14 ml/h Which one of the following statements on glomerular filtration is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Her urine output tails off in the proceeding 24 h to 14 ml/h Which one of the following statements on glomerular filtration is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ADH acts on the proximal tubule\",\"e\":\"ADH acts upon the collecting ducts to increase the permeability to water. This increases water reabsorption.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Allows the passage of glucose into the ultrafiltrate in the normal individual\",\"e\":\"Glucose is able to cross the glomerular basement membrane into the ultrafiltrate: In normal individuals it is then actively reabsorbed.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Depends on venous return\",\"e\":\"The glomerular filtration rate is dependent on the rate of glomerular (arterial) blood flow. which is governed by the tone of BOTH the afferent and efferent arterioles.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is normally governed by the blood pressure\",\"e\":\"The filtration rate is kept constant over a range of blood pressures by autoregulation. It is kept constant by the tone of the afferent and efferent arterioles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Usually takes place at 24 ml/min\",\"e\":\"in the normal individual the glomerular filtration rate is kept fairly constant at 120 ml/min.\\n\\nin the normal individual the glomerular filtration rate is kept fairly constant at 120 ml/min. The ultrafiltrate is essentially plasma without the proteins (these are not able to cross the basement membrane of the glomerulus). The GFR is governed by the rate of glomerular blood flow, which is governed by the tone of BOTH the afferent and efferent arterioles. This is kept constant over a range of blood pressures. Glucose is able to cross the glomerular basement membrane into the ultrafiltrate. In normal individuals it is then actively re-absorbed. The resorption mechanism has a maximal rate. If the glucose concentration of the serum, and consequently the ultrafiltrate is too high, then the mechanism is overwhelmed and glycosuria occurs (as occurs in diabetes mellitus).\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"Glucose is an essential substrate for the metabolism of most cells. Its uptake into cells is an important process to understand The transport of glucose in the renal tubular cells occurs via which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Its uptake into cells is an important process to understand The transport of glucose in the renal tubular cells occurs via which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Passive transport\",\"e\":\"Glucose is reabsorbed by active transport but it requires secondary active transport with sodium.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"concentration gradient\",\"e\":\"Glucose reabsorption requires the energy (ATP) provided by co-transport of Nations.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Failed dilution 1\",\"e\":\"Glucose is reabsorbed by active transport rather than diffusion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Secondary active transport with sodium\",\"e\":\"Glucose is reabsorbed by using the energy (ATP) provided by co-transport of Na+ ions down their electrochemical gradient.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Secondary active transport with potassium\",\"e\":\"Glucose uses the secondary active transport with sodium; not potassium.\\n\\nGlucose is an essential substrate for the metabolism of most cells. Glucose transport through biological membranes requires specific transport proteins. Transport of glucose through the apical membrane of renal tubular as weil as intestinal epithelial cells depends on the presence of secondary active Nat-glucose symporters, sodium-glucose linked transporter (SGLT) -1 and SGLT-2, which concentrate glucose inside the cells, using the energy provided by co-transport of Nations down their electrochemical gradient.\"}]},{\"t\":82,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old man is due to have an elective hydrocele repair and attends the pre-assessment clinic. He is a type 2 diabetic on metformin. His urinalysis shows glucose 3+ (result 1000 mg/dL; normal glucose ≤15 mg/dL). Where does glucose reabsorption in the kidney take place?\",\"lead\":\"Where does glucose reabsorption in the kidney take place?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Proximal convoluted tubule\",\"e\":\"Glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule along with other substances including sodium, water and potassium. When the amount of glucose in the filtrate exceeds threshold, glucosuria results.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Descending limb of loop of Henle\",\"e\":\"The descending limb is highly permeable to water which is reabsorbed by osmosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ascending limb of loop of Henle\",\"e\":\"The ascending limb of the loop of Henle is relatively impermeable to water but active reabsorption of NaCi occurs here:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Distal convoluted tubule\",\"e\":\"The distal convoluted tubule reabsorbs water and sodium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Collecting duct\",\"e\":\"The collecting duct is the last component of the nephron responsible for the reabsorption of water sodium and\"}]}]}");